VEL.register("cvi",{"id":"cvi","track":{"code":"CVI","name":"Certified Ventilation Inspector","kind":"Exam prep","org":"NADCA","domains":[["HVAC Systems",30],["Standards",10],["IAQ",10],["Inspection",40],["Risk Management",10]],"exam":{"questions":150,"minutes":210,"cut":72,"cutNote":"NADCA does not publish the CVI cut score. 72% is borrowed from ASCS as a working line.","published":true,"weightsPublished":true,"note":"Format and weights are from the 2023 CVI Candidate's Guide, the newest guide NADCA links as of September 2026. Check nadca.com for a newer edition."},"facts":["150 multiple-choice questions, 3.5 hours, proctored. Not offered online.","You need an active ASCS to sit it.","Performing an inspection is 40% of the exam; HVAC systems and components is 30%.","Register: get a voucher code from NADCA HQ (1-855-GO-NADCA, membership@nadca.com), then create an account and buy the exam at nadcatesting.com.","This bank is built from NADCA's HVAC Inspection Manual (the basis of the CVI), EPA's I-BEAM (the base of NADCA's CVI course), ACR 2025, and public standards."],"sims":[[30,"Quick check"],[50,"Short form"],[150,"Full length"]],"sources":[["NADCA CVI Candidate's Guide (2023 edition)","https://nadca.com/sites/nadca/files/docs/2023/CVI%20candidate%27s%20guide%202023.pdf"],["NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual (members)","https://nadca.com/store"],["ACR 2025, The NADCA Standard","https://acrstandard.nadca.com/"],["EPA I-BEAM text modules","https://19january2021snapshot.epa.gov/sites/static/files/2014-12/documents/text_modules_all.pdf"],["ASHRAE 62.1 and 62.2","https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/standards-62-1-62-2"],["EPA Building Air Quality guide","https://www.epa.gov/indoor-air-quality-iaq/building-air-quality-guide-guide-building-owners-and-facility-managers"],["OSHA 29 CFR 1910.146 permit-required confined spaces","https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-XVII/part-1910/subpart-J/section-1910.146"]]},"units":[{"id":"acr:G-1","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"ACR 2025 is the standard of care for the HVAC cleaning and restoration industry.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What is ACR 2025 to the HVAC cleaning and restoration industry?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-2","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"ACR 2025 supersedes all previous editions of the Standard.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What happens to earlier editions of the Standard now that ACR 2025 is out?","trap":"Guides built on ACR 2021 cost points. 2021 wording (mold, AHJ, old method numbers) is superseded.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-3","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"ACR is a performance standard. It also gives minimum procedural requirements.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What type of standard is ACR, and what else does it give beyond performance?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-4","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The Standard defines the minimum performance requirements and procedural requirements.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Which two kinds of minimum requirements does the Standard define?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-9a","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The Standard gives minimum standards and guidance to industry professionals and service providers.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What does the Standard give to industry professionals and service providers?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-9b","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"ACR 2025 also gives guidance to building owners and others who manage HVAC systems and projects.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Besides industry professionals, who else does the Standard give guidance to?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-5","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The Standard applies to the airside surfaces of HVAC components.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Which surfaces of HVAC components does the Standard apply to?","trap":"Airside surface excludes open air plenums and interstitial cavities (Definitions).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-6","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The scope excludes non-ducted open air plenums.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Which type of plenum does the ACR 2025 scope exclude?","trap":"A return plenum inside ductwork is in scope (1.7.3). The scope clause excludes non-ducted open air plenums, not ducted return plenums.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-7","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The scope excludes mechanical repair of electrical components and pneumatic components.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Mechanical repair of which two kinds of components is outside the ACR scope?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-8","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The scope excludes repair of high-pressure vessels, gas controls, and oil controls.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Repair of which vessels and which controls is outside the ACR scope?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0384"]},{"id":"acr:G-9","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The scope excludes preventative maintenance tasks that the OEM prescribes or recommends.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Which maintenance tasks does the ACR scope exclude?","trap":"OEM preventative maintenance is outside the scope, yet cleaning as part of OEM recommended maintenance is still a need-to-clean trigger.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0120"]},{"id":"acr:G-13","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The Standard does not address all hazards and risks.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Does the Standard claim to address every hazard and risk of the work?","trap":"The Standard also names no PPE anywhere. It is an engineering controls document.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-16","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"NOTE","text":"NADCA gives no warranty of energy savings.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What warranty does NADCA give about energy savings from work to the Standard?","trap":"Any answer that promises a fixed or typical percentage of energy savings is wrong.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0329"]},{"id":"acr:G-17","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"NOTE","text":"NADCA gives no warranty that the work corrects indoor air quality concerns.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Does NADCA warrant that work to the Standard corrects indoor air quality concerns?","trap":"An IAQ program is a reason to clean, but NADCA warrants no IAQ correction.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-19","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"DEF","text":"SHALL denotes a mandatory requirement. The conformance criterion permits no deviation.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What does SHALL denote in ACR 2025, and how much deviation does it permit?","trap":"SHALL is the only one of the four words that is a requirement.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-20","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"DEF","text":"RECOMMEND means that the practice is advised or suggested. It is not a requirement.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What does RECOMMEND mean in the Standard, and is it a requirement?","trap":"Recommended clauses often read mandatory: hazmat consultation, ASCS oversight, all 16 triggers to clean. None of them is required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-21","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"DEF","text":"MAY signifies permission that the document expresses. It is not a requirement.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What does MAY signify in the Standard, and is it a requirement?","trap":"MAY is permission, not ability. Swapping MAY and CAN is the most missed definition.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-22","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"DEF","text":"CAN signifies an ability or a possibility open to the user. It is not a requirement.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What does CAN signify in the Standard, and is it a requirement?","trap":"CAN is ability or possibility, not permission. Neither word creates a requirement.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-23","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"DEF","text":"A Requirement is a mandatory practice for compliance with the Standard.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"How does the Standard define a Requirement?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-24","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"NOTE","text":"MAY gives permission. CAN gives ability or possibility.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Which pair of obligation words is missed most, and what separates them?","trap":"This is the most missed obligation-word pair in ACR.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-25","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"NOTE","text":"Component lists in the Standard read \"but not limited to\". They are open lists, not closed lists.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"How do you read a component list in the Standard that says 'but not limited to'?","trap":"An item missing from a listed set is not excluded by that omission. The list gives examples.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-10","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"SHALL","text":"A person who does work to this Standard has training and qualifications first.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Besides relevant knowledge and experience, what must a person have before doing work to this Standard?","trap":"Training and qualification are required. ASCS is only recommended: for project oversight here, and as an example qualified inspector in 1.2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-11","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"SHALL","text":"A person who does work to ACR 2025 has relevant knowledge and experience before work of any type, in addition to training and qualifications.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Besides training and qualifications, what must a person have before work of any type?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-12","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"REC","text":"An ASCS, or a person with equivalent certification, has responsibility for project oversight.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Who is recommended to have responsibility for project oversight?","trap":"Recommended, not required. An equivalent certification also meets it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-9","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"NOTE","text":"ASCS oversight is a recommendation. Training and qualification are mandatory.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Of ASCS oversight and training and qualification, which is recommended and which is mandatory?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-14","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"SHALL","text":"The user does the work in strict accordance with applicable codes, laws, and regulations.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"How must the user do the work with respect to codes, laws, and regulations?","trap":"2025 dropped the Authority Having Jurisdiction wording. An answer that cites the AHJ reflects the 2021 edition.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0121"]},{"id":"acr:G-14a","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition directed the user to the Authority Having Jurisdiction here.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"In the clause on following codes, laws, and regulations, what did the 2021 edition direct the user to that 2025 no longer names?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0121"]},{"id":"acr:G-15","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"SHALL","text":"A code, law, or regulation on microbial assessment or remediation supersedes contradictory text.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"When a code, law, or regulation on microbial assessment or remediation contradicts the Standard, which governs?","trap":"The law wins over the Standard's text, not the other way around.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-18","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"MAY","text":"Common sense, experience, and professional judgment may justify a deviation in certain circumstances.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What may justify a deviation from the Standard in certain circumstances?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0394"]},{"id":"acr:N-0","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"ACR 2025 recommends cleaning when a cleanliness inspection or building history shows any one of the listed need-to-clean conditions.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Under Determining the Need to Clean, what is recommended to start a cleaning, and how many listed conditions does it take?","trap":"All 16 triggers are recommendations. None of them makes cleaning mandatory.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0266","cvi-0365"]},{"id":"acr:N-1","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Under ACR 2025, cleaning is recommended when components show the presence of particulate.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"What is the lowest particulate trigger for cleaning in ACR 2025?","trap":"2021 needed components contaminated with an accumulation. 2025 needs only the presence of particulate.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0265"]},{"id":"acr:N-18","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"NOTE","text":"ACR 2025 lowered the first need-to-clean trigger from components \"contaminated with an accumulation\" to components showing the presence of particulate.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"How did ACR 2025 change the first need-to-clean trigger from the 2021 wording?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0265"]},{"id":"acr:N-2","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when accumulated particulate compromises the performance of the components.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger ties particulate to how well the components work?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-3","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when the components are a source of unacceptable odors.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger is based on smell?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-4","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when the components discharge visible dirt or debris into the conditioned space.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger is about what the system blows into the conditioned space?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-5","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when fire, smoke, or water damage contaminates the components.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which three kinds of damage that contaminate components are a need-to-clean trigger?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-6","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when birds, rodents, insects, or their byproducts infest the components.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which infestations of components are a need-to-clean trigger?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0266"]},{"id":"acr:N-7","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when the components are at risk for fire hazard.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger is about a risk rather than a contaminant already present?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0267"]},{"id":"acr:N-8","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when construction particulate or debris contaminates existing components.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger covers construction contamination of existing components?","trap":"A separate trigger covers a newly installed component or duct contaminated by construction dust.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-16","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when construction dust or other debris contaminates a newly installed component or duct.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger applies to a newly installed component or duct?","trap":"Newly installed does not mean exempt. Construction dust on new work is its own trigger.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-9","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when microbial contamination reaches Condition 2 or Condition 3.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"At which microbial Conditions is cleaning a listed trigger?","trap":"Condition 1 is a normal fungal ecology and is not a trigger.","delta":true,"num":{"q":"Microbial contamination at Condition __ is a need-to-clean trigger.","a":"2 or 3","d":["1 or 2","3 only","1, 2, or 3"]},"qs":["cvi-0282"]},{"id":"acr:N-10","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when fibrous glass duct liner, duct board, or other porous components deteriorate.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger covers deterioration, and of which materials?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0259"]},{"id":"acr:N-11","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when the work is part of an HVAC maintenance program defined by ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which standard's HVAC maintenance program is a need-to-clean trigger?","trap":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 covers inspection and maintenance. NFPA 90A covers installation.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0365"]},{"id":"acr:N-12","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when the work is part of the equipment manufacturer's recommended maintenance practices.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger comes from the equipment maker's guidance?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-13","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when the work is part of a proactive energy management program.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger is tied to energy?","trap":"An energy program justifies cleaning, but NADCA warrants no energy savings.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-14","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when the work is part of a proactive indoor air quality management program.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger is tied to managing indoor air quality?","trap":"NADCA still gives no warranty that cleaning corrects IAQ concerns.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-15","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Cleaning is recommended when the work is a component of LEED Certification.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which green building certification is listed as a need-to-clean trigger?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-17","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"MAY","text":"When the need to clean is in question, Method 2 or Method 3 determines that need.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"When the need to clean is in question, which methods may determine it?","trap":"Method 3 is the NADCA Vacuum Test. 2021 had two Method 2s, so older guides number the methods differently.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0274","cvi-0275"]},{"id":"acr:1.0-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspection determines the need for cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.0","prompt":"Per the Section 1 overview, what does an inspection determine about cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0210"]},{"id":"acr:1.0-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspection determines the scope of work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.0","prompt":"Besides the need for cleaning, what does an inspection determine about the extent of the job?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0210"]},{"id":"acr:1.0-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspection determines the engineering controls, the safety measures, and the equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.0","prompt":"Which three job setup items does an inspection determine under 1.0?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0210","cvi-0279","cvi-0378"]},{"id":"acr:1.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspection occurs before the cleaning and restoration project.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.1","prompt":"When must the first inspection of a cleaning and restoration project occur?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0280","cvi-0366"]},{"id":"acr:1.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspection occurs after the cleaning and restoration project.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.1","prompt":"Besides before the project, when else must an inspection occur?","trap":"Do not merge it with verification, which occurs right after each component is cleaned, before the system operates (5.1).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0280","cvi-0366"]},{"id":"acr:1.1-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"REC","text":"Routine inspections are part of a maintenance, energy, and indoor air quality plan.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.1","prompt":"Where do routine inspections fit, according to 1.1?","trap":"Routine inspections are recommended. The before and after project inspections are required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.10-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"Information from the inspection is documented and evaluated in the system component assessment.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"What happens to the information gathered in the inspection, per 1.10?","trap":"An inspection gathers information. An assessment reviews and evaluates it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.10-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The system component assessment gives a recommendation on the need for cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"What does the system component assessment give about whether to clean?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0213","cvi-0293"]},{"id":"acr:1.10-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The system component assessment gives a clearly defined scope of work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"What kind of scope of work must the system component assessment give?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0213"]},{"id":"acr:1.10-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The system component assessment gives the recommended cleaning techniques.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"Which assessment output covers how the components will be cleaned?","trap":"The assessment shall give recommended techniques, but means and methods in the work plan (2.3) are only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0288"]},{"id":"acr:1.10-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The system component assessment determines the required environmental engineering controls.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"Which assessment output covers protection of the work environment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0279","cvi-0289","cvi-0378"]},{"id":"acr:1.10-f","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The system component assessment gives any unique requirements for the job.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"What catch-all item must the system component assessment give?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0281","cvi-0290"]},{"id":"acr:1.2-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"REC","text":"A qualified inspector, such as an ASCS or a CVI, determines the preliminary state of cleanliness.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"Who is recommended to determine the preliminary state of cleanliness?","trap":"ASCS or CVI is a recommendation. The four areas of verifiable knowledge are the requirement.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.2-h","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"NOTE","text":"Certification is a recommendation. The four knowledge areas are the mandatory minimum.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"In 1.2, which part is a recommendation and which is the mandatory minimum?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0221"]},{"id":"acr:1.2-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"At minimum, the inspector has verifiable working knowledge of basic HVAC system design.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"Which required inspector knowledge area concerns how HVAC systems are designed, and at what level?","trap":"The word is verifiable working knowledge. It is required whether or not the inspector holds a certification.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0221"]},{"id":"acr:1.2-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"The inspector has verifiable working knowledge of fundamental HVAC engineering practices.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"Which required inspector knowledge area concerns HVAC engineering?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.2-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"The inspector has verifiable working knowledge of current cleaning and restoration techniques.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"Which required inspector knowledge area concerns cleaning methods?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.2-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"The inspector has verifiable working knowledge of applicable industry standards.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"Which required inspector knowledge area concerns standards?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.2-f","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspector for microbial contamination has qualification through training and experience.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"How must an inspector for microbial contamination be qualified?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0122"]},{"id":"acr:1.2-g","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspector for microbial contamination holds a license, where the law requires one, to determine Conditions 1, 2, and 3.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"When must a microbial inspector hold a license to determine Conditions 1, 2, and 3?","trap":"A license is required only where the law requires one, not everywhere.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0122","cvi-0222","cvi-0383"]},{"id":"acr:1.2.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"Before the inspection, the inspector understands the effect of the inspection on the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2.1","prompt":"Before the inspection, what must the inspector understand about the building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0380"]},{"id":"acr:1.2.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"The inspector understands the effect of the inspection on the occupants.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2.1","prompt":"In the 1.2.1 risk assessment, whose welfare, besides the building, must the inspector consider?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0223","cvi-0380"]},{"id":"acr:1.3-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"DEF","text":"HVAC mechanical plans and building plans give information for the scope of work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3","prompt":"What do HVAC mechanical plans and building plans give for the job?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.3-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"Building floor plans and mechanical plans are in use during the inspection, if they exist.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3","prompt":"When must building floor plans and mechanical plans be in use during an inspection?","trap":"Required only if the plans exist.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0217"]},{"id":"acr:1.3-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"Building floor plans and mechanical plans are also in use during the cleaning work and the restoration work, not only during the inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3","prompt":"Besides the inspection, when else must the floor plans and mechanical plans be in use?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0217"]},{"id":"acr:1.4-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"DEF","text":"Inspection activities can influence the indoor environment of a building.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.4","prompt":"What can inspection activities influence in a building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.4-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"DEF","text":"The primary concern is disturbance of settled particulate.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.4","prompt":"What is the primary concern when inspection activities affect the indoor environment?","trap":"Disturbing settled particulate is first. Release into occupied areas is the second concern.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0239","cvi-0368"]},{"id":"acr:1.4-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"DEF","text":"The second concern is release of disturbed particles into occupied areas.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.4","prompt":"What is the second concern for the indoor environment during an inspection?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0239","cvi-0368"]},{"id":"acr:1.4-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"Appropriate engineering controls manage the general workspace environment during an inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.4","prompt":"What must manage the general workspace environment during an inspection?","trap":"Engineering controls are required during the inspection itself, not only during cleaning.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0223","cvi-0367"]},{"id":"acr:1.5-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"REC","text":"Component inspections are part of the building maintenance, energy, and indoor air quality plan.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5","prompt":"Per 1.5, component inspections are recommended as part of which building plan?","trap":"Recommended, not required. Only the visual means of routine inspection (1.5.1) is a SHALL.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0123"]},{"id":"acr:1.5-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"REC","text":"The inspections follow ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 and NFPA 90A.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5","prompt":"Which two standards are routine inspections recommended to follow?","trap":"Recommended, not required. ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 is the maintenance standard, NFPA 90A the installation standard.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0123"]},{"id":"acr:1.5-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 covers inspection and maintenance of commercial building HVAC systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5","prompt":"What does ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.5-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"NFPA 90A covers installation of air-conditioning systems and ventilating systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5","prompt":"What does NFPA 90A cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Routine inspection of components for cleanliness occurs by visual means.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1","prompt":"By what means must routine inspection of components for cleanliness occur?","trap":"The visual means is a SHALL, even though the Table 1 schedule itself is only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Table 1 gives a recommended inspection schedule. The intervals are minimum recommendations.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1","prompt":"What kind of schedule does Table 1 give, and what are its intervals?","trap":"Table 1 is recommended, not required, and its intervals are minimums. More frequent inspection fits it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0270","cvi-0362"]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"REC","text":"When geographical, human, or mechanical conditions make it necessary, inspections occur more often.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1","prompt":"When are inspections recommended more often than the Table 1 intervals?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0270","cvi-0362"]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"The air-handling unit interval is 1 year for all five building use classifications.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1, Table 1","prompt":"What is the Table 1 air-handling unit inspection interval, and for which building uses?","trap":"Residential ducts are 2 years, but the Residential AHU is still 1 year.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 1 AHU interval, Residential vs Healthcare: __.","a":"1 year vs 1 year","d":["2 years vs 1 year","1 year vs 2 years","2 years vs 2 years"]},"qs":["cvi-0361"]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Residential and Marine share the same intervals. Both are 1 year, 2 years, 2 years.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1, Table 1","prompt":"Which two Table 1 building uses have 2-year duct intervals, and what are their three intervals?","trap":"Marine groups with Residential, not Commercial. Ship ducts are 2 years.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 1 intervals for Residential and Marine (AHU, supply, return and exhaust) are __.","a":"1 year, 2 years, 2 years","d":["1 year, 1 year, 1 year","2 years, 2 years, 2 years","1 year, 1 year, 2 years"]},"qs":["cvi-0268","cvi-0269","cvi-0361"]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-f","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Commercial, Industrial, and Healthcare share the same intervals. All three are 1 year.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1, Table 1","prompt":"Which three Table 1 building uses have 1-year intervals for everything?","trap":"Healthcare is not stricter than Commercial or Industrial. The third column covers return and exhaust ducts together.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 1 intervals for Commercial, Industrial, and Healthcare (AHU, supply, return and exhaust) are __.","a":"1 year, 1 year, 1 year","d":["1 year, 2 years, 2 years","2 years, 2 years, 2 years","1 year, 2 years, 1 year"]},"qs":["cvi-0269"]},{"id":"acr:1.6-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Components collect large amounts of debris and particulate during construction activities.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.6","prompt":"Why do construction activities matter for HVAC components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.6-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"REC","text":"An inspection for cleanliness occurs before the system operates.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.6","prompt":"For construction, retrofit, or remodel work, what is recommended before the system operates?","trap":"2025 recommends inspection only. 2021 required inspection and verification as clean.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0125","cvi-0212"]},{"id":"acr:1.6-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition required inspection and verification as clean. The 2025 edition asks only for inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.6","prompt":"How did the 1.6 construction clause change from 2021 to 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0125","cvi-0212"]},{"id":"acr:1.7-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cleanliness inspection includes a minimum of 10% of the components under inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7","prompt":"What minimum share of the components under inspection must a cleanliness inspection include?","trap":"10% is the inspection minimum only. Verification covers all specified components, and S520 projects inspect all components.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Outside an S520 remediation project, the minimum share of components a cleanliness inspection must include is __.","a":"10%","d":["5%","100%","99.97%"]},"qs":["cvi-0240","cvi-0286"]},{"id":"acr:1.7-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"DEF","text":"Components include supply air ducts, return air ducts, and VAV boxes.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7","prompt":"Besides fan powered terminal boxes and mixing boxes, which components does 1.7 name?","trap":"The list is open ('but not limited to'). Unlisted components still count.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"DEF","text":"Components also include fan powered terminal boxes and mixing boxes.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7","prompt":"Which two other box-type components does 1.7 name besides VAV boxes?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"For a microbial remediation project to ANSI/IICRC S520, the inspection includes all components.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7","prompt":"How much of the system must the inspection include for a microbial remediation project to ANSI/IICRC S520?","trap":"S500 is water damage and S590 is HVAC assessment. Only S520 remediation triggers the all-components inspection.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The all-components inspection rule applies to a microbial remediation project done to __.","a":"ANSI/IICRC S520","d":["ANSI/IICRC S500","ANSI/IICRC S590","ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001"]},"qs":["cvi-0241"]},{"id":"acr:1.7-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"NOTE","text":"The S520 exception replaces the 10% minimum. It does not add to it.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7","prompt":"Does the S520 all-components rule add to the 10% minimum or replace it?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0241"]},{"id":"acr:X-7","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 10% figure is for inspection. Verification covers all specified components.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7, §5.0","prompt":"Is the 10% figure for inspection or for verification, and what does verification cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0242","cvi-0292"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes filters and air bypass.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which filtration items must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"1.7.1 is the only 1.7 component list written as 'shall include'. Supply and return lists say 'includes'.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes heating coils and cooling coils.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which coils must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"Reheat coils are on the supply list (1.7.2), not the AHU list.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes condensate pans and condensate drain lines.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which water-handling items under the coil must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0244"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes humidification systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which moisture-adding system must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0277"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes acoustic insulation.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which insulation must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0243"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-f","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes fans and fan compartments.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which air-moving items must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-g","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes dampers and door gaskets.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which two items that control or seal airflow must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"Dampers appear on both the AHU list and the return list (1.7.3).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0287"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-h","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes general unit integrity.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which overall condition item must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0243","cvi-0287"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-i","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"DEF","text":"The AHU category includes fan coil units and evaporative coolers.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which two unit types does the Standard place in the AHU inspection category?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0025","cvi-0038","cvi-0278"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.2-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"The supply inspection includes air ducts and mixing boxes.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.2","prompt":"Besides VAV boxes, fan powered terminal boxes, and reheat coils, what does the 1.7.2 supply inspection include?","trap":"Mixing boxes are named on the supply list, not the return list.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0218","cvi-0245"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.2-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"The supply inspection includes VAV boxes and fan powered terminal boxes.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.2","prompt":"Which terminal units does the supply air duct inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0245"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.2-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"The supply inspection includes reheat coils.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.2","prompt":"Which coils does the supply air duct inspection include?","trap":"Heating and cooling coils are on the AHU list. Reheat coils are on the supply list.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0245"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.3-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"The return inspection includes return air ducts and dampers.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.3","prompt":"Which ducts and which airflow devices does the 1.7.3 return inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.3-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"The return inspection includes return plenums.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.3","prompt":"Which plenums does the return air duct inspection include?","trap":"A return plenum in the duct system is in scope. A non-ducted open air plenum falls under 1.7.4.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0246"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.3-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"NOTE","text":"A return plenum inside ductwork is different from a non-ducted open air plenum.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.3","prompt":"Is a return plenum inside ductwork the same as a non-ducted open air plenum?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0120","cvi-0246"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.3-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"NOTE","text":"ACR §1.7.1 says the AHU inspection \"shall include\" its items, while §1.7.2 and §1.7.3 say only \"includes\" for supply and return items.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.3","prompt":"How does the wording of 1.7.1 differ from 1.7.2 and 1.7.3?","trap":"The wording difference is deliberate.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.4-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"MAY","text":"An open air plenum inspection is part of the contractual scope of work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.4","prompt":"How may a non-ducted open air plenum inspection become part of a project?","trap":"It is a MAY: included only by contract. Open air plenums are outside the Standard's scope.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0246"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.4-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"Open air plenums present unique cleaning challenges and environmental control challenges.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.4","prompt":"Why are non-ducted open air plenums harder to work in?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.4-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"REC","text":"The NADCA White Paper on open air plenums gives the guidance for this work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.4","prompt":"Which document is recommended as guidance for open air plenum work?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.4-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"NOTE","text":"Subsection 1.7.4, non-ducted open air plenum inspection, is new in ACR 2025. It moved Exhaust to 1.7.5 and Damaged Components to 1.7.6.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.4","prompt":"What did the new 2025 subsection 1.7.4 do to the subsections after it?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.5-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"General exhaust, bathroom exhaust, and heat recovery exhaust are part of the ventilation system.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.5","prompt":"Which three kinds of exhaust does the Standard name as part of the ventilation system?","trap":"The exhaust clause is not new in 2025. Only its number moved from 1.7.4 to 1.7.5.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.5-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"General, bathroom, and heat recovery exhaust components are part of the overall ACR inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.5","prompt":"How do general, bathroom, and heat recovery exhaust components relate to the inspection?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.6-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"SHALL","text":"Damaged components or unsafe components under observation receive documentation.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.6","prompt":"What must happen to damaged or unsafe components observed during an inspection?","trap":"Documentation is required. This clause does not require repair.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0384"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.6-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Standard requires documentation only. It does not require repair at this clause.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.6","prompt":"Does 1.7.6 require repair of damaged components, or only documentation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.8-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"REC","text":"When exposure to hazardous materials is possible, consultation with a qualified IEP occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.8","prompt":"When exposure to hazardous materials is possible, which professional is named first for the recommended consultation?","trap":"Recommended, not required, though most candidates read it as mandatory.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0333"]},{"id":"acr:1.8-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"REC","text":"A health and safety professional is an alternative to the IEP.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.8","prompt":"Who is the alternative to the IEP for a hazardous materials consultation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0333"]},{"id":"acr:1.8-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"NOTE","text":"ACR §1.8 only recommends consulting a qualified IEP or health and safety professional when hazardous material exposure is possible. It is not a requirement.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.8","prompt":"Is consulting an IEP about possible hazardous material exposure a requirement under ACR §1.8?","trap":"The clause reads like a shall. It is a recommendation.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-8","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"NOTE","text":"Hazardous material consultation (1.8) is a recommendation, not a requirement.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.8","prompt":"Is consultation on possible hazardous material exposure (1.8) required or recommended?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.9-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"REC","text":"When suspect contamination appears, a preliminary determination of Condition 1, 2, or 3 occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9","prompt":"When suspect microbial contamination appears, what preliminary determination is recommended?","trap":"The determination is recommended. A license to determine Conditions is needed only where the law requires one.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0252"]},{"id":"acr:1.9-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"REC","text":"An IEP or other appropriate professional assesses the cause and the extent.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9","prompt":"Who is recommended to assess the cause and extent of suspect microbial contamination?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0252","cvi-0383"]},{"id":"acr:1.9-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 heading was \"Inspecting for Mold Contamination\". The words \"other biological activity\" are gone.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9","prompt":"How did the 1.9 heading change from the 2021 edition?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.9.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"SHALL","text":"Suspect microbial contamination in the AHU air-handling components starts a duct inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","prompt":"What must suspect microbial contamination in the AHU air-handling components start?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0216","cvi-0283"]},{"id":"acr:1.9.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"SHALL","text":"The supply ducts and the return ducts get an inspection during that same inspection time.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","prompt":"When suspect microbial contamination is in the AHU, which ducts must be inspected, and when?","trap":"Not at the next Table 1 date and not after an IEP confirms growth. Same inspection time.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0216","cvi-0253","cvi-0363"]},{"id":"acr:1.9.1-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"SHALL","text":"When suspect microbial growth in the AHU triggers a same-visit duct inspection, that inspection replaces the Table 1 interval for that system.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","prompt":"How does the same-visit duct inspection for suspect microbial growth in the AHU relate to Table 1?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0253","cvi-0363"]},{"id":"acr:1.9.1-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"NOTE","text":"The same-visit duct inspection rule for suspect growth in the AHU also existed in 2021. ACR 2025 only changed the word \"mold\" to \"microbial\".","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","prompt":"Is the same-visit duct inspection rule for microbial growth in the AHU new in 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.9.2-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"DEF","text":"Water damage or suspect growth on building products or furnishings is outside this scope.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.2","prompt":"Is water damage or suspect growth on building products or furnishings within the 1.9 scope?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0254"]},{"id":"acr:1.9.2-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"REC","text":"Water damage or suspect growth on building products or furnishings falls under ANSI/IICRC S520 and applicable codes, not ACR.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.2","prompt":"What governs water damage or suspect growth on building products or furnishings?","trap":"S520 is named here, not S500, even though water damage is part of the condition.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0126","cvi-0254"]},{"id":"acr:2.0-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"SHALL","text":"The contractor gives a written work plan before any cleaning work starts.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.0","prompt":"What must the contractor give before any cleaning work starts?","trap":"The plan itself is mandatory, but most of its subsections are only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0323"]},{"id":"acr:2.0-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"DEF","text":"The work plan communicates responsibilities and specific tasks for the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.0","prompt":"What does the work plan communicate for the project?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.0-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"DEF","text":"The work plan uses the information from the inspection and the system assessment.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.0","prompt":"Which earlier steps supply the information the work plan uses?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.0-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"NOTE","text":"The plan itself is mandatory. Most of its subsections are recommendations.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.0","prompt":"In Section 2, which is mandatory: the plan itself or most of its subsections?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0324"]},{"id":"acr:2.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"DEF","text":"The plan gives the client, the contractor, and the field personnel a clear understanding.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.1","prompt":"Which three parties does the work plan give a clear understanding to?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"DEF","text":"The written work plan gives the client, contractor, and field personnel a clear understanding of the work tasks and procedures for the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.1","prompt":"What does the clear understanding from the work plan cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.2-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"SHALL","text":"The scope of work identifies the components for cleaning or restoration.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.2","prompt":"Besides engineering controls and unique requirements, what must the scope of work identify?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0293"]},{"id":"acr:2.2-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"SHALL","text":"The scope of work includes the environmental engineering controls for the workspace.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.2","prompt":"Which workspace item must the scope of work include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0289"]},{"id":"acr:2.2-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"SHALL","text":"The scope of work includes any unique requirements.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.2","prompt":"Besides components and engineering controls, what must the scope of work include?","trap":"The scope of work (2.2) is a SHALL. Means and methods right after it (2.3) is only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0281","cvi-0290"]},{"id":"acr:2.3-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The work plan identifies the specific means and methods of cleaning and restoration.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.3","prompt":"What is the work plan recommended to identify about how cleaning and restoration will be done?","trap":"Recommended here, yet the 1.10 assessment shall give recommended cleaning techniques.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.4-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The work plan names all firms, contractors, and representatives, with contact information.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.4","prompt":"For other trades, who is the work plan recommended to name, and with what details?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0324"]},{"id":"acr:2.4-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The work plan identifies the tasks of the other parties.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.4","prompt":"Besides naming the other parties, what is the work plan recommended to identify about them?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.5-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The work plan gives the dates and times of the work.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.5","prompt":"What detailed timing is the work plan recommended to give?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0324"]},{"id":"acr:2.5-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The work plan gives an overall timeframe for completion.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.5","prompt":"What overall timing is the work plan recommended to give?","trap":"The project schedule is recommended, not required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.6-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"When more than one company works on the project, a communication plan applies.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.6","prompt":"When does a work site communication plan apply?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0328"]},{"id":"acr:2.6-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The plan names the individuals, their companies, contact information, and responsibilities.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.6","prompt":"What does the work site communication plan name?","trap":"2.4 names firms and their tasks. 2.6 names individuals and responsibilities. Both are recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0328"]},{"id":"acr:2.7-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The work plan lists all general use products and specific chemical type products.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7","prompt":"Which products must the work plan list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0327"]},{"id":"acr:2.7-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The work plan lists all coatings specific to the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7","prompt":"Besides products, what else specific to the project must the work plan list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.7-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The manufacturer instructions for use and application are available to workers at all times.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7","prompt":"What product information must be available to workers at all times?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.7.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The work plan includes Safety Data Sheets for all chemical products on the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.1","prompt":"Which safety documents must the work plan include for chemical products?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0325"]},{"id":"acr:2.7.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The Safety Data Sheets stay on site for review for the duration of the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.1","prompt":"Where, and for how long, must the Safety Data Sheets be kept?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0325"]},{"id":"acr:2.7.1-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"CAN","text":"The plan includes documentation of product submittal to the client.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.1","prompt":"Besides Safety Data Sheets, what client-facing product record can the plan include under 2.7.1?","trap":"This is a CAN, a possibility. SDS in the plan and on site are SHALL.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.7.2-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The plan describes the engineering controls for chemical vapors and odors, where applicable.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.2","prompt":"What must the plan describe for chemical vapors and odors?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0326"]},{"id":"acr:2.7.2-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"DEF","text":"Work plan engineering controls for chemical vapors and odors limit both occupant exposure and worker exposure.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.2","prompt":"Whose exposure do the controls for chemical vapors and odors limit?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.8-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"When detection equipment goes off-line, the work plan addresses life safety concerns.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.8","prompt":"What must the work plan address when detection equipment goes off-line?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0306"]},{"id":"acr:2.8-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"DEF","text":"Life safety detection equipment includes air duct sensors and smoke detectors.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.8","prompt":"What does life safety detection equipment include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.8-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"DEF","text":"Addressing life safety when detection equipment goes off-line will need input from other parties, not the contractor alone.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.8","prompt":"Can the contractor handle the off-line detection safety task alone?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.8-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"REC","text":"The plan defines the responsibilities of each designated representative for the project duration.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.8","prompt":"What is the safety plan recommended to define for the project duration?","trap":"Recommended, while addressing life safety for off-line detectors (2.8) is required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0306"]},{"id":"acr:3.0-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Under ACR §3.0, engineering controls must prevent cross-contamination.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"What does ACR 2025 §3.0 say engineering controls must do?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include source control and isolation barriers.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which two engineering controls in §3.0 deal with stopping contamination at its origin and physically separating the work area?","trap":"The 3.0 list is permissive (may include). Barriers become mandatory only at Level 2 (3.10.2).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include pressure differentials and dust suppression methods.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which two engineering controls in §3.0 deal with air pressure and with keeping dust from getting airborne?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-d","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include HEPA vacuuming and HEPA filtration.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which two HEPA-based methods does §3.0 list as engineering controls?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-e","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include detailed cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which cleaning practice does §3.0 list on its own as an engineering control?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-f","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include temperature control and humidity control.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which two indoor conditions does §3.0 say can be controlled as engineering controls?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-g","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include a sanitary approach.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which general work approach closes the §3.0 list of engineering controls?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-h","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Standard names no personal protective equipment. PPE is not an engineering control.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Does ACR 2025 name any personal protective equipment, and is PPE an engineering control?","trap":"Engineering controls change the work environment. A respirator or suit protects one worker and is not on the 3.0 list.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0231"]},{"id":"acr:X-12","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Standard never specifies personal protective equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"In which ACR 2025 clause is personal protective equipment specified?","trap":"Any option citing an ACR respirator or PPE rule is wrong. PPE requirements come from OSHA, not ACR.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.13-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Appropriate engineering controls are mandatory on every cleaning and restoration project.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13","prompt":"On which cleaning and restoration projects are appropriate engineering controls mandatory?","trap":"Not only microbial or healthcare jobs. Every project, including small residential ones.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.13-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Protection of workers and occupants is a priority on every project.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13","prompt":"Per the §3.13 summary, whose protection is a priority on every project?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.13-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Prevention of cross-contamination is a priority on every project.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13","prompt":"Besides protecting people, what other priority does §3.13 set for every project?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.13-d","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"REC","text":"The listed engineering controls are minimum requirements.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13","prompt":"How does the §3.13 summary characterize the engineering controls listed in Section 3?","trap":"This summary line carries a recommendation, not a shall. Read the obligation word, not the word 'minimum'.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.13-e","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"REC","text":"An IEP gives advice on project-specific engineering controls.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13","prompt":"Who does §3.13 recommend for advice on project-specific engineering controls?","trap":"Consulting an IEP is recommended, not required, just like the hazardous material consultation at 1.8.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0379"]},{"id":"acr:3.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"All contractor equipment stays in good working order, consistent with jurisdictional requirements.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"In what condition must all contractor equipment be kept under §3.1?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"Contractor equipment under ACR §3.1 includes vacuum collection equipment, power tools, and pressurized air sources.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which vacuum, power tool, and compressed-air items head the §3.1 list of contractor equipment?","trap":"Equipment lists read 'but not limited to' (G-25). A tool missing from the list is not exempt.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"Contractor equipment under ACR §3.1 includes electrical power cords, plugs, and ground fault protection devices.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which electrical items does §3.1 list as contractor equipment to keep in good working order?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-d","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"Contractor equipment under ACR §3.1 includes vacuum collection hoses, fluid lines, and pneumatic lines.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which hoses and lines does §3.1 list as contractor equipment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-e","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"Contractor equipment under ACR §3.1 includes manual and mechanical rotary brush systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which brush systems does §3.1 list as contractor equipment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-f","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"Contractor equipment under ACR §3.1 includes robotic equipment and pneumatic cleaning systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which automated and air-driven cleaning systems does §3.1 list as contractor equipment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0232"]},{"id":"acr:3.1-g","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"Contractor equipment under ACR §3.1 includes air duct zoning devices, ladders, staging equipment, and hand tools.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which zoning, access, and small-tool items close the §3.1 list of contractor equipment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"Equipment receives cleaning and inspection before it enters the work site.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.1","prompt":"What must happen to equipment before it enters the work site?","trap":"This is a shall. The on-site vacuum check at 3.1.4 is only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0232"]},{"id":"acr:3.1.1-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"Cleaning and inspecting equipment before it enters the work site keeps contaminants out of the indoor environment and the HVAC system components.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.1","prompt":"Why must equipment be cleaned and inspected before it enters the work site?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.2-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"Equipment receives service during the project as the conditions need it.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2","prompt":"When must equipment be serviced during a project?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.2-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"Servicing equipment during the project limits cross-contamination from poor hygiene and unsafe operating conditions.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2","prompt":"Servicing equipment during the project limits cross-contamination from which two problems?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.2.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"Any on-site opening of contaminated vacuum equipment occurs in a containment area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2.1","prompt":"Inside the building, where is the only place contaminated vacuum equipment may be opened on site?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.2.1-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"Contaminated vacuum equipment may be opened on site only in a containment area or outside the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2.1","prompt":"Besides a containment area, where may contaminated vacuum equipment be opened on site?","trap":"A mechanical room or hallway does not qualify unless it is set up as a containment area.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.2.1-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"The rule on where contaminated vacuum equipment may be opened covers both servicing and filter maintenance.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2.1","prompt":"Which vacuum tasks does the rule on opening contaminated vacuum equipment cover?","trap":"Changing a vacuum filter counts as opening it. It follows the same containment-or-outside rule.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.3-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"Collection devices, vacuums, and tools receive cleaning or sealing before relocation in the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.3","prompt":"What must happen to collection devices, vacuums, and tools before they move to another area inside the building?","trap":"Cleaning OR sealing satisfies it. Full cleaning between areas is not the only option.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.3-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"The same equipment receives cleaning or sealing before it leaves the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.3","prompt":"What must happen to collection devices, vacuums, and tools before they leave the building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.4-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"REC","text":"An on-site maintenance verification of vacuum equipment occurs before work starts.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.4","prompt":"What check of vacuum equipment does §3.1.4 recommend before work starts?","trap":"Recommended, not required. Its neighbor 3.1.1, clean and inspect before entering the site, is the shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0381"]},{"id":"acr:3.1.4-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"NOTE","text":"3.1.1 is mandatory. 3.1.4 directly below it is only a recommendation.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.4","prompt":"Of the two equipment checks at 3.1.1 and 3.1.4, which is mandatory and which is only recommended?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0381"]},{"id":"acr:3.2-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Generators, vacuum trucks, and air compressors stand in a position that stops emissions entry.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.2","prompt":"How must generators, vacuum trucks, and air compressors be positioned on a job?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0304","cvi-0370"]},{"id":"acr:3.2-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"DEF","text":"The emissions of concern are combustion emissions and air exhaust emissions.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.2","prompt":"Which two kinds of emissions from fuel-powered equipment does §3.2 aim to keep out of the building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0304"]},{"id":"acr:3.2.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"The location receives monitoring and management during the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.2.1","prompt":"After fuel-powered equipment is placed, what must happen to its location for the rest of the project?","trap":"Placing it once is not enough. The location is monitored and managed for the whole project.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0305","cvi-0370"]},{"id":"acr:3.3-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Vacuum equipment that exhausts inside the building envelope uses HEPA filtration.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.3","prompt":"What filtration must vacuum equipment use when it exhausts inside the building envelope?","trap":"Non-HEPA equipment is not banned. It must exhaust outdoors (4.1.3).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0307","cvi-0369"]},{"id":"acr:3.4-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Continuous negative pressure exists in the portion of the components under cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.4","prompt":"What pressure condition must exist in the portion of components under cleaning, and for how long?","trap":"ACR gives no numeric negative pressure. Any answer in inches of water column is wrong.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.4-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"DEF","text":"During cleaning, the section being cleaned is kept at negative pressure relative to the surrounding indoor spaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.4","prompt":"Relative to what is the section under cleaning kept negative in §3.4?","trap":"The reference is the surrounding indoor spaces, not outdoors.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.4-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"The negative pressure is verifiable at representative locations during the cleaning process.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.4","prompt":"Where and when must the §3.4 negative pressure be verifiable?","trap":"2025 says verifiable, not verified. Clause 4.1.1 still requires verification of the differential during the project.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0303"]},{"id":"acr:3.4-d","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition said \"verified\". The 2025 edition says \"verifiable\".","src":"ACR 2025 §3.4","prompt":"What single word changed in the negative pressure clause 3.4 between 2021 and 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.5-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"All contaminated materials receive containment before they leave the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.5","prompt":"What must happen to contaminated materials before they leave the building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.5.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Materials that government agencies deem hazardous follow applicable codes, laws, and regulations.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.5.1","prompt":"What governs the handling of materials that government agencies deem hazardous?","trap":"ACR sets no hazardous waste procedure of its own. It defers to applicable codes, laws, and regulations.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.6-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"REC","text":"Ambient air cleaning with HEPA-filtered air scrubbers is a supplemental engineering control.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.6","prompt":"Under §3.6, what role does ambient air cleaning with HEPA-filtered air scrubbers play?","trap":"Recommended at 3.6. It becomes a shall, at 4 ACH, only inside Level 2 and higher containment (3.10.7).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0377"]},{"id":"acr:3.6-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"DEF","text":"The purpose is particle reduction during the work and immediately after the work.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.6","prompt":"What is the purpose of ambient air cleaning, and when does it apply?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.6-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition recommended 4 air changes per hour here. The 2025 edition gives no number.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.6","prompt":"What air change rate does 2025 §3.6 give for ambient air cleaning, and what did 2021 say there?","trap":"'4 ACH on every job' is 2021 thinking. In 2025, 4 ACH lives only at Level 2.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0377"]},{"id":"acr:3.7-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning agents and chemicals follow the manufacturer recommended procedures.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.7","prompt":"Whose procedures govern the use of cleaning agents and chemicals under §3.7?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0375"]},{"id":"acr:3.7-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning agents and chemicals also follow the product application instructions, with exhaust ventilation where required.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.7","prompt":"Besides manufacturer procedures, what else must chemical use follow under §3.7, including ventilation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0375"]},{"id":"acr:3.8-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"REC","text":"Redundant equipment stays on site for a negative pressure failure.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.8","prompt":"What does §3.8 recommend keeping on site in case negative pressure fails?","trap":"Recommended only. Candidates often mark backup equipment as required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3-L1","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 1 = negative pressure, protective coverings, cross-contamination control.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"What three things make up Level 1 containment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0294","cvi-0372"]},{"id":"acr:3-L2","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 2 = Level 1 + barriers + two-layer 6-mil floor + 4 ACH + manometer + dismantle hygiene.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10","prompt":"What does Level 2 containment add on top of Level 1?","trap":"The barriers are required, but their specs (floor to ceiling, 6-mil, airtight seals) are only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3-L3","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 3 = Level 2 + single chamber decon + continuous monitoring + particulate baseline.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11","prompt":"What does Level 3 containment add on top of Level 2?","trap":"Level 3 is one decon chamber. Two chambers is Level 4.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0298"]},{"id":"acr:3-L4","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 4 = Level 3 + second decon chamber + constant recording monitor with alarm.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12","prompt":"What does Level 4 containment add on top of Level 3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0300"]},{"id":"acr:3-Lx","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"DEF","text":"The instrument ladder is validate, then monitor continuously, then record with an alarm.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.6, 3.11.3, 3.12.3","prompt":"What is the pressure-instrument ladder from Level 2 to Level 4?","trap":"Level 2 validates with a manometer. Continuous monitoring starts at Level 3. Recording with an alarm is Level 4 only.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Level 1 is the minimum containment on all component cleaning projects. No project is exempt.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"Which component cleaning projects need at least Level 1 containment?","trap":"Even a small residential job. There is no exemption for project size or type.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0294"]},{"id":"acr:3.9.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"The component or area under cleaning stays under negative pressure during all cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.1","prompt":"What pressure requirement is part one of Level 1 containment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9.1-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Level 1 keeps the component under negative pressure so particulate cannot migrate out of the component.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.1","prompt":"Why does Level 1 keep the component under negative pressure?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9.2-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Clean protective coverings stay in place within the work area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.2","prompt":"What does part two of Level 1 require within the work area?","trap":"Level 1 coverings are not the Level 2 poly floor. No mil thickness or layer count applies at Level 1.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9.2-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"The coverings extend past the work area to protect flooring, equipment, and furniture, whenever necessary.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.2","prompt":"When must Level 1 protective coverings extend past the work area, and what do they protect?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9.3-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Engineering controls limit contaminant discharge from the components.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.3","prompt":"Under part three of Level 1, what must engineering controls limit coming out of the components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9.3-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Engineering controls limit cross-contamination into occupied space during cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.3","prompt":"Under part three of Level 1, what must engineering controls limit reaching occupied space during cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"NOTE","text":"Level 1 has three parts. Barriers, scrubbers, and floors belong to Level 2.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"How many parts does Level 1 containment have, and which common items are not among them?","trap":"Four parts is the 2021 edition. 2025 has three.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Level 1 containment in ACR 2025 has __ parts.","a":"three","d":["two","four","five"]},"qs":["cvi-0372"]},{"id":"acr:3.9-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition had four parts. 3.9.3 Cleaning Equipment and Tools is gone.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"Which Level 1 subsection from 2021 was removed in 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9-d","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 clause 3.9.4 Cross-Contamination Control became 3.9.3 in 2025.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"What is the 2025 clause number for Level 1 cross-contamination control, and what was it in 2021?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-2","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"NOTE","text":"Level 1 applies to every project. It needs no barriers.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"Does Level 1 containment apply to every project, and does it require barriers?","trap":"Options that add poly barriers or air scrubbers to Level 1 are describing Level 2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"All Level 1 requirements apply to Level 2.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.1","prompt":"Which Level 1 requirements carry into Level 2?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.2-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"In Level 2 containment, temporary barriers isolate the work area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2","prompt":"What isolates the work area in Level 2 containment?","trap":"The barriers are required. Only their specs in 3.10.2.1 to 3.10.2.4 are recommendations.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.2.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"REC","text":"The barriers extend from floor to ceiling, where applicable.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.1","prompt":"How far should Level 2 barriers extend vertically?","trap":"Recommended, and only where applicable.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.2.2-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"REC","text":"Above-ceiling work areas stay isolated from occupied spaces and non-work areas, where applicable.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.2","prompt":"What does §3.10.2.2 recommend for work areas above the ceiling?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.2.3-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"REC","text":"The barrier material is 6-mil fire retardant polyethylene sheeting or equivalent.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.3","prompt":"What barrier material does §3.10.2.3 recommend for Level 2?","trap":"Recommended. The same 6-mil material is mandatory only for the two-layer floor at 3.10.3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.2.4-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"REC","text":"The barriers have an airtight seal at the ceiling, the floor, and the walls.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.4","prompt":"Where should Level 2 barriers be sealed airtight?","trap":"Barrier seals are recommended. The floor-to-wall seal at 3.10.3 is required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.3-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"The containment has a two-layer floor of 6-mil fire retardant polyethylene or equivalent.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.3","prompt":"What floor must Level 2 containment have?","trap":"Two layers, and mandatory. Do not confuse it with the recommended barrier spec.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Level 2 containment floor is two layers of __ fire retardant polyethylene or equivalent.","a":"6-mil","d":["4-mil","10-mil","15-mil"]},"qs":["cvi-0295"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.3-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"The floor material extends at least 6 inches (15.2 centimeters) up the side walls.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.3","prompt":"How far up the side walls must the Level 2 floor poly run?","trap":"6 inches is the floor turn-up. 4 inches is panel fastener spacing and 1 inch is panel overlap.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The minimum distance Level 2 floor poly must run up the side walls is __.","a":"6 inches","d":["1 inch","4 inches","12 inches"]},"qs":["cvi-0302"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.3-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"The floor material has a seal to the side walls that stays airtight during depressurization.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.3","prompt":"How must the Level 2 floor poly be sealed to the side walls?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0302"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.4-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"DEF","text":"A zipper-type access with a single flap over the zipper is the most common practice.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.4","prompt":"What is the most common way to enter Level 2 containment?","trap":"Described as common practice, not as a requirement.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.4.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"CAN","text":"When a zipper is not practical, a vertical cut in the side wall gives access.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.4.1","prompt":"What entry option exists when a zipper is not practical in Level 2 containment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.4.1-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Two flaps cover that vertical cut, one flap on each side of the polyethylene.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.4.1","prompt":"How must a vertical-cut entry in Level 2 poly be covered?","trap":"Zipper entry: one flap over it. Vertical cut: two flaps, one on each side.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The number of flaps required over a vertical-cut entry in Level 2 poly is __.","a":"two","d":["one","three","four"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"NOTE","text":"The barrier material at 3.10.2.3 is a recommendation. The floor at 3.10.3 is mandatory.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10","prompt":"Which Level 2 clause is mandatory: the barrier material at 3.10.2.3 or the floor at 3.10.3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0373"]},{"id":"acr:X-3","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 6-mil barrier material is a recommendation. The 6-mil two-layer floor is mandatory.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.3, 3.10.3","prompt":"Same 6-mil material, opposite obligations: which Level 2 use is recommended and which is mandatory?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0295","cvi-0373"]},{"id":"acr:3.10-d","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 heading read \"Level 2 Containment (Temporary Barriers)\".","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10","prompt":"What did the 2021 heading for Level 2 containment include that 2025 dropped?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.5-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"The containment area stays under negative pressure at all times.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.5","prompt":"When must the Level 2 containment area be under negative pressure?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.5-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Level 2 keeps the containment area under negative pressure so airborne particulate cannot migrate out of it.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.5","prompt":"What does negative pressure in Level 2 containment prevent?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.6-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"A manometer or an airflow measuring device validates the negative pressurization.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.6","prompt":"What instrument validates negative pressurization at Level 2?","trap":"Level 2 validates. Continuous monitoring is Level 3, and a recording monitor with alarm is Level 4.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.7-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Ambient air cleaning with HEPA-filtered air scrubbers occurs at Level 2.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.7","prompt":"What ambient air cleaning is required inside Level 2 containment?","trap":"At 3.6 air scrubbing is only recommended. At Level 2 it is a shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.7-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Level 2 HEPA air scrubbing maintains a minimum of four (4) air changes per hour.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.7","prompt":"What minimum air change rate must Level 2 air scrubbing maintain?","trap":"A minimum, not a target or a maximum.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The minimum rate Level 2 HEPA air scrubbing must maintain is __.","a":"4 air changes per hour","d":["2 air changes per hour","6 air changes per hour","12 air changes per hour"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.7-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Level 2 air scrubbing rule of 4 air changes per hour is the only air changes per hour figure in ACR 2025.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.7","prompt":"Besides Level 2 air scrubbing, where does ACR 2025 give an air changes per hour figure?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-1","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"NOTE","text":"The only ACH figure in the Standard is 4 ACH at Level 2 (3.10.7).","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.7","prompt":"What is the only ACH figure in ACR 2025, and in which clause does it appear?","trap":"It left 3.6 in 2025. 'Scrub at 4 ACH on every job' is wrong.","delta":true,"num":{"q":"ACR 2025 states its only air-change figure, 4 ACH, in the clause for Level __ containment.","a":"2","d":["1","3","4"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.8-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Interior surfaces get a wet wipe or a HEPA vacuum before removal of the enclosure.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.8","prompt":"What must happen to the interior surfaces of Level 2 containment before the enclosure is removed?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.8-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"In healthcare, a post-remediation verification occurs before removal of the containment.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.8","prompt":"What extra step comes before containment removal on healthcare projects?","trap":"Only healthcare is named. It is not a requirement on every Level 2 job.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0297"]},{"id":"acr:3.11-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 3 is a containment with a single chamber decontamination unit.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11","prompt":"What defines Level 3 containment?","trap":"Single chamber is Level 3. Two chambers is Level 4.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Level 3 containment uses a __ decontamination unit.","a":"single-chamber","d":["two-chamber","three-chamber","four-chamber"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"All Level 1 and Level 2 requirements apply to Level 3.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.1","prompt":"Which lower-level requirements apply to Level 3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.2-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"A single chamber decontamination facility works together with the containment area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.2","prompt":"What facility must work together with the containment area at Level 3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.2-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The chamber has an attachment and a seal directly to the containment area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.2","prompt":"How is the Level 3 decon chamber joined to the containment area?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.2-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"A zipper access with a single flap separates the chamber from the containment area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.2","prompt":"What normally separates the Level 3 decon chamber from the containment area?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.2-d","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"Two flaps, as Level 2 describes them, are the alternative separation.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.2","prompt":"What is the alternative separation between the Level 3 chamber and the containment area?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The containment gets continuous monitoring for negative pressure.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"How must negative pressure be monitored at Level 3?","trap":"Level 2 only validates. Level 3 monitors continuously but need not record. Recording starts at Level 4.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The instrument is sensitive enough to detect a loss of negative pressure.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"How sensitive must the Level 3 pressure monitoring instrument be?","trap":"No numeric sensitivity. ACR gives no inches-of-water figure anywhere.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-c","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"Background monitoring for total particulate occurs before the set-up of the containment.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"When must background total particulate monitoring happen at Level 3?","trap":"Before set-up is the shall. Monitoring during set-up is only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0230","cvi-0298"]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-d","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 3 background monitoring before containment set-up establishes the baseline airborne total particulate concentrations.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"What does the Level 3 background particulate monitoring establish?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0230"]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-e","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"REC","text":"Monitoring also occurs during the set-up of the containment.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"What additional particulate monitoring does §3.11.3 recommend at Level 3?","trap":"Recommended, sitting between two shall items: before set-up and during the work.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-f","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"Real time monitoring for total particulate occurs on a regular basis during the work.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"What particulate monitoring must happen during the work at Level 3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-g","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"When airborne particulate levels go above the background levels, work stops.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"At Level 3, what triggers a work stoppage?","trap":"The trigger is any rise above background, not a fixed number or a percentage.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-h","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"Work restarts only after the levels return to background AND the cause has a correction.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"What two conditions must both be met before Level 3 work restarts?","trap":"Levels falling back alone is not enough. The cause must also be corrected.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0299","cvi-0374"]},{"id":"acr:3.12-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 4 is a containment with a two-chamber decontamination unit.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12","prompt":"What defines Level 4 containment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.12.1-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"All Level 1, Level 2, and Level 3 requirements apply to Level 4.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12.1","prompt":"Which lower-level requirements apply to Level 4?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.12.2-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The Level 4 decontamination facility has two chambers.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12.2","prompt":"How many chambers must the Level 4 decontamination facility have?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"A Level 4 decontamination facility has __ chambers.","a":"two","d":["one","three","four"]},"qs":["cvi-0301"]},{"id":"acr:3.12.2-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"Each chamber follows the construction requirements for a Level 3 chamber.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12.2","prompt":"What construction rules does each Level 4 decon chamber follow?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0301"]},{"id":"acr:3.12.3-a","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The Level 3 monitoring requirements also apply to Level 4 containment.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12.3","prompt":"Which monitoring requirements carry into Level 4?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.12.3-b","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The containment also has a constant recording pressurization monitor with an appropriate alarm.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12.3","prompt":"What pressure monitor does Level 4 add?","trap":"Recording plus alarm is Level 4 only. Continuous monitoring without recording satisfies Level 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0300"]},{"id":"acr:4.0-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"All procedures achieve the minimum level of visibly clean.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.0","prompt":"What minimum cleanliness level must all Section 4 procedures achieve?","trap":"Visibly clean is the floor, not the ceiling. A contract can specify a level of cleanliness verification instead.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.0-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"The specified level of cleanliness verification in the contractual documents applies instead.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.0","prompt":"When the contract documents specify a level of cleanliness verification, which level applies?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0119","cvi-0338"]},{"id":"acr:4.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"The components and ducts stay at an appropriate negative pressure differential.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1","prompt":"What pressure condition must components and ducts be held at under §4.1?","trap":"'Appropriate' is the only size given. ACR sets no inches-of-water value.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.1-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"DEF","text":"Under §4.1, duct negative pressure is measured relative to the indoor non-work area.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1","prompt":"Relative to what area is the duct kept negative under §4.1?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.1-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Negative duct pressure is in place before the cleaning process starts and holds through the whole cleaning process.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1","prompt":"When must negative duct pressure be in place, and for how long?","trap":"Before cleaning starts, not once agitation begins.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.1-d","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"The differential holds between the portion under cleaning and surrounding indoor occupant spaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1","prompt":"Between which two areas must the §4.1 pressure differential hold?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.1.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Under all circumstances, verification of the pressurization differential occurs during the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1.1","prompt":"Under what circumstances must the pressurization differential be verified during the project?","trap":"Clause 3.4 says verifiable. Clause 4.1.1 still requires actual verification under all circumstances.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0303"]},{"id":"acr:4.1.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Vacuum equipment that exhausts indoors is HEPA-filtered.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1.2","prompt":"What filtration must vacuum equipment have if it exhausts indoors, under §4.1.2?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0369"]},{"id":"acr:4.1.2-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Vacuum equipment that exhausts indoors must retain the dislodged debris as well as being HEPA-filtered.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1.2","prompt":"Besides HEPA filtration, what must indoor-exhausting vacuum equipment do with dislodged debris?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.1.3-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Equipment for negative duct pressurization without HEPA filtration exhausts outdoors.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1.3","prompt":"Where must negative duct pressurization equipment exhaust if it lacks HEPA filtration?","trap":"Non-HEPA equipment is allowed. It just may not exhaust inside the building.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0307"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening does not degrade the structural integrity of the components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.1","prompt":"What must a service opening not degrade about the strength of the components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.1-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening does not degrade the thermal or functional integrity.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.1","prompt":"Besides structural integrity, which other kinds of integrity must a service opening not degrade?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"Every service opening allows proper closure once the work is done.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.2","prompt":"What must every service opening allow once the work is done?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.3-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening does not hinder, restrict, or alter the airflow in the duct.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.3","prompt":"What must a service opening not do to the airflow in the duct?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.4-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening complies with industry standards and local codes.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.4","prompt":"What must a service opening comply with under §4.2.4?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.4-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"The materials are acceptable under those standards and codes.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.4","prompt":"What must be true of the materials used for a service opening?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening cut in fibrous glass or internally lined duct, together with its closure, must leave no exposed fibrous glass edges in the airstream.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.1","prompt":"What must a service opening in fibrous glass duct not leave in the airstream?","trap":"4 in. screw spacing and 1 in. panel overlap are compliant; exposed liner edges are not.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0063"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"Removed fibrous glass gets a repair or a replacement with like material.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"What must happen to fibrous glass removed to make a service opening?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0262"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.2-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"The replacement material has the same thickness as the original material.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"How thick must replacement fibrous glass at a service opening be?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.2-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"The repair leaves no break that degrades the R value or the service rating.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"Which two ratings must a fibrous glass repair at a service opening not degrade?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.2-d","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"The repair leaves no break that degrades the vapor barrier or the air barrier.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"Which two barriers must a fibrous glass repair at a service opening not degrade?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.2-e","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"Since 2025, the fibrous glass repair rule at service openings also covers any disruption to the external duct wrap.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"What external insulation did 2025 bring under the fibrous glass repair rule at service openings?","trap":"New in 2025. Cutting through exterior duct wrap now triggers the same repair rules.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0262"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.9-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"No service opening goes into flexible ductwork.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.9","prompt":"Into which type of duct may no service opening be cut?","trap":"Flex is accessed by disconnecting it, never by cutting an opening.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.9.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"REC","text":"The manufacturer instructions govern disconnection and reattachment of flexible ductwork.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.9.1","prompt":"What does §4.2.9.1 recommend should govern disconnecting and reattaching flex duct?","trap":"The flex opening ban is a shall. Following the manufacturer for reattachment is a recommendation.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.10-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"All service openings comply with applicable UL, IMC, SMACNA, and NFPA standards.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.10","prompt":"Which four standard families must all service openings comply with under §4.2.10?","trap":"IMC is new to this list in 2025.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.10-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"Service openings also comply with applicable local, regional, provincial, and national codes.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.10","prompt":"Which levels of code must service openings comply with under 2025 §4.2.10?","trap":"2025 says provincial and national. 'State codes' is the 2021 wording.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.10-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition read \"local, regional, and state codes\".","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.10","prompt":"How did the 2021 edition word the code list in §4.2.10?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service panel has an equivalent gauge to the duct, or a heavier gauge.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.1","prompt":"What gauge must a service panel be?","trap":"Duct gauge sizes a field-made panel. Pressure class sizes a prefabricated access door.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0115"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.1-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"DEF","text":"A service panel of equal or heavier gauge than the duct protects the structural integrity of the duct.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.1","prompt":"Why must a service panel be of equal or heavier gauge than the duct?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service panel has mechanical fastening at minimum every 4 inches on center.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.2","prompt":"How often must a service panel be mechanically fastened?","trap":"4 inches is fastener spacing. 1 inch is the overlap. Swapping them is the classic miss.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The maximum on-center spacing of service panel screws or rivets is __.","a":"4 inches","d":["1 inch","6 inches","12 inches"]},"qs":["cvi-0115"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.2-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"Service panels are fastened with screws or rivets.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.2","prompt":"What fasteners does §4.2.5.2 require for service panels?","trap":"Tape is a seal, not a mechanical fastener.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.2-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"The panel overlaps the ductwork surfaces by a minimum of 1 inch on all sides.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.2","prompt":"By how much must a service panel overlap the duct, and on which sides?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"A service panel overlaps the ductwork surfaces by a minimum of __ on all sides.","a":"1 inch","d":["2 inches","4 inches","6 inches"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.3-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service panel has a seal of gaskets, duct sealants, mastic, or tape.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.3","prompt":"With what must a service panel be sealed?","trap":"Mandatory in 2025. Older study guides call panel sealing a recommendation.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0116"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.3-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition made this seal a recommendation. The 2025 edition makes it mandatory.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.3","prompt":"How did the obligation to seal service panels change from 2021 to 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0116"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.4-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"All tapes and mastics for closure meet the requirements of UL 181A/B.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.4","prompt":"What UL listing must tapes and mastics used to close service openings meet?","trap":"UL 181A/B for tapes and mastics. Plain UL 181 is for surface treatments (4.15.1).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.6-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"The gauge of the door follows the pressure class of the duct system.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.6","prompt":"What sets the gauge of a prefabricated duct access door?","trap":"Not the duct gauge. That rule is for service panels (4.2.5.1).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.6-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"A prefabricated duct access door is installed to the manufacturer specifications.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.6","prompt":"What governs installation of a prefabricated duct access door?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.6-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"NOTE","text":"Pressure class governs the prefabricated door. Duct gauge governs the field-made panel.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.6","prompt":"What governs gauge for a prefabricated access door, and what governs it for a field-made panel?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.7-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"When the ducted airstream contacts door materials or panel materials, those materials have a classification.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"When must access door or panel materials carry a fire classification?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.7-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"The flame-spread rating is not over 25, with no continued progressive combustion.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"What flame-spread rating limit applies to door and panel materials in the airstream?","trap":"Flame 25, smoke 50. The exam flips the pair.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The maximum flame-spread rating for door and panel materials in the airstream is __.","a":"25","d":["50","75","200"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.7-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"Access door and panel materials in the airstream have a smoke-developed rating not over 50.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"What smoke-developed rating limit applies to door and panel materials in the airstream?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The maximum smoke-developed rating for door and panel materials in the airstream is __.","a":"50","d":["25","75","450"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.7-d","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"UL 723 sets both the flame-spread and smoke-developed ratings for access door and panel materials in the airstream.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"Which test standard determines the flame-spread and smoke-developed ratings?","trap":"Limits are flame-spread 25 or less and smoke-developed 50 or less. UL 181 and 181A/B are product listings, not the fire test.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.7-e","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"Access door and panel materials in the airstream also comply with SMACNA and other applicable standards.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"Besides the UL 723 ratings, which standards must door and panel materials comply with?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-14","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"NOTE","text":"Access door and panel materials in the airstream need flame-spread 25 or less and smoke-developed 50 or less.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"Which limit is 25 and which is 50: flame-spread or smoke-developed?","trap":"The two numbers are often swapped.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.3-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"A suitable agitation device dislodges non-adhered substances from the airside surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.3","prompt":"In the core cleaning method, what dislodges non-adhered substances from airside surfaces?","trap":"Only non-adhered substances. Paint, mastic, sealant, and stains are adhered and are not the target.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.3-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"A vacuum collection device then captures the dislodged debris.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.3","prompt":"After agitation, what captures the dislodged debris?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.3-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"NOTE","text":"Agitation and capture together are the method. Negative pressure alone is not cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.3","prompt":"Does running negative pressure alone count as cleaning under ACR?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.4-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Wet process cleaning does not damage the components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4","prompt":"What must wet process cleaning not damage within the HVAC system itself?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.4-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Wet process cleaning does not damage the facility equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4","prompt":"What did 2025 add to the list of things wet process cleaning must not damage?","trap":"Added in 2025: the facility's own equipment, not only the HVAC components.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.4-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning agents and water never contact electrical components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4","prompt":"Under §4.4, which components other than porous ones must water and cleaning agents never contact?","trap":"The only exception is electric resistance coils, cleaned wet only after de-energizing and lockout/tagout (4.11.8).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.4-d","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning agents and water never contact fibrous glass or other porous components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4","prompt":"Under §4.4, which materials must water and cleaning agents never contact besides electrical components?","trap":"Wet cleaning is for non-porous surfaces. Duct liner and duct board stay dry.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0315"]},{"id":"acr:4.4-e","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"NOTE","text":"The only carve-out is 4.11.8 for electric resistance coils.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4","prompt":"What is the only carve-out from the rule keeping water off electrical components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.7-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Duct cleaning removes all non-adhered substances from the air ducts.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7","prompt":"What must be removed from air ducts under §4.7?","trap":"All non-adhered substances, not just the visible ones. Adhered substances like paint are not the target.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.7-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Air ducts are capable of a pass on the NADCA cleanliness verification tests.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7","prompt":"What test performance must cleaned air ducts be capable of?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.7.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Service openings are large enough for mechanical cleaning and for verification.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7.1","prompt":"How large must service openings be for air duct cleaning?","trap":"Sized for verification too, not just for the brush or hose.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.7.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Mechanical agitation methods remove particulate, debris, and surface contamination.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7.2","prompt":"What method removes particulate, debris, and surface contamination from air ducts?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.7.3-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"A vacuum collection device captures the dislodged substances.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7.3","prompt":"In air duct cleaning, what captures the substances that agitation dislodges?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.7.4-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning activities do not damage any components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7.4","prompt":"What must duct cleaning activities not do to any components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.5-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"SHALL","text":"Vacuum collection equipment operates continuously during cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.5","prompt":"When must vacuum collection equipment run?","trap":"Continuously during cleaning, not only while the brush is turning.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.5-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"SHALL","text":"The equipment works with agitation tools to convey debris and prevent cross-contamination.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.5","prompt":"What two jobs does vacuum collection equipment do while working with agitation tools?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.5.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"SHALL","text":"The device maintains sufficient velocity in the portion under cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.5.1","prompt":"What airflow condition must the vacuum collection device maintain in the portion under cleaning?","trap":"'Sufficient' velocity is the shall. Table 2 values are recommendations, not required numbers.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.5.1-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"SHALL","text":"The device also maintains a sufficient negative pressure differential.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.5.1","prompt":"Besides velocity, what must the vacuum collection device maintain?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.5.1-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"Table 2 gives recommended velocities for different types of contaminant.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.5.1","prompt":"What does Table 2 give, and how is it organized?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"The ACGIH Industrial Ventilation manual is the source of Table 2.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What document is the source of ACR Table 2?","trap":"ACGIH, not ASHRAE or SMACNA.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"Vapors, gases, and smoke are the only class with no fixed minimum velocity.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"Which Table 2 contaminant class has no fixed minimum velocity?","trap":"Metal smokes from welding are not in this class. Table 2 lists them at 2,000 to 2,500 fpm.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r1","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, vapors, gases, and smoke take any desired velocity. The economic optimum is usually 1,000 to 2,000 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What velocity does Table 2 give for vapors, gases, and smoke?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"For vapors, gases, and smoke, Table 2 allows any velocity, with an economic optimum usually of __.","a":"1,000 to 2,000 fpm","d":["2,000 to 2,500 fpm","2,500 to 3,000 fpm","3,000 to 3,500 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r2","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, fumes and metal smokes, such as welding fumes, have a minimum duct design velocity of 2,000 to 2,500 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for fumes and metal smokes such as welding fumes?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives fumes and metal smokes, such as welding, a minimum duct design velocity of __.","a":"2,000 to 2,500 fpm","d":["1,000 to 2,000 fpm","2,500 to 3,000 fpm","3,500 to 4,000 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r3","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, fine light dust, such as cotton lint, wood flour, and litho powder, has a minimum duct design velocity of 2,500 to 3,000 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for fine light dust such as cotton lint?","trap":"Cotton lint is fine light dust (2,500 to 3,000). Jute lint is a dry dust (3,000 to 3,500).","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives fine light dust, such as cotton lint and wood flour, a minimum duct design velocity of __.","a":"2,500 to 3,000 fpm","d":["2,000 to 2,500 fpm","3,000 to 3,500 fpm","4,000 to 4,500 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r4","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, dry dusts and powders, such as fine rubber dust, jute lint, and soap dust, have a minimum duct design velocity of 3,000 to 3,500 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for dry dusts and powders such as soap dust?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives dry dusts and powders, such as soap dust and jute lint, a minimum duct design velocity of __.","a":"3,000 to 3,500 fpm","d":["2,500 to 3,000 fpm","3,500 to 4,000 fpm","4,500 fpm and up"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r5","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, average industrial dust, such as grinding dust, granite dust, and silica flour, has a minimum duct design velocity of 3,500 to 4,000 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for average industrial dust such as grinding dust?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives average industrial dust, such as grinding dust and silica flour, a minimum duct design velocity of __.","a":"3,500 to 4,000 fpm","d":["3,000 to 3,500 fpm","4,000 to 4,500 fpm","2,000 to 2,500 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r6","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, heavy dusts, such as wet sawdust, metal turnings, and sand blast dust, have a minimum duct design velocity of 4,000 to 4,500 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for heavy dusts such as wet sawdust?","trap":"Wet sawdust is a heavy dust (4,000 to 4,500). Moist cement dust is heavy or moist (4,500 and up).","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives heavy dusts, such as wet sawdust and metal turnings, a minimum duct design velocity of __.","a":"4,000 to 4,500 fpm","d":["3,500 to 4,000 fpm","4,500 fpm and up","3,000 to 3,500 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r7","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, heavy or moist dusts, such as lead dust with small chips, moist cement dust, and quick-lime, have a minimum duct design velocity of 4,500 fpm and up.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for heavy or moist dusts such as moist cement dust?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives heavy or moist dusts, such as moist cement dust and quick-lime, a minimum duct design velocity of __.","a":"4,500 fpm and up","d":["4,000 to 4,500 fpm","3,500 to 4,000 fpm","2,500 to 3,000 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.6-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"REC","text":"Coils, fans, condensate pans, drains, and similar non-porous surfaces get wet cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.6","prompt":"Which air-handling unit surfaces does §4.6 recommend for wet cleaning?","trap":"Recommended, and only for non-porous surfaces. Porous AHU liner never gets wet (4.4).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.6.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Water extraction control collects the debris and prevents water damage.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.6.1","prompt":"When wet cleaning an AHU, what two things must water extraction control do?","trap":"Wet cleaning is only recommended, but once you do it, water extraction control is a shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.6.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Waste water capture, containment, testing, and disposal follow applicable regulations.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.6.2","prompt":"What must waste water capture, containment, testing, and disposal follow?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.6.2-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"DEF","text":"AHU waste water capture, containment, testing, and disposal follow local, regional, state, and federal regulations.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.6.2","prompt":"Which levels of regulation govern AHU waste water under §4.6.2?","trap":"This clause says state and federal. The service opening clause 4.2.10 says provincial and national.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.8-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Dampers and air-directional devices get a position mark before cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.8","prompt":"What must be done to dampers and air-directional devices before cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.8-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Dampers and air-directional devices are returned to their marked positions after cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.8","prompt":"Where must dampers and air-directional devices be set after cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.9-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"REC","text":"Air distribution devices come out for proper cleaning, where removal is possible.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.9","prompt":"What does §4.9 recommend for registers, grilles, and diffusers during cleaning?","trap":"Removal is recommended, where possible. Returning them to position is the shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.9-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"After cleaning, registers, grilles, and diffusers go back to their previous positions.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.9","prompt":"Where must registers, grilles, and diffusers go after cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.9-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"NOTE","text":"Removal is a recommendation. Restoration of position is mandatory.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.9","prompt":"For registers, grilles, and diffusers, which step is recommended and which is mandatory?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.10-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning activities do not impair, alter, or damage detection equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.10","prompt":"What must cleaning activities not do to smoke and fire detection equipment?","trap":"When detection equipment goes off-line, the work plan must address life safety (2.8).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.10-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"DEF","text":"The rule against impairing detection equipment covers equipment in the facility and equipment that serves the HVAC components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.10","prompt":"Which smoke and fire detection equipment does §4.10 protect?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Coil cleaning requires access to both the upstream side and the downstream side of each coil section.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11","prompt":"Which sides of each coil section must be accessed for cleaning?","trap":"Both faces, upstream and downstream. Cleaning from one side only does not comply.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0271"]},{"id":"acr:4.11-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"CAN","text":"When both sides of a coil section have no access, removal or replacement becomes necessary.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11","prompt":"What may become necessary when neither side of a coil section can be accessed?","trap":"CAN, a possibility. Not an automatic requirement to replace.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0271"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"A visual inspection of the coil and the drain pan occurs before cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1","prompt":"What must be visually inspected before coil cleaning begins?","trap":"Coil AND drain pan. Suspect microbial growth on either one sends you to Type 2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"DEF","text":"The pre-cleaning visual inspection of the coil and drain pan determines whether Type 1 or Type 2 cleaning is needed.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1","prompt":"What decision does the pre-cleaning coil inspection make?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"When Type 1 cannot clean the coil properly, Type 2 occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.1","prompt":"What happens when Type 1 cannot properly clean the coil?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"When suspect microbial contamination appears on the coil or the drain pan, Type 2 occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.2","prompt":"What finding on the coil or drain pan requires Type 2?","trap":"Suspected contamination is enough. It sends the coil straight to Type 2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0108","cvi-0309"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1.3-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"CAN","text":"Replacement becomes necessary for damaged, deteriorating, or corroded metal fins.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.3","prompt":"Which fin conditions can make coil replacement necessary?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0311"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1.3-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"REC","text":"When cleaning will cause more damage to the coil, replacement happens.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.3","prompt":"What does §4.11.1.3 recommend when cleaning would cause more damage to the coil?","trap":"Recommended, not required. The fin-damage line beside it is CAN, a possibility.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0311"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Type 1 dry cleaning methods remove loose dust, dirt, or debris from coil surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"What does Type 1 dry cleaning remove from coil surfaces?","trap":"Type 1 is dry. Water at any pressure is Type 2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Negative air machines operate continuously during the Type 1 process.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"What equipment must run continuously during Type 1 coil cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"The coil stays isolated from the duct system during the process.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"During Type 1 cleaning, what must the coil be isolated from?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-d","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"DEF","text":"Isolation stops migration and redeposit of disrupted particulate.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"Why is the coil isolated from the duct system during Type 1?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-e","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 1 methods include HEPA-filtered contact vacuuming.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"Which vacuuming method is a Type 1 coil cleaning option?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-f","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 1 methods include brushes for penetration between the coil fins.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"Which hand tool that reaches between the fins is a Type 1 option?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-g","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 1 methods include compressed air.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"Which air-driven method is a Type 1 coil cleaning option?","trap":"Compressed air is Type 1 (dry). Pressure washing is Type 2 (wet).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-h","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 1 methods include fin straightening tools.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"Which tool for bent fins is listed among Type 1 methods?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.3-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"A post-cleaning inspection follows Type 1 cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.3","prompt":"What must follow Type 1 coil cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.3-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"When debris remains or the coil is impacted, Type 2 occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.3","prompt":"After Type 1, which findings send the coil to Type 2?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"DEF","text":"Type 2 is wet cleaning. It removes debris on coil, drain pan, and drain line surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"What is Type 2 coil cleaning, and which surfaces does it address?","trap":"Type 2 covers the drain pan and drain line surfaces too, not only the coil.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0309"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"When assessment determines that Type 1 will not be sufficient, Type 2 occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Under 2025 §4.11.4, what assessment result triggers Type 2?","trap":"New in 2025. You no longer have to try Type 1 and fail first.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0109","cvi-0308"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"When debris remains after Type 1 methods, Type 2 also occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Under §4.11.4, besides an assessment that Type 1 will not be sufficient, what result also triggers Type 2?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-h","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition required Type 1 first, always. The 2025 edition does not.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"How did the rule on doing Type 1 before Type 2 change between 2021 and 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0108","cvi-0109","cvi-0308"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-d","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 2 methods include application of coil cleaning products.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Which chemical method is a Type 2 option?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-e","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 2 methods include water washing at normal water line pressure.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Which low-pressure water method is a Type 2 option?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-f","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 2 methods include pressure washing equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Which high-pressure water equipment is a Type 2 option?","trap":"Permitted, but 4.11.4.2 still forbids damaging or eroding the coil or fins.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-g","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 2 methods include hot water equipment or steam cleaning equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Which heated methods are Type 2 options?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-g2","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"Coil cleaning products follow the product labeling of the manufacturer.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"What governs the use of coil cleaning products?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-g3","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 Type 2 list opened with \"All methods under Type 1\". That bullet is gone.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Which bullet was removed from the start of the Type 2 methods list in 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The condensate drain pan and the drain line get cleaning and flushing.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.1","prompt":"What must be done to the condensate drain pan and drain line in Type 2?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4.1-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The drain pan gets inspection and a drainage test before cleaning and after cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.1","prompt":"When must the drain pan be inspected and drainage tested?","trap":"Before AND after cleaning. A test only at the end does not comply.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"Methods and products do not damage or erode the coil surface or the fins.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.2","prompt":"What must coil cleaning methods and products not do to the coil surface or fins?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4.2-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"Type 2 coil cleaning methods and products conform to the coil manufacturer recommendations, where those exist.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.2","prompt":"Whose recommendations must coil cleaning methods and products conform to, where they exist?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0118"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4.2-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"REC","text":"The coil cleaning solutions are as close to pH neutral as possible.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.2","prompt":"What pH does §4.11.4.2 recommend for coil cleaning solutions?","trap":"Recommended, not required. ACR does not ban acid or alkaline coil cleaners.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0118"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.5-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"A post-cleaning inspection follows Type 2 cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.5","prompt":"What must follow Type 2 coil cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.5-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"When debris remains on the coil, the process repeats.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.5","prompt":"What happens when debris remains on the coil after Type 2?","trap":"Repeat first. Replacement comes up only when Type 2 methods cannot remove the debris.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.5-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"CAN","text":"When Type 2 methods cannot remove the debris, replacement becomes necessary.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.5","prompt":"What may become necessary when Type 2 methods cannot remove the debris?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.6-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"DEF","text":"Visual observation of coil surfaces can mislead the inspector.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.6","prompt":"Why is visual observation alone a weak test of coil cleanliness?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.6-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"REC","text":"A static pressure drop measurement occurs before and after the cleaning process.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.6","prompt":"What measurement does §4.11.6 recommend before and after coil cleaning?","trap":"Recommended, not required, and ACR gives no target pressure drop value.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0310"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.7-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"DEF","text":"Coil cleaning normally needs pressurized water and chemical agents.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.7","prompt":"What does coil cleaning normally require, per §4.11.7?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.7-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"Precautions capture the rinse water for duct mounted coils without drain pans.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.7","prompt":"What must be done about rinse water when cleaning duct-mounted coils without drain pans?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.7-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"Type 1 methods, Type 2 methods, or both apply to inline coils.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.7","prompt":"Which cleaning types apply to inline coils?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.8-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"The power source to electric resistance coils gets de-energization and lockout/tagout.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.8","prompt":"What must happen to the power source before cleaning electric resistance coils?","trap":"Switching it off is not enough. De-energize AND lockout/tagout.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.8-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"Only non-corrosive detergents apply during wet process cleaning of these coils.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.8","prompt":"What type of detergent may be used when wet cleaning electric resistance coils?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.8-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"The coil gets a rinse free of chemicals.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.8","prompt":"After detergent, what must be done to an electric resistance coil?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.8-d","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"The coil is thoroughly dry before it returns to an energized state.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.8","prompt":"What condition must an electric resistance coil be in before it is re-energized?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.12-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"All products comply with local, regional, state, and federal regulations.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.12","prompt":"What regulations must all products used for odor and emission control comply with?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.13-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Remediation follows ANSI/IICRC S520 for professional mold remediation.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.13","prompt":"Which standard governs remediation of microbial contamination under §4.13?","trap":"S520 is mold remediation. S500 is water damage. S590 is HVAC assessment after water, fire, or mold.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.13-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Remediation also follows the component provisions of this Standard.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.13","prompt":"Besides S520, what else must microbial remediation follow?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.13-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 heading read \"Remediation of Mold Contamination\".","src":"ACR 2025 §4.13","prompt":"What was the 2021 heading of clause 4.13?","trap":"2025 says microbial throughout, where 2021 said mold.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.14-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Restoration procedures occur only after mechanical cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14","prompt":"When may restoration procedures begin?","trap":"Restoration needs mechanical cleaning first. Surface treatments need more: a PASS on verification (4.15).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.14.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Fire, smoke, flood, or water damage starts the procedures in 4.23 and 4.24.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.1","prompt":"Which types of damage start the procedures in 4.23 and 4.24?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.14.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Compromised components get attention as part of the restoration, to the extent possible.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.2","prompt":"How does restoration treat compromised components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.14.3-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"REC","text":"When cleaning and restoration cannot achieve the levels, components get a replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.3","prompt":"What does §4.14.3 recommend when cleaning and restoration cannot reach the required levels?","trap":"Replacement here is recommended. For fire-exposed parts that cannot withstand cleaning, 4.23 makes replacement a shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.14.4-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Components that cannot withstand proper cleaning receive documentation.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.4","prompt":"What record must be made of components that cannot withstand proper cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0117","cvi-0291"]},{"id":"acr:4.14.4-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"The customer receives notice of components that cannot withstand proper cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.4","prompt":"Who must be told about components that cannot withstand proper cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0117"]},{"id":"acr:4.14.4-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Components that cannot withstand proper cleaning get a recommendation for repair or replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.4","prompt":"What must be recommended for components that cannot withstand proper cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0117","cvi-0291"]},{"id":"acr:4.15-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"MAY","text":"Surface treatments restore the integrity of material surfaces as an alternative to replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.15","prompt":"What may surface treatments be used for instead of replacement?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.15-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Surface treatments apply only after confirmation that the system is clean.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.15","prompt":"What must be confirmed before surface treatments are applied?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0322"]},{"id":"acr:4.15-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Surface treatments apply only after the system passes the specified level of cleanliness verification.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.15","prompt":"What verification result must come before surface treatments are applied?","trap":"Not just after cleaning. A PASS on the specified verification comes first.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0322"]},{"id":"acr:4.15.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Surface treatments meet the requirements of UL 181.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.15.1","prompt":"What UL listing must surface treatments meet?","trap":"UL 181, not UL 181A/B. 181A/B is for closure tapes and mastics.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.15.1-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"NOTE","text":"UL 181 covers surface treatments. UL 181A/B covers closure tapes and mastics.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.15.1","prompt":"In ACR, what does UL 181 cover and what does UL 181A/B cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-10","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"NOTE","text":"Restoration follows cleaning. Surface treatments follow a PASS on verification.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14, 4.15","prompt":"What must come before restoration, and what must come before surface treatments?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.17-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"SHALL","text":"Fibrous glass duct liner and duct board follow Section 4.7 for cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.17","prompt":"Which section's cleaning rules apply to fibrous glass duct liner and duct board?","trap":"Same agitate-and-capture method as any duct, but water and cleaning agents stay off it (4.4).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.17.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"SHALL","text":"The methods create no abrasions, breaks, or tears on the fibrous glass surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.17.1","prompt":"What damage must fibrous glass cleaning methods not create?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.18-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"MAY","text":"Resurfacing applies to friable components or components with abrasion or degradation.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18","prompt":"Which fibrous glass conditions may call for resurfacing?","trap":"MAY, a permission. Resurfacing is optional here. Only fire-damaged friable or odor-retaining areas must be resurfaced or replaced (4.23.2).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.18-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"MAY","text":"When the work plan asks for smoother fibrous glass surfaces, resurfacing also applies.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18","prompt":"Besides damage, what work plan goal may justify resurfacing fibrous glass?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.18.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"SHALL","text":"An assessment determines that the surface gives a strong bondable surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18.1","prompt":"What must an assessment confirm before fibrous glass is resurfaced?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0113","cvi-0312"]},{"id":"acr:4.18.1-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"DEF","text":"The bondability assessment for resurfacing fibrous glass happens after proper mechanical cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18.1","prompt":"When does the bondability assessment for resurfacing happen?","trap":"After proper mechanical cleaning, not before.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0113","cvi-0312"]},{"id":"acr:4.18.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"SHALL","text":"No resurfacing occurs on material that is beyond restoration and fails one further test.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18.2","prompt":"When is resurfacing of fibrous glass forbidden?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.18.2-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"DEF","text":"Fibrous glass beyond restoration is not resurfaced if it cannot support proper application of a surfacing product.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18.2","prompt":"Which condition about applying a surfacing product rules out resurfacing material beyond restoration?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.18.2-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"DEF","text":"The alternative further test is inability to give a long-term bondable surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18.2","prompt":"Which long-term bonding condition is the alternative test that rules out resurfacing?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.19-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"REC","text":"Replacement follows damage, deterioration, delamination, or friable material.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.19","prompt":"Which fibrous glass conditions does §4.19 recommend replacement for?","trap":"Recommended, and only after cleaning and resurfacing cannot restore the material.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0291"]},{"id":"acr:4.19-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"DEF","text":"The §4.19 replacement recommendation for damaged fibrous glass applies only after cleaning and resurfacing cannot restore the material.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.19","prompt":"When does the §4.19 replacement recommendation for damaged fibrous glass apply?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.21-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"REC","text":"When a cleaned non-porous surface will still contribute particulate or odors, restoration follows.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.21","prompt":"When does §4.21 recommend restoring a non-porous surface after cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.16-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"REC","text":"Porous materials with growth at Condition 3 get proper removal and replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.16","prompt":"What does §4.16 recommend for porous materials with Condition 3 growth?","trap":"Removal is recommended, not required. The mechanical surface cleaning that follows is the shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.16-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"After Condition 3 porous material is removed, the remaining surfaces get mechanical surface cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.16","prompt":"What must follow removal of microbial contaminated porous material?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.20-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"Metal surfaces get a scrape clean after removal of degraded thermal-acoustic material.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20","prompt":"What must be done to metal surfaces after degraded thermal-acoustic material is removed?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.20-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"The base surface is free of loose visible debris before the new insulation.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20","prompt":"What condition must the base surface be in before new insulation is installed?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.20.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"A microbial cause of removal makes the base surface reach a Condition 1 status first.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.1","prompt":"When insulation was removed for a microbial cause, what status must the base surface reach before new insulation?","trap":"Condition 1 is normal fungal ecology and can include traces of growth. It does not mean sterile.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0313"]},{"id":"acr:X-11","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"NOTE","text":"Condition 1 is a cleaning target at one clause only. That clause is 4.20.1.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.1","prompt":"At which single clause does ACR use Condition 1 as a cleaning target?","trap":"Other clauses target visibly clean or a verification PASS, not a Condition.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.20.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"Replacement materials meet or exceed the original specifications or current codes.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.2","prompt":"What must replacement insulation materials meet or exceed?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0314"]},{"id":"acr:4.20.2-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"Installation follows the written instructions of the manufacturer.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.2","prompt":"Whose instructions govern installation of replacement insulation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.20.3-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"Installation complies with current SMACNA, NAIMA, and other applicable codes.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.3","prompt":"Which industry standards govern installation of replacement thermal-acoustic insulation?","trap":"SMACNA and NAIMA for insulation installation. UL 723 is the fire test and UL 181 the coating listing.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.20.4-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"New fibrous glass surfaces are capable of meeting the NADCA verification requirements.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.4","prompt":"What verification capability must new fibrous glass surfaces have?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.20.5-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"No cleaning process damages a properly designed, installed, and sound system.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.5","prompt":"What must no cleaning process do to a properly designed, installed, and sound system?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.20.5-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"No cleaning process affects the performance, operation, or normal life expectancy.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.5","prompt":"Which three things about a sound system must no cleaning process affect?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.22-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Water-damaged surfaces get evaluation and categorization by recognized methods.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.22","prompt":"What must happen to water-damaged surfaces under §4.22?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0114"]},{"id":"acr:4.22-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S500 gives those methods for professional water damage restoration.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.22","prompt":"Which standard gives the recognized methods for evaluating and categorizing water damage?","trap":"S500 is water. S520 is mold. S590 is assessing HVAC after water, fire, or mold.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0114"]},{"id":"acr:4.22-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"DEF","text":"The category of the water largely dictates the cleaning methods and the engineering controls.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.22","prompt":"What largely dictates the cleaning methods and engineering controls on a water-damage job?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0318"]},{"id":"acr:4.22-d","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"All components with salvage value get thorough cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.22","prompt":"Which water-damaged components must be thoroughly cleaned?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0319"]},{"id":"acr:4.23-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Components under heat and smoke get an evaluation for restoration.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23","prompt":"What must happen to components exposed to heat and smoke?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.23-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Components that cannot withstand proper cleaning and restoration get a replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23","prompt":"What must happen to fire-exposed components that cannot withstand proper cleaning and restoration?","trap":"A shall here. The general replacement line at 4.14.3 is only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.23.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Porous surfaces get an evaluation for friability and odor retention after cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23.1","prompt":"After cleaning fire-damaged porous surfaces, which two properties must be evaluated?","trap":"Evaluate after cleaning, not before.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0316"]},{"id":"acr:4.23.2-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Friable areas or odor-retaining areas get resurfacing or replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23.2","prompt":"What must happen to fire-damaged areas that are friable or retain odor?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0316"]},{"id":"acr:4.23.3-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Heat-damaged surfaces reach an acceptable condition, or they get a replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23.3","prompt":"What are the two acceptable outcomes for heat-damaged surfaces?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.23.4-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"REC","text":"Sampling follows ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 for a question about soot or smoke.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23.4","prompt":"Which standard does §4.23.4 recommend for sampling when soot or smoke is in question?","trap":"Recommended. IESO/RIA 6001 is for fire residue. NIOSH 0500 is the Method 3 dust analysis.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0236","cvi-0317"]},{"id":"acr:4.24-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Pre-existing damage found during cleaning receives documentation.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.24","prompt":"What record must be made of pre-existing damage found during cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.24-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"The client receives notice of pre-existing damage found during cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.24","prompt":"Who must be notified of pre-existing damage found during cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.24.1-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"DEF","text":"Repair or replacement of malfunctioning mechanical devices is outside this scope.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.24.1","prompt":"What mechanical work falls outside the §4.24 component repair scope?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.24.1-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"DEF","text":"Restoration does not include the sealing of air leaks in duct systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.24.1","prompt":"Which duct system task does ACR restoration not include?","trap":"Sealing duct leaks is not restoration. Sealing a service panel (4.2.5.3) is a separate shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0335"]},{"id":"acr:5.0-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"The purpose of verification is a determination of the presence of particulate.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0","prompt":"Under ACR 2025, what is the purpose of cleanliness verification?","trap":"Verification checks for particulate only. It is not a mold test and not a hazardous material test.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.0-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"Verification does not determine microbial contamination.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0","prompt":"Does ACR 2025 cleanliness verification determine microbial contamination?","trap":"A verification PASS says nothing about mold. Condition 1, 2 or 3 status is an IEP determination.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0336"]},{"id":"acr:5.0-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"Verification does not determine the presence of hazardous materials.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0","prompt":"Does ACR 2025 cleanliness verification determine whether hazardous materials are present?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.0-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"SHALL","text":"Verification occurs on all specified components.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0","prompt":"Which components must receive cleanliness verification?","trap":"The 10% minimum belongs to inspection (1.7), not verification. Verification covers all specified components.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0242","cvi-0292"]},{"id":"acr:5.0-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"SHALL","text":"Components reach visibly clean, at minimum, or the specified method of verification.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0","prompt":"What cleanliness result must cleaned components meet, at minimum, under Section 5?","trap":"Visibly clean is the floor. When the contract documents specify a verification method, that method applies.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0119","cvi-0338"]},{"id":"acr:5.0-f","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 10% figure belongs to component inspection at 1.7. It is not a verification figure.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0, §1.7","prompt":"Does the 10% minimum in ACR 2025 belong to verification or to component inspection?","trap":"Verification covers all specified components. 10% is the inspection minimum, and S520 microbial projects inspect all components.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0110"]},{"id":"acr:5.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"SHALL","text":"Verification occurs immediately after the cleaning of the component.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.1","prompt":"How soon after cleaning a component must verification occur?","trap":"Two timing rules go together: immediately after cleaning, and before the system operates.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0110","cvi-0337"]},{"id":"acr:5.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"SHALL","text":"Verification occurs before the system operates.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.1","prompt":"Relative to putting the system back into operation, when must verification occur?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0110","cvi-0337"]},{"id":"acr:5-M1","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"Method 1 is Visual Inspection. It applies to porous and non-porous components.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2","prompt":"What is Method 1, and which component surfaces does it apply to?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5-M2","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"Method 2 is the Surface Comparison Test. It applies to porous and non-porous components.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3","prompt":"What is Method 2, and which component surfaces does it apply to in ACR 2025?","trap":"2021 limited Method 2 to porous surfaces, and the 2025 Definitions entry still says porous. Section 5.3 governs.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0274"]},{"id":"acr:5-M3","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"Method 3 is the NADCA Vacuum Test. It applies to non-porous components only.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4, §5.3.3","prompt":"What is Method 3, and which component surfaces does it apply to?","trap":"The 2021 numbering had two \"Method 2\"s. In 2025 the Vacuum Test is Method 3, and never on porous surfaces.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.2-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"SHALL","text":"A visual inspection of porous and non-porous components determines visibly clean status.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2","prompt":"How is visibly clean status determined for porous and non-porous components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.2-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"A surface is visibly clean only if it is free from non-adhered substances and debris.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2","prompt":"Under Method 1 (5.2), when is a surface visibly clean?","trap":"Adhered substances such as paint or stains are not the test. Visibly clean means free of non-adhered substances and debris.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.2-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"A visibly clean component needs no further verification method.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2","prompt":"When a component is found visibly clean, is another verification method required?","trap":"Method 2 or 3 follows only when Method 1 is inconclusive or disputed, or the contract specifies it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.2.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"REC","text":"When Method 1 is inconclusive or in dispute, Method 2 or Method 3 verifies cleanliness.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2.1","prompt":"What does ACR 2025 recommend when a Method 1 visual result is inconclusive or in dispute?","trap":"This escalation is recommended, not required. Method 3 still applies only to non-porous surfaces.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"MAY","text":"Method 2 determines the cleanliness of porous and non-porous component surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3","prompt":"Which component surfaces may Method 2, the Surface Comparison Test, evaluate in ACR 2025?","trap":"The 2021 edition said porous only. The 2025 edition widened Method 2 to porous and non-porous.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0124","cvi-0340"]},{"id":"acr:5.3-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition limited Method 2 to porous surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3","prompt":"To which surfaces did the 2021 edition limit the Surface Comparison Test?","trap":"A study guide built on 2021 says porous only. That answer is wrong for the 2025 exam.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0124","cvi-0340"]},{"id":"acr:5.3-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2025 Definitions entry still reads \"porous\". The Section 5.3 body text governs.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3, Definitions","prompt":"When the 2025 Definitions entry and Section 5.3 disagree on Method 2 scope, which text governs?","trap":"An option quoting the Definitions entry (porous only) looks authoritative. The 5.3 body text says porous and non-porous.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3.3-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"MAY","text":"When Method 2 is inconclusive or in dispute, Method 3 makes the final determination.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.3","prompt":"What makes the final determination when a Method 2 result is inconclusive or in dispute?","trap":"Only on non-porous surfaces. On porous material Method 2 ends the escalation. Clause 5.3.3 is new in 2025.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3.3-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"The NADCA Vacuum Test does not apply to porous components.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.3","prompt":"Does the NADCA Vacuum Test apply to porous components?","trap":"Polymetric flex duct film is non-porous, so Method 3 can apply to it. Duct liner and duct board are porous.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0274","cvi-0339","cvi-0346"]},{"id":"acr:5-M4","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"NOTE","text":"The escalation is Method 1, then Method 2 or Method 3, then Method 3 as the final word.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2.1, §5.3.3","prompt":"What is the verification escalation order across the three methods?","trap":"Method 1 disputed goes to Method 2 or 3. Method 2 disputed goes to Method 3. Nothing escalates past Method 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5-M5","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"NOTE","text":"On porous material, Method 2 is the end of the escalation. Method 3 cannot apply.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.3","prompt":"On porous material, where does the verification escalation end?","trap":"Wood and concrete are porous too, so a disputed Method 2 on them cannot go to Method 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0339","cvi-0346"]},{"id":"acr:5.3-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"DEF","text":"The test compares the visible characteristics of the surface before and after contact vacuuming.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3","prompt":"What does the Method 2 Surface Comparison Test compare?","trap":"Method 2 is still a visual judgment. Nothing is weighed; weighing is Method 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"A vacuum brush attaches to a running contact vacuum.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"In Method 2, what machine is the vacuum brush attached to, and what state must that machine be in?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The brush passes over the surface test area four (4) times.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"How many times does the brush pass over the Method 2 test area?","trap":"Method 2 is 4 brush passes. Method 3 is 2 passes per slot, once in each direction.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"In Method 2, the brush passes over the surface test area __.","a":"4 times","d":["2 times","3 times","6 times"]},"qs":["cvi-0341"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The pressure is light to moderate, as in routine cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"What brush pressure does Method 2 use?","trap":"Method 2 uses light to moderate pressure. The Method 3 cassette rides the template with no downward pressure.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0344"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The Method 2 contact vacuum must be HEPA-filtered.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"What filtration must the Method 2 contact vacuum have?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0342"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The contact vacuum achieves a minimum of 80 inches of static lift (WC).","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"What minimum static lift must the Method 2 contact vacuum achieve?","trap":"80 in. WC is vacuum lift for Method 2. The Standard gives no numeric value for containment negative pressure.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The minimum static lift the Method 2 contact vacuum must achieve is __ (WC).","a":"80 inches","d":["15 inches","37 inches","100 inches"]},"qs":["cvi-0342"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-f","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The contact vacuum has a 2.5 inch round nylon brush.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"What size and type of brush does the Method 2 contact vacuum use?","trap":"Brush 2.5 in., hose 1.5 in. The two sizes swap easily.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 2 contact vacuum has a __ round nylon brush.","a":"2.5 inch","d":["1.5 inch","4 inch","6 inch"]},"qs":["cvi-0343"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-g","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The brush attaches to a 1.5 inch diameter vacuum hose.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"What diameter vacuum hose does the Method 2 brush attach to?","trap":"Hose 1.5 in., brush 2.5 in. The two sizes swap easily.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 2 brush attaches to a __ diameter vacuum hose.","a":"1.5 inch","d":["1 inch","2.5 inch","4 inch"]},"qs":["cvi-0343"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.2-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"A comparison determines any significant change in the visible characteristics.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.2","prompt":"After the Method 2 brush passes, what does the comparison look for?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3.2-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"DEF","text":"The surface is clean only if no significant visible difference exists.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.2","prompt":"What is the Method 2 pass condition?","trap":"A visible difference after brushing means dirt came off, so the surface fails. No significant difference is a pass.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"Method 3 evaluates particulate levels on non-porous component surfaces by scientific means.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"What does Method 3 evaluate, on which surfaces, and by what means?","trap":"Non-porous only. It is the only method of the three that weighs what it collects.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"A template applies to the ducted airside surface of the component.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"In Method 3, where is the template applied?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"A closed-face vacuum cassette attaches to a calibrated air sampling pump.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"In Method 3, what does the closed-face vacuum cassette attach to?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"The cassette passes over two (2) openings of 2 cm x 25 cm in the template.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"In the Method 3 overview, how many template openings does the cassette pass over, and what size is each?","trap":"Two slots of 2 cm x 25 cm are 50 cm2 each, 100 cm2 together. Use that math to check the numbers.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 cassette passes over two template openings of __ x 25 cm.","a":"2 cm","d":["2.5 cm","3.7 cm","5 cm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"CAN","text":"No portion of the cassette can contact the component surface at any time.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"Can any portion of the Method 3 cassette contact the component surface at any time?","trap":"The cassette edges rest on the template, never on the duct surface.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-f","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"The template holds the cassette above the surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"In Method 3, what holds the cassette above the sampled surface?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-g","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"Airflow accelerates through the narrow opening between the template and the surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"During the NADCA Vacuum Test, where does the airflow accelerate?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-h","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"The increased velocity dislodges latent particulate onto the filter media.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"In Method 3, what does the increased air velocity do?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-i","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"After the procedure, the cassette gets preparation and a weight measurement.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"What happens to the Method 3 cassette after the sampling procedure?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-j","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"Weighing the Method 3 cassette after sampling gives the total debris on the filter media.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"What does the Method 3 cassette weight measurement give?","trap":"The pass limit is a net weight per 100 cm2, not a particle count.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The air sampling pump draws a minimum of 15 liters per minute.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"What minimum flow must the Method 3 air sampling pump draw?","trap":"Three flow numbers: pump capable of 15, sample at 17 plus or minus 2, calibrator accurate at 15.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The minimum flow the Method 3 air sampling pump must draw is __.","a":"15 L/min","d":["5 L/min","17 L/min","19 L/min"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cassette is closed-face and holds 37 mm matched weight filters.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"What type of cassette does Method 3 use, and what filters does it hold?","trap":"37 mm is the filter. 3.7 cm is the maximum alternate slot width. Same digits, different parts.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 cassette is closed-face and holds __ matched weight filters.","a":"37 mm","d":["3.7 mm","15 mm","25 mm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The filter media is 37 mm mixed cellulose ester matched weight filters.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"What material are the Method 3 filters made of?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The pore size is 0.8 or 0.45 micrometer.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"What pore sizes are allowed for the Method 3 filter media?","trap":"Filter pore size is 0.8 or 0.45 micrometer. HEPA's 0.3 micron is a different number for a different job.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 filter pore size is 0.8 or __.","a":"0.45 micrometer","d":["0.3 micrometer","0.7 micrometer","0.75 micrometer"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"Two filters go in series, preloaded in a three-piece cassette.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"How many Method 3 filters are used, how are they arranged, and in what kind of cassette?","trap":"Two filters, three-piece cassette. The two counts swap easily.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Two filters go in series, preloaded in a __ cassette.","a":"three-piece","d":["one-piece","two-piece","four-piece"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-f","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The calibration device is accurate to ±5% at 15 liters per minute.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"How accurate must the Method 3 calibration device be, and at what flow?","trap":"The calibrator is checked at 15 L/min, but the sampling flow is set to 17 plus or minus 2 L/min.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The required accuracy of the Method 3 calibration device at 15 liters per minute is __.","a":"±5%","d":["±2%","±10%","±15%"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-g","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The template is 15 mil thick (0.381 mm).","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"How thick is the Method 3 template?","trap":"15 mil is template thickness. 15 L/min is pump capability. Same number, different parts.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 template is __ thick.","a":"15 mil","d":["6 mil","10 mil","37 mil"]},"qs":["cvi-0358"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-h","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The template gives a 100 cm2 sampling area.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"What sampling area does the standard Method 3 template give?","trap":"Each slot is 50 cm2. Two slots make 100 cm2, the same area the pass limit is expressed in.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 template gives a __ sampling area.","a":"100 cm2","d":["25 cm2","50 cm2","75 cm2"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-i","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The template has two (2) slots of 2 cm x 25 cm, at least 2.5 cm apart.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"How many slots does the Method 3 template have, what size are they, and how far apart?","trap":"2.5 cm is the minimum slot spacing. 2.5 in. is the Method 2 brush. Watch the units.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The minimum spacing between the two 2 cm x 25 cm template slots is __.","a":"2.5 cm","d":["2.0 cm","3.7 cm","5 cm"]},"qs":["cvi-0358"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"The standard template openings are 2 centimeters wide by 25 centimeters long.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.1","prompt":"What are the width and length of the standard Method 3 template openings?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The standard template openings are 2 centimeters wide by __ long.","a":"25 centimeters","d":["2.5 centimeters","3.7 centimeters","15 centimeters"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.1-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"MAY","text":"When the standard slot does not fit the space, slots of other sizes apply.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.1","prompt":"What may be used when the standard 2 cm x 25 cm template slot does not fit the space?","trap":"Other slot sizes are allowed, but the total must still be 100 cm2 and each width 2.0 to 3.7 cm.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.2-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The total sampled area equals 100 square centimeters.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.2","prompt":"With an alternate-size template, what must the total sampled area equal?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.2-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The maximum width of the opening does not exceed 3.7 centimeters.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.2","prompt":"What is the maximum opening width for an alternate Method 3 template slot?","trap":"3.7 cm maximum slot width, not the 37 mm filter. The 2.0 cm minimum and 3.7 cm maximum bracket the width.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The largest width allowed for an alternate template opening is __.","a":"3.7 centimeters","d":["2.0 centimeters","2.5 centimeters","5 centimeters"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.2-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"The 3.7 cm maximum width of an alternate Method 3 template opening stops the cassette from contacting the sampled surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.2","prompt":"Why does an alternate template opening have a maximum width?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.2-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The minimum opening width is 2.0 centimeters or more.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.2","prompt":"What is the minimum opening width for an alternate Method 3 template slot?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The smallest width allowed for an alternate template opening is __.","a":"2.0 centimeters","d":["1.5 centimeters","2.5 centimeters","3.7 centimeters"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The template has a secure attachment, for example tape at four corners.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How is the Method 3 template secured to the sampled surface?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The template lies flat against the sampled surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How must the Method 3 template sit against the sampled surface?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-c","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"For Method 3 (vacuum test) sampling, the surface must be dry.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What condition must the surface be in for Method 3 sampling?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0348"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-d","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The air handler does not run during the sampling.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"May the air handler run during Method 3 sampling?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0347"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-e","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The supplier seals the cassettes with shrink tape.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Who seals new Method 3 cassettes, and with what?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-f","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The protective plugs come out of the new cassette.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What is removed from a new Method 3 cassette before sampling?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-g","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The outlet end of the cassette attaches to the vacuum pump tubing.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Which end of the Method 3 cassette attaches to the vacuum pump tubing?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-h","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"A calibration device adjusts the air flow to 17 liters per minute, plus or minus 2.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"To what airflow is the Method 3 sampling flow adjusted, and with what?","trap":"Sampling flow is 17 plus or minus 2 L/min. 15 L/min is the pump minimum and the calibrator accuracy point.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"A calibration device adjusts the Method 3 sampling air flow to __, plus or minus 2.","a":"17 L/min","d":["15 L/min","19 L/min","25 L/min"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-4","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"NOTE","text":"Three flow numbers exist for Method 3. They are 15 L/min, 17±2 L/min, and ±5% at 15 L/min.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1, §5.4.2","prompt":"What are the three flow numbers in Method 3?","trap":"Pump capable of 15, sample at 17 plus or minus 2, calibrator accurate to plus or minus 5% at 15.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-i","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cassette slides from one end of each template opening to the other.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How does the Method 3 cassette travel along each template opening?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-j","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The rate is not greater than 5 cm per second, about 5 seconds per slot each direction.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How fast may the cassette travel along a Method 3 template slot?","trap":"5 cm per second over a 25 cm slot is about 5 seconds each way. Not the plus or minus 5% calibrator.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The fastest the cassette may travel along each Method 3 template slot is __.","a":"5 cm per second","d":["2 cm per second","2.5 cm per second","25 cm per second"]},"qs":["cvi-0350"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-k","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The edges of the cassette always rest on the template.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"During Method 3 sampling, what do the cassette edges rest on?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0351"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-l","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cassette does not touch the duct surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"During the sampling passes, may the Method 3 cassette touch the duct surface?","trap":"The Method 2 brush contacts the surface. The Method 3 cassette never does.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0351"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-m","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"Each template opening gets a vacuum twice, once in each direction.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How many times is each Method 3 template opening vacuumed, and in which directions?","trap":"Two passes per slot in Method 3. Four brush passes is Method 2.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"In total, each Method 3 template opening is vacuumed __.","a":"twice","d":["once","three times","four times"]},"qs":["cvi-0350"]},{"id":"acr:X-5","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"NOTE","text":"Method 2 uses four brush passes. Method 3 uses two passes per slot.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1, §5.4.2","prompt":"How many passes does Method 2 use compared with Method 3?","trap":"Four is the Method 2 brush count. Two per slot, one in each direction, is Method 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0341"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-n","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cassette touches the template surface with no downward pressure.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How much downward pressure does the Method 3 cassette put on the template?","trap":"Method 2 uses light to moderate brush pressure. Method 3 uses no downward pressure at all.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0344","cvi-0351"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-o","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"MAY","text":"The vacuum pump goes off after the two passes. Then the plugs go back.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What happens to the pump and the cassette plugs after the two passes?","trap":"The 2021 clear plastic cover step before pump shutdown is gone in 2025.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-y","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition had a \"clear plastic cover\" step before pump shutdown. It is gone.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What 2021 Method 3 step before pump shutdown was removed in 2025?","trap":"A 2021 study guide still lists the clear plastic cover. It is not a 2025 step.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-p","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cassette gets a label and a record of the sampled surface area.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"After Method 3 sampling, what must be put on or recorded with each cassette?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0355"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-q","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"Analysis follows NIOSH Method 0500 for total nuisance dust.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What analytical method is used for Method 3 cassettes?","trap":"NIOSH 0500 measures total nuisance dust by weight. It is not a microbial analysis.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-r","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"The scale sensitivity is equal to or greater than 0.7 milligram.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What scale sensitivity does ACR 2025 require for weighing Method 3 filters?","trap":"It reads 0.7 mg or more, backwards from lab logic, but it is verbatim. 0.75 is the pass limit, not the scale.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"ACR 2025 words the Method 3 scale sensitivity as equal to or greater than __.","a":"0.7 mg","d":["0.45 mg","0.75 mg","0.8 mg"]},"qs":["cvi-0352"]},{"id":"acr:X-6","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"NOTE","text":"Scale sensitivity is 0.7 mg or MORE. This reads backwards but it is verbatim.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Is the Method 3 scale sensitivity worded as 0.7 mg or less, or 0.7 mg or more?","trap":"Lab logic says a finer scale is better, so \"or less\" feels right. The Standard says equal to or greater than.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-s","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"The scale has calibration to the written recommendations of the manufacturer.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"To what is the Method 3 weighing scale calibrated?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-t","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"Results appear in milligrams per 100 square centimeters.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"In what units are Method 3 results reported?","trap":"The reporting area, 100 cm2, matches the template sampling area.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.5-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"The net weight of the collected debris does not exceed 0.75 mg/100 cm2.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.5","prompt":"What is the Method 3 passing criterion?","trap":"0.75 mg/100 cm2 is the pass limit. 0.7 mg is the scale sensitivity. The limit is on net weight.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 pass limit, the maximum net weight of collected debris, is __.","a":"0.75 mg/100 cm2","d":["0.45 mg/100 cm2","0.7 mg/100 cm2","0.8 mg/100 cm2"]},"qs":["cvi-0275","cvi-0352"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-u","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"REC","text":"A qualified individual of the client or the client agent procures the samples.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Who does ACR 2025 recommend should procure Method 3 samples?","trap":"Recommended, not required. Answers that make client sampling mandatory are wrong.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0353"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-v","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"REC","text":"An accredited laboratory analyzes the samples.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Who does ACR 2025 recommend should analyze Method 3 samples?","trap":"An accredited lab is recommended. Analysis by NIOSH 0500 is the SHALL.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-w","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"CAN","text":"Sampling equipment is capable of transport onto the work site.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Can Method 3 sampling equipment be transported onto the work site?","trap":"CAN states ability. It is neither a permission nor a requirement.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-x","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"NOTE","text":"Samples generally go to a laboratory. On-site sampling is still permitted.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Where do Method 3 samples generally go, and is on-site sampling permitted?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.6-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"REC","text":"Documentation of compliance with this Standard covers all work performed.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6","prompt":"What does ACR 2025 recommend that post-project documentation of compliance cover?","trap":"Overall documentation is recommended. Lab results after a Vacuum Test and chain of custody for outside labs are SHALL.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.6-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"CAN","text":"Documentation includes organized and legible written records and visual records.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6","prompt":"In broad terms, what two kinds of records can post-project documentation include, and in what condition?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.6.1-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"A copy of the lab results goes into the documentation after a NADCA Vacuum Test.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6.1","prompt":"When a NADCA Vacuum Test was performed, what must go into the post-project documentation?","trap":"One of only two SHALLs in 5.6. Photos, plans and submittals are only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0357","cvi-0360"]},{"id":"acr:5.6.2-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"When outside laboratories or agencies do the work, chain of custody documentation applies.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6.2","prompt":"When is chain of custody documentation required in post-project documentation?","trap":"Required only when outside laboratories or agencies do the work.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0356","cvi-0386"]},{"id":"acr:5.6.3-a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"REC","text":"The documentation includes photo images and HVAC plans.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6.3","prompt":"Which images and drawings does ACR 2025 recommend including in post-project documentation?","trap":"Photos are recommended, not required. Before and after photos are not a SHALL.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.6.3-b","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"REC","text":"The documentation includes submittal forms for materials, warrantees, and guarantees.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6.3","prompt":"What submittal forms does ACR 2025 recommend including in post-project documentation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-1","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Cleaning is the removal of non-adhered substances.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Cleaning?","trap":"2021 said removal of visible particulate and contaminants. 2025 says removal of non-adhered substances.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-11","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition defined Cleaning as removal of visible particulate and contaminants.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How did the 2021 edition define Cleaning?","trap":"That is the old wording. Pick \"removal of non-adhered substances\" for the 2025 exam.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-2","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Mechanical Cleaning removes contaminants and debris not intended to be present on internal surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Mechanical Cleaning remove, per the ACR 2025 definition?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-3","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"A Contaminant is any substance not intended to be present within the HVAC system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Contaminant?","trap":"Contaminant is ANY unwanted substance. Debris is limited to non-adhered substances.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-4","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Debris is non-adhered substances not intended to be present within the HVAC system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Debris?","trap":"Debris adds \"non-adhered\" to the Contaminant wording. Particulate adds \"removable by an agitation device\".","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-5","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Particulate is any non-adhered substance that an agitation device can remove.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Particulate?","trap":"The agitation-device clause is unique to Particulate. Contaminant and Debris say nothing about how it is removed.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-10","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"NOTE","text":"The agitation-device clause is what separates Particulate from Contaminant and Debris.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which clause separates the definition of Particulate from Contaminant and Debris?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-6","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"A Non-Adhered Substance is material not intended or designed to be present in the system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Non-Adhered Substance?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-6a","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Section 4 cleaning and restoration procedures can remove a non-adhered substance.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What can remove a non-adhered substance, per its ACR 2025 definition?","trap":"Non-adhered: Section 4 procedures can remove it. Adhered: Section 4 procedures do not.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-7","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"An Adhered Substance is material that Section 4 procedures do not remove.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Adhered Substance?","trap":"Visibly clean does not require removing adhered substances. It requires freedom from non-adhered substances.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-8","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Examples of adhered substances are paint, mastic, sealant, and stains.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What are the ACR 2025 examples of adhered substances?","trap":"Stains count as adhered. A stained surface can still be visibly clean.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0112","cvi-0345"]},{"id":"acr:D-9","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Visibly Clean means the interior surfaces are free of non-adhered substances.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does the Definitions section define Visibly Clean?","trap":"Not \"free of all contaminants\". Only non-adhered substances must be gone.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0112","cvi-0345"]},{"id":"acr:D-12","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"A Non-Porous Component contacts the air stream and resists penetration by water or air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Non-Porous Component?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-13","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Non-porous examples are sheet metal, aluminum foil, and polymetric flexible duct film.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What are the ACR 2025 examples of non-porous components?","trap":"Flex duct film is non-porous, so Method 3 can apply to it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-14","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"A Porous Component contacts the air stream and allows penetration by water or air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Porous Component?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-15","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Porous examples are fibrous glass duct liner and fibrous glass duct board.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which fibrous glass products does ACR 2025 list as porous components?","trap":"Porous means Method 3 never applies. A disputed result on liner or duct board ends at Method 2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-16","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Sound attenuated duct, wood, and concrete are also porous components.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Besides fibrous glass liner and duct board, what else does ACR 2025 list as porous?","trap":"Wood and concrete are porous even though they feel hard.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-18","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Condition 1 is a normal fungal ecology for a similar indoor environment.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How is Condition 1 defined?","trap":"Condition 1 is not sterile. It can include settled spores and traces of actual growth.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-19","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Condition 1 can have settled spores, fungal fragments, or traces of actual growth.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What can a Condition 1 environment still contain?","trap":"Settled spores alone do not make Condition 2. Condition 2 spores come from a Condition 3 area.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-20","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Condition 2 is contamination primarily by settled spores or fungal fragments.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How is Condition 2 defined?","trap":"Condition 2 is set by where the settled spores came from: directly or indirectly from a Condition 3 area.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0272"]},{"id":"acr:D-20a","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"A Condition 2 environment may also have traces of actual growth.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Can a Condition 2 environment have traces of actual growth?","trap":"Traces of actual growth can appear in Condition 1 and Condition 2. Traces alone do not mean Condition 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-21","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"In a Condition 2 environment, the settled spores or fragments come directly or indirectly from a Condition 3 area.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Where do the settled spores or fragments of a Condition 2 environment come from?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0272"]},{"id":"acr:D-22","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Condition 3 is the presence of actual microbial growth, spores, and fungal fragments.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How is Condition 3 defined?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-23","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Actual growth includes active or dormant growth, and visible or hidden growth.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What forms of growth count as actual growth for Condition 3?","trap":"Dormant and hidden growth still count. Condition 3 does not need visible, active growth.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-24","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S520 is the source of all three condition definitions.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which standard is the source of the Condition 1, 2 and 3 definitions?","trap":"S520 is mold remediation. S500 is water damage. S590 is HVAC assessment after damage.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-25","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Microbial Contamination is past or current active indoor microbial growth.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Microbial Contamination?","trap":"2025 says \"microbial\" throughout where 2021 said \"mold\". Past growth counts, not only current growth.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-26","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Microbial Contamination is indoor growth whose identity, location, and amplification do not reflect a normal microbial ecology.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What makes indoor microbial growth count as Microbial Contamination rather than normal ecology?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-27","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"HEPA means 99.97% filtration efficiency at 0.3 micron-sized particles.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What filtration efficiency and particle size define HEPA in ACR 2025?","trap":"HEPA is rated at 0.3 micron. The Method 3 filter pore sizes are 0.8 or 0.45 micrometer.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"HEPA means 99.97% filtration efficiency at __ particles.","a":"0.3 micron","d":["0.45 micron","0.7 micron","0.8 micron"]},"qs":["cvi-0307"]},{"id":"acr:D-28","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"The US Department of Energy is the source of the HEPA definition.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which agency is the source of the HEPA definition in ACR 2025?","trap":"The US Department of Energy, not EPA, OSHA or NIOSH.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-29","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Air Filtration Device is a self-contained blower assembly with particulate filtration.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Air Filtration Device (AFD)?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-30","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Air Scrubber is an AFD with HEPA filtration that recirculates air in a defined space.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Air Scrubber?","trap":"Scrubber recirculates air in the space. Negative air machine controls pressure differentials.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-31","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Negative Air Machine is a HEPA-filtered AFD that controls workspace pressure differentials.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Negative Air Machine?","trap":"Negative air machine controls pressure differentials. Scrubber recirculates air in the space.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-32","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Negative Air Machine can have ducting outside the building envelope, or no ducting.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Must a Negative Air Machine be ducted outside the building envelope?","trap":"Ducting outdoors is optional in the definition. A negative air machine can have no ducting.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-33","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Collection Device is a HEPA machine that discharges air to the indoor environment.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Where may a HEPA collection device discharge its air?","trap":"Only a HEPA collection device may discharge indoors. A non-HEPA one discharges outside the building envelope.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-34","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Collection Device is also a fan-driven non-HEPA machine that discharges outside the envelope.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Where does a fan-driven non-HEPA collection device discharge its air?","trap":"No HEPA, exhaust outdoors. Only a HEPA collection device may discharge to the indoor environment.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-35","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Contact Vacuum is a collection device, usually portable, with a nylon brush nozzle.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Contact Vacuum?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-36","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Agitation Device dislodges or moves non-adhered substances in the system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Agitation Device?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-37","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Agitation device examples are brushes, whips, compressed air, and contact vacuum attachments.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What are the ACR 2025 examples of agitation devices?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-38","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Source Removal is mechanical cleaning that eliminates contaminants at their point of origin.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Source Removal?","trap":"New term in 2025, along with Damp Wiping, Open Air Plenum and Duct Wrap.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-39","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Damp Wiping is a process for wiping component surfaces on restoration projects.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Damp Wiping?","trap":"New term in 2025, along with Source Removal, Open Air Plenum and Duct Wrap.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-40","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Air Duct is a passageway for distribution and extraction of air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Air Duct?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-41","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"The Air Duct definition excludes plenums that do not follow SMACNA Standards.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which plenums does the Air Duct definition exclude?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-42","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct Board is rigid insulation board with a finishing material on one or both sides.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Duct Board?","trap":"Duct board is porous, so Method 3 never applies to it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-43","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct Liner is insulation, usually fibrous glass, applied inside a metal duct.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Duct Liner?","trap":"Liner is inside the duct and porous. Wrap and covering are on the outside.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-44","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct Wrap is insulation for the exterior of ducts, with sealed seams and joints.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Duct Wrap?","trap":"Duct Wrap is a new 2025 term. Wrap is exterior insulation; liner is interior.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-45","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Air Duct Covering is insulation and banding on the external surface of a duct.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Air Duct Covering?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-46","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Service Panel closes a service opening in an HVAC system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does a Service Panel do, per ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-47","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Duct Access Door is a fabricated metal hatch for permanent installation.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Duct Access Door?","trap":"Prefabricated door gauge follows the system pressure class. A field-made panel follows the duct gauge.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-48","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Flush Mount door extends into the duct and sits flush with the outside duct wall.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does a Flush Mount access door sit in the duct?","trap":"Flush mount extends into the duct. Surface mount extends out from the duct wall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-49","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Surface Mount door extends out from the surface of the outside duct wall.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does a Surface Mount access door sit on the duct?","trap":"Surface mount extends out from the duct wall. Flush mount extends into the duct.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-50","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Hinged door has a door and a doorframe with a hinge between them.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What defines a Hinged access door?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-51","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Sandwich door has two parts, fastened on both sides of the duct wall.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What defines a Sandwich access door?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-52","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Spin Door is round. The frame spins into a round opening.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What defines a Spin Door?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-53","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Closure is an access door or panel that creates a permanent seal.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Closure?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-54","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Permanent means the life of the system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How long is \"permanent\" in ACR 2025?","trap":"Permanent is tied to the life of the system, not to a set number of years.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-55","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Mastic caulks, seals, or cements gaps and cracks in duct connections and joints.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Mastic do, per ACR 2025?","trap":"Mastic is an adhered substance. Closure mastics must meet UL 181A/B, not UL 181.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-56","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Mechanically Fasten means to affix objects with screws, clamps, locks, or straps.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What four fastener types does the Mechanically Fasten definition allow?","trap":"Service panels are narrower: screws or rivets only, at minimum every 4 inches on center (4.2.5.2).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-57","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"NOTE","text":"The definition allows four fastener types. Clause 4.2.5.2 narrows panels to screws or rivets.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions; §4.2.5.2","prompt":"How does clause 4.2.5.2 narrow the Mechanically Fasten definition for service panels?","trap":"Rivets are not in the definition list yet are allowed for panels. Clamps, locks and straps are not.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-70","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Air-Handling Unit moves air. It can also clean and condition the air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does an Air-Handling Unit do, per ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-71","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Central-Station AHU is a factory-made encased assembly with fans and other equipment.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Central-Station air-handling unit?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-72","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Central-Station AHU does not include a heating source or a cooling source.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Does a Central-Station AHU include a heating source or a cooling source?","trap":"A central-station AHU has no heating or cooling source of its own. A cooling-heating unit provides both.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-73","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Cooling-Heating Unit gives both cooling and heating.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does a Cooling-Heating Unit provide?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-74","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Cooling-Heating examples are mini-splits, PTACs, wall units, window units, and fan coil units.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What are the ACR 2025 examples of Cooling-Heating Units?","trap":"Split systems also count as cooling-heating units.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-74a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Split systems are also Cooling-Heating Units.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which unit type do split systems fall under in ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-75","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Cooling Unit gives cooling. A Heating Unit gives heating.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Cooling Unit and a Heating Unit?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-76","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Make-Up Air Unit supplies tempered fresh air that replaces exhausted air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does a Make-Up Air Unit supply?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-77","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"In ACR's unit categories, a Ventilating Unit provides ventilation, while cooling, heating, and cooling-heating units condition the air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does a Ventilating Unit provide?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-78","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Coils temper or dehumidify the air that the system handles.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What do coils do, per ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-79","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Coil media are water, ethylene glycol solution, brine, volatile refrigerant, or steam.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What media can flow through coils, per ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-80","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Total cooling is sensible cooling plus latent cooling.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What makes up total cooling?","trap":"Sensible cooling changes temperature; latent cooling removes moisture. Total cooling is the sum.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-58","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Airside Surface is the internal surface that air impacts during travel through the system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Airside Surface?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-59","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Airside Surface excludes open air plenums and interstitial cavities.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does the Airside Surface definition exclude?","trap":"Changed in 2025. Open air plenums and interstitial cavities are not airside surfaces.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-60","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An Open Air Plenum is a non-ducted air pathway in building cavities, voids, and spaces.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Open Air Plenum?","trap":"A new 2025 term. Open air plenums are not part of the HVAC system and are not airside surfaces.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-61","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Open air plenums are outside the occupied zone of the building.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Where are open air plenums located relative to the occupied zone of the building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-62","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"The HVAC System runs from the point of air entry to the point of air discharge.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What are the boundaries of the HVAC System in ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-63","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"The HVAC System includes return grilles, return ducts, and the air-handling unit.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which return-side parts and central equipment does the HVAC System definition include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-64","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"The ACR HVAC System definition includes the mixing box, coil compartment, and condensate drain pans.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which AHU sections and pans does the HVAC System definition include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-65","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"The ACR HVAC System definition includes humidifiers, dehumidifiers, supply ducts, fans, fan housing, and fan blades.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which moisture-control devices, supply-side air paths and fan parts does the HVAC System definition list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-66","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"The ACR HVAC System definition includes air wash systems, spray eliminators, and turning vanes.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which water-spray air treatment parts and duct fittings that redirect airflow does the HVAC System definition list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-67","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"The ACR HVAC System definition includes filters, filter housings, reheat coils, and supply diffusers.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which filtration parts, secondary heating coils and supply-air outlets does the HVAC System definition list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-68","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"The ACR HVAC System definition includes dedicated exhaust components, ventilation components, and make-up air systems.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which parts that remove air from the building or bring outdoor air in does the HVAC System definition list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-69","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Non-ducted open air plenums of all types are not part of the HVAC system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Are non-ducted open air plenums part of the HVAC system in ACR 2025?","trap":"Changed in 2025. A non-ducted ceiling return plenum is not part of the HVAC system as ACR defines it.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-81","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An ASCS holds the NADCA designation after a pass on a written certification examination.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an ASCS?","trap":"ASCS oversight of a project is only recommended. Training and qualification of workers are mandatory.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-82","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An IEP is qualified by education, training, and experience to assess fungal ecology.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What qualifies an Indoor Environmental Professional (IEP), per ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-83","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An IEP creates a sampling strategy and interprets laboratory data.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What two sampling and lab tasks does an IEP perform?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-84","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An IEP determines Condition 1, 2, and 3 status and verifies the return to Condition 1.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Who determines Condition 1, 2 and 3 status and verifies the return to Condition 1?","trap":"The IEP, not Section 5 verification. Cleanliness verification does not determine microbial contamination.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0273","cvi-0321","cvi-0336"]},{"id":"acr:D-85","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An Inspection is a gathering of information for determinations and assessments.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Inspection?","trap":"Inspection gathers information. Assessment reviews and evaluates. A preliminary determination is the first conclusion.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0234"]},{"id":"acr:D-86","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An Assessment is a review and evaluation of the system or representative portions.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Assessment?","trap":"Assessment reviews and evaluates. Inspection only gathers the information.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0234"]},{"id":"acr:D-87","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"A Preliminary Determination is a conclusion from information at an initial inspection.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Preliminary Determination?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-88","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Standard of Care is the practice common to reasonably prudent members of the trade.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Standard of Care?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-89","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Restoration means to bring back to a former or original state.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Restoration?","trap":"Restoration does not include sealing duct air leaks or repairing malfunctioning mechanical devices.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-90","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Chain of Custody Documentation shows seizure, custody, control, transfer, analysis, and disposition.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Chain of Custody Documentation show?","trap":"Required under 5.6.2 only when outside laboratories or agencies do the work.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0356","cvi-0386"]},{"id":"acr:D-91","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Environmental Engineering Controls are modifications to the work environment.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Environmental Engineering Controls?","trap":"ACR is an engineering-controls document. It never specifies personal protective equipment.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-92","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Environmental Engineering Controls permit safe operations and prevent escape or transfer of contaminants.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What two purposes do Environmental Engineering Controls serve?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-93","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Containment Area is an engineered space that controls migration of contaminants.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Containment Area?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-94","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Appropriate Negative Pressure prevents debris entry to occupied space.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Appropriate Negative Pressure prevent from reaching occupied space?","trap":"No numeric value is given. Any answer in inches of water column for negative pressure is wrong.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-95","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Appropriate Negative Pressure also prevents debris from leaving the contained area.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Besides keeping debris out of occupied space, what else does Appropriate Negative Pressure prevent?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-96","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Standard gives no numeric value for negative pressure anywhere.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Does ACR 2025 set a numeric value for negative pressure?","trap":"The 80 in. WC figure is Method 2 vacuum lift, not a containment pressure.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-97","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Negative Duct Pressurization is a differential inside the duct against the indoor non-work area.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Negative Duct Pressurization?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-98","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Ambient Air Cleaning removes particulate from indoor air outside of the HVAC system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Ambient Air Cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-99","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Pressure Drop is a loss in pressure from friction, static, or heat.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Pressure Drop?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-100","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Frictional resistance to fluid flow usually causes a pressure drop.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What usually causes a pressure drop?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-101","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Friable means easily crumbled or pulverized.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Friable mean in ACR 2025?","trap":"Porous fire-damaged surfaces are checked for friability and odor after cleaning.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-102","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Abrasion is a surface loss of material from friction.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Abrasion?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-103","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Antimicrobial describes an agent that kills or inactivates microorganisms, or suppresses growth.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Antimicrobial describe in ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-104","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Surface Treatment repairs surface defects or modifies surface characteristics.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does a Surface Treatment do, per ACR 2025?","trap":"Surface treatments go on only after cleaning and a PASS on verification, and must meet UL 181, not 181A/B.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-105","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Wet Process Cleaning uses water or liquid chemicals as part of the process.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Wet Process Cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-106","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Bioaerosols are airborne particles of biological origin.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Bioaerosols?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-107","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"SDS means Safety Data Sheet.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does SDS stand for in ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-109","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Agitation is a process that uses an agitation device to dislodge non-adhered substances.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Agitation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-110","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Mechanical Agitation is the same as Agitation.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Mechanical Agitation in relation to Agitation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-111","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Visual Inspection is examination of cleanliness with the naked eye.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Visual Inspection?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-112","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Surface Comparison Testing determines the cleanliness of porous component surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which surfaces does the 2025 Definitions entry say Surface Comparison Testing evaluates?","trap":"The Definitions entry says porous only, but Section 5.3 says porous and non-porous. The 5.3 body text governs.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-113","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"NOTE","text":"The ACR 2025 definition of Surface Comparison Testing still says porous surfaces, while Section 5.3 applies it to porous and non-porous surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions; §5.3","prompt":"How does the Surface Comparison Testing definition conflict with Section 5.3?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-114","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Access is the ability to gain entry to the interior of the air duct or component.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Access?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-115","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Access Door is a fabricated metal barrier that opens or closes a service opening.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Access Door?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-116","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Panel is a fabricated metal section that forms the structural shell of equipment.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Panel?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-117","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"To Seal is to make secure against leakage by a fastener, a coating, or a filler.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does To Seal mean in ACR 2025?","trap":"Service panel sealing is a SHALL in 2025. It was only recommended in 2021.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-118","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Fibrous Glass Insulation is a synthetic material for thermal retention and sound attenuation.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Fibrous Glass Insulation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-119","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Standard defines SHALL, RECOMMEND, MAY, CAN, and Requirement. See G-19 through G-23.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which obligation words does ACR 2025 define?","trap":"MAY is permission. CAN is ability. That pair is the most-missed definition in the Standard.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-108","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 term \"Authorities Having Jurisdiction\" has no definition in 2025.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What happened to the 2021 definition of Authorities Having Jurisdiction in ACR 2025?","trap":"AHJ was deleted. 2025 refers to applicable local, regional, provincial or national codes instead.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-1","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S500 covers professional water damage restoration.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/IICRC S500 cover?","trap":"S500 water, S520 mold, S590 HVAC assessment after damage. Flood water is categorized per S500.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-2","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S520 covers professional mold remediation.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/IICRC S520 cover?","trap":"S520 projects inspect all components, not the 10% minimum. S520 also defines Conditions 1, 2 and 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-3","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S590 covers assessment of HVAC systems after water, fire, or mold damage.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/IICRC S590 cover?","trap":"New reference in 2025. S590 assesses the HVAC system; S500 and S520 restore and remediate.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-4","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 covers fire-related particulate on HVAC interior surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-7","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 181 applies to surface treatments.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does UL 181 apply to in ACR 2025?","trap":"UL 181 is for surface treatments. UL 181A/B is for closure tapes and mastics.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-8","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 181A/B applies to tapes and mastics for closure of service openings.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does UL 181A/B apply to in ACR 2025?","trap":"UL 181A/B is for closure tapes and mastics. UL 181 is for surface treatments.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-10","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"NIOSH Method 0500 covers total nuisance dust for the Method 3 analysis.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"Which referenced method covers the Method 3 lab analysis?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-12","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IKECA C10-2021 covers cleaning methodology for commercial kitchen exhaust systems.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/IKECA C10-2021 cover?","trap":"New reference in 2025, along with S590.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-13","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA and NAIMA govern installation of thermal-acoustic insulation in the air stream.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"Which organizations govern installation of thermal-acoustic insulation in the air stream?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:health-claim","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"The most defensible health-related claim is that cleaning removes contaminants from the system. Claims that it cures allergy or asthma symptoms, or eliminates airborne mold in the building, are unsupportable.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"What is the most defensible claim about duct cleaning and occupant health?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:epa-health-evidence","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA states that duct cleaning has never been shown to actually prevent health problems, so a health outcome is not something a contractor can promise.","src":"EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"What does EPA say about duct cleaning and preventing health problems?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:iaq-claim","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Improved indoor air quality is a core benefit of cleaning, but cleaning supports IAQ rather than guaranteeing it. Never promise a specific IAQ result, on any type of job.","src":"NADCA, Benefits of HVAC System Cleaning","prompt":"How should an ASCS describe the IAQ benefit of cleaning?","trap":"Residential and healthcare jobs are not exceptions. No job type allows an IAQ guarantee.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:allergy-response","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"When a customer wants cleaning to end allergy symptoms, document the conditions, define the scope and explain what source removal does. Do not promise a medical outcome; refer medical questions to a physician.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"How do you respond to a homeowner who wants a guarantee that cleaning will eliminate allergy symptoms?","trap":"Adding an antimicrobial or including the whole supply side never makes a health guarantee acceptable.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:no-stay-clean-guarantee","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Do not guarantee that a system will stay clean for years. Reloading depends on filtration, use and conditions you do not control, and a duct coating does not change that.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"How should you answer an owner who wants a five-year guarantee that the system will stay clean?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:expectations-scope","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Set expectations before the job: cleaning removes non-adhered contaminants. It does not repair mechanical faults, replace filter maintenance, or guarantee odor or IAQ numbers.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"Why must customer expectations be set before an HVAC cleaning project?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:proposal-language","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Proposal language that manages expectations states the findings, the components in scope, the exclusions, and how cleanliness will be verified.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"What should proposal language state to manage customer expectations?","trap":"Price, schedule and crew size do not manage expectations; promising resolved complaints overpromises.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:epa-routine-position","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA does not recommend cleaning air ducts routinely on a schedule. It recommends cleaning only as needed, when specific conditions exist.","src":"EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"What is EPA's position on routine duct cleaning?","trap":"EPA sets no annual interval and asks for no documented complaint; it ties cleaning to conditions such as mold, vermin or heavy debris.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:sbs-vs-bri","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Sick building syndrome: occupants report discomfort symptoms with no known cause, and most get relief soon after leaving. Building-related illness: a clinically defined illness with an identifiable cause, such as Legionnaires' disease, and recovery can take longer.","src":"EPA Indoor Air Facts No. 4, Sick Building Syndrome","prompt":"How does sick building syndrome differ from building-related illness?","trap":"Distractors swap the pair. SBS clears after leaving; BRI has a diagnosable cause.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:soot","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Soot is a byproduct of incomplete combustion. It deposits in plenums and ductwork near combustion equipment such as boilers and generators.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VII; industry practice","prompt":"Which contaminant is a byproduct of incomplete combustion found near boilers and generators?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:biological-vs-particulate","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Biological contaminants include bacteria, viruses, mold, and animal or insect byproducts. Drywall dust, fiberglass fragments and soot are non-biological particulate.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VII (contaminant list); industry practice","prompt":"Which contaminants are biological rather than particulate?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:respirable-particulate","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Respirable particulate is small enough to stay suspended in air and reach the deep lung, which is why HEPA filtration and containment matter during source removal.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"What are particles small enough to stay airborne and reach the deep lung called?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:voc-sources","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"VOCs in an HVAC system mostly off-gas from solvents, adhesives, paints, coatings and cleaning chemicals.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"Where do VOCs entering an HVAC system most commonly come from?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:infestation-contaminates","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Bird, rodent and insect infestation matters because droppings, carcasses and nesting material contaminate the airstream and create health hazards.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"Why is bird, rodent or insect infestation a reason to clean HVAC components?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:construction-particulate","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Construction and remodel particulate typically includes gypsum dust, sawdust and silica, which sets it apart from ordinary household dust.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"What materials make construction particulate different from ordinary dust?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:plenum-infiltration","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Open air plenums are not fully sealed, so humidity and unconditioned outside air can infiltrate, leading to condensation, odors and microbial growth.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §V","prompt":"Why can condensation form inside an open air plenum?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:osha-governs","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA sets and enforces worker safety regulations on U.S. HVAC cleaning job sites. EPA handles environmental rules, not worker safety.","src":"Occupational Safety and Health Act; 29 CFR 1910 and 1926","prompt":"Which agency's regulations govern worker safety on U.S. HVAC cleaning job sites?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:loto-sequence","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA energy control runs in a fixed order: know the energy, shut down, isolate, apply locks or tags, relieve stored energy, then verify isolation before work starts.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(d)","prompt":"What is the required order of steps when applying lockout/tagout?","trap":"Locking after work starts, or bleeding stored energy before isolating, breaks the sequence.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:loto-authorized","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"The authorized employee is the person who applies the lockout or tagout devices in order to service or maintain the equipment.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(b)","prompt":"Who is the authorized employee under lockout/tagout?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:loto-affected","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"An affected employee operates or uses the equipment being serviced, or works in the area, but does not apply the lockout. Affected employees are notified before locks go on and after they come off.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(b), (c)(9)","prompt":"Who is an affected employee under lockout/tagout?","trap":"The worker who applies the device and verifies isolation is authorized, not affected.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:loto-verify","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"After locking and tagging, verify isolation by trying a normal start to confirm the equipment will not run, then return the operating control to neutral or off.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(d)(6); Appendix A","prompt":"How do you verify isolation after locking and tagging an AHU disconnect?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:loto-removal","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"Each lockout or tagout device is removed only by the employee who applied it. The only exception is a documented employer procedure used when that employee is not available.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(e)(3)","prompt":"Who may remove a personal lockout device?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:osha-1910-147","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"29 CFR 1910.147, the control of hazardous energy standard, is OSHA's lockout/tagout rule.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147","prompt":"Which OSHA standard is the lockout/tagout rule?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's lockout/tagout standard is 29 CFR 1910.__.","a":"147","d":["146","134","1200"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:gfci-tools","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"On construction sites, workers using portable electric tools must be protected by GFCIs or by an assured equipment grounding conductor program.","src":"29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)","prompt":"What must protect workers using portable electric tools on a job site?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:double-insulated","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"A listed tool protected by double insulation, and marked as such, need not be grounded, so it may lack a grounding prong. Every other safe-use rule still applies.","src":"29 CFR 1926.404(f)(7)(iv)(C)(6)","prompt":"What may a tool marked double insulated legitimately do?","trap":"Double insulation does not approve a tool for wet locations, removing guards or skipping cord inspection.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:cord-handling","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"Never raise, lower or carry a tool by its cord, and unplug by the plug body, never by yanking the cord.","src":"29 CFR 1910.334(a)(1); OSHA 3080 Hand and Power Tools","prompt":"Which cord handling practices are prohibited with portable electric tools?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:damaged-cord","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"Inspect cords before use on each shift. A cord with damaged outer insulation is removed from service until properly repaired and tested; wrapping it with tape does not restore it.","src":"29 CFR 1910.334(a)(2)","prompt":"What must happen to an extension cord with cracked insulation exposing conductors?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:extension-cord-type","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"Extension cords used with portable electric tools on construction sites must be three-wire type and designed for hard or extra-hard usage.","src":"29 CFR 1926.405(a)(2)(ii)(J)","prompt":"What type of extension cord is required for portable tools on a job site?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:confined-space-def","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"A confined space is large enough to bodily enter and work in, has limited or restricted entry or exit, and is not designed for continuous occupancy. A walk-in AHU can meet all three.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"What three conditions make a space a confined space under OSHA?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:confined-not-permit","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"Meeting the confined space definition does not by itself require a permit. The space must be evaluated, and whether it is permit-required depends on the hazards found.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b), (c)(1)","prompt":"Is an enterable AHU automatically a permit-required confined space?","trap":"Locking out the unit does not exempt the space from evaluation.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:permit-space-triggers","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"A confined space is permit-required if it has any one of four hazards: a hazardous atmosphere, material that can engulf, an entrapping internal configuration, or any other recognized serious hazard.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"Which four characteristics make a confined space permit-required?","trap":"Size, entry frequency and crew size are never triggers.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:entrapment-config","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"Inwardly converging walls, or a floor that slopes down and tapers to a smaller cross-section, make a space permit-required because they can trap or asphyxiate an entrant.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"Why do inwardly converging walls or a tapering floor make a space permit-required?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:o2-deficient","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA defines an oxygen deficient atmosphere as one containing less than 19.5 percent oxygen by volume.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"Below what oxygen level is an atmosphere oxygen deficient?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's cutoff for an oxygen deficient atmosphere is __ oxygen by volume.","a":"19.5 percent","d":["23.5 percent","20.9 percent","18 percent"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:o2-enriched","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"An atmosphere with more than 23.5 percent oxygen by volume is oxygen enriched, which raises the fire and combustion hazard.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"Above what oxygen level is an atmosphere oxygen enriched?","trap":"","num":{"q":"An atmosphere with more than __ oxygen by volume is oxygen enriched.","a":"23.5 percent","d":["19.5 percent","20.9 percent","22 percent"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:flammable-10-lfl","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"A flammable gas, vapor or mist above 10 percent of its lower flammable limit (LFL, also called LEL) makes a confined space atmosphere hazardous.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"What concentration of flammable gas makes a confined space atmosphere hazardous?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's hazardous atmosphere cutoff for flammable gas, vapor or mist is __ of its lower flammable limit.","a":"10 percent","d":["1 percent","50 percent","100 percent"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:test-order","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"Test a permit space atmosphere in this order: oxygen first, then combustible gases and vapors, then toxic gases and vapors. Combustible gas meters need oxygen to read correctly.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(d)(5)(iii)","prompt":"In what order do you test the atmosphere before permit space entry?","trap":"The order is written into the regulation; 'any order if all three are tested' is wrong.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:attendant-duty","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"The attendant stays outside the permit space for the whole entry, keeps count of entrants, monitors conditions inside and out, and orders evacuation when conditions turn unsafe.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(i)","prompt":"What is the attendant's primary duty during a permit-required entry?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:attendant-rescue","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"If an entrant collapses, the attendant summons rescue services and attempts non-entry rescue with the retrieval system. Untrained would-be rescuers who go in often become added fatalities.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(i)(4), (i)(7), (i)(9), (k)(3)","prompt":"What should the attendant do first when an entrant collapses inside a permit space?","trap":"An attendant may enter only if trained and equipped for rescue and relieved by another attendant.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:permit-flow","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"Before entry, the permit records completed preparations and test results, the entry supervisor signs it, and it is posted at the portal. The supervisor cancels it when the work ends; cancelled permits are kept at least 1 year.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(e), (f)","prompt":"How is a confined space entry permit authorized, displayed, closed out and retained?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's minimum retention period for cancelled confined space entry permits is __.","a":"1 year","d":["30 days","3 years","6 months"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:osha-1910-146","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"29 CFR 1910.146 is OSHA's permit-required confined spaces standard for general industry. Confined spaces in construction fall under 29 CFR 1926 Subpart AA.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(a); 29 CFR 1926 Subpart AA","prompt":"Which OSHA standard covers permit-required confined spaces?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's general industry permit-required confined spaces standard is 29 CFR 1910.__.","a":"146","d":["147","134","1200"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:engineering-first","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA's first line against airborne contaminants is engineering controls such as enclosure and ventilation. Respirators are required when those controls are not feasible or while they are being put in place.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(a)(1)","prompt":"When does OSHA require respirators instead of relying on engineering controls alone?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:resp-hazard-selection","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"Respirator selection is based on the respiratory hazard present and on workplace and user factors, found through an assessment. Contract terms, company minimums and exposure duration are inputs, not the basis.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(d)(1)(i)","prompt":"What drives respirator selection in a contaminated system?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:resp-program","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"Required respirator use needs a written program with hazard-based selection, medical evaluation, training and, for tight-fitting facepieces, fit testing.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(c), (d), (e), (f), (k)","prompt":"What must be in place before an employee is required to wear a tight-fitting respirator?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:resp-order","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"For a required tight-fitting respirator, medical evaluation comes first, then fit testing, repeated at least annually, and a user seal check every time the respirator is put on.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(e)(1), (f)(2), (g)(1)(iii)","prompt":"In what order do medical evaluation, fit testing and user seal checks happen, and how often?","trap":"Fit testing is at least annual; the user seal check is every donning. Do not swap them.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:facial-hair","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"A tight-fitting respirator may not be worn when facial hair comes between the facepiece sealing surface and the face, or interferes with valve function.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(g)(1)(i)(A)","prompt":"Which condition makes wearing a required tight-fitting respirator unacceptable?","trap":"Corrective glasses are allowed if they do not disturb the facepiece seal.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:apf-half-mask","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"A half-mask air-purifying respirator, including an N95 filtering facepiece, has an OSHA assigned protection factor of 10.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(d)(3)(i)(A), Table 1","prompt":"What is OSHA's assigned protection factor for a half-mask or filtering facepiece respirator?","trap":"A full facepiece air-purifying respirator is 50; a quarter mask is 5.","num":{"q":"A half-mask air-purifying respirator has an assigned protection factor of __.","a":"10","d":["5","25","50"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:sds-content","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"A Safety Data Sheet gives a chemical's hazards, safe handling and storage, exposure controls and PPE, and first-aid measures in 16 standard sections. Employers keep one readily accessible for each hazardous chemical used.","src":"29 CFR 1910.1200(g)","prompt":"What does a Safety Data Sheet give workers, and how must it be kept?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:osha-1910-134","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"29 CFR 1910.134 is OSHA's respiratory protection standard.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134","prompt":"Which OSHA standard covers respiratory protection?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's respiratory protection standard is 29 CFR 1910.__.","a":"134","d":["146","147","1200"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:osha-1910-1200","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"29 CFR 1910.1200 is OSHA's hazard communication standard, which requires container labels, Safety Data Sheets and employee training on hazardous chemicals.","src":"29 CFR 1910.1200(e), (f), (g), (h)","prompt":"Which OSHA standard is hazard communication, and what does it require?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's hazard communication standard is 29 CFR 1910.__.","a":"1200","d":["134","146","147"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:risk-starts-hazards","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Risk management on an HVAC project begins by identifying job-site hazards, then applying controls such as engineering controls, PPE and procedures throughout the work.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA Safety Manual)","prompt":"What does risk management on an HVAC project begin with?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:exposure-effects","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Microbial and particulate contaminants disturbed during cleaning can harm both workers and occupants, even when they are not visible, so exposure must be controlled.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA Safety Manual)","prompt":"Why must worker and occupant exposure be controlled during HVAC cleaning?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:suspect-acm-action","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"When you find suspect asbestos-containing material, treat it as asbestos, stop any work that would disturb it, and notify the owner. Do not bag, wet, encapsulate or clean it yourself.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101; industry practice","prompt":"What do you do when suspect asbestos-containing insulation is found on a job?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:acm-licensed-only","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Sampling, assessing or abating suspect asbestos is work for qualified, licensed asbestos professionals, not for the duct cleaning crew.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101; state asbestos licensing","prompt":"Who samples or abates suspect asbestos-containing material?","trap":"Collecting a bulk sample yourself is the classic wrong answer.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:osha-asbestos-lead-map","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"REG","text":"In construction work, OSHA's asbestos standard is 29 CFR 1926.1101 and its lead standard is 29 CFR 1926.62. Paid renovation in pre-1978 homes also falls under EPA's RRP rule, 40 CFR 745.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101; 29 CFR 1926.62; 40 CFR 745 Subpart E","prompt":"Which federal rules cover asbestos and lead exposure during construction and renovation work?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's construction asbestos standard is 29 CFR 1926.__.","a":"1101","d":["62","501","1200"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:plenum-hazmat","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"In older buildings, open air plenums may contain asbestos and other hazardous materials, so PPE and job site safety are evaluated before anyone enters.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §V, §VII","prompt":"Which hazardous materials does NADCA flag as possible in older-building open air plenums?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:lead-presume","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"REG","text":"In housing and child-occupied facilities built before 1978, treat painted surfaces as lead-based paint unless a certified inspector, risk assessor or renovator has tested them free of lead-based paint.","src":"40 CFR 745.82(a); 745.103","prompt":"How do you treat painted surfaces in pre-1978 housing before cutting access holes?","trap":"","num":{"q":"EPA's target housing cutoff year is __ ; housing built before that year is covered.","a":"1978","d":["1980","1989","1970"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:rrp-scope","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"REG","text":"EPA's Renovation, Repair and Painting rule covers paid work that disturbs paint in pre-1978 housing and child-occupied facilities. Cutting holes in painted surfaces to gain access counts as renovation.","src":"40 CFR 745.82, 745.83","prompt":"Which rule applies when HVAC access work disturbs paint in a pre-1978 home?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:osha-mold-guide","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"OSHA publishes 'A Brief Guide to Mold in the Workplace', the OSHA reference on the ASCS reading list.","src":"OSHA SHIB 03-10-10; ASCS Candidate Guide","prompt":"Which OSHA publication on the ASCS reference list addresses mold?","trap":"EPA, not OSHA, publishes Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:epa-mold-guide","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA publishes 'Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings' (EPA 402-K-01-001), which appears in ACR's referenced documents.","src":"EPA 402-K-01-001","prompt":"Which agency publishes Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:plenum-rmp","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Technicians cleaning an open air plenum shall follow risk-reduction protocols, and NADCA recommends a Risk Management Plan or Job Hazard Analysis, because plenums hold essential electrical, plumbing, gas, fire protection, sewer and telecom systems.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VI","prompt":"What does NADCA recommend developing before working in an open air plenum, and why?","trap":"The risk-reduction protocols are 'shall'; the RMP or JHA itself is only recommended.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:rmp-contents","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"A plenum Risk Management Plan communicates each risk item near essential systems, the likelihood and severity of a risk event, risk-reduction actions, emergency-response actions, and an emergency contact.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VI","prompt":"What should an open air plenum Risk Management Plan communicate?","trap":"Payment terms and change-order pricing are not part of it.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:plenum-training","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Extra training that may be required before entering an open air plenum includes confined space, air monitoring, rig to rescue or rope work, ladder safety, fall protection and combustible dust.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VII","prompt":"What additional training may be required before entering an open air plenum?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:nfpa-90a-life-safety","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA 90A, the standard for installing air-conditioning and ventilating systems, drives fire damper placement, smoke detection, and flame and smoke limits for materials in air-handling spaces.","src":"NFPA 90A","prompt":"Which standard governs fire dampers and smoke detection in air distribution systems?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:nfpa-90b","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA 90B is the standard for installing warm air heating and air-conditioning systems.","src":"NFPA 90B","prompt":"What does NFPA 90B cover?","trap":"90A is air-conditioning and ventilating systems; 90B adds warm air heating.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:nfpa-90a-90b-split","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA 90B applies to one- and two-family dwellings and to spaces of 25,000 cubic feet or less. NFPA 90A applies to larger spaces, typically commercial and institutional buildings.","src":"NFPA 90A §1.3; NFPA 90B §1.3","prompt":"How is the scope split between NFPA 90A and NFPA 90B?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:surface-burning-tests","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"ASTM E84, UL 723 and the former NFPA 255 are the same surface burning characteristics test, giving a material's flame spread and smoke developed ratings.","src":"ASTM E84; UL 723; NFPA 255 (withdrawn)","prompt":"What do ASTM E84, UL 723 and NFPA 255 have in common?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0127","cvi-0131"]},{"id":"ascs:ul-181","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"UL 181 is the standard for factory-made air ducts and air connectors.","src":"UL 181","prompt":"What is UL 181 the standard for?","trap":"Closure systems are UL 181A and 181B, not UL 181 itself.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:ul-181a-181b","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"UL 181A covers closure systems such as tapes and mastics for rigid air ducts and connectors. UL 181B covers closure systems for flexible air ducts and connectors.","src":"UL 181A; UL 181B","prompt":"What is the difference between UL 181A and UL 181B?","trap":"A is rigid, B is flexible. The exam swaps them.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:smacna-006","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"ANSI/SMACNA 006 is the HVAC Duct Construction Standards, Metal and Flexible. Duct cleanliness for new work is covered by a separate SMACNA guideline.","src":"ANSI/SMACNA 006","prompt":"What is ANSI/SMACNA 006?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:smacna-cleanliness-levels","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"SMACNA's Duct Cleanliness for New Construction Guidelines set three levels, in order of increasing restriction: basic, intermediate and advanced. Basic is assumed when the specifications name no level.","src":"SMACNA, Duct Cleanliness for New Construction Guidelines","prompt":"What cleanliness levels do SMACNA's new construction guidelines set, and which applies by default?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:naima-ah116","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NAIMA AH116 is the Fibrous Glass Duct Construction Standards.","src":"NAIMA AH116","prompt":"What is NAIMA AH116?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:astm-c1071","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"ASTM C1071 is the standard specification for fibrous glass duct lining insulation.","src":"ASTM C1071","prompt":"What is ASTM C1071?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:nfpa-org","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA, the National Fire Protection Association, publishes fire and life-safety standards such as NFPA 90A, 90B and 96.","src":"NFPA","prompt":"Which organization publishes NFPA 90A and related fire and life-safety standards?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:smacna-org","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"SMACNA is the Sheet Metal and Air Conditioning Contractors' National Association, publisher of the HVAC Duct Construction Standards and other sheet metal duct guidance.","src":"SMACNA","prompt":"What does SMACNA stand for, and what does it publish?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:ashrae-62-1","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE Standard 62.1 covers ventilation and acceptable indoor air quality in commercial and other non-residential buildings.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 62.1","prompt":"What does ASHRAE Standard 62.1 cover?","trap":"62.1 is commercial, 62.2 is residential, 170 is healthcare, 180 is maintenance.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:ashrae-62-2","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE Standard 62.2 covers ventilation and acceptable indoor air quality in residential buildings.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 62.2","prompt":"What does ASHRAE Standard 62.2 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:ashrae-170","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ASHE Standard 170 covers ventilation of health care facilities.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ASHE Standard 170","prompt":"What does ASHRAE/ASHE Standard 170 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:naima-ah122","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"NAIMA, the North American Insulation Manufacturers Association, publishes AH122, the recommended practice for cleaning fibrous glass insulated air duct systems.","src":"NAIMA AH122","prompt":"Which organization and document cover cleaning fibrous glass insulated air duct systems?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:naima-ah110","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"NAIMA AH110 addresses fiber glass HVAC insulation and indoor environmental quality.","src":"NAIMA AH110","prompt":"What does NAIMA AH110 address?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:nadca-std-05","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA Standard 05 sets the requirements for installing service openings in HVAC systems.","src":"NADCA Standard 05","prompt":"What does NADCA Standard 05 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:nadca-std-03","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA Standard 03 sets the requirements for testing vacuum collection equipment.","src":"NADCA Standard 03","prompt":"What does NADCA Standard 03 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:nadca-position-paper","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"ACR 2025's reference list names two NADCA position papers: Using Chemical Products in HVAC Systems, and Inspection and Cleaning of Open Air Plenums in HVAC Systems. NADCA Standard 05, NAIMA AH122 and IESO/RIA 6001 are not position papers.","src":"ACR 2025, Reference Documents and Resources","prompt":"Which two NADCA documents in ACR 2025's reference list are position papers?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:nmam-5th","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"The NIOSH publication ACR references is the NIOSH Manual of Analytical Methods, Fifth Edition, which contains Method 0500 used for Method 3 analysis.","src":"NIOSH Manual of Analytical Methods, 5th ed.; ACR Referenced Documents","prompt":"Which NIOSH publication does ACR reference for Method 3 analysis?","trap":"Industrial Ventilation is ACGIH's manual; the Pocket Guide to Chemical Hazards is a different NIOSH book.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:nyc-mold-guidelines","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"The city health department mold guidance in ACR's references is New York City's Guidelines on Assessment and Remediation of Fungi in Indoor Environments.","src":"NYC DOHMH; ACR Referenced Documents","prompt":"Which city's health department mold guidelines are in ACR's references?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:prelim-determination-s520","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"DEF","text":"ACR's definition of preliminary determination comes from ANSI/IICRC S520: a conclusion from an initial inspection about moisture intrusion, actual or potential growth, and the need for other specialized experts.","src":"ACR Definitions (citing ANSI/IICRC S520)","prompt":"Which document is the source of the term preliminary determination, and what does it mean?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:acca-org","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"ACCA is the Air Conditioning Contractors of America, which co-sponsors ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180 with ASHRAE.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180","prompt":"What is ACCA, and which standard does it co-sponsor?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:iep-acronym","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"IEP stands for Indoor Environmental Professional, the qualified party who assesses contamination.","src":"ASCS Candidate Guide acronym list","prompt":"What does IEP stand for?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:mua-acronym","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"MUA stands for make up air, the outdoor air brought in to replace air exhausted from a space.","src":"ASCS Candidate Guide acronym list","prompt":"What does MUA stand for?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:acr-consensus","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"ACR is a consensus industry standard, not a regulation. It becomes binding when a contract specifies it or an authority adopts it.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"How is ACR best described, and when does it become binding?","trap":"ACR is not an OSHA code or an EPA regulation.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:code-over-standard","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"An industry standard never overrides an adopted code. When ACR and the local mechanical code conflict, the code governs.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"What is the relationship between ACR and the local mechanical code when they conflict?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:spec-conflict","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"When a project specification and ACR conflict on a method, resolve it with the specifier before proceeding. Silently following either one leaves you exposed.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"What should you do when a project specification and ACR conflict on a cleaning method?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:epa-role","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"REG","text":"EPA's role in duct cleaning is to register antimicrobial products under FIFRA and publish guidance. It does not certify or license duct cleaners, set cleaning intervals, or enforce ACR.","src":"FIFRA; EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"What is EPA's role relative to duct cleaning?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:hepa-type","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"HEPA is defined by performance, not by name. Filters sold as 'HEPA-type' or 'HEPA-like' do not meet the HEPA definition and are not acceptable where ACR requires HEPA.","src":"DOE HEPA definition; industry practice","prompt":"Is a filter marketed as HEPA-type acceptable where ACR requires HEPA?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-mechanical-plans-walkthrough","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Mechanical drawings are the plan set that shows AHU locations, duct routing, terminal units and zoning, which is what an inspection walk-through needs. Structural, electrical and site plans do not show these.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"Which drawing set best locates AHUs, duct routing and terminal units before an inspection?","trap":"ACR 1.3 requires using both floor and mechanical plans if they exist; this card is about which set shows the equipment.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-black-fallout-liner","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Black particulate settling from supply diffusers classically points to deteriorating internal fibrous glass liner or insulation shedding into the airstream, so the liner's condition is the first thing to assess.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"A client reports black particulate falling from the diffusers onto desks. What should the inspection check first?","trap":"External duct wrap and jacketing sit outside the airstream and cannot shed into the space.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-filter-bypass","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Filter bypass is gaps around a filter rack or frame that let unfiltered air go around the media, carrying particulate to the coil and ductwork no matter what MERV the filter has.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"What is filter bypass, and why does a higher MERV filter not fix it?","trap":"Not filter loading (dirt held in the media) or face velocity (air speed at the filter face).","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-multizone-damper-leak","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"On a multizone unit, leaking zone dampers blend hot deck and cold deck air unintentionally, causing simultaneous heating and cooling, wasted energy and temperature complaints across the zones.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"During inspection of a multizone unit, what do leaking zone dampers most directly cause?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-drain-pan-root-cause","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Standing water and slimy biofilm in a drain pan usually trace to a clogged drain line, a missing or wrong P-trap, or poor pan slope. Clean the pan and verify drainage works, or it comes back.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"A drain pan holds standing water and biofilm. What is the likely root cause, and what must the scope include?","trap":"An antimicrobial tablet, a new pan, or cleaning alone leaves the drainage fault in place.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-liner-growth-remediation","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Visible microbial growth on porous duct liner makes the job remediation, with its own scope, plus containment and an IEP where the situation warrants. A modest area such as 40 sq ft does not make it routine cleaning.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"Visible growth covers about 40 sq ft of porous duct liner. How is the job treated?","trap":"Under 100 sq ft is not a routine cleaning cutoff, and antimicrobial plus cleaning does not fix growth on liner.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-suspect-acm-scope","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Suspect asbestos-containing material found during an assessment stops the work and changes the scope, bringing in other trades. Heavy dust, a loaded blower wheel or an undersized filter rack are ordinary findings.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"Which assessment finding most clearly changes the scope of work?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-scope-by-component","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"Scope follows each component's documented condition. A heavily fouled AHU with visually clean supply ducts is scoped and reported component by component, so the customer can make an informed decision.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"The AHU is heavily fouled but the supply ducts look clean. How is the scope set?","trap":"Not an automatic single whole-system scope, not deferral, and not replacing the clean ducts.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-limited-scope-documented","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"A customer may limit the scope, such as supply side only. Do the work, document the limited scope and its implications, and never present it as a whole-system cleaning.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"A customer wants the supply side cleaned and declines the return. What should you do?","trap":"Refusing the job is wrong too; the violation is issuing a standard system cleaning certificate.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-representative-sampling","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"When a specification calls for gravimetric verification, take the samples from representative locations. Picking only the dirtiest or only the easiest spots invalidates the result.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"A specification calls for gravimetric verification. Where are the samples taken?","trap":"Every branch run or the air handler only are also wrong answers.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-closeout-documentation","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"Closeout documentation records the scope, methods, before and after conditions, findings and verification results, so the work and its proof can be reconstructed. Invoices, timesheets and satisfaction statements are not that record.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"What should the closeout documentation for a cleaning project record?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-return-indicators","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Transfer ducts, transfer grilles, fire dampers or louvered doors may indicate that a ceiling or other building cavity is working as an open air return plenum.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §IV","prompt":"Which features may indicate that a ceiling cavity is an open air return plenum?","trap":"Condensate traps, duct wrap and VAV boxes do not indicate a plenum return.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-ceiling-tiles","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"In a ceiling return plenum, removing ceiling tiles disrupts the designed airflow. Keep the ceiling and adjacent walls intact, especially around spaces designed to be exhausted, such as bathrooms, supply closets and chemical storage.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §IV","prompt":"Why must ceiling tile removal be managed carefully when inspecting a plenum-return building?","trap":"The reason is airflow, not a presumption that the tiles contain asbestos.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-unfiltered-air","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Air moving through an open air plenum is generally unfiltered, so dust and dirt drop out of the airstream and build up on everything in the plenum over time.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §V","prompt":"Why does particulate accumulate throughout an open air plenum?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-non-hvac-contents","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Unlike sealed ductwork, open air plenums often hold many items unrelated to HVAC, such as light fixtures, fire suppression, data cabling, wiring, plumbing, insulation and other construction materials.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §V","prompt":"Compared with sealed ductwork, what do open air plenums typically contain?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-iep-consult","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"When open air plenum contaminants cannot be readily identified and the scope is unclear, a consultation with an IEP may be necessary to identify the contaminants and develop the scope of work.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VII","prompt":"Who may need to be consulted when open air plenum contaminants and scope are unclear?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-fire-rated-closure","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-p-cleaning-practice","tag":"FACT","text":"An opening cut in a fire-rated duct or assembly must be closed so the assembly keeps its listed rating, for example with an access door listed to the same rating. A sheet metal patch or tape does not.","src":"Field practice; fire-rated assembly listings","prompt":"How is an access opening cut in a fire-rated duct or assembly closed?","trap":"The rating belongs to the duct assembly too, not only to the shaft.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-fuel-equipment-co","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"The key hazard of fuel-powered vacuums, generators and compressors staged for a job is carbon monoxide drawn into outside air intakes, openings or occupied areas, so place them and control CO exposure.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.2; industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What specific hazard does fuel-powered vacuum or compressor equipment staged for a job create?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-occupied-building-scope","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"When a building stays fully occupied during business hours, the most important scope items are engineering controls, work sequencing and often off-hours scheduling that keep contaminants away from occupants.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"A building stays fully occupied during business hours. What is the most important scope consideration?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-healthcare-containment-first","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"In an occupied healthcare space, containment is established before any system opening is made, because contaminant release begins when the first access is cut.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"When should containment be established for work in an occupied healthcare space?","trap":"Not after the first access is cut, and not only in areas rated for infection control.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-healthcare-barriers-purpose","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"On an occupied hospital project, critical barriers and negative pressure exist mainly to keep contaminants from migrating into sensitive areas. Occupancy and space sensitivity set the level of engineering control.","src":"Field practice","prompt":"On an occupied hospital corridor project, what is the primary reason for critical barriers and negative pressure?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-621-scope","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"DEF","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 covers ventilation in spaces for human occupancy except inside dwelling units with nontransient occupants, which ASHRAE 62.2 covers. An apartment building's corridors and lobby are 62.1 spaces.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §2.1","prompt":"Which ASHRAE standard sets ventilation for the corridors and lobby of an apartment building, and why?","trap":"Calling the whole residential building 62.2. Only the dwelling units themselves go to 62.2.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0133"]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-55","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"DEF","text":"ASHRAE 55 defines acceptable thermal conditions using six factors: metabolic rate, clothing insulation, air temperature, radiant temperature, air speed, and humidity. It does not address air quality; ventilation belongs to ASHRAE 62.1.","src":"ASHRAE 55, purpose and scope","prompt":"Which ASHRAE standard defines thermal comfort, what six factors does it use, and does it cover air quality?","trap":"Outdoor air ventilation rate is not one of the six factors.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0134","cvi-0206"]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-901","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"DEF","text":"ASHRAE/IES 90.1 is the energy standard for sites and buildings other than low-rise residential, setting minimum efficiency for the envelope, lighting and HVAC.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE/IES 90.1-2022, Energy Standard for Sites and Buildings Except Low-Rise Residential Buildings","prompt":"What does ASHRAE 90.1 cover, and which building type is outside its scope?","trap":"Mixing up the ASHRAE numbers: 90.1 is energy, 62.1 ventilation, 55 comfort, 52.2 filter testing.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-522","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"DEF","text":"The MERV rating comes from the ASHRAE 52.2 test, which measures removal efficiency by particle size. ISO 16890 rates filters in ePM classes instead, and ASHRAE 62.1 sets minimum MERV values without testing for them.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2","prompt":"Which test standard produces a filter's MERV number?","trap":"ASHRAE 62.1 names minimum MERV values but is not the test method.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0135"]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-170","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"DEF","text":"ASHRAE/ASHE 170 sets ventilation for health care facilities such as operating rooms and inpatient areas. ASHRAE 62.1 sends occupancies within 170's scope to 170's rates, which target asepsis and odor control.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §6.2.1; ASHRAE/ASHE 170","prompt":"Which standard's ventilation rates govern a hospital's operating rooms and inpatient areas?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0140"]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-188","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"DEF","text":"ASHRAE 188 sets minimum legionellosis risk management requirements for centralized building water systems, such as cooling towers and potable piping.","src":"ASHRAE 188-2021","prompt":"Which ASHRAE standard sets the minimum Legionella risk management program for building water systems?","trap":"ASHRAE 241 covers airborne infectious aerosols, not water systems.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0141"]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-241","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"DEF","text":"ASHRAE 241 (2023) controls infectious aerosols with equivalent clean airflow targets and an infection risk management mode. It layers on top of 62.1, 62.2, or 170; it does not replace them.","src":"ASHRAE 241-2023","prompt":"What does ASHRAE 241 set, and does it replace the building's ventilation standard?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0140","cvi-0142"]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-180-role","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"NOTE","text":"In the ASHRAE family, ASHRAE/ACCA 180 is the commercial HVAC inspection and maintenance practice standard. It sets no ventilation rates, filter test, comfort criteria, or health care rates.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180","prompt":"Which ASHRAE document is the maintenance practice standard, and what does it not set?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0134","cvi-0140","cvi-0141","cvi-0364"]},{"id":"cvi:621-merv6-old","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"NOTE","text":"The MERV 8 coil rule arrived with the 2013 edition of ASHRAE 62.1. Editions through 2010 required MERV 6, and 2019 and later editions also accept the ISO ePM10 equivalent.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2013 §5.8; 62.1-2022 §5.5","prompt":"Which ASHRAE 62.1 edition raised the coil filter minimum from MERV 6 to MERV 8?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 raised the coil filter minimum from MERV 6 to MERV 8 in its __ edition.","a":"2013","d":["2010","2016","2019"]},"qs":["cvi-0196","cvi-0257"]},{"id":"cvi:621-merv11-pm25","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"FACT","text":"Where outdoor air exceeds the PM2.5 standard, ASHRAE 62.1 requires outdoor air filtration of at least MERV 11 (or the ISO ePM2.5 minimum). That is an outdoor air rule, separate from the coil rule.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §6.1.4.2","prompt":"When does ASHRAE 62.1 call for MERV 11 filtration, and on which air?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0136","cvi-0196"]},{"id":"cvi:621-pan-seal","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"FACT","text":"Where a drain pan is under negative pressure, as in a draw-through unit, ASHRAE 62.1 requires a P-trap or other seal. Without it the fan pulls air up the drain and holds water in a properly sloped pan.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.7.3","prompt":"What does ASHRAE 62.1 require on the drain of a pan under negative pressure, and what happens without it?","trap":"A pan with correct slope and a low outlet can still hold water if the seal is missing.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0258"]},{"id":"cvi:621-t81-filters","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 8-1 calls for checking filter pressure drop and scheduled replacement dates quarterly.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 8-1","prompt":"How often does ASHRAE 62.1 Table 8-1 call for checking filter pressure drop and replacement dates?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 8-1 checks filter pressure drop every __.","a":"3 months","d":["1 month","6 months","12 months"]},"qs":["cvi-0138"]},{"id":"cvi:621-t81-tower-louvers","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 8-1 sets cooling tower water treatment monthly and outdoor air louvers and bird screens semiannually.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 8-1","prompt":"What Table 8-1 intervals apply to cooling tower water treatment and to outdoor air louvers?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0138"]},{"id":"cvi:621-t81-pans","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 8-1 sets drain pans, drain lines, and coils at an annual minimum.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 8-1","prompt":"What Table 8-1 interval applies to drain pans, drain lines, and coils?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 8-1 inspects drain pans, drain lines, and coils every __.","a":"12 months","d":["1 month","3 months","6 months"]},"qs":["cvi-0138"]},{"id":"cvi:621-no-filters","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 bars operating a system designed with particle filters unless the filters are in place, even during construction. Its separate rule that ducts be clean at start-up is an added requirement, not a waiver.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §7.1.2, §7.2.4","prompt":"Under ASHRAE 62.1, may a new AHU run without its filters during construction, and does the start-up cleanliness rule change that?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0139"]},{"id":"cvi:fd-interval","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 80 requires fire damper testing and inspection 1 year after installation, then every 4 years.","src":"NFPA 80 Ch. 19 (2010 §19.4.1)","prompt":"How often does NFPA 80 require fire damper testing after installation?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Outside hospitals, NFPA 80 retests fire dampers every __ after the first-year test.","a":"4 years","d":["1 year","5 years","6 years"]},"qs":["cvi-0146","cvi-0250","cvi-0251"]},{"id":"cvi:damper-hospital","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"Hospitals are the exception: after the first-year test, NFPA 80 and NFPA 105 allow hospital fire and smoke dampers to be tested every 6 years.","src":"NFPA 80 Ch. 19; NFPA 105 (2010 §6.5.2)","prompt":"What damper test cycle do NFPA 80 and 105 allow hospitals after the first-year test?","trap":"Six years is hospitals only. A 5-year-old test in an office is overdue.","num":{"q":"After the first-year test, hospitals may test fire dampers every __.","a":"6 years","d":["4 years","5 years","1 year"]},"qs":["cvi-0145","cvi-0146","cvi-0251"]},{"id":"cvi:sd-interval","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 105 tests smoke dampers 1 year after installation, then every 4 years, the same pattern NFPA 80 uses for fire dampers.","src":"NFPA 105 (2010 §6.5.2)","prompt":"When does NFPA 105 require smoke damper testing and inspection?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0145","cvi-0251"]},{"id":"cvi:damper-combination","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"A combination fire/smoke damper is both a fire damper and a smoke damper, so its inspection and testing must meet NFPA 80 and NFPA 105 together.","src":"NFPA 80 Ch. 19; NFPA 105","prompt":"Which standards govern testing of a combination fire/smoke damper?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0147"]},{"id":"cvi:damper-code-chain","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 101 sends HVAC ductwork to NFPA 90A or 90B as applicable, and NFPA 90A sends fire dampers to NFPA 80 and smoke dampers to NFPA 105. NFPA 90A sets no periodic test procedure itself.","src":"NFPA 101 (2012) §9.2.1; NFPA 90A (2012) §5.4.8","prompt":"What is the chain of references from the Life Safety Code to fire damper testing?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0147","cvi-0152"]},{"id":"cvi:damper-fusible-test","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"To test a fusible-link fire damper under NFPA 80, release or remove the link with the damper fully open. It must close fully on its own, latch if designed to, then be reset with the link reinstalled.","src":"NFPA 80-2019 §19.5.2.2","prompt":"How is a fusible-link fire damper tested under NFPA 80?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0148","cvi-0250"]},{"id":"cvi:damper-command-test","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"Fire dampers without fusible links and NFPA 105 smoke dampers are tested by commanding them fully closed or open, as designed, then restoring them. A position indicator counts only under the remote inspection rules.","src":"NFPA 80-2019 §19.5.2.3; NFPA 105","prompt":"How are fire dampers without fusible links and smoke dampers tested, and when does a position indicator count?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0148"]},{"id":"cvi:ul-555-family","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"UL 555 lists fire dampers, UL 555S smoke dampers, UL 555C ceiling dampers, and UL 33 heat responsive links. A combination damper carries both UL 555 and UL 555S.","src":"UL 555; UL 555S","prompt":"Which UL standards list fire dampers, smoke dampers, ceiling dampers, and fusible links?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0153","cvi-0155"]},{"id":"cvi:damper-rating","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"A fire damper is rated 1½ hours in an assembly rated under 3 hours, and 3 hours in an assembly rated 3 hours or more. It is not matched to the wall's own rating.","src":"IBC 717; IMC 607; UL 555","prompt":"What fire protection rating does a fire damper need in a 2-hour wall?","trap":"Matching the damper to the 2-hour wall.","num":{"q":"A fire damper in a 2-hour rated wall needs a __ rating.","a":"1½ hours","d":["2 hours","3 hours","45 minutes"]},"qs":["cvi-0154"]},{"id":"cvi:damper-dynamic","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"Where fans keep running during a fire, fire dampers must be dynamic-rated: tested to close against airflow and marked with velocity and pressure limits. Static dampers suit only systems whose fans shut down automatically.","src":"UL 555 (dynamic rating); ICC CodeNotes","prompt":"When must a fire damper be dynamic-rated rather than static?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0155"]},{"id":"cvi:damper-label","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"Damper access doors must be permanently labeled FIRE DAMPER, SMOKE DAMPER, or FIRE/SMOKE DAMPER in letters at least 1/2 inch high. An unlabeled access panel is a finding.","src":"IMC 607.4; NFPA 90A (2012) §4.3.5.2","prompt":"How must a damper access panel be labeled?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Damper access labels need letters at least __ high.","a":"1/2 inch","d":["1/4 inch","1 inch","4 inches"]},"qs":["cvi-0159"]},{"id":"cvi:nfpa-90b-scope","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 90B covers warm air heating and air-conditioning systems in one- and two-family dwellings and in spaces of 25,000 cubic feet or less.","src":"NFPA 90B §1.3","prompt":"Which NFPA standard covers the forced-air system in a single-family house?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0143","cvi-0144"]},{"id":"cvi:nfpa-90a-scope","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 90A covers systems serving spaces over 25,000 cubic feet, plus Type III, IV, and V buildings over three stories regardless of volume.","src":"NFPA 90A §1.3","prompt":"Above what space volume does NFPA 90A apply instead of 90B?","trap":"2,000 and 15,000 are 90A duct detector airflow thresholds in cfm, not volumes.","num":{"q":"NFPA 90A applies to systems serving spaces over __.","a":"25,000 cubic feet","d":["2,000 cubic feet","15,000 cubic feet","50,000 cubic feet"]},"qs":["cvi-0143","cvi-0144"]},{"id":"cvi:nfpa-90a-supply-det","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 90A requires a duct smoke detector on systems over 2,000 cfm, in the supply downstream of the filters and ahead of any branch takeoff.","src":"NFPA 90A §6.4.2.1","prompt":"Where does NFPA 90A require a duct smoke detector on a single-floor unit over 2,000 cfm?","trap":"","num":{"q":"NFPA 90A requires a supply duct smoke detector on systems over __.","a":"2,000 cfm","d":["400 cfm","15,000 cfm","50 cfm"]},"qs":["cvi-0144","cvi-0151"]},{"id":"cvi:nfpa-90a-return-det","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"When a return system over 15,000 cfm serves more than one story, NFPA 90A adds detectors at each story ahead of the connection to the common return.","src":"NFPA 90A §6.4.2.1","prompt":"When does NFPA 90A add return detectors at each story?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0144","cvi-0151"]},{"id":"cvi:imc-return-det","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"The IMC 606.2.1 return detector goes upstream of any filters, exhaust air connections, outdoor air connections, or decontamination equipment.","src":"IMC 606.2.1","prompt":"Where does IMC 606.2.1 place the return-side duct smoke detector?","trap":"Upstream of filters in the return is the IMC rule; downstream of filters in the supply is the 90A rule.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0151"]},{"id":"cvi:irc-scope","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"The IRC covers detached one- and two-family dwellings and townhouses up to three stories above grade plane with a separate means of egress, including their mechanical systems.","src":"IRC R101.2","prompt":"Which I-code governs the mechanical system of a three-story townhouse with its own egress?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0158"]},{"id":"cvi:imc-irc-exception","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"The 2024 IMC 101.2 exception lets IRC-scope buildings comply with either the IMC or the IRC. A fourth story or shared egress moves the building to the IBC and IMC.","src":"IMC 101.2 (2024)","prompt":"What moves a townhouse out of the IRC and into the IBC and IMC?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0158"]},{"id":"cvi:flex-connector","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"A flexible air connector (round Listed Air Connector label) is limited to 14 feet and cannot pass through walls, floors, or ceilings.","src":"IMC 603.6.2","prompt":"What length and routing limits apply to a flexible air connector?","trap":"A flexible air duct (rectangular Listed Air Duct label) has no IMC length limit. Read the label.","num":{"q":"The IMC limits a flexible air connector to __ in length.","a":"14 feet","d":["8 feet","25 feet","6 feet"]},"qs":["cvi-0157"]},{"id":"cvi:smacna-016","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"DEF","text":"The SMACNA HVAC Air Duct Leakage Test Manual (ANSI/SMACNA 016) gives leakage test procedures, apparatus, setup, and expected rates. The Duct Construction Standards set gauge, reinforcement, and seal class and defer testing to it.","src":"ANSI/SMACNA 016-2012","prompt":"Which SMACNA document gives duct leakage test procedures and expected rates?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0129"]},{"id":"cvi:smacna-008","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA's IAQ Guidelines for Occupied Buildings Under Construction (ANSI/SMACNA 008) organizes construction IAQ into HVAC protection, source control, pathway interruption, housekeeping, and scheduling. LEED's construction IAQ credit points to it.","src":"ANSI/SMACNA 008 (2nd ed. 2007)","prompt":"Which document organizes construction IAQ control into five measures, and what are they?","trap":"SMACNA's Duct Cleanliness for New Construction is the sibling guideline with three cleanliness levels for new ductwork.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0130"]},{"id":"cvi:astm-c1071","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"DEF","text":"ASTM C1071 is the specification for fibrous glass duct lining insulation, the thermal and sound-absorbing liner used in ducts, plenums, and air handlers carrying air up to 250°F.","src":"ASTM C1071","prompt":"What does ASTM C1071 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0131"]},{"id":"cvi:ul-181","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"CODE","text":"UL 181 classifies factory-made air ducts and air connectors, such as Class 1 duct board. It lists the duct, not the closure tape.","src":"UL 181","prompt":"What does a UL 181 listing classify?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0131","cvi-0132"]},{"id":"cvi:class-a-450","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"NOTE","text":"A 450 smoke-developed index is the ASTM E84 cap for interior finish Classes A, B, and C (Class A flame spread 0 to 25). ACR limits airstream door and panel materials to flame spread 25, smoke-developed 50.","src":"ASTM E84 classes (Intertek); ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"Where does a 450 smoke-developed limit apply, and does it satisfy airstream material rules?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASTM E84 interior finish classes allow a smoke-developed index up to __.","a":"450","d":["50","25","200"]},"qs":["cvi-0127"]},{"id":"cvi:ul-900","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"CODE","text":"UL 900 rates the combustibility and smoke generated by air filter units. Particle removal efficiency comes from ASHRAE 52.2, not UL 900.","src":"UL 900","prompt":"What property does a UL 900 filter classification address?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0135","cvi-0156"]},{"id":"cvi:s500-categories","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S500 water categories: Category 1 is from a sanitary source, Category 2 is significantly contaminated, and Category 3 is grossly contaminated, such as sewage or backflow from beyond the toilet trap.","src":"ANSI/IICRC S500","prompt":"What are the three S500 water categories?","trap":"Classes 1 to 4 rate drying difficulty (Class 4 is specialty drying), not contamination.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0160","cvi-0237"]},{"id":"cvi:s500-time","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"DEF","text":"S500 categories follow the contamination present, not just the source. Time and temperature amplify contaminants, so clean water left warm for days can degrade to Category 2 or 3.","src":"ANSI/IICRC S500","prompt":"Can a potable-water leak stay Category 1 after sitting warm for five days?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0320"]},{"id":"cvi:s590-purpose","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S590 (first edition 2023) is an assessment-only standard for HVAC systems after water, fire, or mold damage. The assessor sorts internal HVAC surfaces into impacted and non-impacted.","src":"ANSI/IICRC S590-2023","prompt":"What core call does an S590 assessor make on each internal HVAC surface?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0161","cvi-0211"]},{"id":"cvi:s590-output","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"DEF","text":"Under S590 the assessor writes a report and a Restoration Work Plan (RWP) for the remediation contractor. Water categories are defined in S500 and mold Conditions in S520, not in S590.","src":"ANSI/IICRC S590-2023","prompt":"What does an S590 assessment produce for the restorer?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0211"]},{"id":"cvi:epa-duct-triggers","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA's home duct guide lists three reasons to consider cleaning: substantial visible mold inside hard-surface ducts, vermin infestation, and ducts clogged with debris or releasing particles into the home.","src":"EPA 402-K-97-002","prompt":"What three conditions does EPA list as reasons to consider duct cleaning?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0162"]},{"id":"cvi:epa-duct-health","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA states duct cleaning has never been shown to prevent health problems, studies do not conclusively show dirty ducts raise home particle levels, and ducts should be cleaned as needed rather than routinely.","src":"EPA 402-K-97-002","prompt":"What does EPA say about duct cleaning, health, and cleaning schedules?","trap":"ACR Table 1 sets inspection intervals, not cleaning intervals.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0162","cvi-0207"]},{"id":"cvi:epa-no-biocide-liner","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA says no biocides are registered for fiber glass duct board or lined ducts, a biocide on other surfaces goes on only after proper cleaning, and routine use of sealants to encapsulate contaminants is not recommended.","src":"EPA 402-K-97-002","prompt":"What does EPA say about biocides on fiberglass-lined ducts and about encapsulating contamination?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0163","cvi-0202","cvi-0207"]},{"id":"cvi:epa-baq-guide","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"DEF","text":"Building Air Quality: A Guide for Building Owners and Facility Managers (EPA and NIOSH, 1991) covers IAQ profiles, management plans, and diagnosing IAQ problems in commercial buildings.","src":"EPA 402-F-91-102","prompt":"Which EPA guide, co-published with NIOSH, covers IAQ problems in commercial buildings?","trap":"IAQ Tools for Schools is EPA's school kit; A Brief Guide to Mold in the Workplace is an OSHA bulletin.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0164"]},{"id":"cvi:hvac-made-vs-carried","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"Microbial growth on coils, drain pans, humidifiers, and ductwork is generated inside the HVAC system. Radon, vehicle exhaust, and construction dust come from outside it; the system only distributes them.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, pollutant sources","prompt":"Which pollutants does the HVAC system generate itself, and which does it only distribute?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0165","cvi-0179"]},{"id":"cvi:distributed-fix","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"NOTE","text":"For a pollutant the system only distributes, the fix starts at the source and the pathway, not in the ducts. Duct cleaning does nothing for radon or formaldehyde.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality","prompt":"Where does the fix start for a pollutant the HVAC system only distributes?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0165","cvi-0166","cvi-0175"]},{"id":"cvi:radon-action","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA's radon action level is 4 pCi/L, and EPA suggests considering a fix between 2 and 4 pCi/L.","src":"EPA, Health Risk of Radon (action level; 2 to 4 range; 1.3 indoor average)","prompt":"What is EPA's radon action level?","trap":"","num":{"q":"EPA's radon action level is __.","a":"4 pCi/L","d":["2 pCi/L","0.4 pCi/L","1.3 pCi/L"]},"qs":["cvi-0166"]},{"id":"cvi:radon-returns","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"Leaky return ducts in crawlspaces and under slabs collect soil gas and distribute radon, which is why radon-resistant construction wants seamless ducts there. Record the seams and refer testing and mitigation to a radon professional.","src":"FL DOH radon-resistant construction; EPA radon","prompt":"How can leaky crawlspace return ducts affect radon, and what should the report do?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0166","cvi-0185"]},{"id":"cvi:legionella-growth","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"CDC says Legionella grows best at 77 to 113°F in stagnant water with little disinfectant, and spreads through aerosolized droplets.","src":"CDC Legionella","prompt":"At what temperatures and in what water does Legionella grow best, and how does it spread?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0167","cvi-0182","cvi-0406"]},{"id":"cvi:humidifier-legionella","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"A heated pan humidifier lets warm water stagnate and can support Legionella, while a direct steam humidifier normally runs above 158°F and inhibits it.","src":"OSHA Legionnaires' disease control","prompt":"Which humidifier type is the stronger Legionella concern, heated pan or direct steam, and why?","trap":"Record humidifier type, water condition, and upkeep on the inspection.","num":{"q":"Direct steam humidifiers normally run above __, which inhibits Legionella.","a":"158°F","d":["113°F","77°F","250°F"]},"qs":["cvi-0167"]},{"id":"cvi:ozone-air-cleaners","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA warns that electrostatic precipitators, ionizers, some UV lamps, and plasma air cleaners can emit ozone, a lung irritant. CARB and AHAM list cleaners tested to emit little or no ozone.","src":"EPA Guide to Air Cleaners in the Home","prompt":"Which in-duct air cleaners can emit ozone, and how do you check a low-ozone listing?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0168","cvi-0179"]},{"id":"cvi:co-odorless","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"Carbon monoxide is colorless and odorless, so an odor complaint does not point to CO. It comes from worn or poorly maintained combustion devices and causes fatigue at low levels.","src":"EPA, CO and indoor air quality","prompt":"Can an odor complaint point to carbon monoxide?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0168","cvi-0176"]},{"id":"cvi:combustion-first","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"Soot at a furnace draft hood plus symptoms that ease away from home point to combustion spillage. Advise an immediate combustion safety check by a qualified tech and record the finding without diagnosing anyone.","src":"EPA, CO and indoor air quality","prompt":"What should the inspector do first on finding draft hood soot and occupant headaches?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0176"]},{"id":"cvi:trap-seal","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"A dried-out floor drain trap passes sewer gas, and exhaust fans that lower room pressure make it worse. The fix is adding water regularly or installing a trap primer.","src":"ND Dept of Health Sewer Gas Guide","prompt":"What causes sewer odor from a rarely used floor drain, and what fixes it?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0171"]},{"id":"cvi:liner-past-filters","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"Eroded liner downstream of the fan is a source past the filters, so no filter upgrade stops its fibers. Record the location and extent and recommend restoring or replacing the liner.","src":"ACR 2025 N-10, 4.18, 4.19","prompt":"Will better filters stop fibers from eroded liner downstream of the fan?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0172"]},{"id":"cvi:mite-reservoirs","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"Most dust mite allergen sits in mattresses, pillows, carpet, and upholstery on 10 to 35 micron particles that settle fast after disturbance. Do not promise duct cleaning will relieve a mite allergy.","src":"IOM, Indoor Allergens (dust mites)","prompt":"Where does dust mite allergen live, and does it stay airborne?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0173"]},{"id":"cvi:mite-rh","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"Dust mites depend on humidity, so mite control keeps indoor RH below 50%.","src":"IOM, Indoor Allergens; EPA IAQ Design Tools for Schools","prompt":"Below what RH does dust mite control aim to keep a home?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Dust mite control keeps indoor RH below __.","a":"50%","d":["30%","60%","40%"]},"qs":["cvi-0173","cvi-0198"]},{"id":"cvi:hidden-mold","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"When a system smells musty but no growth is visible, suspect hidden mold and check the coil, drain pan, liner, and humidifier. EPA finds ozone ineffective against mold at concentrations that meet health standards.","src":"EPA Mold Remediation, Ch. 3; EPA, Ozone Generators that are Sold as Air Cleaners","prompt":"What should a musty odor with no visible growth prompt the inspector to check, and does ozone fix it?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0174"]},{"id":"cvi:formaldehyde","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"Formaldehyde from particleboard, plywood, MDF, glues, and fabrics is a gas, so particle filters and duct cleaning do not remove it. Outdoor air dilutes it, and source control is usually most effective.","src":"EPA formaldehyde facts; EPA Improving IAQ","prompt":"How does the HVAC system relate to formaldehyde from new casework?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0175"]},{"id":"cvi:amplification-site","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"Microbial amplification needs water plus a food source. Porous liner just downstream of a cooling coil catches carryover and stays damp, making it the likeliest growth site in cooling season.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality; EPA duct guidance","prompt":"Where is microbial amplification most likely in an air handler during cooling season, and why?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0177"]},{"id":"cvi:ultrasonic-dust","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"Ultrasonic and impeller (cool mist) humidifiers can disperse tank minerals and microbes as white dust; steam and evaporative units are not expected to. EPA advises low-mineral water and staying under 50% RH.","src":"EPA, Use and Care of Home Humidifiers","prompt":"Which humidifier type and water cause fine white dust on furniture?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0178"]},{"id":"cvi:pesticides","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-sources","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA puts about 80% of most people's pesticide exposure indoors, from products used inside and soil or dust tracked in. The HVAC system distributes these residues; it does not generate them.","src":"EPA, Pesticides and indoor air quality","prompt":"How should an inspector classify indoor pesticide residues as an HVAC pollutant?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0179"]},{"id":"cvi:intake-plumbing-vent","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 keeps a plumbing vent 10 ft from an outdoor air intake when the vent ends less than 3 ft above the intake, and 3 ft when it ends at least 3 ft above.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 5-1 (Table 5.5.1 in 2019)","prompt":"How far must a plumbing vent be from an outdoor air intake?","trap":"The short 3 ft distance applies only when the vent ends at least 3 ft above the intake.","num":{"q":"A plumbing vent ending less than 3 ft above an intake must be __ from it.","a":"10 ft","d":["3 ft","15 ft","30 ft"]},"qs":["cvi-0180"]},{"id":"cvi:intake-truck","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 5.5.1 keeps outdoor air intakes 25 ft from a truck loading area or dock and from bus parking or idling.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 5-1 (Table 5.5.1 in 2019)","prompt":"How far must an intake be from a truck loading dock?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 keeps an intake at least __ from a truck loading dock.","a":"25 ft","d":["15 ft","5 ft","30 ft"]},"qs":["cvi-0181"]},{"id":"cvi:intake-garage-entry","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 5.5.1 keeps outdoor air intakes 15 ft from a garage entry or automobile loading area.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 5-1 (Table 5.5.1 in 2019)","prompt":"How far must an intake be from a garage entry or automobile loading area?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 keeps an intake at least __ from a garage entry.","a":"15 ft","d":["5 ft","25 ft","10 ft"]},"qs":["cvi-0181"]},{"id":"cvi:intake-driveway","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 5.5.1 keeps outdoor air intakes 5 ft from driveways, streets, and parking places.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 5-1 (Table 5.5.1 in 2019)","prompt":"How far must an intake be from a driveway, street, or parking place?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 keeps an intake at least __ from a street or parking place.","a":"5 ft","d":["15 ft","25 ft","10 ft"]},"qs":["cvi-0181"]},{"id":"cvi:intake-cooling-tower","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 5.5.1 keeps intakes 15 ft from a cooling tower intake or basin and 25 ft from cooling tower exhaust, whose plume carries drift that can spread Legionella.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 5-1 (Table 5.5.1 in 2019); CDC Legionella","prompt":"What separations does ASHRAE 62.1 require between an intake and a cooling tower?","trap":"The inverted pair: 25 ft is for the tower exhaust, 15 ft for the tower intake or basin.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0182"]},{"id":"cvi:intake-exhaust-classes","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 5.5.1 separates intakes from exhaust outlets by air class: 10 ft for Class 2, 15 ft for Class 3, and 30 ft for Class 4.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 5-1 (Table 5.5.1 in 2019)","prompt":"What intake separations does ASHRAE 62.1 require from Class 2, 3, and 4 exhaust outlets?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 keeps an intake __ from a Class 4 exhaust outlet.","a":"30 ft","d":["10 ft","15 ft","25 ft"]},"qs":["cvi-0181","cvi-0183","cvi-0188"]},{"id":"cvi:air-class-examples","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 rates toilet exhaust as Class 2 air; janitor closets, trash rooms, and recycling as Class 3; and lab hoods and spray booths as Class 4.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Tables 6-2 and 6-3","prompt":"What air class does ASHRAE 62.1 give toilet exhaust, janitor and trash rooms, and lab hoods?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0183","cvi-0188","cvi-0193"]},{"id":"cvi:four-elements","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA frames an IAQ problem as four elements: a source, an HVAC system that cannot control it, a pathway, and occupants, with a driving force moving the pollutant along the pathway.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, four elements","prompt":"What four elements does EPA say make up an IAQ problem?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0194"]},{"id":"cvi:pressure-drives","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"Air moves from higher to lower pressure through any opening, so the pressure difference decides whether an odor travels along a pathway; reverse the pressure and the flow reverses.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, pathways","prompt":"What controls whether a pollutant moves along an open pathway between spaces?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0185","cvi-0194"]},{"id":"cvi:stack-effect","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"DEF","text":"Stack effect is flow driven by rising heated air: positive pressure at the top of a building and negative at the bottom. In winter the negative base draws garage air in and shafts carry it upward.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality glossary","prompt":"How does stack effect move garage exhaust to upper floors in winter?","trap":"The base of a heated building is negative, not positive.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0186"]},{"id":"cvi:hot-humid-negative","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"In hot, humid climates even slight negative building pressure pulls moist outdoor air into chilled wall cavities, where vinyl wallcovering traps it at the cold surface. Keeping the building slightly positive helps.","src":"EPA IAQ Design Tools for Schools, Moisture Control","prompt":"Why does a slightly negative hotel in a humid climate grow mold behind vinyl wallcovering?","trap":"Moist room air pushed outward is the cold-climate version of the problem.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0187"]},{"id":"cvi:reentrainment","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"DEF","text":"Re-entrainment is exhaust air brought right back in through an outdoor air intake or other envelope openings.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality glossary","prompt":"What is re-entrainment?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0188"]},{"id":"cvi:short-circuiting","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"DEF","text":"Short-circuiting is supply air reaching an exhaust or return before it enters the breathing zone, so the room gets less effective ventilation and CO2 climbs.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality glossary","prompt":"What is short-circuiting, and what does it do to a room?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0188","cvi-0189"]},{"id":"cvi:garage-ducts","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"Ducts and air handlers in an attached garage pull car exhaust, fuel vapors, and chemicals in through return-side leaks, so DOE and EPA Indoor airPLUS guidance keeps them out of garages. Flag CO as life safety.","src":"DOE Building America; EPA Indoor airPLUS","prompt":"Why should ducts and air handlers stay out of attached garages?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0184"]},{"id":"cvi:crawlspace-return","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"An open or leaky return in a crawlspace is the low-pressure point that pulls damp, spore-laden air into the supply. Recommend sealing plus crawlspace moisture control; cleaning alone will not stop the odor.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, pathways","prompt":"What pathway explains a musty odor when a crawlspace return plenum is open?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0185"]},{"id":"cvi:open-return-dust","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"An open ceiling return plenum carries room and ceiling-cavity air straight back to the AHU, so renovation dust loads the filters and, through bypass, the coil and ducts. A return plenum runs negative.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality App. B; ACR 2025 G-6","prompt":"Where does renovation dust go in a building with an open ceiling return plenum?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0190"]},{"id":"cvi:makeup-air-400","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"CODE","text":"IRC M1503.6 (2018 to 2024) requires makeup air for kitchen exhaust hoods over 400 cfm when a fuel-burning appliance that is neither direct-vent nor mechanical-draft sits inside the dwelling's air barrier.","src":"IRC 2021 M1503.6","prompt":"When does the IRC require makeup air for a kitchen hood?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The IRC requires makeup air for kitchen hoods over __ when natural-draft appliances are inside.","a":"400 cfm","d":["2,000 cfm","15,000 cfm","50 cfm"]},"qs":["cvi-0191"]},{"id":"cvi:depressurization-flue","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"Large exhaust depressurizes a tight house, and replacement air enters through any opening, including a natural-draft appliance flue. Flue spillage is a CO hazard to record.","src":"IRC 2021 M1503.6; PSU PHRC","prompt":"What distribution risk does a big range hood create in a tight house with a natural-draft water heater?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0191"]},{"id":"cvi:oa-exceeds-exhaust","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA says make-up air drawn in at the air handler should always slightly exceed total relief and exhaust, so the building does not run negative. Exact balance leaves no positive margin.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality App. B","prompt":"How should outdoor air compare with total exhaust and relief?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0192"]},{"id":"cvi:exhaust-odor-rooms","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"Rooms that produce odors and contaminants, such as copy rooms, janitor closets, and bathrooms, should be exhausted outdoors rather than recirculated, which keeps them negative so air flows in.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality; ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 6-2","prompt":"How should copy rooms and janitor closets be ventilated so their odors stay put?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0193"]},{"id":"cvi:rh-band","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA wants indoor RH below 60%, ideally 30 to 50%. Above about 60%, mold grows and dust mites thrive; look for causes such as oversized equipment or excess humid outdoor air.","src":"EPA IAQ Design Tools for Schools; EPA mold guidance","prompt":"What RH range does EPA recommend, and what happens above 60%?","trap":"","num":{"q":"EPA wants indoor RH below __, ideally 30 to 50%.","a":"60%","d":["50%","40%","20%"]},"qs":["cvi-0198","cvi-0199"]},{"id":"cvi:rh-low","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"Low RH brings static shocks, dry eyes, dry throats, and itchy skin; office research links RH below 40% to more such complaints. EPA suggests keeping RH above 20 or 30% in the heating season.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality App. B; CDC (Jones et al.)","prompt":"What complaints go with low winter humidity, and what floor does EPA suggest?","trap":"Mold and dust mites are high-humidity problems, not low.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0198","cvi-0199"]},{"id":"cvi:sbs","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"DEF","text":"Sick building syndrome is acute health or comfort effects linked to time in a building, easing soon after leaving, with no specific illness or cause identified.","src":"EPA Indoor Air Facts No. 4","prompt":"What pattern defines sick building syndrome?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0203"]},{"id":"cvi:bri","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"DEF","text":"Building-related illness is a diagnosable illness whose cause traces to building contaminants, and recovery can be prolonged. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis and Legionnaires' disease are examples.","src":"EPA Indoor Air Facts No. 4","prompt":"What defines building-related illness, and what are examples?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0203"]},{"id":"cvi:vrp","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"The ASHRAE 62.1 Ventilation Rate Procedure adds a people rate and an area rate for breathing-zone outdoor air. For offices that is 5 cfm per person plus 0.06 cfm per square foot.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1 VRP; IMC Table 403.3.1.1","prompt":"What two components make up breathing-zone outdoor air under the 62.1 VRP, with office values?","trap":"Per-fixture rates (50 or 70 cfm per water closet) are exhaust rates, not VRP terms.","num":{"q":"For offices the 62.1 VRP uses __ per person plus 0.06 cfm per square foot.","a":"5 cfm","d":["15 cfm","50 cfm","70 cfm"]},"qs":["cvi-0195"]},{"id":"cvi:co2-not-iaq","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"Indoor CO2 tracks occupant bioeffluents, not overall IAQ. Emissions from building materials and outdoor pollutants do not correlate with it, so an acceptable CO2 reading says nothing about VOCs.","src":"ASHRAE Position Document on Indoor CO2","prompt":"What does a CO2 reading tell you about VOCs from new carpet?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0204","cvi-0205"]},{"id":"cvi:co2-no-limit","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"NOTE","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 sets no indoor CO2 level as an IAQ limit. Its newer CO2-above-outdoor values are setpoints for CO2 demand-controlled ventilation, and it says not to use them as IAQ indicators.","src":"NIST, Quit Blaming ASHRAE 62.1 for 1000 ppm CO2; 62.1-2022 Addendum ab","prompt":"Does ASHRAE 62.1 set an indoor CO2 limit for IAQ?","trap":"1,000 ppm is shorthand for old informative guidance, not a 62.1 limit.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0205","cvi-0427"]},{"id":"cvi:co2-700-5000","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"NOTE","text":"700 ppm above outdoors (about 1,000 ppm indoors) came from informative guidance in older 62.1 editions, not a requirement. 5,000 ppm is the OSHA 8-hour PEL, a workplace exposure limit.","src":"NIST (Persily); ASHRAE IC-62-1999-03; NIOSH NPG","prompt":"Where do the 700 ppm and 5,000 ppm CO2 figures come from?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's 8-hour PEL for carbon dioxide is __.","a":"5,000 ppm","d":["1,000 ppm","700 ppm","420 ppm"]},"qs":["cvi-0205"]},{"id":"cvi:filter-bypass","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"Gaps around filter cells let unfiltered air load the coil and seriously cut filtration efficiency. A higher MERV in a leaky rack changes nothing; seal the gaps before cleaning the coil.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality App. B; ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"What does a gap around a filter rack do, and does a higher MERV fix it?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0197","cvi-0256"]},{"id":"cvi:flat-pan-reset","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"Standing water builds when a drain pan cannot drain fully, and molds and bacteria then proliferate. Cleaning a flat pan only resets the clock; correct its slope.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality App. B; ASHRAE 62.1","prompt":"Why is cleaning alone the wrong fix for a flat, slimy drain pan?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0137","cvi-0201"]},{"id":"cvi:epa-wet-insulation","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA says wet or moldy duct insulation cannot be effectively cleaned and should be removed and replaced. Fix the water source first, or the new liner gets wet again.","src":"EPA duct cleaning guidance","prompt":"What does EPA advise for wet or moldy duct insulation?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0177","cvi-0202","cvi-0260"]},{"id":"cvi:epa-hvac-off","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA says not to run an HVAC system known or suspected to be contaminated with mold, because running the fan spreads spores. Ventilate by other means until remediation.","src":"EPA Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings, Ch. 3","prompt":"Should a mold-contaminated HVAC system keep running until remediation?","trap":"Running the fan does not dry the growth.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0200","cvi-0284"]},{"id":"cvi:visible-no-sampling","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"Per EPA, visible mold growth in most cases makes air sampling unnecessary before planning remediation.","src":"EPA Mold Remediation, Ch. 3","prompt":"Is air sampling needed before acting on visible mold growth?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0200"]},{"id":"cvi:no-mold-limit","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"cvi-iaq-comfort","tag":"NOTE","text":"EPA sets no federal mold limits, so sampling cannot show compliance. That does not make observed growth harmless or unrelated to complaints.","src":"EPA Mold Remediation, Ch. 3","prompt":"What does the absence of federal mold limits mean for a report?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0209"]},{"id":"cvi:timing-start","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"Per EPA, symptoms that begin or are worst at the start of the occupied period point to reviewing HVAC operating cycles, since emissions from materials or the HVAC system can build up while the building is unoccupied.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","prompt":"What lead does EPA suggest when symptoms are worst first thing in the morning and on Mondays?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0214"]},{"id":"cvi:timing-other","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA's other timing leads: symptoms worsening through the occupied period point to ventilation not keeping up with routine activities, and a single episode points to a spill or other one-time release.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","prompt":"What does EPA say symptoms that worsen through the day, or a single episode, point to?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0214","cvi-0285"]},{"id":"cvi:spatial-local","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"For complaints localized to one zone or air handler, EPA says check ventilation and temperature control there, nearby sources, and local HVAC components acting as pollutant sources or distributors.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","prompt":"What does EPA suggest checking when complaints come only from rooms served by one AHU?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0215"]},{"id":"cvi:spatial-wide","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"For widespread complaints EPA points to whole-building ventilation, temperature control, and outdoor air quality at the intakes; for a single person's complaint, to drafts and radiant heat near that person.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","prompt":"What does EPA suggest checking for building-wide complaints versus one person's complaint?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0215"]},{"id":"cvi:icra","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"Before lifting ceiling tiles or opening ducts near high-risk hospital patients, coordinate an infection control risk assessment (ICRA) with the facility's infection control staff, as CDC guidance calls for before dust-generating work.","src":"CDC EIC Guidelines; ACR 2025 §1.2.1","prompt":"What pre-inspection step fits inspecting above a hospital oncology unit?","trap":"A dust waiver shifts liability without protecting patients, and a respirator protects only the inspector.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0220"]},{"id":"cvi:detector-coordination","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"Before opening a duct beside a smoke detector, agree detector handling with the owner and the alarm company. Removing, taping, or moving a detector is an impairment the inspector has no authority to make.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.8, §4.10","prompt":"What must be arranged before opening a return duct beside a duct smoke detector?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0224"]},{"id":"cvi:oap-exhausted-rooms","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA's open air plenum white paper says removing ceiling tiles disrupts airflow; keep the ceiling and walls intact over rooms designed to be exhausted, such as bathrooms, supply closets, and chemical storage.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §IV","prompt":"What does the NADCA open air plenum white paper stress about lifting tiles over exhausted rooms?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0248"]},{"id":"cvi:oap-asbestos","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"The NADCA open air plenum white paper warns that plenums in older buildings may hold asbestos and other hazardous materials.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §V","prompt":"What hazard does the NADCA white paper flag for open plenums in older buildings?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0247"]},{"id":"cvi:oap-raised-floor","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"The NADCA white paper treats a raised-floor data center supply as a positive supply plenum, a non-ducted open air plenum rather than supply duct.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §IV; ACR 2025 §1.7.4","prompt":"How does NADCA classify the air space under a data center raised floor?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0235"]},{"id":"cvi:borescope-neck","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"To see inside a closed branch without cutting, feed a borescope through an existing opening such as the diffuser neck. Existing doors, grilles, and panels disturb least and add no new closures.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2, §1.4","prompt":"How can a CVI see inside a branch duct that has no access door without making a new opening?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0229","cvi-0261"]},{"id":"cvi:micromanometer","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"A micromanometer reads pressure differentials, such as static pressure drop across a coil, which puts airflow restriction in numbers rather than photos.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6; ACR 2025 §4.11.6","prompt":"Which instrument shows how much a loaded coil restricts airflow?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0225"]},{"id":"cvi:flow-hood","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"For supply airflow in cfm at a diffuser, EPA names a flow hood as the accurate tool; an anemometer or velometer gives only a rough reading, and chemical smoke shows airflow patterns, not cfm.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","prompt":"Which tool gives an accurate cfm reading at a diffuser?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0226","cvi-0227"]},{"id":"cvi:min-kit","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA's minimum IAQ kit is heatless chemical smoke plus temperature and humidity instruments; CO2 devices help in most investigations. Hygrothermographs, tracer gas, and particle instruments are consultant add-ons.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","prompt":"What instruments make up EPA's minimum IAQ kit, and which are consultant add-ons?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0227","cvi-0285"]},{"id":"cvi:moisture-meter","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"A moisture meter measures moisture content in a building material by probe or surface contact. A thermo-hygrometer reads the air's temperature and RH, not the material.","src":"EPA mold remediation guide, Equipment","prompt":"Which instrument confirms whether duct liner itself is wet?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0228"]},{"id":"cvi:co2-meter","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"FACT","text":"To judge whether outdoor air keeps up with occupancy, use a direct-reading CO2 meter or detector tubes. Moisture meters, particle counters, and manometers measure other things.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","prompt":"Which instrument helps judge whether ventilation keeps up with a full conference room?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0285"]},{"id":"cvi:particle-counter","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-before","tag":"NOTE","text":"A handheld particle counter reads what is airborne at that moment. When the need to clean is in question, ACR lets Method 2 or 3, which test settled material on surfaces, determine that need.","src":"ACR 2025 N-17","prompt":"Can airborne particle counts at diffusers prove ducts need cleaning under ACR?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0276"]},{"id":"cvi:dp-square","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"FACT","text":"Pressure drop across a coil or duct scales roughly with the square of airflow, so a before-and-after comparison means something only at the same airflow.","src":"DOE Fan Sourcebook; ACR 2025 §4.11.6","prompt":"Why must before and after coil pressure drops be taken at the same airflow?","trap":"","num":{"q":"If airflow falls to 75%, a coil's pressure drop falls to about __ of the original.","a":"56%","d":["75%","42%","87%"]},"qs":["cvi-0424"]},{"id":"cvi:component-compare","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"FACT","text":"Compare static pressure drops component by component and credit cleaning only for the parts cleaned, with filters and fan speed unchanged. A fresh filter bank alone can explain most of a total drop.","src":"NCI static pressure guide; ACR 2025 §4.11.6","prompt":"How should before and after static pressures be compared to credit cleaning?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0435"]},{"id":"cvi:oaf-formula","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"FACT","text":"Outdoor air fraction equals (mixed minus return) divided by (outdoor minus return air temperature). PNNL trusts it only when outdoor and return air differ by more than about 5°F.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"How is outdoor air fraction figured from temperatures, and when is it unreliable?","trap":"","num":{"q":"PNNL trusts the mixed-air OA fraction only when outdoor and return differ by more than about __.","a":"5°F","d":["1°F","3°F","20°F"]},"qs":["cvi-0426"]},{"id":"cvi:return-leak-mix","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"FACT","text":"Mixing math estimates return leakage: attic share equals (plenum minus grille) divided by (attic minus grille temperature), an upper bound since some rise is conduction. Record the leaks as a dust and fiber pathway.","src":"DOE Energy Saver: duct losses; PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"How do you estimate the attic air leaking into a return from temperatures, and how is it recorded?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0433"]},{"id":"cvi:target-split","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"FACT","text":"A coil's target temperature split depends on return dry bulb and wet bulb; there is no universal 18 to 20°F split. Proctor treats a split within 3°F of target as normal.","src":"Proctor Engineering / Carrier target split table","prompt":"Does a smaller temperature split after cleaning, on a different day, prove lost capacity?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0425"]},{"id":"cvi:face-velocity","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"FACT","text":"Coil face velocity is airflow divided by the finned coil face area. Above the long-used 500 fpm rule of thumb, moisture carryover becomes a risk and can soak liner behind a pan that drains.","src":"Trane ENL Coil Selection (APP-CMC054)","prompt":"How is coil face velocity figured, and above what value does carryover become a risk?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The long-used rule of thumb limits coil face velocity to about __ to avoid carryover.","a":"500 fpm","d":["1,000 fpm","2,000 fpm","2,500 fpm"]},"qs":["cvi-0434"]},{"id":"cvi:vav-failed","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"FACT","text":"A VAV box commanded 100% open with near-zero airflow, while other boxes and duct static are normal, points to a failed box component: damper actuator or linkage, plugged sensing tube, or controller.","src":"PNNL-29078 §3.3.4","prompt":"What does a VAV box stuck at 100% command with near-zero airflow indicate?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0432"]},{"id":"cvi:ach-cfm","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"FACT","text":"To size air scrubbing, cfm equals room volume in cubic feet times air changes per hour, divided by 60. A 6,000 cubic foot room at 4 ACH needs 400 cfm.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.7 (arithmetic)","prompt":"How do you convert a required ACH into scrubber cfm?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A 20 x 30 x 10 ft room at 4 ACH needs __ of HEPA scrubbing.","a":"400 cfm","d":["40 cfm","600 cfm","24,000 cfm"]},"qs":["cvi-0296"]},{"id":"cvi:manometer-lapse","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"FACT","text":"When a containment must stay negative at all times, one positive manometer reading during work is a lapse, whatever the average of the log shows.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.5 to 3.10.6","prompt":"How should a single positive reading in a Level 2 manometer log be recorded?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0430"]},{"id":"cvi:npe-002","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"NOTE","text":"The -0.02 inch w.c. figure is OSHA's minimum for an asbestos negative pressure enclosure (1926.1101), not an ACR value; ACR sets no numeric negative pressure.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101(g)(5)(i); ACR 2025 D-96","prompt":"Where does the -0.02 in. w.c. negative pressure figure come from?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0371","cvi-0430"]},{"id":"cvi:niosh-0500-0600","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-insp-readings","tag":"NOTE","text":"NIOSH 0500 is the total dust method whose weighing Method 3 borrows; for air sampling it uses a single 37 mm PVC filter at 1 to 2 L/min. NIOSH 0600 is the respirable method with a cyclone.","src":"NIOSH NMAM 0500; ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How do NIOSH 0500 and 0600 differ, and what does 0500 use for air sampling?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0349","cvi-0354","cvi-0359"]},{"id":"cvi:photo-keying","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"FACT","text":"Key each inspection photo and finding to a component tag and plan location, so the same spot can be re-shot after cleaning and the scope can name components. A set of worst-case photos misrepresents the system.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3, §1.10, §5.6.3","prompt":"How should inspection photos and findings be labeled to support a before-and-after comparison?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0264","cvi-0331"]},{"id":"cvi:not-inspected","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"FACT","text":"List any component you could not reach as not inspected, with the reason. Rating it clean or dirty from adjoining ducts reports a condition nobody saw.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7, §1.10","prompt":"How should a report handle a plenum above a hard-lid ceiling that could not be reached?","trap":"A visual-only contract is the reason to write the limitation down, not a reason to leave it out.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0332","cvi-0388"]},{"id":"cvi:no-health-causation","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"FACT","text":"The inspector documents system conditions and recommends scope; whether the system caused an illness is a medical judgment. Refer occupants with health concerns to a physician or health professional.","src":"EPA Mold Remediation, Ch. 3; ACR 2025 General","prompt":"How should an inspector answer an occupant who asks whether the ducts caused her asthma?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0209","cvi-0330","cvi-0382"]},{"id":"cvi:no-certify-claims","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"NOTE","text":"A report states observed conditions and particulate results. It never certifies a system mold-free, asbestos-free, lead-free, or safe for a health condition, because no inspection or verification test measured that.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0, General","prompt":"Why can't a CVI report certify a system mold-free or safe for asthmatic children?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0208","cvi-0238","cvi-0330","cvi-0334","cvi-0385"]},{"id":"cvi:eo-vs-gl","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"FACT","text":"Professional liability (errors and omissions) covers claims of negligence, misrepresentation, or inaccurate advice, such as a report that missed contamination. General liability covers third-party bodily injury and property damage.","src":"Insurance Information Institute","prompt":"Which insurance covers a missed finding in a report, and which covers a tenant tripping over a cable?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0389"]},{"id":"cvi:claims-made","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"FACT","text":"Most professional liability policies are claims-made: the policy must be in force both when the event happened and when the claim is filed. An occurrence policy covers events in its period even if the suit comes later.","src":"Insurance Information Institute","prompt":"What must be true for a claims-made E&O policy to cover a suit?","trap":"Letting a claims-made policy lapse at retirement leaves earlier inspections uncovered.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0390"]},{"id":"cvi:confidentiality","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"FACT","text":"The inspection report belongs to the client. Inspection ethics codes bar releasing results without client approval, except at the inspector's discretion for immediate safety hazards to exposed occupants.","src":"ASHI Code of Ethics 2.C","prompt":"May a CVI give a tenant the landlord's inspection report?","trap":"Releasing only the photos is still a disclosure.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0391"]},{"id":"cvi:coi-necessary","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA's Code of Ethics commits members to accurate inspections that decide what cleaning is required, if any, and to provide only necessary and desired services.","src":"NADCA Code of Ethics items 4 and 5","prompt":"What principle governs a report when the inspecting firm will also bid the cleaning?","trap":"Disclosing the cleaning bid does not make an unsupported scope acceptable.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0387","cvi-0388"]},{"id":"cvi:other-trades","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA's Code of Ethics directs members to use other trades when work calls for skills they lack. Record a failed actuator or wiring fault and refer it rather than repairing it.","src":"NADCA Code of Ethics item 7; ACR 2025 §1.7.6","prompt":"What should a CVI do when asked to rewire a failed VAV damper actuator during an inspection?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0393"]},{"id":"cvi:damper-unsafe","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"FACT","text":"A fire damper that cannot close, such as one with a painted link or a packed blade track, is documented as unsafe and referred to the owner's NFPA 80 test program. The inspector records it and does not repair it.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.6; NFPA 80 ch. 19","prompt":"How should an inspector record a fire damper with a painted-over fusible link?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0250"]},{"id":"cvi:damper-records","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"cvi-reports-liability","tag":"FACT","text":"Fire damper test records identify each damper by location. When the tag, the log, and the drawing disagree, no test result can be tied to the damper; record the mismatch and refer testing.","src":"NFPA 80 (2010) 19.4; ACR 2025 §1.3","prompt":"What should the CVI record when a damper's tag, test log, and drawing location disagree?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0429"]},{"id":"cvi:cs-entry","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"Under 1910.146, entry into a permit-required confined space occurs as soon as any part of the body breaks the plane of an opening. A head and arm through an access door is entry.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"When does entry into a permit space occur under 1910.146?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0395"]},{"id":"cvi:fall-gi-4","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"General industry fall protection starts at 4 feet above a lower level (1910.28(b)(1)).","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(1)","prompt":"At what height does general industry require fall protection at an unprotected edge?","trap":"","num":{"q":"General industry fall protection starts at __ above a lower level.","a":"4 feet","d":["6 feet","24 feet","3 feet"]},"qs":["cvi-0396"]},{"id":"cvi:fall-cons-6","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"Construction fall protection starts at 6 feet (1926.501(b)(1)). General industry is 4 feet.","src":"29 CFR 1926.501(b)(1)","prompt":"At what height does construction require fall protection?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Construction fall protection starts at __ above a lower level.","a":"6 feet","d":["4 feet","10 feet","15 feet"]},"qs":["cvi-0396"]},{"id":"cvi:break-19","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"In construction, a personnel access point with a break in elevation of 19 inches or more needs a stairway or ladder, unless a ramp, runway, sloped embankment, or personnel hoist is provided.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1051(a)","prompt":"What break in elevation requires a stairway or ladder in construction?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Construction requires a stairway or ladder at a break in elevation of __ or more.","a":"19 inches","d":["30 inches","36 inches","42 inches"]},"qs":["cvi-0396","cvi-0399"]},{"id":"cvi:fixed-ladder-24","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"A fixed ladder extending more than 24 feet above a lower level needs fall protection. Ladders installed since November 19, 2018 need a ladder safety or personal fall arrest system; older ladders may keep a cage or well until 2036.","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(9)","prompt":"Above what height does a fixed ladder need fall protection, and which systems qualify on ladders installed since November 2018?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0396"]},{"id":"cvi:roof-under-6","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"On a low-slope roof, work less than 6 feet from the edge needs a guardrail, safety net, travel restraint, or personal fall arrest system (1910.28(b)(13)).","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(13)(i)","prompt":"What fall protection does work within 6 feet of a low-slope roof edge need?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0397"]},{"id":"cvi:roof-6-15","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"From 6 to under 15 feet from a low-slope roof edge, a designated area may replace conventional fall protection when the work is infrequent and temporary.","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(13)(ii)","prompt":"When may a designated area be used on a low-slope roof?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0397"]},{"id":"cvi:roof-15","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"At 15 feet or more from a low-slope roof edge, work that is both infrequent and temporary needs no fall protection if the employer enforces a rule keeping workers out of the 15-foot zone.","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(13)(iii)","prompt":"When may low-slope roof work proceed with no fall protection?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Infrequent, temporary work at least __ from a low-slope roof edge may skip fall protection under a work rule.","a":"15 feet","d":["6 feet","4 feet","10 feet"]},"qs":["cvi-0397"]},{"id":"cvi:prework-exempt","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"1910.28 exempts inspecting or assessing conditions before work starts or after all work ends, unless compliant fall protection is installed and available. Construction has a similar exception (1926.500(a)(1)) without that installed-equipment condition.","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(a)(2)(ii); 1926.500(a)(1)","prompt":"Does 1910.28 apply to a CVI's pre-work walk of a roof with no fall protection installed?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0398"]},{"id":"cvi:ladder-36","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"Portable ladder side rails must extend at least 3 feet (36 inches) above an upper landing (1910.23(c)(11); 1926.1053(b)(1)).","src":"29 CFR 1910.23(c)(11); 1926.1053(b)(1)","prompt":"How far must portable ladder side rails extend above the roof edge?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A portable ladder's side rails must extend at least __ above the upper landing.","a":"36 inches","d":["42 inches","30 inches","19 inches"]},"qs":["cvi-0399"]},{"id":"cvi:ladder-fixed","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"Fixed through or side-step ladders extend their side rails 42 inches above the access level, and a fixed ladder without a cage or well needs 30 inches of clearance on the climbing side.","src":"29 CFR 1910.23(d)(4), (d)(13)","prompt":"What side-rail extension and climbing clearance apply to fixed ladders?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0399"]},{"id":"cvi:ladder-4to1","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"A non-self-supporting ladder's foot goes out about one-quarter of its working length (the 4-to-1 rule), so 24 feet of working length puts the foot about 6 feet from the wall.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(5)(i)","prompt":"How far from the wall should a ladder with 24 feet of working length sit?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A ladder with 24 feet of working length sets its foot about __ from the wall.","a":"6 feet","d":["8 feet","12 feet","4 feet"]},"qs":["cvi-0400"]},{"id":"cvi:powerline-10","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"An unqualified worker and any conductive object he handles stay at least 10 feet from unguarded overhead lines of 50 kV or less, plus 4 inches for every 10 kV above 50 kV.","src":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(3)","prompt":"How close may an unqualified worker bring an aluminum ladder to a 50 kV line?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Unqualified workers keep conductive objects at least __ from overhead lines of 50 kV or less.","a":"10 feet","d":["3 feet","15 feet","20 feet"]},"qs":["cvi-0410"]},{"id":"cvi:ladder-nonconductive","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"Portable ladders used where they could contact exposed energized parts need nonconductive side rails (1910.333(c)(7)).","src":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(7)","prompt":"What kind of side rails must a ladder have near exposed energized parts?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0410"]},{"id":"cvi:loto-inspecting","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA 1910.147 counts inspecting as servicing, so reaching into an AHU fan section just to photograph the wheel needs lockout. A helper at the disconnect is no substitute.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(a)(1), (b)","prompt":"Does lockout apply when a CVI reaches into a fan section only to take a photo?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0233","cvi-0401"]},{"id":"cvi:loto-control-circuit","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"Push buttons, selector switches, and other control circuit devices, such as a thermostat or BAS command, are not energy isolating devices. The lock goes on a disconnect or breaker that physically blocks energy.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(b)","prompt":"Why is setting the thermostat to OFF and commanding the unit off at the BAS not lockout?","trap":"Unplugged cord-and-plug equipment under the worker's exclusive control is an exception, not the limit of the standard.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0401"]},{"id":"cvi:fit-test","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"1910.134 requires a respirator fit test before first use, whenever a different facepiece size, style, model, or make is used, and at least annually.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(f)(2), (e)(7)","prompt":"When does 1910.134 require a respirator fit test?","trap":"A new make of facepiece triggers a fit test, not a new medical evaluation.","num":{"q":"1910.134 repeats respirator fit testing at least every __.","a":"12 months","d":["6 months","24 months","3 months"]},"qs":["cvi-0376","cvi-0402"]},{"id":"cvi:seal-check","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"A user seal check is done every time a respirator is put on, but it never replaces a fit test.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134","prompt":"Can a user seal check stand in for a fit test after switching respirator brands?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0402"]},{"id":"cvi:sds-8","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"HazCom requires a 16-section SDS, and Section 8 covers exposure controls and personal protection, including gloves and respirators.","src":"29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(2), App. D","prompt":"Which SDS section lists exposure controls and PPE?","trap":"","num":{"q":"SDS Section __ lists exposure controls and personal protection.","a":"8","d":["2","4","7"]},"qs":["cvi-0403"]},{"id":"cvi:sds-2-4-7","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"SDS Section 2 identifies hazards, Section 4 gives first-aid measures, and Section 7 covers handling and storage.","src":"29 CFR 1910.1200 App. D","prompt":"What do SDS Sections 2, 4, and 7 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0403"]},{"id":"cvi:heat-stroke","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"FACT","text":"Confusion, slurred speech, and hot skin signal heat stroke: call 911, stay with the worker, and cool him quickly with cold water, wet cloths, or ice.","src":"NIOSH heat-related illnesses","prompt":"What should a CVI do first when a helper in a hot attic becomes confused with hot skin?","trap":"Sips of water and rest in shade are heat exhaustion first aid; for heat stroke that delay can be fatal.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0408"]},{"id":"cvi:noise-85","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"1910.95 requires a hearing conservation program (monitoring, audiometric testing, hearing protectors) at an 8-hour TWA of 85 dBA, the action level.","src":"29 CFR 1910.95(c)","prompt":"At what exposure does 1910.95 require a hearing conservation program?","trap":"","num":{"q":"1910.95's hearing conservation action level is an 8-hour TWA of __.","a":"85 dBA","d":["90 dBA","80 dBA","130 dBA"]},"qs":["cvi-0409"]},{"id":"cvi:noise-90","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"The 1910.95 PEL is 90 dBA for 8 hours; above it, feasible controls are required. A 10 dB standard threshold shift triggers follow-up, not the program itself.","src":"29 CFR 1910.95(b), (g)(10)","prompt":"What is the 1910.95 PEL, and what does a 10 dB threshold shift trigger?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0409"]},{"id":"cvi:owner-acm-survey","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Under 1926.1101(k)(2), building owners must determine the presence, location, and quantity of ACM and PACM before work begins and inform employers who will do the work.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101(k)(2)","prompt":"What must a building owner provide before inspection ports are cut in insulated ductwork in a 1975 building?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0392"]},{"id":"cvi:pacm","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA presumes thermal system insulation and surfacing material in buildings built no later than 1980 contain asbestos (PACM). It is treated as ACM until testing rebuts it.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101(b)","prompt":"Which materials does OSHA presume contain asbestos, and in which buildings?","trap":"1978 is the lead paint cutoff; 1980 is the asbestos presumption.","num":{"q":"OSHA presumes TSI and surfacing material contain asbestos in buildings built no later than __.","a":"1980","d":["1978","1970","1991"]},"qs":["cvi-0255","cvi-0392","cvi-0404"]},{"id":"cvi:tsi-breeching","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA's thermal system insulation definition expressly includes breeching insulation, along with pipes, fittings, boilers, tanks, and ducts.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101(b)","prompt":"Does OSHA's TSI definition include boiler breeching insulation?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0404"]},{"id":"cvi:neshap-notice","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Under the asbestos NESHAP, the owner or operator of a covered demolition or renovation gives the EPA Administrator written notice at least 10 working days before asbestos removal or demolition begins.","src":"40 CFR 61.145(b)","prompt":"Who files a NESHAP notice, with whom, and how far ahead?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0392"]},{"id":"cvi:neshap-thresholds","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"NESHAP renovation rules apply when regulated asbestos disturbed totals at least 260 linear feet on pipes, 160 square feet on other components, or 35 cubic feet where it could not be measured.","src":"40 CFR 61.145(a)(4)","prompt":"What amounts of regulated asbestos bring a renovation under NESHAP?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:rrp-scope","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"EPA's RRP rule applies to renovation for compensation in target housing (built before 1978) and child-occupied facilities.","src":"40 CFR 745.82; 745.103","prompt":"Where does the EPA lead RRP rule apply?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0405"]},{"id":"cvi:rrp-minor","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Minor HVAC work is exempt from RRP renovation rules if it disturbs 6 square feet or less of painted surface per room inside, or 20 square feet or less outside, with no prohibited practices, window replacement, or demolition.","src":"40 CFR 745.83","prompt":"What makes HVAC work in pre-1978 housing exempt minor repair under RRP?","trap":"The exemption applies whether or not a child under 6 lives there.","num":{"q":"RRP minor repair covers disturbing __ or less of interior painted surface per room.","a":"6 square feet","d":["20 square feet","160 square feet","4 square feet"]},"qs":["cvi-0405"]},{"id":"cvi:rrp-demolition","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Cutting painted drywall out cleanly is not demolition, but knocking the same hole out with a hammer is, and it loses the RRP minor repair exemption.","src":"40 CFR 745.83; EPA RRP FAQ 23002-18515","prompt":"Does knocking an opening in painted drywall with a hammer keep the RRP minor repair exemption?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0405"]},{"id":"cvi:rrp-30-day","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Non-emergency jobs in the same room within 30 days count as one job for the RRP minor repair limit, and a removed painted component counts in full.","src":"40 CFR 745.83","prompt":"How does RRP count several small jobs in one room?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:rrp-firm","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Under RRP, firms that perform renovations for compensation must be EPA-certified. The certification belongs to the renovating firm, not the building owner.","src":"40 CFR 745.89","prompt":"Who must hold RRP certification, the renovating firm or the owner?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0392"]},{"id":"cvi:histo-source","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"FACT","text":"Histoplasma grows in soil enriched with bird or bat droppings, and people get sick by breathing spores released when dry material is disturbed. NIOSH names HVAC installers and service workers as at risk.","src":"NIOSH Histoplasmosis; CDC histoplasmosis","prompt":"What causes histoplasmosis, and why are HVAC workers at risk?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0169","cvi-0406"]},{"id":"cvi:histo-wet","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"FACT","text":"NIOSH advises wetting bird or bat droppings rather than dry sweeping before removal. It describes a school outbreak spread through courtyard air intakes.","src":"NIOSH Histoplasmosis: Protecting Workers at Risk","prompt":"How should caked pigeon droppings at an intake be handled before removal?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0169"]},{"id":"cvi:hanta-source","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"FACT","text":"Hantavirus comes from rodent urine, droppings, and nesting material, not bird droppings. Cooling tower mist is a Legionella source, not a hantavirus source.","src":"CDC hantavirus; CDC Legionella","prompt":"Which HVAC contaminant sources carry hantavirus, histoplasmosis, and Legionella?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0169","cvi-0170","cvi-0406"]},{"id":"cvi:rodent-cleanup","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"FACT","text":"CDC says never sweep or vacuum rodent droppings or nests. Spray them with bleach solution or an EPA-registered disinfectant until very wet, let soak about 5 minutes, then wipe up with paper towels.","src":"CDC rodent clean-up guidance","prompt":"How does CDC say to clean fresh mouse droppings from a return plenum?","trap":"The normal ACR agitate-and-HEPA-vacuum method is wrong for fresh droppings before they are wetted.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0170","cvi-0407"]},{"id":"cvi:rodent-pros","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"FACT","text":"For rodents in ventilation systems, CDC points to a professional exterminator and duct cleaning specialists.","src":"CDC rodent clean-up guidance","prompt":"Whom does CDC point to for rodents in a ventilation system?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0407"]},{"id":"hvac:s-single-zone","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"DEF","text":"A single-zone system has one thermostat controlling one unit for one space. A constant-volume single-zone unit needs only trunk and branch ducts to diffusers downstream, with no VAV boxes, mixing boxes or zone dampers.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment; Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"What defines a single-zone system, and what sits downstream of a constant-volume single-zone unit?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0016"]},{"id":"hvac:s-cav-control","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"A constant air volume (CAV) system holds airflow fixed and changes the supply air temperature to match the load.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How does a constant air volume (CAV) system control space temperature?","trap":"VAV is the reverse: fixed supply temperature, varying airflow.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-cav-reheat","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"Constant-volume reheat sends a fixed volume of cold air down one main and adds heat at each zone terminal. It wastes energy because air cooled for the zone with the biggest cooling load gets reheated at every zone needing less.","src":"DOE Minimizing Reheat Guide","prompt":"How does a constant-volume reheat system meet falling zone loads, and why does it waste energy?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0411"]},{"id":"hvac:s-simultaneous-heat-cool","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"Constant-volume reheat, multizone and dual-duct systems inherently heat and cool at the same time, because each cools the air first and then reheats or remixes it.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Which system types inherently heat and cool at the same time?","trap":"Packaged rooftops with integrated economizers and VAV with static pressure reset are not on this list.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-reheat","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"DEF","text":"Reheat is heating air downstream of the cooling coil, at the unit or at a zone terminal, to meet zone demand or to warm air overcooled for dehumidification. Reheat coils are supply-side components.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is reheat, and where does a reheat coil sit?","trap":"A coil in the intake ahead of the cooling coil is a preheat coil, not reheat.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-trend-id","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"NOTE","text":"As zone load falls: VAV drops airflow while air stays near 55°F; constant-volume reheat holds airflow and warms air at the zone; a dual-duct box shows two inlets; a multizone blends at the unit.","src":"DOE Minimizing Reheat Guide; PNNL VAV O&M","prompt":"How do trend readings tell VAV, constant-volume reheat, dual duct and multizone apart as zone load falls?","trap":"Constant airflow with warming air from one 55°F main is CV reheat, not VAV.","qs":["cvi-0411"]},{"id":"hvac:s-doas","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"A dedicated outdoor air system (DOAS) conditions and dehumidifies only the outdoor air needed for ventilation. It has no return-air mixing box, and terminal units such as fan coils handle the space load.","src":"Trane Engineers Newsletter ADM-APN062","prompt":"What does a DOAS do, and what section does it lack compared with a rooftop unit?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0030"]},{"id":"hvac:s-active-beam","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"An active chilled beam uses ducted primary air through nozzles to induce room air over its coil. A passive beam is a ceiling coil working by natural convection with no duct connection.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How does an active chilled beam differ from a passive one?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-ufad","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"Underfloor air distribution (UFAD) supplies air through a pressurized raised-floor plenum and floor diffusers, so the raised floor works as a positive-pressure supply plenum.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment; NADCA Open Air Plenums white paper","prompt":"How does underfloor air distribution (UFAD) deliver supply air?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-mua","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"DEF","text":"A make-up air unit supplies tempered outdoor air to replace air removed by large exhausts such as kitchens, labs and paint booths.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a make-up air unit do?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-control","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A variable air volume (VAV) system holds supply air at a fixed temperature, typically about 55°F, and each box throttles airflow to match its zone load.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How does a VAV system control space temperature, and at what typical supply temperature?","trap":"CAV is the reverse: fixed airflow, varying supply temperature.","num":{"q":"A typical VAV cooling supply air setpoint is about __.","a":"55°F","d":["44°F","68°F","72°F"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-fan-vfd","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"As VAV boxes close and duct static rises, a VFD slows the supply fan to hold duct static pressure at a sensor down the main. Supply air temperature is a separate control loop.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"As VAV boxes close, what is the supply fan controlled to hold, and how?","trap":"The fan does not hold a fixed rpm, a supply temperature or building static.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-bypass-vav","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A bypass VAV system pairs a constant-volume fan with VAV boxes and dumps surplus supply air to the return through a bypass damper, keeping fan airflow steady. It is common on small systems without a VFD.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does the bypass damper in a bypass VAV system do?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-box","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"DEF","text":"A VAV box is a supply-side terminal that varies conditioned airflow to one zone. A single-duct box is an inlet damper, actuator and controller, plus an inlet flow sensor if pressure-independent, sometimes with a reheat coil.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is a single-duct VAV box and what does it contain?","trap":"An induction opening to the plenum belongs to fan-powered boxes, not a plain single-duct box.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-pressure-independent","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A pressure-independent VAV box reads a velocity-pressure flow sensor, a cross or ring of tubing at its own inlet collar, so it holds its airflow setpoint whatever the inlet static does.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a pressure-independent VAV box read to hold its airflow setpoint?","trap":"The duct static sensor two-thirds down the main controls the fan, not the box.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-pressure-dependent","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A pressure-dependent VAV box has no flow sensor, so at a fixed damper angle its airflow rises and falls with the inlet duct pressure.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How does a pressure-dependent VAV box's airflow behave?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-flow-sensor-fouling","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"If a VAV inlet flow sensor is bent, coated or has plugged ports, for example from encapsulant, the controller misreads airflow and mispositions the damper, overfeeding or starving the zone. Protect flow sensors during cleaning.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What happens when a VAV box's inlet flow sensor is coated or plugged?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-minimum","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"VAV boxes throttle to a set minimum airflow instead of closing fully, so the zone still gets ventilation air when its cooling load is gone.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Why do VAV boxes stop at a minimum airflow instead of closing fully?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-cooling-only-overcool","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A cooling-only VAV box at minimum flow can still overcool its zone, because the primary air is still about 55°F; volume drops but temperature does not. Such zones get reheat or a fan box.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Why can a cooling-only VAV box at minimum flow still overcool its zone?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-reheat-sequence","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"As zone load falls, a single-duct VAV reheat box throttles to minimum airflow; at setpoint the zone is satisfied. If the zone keeps cooling below setpoint, the reheat coil energizes at that minimum flow.","src":"DOE Minimizing Reheat Guide","prompt":"What is the single-duct VAV reheat sequence as zone load falls?","trap":"Reheat never runs with the damper wide open or fully closed.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-damper-fail","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A VAV damper that fails to close, stuck open or with a failed actuator, overfeeds its zone. A box reading minimum while its space keeps overcooling points to the damper or actuator.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a VAV box at minimum position with its space overcooling suggest?","trap":"Rule out design first: a cooling-only box still delivers 55°F air at a true minimum.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-leaking-reheat-valve","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"If air leaving a VAV box's coil is warmer than the primary air while the box passes maximum cooling airflow, the hot water reheat valve is leaking or stuck open.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"What does air leaving a VAV reheat coil warmer than primary air, at maximum cooling airflow, indicate?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0019"]},{"id":"hvac:s-open-box-cleaning","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"Before cleaning duct downstream of a VAV box, command its damper open through the BAS or box controller so air and tools can pass, note the original state, and restore it afterward.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How should a VAV box damper be set before cleaning the duct downstream of it?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-primary-air","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"DEF","text":"Primary air is the air sent from the central AHU through the main duct. Secondary or induced air is what a fan-powered box pulls from the ceiling plenum.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does 'primary air' mean at a terminal unit?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-series-fan","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"In a series fan-powered box the fan sits in the primary air path, runs continuously through occupied hours, and moves all the air delivered to the zone.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"Where does the fan sit in a series fan-powered box, and when does it run?","trap":"A fan that runs only on heating calls is the parallel box.","qs":["cvi-0412"]},{"id":"hvac:s-series-constant","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"A series fan-powered box delivers constant discharge airflow; as primary air varies, the fan makes up the difference with induced plenum air, so only the primary-to-plenum ratio changes.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"What discharge airflow does a series fan-powered box deliver as primary air varies?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0412"]},{"id":"hvac:s-parallel-fan","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"In a parallel fan-powered box the fan sits beside the primary air path and runs only on a heating call. In cooling, discharge airflow simply tracks the primary air.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"Where does the fan sit in a parallel fan-powered box, and when does it run?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0412"]},{"id":"hvac:s-parallel-backdraft","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"A parallel fan-powered box has a backdraft damper on the fan outlet, so cold primary air cannot spill backward through the idle fan into the ceiling plenum during cooling.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"What stops primary air from flowing backward through the idle fan of a parallel box?","trap":"The plenum inlet filter loads with dust but does not block reverse flow.","qs":["cvi-0021","cvi-0219"]},{"id":"hvac:s-series-low-static","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"A central fan serving series boxes can run at lower duct static than one serving parallel boxes, because primary air only has to reach each box fan, which is sized for the total airflow.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"Why can the central fan run at lower duct static with series fan-powered boxes?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0022"]},{"id":"hvac:s-plenum-free-heat","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"In heating, a fan-powered box recovers free heat by pulling ceiling plenum air already warmed by lights and roof gain, before any reheat is needed.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Where does the free heat a fan-powered box uses in heating mode come from?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-induction-filter","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"A fan-powered box has a small filter on its plenum induction inlet. When it loads with dust the box fan starves and the zone can run cold or noisy.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What cleaning-relevant part does a fan-powered VAV box have, and what happens when it clogs?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-series-backward","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"If primary air reaches a series box while its fan is off, the forward-curved wheel spins backward. The box fan must be energized before any primary air flows.","src":"Krueger white paper, Anti-Backward Rotation for SFPTUs","prompt":"What prevents a series fan-powered box's fan from spinning backward?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0020"]},{"id":"hvac:s-multizone","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"A multizone AHU has a hot deck and a cold deck with zone dampers at the unit, mixing deck air in different proportions for each zone before it leaves the unit.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Where and how does a multizone system mix air for its zones?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-multizone-ducts","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"Each multizone zone damper set discharges into its own duct, so a multizone unit serving eight zones has eight supply ducts leaving it, each carrying air already mixed.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How many supply ducts leave a multizone unit serving eight zones?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A multizone unit serving eight zones has __ supply ducts leaving it.","a":"eight","d":["one","two","sixteen"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-dual-duct","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"A dual-duct system runs separate hot and cold ducts from the unit's decks to every zone, where a terminal mixing box blends them.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a dual-duct system deliver to each zone?","trap":"Multizone mixes at the unit and runs one duct per zone; dual duct mixes at the zone box.","qs":["cvi-0218"]},{"id":"hvac:s-dual-duct-terminal","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"A dual-duct terminal is recognized by two inlet collars, one fed from the hot deck and one from the cold deck; it blends them for the zone, and VAV versions also vary the volume.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"What makes a dual-duct terminal recognizable?","trap":"Fan-powered and reheat boxes have one inlet.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-dual-duct-fault","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"In a dual-duct building, one zone running hot in summer while its neighbors are comfortable and the cold duct is at design points to that terminal's hot-air inlet damper stuck open.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"In a dual-duct building, what fault makes one zone run hot while its neighbors are fine?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0018"]},{"id":"hvac:s-multizone-waste","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"Multizone and dual-duct systems waste energy because they make hot air and cold air and then blend them for each zone, heating and cooling at the same time.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Why do multizone and dual-duct systems waste energy?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-multizone-leak","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"A multizone zone commanded to full cooling that reads warmer than the cold deck has hot-deck air leaking past its damper. Hot share = (zone minus cold deck) divided by (hot deck minus cold deck).","src":"USACE ERDC-CERL Multizone Tech Note","prompt":"How do you spot and estimate hot-deck leakage into a multizone zone set to full cooling?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0422"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-coil","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"DEF","text":"A fan coil unit is a local blower, a hydronic or DX coil and a filter serving one space, with little or no duct.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is a fan coil unit and what does it contain?","trap":"A compressor or a VAV damper is not part of a standard fan coil.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-two-four-pipe","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"A two-pipe fan coil system carries either hot or chilled water, not both, so it changes over by season. A four-pipe system has separate hot and chilled water and can heat one unit while cooling another.","src":"Krueger Fan Coil Engineering","prompt":"How do two-pipe and four-pipe fan coil systems differ?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0024"]},{"id":"hvac:s-ptac","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"DEF","text":"A packaged terminal air conditioner (PTAC) is a self-contained through-wall unit serving one room, such as a hotel room, with its whole refrigeration circuit in the wall sleeve.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is a packaged terminal air conditioner (PTAC)?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-pthp","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"DEF","text":"A packaged terminal heat pump (PTHP) is a PTAC that uses reverse-cycle refrigeration as its main heat source. A plain PTAC heats with hot water, steam or electric resistance.","src":"10 CFR 431.92","prompt":"What distinguishes a PTHP from a PTAC?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0027"]},{"id":"hvac:s-ptac-slinger","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"Many PTACs have no drain: a slinger ring on the condenser fan throws base-pan condensate onto the hot condenser coil to evaporate, so some water in the outdoor-side pan is normal in cooling.","src":"Friedrich PTAC service manual","prompt":"What normally removes condensate from a PTAC's outdoor-side base pan?","trap":"Sizing a trap to negative static belongs to draw-through air handlers, not PTACs.","qs":["cvi-0026"]},{"id":"hvac:s-wshp-loop","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"In a boiler-tower water-source heat pump system, the units share one closed water loop; the boiler adds heat when the loop runs cold and the tower rejects heat when it runs warm.","src":"Armstrong WSHP application guide; 10 CFR 431.92","prompt":"In a boiler-tower water-source heat pump system, what do the boiler and tower do?","trap":"The loop is a heat source and sink for the units, not chilled water for air coils.","qs":["cvi-0028"]},{"id":"hvac:s-wshp-access","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 counts water-source heat pumps and fan-coil units as ventilation system components that need access doors or panels sized for inspection and maintenance, even above a hard ceiling.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.19.2","prompt":"What does ASHRAE 62.1 require for access to ceiling-hung water-source heat pumps?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0017"]},{"id":"hvac:s-induction-unit","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"Induction units have no fans: high-pressure primary air through small nozzles creates a venturi that pulls room air over the unit's coil, so lint on the coil and nozzles is the inspection target.","src":"Energy Efficiency Manual 4.9, Induction Systems","prompt":"How does an induction unit move room air over its coil?","trap":"Fan coils, unit ventilators and PTACs all have their own fans.","qs":["cvi-0023"]},{"id":"hvac:s-mini-split","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"DEF","text":"A ductless mini-split has an outdoor condensing unit and one or more wall or ceiling indoor heads, each with its own fan and coil, joined by refrigerant lines.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is a ductless mini-split and its indoor unit?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-mini-split-blower","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"In a ductless wall head the cross-flow blower wheel sits right behind the wet coil and pan, a classic growth site that can throw musty black specks into the room even when the mesh filters look clean.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is the likely source of black specks and musty odor from a ductless wall head with clean filters?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0007"]},{"id":"hvac:s-multi-split","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"A multi-split has one outdoor unit and two or more indoor units that run independently, so each head counts as its own air-side unit with its own fan, coil and condensate pan.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M definitions","prompt":"How should the indoor heads of a multi-split system be counted in a scope?","trap":"There is no shared duct system or zoning damper to count.","qs":["cvi-0008"]},{"id":"hvac:s-ahu-path","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"Air through a typical draw-through AHU runs mixing box, filters, preheat coil if present, cooling coil, supply fan, then supply duct. Filters come ahead of the coils to protect them.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is the order of air through a typical draw-through AHU?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-mixing-box","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"DEF","text":"The mixing box, or mixed air plenum, is where the outdoor air and return air dampers meet and the two streams blend before the filters.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What happens in an AHU mixing box?","trap":"Hot deck and cold deck air combining for each zone is a multizone unit, not a mixing box.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-preheat","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A preheat coil sits ahead of the cooling coil in cold climates or high outdoor air units, warming cold intake air to protect the cooling coil from freezing.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a preheat coil do and where does it sit?","trap":"A coil downstream of the cooling coil is reheat.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-draw-through","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"In a draw-through AHU the fan sits downstream of the coils and pulls air through them, so the coil section and its drain pan run under negative pressure.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Where is the fan in a draw-through AHU, and what pressure does the coil section see?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-blow-through","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"In a blow-through AHU the fan sits upstream of the coils and pushes air through them, so the coil section runs under positive pressure.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Where is the fan in a blow-through AHU, and what pressure does the coil section see?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-blow-through-wet","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"In a blow-through unit, air leaving the cooling coil is at or near saturation and goes straight into whatever sits downstream, so final filters right after the coil can soak. In draw-through units, fan heat moves air away from saturation.","src":"HPAC Engineering, The Case of the Wet Filters","prompt":"Why can final filters just after the cooling coil in a blow-through AHU get soaked?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0036"]},{"id":"hvac:s-return-relief-fans","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A return fan runs whenever the supply fan runs and pressurizes the return plenum, so leaks there push air out. A relief fan runs only when building pressure needs relief, usually in economizer mode.","src":"Trane Engineers Newsletter ADM-APN003","prompt":"How does a return fan differ from a relief fan?","trap":"With a relief fan system, the return plenum runs negative while the relief fan is off.","qs":["cvi-0037"]},{"id":"hvac:s-oa-intake","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"An outdoor air intake must sit away from exhaust discharges, cooling towers, loading docks and vents, or it pulls contaminated or moist air into the supply.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1 (outdoor air intake separation)","prompt":"Where should an outdoor air intake be located, and what is the risk of a bad location?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-humidifier-types","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A steam humidifier, the commercial norm, injects steam through dispersion tubes in the airstream. An evaporative wetted-media humidifier holds a wet pad and sump that can grow organisms into the airstream.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How do steam and evaporative AHU humidifiers work, and which is the growth concern?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-ultrasonic","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"Ultrasonic and impeller humidifiers spray dissolved tank minerals, and any microorganisms, into the air, leaving fine white dust; low-mineral water is the fix.","src":"EPA, Use and Care of Home Humidifiers","prompt":"What causes fine white dust near an ultrasonic humidifier fed with tap water?","trap":"Steam and evaporative types are not expected to disperse significant minerals.","qs":["cvi-0050"]},{"id":"hvac:s-absorption-distance","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires turning vanes, volume dampers, duct offsets over 15 degrees and air cleaners downstream of a humidifier to sit at or beyond its absorption distance, or unabsorbed moisture wets them.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.8.2","prompt":"What ASHRAE 62.1 rule governs obstructions downstream of a humidifier?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0049"]},{"id":"hvac:s-drain-pan","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"DEF","text":"The drain pan sits under the cooling coil to collect condensate and send it out the drain line. Pans and drain lines are AHU components inspected with the coil section.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a drain pan do, and which component group is it part of?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-pan-slope","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires drain pans to slope at least 1/8 inch per foot toward the outlet, or otherwise drain freely, with the outlet at the pan's lowest point.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.7.1","prompt":"What slope does ASHRAE 62.1 require of a drain pan?","trap":"1-1/2 inch deep and 3 inches larger are the IRC auxiliary pan, not the 62.1 slope.","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires drain pans to slope at least __ per foot toward the drain.","a":"1/8 inch","d":["1/2 inch","1-1/2 inches","3 inches"]},"qs":["cvi-0051","cvi-0137","cvi-0201","cvi-0258"]},{"id":"hvac:s-draw-through-trap","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A draw-through pan sits below room pressure, so its drain needs a P-trap or other seal. Without one, suction pulls air up the drain, so the pan cannot drain while the fan runs and can overflow.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.7.3","prompt":"Why does a draw-through unit's drain pan need a trap?","trap":"Not because every gravity drain needs a trap by code.","qs":["cvi-0031"]},{"id":"hvac:s-trap-height","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A common coil-maker rule: for negative pressure, trap height H is the maximum negative static plus 1 inch, so a minus 2.0 in. w.c. pan needs 3.0 inches; for positive pressure, H is static plus 1/2 inch.","src":"Marlo Coil, Condensate Drain Trapping","prompt":"How tall must a condensate trap be for negative and for positive pressure pans?","trap":"","num":{"q":"By the coil-maker rule, the minimum trap height H for a draw-through pan section at minus 2.0 in. w.c. is __.","a":"3.0 inches","d":["1.5 inches","2.0 inches","2.5 inches"]},"qs":["cvi-0032"]},{"id":"hvac:s-float-switch","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A float switch in the drain pan locks the unit out when the water rises too high, before the pan overflows onto the ceiling.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a float switch in a condensate drain pan do?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-overflow-protection","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"CODE","text":"Where overflow would damage the building, the IRC requires an auxiliary pan at least 1-1/2 inches deep with its own drain, a separate overflow drain, or a water-level shutoff. An overflow tied into the primary drain does not count.","src":"IRC M1411 (auxiliary drain pan); IMC 307.2.3","prompt":"What overflow protection does the IRC require for a cooling coil above finished space?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The minimum depth the IRC sets for an auxiliary drain pan is __.","a":"1-1/2 inches","d":["1/8 inch","3/4 inch","3 inches"]},"qs":["cvi-0010"]},{"id":"hvac:s-economizer","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"DEF","text":"An economizer modulates the outdoor and return air dampers to cool with outdoor air when conditions allow, which is called free cooling.","src":"PNNL, Air-Side Economizer O&M Best Practices","prompt":"What does an air-side economizer do?","trap":"Recovering exhaust heat into the supply is an ERV or HRV, not an economizer.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-econ-drybulb-enthalpy","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"A dry-bulb economizer judges outdoor air by temperature alone, so it admits cool but very humid air. Differential enthalpy control compares the total heat, including moisture, of outdoor and return air.","src":"PNNL, Air-Side Economizer O&M Best Practices","prompt":"Why is a dry-bulb economizer weak in a humid climate, and what does enthalpy control add?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0034"]},{"id":"hvac:s-econ-integrated","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"An integrated economizer keeps outdoor-air free cooling running alongside mechanical cooling: outdoor air is the first stage, and the compressor or chiller adds the rest while the damper stays open.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does an integrated economizer mean?","trap":"It does not lock out whenever the compressor runs.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-econ-stuck-open","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"An economizer or outdoor air damper stuck wide open in winter raises the heating load, and outdoor air below 32°F can freeze and split a hydronic coil.","src":"PNNL, Air-Side Economizer O&M Best Practices","prompt":"What does an economizer stuck at full outdoor air in winter cause?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0035"]},{"id":"hvac:s-oa-fraction","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"Mixed air temperature is the weighted average of outdoor and return air, so outdoor air fraction = (mixed minus return) divided by (outdoor minus return). Example: 20% outdoor air at 30°F with return at 70°F mixes to 62°F.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"How do you find the outdoor air fraction from outdoor, return and mixed air temperatures?","trap":"Dividing by mixed minus outdoor is a common slip that overstates outdoor air.","qs":["cvi-0413"]},{"id":"hvac:s-econ-readings","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"Compare the outdoor air fraction calculated from temperatures with the damper command; they should track within about 25 percent. A damper commanded open while mixed air sits near return points to blades, linkage or actuator.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"How can temperature readings show an economizer damper is not doing what the BAS commands?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0415"]},{"id":"hvac:s-min-oa-fixed","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"In a VAV unit, a minimum outdoor air damper locked in one position lets outdoor airflow fall roughly in step with supply airflow as the fan slows. Holding the minimum takes active outdoor air control.","src":"CEC Nonres. Compliance Manual 2016 §4.3","prompt":"What happens to outdoor airflow through a fixed minimum damper as a VAV fan slows?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0414"]},{"id":"hvac:s-relief-air","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"DEF","text":"Relief air is surplus return air spilled outdoors through a relief damper or fan when the economizer brings in extra outdoor air. Exhaust air is pulled from restrooms, kitchens and labs by dedicated exhaust fans.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How does relief air differ from exhaust air?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-erv-hrv","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"An energy recovery ventilator (ERV) transfers moisture as well as sensible heat between exhaust and outdoor air. A heat recovery ventilator (HRV) transfers sensible heat only.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How does an ERV differ from an HRV?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-hrv-drain","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"An HRV needs a condensate drain because its core moves only heat, so moisture in warm exhaust air condenses on the cold core in winter; many ERVs do not. Paper ERV cores are vacuumed, never washed.","src":"Broan HRV/ERV installation guide; RenewAire EV manual","prompt":"Why does an HRV need a condensate drain when many ERVs do not?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0014"]},{"id":"hvac:s-enthalpy-wheel","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"An enthalpy wheel is a desiccant rotor that carries sensible heat and moisture between exhaust and outdoor air. Its purge section flushes the rotor with outdoor air to cut exhaust carryover into the supply.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does an enthalpy wheel transfer, and what does its purge section do?","trap":"Sealed aluminum plate cores transfer sensible heat only.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-wheel-leakage","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"A wheel leaks exhaust air into the supply through seal leakage and carryover, and a higher static on the exhaust side forces more across. Keep the supply side at the higher pressure.","src":"ASHRAE, Practical Guidance for Epidemic Operation of ERVs","prompt":"Why would restroom odor appear in supply air from an enthalpy wheel?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0033"]},{"id":"hvac:s-runaround","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"A runaround loop is two coils joined by pumped fluid, one in each airstream, so the airstreams stay completely separate even in different air handlers; wheels always pass some air across.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Which energy recovery device keeps the two airstreams completely separate, even at a distance?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-function","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"DEF","text":"Coils are heat exchangers that temper, meaning heat or cool, and dehumidify the air passing through them.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What do coils in an HVAC system do?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-dx-coil","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"A direct expansion (DX) coil is the evaporator itself: refrigerant boils inside its tubes. It is recognized by a distributor feeding many small tubes and a large insulated suction line.","src":"Marlo Coil, How to Identify the Four Main Types of Coils","prompt":"What is a DX coil and how is it recognized?","trap":"Chilled and hot water coils use supply and return headers, not a distributor.","qs":["cvi-0043"]},{"id":"hvac:s-evap-cond","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"In cooling, the evaporator (indoor coil) boils low-pressure refrigerant at about 40°F, absorbing heat and moisture from the air. The condenser (outdoor coil) rejects that heat plus compressor work, turning vapor back to liquid.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What do the evaporator and the condenser do in the cooling cycle?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-chilled-water","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"In a chilled water system a central chiller makes water around 44°F that is pumped through the AHU coil (CHWS and CHWR). Condenser water (CWS and CWR) links the chiller and cooling tower and does not feed the AHU coil.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What feeds a chilled water cooling coil, and where does condenser water go?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0029"]},{"id":"hvac:s-water-steam-coils","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"A hydronic heating coil carries hot water from a boiler. Steam coils are a separate type, and distributing (tube-in-tube) steam coils spread steam evenly to resist freezing where air can fall below 40°F.","src":"Marlo Coil, How to Identify the Four Main Types of Coils","prompt":"What feeds a hydronic heating coil, and why use a distributing steam coil for preheat?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0044"]},{"id":"hvac:s-ton","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"DEF","text":"One ton of cooling equals 12,000 BTU per hour, the heat needed to melt a ton of ice in 24 hours.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How many BTU per hour is one ton of cooling?","trap":"3,412 BTU per hour is one kilowatt, not one ton.","num":{"q":"One ton of cooling equals __.","a":"12,000 BTU/h","d":["3,412 BTU/h","24,000 BTU/h","60,000 BTU/h"]},"qs":["cvi-0090"]},{"id":"hvac:s-cfm-per-ton","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"The rule of thumb for DX coil airflow is about 400 cfm per ton, 350 to 450 by humidity design, so a 3-ton system moves about 1,200 cfm. Too little air freezes the coil; too much hurts dehumidification.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is the rule-of-thumb airflow per ton across a DX cooling coil?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Rule-of-thumb airflow across a DX cooling coil is about __ per ton.","a":"400 cfm","d":["100 cfm","200 cfm","800 cfm"]},"qs":["cvi-0090"]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-delta-t","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"A healthy DX cooling coil drops air temperature about 15 to 20°F. A bigger split at a similar load usually means low airflow, such as a loaded coil or dirty filter.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is the typical temperature drop across a healthy DX coil, and what does a larger split suggest?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A healthy DX cooling coil typically drops air temperature about __.","a":"15 to 20°F","d":["5 to 8°F","30 to 35°F","40 to 45°F"]},"qs":["cvi-0092"]},{"id":"hvac:s-face-velocity","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"Cooling coils are sized for roughly 400 to 550 fpm face velocity, about 500 fpm. Faster air blows condensate off the fins into the duct and liner past the drain pan.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Why is cooling coil face velocity kept around 500 fpm?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Cooling coil face velocity is normally kept around __ to avoid condensate carryover.","a":"500 fpm","d":["50 fpm","900 fpm","4,005 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-rows","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"Adding coil rows or fins per inch raises capacity and air pressure drop and gives dirt more places to bridge, so deep coils are harder to clean and are cleaned from both faces.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does adding rows or fins per inch to a coil do?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-fin-straightening","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"Fin straightening tools restore bent fins so air passes evenly across the coil; bent fins block airflow.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What do fin straightening tools do?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-loading","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"As a coil loads, its air pressure drop rises and airflow falls in a constant-volume system, cutting capacity and risking coil freeze. Comparing coil pressure drop before and after cleaning shows the change.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a rising pressure drop across a cooling coil do, and how is cleaning improvement shown?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-leaking-heat-valve","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"A temperature rise across an AHU heating coil whose valve is commanded closed means a leaking hot water valve; the cooling valve then opens to remove that heat, heating and cooling at once.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"What does a temperature rise across a heating coil with its valve commanded closed indicate?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0421"]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-access","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"NOTE","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires access to the upstream face of coils four rows deep or less, and to both faces above four rows. ACR 2025 requires access to both sides of every coil for cleaning.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.19.3; ACR 2025 §4.11","prompt":"What coil access does ASHRAE 62.1 require, and how does ACR 2025 differ?","trap":"A 62.1-compliant 4-row coil with one door can still need a second door for an ACR cleaning scope.","qs":["cvi-0045"]},{"id":"hvac:s-split-system","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"DEF","text":"A split system has an indoor assembly holding the indoor coil, with its own blower or relying on a furnace blower, and a separate outdoor assembly with the outdoor coil, joined by refrigerant lines.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M definitions","prompt":"What is a split system?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-packaged-unit","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A packaged (single-package) unit, such as a rooftop unit, holds the compressor, both coils, the fan and often heat in one cabinet, so its evaporator coil, blower and drain pan are inspected at that outdoor cabinet.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M definitions","prompt":"What does a packaged unit contain, and where are its evaporator coil and drain pan?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0000"]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-only","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A cased coil on a furnace with no blower of its own relies on the furnace's circulating blower to move indoor air across it, so the blower compartment is part of the same air-side inspection.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M definitions","prompt":"On a split system with a coil-only cabinet on a furnace, what moves air across the coil?","trap":"The inducer moves flue gas and the condenser fan moves outdoor air; neither moves supply air.","qs":["cvi-0001"]},{"id":"hvac:s-furnace-coil-location","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"The cooling coil goes on a furnace's supply side, since the IRC bars coils upstream of the heat exchanger unless listed. An upflow furnace has the coil on top; a downflow furnace, below it on the supply plenum.","src":"IRC M1411 (cooling coil location)","prompt":"Where is the cooling coil on an upflow versus a downflow furnace, and why?","trap":"The top of a downflow furnace is its return side.","qs":["cvi-0009"]},{"id":"hvac:s-high-limit","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A furnace high-limit switch opens when the heat exchanger area overheats from low airflow, such as a dirty filter, closed registers or a failed blower; it shuts the gas valve while the blower keeps running.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"When does a furnace high-limit switch open, and why?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-cracked-hx","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A cracked furnace heat exchanger is dangerous because it can pass flue gas, including carbon monoxide, into the supply air; the exchanger is what separates combustion gas from house air.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Why is a cracked furnace heat exchanger dangerous?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-zoning-bypass","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"In a zoned house, a bypass duct with a weighted damper dumps supply air into the return. With one small zone calling, cold air is reconditioned, return air cools and the indoor coil can freeze.","src":"JLC, Troubleshooting Zone Damper Systems","prompt":"How can a zoning bypass duct lead to a frozen indoor coil?","trap":"The bypass exists to relieve static when zones close, not to add it.","qs":["cvi-0011"]},{"id":"hvac:s-reversing-valve","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A heat pump's reversing valve changes refrigerant flow direction. In heating the indoor coil becomes the condenser, so it runs warm and dry and its drain pan makes no condensate.","src":"DOE Energy Saver, Air-Source Heat Pumps","prompt":"What does a heat pump's reversing valve do to the indoor coil in heating mode?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0002"]},{"id":"hvac:s-defrost","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"Defrost briefly switches a heat pump to cooling mode so hot gas melts frost off the outdoor coil. The outdoor fan stops, the coil may steam, and auxiliary heat keeps the supply air warm.","src":"Carrier, What Is Auxiliary Heat","prompt":"What happens during a heat pump defrost cycle?","trap":"Emergency heat shuts the heat pump off, so the outdoor coil would not steam.","qs":["cvi-0003"]},{"id":"hvac:s-aux-heat","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"Auxiliary heat is the heat pump's electric strips, added automatically alongside the compressor when it falls behind below the balance point, and during defrost.","src":"Carrier, What Is Auxiliary Heat","prompt":"What is heat pump auxiliary heat and when does it run?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0004"]},{"id":"hvac:s-emergency-heat","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"Emergency heat is a manual thermostat setting that shuts the heat pump's compressor off and runs the resistance strips alone, which costs far more to operate.","src":"Carrier, What Is Auxiliary Heat","prompt":"What does the EM HEAT setting on a heat pump thermostat do?","trap":"Aux heat is automatic and runs alongside the compressor; emergency heat is manual and replaces it.","qs":["cvi-0004"]},{"id":"hvac:s-balance-point","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"DEF","text":"A heat pump's balance point is the outdoor temperature where its heating output equals the building's heat loss. Below it, supplemental heat, strips or a furnace in dual fuel, is staged on.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is a heat pump's balance point?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-dual-fuel","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"In a dual-fuel system the heat pump and gas furnace take turns: the heat pump carries mild weather and the furnace takes over when it gets cold, typically near the balance point.","src":"Trane, Dual Fuel Heat Pump glossary","prompt":"How do the heat pump and furnace share the load in a dual-fuel system?","trap":"They do not run together; running alongside the compressor is how electric aux strips work.","qs":["cvi-0005"]},{"id":"hvac:s-geothermal","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A typical water-to-air ground-source (geothermal) heat pump has no outdoor unit: buried loop pipes feed an indoor cabinet holding the compressor, coil, blower and drain pan, inspected like any air handler.","src":"DOE Energy Saver, Geothermal Heat Pumps; 10 CFR 431.92","prompt":"What does a ground-source heat pump look like, and how is its air side inspected?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0006"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-fc","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A forward-curved centrifugal (squirrel cage) fan, with many small cupped blades curved toward rotation, is typical in residential furnaces and small air handlers; its blades load with dust.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook, ch. 2","prompt":"Where is a forward-curved centrifugal fan typically found?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0039"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-bi","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A backward-inclined fan has blades tilted away from rotation, flat, curved or airfoil, and is common in larger AHUs; its low angle of impingement lets dust build up on the blades.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook, ch. 2","prompt":"How is a backward-inclined fan recognized, and what cleaning issue does it have?","trap":"Radial blades are the type whose flat shape limits buildup.","qs":["cvi-0039"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-plenum","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A plenum (plug) fan is a centrifugal wheel with no scroll housing that pressurizes the whole surrounding cabinet section, so air can leave in several directions.","src":"Trane Engineers Newsletter ADM-APN036","prompt":"What is a plenum fan and what does it do to its cabinet?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0041"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-axial","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A vaneaxial fan is a tubeaxial fan with fixed outlet vanes that straighten swirl for medium to high pressure duty. A tubeaxial is a propeller in a cylinder; a propeller fan moves high flow at low pressure.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook, ch. 2","prompt":"How do vaneaxial, tubeaxial and propeller fans differ?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0042"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fc-overload","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A forward-curved fan's power rises steadily with airflow, so cleaning a loaded wheel or removing a duct restriction can raise motor amps toward nameplate and risk overload.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook, ch. 2","prompt":"Why can motor amps rise after a forward-curved blower is cleaned and restrictions removed?","trap":"Backward-inclined fans are non-overloading; their power falls off at high airflow.","qs":["cvi-0040"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-law-flow","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Fan airflow varies directly with fan speed: a fan moving 8,000 cfm at 900 RPM moves about 9,600 cfm at 1,080 RPM.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook","prompt":"How does fan airflow change with fan speed?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A fan moving 8,000 cfm at 900 RPM, sped up to 1,080 RPM, moves about __.","a":"9,600 cfm","d":["6,700 cfm","11,500 cfm","13,800 cfm"]},"qs":["cvi-0087"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-law-pressure","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Fan static pressure varies with the square of fan speed, so doubling speed doubles airflow but makes static pressure about four times higher.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook","prompt":"Per the fan laws, what does doubling fan speed do to static pressure?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Doubling fan speed makes static pressure about __ as high.","a":"4 times","d":["2 times","8 times","16 times"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-law-power","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Fan power varies with the cube of speed: doubling speed takes about eight times the power, and even a 20 percent speed-up raises 5.0 bhp to about 8.6 bhp, which can overload the motor.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook","prompt":"Per the fan laws, how does fan power change with speed?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Doubling fan speed raises fan power about __.","a":"8 times","d":["2 times","4 times","16 times"]},"qs":["cvi-0088"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-half-speed","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Per the fan laws, cutting fan speed in half cuts fan power to about one-eighth, which is why VFDs and VAV save fan energy as boxes throttle.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook","prompt":"Per the fan laws, what does halving fan speed do to fan power?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Cutting fan speed in half reduces fan power to about __ of the original.","a":"one-eighth","d":["one-half","one-quarter","one-sixteenth"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-vfd","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"DEF","text":"A variable frequency drive (VFD) controls fan output by changing the motor's supply frequency, so speed follows. Inlet guide vanes and discharge dampers are the older methods.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How does a variable frequency drive control fan output?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-ecm","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A constant-airflow ECM is a variable-speed motor that ramps up to hold its programmed cfm as static rises, drawing more watts, until it hits its limit and airflow falls. A PSC motor simply loses airflow.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How does a constant-airflow ECM blower respond to rising static pressure?","trap":"Direct-drive ECMs have no belts or sheaves to blame.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-belt-drive","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"On a belt-driven fan the adjustable motor sheave sets fan speed. Slipping or worn belts and worn or misaligned sheaves cut speed and airflow, so check them first when airflow is low.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What sets a belt-driven fan's speed, and what is checked first when its airflow is low?","trap":"Belt tension affects slip and wear, not the design drive ratio.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-dirty-wheel","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A dirty blower wheel moves less air at the same RPM because dust fills its cupped blades; cleaning the wheel is often the biggest airflow gain on a duct cleaning job.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a dirty blower wheel most directly cause?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-system-resistance","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Adding resistance, such as closing supply dampers to balance a constant-volume system, raises system static pressure and moves the fan back on its curve to lower airflow.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"In a constant-volume system, what does closing supply dampers do to the fan?","trap":"Fan power does not fall in proportion and total airflow does not rise.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-dampers-balancing","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"DEF","text":"Dampers are air-directional devices that control or direct airflow. A balancing damper is set during test and balance to fix a branch's airflow and then locked; it does not modulate zone temperature.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What do dampers do, and what is a balancing damper for?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-backdraft","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"DEF","text":"A backdraft (gravity) damper is a flap with no actuator that lets air pass one way and stops reverse flow, used on exhaust discharges and intakes.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What is a backdraft damper?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0015"]},{"id":"hvac:s-opposed-blade","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"Opposed-blade dampers give a more linear flow response and are preferred for modulating duty; parallel-blade dampers are fine for two-position (open or closed) duty.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"Why are opposed-blade dampers preferred for modulating?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-face-bypass","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"Face-and-bypass dampers control a coil's output by routing part of the air around the coil while the water or steam flow stays constant; they are common on preheat and steam coils.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How do face-and-bypass dampers control coil output?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-fire-damper","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"A fire damper closes when its fusible link melts at a set temperature. It responds to heat, not to a smoke detector, alarm or BAS signal.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What closes a fire damper?","trap":"Closing on a detector signal is the smoke damper.","qs":["cvi-0100"]},{"id":"hvac:s-smoke-damper","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"A smoke damper is closed by an actuator driven by a smoke detector or smoke-control signal, not by a fusible link.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What closes a smoke damper?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-combination-damper","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"DEF","text":"A combination fire and smoke damper does both jobs, closing on heat like a fire damper and on a detection signal like a smoke damper; plan legends often tag it FSD.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a combination fire and smoke damper do?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0015"]},{"id":"hvac:s-static-dynamic","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"A static-rated fire damper is meant for systems whose fans shut down in a fire and is not rated to close against moving air. Where fans keep running, dynamic-rated dampers are needed.","src":"Ruskin IBD2 fire damper spec (UL 555)","prompt":"Why is a static-rated fire damper a concern where fans keep running during a fire?","trap":"","qs":["cvi-0046"]},{"id":"hvac:s-link-165","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"The standard fire damper fusible link is rated 165°F, with 212°F links available as an option.","src":"Ruskin IBD2 fire damper spec (UL 555)","prompt":"What is the standard fire damper fusible link rating?","trap":"160°F is the IMC floor for the actuating device, not the standard link rating.","num":{"q":"The standard fire damper fusible link is rated __.","a":"165°F","d":["160°F","212°F","350°F"]},"qs":["cvi-0046","cvi-0047"]},{"id":"hvac:s-imc-160","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"The IMC sets a fire damper's heat-actuating device about 50°F above normal duct temperature, but not below 160°F, and not above 350°F in a smoke control system.","src":"IMC 607.3.3.1","prompt":"What temperature limits does the IMC set for a fire damper's heat-actuating device?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Per the IMC, a fire damper's heat-actuating device operates about 50°F above normal duct temperature, but not below __.","a":"160°F","d":["165°F","212°F","350°F"]},"qs":["cvi-0047"]},{"id":"hvac:s-nfpa80-test","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 80 has fire dampers tested 1 year after installation and then every 4 years, except hospitals, which may go every 6 years. NFPA 105 sets the same cycle for smoke dampers.","src":"NFPA 80 §19.4.1 (2010 ed.); NFPA 105","prompt":"How often does NFPA 80 require fire dampers to be tested?","trap":"A 1-year repeat is ACR's recommended healthcare inspection interval; NFPA 80 uses 1 year only for the first damper test.","num":{"q":"Outside hospitals, NFPA 80 fire damper tests repeat every __ after the first-year test.","a":"4 years","d":["1 year","2 years","6 years"]},"qs":["cvi-0048"]},{"id":"hvac:s-static-sensor","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"A VAV duct static pressure sensor usually sits about two-thirds of the way down the main supply duct, a compromise between the fan and the worst-case box.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"Where is a VAV duct static pressure sensor usually placed?","trap":"Not at the fan discharge, not in the return, and not at the farthest diffuser.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-static-reset","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"Static pressure reset (trim and respond) keeps lowering the duct static setpoint until the most-demanding box damper is nearly wide open, so the fan does no more work than the worst zone needs.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What does VAV static pressure reset lower the fan setpoint until?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-static-too-low","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"If most VAV boxes sit over about 75 percent open in cooling, below their maximum airflow, with warm zones while the fan has speed to spare, the duct static setpoint is most likely too low.","src":"PNNL-29078 §4.3.8","prompt":"What does it mean when most VAV boxes sit nearly wide open with warm zones and spare fan speed?","trap":"A setpoint set too high shows the opposite: boxes mostly throttled.","qs":["cvi-0419"]},{"id":"hvac:s-sat-reset","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"Supply air temperature reset raises the cooling supply setpoint in mild weather, cutting reheat and cooling energy at the cost of more fan energy, because boxes then need more airflow.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What does supply air temperature reset trade off?","trap":"It spends fan energy to save reheat and cooling, not the reverse.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-sequence-of-operation","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"DEF","text":"The sequence of operation is the written description of how the controls run the fans, dampers and valves in each mode; get it before touching a BAS.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What is a sequence of operation?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-freeze-stat","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"A freeze stat (low-limit) has a capillary across the coil face that trips at about 35 to 38°F anywhere along its length, stopping the fan and closing the outdoor air damper to protect water coils.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What does a freeze stat across a coil face do?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-duct-smoke-detector","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"A duct smoke detector senses smoke in the airstream and shuts the air handler fan down; it is a life safety device that cleaning work must not impair.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What does a duct smoke detector do?","trap":"Closing at a rated temperature is a fire damper's fusible link, not the detector.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-dcv","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"Demand control ventilation trims outdoor air on CO2 readings when occupancy is low, within code minimums, so the unit conditions less outdoor air. It suits conference rooms, gyms and assembly spaces.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"How does demand control ventilation save energy?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-unoccupied","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"In unoccupied mode a typical commercial AHU closes the outdoor air damper and lets the space float to setback; some sequences add a pre-occupancy purge or optimal start.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What does a typical commercial AHU sequence do in unoccupied mode?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-pressure-interlock","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"A supply fan interlocked with an exhaust fan and a motorized outdoor air damper is a pressure-control sequence against depressurization; defeating an interlock during cleaning changes building pressure.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What is a supply fan interlocked with an exhaust fan and a motorized outdoor air damper?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-galvanized-nonporous","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"Bare galvanized sheet metal duct, rectangular or spiral round, is non-porous. It can take a wet process that damages nothing and keeps water and agents off electrical parts and porous materials, and the NADCA Vacuum Test can verify it.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions; §4.4; §5.3.3","prompt":"What makes bare galvanized sheet metal duct eligible for wet cleaning and the NADCA Vacuum Test?","trap":"Liner, duct board and attenuated duct are porous: no water, no Vacuum Test.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0068","cvi-0070"]},{"id":"hvac:a-spiral-flat-oval","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"FACT","text":"Spiral duct is round duct wound from a metal strip with a continuous helical lock seam. Flat oval duct keeps much of round duct's low friction and stiffness in a lower profile where round will not fit.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Standards","prompt":"How are spiral duct and flat oval duct built, and when is flat oval used?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-duct-board","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"Fibrous glass duct board is rigid insulation board with a foil outer facing that serves as the vapor barrier. The board is formed into the duct itself.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What is fibrous glass duct board?","trap":"Board IS the duct. Liner is glued inside a metal duct; wrap goes outside one.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-duct-liner","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct liner is insulation, usually fibrous glass, applied inside a metal duct for thermal and sound control. It faces the airstream, so it is a porous airside surface.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What is duct liner and where is it installed?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-duct-wrap","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct wrap is insulation applied to the outside of a duct with all seams and joints sealed to form a continuous vapor barrier. It never touches the airstream.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What is duct wrap, and how does it differ from duct liner?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-wrap-repair","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"SHALL","text":"When a service opening disturbs external duct wrap, the wrap must be repaired so no break degrades its R-value, service rating, vapor barrier or air barrier.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"What must be confirmed about external duct wrap after a service opening is closed?","trap":"Restoring the wrap is not left to the owner's option.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0069"]},{"id":"hvac:a-porous-vs-nonporous","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"Porous components can be penetrated by air or water: fibrous glass liner and duct board, sound attenuated duct, wood and concrete. Non-porous: sheet metal, aluminum foil and the polymeric film liner of flex duct.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"Which HVAC duct materials does ACR classify as porous and which as non-porous?","trap":"Flex duct's inner polymer film is non-porous even though flex is soft.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0068"]},{"id":"hvac:a-porous-dry-only","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"SHALL","text":"Water and cleaning agents never contact fibrous glass or other porous duct. Duct board, liner and attenuators get dry mechanical cleaning, and Method 2, not the Vacuum Test, ends their verification.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4; §4.17; §5.3.3","prompt":"How must porous duct such as duct board be cleaned and verified?","trap":"A low-pressure rinse with extraction is still water on porous board.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0061","cvi-0068","cvi-0111"]},{"id":"hvac:a-flex-friction","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"FACT","text":"Flex duct has more friction than rigid metal even when fully stretched. Slack, sag or compression can more than double it and cut airflow well below the chart value.","src":"ACCA Manual D; ADC installation standards","prompt":"How does flex duct compare with rigid metal for friction, and what does slack do?","trap":"Flex is never equal to metal, even pulled taut.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-flex-support","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"FACT","text":"Support flex duct at intervals no greater than 4 ft, with sag no more than 1/2 in. per foot of spacing, on straps or saddles at least 1 1/2 in. wide.","src":"ADC Flexible Duct Installation Standards; DOE Building America","prompt":"What support spacing, sag limit and strap width apply to flex duct?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Horizontal flex duct supports go no more than __ apart, with sag held to 1/2 in. per foot of spacing.","a":"4 ft","d":["2 ft","6 ft","8 ft"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-ul181-classes","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 181 Class 0 air duct has flame spread and smoke developed ratings of zero. Class 1 allows a flame spread index up to 25 and smoke developed up to 50.","src":"UL 181 (via DOE Building America)","prompt":"What separates UL 181 Class 0 from Class 1 flex duct?","trap":"Class 0 is the zero rating. UL 181A and 181B are closure listings, not duct classes.","num":{"q":"UL 181 Class 1 air duct allows a flame spread index up to __ and smoke developed up to 50.","a":"25","d":["0","50","75"]},"qs":["cvi-0062"]},{"id":"hvac:a-ul181-family","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 181 lists factory-made air ducts and air connectors. UL 181A lists closures for rigid duct such as duct board; UL 181B lists closures for flexible duct and connectors.","src":"UL 181; UL 181A; UL 181B","prompt":"What do UL 181, UL 181A and UL 181B each cover?","trap":"UL 723 is the surface burning test (25/50), not a closure listing.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0067"]},{"id":"hvac:a-board-closures","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"CODE","text":"Duct board keeps its UL 181 Class 1 air duct rating only when joints are closed with UL 181A listed closures: 181A-P foil tape, 181A-H heat-activated tape or 181A-M mastic.","src":"UL 181A; duct board manufacturer instructions; ACR 2025 §4.2.5.4","prompt":"What closures must duct board joints use to keep the UL 181 Class 1 rating?","trap":"181B-FX is the flex duct listing; cloth duct tape voids the board's rating.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0067","cvi-0132"]},{"id":"hvac:a-board-pressure","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"FACT","text":"Fibrous glass duct board is a low-pressure UL 181 Class 1 air duct rated to about 2 in. w.g. static. Higher pressure classes are built in metal.","src":"NAIMA; Owens Corning EnduraGold data","prompt":"What static pressure is fibrous glass duct board limited to?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Fibrous glass duct board systems are limited to about __ static pressure.","a":"2 in. w.g.","d":["1/2 in. w.g.","6 in. w.g.","10 in. w.g."]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-flex-no-openings","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"SHALL","text":"No service opening is cut into flexible duct. Flex runs are accessed by disconnecting them at the ends and reattaching them per the manufacturer's instructions.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.9","prompt":"How is the inside of a flexible duct run reached for inspection or cleaning?","trap":"No UL 181B tape or gasketed panel makes a cut in flex acceptable.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0060","cvi-0263"]},{"id":"hvac:a-fabric-duct","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"FACT","text":"Fabric (textile) air dispersion duct, listed to UL 2518 and UL 723, is cleaned the way its manufacturer directs, usually taken down and laundered. It is not cut open for access or pressure washed in place.","src":"Manufacturer instructions; UL 2518","prompt":"How is soiled fabric air dispersion duct cleaned?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0064"]},{"id":"hvac:a-equal-friction","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"The equal friction method sizes every section for the same pressure loss per 100 ft, often 0.08 to 0.10 in. w.g. per 100 ft on low-pressure work, read off a ductulator from each section's cfm.","src":"ACCA Manual D; ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"How does the equal friction duct sizing method work?","trap":"Equal friction does not hold outlet static equal; that is static regain.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-static-regain","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"Static regain sizing lets velocity drop after each takeoff so the velocity pressure regained as static offsets the next section's friction, keeping outlet static about even. It suits high-velocity and long commercial runs.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals (duct design)","prompt":"How does the static regain duct sizing method work?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-round-vs-rect","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"For the same airflow, round duct beats rectangular because a circle has the least perimeter per unit of area: less surface drag, less metal, stiffer under pressure and easier to seal.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"Why is round duct preferred over rectangular for the same airflow?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-aspect-ratio","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"Keep rectangular duct aspect ratio at or below about 4 to 1; 1 to 1 is ideal. Flat, wide ducts add friction and metal and are harder to clean and seal.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Systems Duct Design","prompt":"What aspect ratio limit applies to rectangular duct?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Rectangular duct aspect ratio should generally stay at or below __.","a":"4 to 1","d":["1 to 1","8 to 1","12 to 1"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-equivalent-length","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"DEF","text":"A fitting's equivalent length is the length of straight duct with the same pressure loss. Fitting allowances added to straight runs give the total effective length.","src":"ACCA Manual D","prompt":"What is the equivalent length of a duct fitting?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-velocity-squared","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"In a given duct, friction loss rises roughly with velocity squared, so doubling the airflow roughly quadruples it. Shrinking a duct at constant cfm raises velocity, pressure loss and noise.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals; DOE Fan Sourcebook","prompt":"What happens to friction loss when airflow through an existing duct doubles?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Doubling the airflow through an existing duct raises its friction loss about __.","a":"4 times","d":["2 times","8 times","1.5 times"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-critical-path","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"Fan static is set by the most restrictive run, the critical path. Shorter runs get balancing dampers to throttle down to design airflow.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What sets the static pressure a duct system's fan must deliver?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-takeoffs","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"A 45 degree or conical branch takeoff lets air enter the branch smoothly, with less turbulence and pressure loss than a straight tap.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"Why use a 45 degree or conical takeoff instead of a straight tap?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-turning-vanes","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"Turning vanes guide air around hard rectangular elbows to cut turbulence, so a mitered elbow without vanes loses far more pressure than a radius elbow.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals; ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What do turning vanes do, and where are they installed?","trap":"Vanes belong in rectangular mitered elbows, not round elbows or flex.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-attenuator","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"DEF","text":"A sound attenuator (silencer) is a duct section of perforated metal over fibrous glass fill that reduces fan and airflow noise carried by the duct. ACR counts it as porous.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What is a duct sound attenuator and how does ACR classify it?","trap":"The perforated metal face tempts people to call it non-porous; it is porous.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0052"]},{"id":"hvac:a-undersized-return","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"An undersized return is the usual choke point in a duct system. It shows up as high total external static and low airflow across the coil.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"How does an undersized return duct show up in readings?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-transfer-path","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"With one central return and no transfer path, closing a bedroom door pressurizes the room and depressurizes the rest of the house. Jumper ducts, transfer grilles or door undercuts relieve it.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What happens when a bedroom door closes in a house with one central return and no transfer path?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-acca-manuals","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"DEF","text":"ACCA's residential design manuals run in order: Manual J calculates loads, Manual S selects equipment, Manual D designs the ducts, and Manual T selects registers and grilles.","src":"ACCA Manuals J, S, D, T","prompt":"What does each ACCA manual (J, S, D, T) cover, and in what order are they used?","trap":"Manual D is ducts, not loads; loads are Manual J.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-smacna-pressure-class","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA duct pressure class is the static pressure a duct is built to hold, from 1/2 to 10 in. w.g. It sets sheet gauge and reinforcement.","src":"ANSI/SMACNA 006 HVAC Duct Construction Standards","prompt":"What does a SMACNA duct pressure class describe?","trap":"Pressure class is construction strength; leakage class is tightness.","num":{"q":"SMACNA duct pressure classes run from 1/2 in. w.g. up to __.","a":"10 in. w.g.","d":["2 in. w.g.","4 in. w.g.","6 in. w.g."]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-seal-class-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA Seal Class A seals all transverse joints, all longitudinal seams and all duct wall penetrations.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Standards Table 1-2","prompt":"What must be sealed under SMACNA Seal Class A?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0128"]},{"id":"hvac:a-seal-class-b","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA Seal Class B seals all transverse joints and longitudinal seams, but not duct wall penetrations.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Standards Table 1-2","prompt":"What must be sealed under SMACNA Seal Class B?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0128"]},{"id":"hvac:a-seal-class-c","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA Seal Class C seals transverse joints only; longitudinal seams and penetrations are left unsealed.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Standards Table 1-2","prompt":"What must be sealed under SMACNA Seal Class C?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0128"]},{"id":"hvac:a-leakage-class","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"FACT","text":"SMACNA leakage class (CL) is leakage in cfm per 100 sq ft of duct surface at 1 in. w.g. A lower CL is tighter, and round duct reaches a lower CL than rectangular at the same seal class.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Air Duct Leakage Test Manual","prompt":"What does a SMACNA leakage class number mean?","trap":"A lower CL number means less leakage, not a lower pressure class.","num":{"q":"SMACNA leakage class is leakage in cfm per 100 sq ft of duct surface at __ static pressure.","a":"1 in. w.g.","d":["1/2 in. w.g.","2 in. w.g.","4 in. w.g."]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-leak-test","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"FACT","text":"A duct leakage test seals the openings, pressurizes the duct with a calibrated fan and meters the cfm needed to hold test pressure. SMACNA 016 gives the procedures and expected rates.","src":"ANSI/SMACNA 016 Leakage Test Manual","prompt":"How is duct leakage tested, and which SMACNA document gives the procedure?","trap":"SMACNA 006 is construction; it sends leakage testing to SMACNA 016.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0129"]},{"id":"hvac:a-leakage-calc","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: commercial duct leakage is supply fan airflow minus the sum of outlet airflows, divided by fan airflow. A 10,000 cfm traverse with 8,200 cfm at the diffusers is 18%.","src":"LBNL, Wray, Diamond and Sherman","prompt":"How is commercial supply duct leakage calculated from fan and outlet readings?","trap":"Divide by fan airflow, not the outlet total or the scheduled design cfm.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0418"]},{"id":"hvac:a-supply-vs-return-leaks","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"FACT","text":"Supply leaks push conditioned air out; into an attic, they depressurize the house so it pulls in hot attic air. Return leaks are under suction and pull in unconditioned air and debris, unfiltered when the leak is past the filter.","src":"DOE Energy Saver, Minimizing Energy Losses in Ducts","prompt":"How do supply duct leaks differ from return duct leaks?","trap":"Insulation fibers at registers point to return leaks, not supply leaks.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0013","cvi-0423"]},{"id":"hvac:a-duct-heat-gain","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"FACT","text":"A supply trunk crossing a hot attic warms along its length by conduction through thin insulation. Because it runs positive, its leaks push air out rather than pulling attic air in.","src":"DOE Energy Saver, Minimizing Energy Losses in Ducts","prompt":"Why does supply air warm along a trunk crossing a hot attic?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0423"]},{"id":"hvac:a-afue-duct-loss","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"NOTE","text":"AFUE is measured at the furnace and leaves out duct losses, which DOE says can reach 35% of output when ducts run through attics or garages.","src":"DOE Energy Saver, Furnaces and Boilers","prompt":"Which heat loss does a furnace's AFUE rating leave out?","trap":"Flue loss and pilot fuel are inside the AFUE test; duct loss is not.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0012"]},{"id":"hvac:a-attic-duct-r8","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"CODE","text":"Under the IECC, supply and return ducts in attics need at least R-8 insulation when 3 in. and larger (R-6 when smaller). Ducts in other parts of the building outside conditioned space need R-6 (R-3.3 when under 3 in.).","src":"IECC 2021 R403.3.1 (2024 R403.3.3; 2018 R403.3.1)","prompt":"What insulation does the IECC require for ducts in attics?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The IECC requires attic ducts 3 in. and larger to be insulated to at least __.","a":"R-8","d":["R-6","R-4.2","R-11"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-exhaust-in-scope","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"DEF","text":"Under ACR, general, bathroom and heat recovery exhaust ducts are part of the ventilation system and the overall inspection. Kitchen grease exhaust falls under NFPA 96, with cleaning per ANSI/IKECA C10.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.5; ACR References (ANSI/IKECA C10); NFPA 96","prompt":"Are bathroom and general exhaust ducts part of an ACR system inspection?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0249"]},{"id":"hvac:a-nfpa96-scope","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"DEF","text":"NFPA 96 covers ventilation control and fire protection of commercial cooking operations, including grease duct construction and exhaust inspection frequency.","src":"NFPA 96","prompt":"What does NFPA 96 cover?","trap":"NFPA 90A is general A/C and ventilating installation; 96 is commercial cooking.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-ikeca","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IKECA C10 is the kitchen exhaust cleaning methodology that ACR cites. I10 is the inspection methodology and M10 covers maintenance between cleanings.","src":"ANSI/IKECA C10, I10, M10; ACR 2025 references","prompt":"What do IKECA C10, I10 and M10 each cover?","trap":"ANSI/IICRC S590 assesses HVAC after water, fire, or mold damage, not kitchen exhaust.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0150"]},{"id":"hvac:a-grease-duct","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 96 grease duct is carbon steel at least No. 16 MSG or stainless steel at least No. 18 MSG, with a continuous liquid-tight external weld on all seams and joints.","src":"NFPA 96 §7.5","prompt":"What material and joints does NFPA 96 require for kitchen grease duct?","trap":"The heavier sheet (lower number, 16) goes with carbon steel; stainless is 18.","num":{"q":"NFPA 96 grease duct of carbon steel must be at least No. __ MSG.","a":"16","d":["18","20","24"]},"qs":["cvi-0065"]},{"id":"hvac:a-nfpa96-frequency","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 96 grease buildup inspection: monthly for solid-fuel cooking, quarterly for high volume (24-hour, charbroiling, wok), semiannually for moderate volume, annually for low volume such as churches and seasonal businesses.","src":"NFPA 96 Table 12.4 (11.4 in 2021 and earlier)","prompt":"How often does NFPA 96 require kitchen exhaust inspection for each cooking volume?","trap":"","num":{"q":"NFPA 96 inspects high-volume cooking exhaust (24-hour, charbroiling, wok) every __.","a":"3 months","d":["1 month","6 months","12 months"]},"qs":["cvi-0149"]},{"id":"hvac:a-lab-exhaust-material","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Lab fume exhaust duct is chosen for the chemicals it carries. Welded 316L stainless serves many labs but not hydrochloric acid vapor; PVC-coated steel or FRP is used there.","src":"UC Lab Safety Design Manual, Ducting","prompt":"Which duct materials suit lab exhaust carrying hydrochloric acid vapor?","trap":"Lined galvanized and aluminum are not lab exhaust materials.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0071"]},{"id":"hvac:a-lab-exhaust-hazard","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"REC","text":"Before fume hood exhaust ducts such as FRP runs are opened, get the chemical hazard data and consult a qualified IEP or health and safety professional, since residues may be hazardous.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.8; §3.5.1","prompt":"What should happen before lab fume hood exhaust ducts are opened?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0066"]},{"id":"hvac:a-toilet-exhaust","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"CODE","text":"The IMC exhausts public toilet rooms at 50 cfm per water closet or urinal when the fan runs continuously while occupied, or 70 cfm when it runs intermittently.","src":"IMC 2024 Table 403.3.1.1, note e","prompt":"What exhaust rate does the IMC set for public toilet rooms?","trap":"Toilet rates are exhaust per fixture, not outdoor air per person.","num":{"q":"An IMC public toilet room exhausted continuously while occupied needs __ per water closet or urinal.","a":"50 cfm","d":["70 cfm","15 cfm","5 cfm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-dryer-duct","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Dryer exhaust duct should be smooth rigid metal, kept short, with no screws protruding inside to snag lint. Lint, restriction and heat together make a fire risk.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment; dryer manufacturer instructions","prompt":"How should a dryer exhaust duct be built?","trap":"Foil or vinyl flex run at any length is the classic wrong answer.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-intake-separation","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Outdoor air intakes are kept away from exhaust outlets, cooling towers, loading docks and vents so exhaust, fumes and tower drift are not pulled back in. ASHRAE 62.1 sets separation distances.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1","prompt":"Why must outdoor air intakes be separated from exhausts and cooling towers?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-negative-building","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"When exhaust exceeds the air brought in, the building runs negative: doors are hard to open and air rushes in at every gap. Make-up air or rebalanced outdoor air fixes it.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"How does a building with more exhaust than intake behave?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv-def","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"DEF","text":"MERV (minimum efficiency reporting value) rates a filter's particle capture efficiency by size range, from ASHRAE 52.2 testing. Higher pressure drop often comes with it but is not the definition.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2","prompt":"What does a filter's MERV rating describe?","trap":"MERV is not airflow capacity, pressure drop or service life.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0135"]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv-range","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 52.2 rates general ventilation filters MERV 1 through 16. HEPA and ULPA filters are rated by separate test methods and sit above the scale.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2-2017","prompt":"What range do MERV ratings span under ASHRAE 52.2?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV ratings run from 1 through __.","a":"16","d":["8","12","20"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv-not-others","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"NOTE","text":"Only ASHRAE 52.2 produces a MERV. ISO 16890 rates ePM classes, UL 900 tests filter combustibility and smoke, and ASHRAE 62.1 sets minimums without testing filters.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2; ISO 16890; UL 900; ASHRAE 62.1","prompt":"Which document's test produces a MERV, and what do ISO 16890 and UL 900 rate instead?","trap":"UL 900 is a fire test, not an efficiency rating.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0135","cvi-0156"]},{"id":"hvac:a-e-ranges","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 52.2 builds MERV from three particle size ranges: E1 is 0.3 to 1.0 micron, E2 is 1.0 to 3.0 micron, and E3 is 3.0 to 10.0 micron.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 (NAFA summary)","prompt":"What particle sizes do ASHRAE 52.2 ranges E1, E2 and E3 cover?","trap":"A single 0.3 micron point is the HEPA test, not range E1.","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 52.2 range E1 covers particles from 0.3 to __ micron.","a":"1.0","d":["2.5","3.0","10.0"]},"qs":["cvi-0072"]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv13","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"A filter earns the highest MERV whose minimums it meets in all three ranges. MERV 13 needs 50% E1, 85% E2, 90% E3; MERV 12 needs 35/80/90; MERV 11 needs 20/65/85.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV table (NAFA)","prompt":"What minimum efficiencies does MERV 13 require, and how is a MERV assigned?","trap":"Qualifying in E2 and E3 alone does not earn MERV 13; E1 must reach 50%.","num":{"q":"MERV 13 requires at least __ efficiency in range E1 (0.3 to 1.0 micron).","a":"50%","d":["20%","35%","75%"]},"qs":["cvi-0073"]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv16","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"MERV 16 requires at least 95% in all three size ranges. That is still short of HEPA, so a MERV rating alone cannot show HEPA performance.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV table (NAFA)","prompt":"What does MERV 16 require, and is it HEPA?","trap":"","num":{"q":"MERV 16 requires at least __ efficiency in each ASHRAE 52.2 size range.","a":"95%","d":["85%","90%","99.97%"]},"qs":["cvi-0076"]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv-low","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"When E3 efficiency is under 20%, ASHRAE 52.2 assigns MERV 1 through 4 by average arrestance. MERV 5 starts at 20% E3, and E1 enters the table only at MERV 11.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV table (NAFA)","prompt":"How does ASHRAE 52.2 assign MERV 1 through 4?","trap":"","num":{"q":"MERV 5 begins at __ efficiency in range E3.","a":"20%","d":["35%","50%","65%"]},"qs":["cvi-0074"]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv-a","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"DEF","text":"MERV-A is an optional ASHRAE 52.2 Appendix J rating taken after the media is conditioned with fine particles. A MERV-A well below the MERV warns that in-service capture may drop.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 Appendix J","prompt":"What does a MERV-A value tell you?","trap":"MERV-A is not efficiency at final resistance; it reflects fine-particle conditioning.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0075"]},{"id":"hvac:a-hepa","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"DEF","text":"HEPA filtration captures at least 99.97% of particles at 0.3 micron, the most penetrating particle size.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions (DOE HEPA definition)","prompt":"What removal efficiency defines a HEPA filter?","trap":"","num":{"q":"HEPA means at least 99.97% capture at __ micron.","a":"0.3","d":["1.0","3.0","10.0"]},"qs":["cvi-0076"]},{"id":"hvac:a-hepa-type","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"NOTE","text":"A HEPA-type filter rated 99% at 1 micron is not HEPA. ACR requires true HEPA, 99.97% at 0.3 micron, on vacuum equipment that exhausts indoors.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.3","prompt":"Why is a HEPA-type filter rejected on a negative air machine exhausting indoors?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0076"]},{"id":"hvac:a-filter-families","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"On 52.2 application charts, disposable fiberglass panels typically fall in MERV 1 to 4, pleated media in 5 to 8, and rigid box, cartridge and pocket filters in 9 to 16.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 application chart","prompt":"Which MERV groups do common filter types typically fall in?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Disposable 1 in. fiberglass panel filters typically rate MERV __.","a":"1 to 4","d":["5 to 8","9 to 12","13 to 16"]},"qs":["cvi-0077"]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv8-floor","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires at least MERV 8 (or the ISO ePM10 minimum) upstream of cooling coils and other wetted surfaces, except sensible-only coils. Editions before 2013 said MERV 6.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.5","prompt":"What minimum filter does ASHRAE 62.1 require upstream of cooling coils?","trap":"MERV 11 is 62.1's outdoor air rule where PM2.5 standards are exceeded, not the coil rule. ACR itself sets no MERV.","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires at least MERV __ upstream of cooling coils and wetted surfaces.","a":"8","d":["6","11","13"]},"qs":["cvi-0136","cvi-0196","cvi-0257"]},{"id":"hvac:a-prefilter","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"A prefilter, often MERV 8, catches large particles so the final filter, such as MERV 13 to 14 or HEPA, lasts longer. Gases and odors need a separate carbon stage.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a prefilter ahead of a final filter do?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-high-merv-effect","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"A filter much higher in MERV than the system was designed for adds resistance: static rises and airflow falls unless the fan can overcome it, risking coil freezing and comfort complaints.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment","prompt":"What does a much higher MERV filter than designed do to the system?","trap":"Fitting the rack says nothing about the fan's pressure budget.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-filter-dp-loading","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"A media filter's pressure drop rises slowly as it loads. Read it on a gauge across the bank and replace at the manufacturer's final resistance.","src":"NAFA Technicians on Site guideline","prompt":"When should a loading media filter be replaced?","trap":"0.5 in. w.c. is a typical residential blower rating, not a filter change-out limit.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0082"]},{"id":"hvac:a-filter-dp-falls","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"NOTE","text":"At steady airflow a filter's resistance only climbs as it loads. A reading that falls, especially below its clean rating, means air is slipping past gaps or through torn media.","src":"ACHR News, air filter gages; Donaldson","prompt":"What does a filter pressure drop reading that suddenly falls below its clean rating mean?","trap":"A lower reading is not recovery; check the bank, frames and gauge tubing.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0082","cvi-0431"]},{"id":"hvac:a-bypass","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Filter bypass is unfiltered air slipping through gaps, poor fit or failed seals at the rack. It loads the coil and ducts no matter how high the filter's MERV.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality App. B","prompt":"What is filter bypass, and why does MERV not fix it?","trap":"A higher MERV in a leaky rack changes nothing.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0197"]},{"id":"hvac:a-bypass-acr","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection shall include filters and air bypass. Dust streaks on the coil face lined up with rack gaps are recorded as filter bypass.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"What does ACR require the AHU inspection to include about filters?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0083","cvi-0197","cvi-0256"]},{"id":"hvac:a-electronic-cleaner","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Electronic air cleaners, such as electrostatic precipitators and ionizers, use high voltage to charge and collect particles. They can produce ozone, a lung irritant, as a by-product.","src":"EPA Residential Air Cleaners, 2nd ed.","prompt":"What IAQ drawback comes with electronic air cleaners?","trap":"Media filters, not electronic cleaners, carry the higher pressure drop.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0078","cvi-0168"]},{"id":"hvac:a-ozone-limit","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"CODE","text":"UL 867 limits air cleaner ozone emission to 0.050 ppm (50 ppb), the baseline California's CARB requires. UL 2998 zero-ozone validation is 0.005 ppm.","src":"UL 867; UL 2998; CARB air cleaner regulation","prompt":"What ozone limit does UL 867 set for air cleaners?","trap":"0.10 ppm is the OSHA 8-hour PEL; 0.070 ppm is the EPA outdoor standard.","num":{"q":"UL 867, the CARB baseline, holds air cleaner ozone emission to __.","a":"0.050 ppm","d":["0.005 ppm","0.070 ppm","0.10 ppm"]},"qs":["cvi-0079","cvi-0168"]},{"id":"hvac:a-uvc","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"UV-C (UVGI) lamps, usually low-pressure mercury at 253.7 nm, inactivate microorganisms on coils and drain pans. They capture no particles, so they never replace filters.","src":"EPA Residential Air Cleaners, 2nd ed.","prompt":"What do UV-C lamps at a coil do, and can they replace filters?","trap":"Pulling filters for UV-C lets dust load the coil and ducts.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0080"]},{"id":"hvac:a-uvc-loto","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Before entering an air handler with UV-C lamps, de-energize and lock out the lamp circuit. UV-C damages eyes and skin in seconds and degrades nearby plastics and some liners.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment; lamp manufacturer instructions","prompt":"What must be done before entering an AHU fitted with UV-C lamps?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-carbon","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Particle filters cannot control gases and some odors. Gas-phase filters use a sorbent, most commonly activated carbon, to adsorb them.","src":"EPA, Residential Air Cleaners","prompt":"What filtration targets gases and odors?","trap":"Higher MERV, UV-C and electrostatic units all target particles or microbes, not gases.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0081"]},{"id":"hvac:a-iwc-fpm","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"DEF","text":"HVAC air pressures are read in inches of water column (in. w.c., IWC, also written in. w.g.). Air velocity is in feet per minute (fpm).","src":"NADCA ASCS acronym list","prompt":"What units are used for HVAC air pressure and air velocity?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-sp-vp-tp","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"DEF","text":"Static pressure pushes outward on the duct walls, velocity pressure comes from the air's motion, and total pressure is their sum: TP = SP + VP.","src":"TSI-107; ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What does total pressure in a duct equal?","trap":"VP = TP - SP. It is never static minus total or a product.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-pitot-ports","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"A pitot tube points upstream. Its tip senses total pressure and its side ports sense static, so piping tip and side ports across one manometer reads velocity pressure.","src":"TSI-107","prompt":"What does a manometer read when a pitot's tip and side ports are piped across it?","trap":"External static is a unit measurement at the plenums, not a pitot reading.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0086"]},{"id":"hvac:a-v-4005","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"For standard air, velocity in fpm = 4005 x the square root of velocity pressure in in. w.c. Rearranged, VP = (V / 4005) squared.","src":"TSI-107; MSHA pitot card 7000-59","prompt":"What formula converts velocity pressure to air velocity for standard air?","trap":"Square root first: VP times 4005 skips the root.","num":{"q":"For standard air, V in fpm = __ x the square root of VP in in. w.c.","a":"4005","d":["1.08","4.5","500"]},"qs":["cvi-0085"]},{"id":"hvac:a-v-4005-example","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: a pitot reads 1.10 in. w.c. total and 0.85 static, so VP is 0.25. Its square root is 0.5, and 4005 x 0.5 is about 2,000 fpm.","src":"TSI-107","prompt":"A pitot reads 1.10 in. w.c. total and 0.85 in. w.c. static: what is the velocity?","trap":"Plugging in static or total pressure instead of VP gives 3,700 or 4,200 fpm.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0085"]},{"id":"hvac:a-traverse-why","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"Duct velocity is not uniform: wall friction slows air at the edges and fittings skew the profile. A pitot traverse averages readings on an equal-area or log-Tchebycheff grid across the duct.","src":"TSI AF-106; Dwyer duct traversing guide","prompt":"Why take a multi-point pitot traverse instead of one reading?","trap":"A lone centerline reading runs high because air moves fastest mid-duct.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0095","cvi-0417"]},{"id":"hvac:a-flow-hood","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"A flow hood (balometer) captures a diffuser or grille and reads cfm directly at the outlet. The pitot traverse is the in-duct method.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What does a flow hood measure?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0107"]},{"id":"hvac:a-tesp","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"DEF","text":"Total external static pressure (TESP) is the supply static read just after the unit plus the return static read just before it, added with signs ignored.","src":"NCI static pressure guide; ACHR News","prompt":"How is total external static pressure measured on a furnace or air handler?","trap":"Subtracting the readings, or reading one side only, understates TESP.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0089"]},{"id":"hvac:a-tesp-example","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: +0.55 in. w.c. supply and -0.35 return give a TESP of 0.90 in. w.c., nearly double the 0.50 in. w.c. rating of many residential blowers.","src":"ACHR News; NCI static pressure guide","prompt":"A furnace reads +0.55 supply and -0.35 return in. w.c.: what is its TESP?","trap":"High TESP points to a loaded filter, a fouled coil, or undersized duct.","num":{"q":"Many furnaces and residential blowers are rated for about __ total external static.","a":"0.50 in. w.c.","d":["0.10 in. w.c.","0.90 in. w.c.","2.0 in. w.c."]},"qs":["cvi-0089"]},{"id":"hvac:a-component-drop","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: a component's pressure drop is its upstream reading minus its downstream reading. A filter between -0.08 and -0.52 in. w.c. drops 0.44; compare that with its rated drop.","src":"NCI, Measure and Interpret Static Pressures","prompt":"How do you find a single component's pressure drop from static readings?","trap":"The biggest single reading marks a location, not a restriction.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0416","cvi-0435"]},{"id":"hvac:a-resistance-airflow","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"On a fixed-speed fan, added resistance from a loaded filter, dirty coil or closed dampers raises static pressure and lowers airflow as the fan rides back on its curve.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals; DOE Fan Sourcebook","prompt":"What does added system resistance do to a fixed-speed fan's static and airflow?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-dp-flow-squared","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: pressure drop across a coil scales roughly with airflow squared. A 0.80 in. w.c. drop at 8,000 cfm is about 0.45 at 6,000 cfm, so compare before and after at equal airflow.","src":"DOE Fan Sourcebook; ACR 2025 §4.11.6","prompt":"Why must before and after coil pressure drops be compared at the same airflow?","trap":"A lower drop at a slower fan speed proves nothing about the cleaning.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0424"]},{"id":"hvac:a-q-va","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"DEF","text":"Airflow in cfm = average velocity in fpm x duct area in square feet, so velocity = cfm / area. Convert inches to feet before multiplying.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals (Q = V x A)","prompt":"How are airflow, velocity and duct area related?","trap":"cfm times area, or area divided by cfm, is the inverted-formula trap.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0084"]},{"id":"hvac:a-q-va-example","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: a 24 x 12 in. duct is 288 sq in / 144 = 2.0 sq ft. At an average 900 fpm it carries 2.0 x 900 = 1,800 cfm.","src":"Dwyer duct traversing guide (Q = A x V)","prompt":"A 24 x 12 in. duct averages 900 fpm: what is the airflow?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0084"]},{"id":"hvac:a-round-area","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: round duct area = pi x radius squared, in feet. A 14 in. duct is pi x (7/12) squared = 1.07 sq ft, so a 1,050 fpm traverse average gives about 1,120 cfm.","src":"TSI AF-106; geometry","prompt":"A 14 in. round duct averages 1,050 fpm: what is the airflow?","trap":"Using the diameter as the radius, or treating it as a square, overstates the flow.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0417"]},{"id":"hvac:a-face-velocity","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: coil face velocity = airflow / finned face area. 9,000 cfm across a 3 x 5 ft face is 600 fpm, above the 500 fpm rule of thumb for condensate carryover.","src":"Trane, Coil Selection and Optimization","prompt":"How is coil face velocity found, and what does 9,000 cfm on a 3 x 5 ft face give?","trap":"Divide by the finned coil face, not the cabinet or the duct.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0434"]},{"id":"hvac:a-tons-example","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: a 60,000 Btu/h nameplate is 60,000 / 12,000 = 5 tons, and 5 x 400 = 2,000 cfm nominal. A 3-ton split moves about 1,200 cfm.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What nominal airflow should a 60,000 Btu/h cooling unit move?","trap":"Treating a ton as 10,000 Btu/h gives 2,400 cfm.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0090"]},{"id":"hvac:a-sensible-108","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Sensible heat in Btu/h = 1.08 x cfm x temperature difference in F, for standard air. 1.08 = 0.075 lb per cu ft x 0.24 Btu per lb F x 60 minutes per hour.","src":"Energy Vanguard; Belimo HVAC formulas","prompt":"What formula gives the sensible heat carried by an airstream?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Sensible heat in Btu/h = __ x cfm x delta T for standard air.","a":"1.08","d":["0.68","4.5","500"]},"qs":["cvi-0091"]},{"id":"hvac:a-sensible-example","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: 1,600 cfm cooled from 78 to 58 F carries 1.08 x 1,600 x 20 = 34,560 Btu/h of sensible cooling, about 2.9 tons.","src":"Energy Vanguard; arithmetic","prompt":"How much sensible cooling does 1,600 cfm with a 20 F drop represent?","trap":"Dropping the 1.08 gives 32,000; using 4.5 gives 144,000.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0091"]},{"id":"hvac:a-heat-constants","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"NOTE","text":"Keep the constants straight: 1.08 x cfm x delta T is sensible, 4.5 x cfm x delta enthalpy is total, 0.68 x cfm x delta grains is latent, 500 x GPM x delta T is water side.","src":"Belimo HVAC formulas guide","prompt":"Which constant goes with sensible, total, latent and water-side heat formulas?","trap":"4.5 multiplies enthalpy change, never a temperature difference.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0091"]},{"id":"hvac:a-temp-split","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"The temperature drop (split) across a healthy DX cooling coil is typically about 15 to 20 F, but the target depends on return dry bulb and wet bulb; within about 3 F of target is normal.","src":"Proctor Engineering target split table; ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What temperature split is normal across a DX cooling coil?","trap":"A universal 18 to 20 F target is wrong; cooler, drier return air lowers it.","num":{"q":"A healthy DX coil's temperature split is typically about __.","a":"15 to 20 F","d":["5 to 8 F","30 to 35 F","40 to 45 F"]},"qs":["cvi-0425"]},{"id":"hvac:a-split-meaning","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"From Q = 1.08 x cfm x delta T, a bigger split at similar load means less airflow, such as a loaded coil or filter. A smaller split points to excess airflow or lost capacity, such as low charge.","src":"Sensible heat relationship; ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What does a larger than normal temperature split across a DX coil usually mean?","trap":"Extra airflow shrinks the split; it never widens it.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0092"]},{"id":"hvac:a-dry-wet-bulb","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"DEF","text":"Dry bulb is ordinary air temperature. Wet bulb is read on a wetted wick; it equals dry bulb only at 100% RH, and the spread between them gives RH on a sling psychrometer.","src":"NWS definitions; ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What are dry bulb and wet bulb, and when are they equal?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-dew-point","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"DEF","text":"Dew point is the temperature to which air must be cooled, at constant moisture, to reach saturation. Dew point is at or below wet bulb, which is at or below dry bulb.","src":"NWS glossary","prompt":"What is the dew point of an air sample?","trap":"Dew point is not the coil temperature or the wet bulb.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0093"]},{"id":"hvac:a-condensation","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"Any surface colder than the dew point of the air touching it condenses water. Cold ducts, jackets and drain pans below dew point sweat and can grow biofilm.","src":"NWS glossary","prompt":"When does condensation form on a duct or surface?","trap":"Room RH under 100% does not stop condensation on a colder surface.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0093","cvi-0420"]},{"id":"hvac:a-rh-rises","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"Cooling air without changing its moisture content raises its RH until it reaches 100% at the dew point.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What happens to RH when air is cooled without changing its moisture?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-rh-misleads","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"NOTE","text":"Worked example: RH misleads for condensation. 80 F crawlspace air at 50% RH (dew point 60 F) sweats a 58 F duct, while 72 F office air at 55% RH (dew point 55 F) does not.","src":"NWS, dew point vs humidity","prompt":"Why can air at lower RH condense on a surface that higher-RH air does not?","trap":"EPA's below-60% RH advice is a room target, not a surface test.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0420"]},{"id":"hvac:a-sensible-latent","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"DEF","text":"Sensible heat changes dry-bulb temperature; latent heat is moisture. Total cooling is sensible plus latent, and the sensible heat ratio is sensible divided by total.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions; ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What is the difference between sensible and latent cooling, and what is sensible heat ratio?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0094"]},{"id":"hvac:a-latent-needs-dew","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"A coil removes moisture only where its surface is below the air's dew point. A bone-dry pan under a running coil in humid weather means it is doing almost all sensible work.","src":"NWS glossary; ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What does a dry condensate pan under a running DX coil in humid weather mean?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0094"]},{"id":"hvac:a-reheat-dehumidify","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"Reheat dehumidification overcools air below its dew point to condense moisture, then reheats it so it leaves drier at a comfortable temperature. Hot-gas reheat uses compressor heat; electric reheat pays for it.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"How does a hot-gas or electric reheat dehumidification mode work?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-latent-sources","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"Latent load comes from people, cooking, showers and humid infiltration. Lights, computers and solar gain through glass are sensible load.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"Where does latent load in an occupied space come from?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-enthalpy","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"DEF","text":"Enthalpy is total heat, sensible plus latent, per pound of dry air. It is read along the wet-bulb lines of a psychrometric chart.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What is the enthalpy of moist air?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-grains","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"Air moisture content is given in grains per pound of dry air; 7,000 grains equal one pound of water.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"How many grains equal one pound of water?","trap":"","num":{"q":"__ grains equal one pound of water.","a":"7,000","d":["144","1,000","12,000"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-oversized-ac","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"An oversized air conditioner satisfies the thermostat before the coil has removed much moisture, so its short cycles leave a cool, clammy space.","src":"ACCA Manuals J and S","prompt":"Why does an oversized air conditioner control humidity poorly?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-mixed-air-temp","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: mixed air temperature is the flow-weighted average. 20% outdoor air at 30 F with 80% return at 70 F gives 0.2 x 30 + 0.8 x 70 = 62 F.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, Fundamentals","prompt":"What mixed air temperature do 20% outdoor air at 30 F and 80% return air at 70 F make?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-oa-fraction","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Outdoor air fraction = (MAT - RAT) / (OAT - RAT). Multiply it by supply airflow to get outdoor air cfm.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"What formula gives the outdoor air fraction from mixed, return and outdoor air temperatures?","trap":"Dividing by MAT - OAT is a common slip, printed even in some tip sheets.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0413","cvi-0415","cvi-0426"]},{"id":"hvac:a-oa-fraction-example","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: 12,000 cfm with OAT 30, RAT 72 and MAT 62 F gives (62 - 72) / (30 - 72) = 0.238, so about 2,860 cfm of outdoor air.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"At 12,000 cfm, OAT 30 F, RAT 72 F and MAT 62 F, how much outdoor air is the unit taking in?","trap":"9,140 cfm is the return-air share, not outdoor air.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0413"]},{"id":"hvac:a-oa-fraction-spread","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Use the mixed-air method only when outdoor and return air differ by more than about 5 F. With a smaller spread, a 1 F sensor error swings the answer wildly.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.2","prompt":"When is the mixed-air outdoor air fraction calculation reliable?","trap":"","num":{"q":"PNNL uses the mixed-air OA fraction only when OAT and RAT differ by more than about __.","a":"5 F","d":["1 F","3 F","20 F"]},"qs":["cvi-0426"]},{"id":"hvac:a-oa-vs-damper","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Calculated outdoor air fraction should track the damper command within about 25%. OAT 55, RAT 75 and MAT 74 F mean 5% outdoor air; with the damper commanded open, check blades, linkage and actuator.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"How closely should calculated outdoor air fraction track the damper command?","trap":"A BAS damper command is not proof of airflow.","num":{"q":"PNNL says calculated OA fraction should track the damper command within about __.","a":"25%","d":["5%","10%","50%"]},"qs":["cvi-0415"]},{"id":"hvac:a-fixed-min-oa","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: in VAV, a fixed minimum OA damper lets outdoor air fall about in step with supply airflow. 2,000 cfm at 10,000 cfm supply becomes about 1,100 cfm at 5,500.","src":"CEC Nonresidential Compliance Manual 2016 §4.3","prompt":"What happens to outdoor air through a fixed minimum damper as VAV supply airflow drops?","trap":"A fixed damper position is not a fixed outdoor airflow.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0414"]},{"id":"hvac:a-mixing-leak","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: the mixing formula measures damper leakage too. Hot share = (zone - cold deck) / (hot deck - cold deck), so (62 - 55) / (90 - 55) = 20% leaking past a shut hot-deck blade.","src":"USACE ERDC-CERL Multizone Tech Note; mixing arithmetic","prompt":"How do deck and zone temperatures reveal hot-deck damper leakage?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0422"]},{"id":"hvac:a-leaking-heat-valve","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"A temperature rise across a heating coil whose valve is commanded closed means the valve leaks. Cooling running while outdoor air is colder than the discharge setpoint is the PNNL clue.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"What does a temperature rise across a heating coil with its valve commanded closed mean?","trap":"MAT near OAT shows the economizer dampers are open, so they are not the fault.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0421"]},{"id":"hvac:a-co2-occupants","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Indoor CO2 comes mainly from occupants, so it tracks ventilation per person. It is not an overall IAQ indicator: VOCs from building materials do not track it.","src":"ASHRAE Position Document on Indoor CO2","prompt":"What does an indoor CO2 reading tell an inspector, and what does it miss?","trap":"Low CO2 says nothing about carpet or material emissions.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0204","cvi-0285"]},{"id":"hvac:a-co2-700","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"At steady state, 15 cfm of outdoor air per sedentary adult holds indoor CO2 about 700 ppm above outdoors, with outdoor air near 420 ppm.","src":"NIST (Persily); ASHRAE PD on Indoor CO2","prompt":"How far above outdoors does 15 cfm per person hold CO2 at steady state?","trap":"","num":{"q":"At steady state, 15 cfm of outdoor air per person holds CO2 about __ above outdoors.","a":"700 ppm","d":["420 ppm","1,000 ppm","5,000 ppm"]},"qs":["cvi-0204","cvi-0427","cvi-0428"]},{"id":"hvac:a-co2-steady-state","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"NOTE","text":"The CO2 ventilation estimate works only after CO2 levels off. A reading taken while CO2 is still rising overestimates the ventilation rate.","src":"ASHRAE PD on Indoor CO2 (2025)","prompt":"Why can't a still-rising CO2 reading be used to estimate ventilation?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0427","cvi-0428"]},{"id":"hvac:a-co2-example","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: steady CO2 of 1,470 ppm with 420 ppm outdoors gives 15 x 700 / (1,470 - 420) = 10 cfm of outdoor air per person.","src":"ASHRAE PD on Indoor CO2; ASTM D6245","prompt":"Steady CO2 is 1,470 ppm with 420 ppm outdoors: about how much outdoor air per person?","trap":"Using the raw 1,470 reading instead of the difference gives 7 cfm.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0428"]},{"id":"hvac:a-co2-no-limit","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"NOTE","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 sets no indoor CO2 limit for IAQ. 1,000 ppm is shorthand from old guidance, and 5,000 ppm is the OSHA 8-hour workplace exposure limit.","src":"NIST, Quit Blaming ASHRAE 62.1 for 1000 ppm CO2","prompt":"Does ASHRAE 62.1 set an indoor CO2 limit?","trap":"Newer 62.1 CO2 values are setpoints for demand-controlled ventilation, not IAQ limits.","num":{"q":"The OSHA 8-hour exposure limit for CO2 is __.","a":"5,000 ppm","d":["420 ppm","700 ppm","1,000 ppm"]},"qs":["cvi-0205","cvi-0427"]},{"id":"hvac:a-seer2","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"DEF","text":"SEER2 is seasonal cooling output in Btu divided by electrical input in watt-hours over a modeled cooling season.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M1","prompt":"What does SEER2 express?","trap":"EER2 is the single-point rating; HSPF2 is heating; AFUE is fuel.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-eer2","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"DEF","text":"EER2 is a single-point cooling rating at 95 F outdoor (80 F dry bulb, 67 F wet bulb indoor). The Southwest adds an EER2 minimum for split ACs because peak heat matters there.","src":"10 CFR 430.32(c)(6); App. M1","prompt":"How does EER2 differ from SEER2?","trap":"","num":{"q":"EER2 is rated at a single outdoor condition of __.","a":"95 F","d":["47 F","62 F","80 F"]},"qs":["cvi-0056"]},{"id":"hvac:a-hspf2","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"DEF","text":"HSPF2 is a heat pump's seasonal heating output in Btu divided by all its watt-hours for the season, resistance strip heat included.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M1 §1.2, §4.2","prompt":"What does HSPF2 divide by what?","trap":"Strip heat is counted; a single-condition ratio is COP, not HSPF2.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0055"]},{"id":"hvac:a-afue","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"DEF","text":"AFUE is the share of a furnace's annual fuel energy delivered as heat. About 80% is a standard non-condensing furnace; 90% and up needs a condensing secondary heat exchanger.","src":"DOE Energy Saver; 10 CFR 430 App. N","prompt":"What does AFUE rate, and what AFUE marks a condensing furnace?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A standard non-condensing furnace is about __ AFUE.","a":"80%","d":["35%","90%","96%"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-cop","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"DEF","text":"COP is heat delivered divided by electrical energy used at one set of conditions, with no units. Resistance heat is COP 1.0; a COP 4 heat pump gives 4 units of heat per unit of electricity.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M1 §1.2","prompt":"What does a heating COP mean, and why does a heat pump beat resistance heat?","trap":"COP is not Btu per watt-hour over a season, and not kW per ton.","num":{"q":"Electric resistance heat has a COP of __.","a":"1.0","d":["0.8","3.0","4.0"]},"qs":["cvi-0057"]},{"id":"hvac:a-seer2-test-static","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"FACT","text":"The SEER2 test raised external static for conventional ducted systems to 0.50 in. w.c.; the old test used as little as 0.10. The same unit therefore rates lower in SEER2 than in SEER.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M1 Table 4","prompt":"Why is a unit's SEER2 lower than its old SEER?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The SEER2 test uses __ external static for conventional ducted systems.","a":"0.50 in. w.c.","d":["0.10 in. w.c.","0.20 in. w.c.","0.90 in. w.c."]},"qs":["cvi-0054"]},{"id":"hvac:a-tesp-vs-rating","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"FACT","text":"A duct system measuring well above the 0.50 in. w.c. rating static cuts airflow or adds fan power, so delivered capacity and efficiency fall below the label.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M1 Table 4","prompt":"What happens to delivered efficiency when measured TESP is well above the SEER2 test static?","trap":"SEER2 does not cover whatever duct static the house has.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0059"]},{"id":"hvac:a-min-hp-seer2","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"REG","text":"Federal minimums from January 1, 2023: split-system heat pumps need 14.3 SEER2 and single-package heat pumps 13.4 SEER2.","src":"10 CFR 430.32(c)(5)","prompt":"What is the 2023 federal minimum SEER2 for split and packaged heat pumps?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The 2023 federal minimum for a split-system heat pump is __ SEER2.","a":"14.3","d":["11.9","12","13.4"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-min-hp-hspf2","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"REG","text":"Federal minimums from January 1, 2023: split-system heat pumps need 7.5 HSPF2, single-package heat pumps 6.7, and small-duct high-velocity 6.1. The pre-2023 split heat pump minimum was 8.2 HSPF.","src":"10 CFR 430.32(c)(1), (c)(5)","prompt":"What is the 2023 federal minimum HSPF2 for a split-system heat pump?","trap":"8.2 is the old HSPF scale, not HSPF2.","num":{"q":"Since 2023 the federal minimum HSPF2 for a split-system heat pump is __.","a":"7.5","d":["6.1","6.7","8.2"]},"qs":["cvi-0058"]},{"id":"hvac:a-min-ac","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"REG","text":"The 2023 national minimum for split and single-package central air conditioners is 13.4 SEER2. The Southeast and Southwest raise split ACs under 45,000 Btu/h to 14.3 SEER2.","src":"10 CFR 430.32(c)(5), (c)(6)","prompt":"What is the 2023 federal minimum SEER2 for a split air conditioner, and where is it higher?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The 2023 national (North region) minimum for a split air conditioner is __ SEER2.","a":"13.4","d":["12","13.8","14.3"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-uds-sheet-types","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"In National CAD Standard (UDS) numbering, M is mechanical and the next digit is sheet type: 0 general, 1 plans, 2 elevations, 3 sections, 4 large-scale views, 5 details, 6 schedules and diagrams.","src":"NCS/UDS Module 1","prompt":"What do the digits after M mean in UDS sheet numbers?","trap":"","num":{"q":"In UDS numbering, AHU and VAV schedules sit on the M-__ series.","a":"6","d":["0","3","5"]},"qs":["cvi-0096"]},{"id":"hvac:a-legend","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"FACT","text":"Symbols vary between design offices, so each set defines its own in the legend and notes on the 0-series general sheet. Schedules and details use the symbols but do not define them.","src":"NCS/UDS Module 1","prompt":"Where do you confirm what an unfamiliar symbol on a mechanical plan means?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0097"]},{"id":"hvac:a-duct-size-notation","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"FACT","text":"Rectangular duct tags read width first, then depth: 30x14 is 30 in. wide as seen in plan and 14 in. deep.","src":"Drawing convention; project legend","prompt":"How do you read a rectangular duct tag such as 30x14 on a plan?","trap":"Check the legend for whether sizes are clear inside dimensions.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0098"]},{"id":"hvac:a-equipment-schedules","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"FACT","text":"An equipment tag on the plans points to a schedule row with its design data: airflow, external static, capacity, filters and electrical.","src":"Drawing practice","prompt":"Where do you find the design airflow and external static for a tagged AHU?","trap":"Duct tags give sizes only; diffuser and VAV schedules list terminal airflows.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0099"]},{"id":"hvac:a-air-device-schedule","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"FACT","text":"A diffuser schedule gives design cfm and a flow hood gives actual. 150 cfm at an outlet scheduled for 300 is half of design, so look upstream; neck size proves nothing.","src":"Drawing practice","prompt":"How do you judge a flow hood reading against the diffuser schedule?","trap":"Never resize the design to fit a bad reading.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0107"]},{"id":"hvac:a-schedule-vs-field","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"NOTE","text":"The schedule is the design basis. A scheduled MERV 14 final bank missing in the field is a documented deficiency, even when the scheduled MERV 8 prefilters are present.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1, §1.10; drawing practice","prompt":"How is a filter bank that differs from the AHU schedule recorded?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0106"]},{"id":"hvac:a-plan-vs-section","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"A plan is a horizontal view from above. A section is a vertical cut, the view that shows a duct dropping through a shaft past several floors.","src":"NCS/UDS Module 1","prompt":"Which drawing view shows a vertical duct run through several floors?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0101"]},{"id":"hvac:a-detail","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"A detail is a large-scale drawing of one assembly, such as a condensate trap, a damper installation or a duct support. An elevation shows a vertical face.","src":"NCS/UDS Module 1","prompt":"Which view gives an enlarged look at one connection, such as a drain trap?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0105"]},{"id":"hvac:a-line-types","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"NOTE","text":"Line types differ by firm: return may be dashed and exhaust dot-dash, or both dashed. The set's legend and the RA and EA labels at the grilles settle it.","src":"Drawing practice (firm legends)","prompt":"How do you tell whether a dashed duct on a plan is return or exhaust?","trap":"No universal NCS rule makes dashed lines return or exhaust.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0102"]},{"id":"hvac:a-damper-tags","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"Common legend tags: FD fire damper (fusible link, closes on heat), SD smoke damper (closes on a detection signal), FSD combination fire and smoke damper, VD volume damper.","src":"Typical mechanical legends (ASHRAE design set; ODOT M01)","prompt":"What do FD, SD, FSD and VD mean on a mechanical legend?","trap":"A duct smoke detector is not a damper; confirm every tag on the set's legend.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0015","cvi-0100"]},{"id":"hvac:a-other-tags","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"Other common tags: BDD backdraft damper, BD balancing damper, AD access door, FPB fan powered box, RHC reheat coil. Confirm each on the set's legend.","src":"Typical mechanical legends (ASHRAE design set; ODOT M01)","prompt":"What do BDD, BD, AD, FPB and RHC mean on a mechanical legend?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0015","cvi-0100"]},{"id":"hvac:a-piping-tags","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"CHWS and CHWR are chilled water supply and return to AHU coils, CWS and CWR are condenser water between chiller and cooling tower, and HWS and HWR serve heating coils.","src":"Typical mechanical legend (ASHRAE design set)","prompt":"Which piping tags feed an air-side cooling coil, and which run to the cooling tower?","trap":"Condenser water never reaches an air-side coil.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0029"]},{"id":"hvac:a-acr-plans","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"SHALL","text":"If building floor plans and mechanical plans exist, ACR requires using them during the inspection and again during cleaning and restoration.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3","prompt":"What does ACR require about mechanical plans during an inspection?","trap":"It is a SHALL; plans in storage are retrieved, not skipped.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0103"]},{"id":"hvac:a-field-controls","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"NOTE","text":"Plans inform the scope, but the assessment records what the inspection finds. A duct drawn as bare metal but found lined is scoped as porous duct.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3, §1.10","prompt":"When plans and field conditions disagree, which controls the scope?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0104"]},{"id":"hvac:d-fan-creates-airflow","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"FACT","text":"The supply fan or blower is the component that creates airflow in an air-handling system. Coils, filters, attenuators and dampers only add resistance or control it, and diffusers only release it into the room.","src":"ASHRAE Handbook, HVAC Systems and Equipment; ACR 2025 definitions, air-handling unit","prompt":"Which component in an air handler creates the airflow, and what do the other components do to it?","trap":"A sound attenuator or damper changes airflow; it never creates it.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:d-return-plenum","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"DEF","text":"A return plenum is a chamber or path that carries return air back to the air handler. It conveys air but does not filter it.","src":"ACR 2025 definitions; NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper (2023)","prompt":"What does a return plenum do, and does it filter the air?","trap":"Being a plenum does not make it a filter location. Air is filtered at the filter bank.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:d-diffuser","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"DEF","text":"A supply diffuser is the outlet that spreads supply air into the occupied space in a set pattern so it mixes with room air. A grille is a plain louvered face, most often used on returns.","src":"Titus Engineering Guidelines, grilles, registers and diffusers; ASHRAE Terminology","prompt":"What does a diffuser do that a plain grille does not?","trap":"A damper is not what makes a diffuser. A grille with a damper is a register.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:d-register","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"DEF","text":"A register is a grille with an integral damper, so the airflow through it can be adjusted or shut off at the face.","src":"Titus Engineering Guidelines, grilles, registers and diffusers; ASHRAE Terminology","prompt":"What is a register?","trap":"A balancing damper out in the branch or trunk is not a register. The damper is built into the outlet.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:d-throw","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"DEF","text":"Throw is the distance a supply jet travels from the outlet face before its velocity falls to a stated terminal velocity, which catalogs list at 150, 100 and 50 fpm.","src":"Titus Engineering Corner, What is Throw? What is Terminal Velocity? (2017); CSE, Air Jet Dynamics of Air Diffusers","prompt":"What is an outlet's throw, and how do catalogs state it?","trap":"Drop is how far the jet falls and spread is how wide it fans. Neither one is throw.","num":{"q":"Diffuser catalogs list throw to terminal velocities of 150, 100 and __ fpm.","a":"50","d":["25","75","200"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:d-coanda","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"DEF","text":"The Coanda effect makes a jet cling to a nearby surface. A ceiling diffuser's jet hugs the ceiling, which keeps cold air up, lets it mix with room air, and carries it farther than a free jet.","src":"CSE, Air Jet Dynamics of Air Diffusers","prompt":"How does the Coanda effect help a ceiling diffuser?","trap":"It holds cold air up at the ceiling. It does not push the jet down onto occupants.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:d-dumping","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"FACT","text":"At low VAV airflow a cold supply jet can lose its grip on the ceiling and fall into the occupied space before it mixes, causing cold drafts. Engineers call it excessive drop; techs call it dumping.","src":"CSE, Air Jet Dynamics of Air Diffusers","prompt":"What happens to a ceiling diffuser jet at very low VAV airflow?","trap":"Dumping comes from too little airflow at the outlet, not too much.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:d-smudging","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"FACT","text":"Dark smudging around a ceiling diffuser usually comes from room air the jet induces along the ceiling, carrying lint and dust from the room. By itself it does not prove the ductwork is dirty.","src":"ACHR News, Smudging Around Air Distribution Diffusers (2012-12-03)","prompt":"What usually causes smudge rings around ceiling diffusers, and what do they prove about the ducts?","trap":"Blaming duct debris. The smudge alone proves nothing, so inspect the ducts before recommending cleaning.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0053"]},{"id":"hvac:d-laminar-or","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 170 requires unidirectional, downward airflow from an operating room's primary supply array: nonaspirating or laminar flow diffusers averaging 25 to 35 cfm per sq ft, extending 12 in. past each side of the table.","src":"Price Industries, How Is ASHRAE Standard 170 Applied to Hospital Operating Rooms?; ANSI/ASHRAE/ASHE 170","prompt":"What does ASHRAE 170 require of the primary supply diffuser array over an operating table?","trap":"These diffusers are built not to mix. A high-induction mixing diffuser defeats the clean air curtain over the table.","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 170 sets the minimum extension of the OR primary diffuser array past each side of the surgical table at __.","a":"12 in.","d":["6 in.","18 in.","24 in."]},"qs":[]},{"id":"nim:a-first-step","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-inspection-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"A visual inspection of HVAC components is the first step of NADCA's assess, clean, and restore procedure described in ACR.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","prompt":"Where does the visual inspection sit in NADCA's assessment, cleaning, and restoration procedure?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0500"]},{"id":"nim:a-cleanliness-def","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-inspection-basics","tag":"DEF","text":"HVAC system cleanliness is judged by the presence of dirt, obstructions, excess moisture, and microbial contamination that could affect performance or occupant health and comfort.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","prompt":"Which four conditions define HVAC system cleanliness in the manual?","trap":"Odors, CO2, and VOCs are IAQ measures, not part of the cleanliness definition.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0501"]},{"id":"nim:a-written-plan","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-inspection-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"After contact, document review, a walkthrough, and a purpose and scope discussion, the inspector drafts a written plan that is reviewed and approved before the detailed inspection.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","prompt":"What must be in place before the detailed inspection begins?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0502"]},{"id":"nim:a-scope-components","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-inspection-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"The inspection covers AHU equipment plus supply ducts, return ducts, outdoor intake make-up ducts, and air exhaust systems that fall within the agreed scope.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","prompt":"Which equipment and ducts does the actual inspection include?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0503"]},{"id":"nim:a-samples-optional","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-inspection-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"Measurements and samples are not automatic; the inspector decides whether to take them depending on whether contamination is found.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","prompt":"Must an inspector always take measurements and samples?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0504"]},{"id":"nim:a-report-recs","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-inspection-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"The most important part of the report is its recommendations: whether the system should be cleaned and whether anything needs a specialist HVAC professional.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","prompt":"What is the most important content of the inspection report?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0505"]},{"id":"nim:a-acr-routine","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-why-and-when","tag":"REC","text":"ACR recommends routine inspections within a proactive energy and IAQ management plan, and cleaning when an inspection shows accumulated particulate or microbial growth.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.2","prompt":"Per ACR as cited in the manual, when should routine inspections occur and when should cleaning follow?","trap":"Cleaning is triggered by inspection findings, not by a fixed calendar interval.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0506"]},{"id":"nim:a-due-diligence","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-why-and-when","tag":"FACT","text":"Inspection records let a client establish due diligence if questioned about how the facility was maintained.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.2","prompt":"How can inspection records protect a client questioned about maintenance?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0507"]},{"id":"nim:a-documentation-sale","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-why-and-when","tag":"FACT","text":"Clients may order an inspection to verify a cleaning or to compile a complete building documentation package, which is sometimes required when a building is sold.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.2","prompt":"Which inspection purpose is tied to selling a building?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0508"]},{"id":"nim:a-annual","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-why-and-when","tag":"REC","text":"NADCA formally recommends an annual HVAC system inspection for commercial buildings; other building types are outside the manual's scope.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.3","prompt":"How often does NADCA recommend inspecting commercial HVAC systems?","trap":"Annual is NADCA; quarterly is the NFPA 90A annex suggestion.","num":{"q":"NADCA formally recommends inspecting commercial HVAC systems every __ .","a":"12 months","d":["3 months","6 months","24 months"]},"qs":["cvi-0509"]},{"id":"nim:a-nfpa90a-quarterly","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-why-and-when","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 90A Section B-4 suggests quarterly duct inspections for dust and waste, with a longer interval allowed if several inspections show that frequency is unnecessary.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.3","prompt":"What inspection interval does NFPA 90A Section B-4 suggest?","trap":"Quarterly is the NFPA 90A annex suggestion; annual is NADCA's recommendation.","num":{"q":"NFPA 90A Section B-4 suggests inspecting ducts for dust and waste every __ .","a":"3 months","d":["6 months","12 months","24 months"]},"qs":["cvi-0510"]},{"id":"nim:a-table-1-2","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-a-why-and-when","tag":"FACT","text":"Table 1-2 pairs ACGIH with Bioaerosols: Assessment and Control, NAFA with filtration system maintenance, NAIMA with the insulated air duct guide, and SMACNA with duct construction standards.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 1-2","prompt":"Which publications does Table 1-2 assign to ACGIH, NAFA, NAIMA, and SMACNA?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0511"]},{"id":"nim:a-qual-knowledge","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-inspector-conduct","tag":"REC","text":"Inspectors need verifiable working knowledge of HVAC design, engineering practice, cleaning and restoration methods, and standards, plus experience verifying cleanliness and creating and sealing openings.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.4","prompt":"What knowledge and experience should inspection personnel have at minimum?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0512"]},{"id":"nim:a-disclose-cleaning","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-inspector-conduct","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspector who also performs cleaning or restoration must disclose that to the client before the inspection begins.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.4","prompt":"When must an inspector who also cleans disclose it?","trap":"The manual requires disclosure, not refusal of the job.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0513"]},{"id":"nim:a-disclose-interest","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-inspector-conduct","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspector must disclose to the client any outside interest related to the outcome of the inspection.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.4","prompt":"What must an inspector disclose about outside interests?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0514"]},{"id":"nim:a-objective","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-inspector-conduct","tag":"FACT","text":"During the inspection the inspector stays objective, records whether each component is clean or has an identified issue, and is direct and factual with the client.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.4","prompt":"What should the inspector focus on recording during the inspection?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0515"]},{"id":"nim:a-refer-experts","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-inspector-conduct","tag":"FACT","text":"CVI training does not make an inspector an expert in any single IAQ field; he must contact experts in industrial hygiene, air balancing, filtration, microbiology, and bioaerosols when needed.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.4","prompt":"What must an inspector do when a problem exceeds his training?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0516"]},{"id":"nim:a-specialist-creds","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-inspector-conduct","tag":"REC","text":"Specialists on an inspection team should be credentialed: licensed mechanical engineers, NEBB or AABC certified test and balance firms, and AIHA (or equivalent) certified industrial hygienists.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.4","prompt":"Which credentials does the manual recommend for engineers, TAB firms, and industrial hygienists?","trap":"NEBB and AABC are for balancing firms; AIHA is for industrial hygienists.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0523","cvi-0524"]},{"id":"nim:a-report-product","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-insurance-licensing","tag":"FACT","text":"The inspection is a professional service and the report is its product, so standard liability insurance may not cover errors in the report.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.1","prompt":"Why may standard liability insurance fall short for an inspector?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0517"]},{"id":"nim:a-eo-def","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-insurance-licensing","tag":"DEF","text":"Professional liability insurance, commonly called Errors and Omissions (E&O), covers claims tied directly to providing professional services; inspectors should consult counsel and insurance professionals.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.1","prompt":"What is professional liability insurance commonly called, and what does it cover?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0518"]},{"id":"nim:a-eo-exclusions","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-insurance-licensing","tag":"NOTE","text":"E&O does not cover automobile, general liability, fidelity, property, or workers compensation losses, and it may not cover the inspector for negligence.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.1","prompt":"Which losses fall outside an E&O policy?","trap":"E&O is not a substitute for general liability or workers comp coverage.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0519"]},{"id":"nim:a-licensing-tasks","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-insurance-licensing","tag":"FACT","text":"Some states and provinces license cutting access openings, removing unit access panels, pulling and cleaning fan assemblies, and cleaning evaporator coils.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.2","prompt":"Which inspection-related tasks may require a license in some jurisdictions?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0520"]},{"id":"nim:a-licensing-responsibility","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-insurance-licensing","tag":"FACT","text":"The inspector is responsible for meeting all local, state, country, or province licensing and certification requirements set by the authority having jurisdiction.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.2","prompt":"Who must ensure licensing requirements are met for an inspection?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0521"]},{"id":"nim:a-cite-standards","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-insurance-licensing","tag":"REC","text":"Inspectors should keep a library of industry standards and cite recognized, peer-reviewed documents (NADCA, NIOSH, NFPA, NAFA, ASHRAE, EPA, CDC, USGBC) behind each recommendation.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.3","prompt":"What makes an inspection report's recommendations credible?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0522"]},{"id":"nim:a-detector-permits","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-hazcom-loto-space","tag":"SHALL","text":"Inspections should not impair smoke or fire detection equipment; any permits, code-required notices, or related communications for temporary changes are the facility manager's responsibility.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 A","prompt":"Who handles permits and notifications when detectors must be temporarily altered?","trap":"The facility manager, not the inspector, owns permits and code notices.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0525"]},{"id":"nim:a-osha-act","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-ppe-respirators","tag":"REG","text":"The Occupational Safety and Health Act of 1970 guarantees a safe workplace and compliance is mandatory for every employer and employee, with few exceptions.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 B","prompt":"Who must comply with the OSH Act of 1970?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0526"]},{"id":"nim:a-foot","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-ppe-respirators","tag":"FACT","text":"Work shoes need thick soles and ankle support; steel toes guard against dropped objects and heat-resistant soles against hot surfaces such as rooftops.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","prompt":"Which boot features does the manual pair with which hazards?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0527"]},{"id":"nim:a-eye-reqs","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-ppe-respirators","tag":"REG","text":"Eye protectors must protect against the hazard, be reasonably comfortable, fit snugly without limiting movement or vision, be durable, disinfectable, easily cleanable, and kept clean and in good repair.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","prompt":"What minimum requirements must eye protectors meet?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0528"]},{"id":"nim:a-resp-program","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-ppe-respirators","tag":"REG","text":"Employers are responsible for air monitoring and must develop and implement a written respiratory protection program with workplace-specific procedures, and keep its records.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","prompt":"What program does the OSHA respirator standard require of employers?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0529"]},{"id":"nim:a-resp-approval","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-ppe-respirators","tag":"REG","text":"The manual defines an approved respirator as one certified jointly by NIOSH and MSHA (today NIOSH certifies under 42 CFR Part 84), used under the conditions of that certification.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","prompt":"Which agencies jointly certify an approved respirator?","trap":"OSHA sets the program rule but does not certify respirators.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0530"]},{"id":"nim:a-fit-test-types","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-ppe-respirators","tag":"DEF","text":"A qualitative fit test relies on the wearer detecting an odor or irritant; a quantitative fit test measures aerosol, particulates, or pressure for more accurate results.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","prompt":"How do qualitative and quantitative fit tests differ?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0531"]},{"id":"nim:a-resp-medical","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-ppe-respirators","tag":"REG","text":"Before using a respirator, an employee must be medically cleared by a physician or licensed health care professional using the OSHA Respirator Medical Evaluation Questionnaire.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","prompt":"Who medically clears a respirator user, and with what tool?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0532"]},{"id":"nim:a-hazcom-elements","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-hazcom-loto-space","tag":"REG","text":"HazCom's four major compliance elements are Safety Data Sheets, labels, training, and a written hazard communication program.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 D","prompt":"What are the four major elements of HazCom compliance?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0533"]},{"id":"nim:a-physical-vs-health","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-hazcom-loto-space","tag":"DEF","text":"A physical hazard is flammable, explosive, or reactive; a health hazard causes acute (immediate) or chronic (long-term) health problems from exposure.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 D","prompt":"How does a physical hazard differ from a health hazard?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0534"]},{"id":"nim:a-tagout-when","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-hazcom-loto-space","tag":"REG","text":"When an energy-isolating device cannot be locked, the inspector uses tagout, securing a tag that stays on for the whole inspection and that only he removes.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 E","prompt":"What does the inspector do when an isolating device cannot take a lock?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0535"]},{"id":"nim:a-lock-removal","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-hazcom-loto-space","tag":"REG","text":"If someone else must remove a lock: call the employee, try outside contact, notify the supervisor, notify the safety coordinator, verify the equipment is clear, then remove it.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 E","prompt":"What is the documented order for removing another person's lock?","trap":"Calling the employee comes first; verifying the equipment is clear comes just before removal.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0536"]},{"id":"nim:a-prcs","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-a-hazcom-loto-space","tag":"DEF","text":"A confined space becomes permit-required if it has hazardous atmosphere potential, engulfment material, a trapping configuration, or another serious hazard; entry waits for an approved plan.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 F","prompt":"What makes a confined space permit-required, and what must precede entry?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0537"]},{"id":"nim:b-single-zone","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"DEF","text":"A single zone system is run by one thermostat that cycles the AHU or adjusts coil flow, so the whole zone gets one supply temperature. That limits it to spaces with uniform loads.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"How is a single zone system controlled, and what limits where it fits?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0560"]},{"id":"nim:b-cav-def","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"DEF","text":"A constant air volume (CAV) system sends a fixed airflow to each space and changes temperature by heating or cooling the air or cycling the AHU. It has no fan modulation and no terminal dampering.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"How does a CAV system change space temperature?","trap":"Throttling a terminal damper is VAV behaviour, not CAV.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0550"]},{"id":"nim:b-cav-uniform","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"Without reheat, CAV delivers one uniform supply temperature to all zones, so it is typically limited to large open areas with few windows and even loads, such as factories, exhibit halls, and auditoriums.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What kinds of spaces is a CAV system (without reheat) typically limited to?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0551"]},{"id":"nim:b-cav-vs-vav","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"CAV systems are less energy-efficient than VAV systems, but their outdoor air delivery controls are simpler to manage.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"How does CAV compare with VAV on energy and outdoor air control?","trap":"Two halves: CAV loses on energy but wins on outdoor air control simplicity.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0552"]},{"id":"nim:b-economizer","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"DEF","text":"An air economizer is a damper and control system that adds outdoor air directly to recirculated air for cooling or dehumidification during mild weather.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What is an air economizer?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0553"]},{"id":"nim:b-fan-on","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"REC","text":"Thermostat fan switches should be set to ON, not AUTO, so outdoor air is supplied continuously. On AUTO the fan, and the outdoor air it brings, runs only when heating or cooling is called for.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Why should the thermostat fan switch be ON rather than AUTO?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0554"]},{"id":"nim:b-vav-def","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"DEF","text":"A variable air volume (VAV) system keeps comfort by varying the amount of conditioned air to each zone, using a thermostat-controlled damper in a VAV box. A zone overcools or overheats if the system is not adjusted to its load.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"How does a VAV system control zone comfort?","trap":"VAV changes volume, not supply temperature.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0555"]},{"id":"nim:b-vav-zone-size","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"Because each VAV zone needs its own relatively costly VAV box, VAV zones usually cover large areas grouping spaces with similar solar exposure, occupancy, and loads.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Why do VAV zones usually cover large areas of similar spaces?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0556"]},{"id":"nim:b-vav-oa-quantity","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"VAV under-ventilation is common unless the system is set to bring in at least a minimum quantity (cfm), not a percentage, of outdoor air as it throttles back from full flow.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What must a VAV system hold to avoid under-ventilation as it throttles back?","trap":"Quantity, not percentage. A fixed percentage of a shrinking flow shrinks.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0557"]},{"id":"nim:b-vav-oa-partload","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"With a constant outdoor air percentage, VAV outdoor air volume falls as supply volume falls. A low outdoor air percentage plus a low VAV box minimum can starve spaces of outdoor air at part load.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What happens to VAV outdoor air at part load with a fixed percentage?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0558"]},{"id":"nim:b-vav-sat-low","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-cav-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A VAV system can also under-ventilate when its supply air temperature is set too low for the zone loads, because the boxes then throttle to low airflow.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Besides a fixed outdoor air percentage, what supply setting causes VAV under-ventilation?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0559"]},{"id":"nim:b-mz-def","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","tag":"DEF","text":"In a multizone system each zone has a dedicated duct from the central AHU, where dampers blend warm and cool air to each zone thermostat's call, so zones get different temperatures.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What defines a multizone system?","trap":"Multizone blends at the AHU; dual duct blends at a box in the zone.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0561"]},{"id":"nim:b-mz-temps","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","tag":"FACT","text":"In the most common multizone design the AHU makes warm air near 100°F and cool air at about 55°F, then blends them with dampers for each zone.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What warm and cool air temperatures does a typical multizone AHU produce?","num":{"q":"A typical multizone AHU makes warm air near __ and cool air at about 55°F.","a":"100°F","d":["90°F","120°F","140°F"]},"qs":["cvi-0562"]},{"id":"nim:b-mz-energy","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","tag":"FACT","text":"Multizone systems, popular in the 1960s and 1970s, heat and cool at the same time. That high energy use caused their decline and bans in local building codes around the country.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Why have multizone systems declined and been banned by codes?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0563"]},{"id":"nim:b-mz-dampers","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","tag":"FACT","text":"Multizone blending dampers sit in high-velocity areas near the fan, which causes significant pressure drop and additional energy use.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What does the location of multizone dampers near the fan cause?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0564"]},{"id":"nim:b-dd-def","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","tag":"DEF","text":"A dual-duct system runs separate hot deck and cold deck ducts in parallel and mixes them in a terminal mixing box at each zone, with a damper driven by the zone thermostat, like a shower valve.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"How and where does a dual-duct system mix air for each zone?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0565"]},{"id":"nim:b-dd-cav-vav","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","tag":"FACT","text":"Dual-duct systems may be designed as either CAV or VAV systems.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Are dual-duct systems CAV or VAV?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0566"]},{"id":"nim:b-dd-energy","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","tag":"FACT","text":"Dual-duct systems work well but use more energy than modern systems. They lost favor after the 1973 energy crisis and, like multizone, are prohibited under many new construction codes.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What is the energy and code status of dual-duct systems?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0570"]},{"id":"nim:b-dd-highvel","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","tag":"FACT","text":"In dual-duct systems, expect the hot and cold deck ducts ahead of the mixing boxes to run at high velocity.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Where do dual-duct systems likely have high-velocity duct?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0567"]},{"id":"nim:b-dd-leak","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","tag":"NOTE","text":"Mixing box dampers in dual-duct systems may not seal tight, so they can pass air even when set fully closed.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What is the known weakness of dual-duct mixing box dampers?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0568"]},{"id":"nim:b-dd-humidity","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","tag":"FACT","text":"Dual-duct systems may control humidity poorly because the hot and cold airstreams being mixed have different humidity levels.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Why can dual-duct systems control humidity poorly?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0569"]},{"id":"nim:b-hp-def","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","tag":"DEF","text":"A heat pump works like a refrigerator: it extracts heat from the air or ground and releases it at a higher temperature inside the building.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"How does a heat pump heat a building?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0571"]},{"id":"nim:b-hp-50f","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","tag":"FACT","text":"An air-source heat pump is practical where air temperature does not drop much below 50°F. At lower temperatures heat may be drawn from the ground or groundwater, though this can be costly.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"In what climate is an air-source heat pump practical?","num":{"q":"A heat pump is practical where outdoor air does not drop much below __ .","a":"50°F","d":["32°F","55°F","65°F"]},"qs":["cvi-0572"]},{"id":"nim:b-hp-aux","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","tag":"FACT","text":"Most heat pumps installed now must include an auxiliary electric resistance element to add heating capacity when the heat pump becomes inefficient. The strips also improve heating time.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Why do most heat pumps include auxiliary electric resistance heat?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0573"]},{"id":"nim:b-hp-ari","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","tag":"FACT","text":"ARI rates five heat pump types: unitary air-source (HP), ground water-source (GWHP), packaged terminal (PTHP), water-source (WSHP), and ground-source closed loop (GSHP, geothermal).","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What heat pump categories does ARI list?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0574"]},{"id":"nim:b-wshp","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","tag":"FACT","text":"WSHPs are the most common commercial heat pumps. Each zone has a closed-loop unit giving heating or cooling, filtration, and dehumidification, on a shared loop tied to a cooling tower in summer and a boiler in winter.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What is the most common commercial heat pump arrangement and how is its loop served?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0575"]},{"id":"nim:b-hp-problems","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","tag":"FACT","text":"Harsh field conditions make regular heat pump maintenance critical. Field studies report low airflows, incorrect refrigerant charge, refrigerant leaks, control problems, and excessive duct leakage.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What common heat pump problems do field studies report?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0576"]},{"id":"nim:b-hp-pan","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","tag":"REC","text":"Inspectors should evaluate each heat pump's installation and the pitch of its internal condensate pan; a unit not suspended properly from the structure may not drain.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What should the inspector check on suspended heat pumps?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0577"]},{"id":"nim:b-fcu-def","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","tag":"DEF","text":"A fan coil unit's fan pulls air through a filter and across a hot or chilled water coil. Cooling condensate goes to a drip pan and drain. Most sit under exterior windows; some hang at the ceiling.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"How does a fan coil unit work and where is it usually mounted?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0578"]},{"id":"nim:b-fcu-use","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","tag":"FACT","text":"Fan coil units often discharge directly into the room without ducts and are typical in buildings with many perimeter zones, such as schools, hotels, apartments, and offices.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Where are fan coil units typically used?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0579"]},{"id":"nim:b-fcu-neglect","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","tag":"NOTE","text":"Self-contained units such as fan coils and unit ventilators that are overlooked in maintenance often become a significant contaminant source, especially for occupants close to them.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What happens when fan coils are left out of maintenance?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0580"]},{"id":"nim:b-plenum-def","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-plenum-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Ceiling or underfloor cavities can serve as supply or return plenums. This often cuts first cost but demands strict life safety code compliance for materials and devices in the plenum.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What is the trade-off of using building cavities as plenums?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0581"]},{"id":"nim:b-plenum-materials","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-plenum-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Inspectors should look for materials such as paint and cleaners stored in plenums; they can contaminate the air or disrupt airflow and are usually prohibited by building codes.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Why note stored materials in a ceiling plenum?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0582"]},{"id":"nim:b-plenum-moisture","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-plenum-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"In plenums, look for condensation on pipes and water-damaged tiles (moisture for microbial growth) and for a negative return plenum reaching the exterior, which couples the HVAC system to the building enclosure.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What moisture and pressure signs should an inspector look for in a ceiling plenum?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0583"]},{"id":"nim:b-exhaust-recommended","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-plenum-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Codes usually require exhaust for toilets, janitorial closets, cooking areas, and parking garages. Exhaust is recommended but not always required for reprographics, graphic arts, beauty salons, and other known sources.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"Which areas require exhaust by code, and which are only recommended?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0584"]},{"id":"nim:b-exhaust-isolate","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-plenum-exhaust","tag":"SHALL","text":"An exhausted area must be kept at lower pressure than its surroundings, including wall cavities and plenums, and must be isolated from the return air system. Louvered door panels may be needed for replacement air.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What conditions must an exhausted area meet?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0585"]},{"id":"nim:b-exhaust-duct","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-plenum-exhaust","tag":"REC","text":"For local exhaust areas, check the room runs negative to its surroundings and the fan's discharge duct is intact all the way to the outside vent, or contaminated air returns to the building.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What should the inspector check on local exhaust rooms and ducts?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0586"]},{"id":"nim:b-pressurization","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-b-plenum-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Buildings should be designed and run at slightly positive or neutral pressure to limit outdoor pollutants entering through unintended paths. Slight positive pressure also needs tight construction. Note damper positions.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","prompt":"What building pressure does the manual favor, and what does it require?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0587"]},{"id":"nim:c-coil-45f","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"FACT","text":"A chilled-water cooling coil dehumidifies only when the chilled water stays cold enough, generally below 45°F.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 A","prompt":"How cold must chilled water be for a cooling coil to dehumidify?","trap":"55°F is a typical supply air temperature, not the chilled water limit.","num":{"q":"Chilled water must generally be kept below __ for a cooling coil to dehumidify.","a":"45°F","d":["55°F","50°F","65°F"]},"qs":["cvi-0600"]},{"id":"nim:c-reheat-coil","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"FACT","text":"In CAV systems, zone reheat coils sit at or near the diffuser and switch on or off from the room thermostat to rewarm cooled air.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 A","prompt":"Where are CAV zone reheat coils located and what controls them?","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"nim:c-leaky-valve","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"NOTE","text":"Leaky coil valves pass hot or chilled water with no demand, and dirty coils do the same harm: wasted energy and thermal discomfort.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 A","prompt":"What results from coil valves that leak water through the coil with no demand?","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"nim:c-steam-potable","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"REC","text":"Steam humidifiers should use potable water, not treated boiler water, so occupants are not exposed to boiler treatment chemicals.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 B","prompt":"What water should feed a steam humidifier, and why?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0601"]},{"id":"nim:c-space-humidistat","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"FACT","text":"Mold growth problems become more likely when the occupied-space humidistat set point is above 45%.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 B","prompt":"Above what occupied-space humidistat set point does mold risk rise?","trap":"45% is the space set point; 70% is the duct high-limit; 65% is the ASHRAE 62.1 office ceiling.","num":{"q":"Mold problems are more likely if the occupied-space humidistat is set above __ .","a":"45%","d":["65%","70%","55%"]},"qs":["cvi-0602"]},{"id":"nim:c-highlimit-70","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"FACT","text":"The high-limit humidistat sits in the duct downstream of where vapor is added and is generally set at 70% to prevent duct condensation and mold.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 B","prompt":"Where is a humidifier high-limit humidistat located and what is its typical setting?","num":{"q":"The duct high-limit humidistat is generally set at __ RH.","a":"70%","d":["45%","65%","90%"]},"qs":["cvi-0603"]},{"id":"nim:c-ashrae-rh65","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"FACT","text":"Citing ASHRAE 62.1-2013, the manual says office relative humidity is generally best kept below 65%.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 B","prompt":"Below what RH does ASHRAE 62.1-2013 guidance prefer office buildings stay?","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1-2013 guidance prefers office RH below __ .","a":"65%","d":["45%","70%","55%"]},"qs":["cvi-0604"]},{"id":"nim:c-mist-eliminator","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"FACT","text":"Spray and compressed-air atomizing humidifiers make large droplets that do not evaporate in the AHU, so baffle mist eliminators catch them by inertial impaction into a sloped, drained pan.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 T","prompt":"Why do atomizing humidifiers need mist eliminators, and how do the eliminators work?","trap":"Check for carryover: water past the eliminator into unsealed duct grows microbes.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0605"]},{"id":"nim:c-filter-low","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-filters","tag":"FACT","text":"Low-efficiency filters (dust spot 10% to 20% or less) mainly keep lint and dust from clogging the heating and cooling coils.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","prompt":"What is the main job of low-efficiency filters?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0606"]},{"id":"nim:c-filter-bands","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-filters","tag":"FACT","text":"Dust spot bands in the manual: low 10% to 20% or less, medium 30% to 60% (pleated extended-surface recommended), high-efficiency extended-surface 85% to 95%.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","prompt":"What dust spot ranges define low, medium, and high-efficiency filters?","num":{"q":"Medium-efficiency filters have an ASHRAE dust spot rating of __ .","a":"30% to 60%","d":["10% to 20%","85% to 95%","60% to 80%"]},"qs":["cvi-0607"]},{"id":"nim:c-filter-loading","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-filters","tag":"FACT","text":"As a filter loads, its particle removal efficiency rises but its pressure drop rises too, reducing airflow.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","prompt":"What happens to efficiency and pressure drop as a filter loads with dust?","trap":"A dirty filter filters better, not worse; the cost is airflow.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0608"]},{"id":"nim:c-filter-blowout","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-filters","tag":"FACT","text":"Overloaded low-efficiency filters deform and blow out of the rack; the bypassed air clogs coils and dirties ducts. Unsealed filter cells also let air bypass.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","prompt":"What happens when low-efficiency filters are loaded to excess?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0609"]},{"id":"nim:c-filter-medium-best","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-filters","tag":"REC","text":"In many buildings a medium-efficiency pleated filter is the best choice: better removal than low-efficiency filters and longer life without clogging than high-efficiency ones.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","prompt":"Why is a medium-efficiency pleated filter often the best choice?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0610"]},{"id":"nim:c-hepa","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-filters","tag":"FACT","text":"HEPA filters use thin dry media that traps particles down to 0.01 micrometer; heavy pleating lowers face velocity, but they still add up to 2 inches w.g. pressure drop.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","prompt":"How small a particle can HEPA media trap, and what pressure drop can it add?","num":{"q":"HEPA filters can still add pressure drops of up to __ .","a":"2 inches w.g.","d":["0.5 inch w.g.","2.5 inches w.g.","3 inches w.g."]},"qs":["cvi-0611"]},{"id":"nim:c-merv17-dropped","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-c-filters","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE dropped the MERV 17 to 20 classes because it has no test procedure for HEPA filters; HEPA and ULPA are listed at 99.97% to 99.9999%.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","prompt":"Why were MERV 17 to 20 dropped from the ASHRAE classifications?","trap":"ACR 2025 no longer uses MERV 17-20 either; HEPA is its own rating.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0612"]},{"id":"nim:c-std521","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-c-filters","tag":"FACT","text":"ANSI/ASHRAE 52.1 (dust spot and arrestance) was discontinued in January 2009; its values appear for reference only. MERV comes from ASHRAE 52.2 on a 16-point scale.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","prompt":"What is the status of ANSI/ASHRAE 52.1, and which standard produces MERV?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0613"]},{"id":"nim:c-merv-table","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-filters","tag":"FACT","text":"Table 2-1: MERV 1-4 over 10 um, minimum protection; 5-8 3-10 um, pleated; 9-12 1-3 um, better commercial; 13-16 0.3-1 um, hospitals, general surgery, smoke removal.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","prompt":"What particle sizes and applications go with each MERV group?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0614"]},{"id":"nim:c-ptrap-seal","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"FACT","text":"A P-trap's U-shaped water seal isolates the drain system from the condensate pan; it must stay wet so a draw-through unit does not pull air in through the drain.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 F","prompt":"What does a condensate P-trap do, and why must it stay wet?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0615"]},{"id":"nim:c-trap-negative","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"FACT","text":"Under negative pressure (upstream of the fan), a poor trap can keep the pan from draining, and a drain near a sewer vent can pull contaminants into the system.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 F","prompt":"What two problems come from a poor trap under negative pressure?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0616"]},{"id":"nim:c-lowest-fitting","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"FACT","text":"When a drain pan has several fittings, the inspector checks that the drain is connected to the lowest one so the pan does not hold water.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 F","prompt":"Which pan fitting should the condensate drain use?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0617"]},{"id":"nim:c-trap-primer","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"FACT","text":"Even a properly installed trap becomes a contamination path if its water evaporates; trap primers keep traps from drying out in heating season.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 D","prompt":"Why are trap primers used on condensate traps?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0618"]},{"id":"nim:c-heat-stack-trap","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-coils-humidity","tag":"SHALL","text":"Where a heating stack drain joins the cooling coil drain, a trap belongs in the stack drain first to keep combustion gases out; drains must follow the manufacturer.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 F","prompt":"What goes in a heating stack condensate drain before it joins the coil drain?","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"nim:c-damper-types","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-dampers-fire","tag":"DEF","text":"Four common dampers: round butterfly (round duct), single-blade (square duct), parallel-blade box (deflects air when partly closed), opposed-blade box (keeps air direction).","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 G","prompt":"What are the four common damper types and how do the box dampers differ?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0619"]},{"id":"nim:c-bird-screen","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-dampers-fire","tag":"FACT","text":"An outdoor air intake bird screen should be unobstructed, free of droppings, with mesh no larger than 1/2 inch.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 G","prompt":"What condition should an outdoor air bird screen be in?","num":{"q":"An outdoor air bird screen should have mesh no larger than __ .","a":"1/2 inch","d":["1/4 inch","3/4 inch","1 inch"]},"qs":["cvi-0620"]},{"id":"nim:c-freezestat","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-dampers-fire","tag":"NOTE","text":"A freezestat protects coils by closing the outdoor air damper; ventilation suffers if it trips and is not reset or is set too high.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 G","prompt":"How can a freezestat cause inadequate ventilation?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0621"]},{"id":"nim:c-fd-location","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-c-dampers-fire","tag":"CODE","text":"Fire dampers are installed where a duct passes through a fire-rated barrier such as a wall, partition, or floor. The inspector checks they are fully open and not obstructing airflow.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 H","prompt":"Where are fire dampers installed?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0622"]},{"id":"nim:c-fd-fuselink","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-dampers-fire","tag":"FACT","text":"A fire damper's accordion-folded blade stack closes automatically when a temperature-sensitive fuse link detects elevated temperature.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 H","prompt":"What triggers a fire damper's blade stack to close?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0623"]},{"id":"nim:c-fd-types","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-dampers-fire","tag":"DEF","text":"Fire damper designs: A-type stack at the top protrudes into the airstream; B-type stack sits in a channel out of the airstream; C-type adds a transition plate and collar for higher pressure.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 H","prompt":"How do A, B, and C-type fire dampers differ?","trap":"Static vs dynamic is a separate category: whether it closes with the fan off or running.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0624","cvi-0626"]},{"id":"nim:c-fd-pressure","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-c-dampers-fire","tag":"FACT","text":"A-type and B-type fire dampers are for low pressure systems, generally under 3 inches w.g.; higher pressure systems use C-type.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 H","prompt":"What pressure limit applies to A and B-type fire dampers?","num":{"q":"A and B-type fire dampers are for systems generally under __ .","a":"3 inches w.g.","d":["1 inch w.g.","2 inches w.g.","6 inches w.g."]},"qs":["cvi-0625"]},{"id":"nim:c-fan-types","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-terminals","tag":"DEF","text":"Forward-curved fans move large volumes for a small wheel and are most common; backward-inclined fans are non-overloading; axial fans move lots of air at low pressure quietly.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 I","prompt":"What distinguishes forward-curved, backward-inclined, and axial fans?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0627"]},{"id":"nim:c-packaged-oa","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-terminals","tag":"NOTE","text":"Packaged units seldom supply outdoor air, so ventilation must come from other means; they are often low-priority maintenance and can become contaminant sources.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 K","prompt":"What ventilation and maintenance cautions apply to packaged units?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0628"]},{"id":"nim:c-split","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-terminals","tag":"DEF","text":"A split DX system puts fan, indoor coil, furnace, filters, and dampers indoors and compressor, condenser, and outdoor coil outdoors, lowering indoor noise.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 J","prompt":"Which components go in the indoor and outdoor units of a split system?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0629"]},{"id":"nim:c-induction","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-terminals","tag":"FACT","text":"Induction boxes use high pressure primary air (as high as 2.5 in. w.g.) through a nozzle to pull in room air; as demand drops the nozzle closes and the room air damper opens.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 M","prompt":"How does an induction box hold nearly constant airflow as demand falls?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0630"]},{"id":"nim:c-grille-register","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-terminals","tag":"DEF","text":"A grille delivers air through a slotted or perforated panel with no flow control; a register is a grille with a damper; a diffuser injects air at higher velocity to mix it.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 P","prompt":"How do grilles, registers, and diffusers differ?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0631"]},{"id":"nim:c-throw","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-terminals","tag":"DEF","text":"Throw is the horizontal distance supply air travels before slowing to a set speed, usually 50 fpm; spread is lateral dispersion; drop is downward migration.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 Q","prompt":"What do diffuser throw, spread, and drop each mean?","num":{"q":"Throw is measured to a terminal speed usually of __ .","a":"50 fpm","d":["100 fpm","150 fpm","25 fpm"]},"qs":["cvi-0632"]},{"id":"nim:c-dump","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-terminals","tag":"NOTE","text":"At reduced airflow, as in VAV, diffusers may dump air in a narrow column, giving poor distribution and chilling occupants below.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 Q","prompt":"What happens at diffusers when VAV airflow is low?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0633"]},{"id":"nim:c-silencer","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-terminals","tag":"FACT","text":"The fan is generally the largest HVAC noise source. Passive pod silencers add 0.3 to 0.5 in. w.g.; active silencers cancel noise with a microphone, processor, and speaker.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 S","prompt":"What pressure drop do passive pod silencers add, and how do active silencers work?","num":{"q":"Passive pod silencers typically add __ of pressure drop.","a":"0.3 to 0.5 in. w.g.","d":["1 to 2 in. w.g.","0.05 to 0.1 in. w.g.","2 to 3 in. w.g."]},"qs":["cvi-0634"]},{"id":"nim:c-dw-porous","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-duct-types","tag":"DEF","text":"Double-wall lined duct has fiberglass between two metal layers and counts as porous unless the inner wall is continuously sealed.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 L","prompt":"When is double-wall lined duct considered porous?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0635"]},{"id":"nim:c-porous-area","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-duct-types","tag":"FACT","text":"Fibrous glass liner and duct board are porous: they present more surface than sheet metal to trap dirt and collect water. Water-soaked liner or board must be replaced.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.4 C","prompt":"Why do porous duct materials need extra attention, and what happens when they are soaked?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0636"]},{"id":"nim:c-metal","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-duct-types","tag":"FACT","text":"Metal ducts, usually galvanized steel, are the most durable, do not absorb moisture, and can be cleaned aggressively; spiral oval serves medium and high pressure.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.4 A","prompt":"Why can metal ducts be cleaned aggressively?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0637"]},{"id":"nim:c-pvc-cpvc","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-duct-types","tag":"FACT","text":"PVC duct pipe is corrosion-resistant, 6 to 24 inches round, maximum 140°F; CPVC is also corrosion-resistant with a 200°F maximum for hotter fume exhaust.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.4 H","prompt":"What are the maximum operating temperatures of PVC and CPVC duct pipe?","num":{"q":"CPVC duct pipe has a maximum operating temperature of __ .","a":"200°F","d":["140°F","180°F","250°F"]},"qs":["cvi-0638"]},{"id":"nim:c-concrete","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-duct-types","tag":"FACT","text":"Cracks or holes in concrete return ducts in crawl spaces or below slabs can admit soil gases, moisture, and mold spores to the airstream.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.4 E","prompt":"What can enter through cracks in concrete return ducts below slabs?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0639"]},{"id":"nim:c-flex-friction","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-c-duct-types","tag":"FACT","text":"Long runs of flex duct with sharp bends cause excessive friction; end-of-run rooms suffer first. Not all flex duct can be cleaned.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 I","prompt":"What does long, sharply bent flex duct do to airflow?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0640"]},{"id":"nim:c-transite","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-c-duct-types","tag":"REG","text":"Transite is asbestos cement duct, used for high-temperature fume exhaust and sub-slab systems; treat it as ACM and do not cut into or damage it.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.4 G","prompt":"How must Transite duct be treated during inspection?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0641"]},{"id":"nim:d-indoor-10-100","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-iaq-role","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA and NIOSH studies cited by the manual found indoor pollutant levels can run up to 10 to 100 times higher than outdoor levels.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.1","prompt":"How much higher than outdoor levels can indoor pollutants be, per EPA and NIOSH studies?","trap":"Do not confuse with I-BEAM's 2 to 4% performance loss or VAV's 10 to 20% savings.","num":{"q":"Indoor pollutant levels can be up to __ times higher than outdoor levels.","a":"10 to 100","d":["2 to 5","5 to 10","100 to 1,000"]},"qs":["cvi-0650"]},{"id":"nim:d-no-hypothesis","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-iaq-role","tag":"REC","text":"The inspector should not arrive with a hypothesis already formed, because a preformed idea shapes how the investigation is designed.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.1","prompt":"What does the manual say about forming a hypothesis before an IAQ inspection?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0651"]},{"id":"nim:d-pathway-not-source","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-iaq-role","tag":"FACT","text":"The ventilation system is often the conduit or pathway for pollutants rather than their source, and a facility may have several sources at once.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.1","prompt":"Is the ventilation system usually the source of pollutants or something else?","trap":"Dirty ducts do not prove the HVAC system created the problem.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0652"]},{"id":"nim:d-stay-in-lane","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-iaq-role","tag":"SHALL","text":"Inspectors must recognize the limits of their qualifications and not go beyond them. IAQ issues past that point need IAQ professionals such as industrial hygienists, IEPs, and test and balance firms.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.1","prompt":"Who handles IAQ issues beyond the inspector's expertise?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0653"]},{"id":"nim:d-co2-occupancy","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-iaq-role","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 gives guidance on CO2 and IAQ. High CO2 can come from occupancy above the building's code design, in which case the ventilation system is not the cause.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.4","prompt":"What non-HVAC cause of high CO2 does the manual give?","trap":"High CO2 does not automatically mean the air handler is failing.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0667"]},{"id":"nim:d-air-balance","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-iaq-role","tag":"FACT","text":"Supply and return volumes should generally be equal. Unequal volumes cause pressure imbalances, more infiltration, back drafting of gas appliances, and possible health and safety problems.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.2","prompt":"What results when supply and return air volumes are unequal?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0654"]},{"id":"nim:d-filtration-equipment","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-iaq-role","tag":"FACT","text":"Filtration in most commercial buildings is designed first to protect equipment and removes little fine particulate, so dust slowly accumulates in the system.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.2","prompt":"What is the first design purpose of most commercial building filtration?","trap":"Standard commercial filters are not designed around fine respirable particles.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0655"]},{"id":"nim:d-new-construction","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-iaq-role","tag":"FACT","text":"Building materials and components are normally a bigger pollutant issue in new construction, before products have had time to off-gas.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.2","prompt":"When are building materials normally the most significant pollutant source?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0656"]},{"id":"nim:d-outside-contaminants","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-iaq-role","tag":"FACT","text":"The manual groups smog, airborne dust, pollen, nearby building or vehicle emissions, re-entrained exhaust, and cooling tower mist as outside air contaminants.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.2","prompt":"Which causes does the manual group as outside air contaminants?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0670"]},{"id":"nim:d-pollutant-types","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-iaq-role","tag":"DEF","text":"Pollutant types: particulate (soot, dust, pollen), gas phase (carbon monoxide), aerosols (solid or liquid airborne particles), bioaerosols (biological origin), biological contaminants, and fungi.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.3","prompt":"What pollutant types does the manual list, with examples?","trap":"Carbon monoxide is the manual's gas phase example, not an aerosol.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0657"]},{"id":"nim:d-supply-pathway","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"Through supply air, the HVAC system spreads dust and debris, microbial spores and MVOCs, and deteriorated insulation into occupied space.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.4","prompt":"What pollutants does the manual list for the supply air pathway?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0658"]},{"id":"nim:d-exhaust-pathway","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"Exhaust air becomes a pollutant pathway when exhaust ducts leak contaminants into occupied space. Outside and make-up air pull outdoor contaminants in.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.4","prompt":"How does exhaust air act as a pollutant pathway?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0659"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-stack-pressures","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"When inside air is warmer than outside, stack effect puts higher floors under positive pressure and lower floors under negative pressure, with a neutral pressure plane between.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Driving Forces","prompt":"What pressures does stack effect create on upper and lower floors in heating season?","trap":"Air enters low (negative) and leaves high (positive).","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0660"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-wind","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"Wind creates positive pressure and infiltration on the windward side, and negative pressure and exfiltration on the leeward side.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Driving Forces","prompt":"What does wind do to pressure on the windward and leeward sides?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0661"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-fan-pressure","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-d-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"A fan pushes air in one direction, creating positive pressure in front of it (discharge) and negative pressure behind it (inlet).","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Driving Forces","prompt":"Where does a fan create positive and negative pressure?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0681"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-flow-direction","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"Air moves from high to low pressure, so air should move from occupants toward a source and out of the building, not from the source past occupants.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Air Movement and Pressure","prompt":"Which direction should air move relative to occupants and pollutant sources?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0662"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-negative-building","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-pathways","tag":"FACT","text":"A negatively pressurized building draws air and outdoor pollutants in through any available opening, such as windows, doors, cracks and crevices.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Common Airflow Pathways","prompt":"What happens at openings in a negatively pressurized building?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0663"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-garage-negative","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-pathways","tag":"REC","text":"An underground or attached garage should use exhaust ventilation and be kept negative relative to the building, with airflow checked often and CO monitored.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Indoor Sources","prompt":"What pressure should an attached garage hold relative to the building?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0664"]},{"id":"nim:d-comfort-in-iaq","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"Thermal comfort falls within IAQ and can prompt inspections, but a comfort complaint does not necessarily indicate an air quality problem.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.5","prompt":"How does thermal comfort relate to an IAQ investigation?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0665"]},{"id":"nim:d-std55","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 55, Thermal Environmental Conditions for Human Occupancy, is the manual's reference for thermal comfort, including acceptable ranges.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.5","prompt":"Which standard does the manual cite for thermal comfort ranges?","trap":"62.1 is ventilation and IAQ; 55 is thermal comfort.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0666"]},{"id":"nim:d-comfort-factors","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"Common thermal comfort factors: temperature, drafts, humidity, temperature changes, air stratification or poor mixing, and duct leakage.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.5","prompt":"What are the manual's common thermal comfort factors?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0668"]},{"id":"nim:d-supply-leak-comfort","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"Leaking supply ducts do not deliver the design quantity of tempered air to the space, wasting energy and making the system work harder to hold temperature.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.5","prompt":"How does supply duct leakage affect thermal comfort?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0669"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-warm-emissions","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"Chemicals are usually released from building materials faster at higher temperatures, so occupants who are too warm are likely also exposed to more pollutants.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Factors Affecting Indoor Climate","prompt":"How does room temperature affect chemical emissions from materials?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0676"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-dry-complaint","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"NOTE","text":"Complaints that air is too dry may come from particles irritating mucous membranes, not low humidity. Stuffy may mean too warm or still air; stale may mean a faint odor.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Discomfort","prompt":"What can a 'too dry' air complaint actually indicate?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0672"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-rh-always","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"REC","text":"To control microorganisms, keep RH below 60% for mold and below 50% for dust mites at all times, including unoccupied hours.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, Humidity and Microbial Growth","prompt":"What RH limits does I-BEAM give for microbial control, and when do they apply?","trap":"The limits are not relaxed at night or on weekends.","num":{"q":"To control dust mites, I-BEAM says keep RH below __ at all times.","a":"50%","d":["30%","60%","70%"]},"qs":["cvi-0678"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-acute","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"DEF","text":"Acute effects occur immediately after exposure, for example within 24 hours, and usually fade soon after exposure ends.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Acute Effects","prompt":"How does I-BEAM define acute effects?","trap":null,"num":{"q":"Acute effects occur shortly after exposure, e.g. within __ .","a":"24 hours","d":["1 hour","72 hours","7 days"]},"qs":["cvi-0671"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-sbs-bri","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"DEF","text":"SBS is acute complaints with no obvious cause that ease when people leave. BRI is a defined illness with a known causative agent, often biological.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Building Associated Illnesses","prompt":"How do SBS and BRI differ?","trap":"Known cause and diagnosable illness means BRI, not SBS.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0673"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-bri-examples","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"Legionnaires' disease, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and humidifier fever are common BRI examples. Typical sources include humidifiers, cooling towers, drain pans, filters, and wet materials.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Building Related Illness","prompt":"What are common examples of building related illness?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0674"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-mcs","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"DEF","text":"Multiple chemical sensitivity describes apparent sensitivity to low levels of many chemicals. There is significant professional disagreement on whether it exists.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Multiple Chemical Sensitivity","prompt":"What is MCS and how settled is it professionally?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0675"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-bio-sources","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-comfort","tag":"FACT","text":"I-BEAM lists wet materials, cooling towers, humidifiers, cooling coils, drain pans, damp duct insulation or filters, and re-entrained sanitary exhaust as biological contaminant sources.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Types of Pollutants","prompt":"Which HVAC-related items does I-BEAM list as biological contaminant sources?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0677"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-dilution","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-ventilation","tag":"DEF","text":"Exhaust ventilation removes pollutants from a fixed source. Dilution ventilation uses outdoor air to dilute area sources such as people, building materials, and office equipment.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Ventilation","prompt":"What is the difference between exhaust and dilution ventilation?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0679"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-measure-tools","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"nim-d-ventilation","tag":"FACT","text":"Ventilation measurement tools: smoke tube for airflow, flow hood for air volume, velocity meter for air velocity, and CO2 to estimate percent outdoor air.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Ventilation Measurements","prompt":"Which tool does each ventilation measurement use?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0680"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-oa-formula","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-d-ventilation","tag":"FACT","text":"According to I-BEAM, ASHRAE 62-1999 rates are per occupant: OA = cfm per occupant x occupants. A 20 cfm per person rate does not mean a 20% outdoor air fraction.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, ASHRAE Standard 62-1999","prompt":"How is total outdoor airflow calculated from a per-occupant rate?","trap":"cfm per person is not a percent outdoor air.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0686"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-intermittent","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-d-ventilation","tag":"FACT","text":"According to I-BEAM, citing ASHRAE 62-1989, spaces with peak occupancy under 3 hours may base outdoor air on average occupancy, but never below one-half the maximum.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, Intermittent Occupancy","prompt":"What floor applies when outdoor air is based on average occupancy?","trap":"Edition-specific; I-BEAM cites the 1989 standard.","num":{"q":"With peaks under 3 hours, outdoor air may use average occupancy but never below __ the maximum.","a":"one-half","d":["one-third","two-thirds","one-quarter"]},"qs":["cvi-0687"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-vav-savings","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-d-ventilation","tag":"FACT","text":"CV systems vary supply temperature at constant volume; VAV systems vary volume at constant temperature. I-BEAM says VAV cuts HVAC energy cost 10 to 20% but complicates outdoor air delivery.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, Ventilation Systems","prompt":"How do CV and VAV control temperature, and what does VAV save?","trap":null,"num":{"q":"VAV systems reduce HVAC energy cost by about __ over CV systems.","a":"10 to 20%","d":["2 to 4%","30 to 50%","5 to 10%"]},"qs":["cvi-0682"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-vav-partload","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-d-ventilation","tag":"FACT","text":"With a constant outdoor air fraction, VAV outdoor air volume falls as supply volume falls at part load. A low VAV box minimum can starve spaces of outdoor air.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, VAV Systems","prompt":"What happens to outdoor air in a constant-fraction VAV system at part load?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0683"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-duct-3pct","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-d-ventilation","tag":"FACT","text":"According to I-BEAM, sealed duct systems with leakage under 3% usually have a superior life cycle cost and fewer leaky-duct problems.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, Ducts","prompt":"What duct leakage rate does I-BEAM tie to superior life cycle cost?","trap":null,"num":{"q":"Sealed ducts leaking less than __ usually show a superior life cycle cost.","a":"3%","d":["1%","5%","10%"]},"qs":["cvi-0684"]},{"id":"ibeam:d-filter-fan-off","dom":"HVAC Systems","lesson":"nim-d-ventilation","tag":"REC","text":"Filters should fit tightly and be replaced on schedule or by pressure drop, with fans shut off during changes to avoid contaminating the air.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, Air Filters","prompt":"What should be done with the fans during a filter change?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0685"]},{"id":"nim:e-client-first-call","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-client","tag":"FACT","text":"An inspection typically begins with the client phoning the inspector. Detailed notes start with that call, and the information gathered is used to prepare a proposal and set the fee.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 A","prompt":"How does an inspection typically begin, and when does note-taking start?","trap":"Notes start at the first call, not at the walk-through or scope approval.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0700","cvi-0701"]},{"id":"nim:e-site-visit-half-day","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-client","tag":"FACT","text":"A typical site visit takes about half a day, and the inspector should tell the client this during the initial phone consultation.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 A","prompt":"How long does a typical site visit take?","num":{"q":"A typical site visit takes about __ .","a":"half of a day","d":["one hour","one full day","two full days"]},"qs":["cvi-0702"]},{"id":"nim:e-no-recommendations","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-client","tag":"FACT","text":"If a client does not want recommendations in the report, the inspector respects that wish and formats the report accordingly.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 Table 5-2","prompt":"What does the inspector do if the client does not want recommendations?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0703"]},{"id":"nim:e-deliverables-question","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-client","tag":"FACT","text":"Asking what deliverables the client wants tells the inspector whether to collect photos, samples, detailed field notes, and similar items.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 Table 5-2","prompt":"What does the deliverables interview question tell the inspector?","trap":"Report delivery format comes from a separate question.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0704"]},{"id":"nim:e-report-recipient","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-client","tag":"FACT","text":"The report recipient is agreed with the facility and may differ from the main point of contact. Other staff who ask about findings are directed to the agreed contact person.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 C","prompt":"What should the inspector do when other staff ask about findings?","trap":"Operating personnel help on site but usually do not receive findings.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0705"]},{"id":"nim:e-no-asbuilt","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-client","tag":"REC","text":"When little or no building documentation exists, the final report should note that no as-built documentation was available and that assumptions had to be made.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 D","prompt":"What must the report say when no as-built drawings exist?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0706"]},{"id":"nim:e-walkthrough-discrepancy","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-client","tag":"FACT","text":"On the walk-through, accompanied by someone familiar with the HVAC systems, the inspector compares drawings to installed systems and notes discrepancies on the drawings.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 E","prompt":"What does the inspector do with drawings during the walk-through?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0707"]},{"id":"nim:e-purpose-length","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-purpose","tag":"REC","text":"The purpose statement is a short, descriptive statement of one to five sentences that summarizes the reason for the inspection.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 F","prompt":"How long should a purpose statement be?","num":{"q":"A purpose statement should be __ long.","a":"one to five sentences","d":["six to ten sentences","two to three paragraphs","one full page"]},"qs":["cvi-0708"]},{"id":"nim:e-purpose-no-opinion","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-purpose","tag":"REC","text":"A purpose statement explains why the inspection is being done. It does not judge the facility's condition or offer an opinion on it.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 F","prompt":"What must a purpose statement not contain?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0709"]},{"id":"nim:e-purpose-specific","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-purpose","tag":"FACT","text":"A good purpose statement is specific: it names the system, the problem, and the question the inspection will answer. 'Determine whether the system is dirty' is too general.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 F","prompt":"What makes a purpose statement acceptable?","trap":"A general statement may be accurate but still lacks the needed detail.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0710"]},{"id":"nim:e-purpose-approved","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-purpose","tag":"REC","text":"The purpose statement is presented to and approved by the client before the inspector moves on to writing the scope.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 F","prompt":"What must happen to the purpose statement before the scope is written?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0711"]},{"id":"nim:e-scope-vs-purpose","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The scope gives a detailed overview of what will be examined and defines the inspection's parameters; the purpose statement only summarizes why the inspection is being done.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 F, §5.3","prompt":"How does the scope differ from the purpose statement?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0712"]},{"id":"nim:e-scope-steps","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-purpose","tag":"FACT","text":"Scope of work steps: review plans and reports, determine systems to inspect, determine where to inspect, draft the scope, draft the disclaimer.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 A","prompt":"What are the scope of work steps in order?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0713"]},{"id":"nim:e-scope-facility-desc","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-purpose","tag":"FACT","text":"The scope includes a short description of the facility and its current use, not its condition. It lists systems, portions, measurements, sampling, and possible added areas.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 E","prompt":"What does the scope say about the facility?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0714"]},{"id":"nim:e-scope-report-intro","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-purpose","tag":"FACT","text":"The scope must be client-approved before any inspection work, typically ends with the purpose statement, and later becomes the introduction to the final report.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 E","prompt":"How is the scope used when writing the final report?","trap":"The scope ends with the purpose statement; the report begins with the scope.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0715"]},{"id":"nim:e-disclaimer-what","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-e-purpose","tag":"FACT","text":"The disclaimer outlines what will not be inspected or included. After legal counsel approves it, the client reviews and signs it before the inspection.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 F","prompt":"What does the disclaimer outline, and who reviews it?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0716"]},{"id":"nim:e-disclaimer-negligence","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-e-purpose","tag":"NOTE","text":"A disclaimer may not protect the inspector from responsibility for mistakes or negligence; it only makes clear the limits of accountability. Enforceability varies by state.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 F","prompt":"Does a disclaimer protect against negligence claims?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0717"]},{"id":"nim:e-plans-copies","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"FACT","text":"Plan marking starts by making readable paper copies of the HVAC plans, then confirming with the facility representative any changes since the plans were drawn.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B","prompt":"What is the first step in marking up the plans?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0718"]},{"id":"nim:e-plans-colors","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"FACT","text":"At the study area boundary, mark air supply, air return, and separate exhaust (such as kitchen hoods) each in a different color, then mark airflow direction with arrows.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B","prompt":"How are duct penetrations marked on the plans?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0719"]},{"id":"nim:e-plans-attached-spaces","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"FACT","text":"When outlining study spaces, start with the complaint space, such as a cafeteria, then mark attached spaces such as the kitchen and dishwashing areas.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B","prompt":"What spaces are outlined after the complaint space?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0720"]},{"id":"nim:e-plans-upstream","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"FACT","text":"Work upstream from the space, noting turning vanes, mixing boxes, dampers, humidifiers, and coils, back to the AHU or fan and on to the outdoor air intake.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B","prompt":"How far upstream are supply systems traced on the plans?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0721"]},{"id":"nim:e-plans-deposition","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"FACT","text":"Mark areas where material deposition is expected, which are where air stream velocity slows. Also locate outside components like cooling towers and intake relief dampers.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B","prompt":"Where is deposition expected?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0722"]},{"id":"nim:e-client-specified","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"FACT","text":"When the client specifies which areas to inspect, the inspector respects those instructions and examines only the specified areas.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 C","prompt":"What if the client limits the areas to be inspected?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0723"]},{"id":"nim:e-plenum-always","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"REC","text":"Whatever the situation, check part of the ceiling or wall cavity plenums for cleanliness, in addition to the Table 5-4 minimums.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 C","prompt":"What should be inspected in all situations?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0724"]},{"id":"nim:e-complaint-return","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"REC","text":"For complaint areas, inspect all supply and ventilation systems serving the area; inspect return ducts only if those do not explain the problem or suggest return contamination.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","prompt":"When are return ducts inspected for a complaint area?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0725"]},{"id":"nim:e-supply-always","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"REC","text":"For an air supply system, always inspect the outdoor air intake and the entire AHU, plus the main trunk leaving the AHU.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","prompt":"What parts of a supply system are always inspected?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0726"]},{"id":"nim:e-branch-10","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"REC","text":"Inspect at least 10% of the branches leaving the main supply trunk.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","prompt":"What share of supply branches must be inspected?","num":{"q":"Inspect the main trunk and at least __ of the branches leaving it.","a":"10%","d":["5%","20%","25%"]},"qs":["cvi-0727"]},{"id":"nim:e-branch-inspected-def","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"DEF","text":"A branch counts as inspected if its beginning and end are examined and problem-free and nothing within the run might cause concern.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","prompt":"When is a branch duct considered inspected?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0728"]},{"id":"nim:e-routine-10","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"REC","text":"During routine inspections with no local problems, inspect at least 10% of similar systems.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","prompt":"What share of similar systems is inspected on a routine job?","trap":"The table's own example (10 AHUs, at least two) equals 20%; the stated rule is 10%.","num":{"q":"On a routine inspection, inspect at least __ of similar systems.","a":"10%","d":["5%","25%","50%"]},"qs":["cvi-0729"]},{"id":"nim:e-problems-expand","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"REC","text":"When a sample of similar systems shows problems, inspect more or even all of them; heavy deposits in 2 of 10 hood systems means checking the rest.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","prompt":"What happens when the sampled similar systems show problems?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0730"]},{"id":"nim:e-debris-areas","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"FACT","text":"Debris collects at gravity areas (shaft bottoms, dips, low points), pressure drops (turns), protrusions into the air stream, and changes of duct material, such as metal to flex or duct board.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 D","prompt":"Where do dust and debris tend to collect?","trap":"Bottom of a shaft, not the top.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0731"]},{"id":"nim:e-wet-sections","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"FACT","text":"Wet sections to examine include the condensate drain pan, humidification systems, and the first several duct diameters of supply duct after the cooling coil.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 D","prompt":"Which wet sections should be examined?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0732"]},{"id":"nim:e-access-2-4","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-plans","tag":"FACT","text":"A simple system with fairly straight runs may need only two to four access points; complex systems with longer runs may need significantly more.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 D","prompt":"How many access points does a simple straight system need?","num":{"q":"A simple system with straight runs may need only __ access points.","a":"two to four","d":["one","five to eight","ten to twelve"]},"qs":["cvi-0733"]},{"id":"nim:e-contain-barriers","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-e-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Work that might release contamination must be contained with physical barriers and negative air pressure; released material is removed with equipment such as a HEPA vacuum.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 A","prompt":"How must contamination from inspection work be contained?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0734"]},{"id":"nim:e-containment-extent","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-e-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"The extent of work site containment depends on the building use and the initial HVAC contamination evaluation from the walk-through.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 C","prompt":"What sets the extent of containment?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0735"]},{"id":"nim:e-equipment-hygiene","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-e-controls","tag":"REC","text":"Inspection equipment must not introduce contaminants. With severe microbial growth or known hazardous substances, the equipment gets an on-site hygiene and integrity inspection.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 D","prompt":"When does equipment need an on-site hygiene inspection?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0736"]},{"id":"nim:e-duct-shutoff","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-e-controls","tag":"REC","text":"At a minimum, shut the HVAC system off, or block off the section under inspection on the supply side and at supply registers. Heavy deposits may call for negative pressure.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 F","prompt":"What is the minimum pressurization control during inspection?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0737"]},{"id":"nim:e-negair-not-vac","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-e-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"A negative air machine (fan with HEPA and often carbon filters) holds an area under negative pressure. It collects dust but is not meant to function as a vacuum cleaner.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 F","prompt":"Can a negative air machine be used as a vacuum?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0738"]},{"id":"nim:e-service-cuttings","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"nim-e-controls","tag":"REC","text":"When cutting access doors or service openings, take care that cuttings are not released into the occupied space or into supply or return ducts.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 E","prompt":"What care is needed when cutting service openings?","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"nim:e-pushcam-focal","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-tools","tag":"FACT","text":"Equipment should minimize impact on the system. A push camera's long focal length on long straight runs reduces the entry points needed.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.1 A","prompt":"Why use a push camera on long straight runs?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0739"]},{"id":"nim:e-borescope-cavity","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-tools","tag":"FACT","text":"Confined spaces like wall cavities are best explored with a borescope or fiberscope: short focal length suffices and only small access ports are needed.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.1 A","prompt":"Which tool suits a wall cavity?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0740"]},{"id":"nim:e-hole-sizes","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-tools","tag":"FACT","text":"Table 6-2 access sizes: borescope 1 inch hole, push camera 4 inch hole, robotic camera about 6 x 6 inches or larger, direct visual large to very large.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 6-2","prompt":"What access size does each visualization tool need?","num":{"q":"A push camera needs about a __ access hole.","a":"4 inch","d":["1 inch","6 x 6 inch","2 inch"]},"qs":["cvi-0741"]},{"id":"nim:e-robot-limits","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-tools","tag":"FACT","text":"Robotic cameras cover horizontal runs of 100 feet or more and can move from main to branch ducts, but cannot pass turning vanes and other restrictions.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 6-2","prompt":"What are a robotic camera's range and main limitation?","trap":"Robots can enter branches; turning vanes stop them.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0742"]},{"id":"nim:e-borescope-cons","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-tools","tag":"FACT","text":"Borescope drawbacks: short focal length, a cable that can stir up dust, and short range from its short cable connection.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 6-2","prompt":"What are the borescope's disadvantages?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0743"]},{"id":"nim:e-photo-litigation","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-tools","tag":"NOTE","text":"Digital photos may not be admissible in some court proceedings. When litigation is possible, agree with the client on photo deliverables and format.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.1 E","prompt":"What should be settled about photos when litigation is possible?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0744"]},{"id":"nim:e-license-openings","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-tools","tag":"FACT","text":"New service openings follow NADCA Standard 05, and some areas require a license to create them; the inspector checks state and local contractor licensing rules.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.1 C","prompt":"What must be checked before cutting service openings?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0745"]},{"id":"nim:e-start-oa-intake","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"FACT","text":"Inspections start at the outdoor air intake and progress through filters, AHU, heating and cooling coils, supply ducts, return ducts, and exhaust ducts.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2","prompt":"Where does an inspection start, and what order follows?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0746"]},{"id":"nim:e-dust-patterns","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"FACT","text":"Dust patterns point to their source: dust forced from a crevice fans out from it. In Case Study 1, streaks from AHU penetration gaps showed casing defects, not filter blow-by.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 Case Study #1","prompt":"How do dust patterns help find a source?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0747"]},{"id":"nim:e-slight-dust-normal","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"FACT","text":"Some dust is normal in most commercial buildings; the client should know slight debris does not necessarily mean cleaning is needed.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 B","prompt":"Does slight dust mean the system needs cleaning?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0748"]},{"id":"nim:e-case2-visual","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"FACT","text":"Mold spores occur on nearly all indoor surfaces, so one diffuser swab proves little. In Case Study 2 the inspector recommended a visual inspection to rule the HVAC system in or out.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 Case Study #2","prompt":"What did the inspector recommend after a positive diffuser swab?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0749"]},{"id":"nim:e-cold-deck","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"FACT","text":"In dual-duct systems inspect both decks, more intensely in the cold deck, which is likelier to be contaminated; in high pressure systems focus more on the low pressure side.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 B","prompt":"Which dual-duct deck gets the more intense inspection?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0750"]},{"id":"nim:e-checklist-guideline","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"FACT","text":"The HVAC Inspection Checklist is a guideline: add or remove fields as needed and arrange sections in the order they will be done.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","prompt":"How should the inspection checklist be used?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0751"]},{"id":"nim:e-field-notes-content","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"FACT","text":"Field notes record where a condition was seen, a description, whether a sample or measurement was taken, and any potential causes. With the checklist, they form the report's basis.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","prompt":"What goes into field notes?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0752"]},{"id":"nim:e-sample-client-directed","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"REC","text":"Sampling is agreed at the first client meeting, and in general the inspector samples only when directed by the client. An inspector not comfortable sampling calls in a qualified person.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","prompt":"When should an inspector take samples?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0753"]},{"id":"nim:e-sample-insurers","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"FACT","text":"No government agency requires sampling, but insurance companies may ask for samples when suspicious material is observed.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","prompt":"Who may ask for samples, given no agency requires them?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0754"]},{"id":"nim:e-sample-record","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"REC","text":"Photograph each sample location and record exact location, time and date, collector, temperature and relative humidity, and moisture meter readings if applicable.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","prompt":"What is recorded for each sample?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0755"]},{"id":"nim:e-sample-interpretation","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"FACT","text":"Interpreting results is the hardest part of sampling: no established guidelines exist for microbiological results, and mold and bacteria are normally present.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","prompt":"What is the hardest part of sampling and why?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0756"]},{"id":"nim:e-chain-custody-always","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-e-methods","tag":"REC","text":"Whenever sampling is done, use chain of custody forms to track the sample from collector to analyst. They show professionalism and are essential if the case reaches court.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","prompt":"When are chain of custody forms used?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0757"]},{"id":"nim:f-deliverable","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"FACT","text":"The written report is the only physical deliverable the client receives, so it must be clear, accurate, organized, and well written.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"Why must the inspection report be clear, accurate, and well organized?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0770"]},{"id":"nim:f-gather","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"REC","text":"Before drafting, collect interview notes, history, walk-through notes, purpose and scope statements, field notes, forms, sample results, photos, and client discussions in one place and review them all.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"What should the inspector do before drafting the report?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0771"]},{"id":"nim:f-checklist-prep","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"REC","text":"Checklists going into the report are reworked: unused or N/A sections are removed and handwritten notes are typed. Field notes used in the report are typed and organized too.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"How are checklists prepared before they go into the report?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0772"]},{"id":"nim:f-format","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"SHALL","text":"NADCA does not define a report format, but it does require a formal written report to the client after every inspection.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"What does NADCA require regarding report format?","trap":"No set format is required, but a written report is. A verbal debrief alone does not satisfy NADCA.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0773"]},{"id":"nim:f-three-parts","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"FACT","text":"Whatever headings are used, every report has three main portions: summary of findings, body of the report, and recommendations.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"What three main portions does every inspection report include?","trap":"Introduction, methods, results, discussion, conclusions, references, and appendices are optional headings, not the three core portions.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0774"]},{"id":"nim:f-scope-summary","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"FACT","text":"The scope statement, which guided the inspection, can open the report's Summary section with very few changes.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"Which earlier document can be reused to begin the Summary?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0775"]},{"id":"nim:f-summary-length","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"REC","text":"The Summary should be relatively short: one to four paragraphs.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"How long should the Summary section be?","num":{"q":"The Summary section should be one to __ paragraphs long.","a":"four","d":["two","six","eight"]},"qs":["cvi-0776"]},{"id":"nim:f-summary-facts","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"REC","text":"Main findings in the Summary are stated as observed facts (what was found and where), not subjective judgments such as calling a component dirty.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"How should findings be worded in the Summary?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0777"]},{"id":"nim:f-summary-recs","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"REC","text":"The Summary gives an overview of the kinds of recommendations made without detailing each one.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"How does the Summary treat the recommendations?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0778"]},{"id":"nim:f-summary-readall","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"REC","text":"The Summary should clearly direct the client to read the entire report to see the full range of findings and recommendations.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"What should the Summary tell the reader to do?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0779"]},{"id":"nim:f-body-factual","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"REC","text":"The body is an organized, factual presentation of the field notes; it contains no assessments or recommendations.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"What belongs in the body of the report?","trap":"Recommendations live in their own separate portion, never in the body.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0780"]},{"id":"nim:f-body-order","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"REC","text":"Findings in the body are organized in order of priority or severity.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"How are findings ordered in the body?","trap":"Not in inspection order or airflow order.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0781"]},{"id":"nim:f-appendix","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-report-build","tag":"REC","text":"Lab reports, collected data, and the used checklists (with non-applicable sections discarded) go in the report appendix.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"Where do lab reports, data, and checklists go?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0782"]},{"id":"nim:f-trig-debris","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-recommend","tag":"FACT","text":"Per ACR as quoted in the manual, significant accumulations of contaminants or debris that are visually observed make cleaning necessary.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"When does visible debris make cleaning necessary?","trap":"No test is needed first; visual observation of significant accumulation is enough.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0783"]},{"id":"nim:f-trig-microbial","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-recommend","tag":"FACT","text":"Evidence of microbial growth, either visually observed or confirmed by analytical methods, makes cleaning required.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"When does microbial growth make cleaning required?","trap":"Visual evidence alone qualifies; lab confirmation is one route, not a requirement.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0784"]},{"id":"nim:f-trig-discharge","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-recommend","tag":"FACT","text":"Cleaning is necessary if the system discharges visible particulate into the occupied space or a significant particle contribution to indoor air is confirmed.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"When does particle discharge make cleaning necessary?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0785"]},{"id":"nim:f-trig-performance","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-recommend","tag":"FACT","text":"Cleaning is necessary when coils, airflow control devices, filtration devices, or air handling equipment have restrictions or deposits that may hurt performance, airflow, or design intent.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","prompt":"What is the performance-based cleaning trigger?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0786"]},{"id":"nim:f-building-use","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-recommend","tag":"REC","text":"Consider building use: a thin dust coat may be unacceptable in a hospital yet acceptable at a manufacturing facility. Each recommendation is case by case.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.2","prompt":"How does building use affect cleaning recommendations?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0787"]},{"id":"nim:f-one-per-finding","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-recommend","tag":"REC","text":"Present a recommendation for each finding described in the report.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.2","prompt":"How many recommendations should the report give relative to findings?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0788"]},{"id":"nim:f-no-severity","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-recommend","tag":"REC","text":"Recommendations state only what should be done and how; avoid severity opinions like \"hazard\" or \"only a minor issue\", which can skew the client's response.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.2","prompt":"Why avoid severity opinions in recommendations?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0789"]},{"id":"nim:f-specialist","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-recommend","tag":"REC","text":"If a condition falls in another HVAC specialty, do not describe the specific fix; recommend that the client contact the appropriate specialist.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.2","prompt":"How is a finding outside the inspector's specialty written up?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0790"]},{"id":"nim:f-in-person","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-present","tag":"REC","text":"The report may be mailed or emailed, but it is best presented in person with all appropriate persons present, to help ensure the client understands it.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.3","prompt":"How is the report best presented?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0791"]},{"id":"nim:f-interim","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-present","tag":"FACT","text":"Interim or draft reporting helps when an inspection spans days or weeks or a suspected hazard drives it; text, photos, or video may be emailed soon after collection.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.3","prompt":"When is an interim report helpful?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0792"]},{"id":"nim:f-draft-label","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-present","tag":"SHALL","text":"At the inspector's discretion, interim information should be clearly marked \"Draft\" or \"Preliminary Findings\" so it is not misconstrued as a finished product.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.3","prompt":"Why must interim information be labeled?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0793"]},{"id":"nim:f-limitations","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-present","tag":"FACT","text":"Reports can trigger conflict, finger-pointing, or legal action; a fully informed client who understands the report and its limitations handles those issues best.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.3","prompt":"Why must the client understand the report's limitations?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0794"]},{"id":"nim:f-verification","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-present","tag":"FACT","text":"After cleaning, the inspector explains ACR verification methods: visual inspection, surface comparison testing, and the NADCA Vacuum Test.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.4","prompt":"Which cleanliness verification methods does the manual name?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0795"]},{"id":"nim:f-referral","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-present","tag":"REC","text":"Follow-up includes referrals: suspected microbial growth to a growth identification and removal specialist, improper balance to an air balancer.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.4","prompt":"Who gets referrals for microbial growth and for poor balance?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0796"]},{"id":"nim:f-std180","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-f-present","tag":"FACT","text":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180 sets inspection and maintenance practice for commercial HVAC and states unmaintained systems stop performing as designed, so inspectors should encourage routine inspection.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.5","prompt":"What does Standard 180 say about unmaintained systems?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0797"]},{"id":"nim:f-microbial-signs","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"FACT","text":"Apparent microbial growth may show as unusual dust with contrasting colors, a downy or furry surface, or a musty odor.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What signs suggest microbial growth?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0798"]},{"id":"nim:f-nyc-doh","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"FACT","text":"Microbial growth warrants priority; an industrial hygienist may be needed, and decontamination follows documents such as the NYC DOH fungi guidelines and ACR.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"Which documents guide decontaminating microbial growth?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0799"]},{"id":"nim:f-debris-interval","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"FACT","text":"For debris in fan chambers, mixing plenums, and drain pans, EPA suggests monthly inspection; cleaning follows accumulation but should be thorough at least annually.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What inspection and cleaning intervals apply to plenum and pan debris?","num":{"q":"EPA suggests inspecting mixing plenums and drain pans __, with thorough cleaning at least annually.","a":"monthly","d":["weekly","quarterly","semiannually"]},"qs":["cvi-0800"]},{"id":"nim:f-debris-cause","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"REC","text":"With significant debris loading, the inspector should try to find whether poor maintenance or another condition, such as filter bypass, is the cause.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What should the inspector investigate when debris loading is heavy?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0801"]},{"id":"nim:f-carryover-where","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"FACT","text":"Coil moisture carryover often causes microbial amplification in the first several feet of internally insulated supply duct.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"Where does carryover often cause microbial growth?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0802"]},{"id":"nim:f-carryover-velocity","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"REC","text":"With carryover, measure system velocity against the manufacturer's value and check whether the system was modified to increase airflow.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What should the inspector check when moisture blows off a coil?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0803"]},{"id":"nim:f-rh-control","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"FACT","text":"High RH must be controlled to limit mold and dust mites; data-logging humidistats help, console dehumidifiers can bridge until permanent controls, and a humidity specialist evaluates if all else fails.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"How can elevated relative humidity be handled?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0804"]},{"id":"nim:f-pan-leak","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Condensate pan leaks must be repaired immediately and wetted material dried per documents such as EPA's Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"How are leaking condensate pans handled?","trap":"NYC DOH guidelines are cited for microbial decontamination; the EPA mold document is cited for drying.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0805"]},{"id":"nim:f-trap","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"FACT","text":"A drain upstream of the supply fan is under negative pressure; a poor trap there may keep the pan from draining and can draw in nearby contaminants such as sewer vent gas.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What happens with a poorly trapped drain under negative pressure?","trap":"Negative pressure is upstream of the supply fan; positive is downstream.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0806"]},{"id":"nim:f-humidifier-boiler","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"REC","text":"Humidifiers must be properly installed and frequently inspected, and should not use plant boiler steam or hot water, whose treatment chemicals may irritate occupants.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"Why should humidifiers not use boiler steam?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0807"]},{"id":"nim:f-vav-leaks","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"REC","text":"Leaks at VAVs often come from poorly insulated chilled lines or bad valves; tiles wetted when pipes are bled should be dried or replaced quickly.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"How are ceiling tiles wetted by VAV pipe leaks handled?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0808"]},{"id":"nim:f-refrigerant","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-moisture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Refrigerant leaks, which may contain irritants, asphyxiants, and toxins, must be identified and repaired immediately.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"How are refrigerant leaks handled?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0809"]},{"id":"nim:f-intake-25","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"FACT","text":"Per EPA as cited in Table 7-1, make-up air intakes should be at least 25 feet from fugitive emissions such as vents and local exhaust.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"How far should intakes be from vents and local exhaust?","num":{"q":"EPA says make-up air intakes should be at least __ from vents and local exhaust.","a":"25 feet","d":["10 feet","15 feet","50 feet"]},"qs":["cvi-0810"]},{"id":"nim:f-diffusers","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"REC","text":"Diffuser dust may be due to the Coanda effect; keep diffusers clean, cleaning them with a HEPA vacuum or mild detergent.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"How should dusty diffusers be cleaned?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0811"]},{"id":"nim:f-duct-dust-normal","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"FACT","text":"A slight gray dust coat or adhering oily film in ducts is not unusual; large deposits of dust, dirt, or mold should not be present.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What duct dust condition is not unusual?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0812"]},{"id":"nim:f-earmark","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"REC","text":"For large duct deposits, find and correct the cause and recommend earmarking operating funds for periodic duct inspection and cleaning.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What budget recommendation follows large duct deposits?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0813"]},{"id":"nim:f-spacer","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"REC","text":"For filter bypass: proper-sized filters, seals, and spacers of sheet metal or other non-porous material. Wood should not be used for any HVAC component.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What fills an incompletely filled filter rack?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0814"]},{"id":"nim:f-poor-filtration","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"REC","text":"Filtration efficiency should match equipment capability and airflow; refer the client to an air filtration professional to judge an upgrade.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"How is poor filtration addressed?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0815"]},{"id":"nim:f-delamination","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"FACT","text":"Fiberboard duct or internal insulation may delaminate or erode from high velocity air over time; damaged material is repaired or replaced with exposure precautions.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What causes delamination of duct board or liner?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0816"]},{"id":"nim:f-vav-oa","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"FACT","text":"Little or no air reaching a zone, especially a VAV zone with an improperly set minimum, suggests sufficient outdoor air is not being inducted.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What does little or no zone airflow suggest?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0817"]},{"id":"nim:f-ashrae62","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"FACT","text":"Table 7-1 cites ASHRAE Standard 62 (2013) for minimum ventilation rates in breathing zones.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"Which standard gives minimum breathing zone ventilation rates?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0818"]},{"id":"nim:f-pressure","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"FACT","text":"Buildings should be kept at slight positive pressure, with outdoor air brought in at a designated location, to keep fugitive emissions and untempered air out.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"What building pressure is desired and why?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0819"]},{"id":"nim:f-mech-room","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"REC","text":"Mechanical rooms, especially with a return plenum, are part of the HVAC system: keep them clean and do not use them for storage.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"How should mechanical rooms be kept?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0820"]},{"id":"nim:f-velocity-50","dom":"Standards","lesson":"nim-f-table-air","tag":"FACT","text":"Occupants may feel uncomfortable when air velocity on exposed skin exceeds 50 feet per minute (0.8 feet per second).","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","prompt":"Above what skin air velocity may occupants be uncomfortable?","num":{"q":"Occupants may be uncomfortable when air velocity on skin exceeds __.","a":"50 fpm","d":["25 fpm","100 fpm","150 fpm"]},"qs":["cvi-0821"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-diag-three-steps","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"FACT","text":"I-BEAM's diagnostic process has three steps: decide if it is an emergency and act at once, characterize the problem, then check specific causes until solved.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What are I-BEAM's three diagnostic steps, in order?","trap":"Interviewing occupants is step two, not step one.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0830"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-emergency-examples","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"FACT","text":"I-BEAM emergencies include hazardous spills, flooded porous materials, sewage spills, gas leaks, sudden headaches or nausea with combustion odor (possible CO), widespread breathing trouble, and diagnosed Legionnaires' disease or TB.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"Which situations does I-BEAM treat as IAQ emergencies?","trap":"Sudden onset plus combustion odor means emergency, not a low outdoor air complaint.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0831"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-emergency-actions","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"REC","text":"In an emergency, I-BEAM says to notify the proper authority, evacuate if needed, get medical help, ventilate with large amounts of outdoor air, keep occupants informed, then begin remediation.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What initial actions does I-BEAM list for an IAQ emergency?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0831"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-occupational-limits","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"NOTE","text":"Occupational limits (OSHA PELs, ACGIH TLVs) are not appropriate for commercial buildings. Office sources run far below industrial levels, and specific contaminant measurements are seldom warranted.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"Why does I-BEAM reject PELs and TLVs for office IAQ complaints?","trap":"Being under a PEL does not show office air is acceptable.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0856"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-four-questions","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"FACT","text":"I-BEAM characterizes a complaint by asking what the symptoms are, where they occur, when they occur, and who experiences them, using its Occupant Complaint Record forms.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"Which four questions does I-BEAM use to characterize a complaint?","trap":"Source, pathway, driving force, receptor is a different EPA framework.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0849"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-sym-respiratory-bio","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"FACT","text":"Cough, chest tightness, shortness of breath, fever and chills point first to a biological cause; chemical or particle is secondary. Check the room, ductwork, cooling tower and air handler.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What primary cause does I-BEAM assign to cough, chest tightness, fever and chills?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0832"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-sym-mild-oa","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"FACT","text":"Mild eye, nose or throat discomfort, mild headache and lethargy point first to lack of outdoor air or temperature and humidity; chemical and biological causes are secondary.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What primary causes does I-BEAM assign to mild irritation, headache and lethargy?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0833"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-sym-skin-numbers","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"NOTE","text":"For itching, swelling or rash, I-BEAM makes a biological allergen primary when few people are affected and chemical or particle (such as duct fiberglass) primary when many are.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"How does the number of people affected change I-BEAM's primary cause for skin rash?","trap":"Many people with rash points to chemical or particle, not mold.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0834"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-odor-dirty-socks","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"FACT","text":"I-BEAM ties a dirty socks odor to dirty coils or filters and lack of outdoor air, a musty odor to mold, and a sewer odor to sewer gas, drain traps or sanitary vents.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What does I-BEAM link to a dirty socks odor?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0835"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-where-widespread","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"FACT","text":"Local symptoms suggest a local source or local HVAC component. Widespread symptoms suggest a building-wide source, a central HVAC problem, or a problem common to all air handlers.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What does a local versus widespread complaint pattern suggest per I-BEAM?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0836"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-interior-zone","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"FACT","text":"Interior (core) zones get less supply air and outdoor air than exterior zones because their thermal loads are lower. EPA modeling found the core got about two thirds of average outdoor air.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Indoor Air Quality and Energy Efficiency","prompt":"Why do interior zones tend to get less outdoor air?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0837"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-when-latter-week","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"FACT","text":"Monday-morning complaints that fade suggest failure to flush or late start-up. Complaints building late in the week suggest occupant sources with too little outdoor air to dilute them.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What does I-BEAM infer from complaints that worsen late in the week?","trap":"Monday-worst and Friday-worst patterns point to different causes.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0838"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-when-mild-weather","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"FACT","text":"Mild-weather complaints suggest economizer control faults or a VAV system at part load delivering little supply and outdoor air. Hot weather suggests new furnishings or capacity; cool weather a freezestat trip.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"Which causes does I-BEAM tie to complaints in mild weather?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0839"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-filters-change-anyway","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","tag":"REC","text":"Filters left in place a long time because they never loaded enough may have grown mold. I-BEAM says to change them anyway; filters should be clean and dry.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What does I-BEAM advise for filters left in a long time because they are lightly loaded?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0850"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-smoke-puffs","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ibeam-g-measurements","tag":"FACT","text":"Release smoke in small puffs rather than one large cloud. In occupied areas, smoke dispersing within several seconds suggests good air circulation.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"How should smoke tube smoke be released, and what shows good circulation?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0842"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-smoke-exhausted-room","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ibeam-g-measurements","tag":"FACT","text":"Smoke at the entrance to an exhausted room should be drawn in, showing it is negative to occupied spaces. At a clean room entrance, smoke should move out, showing positive pressure.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What should smoke do at the door of an exhausted room and of a clean room?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0843"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-oa-min-flow","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ibeam-g-measurements","tag":"REC","text":"Measure outdoor airflow at minimum airflow conditions: turn the economizer off or set its shutoff lowest, and drive VAV controls to minimum flow.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"Under what conditions does I-BEAM say to measure outdoor airflow?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0844"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-airflow-ranking","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ibeam-g-measurements","tag":"FACT","text":"I-BEAM ranks airflow methods: flow hood preferred, velocity meter (pitot tube or anemometer) less preferred, and the CO2 percent outdoor air calculation not preferred, a last resort.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"How does I-BEAM rank the three airflow measurement methods?","trap":"Temperature-based percent outdoor air is not advisable at all.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0845"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-velocity-formula","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ibeam-g-measurements","tag":"FACT","text":"Velocity meters need multiple readings across the opening. Airflow (cfm) equals free open area (sq ft) times average velocity (fpm).","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"How is airflow computed from velocity readings?","num":{"q":"A 3 sq ft opening averaging 400 fpm carries __ .","a":"1,200 cfm","d":["800 cfm","600 cfm","2,400 cfm"]},"qs":["cvi-0846"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-co2-percent-oa","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ibeam-g-measurements","tag":"FACT","text":"Percent outdoor air = (return CO2 minus supply CO2) / (return CO2 minus outdoor CO2) x 100. The CO2 streams should differ by at least 200 ppm for accuracy.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What is I-BEAM's CO2 formula for percent outdoor air?","num":{"q":"For accuracy, the CO2 difference between air streams should be at least __ .","a":"200 ppm","d":["100 ppm","500 ppm","700 ppm"]},"qs":["cvi-0847"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-co2-peak-times","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"ibeam-g-measurements","tag":"REC","text":"Measure indoor CO2 at peak values, which in a typical office usually occur around 11 a.m. and 3 p.m.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"When are CO2 peaks in a typical office?","num":{"q":"Office CO2 usually peaks around 11 a.m. and __ .","a":"3 p.m.","d":["1 p.m.","5 p.m.","9 a.m."]},"qs":["cvi-0848"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-co2-above-1000","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-measurements","tag":"FACT","text":"If CO2 exceeds 1,000 ppm, first check for improperly vented combustion appliances and outdoor CO2. If neither explains it, outdoor air ventilation is presumed too low.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What should be checked when CO2 exceeds 1,000 ppm?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0840"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-co2-compare-areas","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-measurements","tag":"FACT","text":"CO2 higher in the complaint area than in non-complaint areas suggests the complaint area's outdoor air ventilation rate may be causing the problem.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"What does higher CO2 in the complaint area suggest?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0841"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-co2-below-threshold","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-measurements","tag":"NOTE","text":"CO2 below threshold does not show IAQ or ventilation is adequate. It ignores non-occupant sources and, since steady state is rarely reached, overestimates ventilation, often by 100% to 200%.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","prompt":"Why doesn't a low CO2 reading prove ventilation is adequate?","trap":"Most spaces never reach 1,000 ppm even with poor ventilation.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ibeam:g-contain-pressure","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ibeam-g-construction","tag":"REC","text":"Per I-BEAM, a construction containment zone should be held under significant negative pressure, for example 5 to 10 Pa (0.02 to 0.04 in. w.c.), using existing and temporary exhaust fans.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","prompt":"What negative pressure does I-BEAM suggest for a containment zone?","trap":"ACR 2025 sets no number; this figure is I-BEAM's example.","num":{"q":"I-BEAM's example containment pressure is 5 to 10 Pa, or __ in. w.c. negative.","a":"0.02 to 0.04","d":["0.004 to 0.008","0.10 to 0.20","0.05 to 0.08"]},"qs":["cvi-0851"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-seal-returns","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ibeam-g-construction","tag":"REC","text":"Isolate construction with a complete physical enclosure and seal all return ducts so contaminants cannot enter the HVAC system. Supply air to the area may also need shutting down.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","prompt":"How does I-BEAM keep construction contaminants out of the HVAC system?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0852"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-fleecy-delay","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ibeam-g-construction","tag":"REC","text":"Delay installing fleecy materials (carpet, furniture, ceiling tile) until wet-product emissions dissipate; otherwise they adsorb contaminants and re-release them after occupancy.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","prompt":"When should fleecy materials be installed relative to wet products?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0853"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-exhaust-reentry","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ibeam-g-construction","tag":"REC","text":"Containment exhaust must not re-enter through open windows or HVAC outdoor air intakes, and occupied spaces should stay positive relative to outdoors.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","prompt":"What must be true of containment exhaust and occupied-space pressure?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0854"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-chem-storage","dom":"Risk Management","lesson":"ibeam-g-construction","tag":"REC","text":"Mix and store chemical products in a room or closet exhausted outdoors and kept negative, with no opening to the return air plenum.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, IAQ Maintenance and Housekeeping Programs","prompt":"How should a chemical storage closet be ventilated?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0855"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-post-construction-vent","dom":"IAQ","lesson":"ibeam-g-construction","tag":"REC","text":"After construction, I-BEAM calls for extended ventilation hours, higher outdoor air fractions in occupied and unoccupied periods, and continuous HVAC operation through the first hot weather.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","prompt":"What ventilation strategy does I-BEAM recommend at initial occupancy after construction?","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ibeam:g-rh-30-60","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ibeam-g-maint-energy","tag":"FACT","text":"I-BEAM cites ASHRAE 62-1999 as preferring 30% to 60% RH in habitable spaces to limit allergenic and pathogenic organisms. Current ASHRAE text differs.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","prompt":"What RH range does I-BEAM cite from ASHRAE 62-1999?","num":{"q":"Per I-BEAM citing ASHRAE 62-1999, habitable-space RH should preferably stay between 30% and __ .","a":"60%","d":["50%","70%","65%"]},"qs":["cvi-0857"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-dry-24h","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ibeam-g-maint-energy","tag":"REC","text":"Dry wet carpet or porous material immediately after a spill. If it is not dry within 24 hours it may have to be discarded, since wet materials breed mold.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, IAQ Maintenance and Housekeeping Programs","prompt":"How fast must wet porous materials be dried per I-BEAM?","num":{"q":"Porous material not dried within __ may have to be discarded.","a":"24 hours","d":["12 hours","48 hours","72 hours"]},"qs":["cvi-0859"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-filter-blowout","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ibeam-g-maint-energy","tag":"FACT","text":"Overloaded low-efficiency filters (10% to 20% dust-spot) deform or blow out, clogging coils and dirtying ducts. Moderate filters (40% to 65%) have more body and resist blowout.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","prompt":"What happens to overloaded low-efficiency filters?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0860"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-vav-fixed-oa","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ibeam-g-maint-energy","tag":"FACT","text":"In EPA modeling, VAV with a fixed outdoor air fraction cut outdoor air to about 1/2 to 2/3 of design most of the year. VAV with constant outdoor airflow plus an economizer performed best.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Indoor Air Quality and Energy Efficiency","prompt":"What did EPA's modeling find for VAV with a fixed outdoor air fraction?","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0858"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-co2-dcv","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ibeam-g-maint-energy","tag":"FACT","text":"A typical CO2-controlled ventilation system raises outdoor air at 600 to 800 ppm to keep peaks below 1,000 ppm, and should keep a minimum outdoor air setting for building contaminants.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, Indoor Air Quality and Energy Efficiency","prompt":"How does a typical CO2-controlled ventilation system operate?","num":{"q":"CO2-controlled ventilation typically raises outdoor air at __ ppm.","a":"600 to 800","d":["900 to 1,000","400 to 500","1,000 to 1,200"]},"qs":["cvi-0861"]},{"id":"ibeam:g-pm-importance","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ibeam-g-maint-energy","tag":"FACT","text":"I-BEAM calls effective preventive maintenance the most important tool for preventing IAQ problems. PM records can also show good-faith IAQ efforts if legal action arises.","src":"EPA I-BEAM, IAQ Maintenance and Housekeeping Programs","prompt":"Why does I-BEAM stress preventive maintenance for IAQ?","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"nim:h-samp-scope","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-sampling","tag":"FACT","text":"Collecting samples is generally outside an HVAC inspection's scope. It is done when a client asks, using precautions against contamination, with samples sent to a reliable laboratory.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","prompt":"When is sampling part of an HVAC inspection, and where do samples go?","trap":"Sampling is a client-requested add-on, not a routine inspection step.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0883"]},{"id":"nim:h-bulk-use","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-sampling","tag":"FACT","text":"Bulk sampling physically removes material for analysis. It suits wallboard, insulation, carpet, and larger deposits of material; loose dust is scooped into a sealed container.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","prompt":"What is bulk sampling and what materials does it suit?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0870"]},{"id":"nim:h-bulk-amount","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-sampling","tag":"FACT","text":"For a bulk sample, about one cubic inch of material is more than enough for laboratory analysis.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","prompt":"How much material is enough for a bulk sample?","trap":null,"num":{"q":"For a bulk sample, about __ of material is more than enough for analysis.","a":"one cubic inch","d":["one cubic foot","one quarter cassette","one 4 inch filter circle"]},"qs":["cvi-0871"]},{"id":"nim:h-filt-bacteria","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-sampling","tag":"NOTE","text":"Filtration sampling collects dust onto a filter using only cassettes and a vacuum pump. It is not recommended for culturable bacteria, because collection causes desiccation stress.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","prompt":"Why is filtration sampling not recommended for culturable bacteria?","trap":"The filter traps dust fine; the issue is bacteria drying out, not capture.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0872"]},{"id":"nim:h-filt-setup","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-sampling","tag":"FACT","text":"For surface dust, 6 inches of plastic tubing goes on the inlet of a white-banded 37 mm MCEF or yellow-banded pre-weighed 37 mm PVC cassette, with the pump on the outlet.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","prompt":"How is a filter cassette set up to collect settled surface dust?","trap":"Tubing on the inlet port, pump on the outlet port.","num":{"q":"A __ length of plastic tubing is attached to the cassette inlet to vacuum settled dust.","a":"6 inch","d":["1 inch","4 inch","25 foot"]},"qs":["cvi-0873"]},{"id":"nim:h-filt-fill","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-sampling","tag":"REC","text":"The tubing's free end is rubbed along the dusty surface, and the cassette becomes the sample container. It should be filled at least one quarter full if at all possible.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","prompt":"How full should a filter cassette be when collecting surface dust?","trap":null,"num":{"q":"A dust collection cassette should be filled at least __ full, if at all possible.","a":"one quarter","d":["one tenth","one half","three quarters"]},"qs":["cvi-0873"]},{"id":"nim:h-tape","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-sampling","tag":"FACT","text":"Tape sampling is mainly for smooth surfaces. Clear 1 inch tape is dabbed until loaded, then stuck to a glass slide or inside a thick zip-lock bag, never folded on itself.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","prompt":"How is a tape sample collected and handled?","trap":"Never fold the tape onto itself.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0874"]},{"id":"nim:h-swab-dry","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-sampling","tag":"SHALL","text":"A swab sample uses a 4 inch filter circle wiped dry, never wet, and loaded as heavily as possible. It is then folded in halves or quarters and sealed in a container.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","prompt":"How is a filter circle swab sample collected?","trap":"Dry, never wet; load heavily, not lightly.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0875"]},{"id":"nim:h-swab-surfaces","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-sampling","tag":"FACT","text":"Swab sampling works on smooth, roughened, or irregular surfaces, and swabs serve both qualitative microscopy and culturable samples.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","prompt":"Which surfaces and analyses does swab sampling suit?","trap":"Tape is mainly for smooth surfaces; swabs also handle rough or irregular ones.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0876"]},{"id":"nim:h-dep-light","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-case","tag":"FACT","text":"Lighter particles usually blow through the system into the room unless they stick to an obstruction such as a turning vane, sheet metal screw, or duct joint.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","prompt":"Where do lighter particles end up in a duct system?","trap":"Obstructions catch light particles; flow disruptions drop heavy ones.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0877"]},{"id":"nim:h-dep-heavy","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-case","tag":"FACT","text":"Heavier particles fall out when air velocity cannot carry them, often at bends, dampers, and branch take-offs where airflow becomes unsteady.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","prompt":"Where do heavier particles tend to deposit, and why?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0877"]},{"id":"nim:h-gray-film","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-case","tag":"FACT","text":"A light film of gray dust on the bottom inside of ductwork is not unusual and generally of little concern. It usually looks transparent and dulls reflected light.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","prompt":"What is a light gray film on the duct bottom, and how serious is it?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0878"]},{"id":"nim:h-rebalance","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-case","tag":"REC","text":"Settled particles can become airborne when duct airflow increases, such as after fan replacement, damper or balance changes, or upstream duct changes. Check duct cleanliness before rebalancing or replacing air handler parts.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","prompt":"Why check duct cleanliness before rebalancing or replacing AHU components?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0879"]},{"id":"nim:h-case-random","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-case","tag":"FACT","text":"In the case study, all four clustered rooftop AHUs were inspected, but two of four largely identical duct systems were chosen at random as a representative sample.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","prompt":"How did the case study choose which AHUs and duct systems to inspect?","trap":"All AHUs were inspected; only two duct systems were.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0880"]},{"id":"nim:h-case-renovation","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-case","tag":"NOTE","text":"A renovated floor was flagged for inspection: construction may have let debris into its system, and the change made it unlike the others, so random selection was no longer advisable.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","prompt":"Why did the case study flag the renovated floor's system for inspection?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0881"]},{"id":"nim:h-case-residue","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-case","tag":"FACT","text":"Dried residue in an AHU condensate pan led the inspector to check that unit's riser with a push camera for microbial growth. It was clean, so the original plan stood.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","prompt":"What did dried condensate pan residue prompt in the case study?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0882"]},{"id":"nim:h-case-no-sample","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-case","tag":"FACT","text":"When the client declined sampling of a suspect stain and wanted the problem eliminated, the inspector documented it and recommended cleaning the coil and ductwork without sampling.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","prompt":"How did the case study handle a client who declined sampling of a stain?","trap":"The client decides on sampling; the finding is still reported.","num":null,"qs":["cvi-0883"]},{"id":"nim:h-case-shredder","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-case","tag":"FACT","text":"A bulk sample of yellowish, whitish duct dust showed cellulose, meaning paper or cardboard dust. A shredder near the returns was the suspected source, and moving it was recommended.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","prompt":"What did the cellulose dust in the case study point to?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0884"]},{"id":"nim:h-case-coil-baseline","dom":"Inspection","lesson":"nim-h-case","tag":"REC","text":"With no as-built coil static pressure or cleaning records, a pressure drop test was impractical, so the report advised cleaning coils and recording pressure drop as a future baseline.","src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","prompt":"What did the case study recommend when coil pressure data was missing?","trap":null,"num":null,"qs":["cvi-0885"]}],"lessons":[{"id":"hvac-system-types","title":"Air system types: CAV, reheat, DOAS and more","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":50,"intro":"How the main central system types hold airflow or temperature and where heat is added. The exam asks what each type is and why some waste energy.","mnemonic":"VAV varies Volume; CAV varies the temperature. Reheat, multizone and dual duct all cool first, then heat or remix.","units":["hvac:s-single-zone","hvac:s-cav-control","hvac:s-cav-reheat","hvac:s-simultaneous-heat-cool","hvac:s-reheat","hvac:s-trend-id","hvac:s-doas","hvac:s-active-beam","hvac:s-ufad","hvac:s-mua"]},{"id":"hvac-vav","title":"VAV systems and VAV boxes","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":55,"intro":"How a VAV system and its boxes vary airflow, what the flow sensor does, and the faults a duct cleaner can cause or spot at a box.","mnemonic":"Box sequence as the zone cools off: throttle, minimum, then reheat. Never closed, never reheating wide open.","units":["hvac:s-vav-control","hvac:s-vav-fan-vfd","hvac:s-bypass-vav","hvac:s-vav-box","hvac:s-pressure-independent","hvac:s-pressure-dependent","hvac:s-flow-sensor-fouling","hvac:s-vav-minimum","hvac:s-cooling-only-overcool","hvac:s-vav-reheat-sequence","hvac:s-vav-damper-fail","hvac:s-leaking-reheat-valve","hvac:s-open-box-cleaning"]},{"id":"hvac-fan-boxes","title":"Series vs parallel fan-powered boxes","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":65,"intro":"The two fan-powered VAV terminals, where the fan sits, when it runs, and the parts that load with dust or fail.","mnemonic":"Series = Steady and in the Stream: fan always on, all air through it. Parallel = Part-time and off to the side: fan runs for heat only, backdraft damper stops spill.","units":["hvac:s-primary-air","hvac:s-series-fan","hvac:s-series-constant","hvac:s-parallel-fan","hvac:s-parallel-backdraft","hvac:s-series-low-static","hvac:s-plenum-free-heat","hvac:s-induction-filter","hvac:s-series-backward"]},{"id":"hvac-multizone","title":"Multizone and dual duct","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":70,"intro":"Two older hot-and-cold systems that are easy to confuse. The exam tests where the mixing happens and what that means at the zone.","mnemonic":"Multizone Mixes at the Machine, one duct per zone. Dual duct Delivers two Ducts to every zone box.","units":["hvac:s-multizone","hvac:s-multizone-ducts","hvac:s-dual-duct","hvac:s-dual-duct-terminal","hvac:s-dual-duct-fault","hvac:s-multizone-waste","hvac:s-multizone-leak"]},{"id":"hvac-room-units","title":"Fan coils, PTACs, water-source and ductless units","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":80,"intro":"The local units a tech meets in hotels, condos and offices, what is inside each, and where each one grows or holds dirt.","mnemonic":"Two pipes, one season; four pipes, both at once.","units":["hvac:s-fan-coil","hvac:s-two-four-pipe","hvac:s-ptac","hvac:s-pthp","hvac:s-ptac-slinger","hvac:s-wshp-loop","hvac:s-wshp-access","hvac:s-induction-unit","hvac:s-mini-split","hvac:s-mini-split-blower","hvac:s-multi-split"]},{"id":"hvac-ahu","title":"Inside the air handler","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":90,"intro":"The parts of an air handler in airflow order, draw-through vs blow-through, return and relief fans, humidifiers, and the drain pan and trap rules.","mnemonic":"Path: Mice Find Cheese First Sometimes = Mixing box, Filters, Coils, Fan, Supply. Traps: pulling needs a full inch, pushing needs half.","units":["hvac:s-ahu-path","hvac:s-mixing-box","hvac:s-preheat","hvac:s-draw-through","hvac:s-blow-through","hvac:s-blow-through-wet","hvac:s-return-relief-fans","hvac:s-oa-intake","hvac:s-humidifier-types","hvac:s-ultrasonic","hvac:s-absorption-distance","hvac:s-drain-pan","hvac:s-pan-slope","hvac:s-draw-through-trap","hvac:s-trap-height","hvac:s-float-switch","hvac:s-overflow-protection"]},{"id":"acr-what-acr-covers","title":"What ACR 2025 is and what it covers","dom":"Standards","order":100,"intro":"ACR 2025 is the industry standard of care: a performance standard with minimum procedures, applied to airside surfaces. Scope exclusions and the no-warranty lines are favorite distractors.","mnemonic":"Three P's are out of scope: non-ducted open air Plenums, Parts repair (electrical, pneumatic, high-pressure vessels, gas and oil controls), and OEM Preventative maintenance.","units":["acr:G-1","acr:G-2","acr:G-3","acr:G-4","acr:G-9a","acr:G-9b","acr:G-5","acr:G-6","acr:G-7","acr:G-8","acr:G-9","acr:G-13","acr:G-16","acr:G-17"]},{"id":"hvac-economizers","title":"Economizers, outdoor air and energy recovery","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":100,"intro":"How outdoor air enters and is checked with temperature readings, how economizers fail, and how ERVs, HRVs and wheels move heat between airstreams.","mnemonic":"ERV is Everything (heat and moisture); HRV is Heat only.","units":["hvac:s-economizer","hvac:s-econ-drybulb-enthalpy","hvac:s-econ-integrated","hvac:s-econ-stuck-open","hvac:s-oa-fraction","hvac:s-econ-readings","hvac:s-min-oa-fixed","hvac:s-relief-air","hvac:s-erv-hrv","hvac:s-hrv-drain","hvac:s-enthalpy-wheel","hvac:s-wheel-leakage","hvac:s-runaround"]},{"id":"acr-obligation-words","title":"Shall, recommend, may, can","dom":"Standards","order":110,"intro":"Most ACR clauses carry an obligation word, and the exam tests the word as often as the fact. Learn what each word binds you to before you learn the clauses.","mnemonic":"Only SHALL binds. RECOMMEND is advice. MAY is 'Mother, may I?': permission given. CAN is 'I can': an ability you have. An answer that makes MAY, CAN, or RECOMMEND a requirement is wrong.","units":["acr:G-19","acr:G-20","acr:G-21","acr:G-22","acr:G-23","acr:G-24","acr:G-25"]},{"id":"hvac-coils","title":"Coils and refrigeration basics","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":110,"intro":"What the coil types are, the refrigeration cycle in plain words, the rule-of-thumb numbers, and what fouling does to a coil.","mnemonic":"12,000 BTU per ton, 400 cfm per ton, 500 fpm face, 15 to 20 degree split.","units":["hvac:s-coil-function","hvac:s-dx-coil","hvac:s-evap-cond","hvac:s-chilled-water","hvac:s-water-steam-coils","hvac:s-ton","hvac:s-cfm-per-ton","hvac:s-coil-delta-t","hvac:s-face-velocity","hvac:s-coil-rows","hvac:s-fin-straightening","hvac:s-coil-loading","hvac:s-leaking-heat-valve","hvac:s-coil-access"]},{"id":"acr-people-and-codes","title":"Qualified people, codes, and judgment","dom":"Standards","order":120,"intro":"Who may do work to ACR 2025, and which rules win when the Standard and the law disagree. Tested because required training sits right beside recommended certification.","units":["acr:G-10","acr:G-11","acr:G-12","acr:X-9","acr:G-14","acr:G-14a","acr:G-15","acr:G-18"],"mnemonic":"Trained is a must, certified is a wish: training, qualifications, knowledge and experience are SHALLs, but ASCS oversight is only recommended. Follow the codes and laws strictly, a microbial code beats contradictory text, and judgment MAY justify a deviation."},{"id":"hvac-heat-pumps","title":"Heat pumps and residential systems","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":120,"intro":"Split vs packaged equipment, furnace coil placement and safeties, and how heat pumps heat, defrost and use backup heat.","mnemonic":"Aux is Automatic and alongside the compressor; EM is Manual and alone. Dual fuel takes turns.","units":["hvac:s-split-system","hvac:s-packaged-unit","hvac:s-coil-only","hvac:s-furnace-coil-location","hvac:s-high-limit","hvac:s-cracked-hx","hvac:s-zoning-bypass","hvac:s-reversing-valve","hvac:s-defrost","hvac:s-aux-heat","hvac:s-emergency-heat","hvac:s-balance-point","hvac:s-dual-fuel","hvac:s-geothermal"]},{"id":"hvac-fans","title":"Fans, motors and the fan laws","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":130,"intro":"Fan types and where they are found, the fan laws, and what drives, motors and dirty wheels do to airflow.","mnemonic":"1-2-3: flow to the 1st power of speed, pressure to the 2nd, power to the 3rd. Double the speed: 2x air, 4x pressure, 8x power.","units":["hvac:s-fan-fc","hvac:s-fan-bi","hvac:s-fan-plenum","hvac:s-fan-axial","hvac:s-fc-overload","hvac:s-fan-law-flow","hvac:s-fan-law-pressure","hvac:s-fan-law-power","hvac:s-fan-half-speed","hvac:s-vfd","hvac:s-ecm","hvac:s-belt-drive","hvac:s-dirty-wheel","hvac:s-system-resistance"]},{"id":"hvac-dampers","title":"Dampers","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":140,"intro":"What each damper type does and what closes it, plus the fire damper temperatures and test intervals the exam uses as traps.","mnemonic":"Fire = Fusible (heat); Smoke = Signal (detector). 160 code floor, 165 standard link, 212 option, 350 smoke control ceiling. Test at 1 year, then every 4; hospitals 6.","units":["hvac:s-dampers-balancing","hvac:s-backdraft","hvac:s-opposed-blade","hvac:s-face-bypass","hvac:s-fire-damper","hvac:s-smoke-damper","hvac:s-combination-damper","hvac:s-static-dynamic","hvac:s-link-165","hvac:s-imc-160","hvac:s-nfpa80-test"]},{"id":"hvac-controls","title":"Controls","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":145,"intro":"The control points a duct cleaner works around: duct static and supply temperature loops, safeties, and outdoor air control.","mnemonic":"Trim until the worst box is almost wide open: static reset lowers duct static, and the sensor sits about two-thirds down the main. Freeze stat trips near 35 to 38°F.","units":["hvac:s-static-sensor","hvac:s-static-reset","hvac:s-static-too-low","hvac:s-sat-reset","hvac:s-sequence-of-operation","hvac:s-freeze-stat","hvac:s-duct-smoke-detector","hvac:s-dcv","hvac:s-unoccupied","hvac:s-pressure-interlock"]},{"id":"acr-need-to-clean","title":"When cleaning is recommended: the 16 triggers","dom":"Inspection","order":150,"intro":"Sixteen conditions that, shown by an inspection or building history, make cleaning recommended. Tested for the wording of each trigger and for the fact that all 16 are recommendations, not requirements.","mnemonic":"4 + 7 + 5 = 16 triggers, all recommendations: 4 you sense, 7 events, 5 programs.","units":["acr:N-0","acr:N-1","acr:N-18","acr:N-2","acr:N-3","acr:N-4","acr:N-5","acr:N-6","acr:N-7","acr:N-8","acr:N-16","acr:N-9","acr:N-10","acr:N-11","acr:N-12","acr:N-13","acr:N-14","acr:N-15","acr:N-17"]},{"id":"hvac-duct-materials","title":"Duct types and materials","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":150,"intro":"Galvanized, spiral, flat oval, duct board, liner, wrap, flex and fabric duct: what each is, which are porous, and the UL 181 listings behind them. Exams test the porous list and the board, liner, wrap confusion.","mnemonic":"Board IS the duct, liner is IN it, wrap is ON it. UL 181A for rigid board, 181B for bendy flex. Class 0 is zero; Class 1 is 25/50. Flex: 4 ft supports, half-inch sag per foot.","units":["hvac:a-galvanized-nonporous","hvac:a-spiral-flat-oval","hvac:a-duct-board","hvac:a-duct-liner","hvac:a-duct-wrap","hvac:a-wrap-repair","hvac:a-porous-vs-nonporous","hvac:a-porous-dry-only","hvac:a-flex-friction","hvac:a-flex-support","hvac:a-ul181-classes","hvac:a-ul181-family","hvac:a-board-closures","hvac:a-board-pressure","hvac:a-flex-no-openings","hvac:a-fabric-duct"]},{"id":"hvac-duct-design","title":"Duct sizing, fittings and noise","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":160,"intro":"How ducts are sized (equal friction, static regain), why round beats rectangular, and how elbows, vanes, takeoffs, returns and attenuators change pressure loss and noise.","mnemonic":"ACCA order J, S, D, T: loads, then equipment, then ducts, then terminals. Double the air, four times the friction. Keep rectangles at 4 to 1 or squarer.","units":["hvac:a-equal-friction","hvac:a-static-regain","hvac:a-round-vs-rect","hvac:a-aspect-ratio","hvac:a-equivalent-length","hvac:a-velocity-squared","hvac:a-critical-path","hvac:a-takeoffs","hvac:a-turning-vanes","hvac:a-attenuator","hvac:a-undersized-return","hvac:a-transfer-path","hvac:a-acca-manuals"]},{"id":"hvac-air-distribution","title":"Outlets, throw and room air","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":165,"intro":"What moves the air and what releases it: the fan, the return plenum, diffusers, grilles and registers, plus how a supply jet throws, clings to the ceiling, dumps at low flow, and why smudge rings do not prove dirty ducts.","mnemonic":"Fan makes it, plenum carries it, diffuser spreads it, grille passes it, register adds a damper. Throw is quoted at 150, 100 and 50 fpm.","units":["hvac:d-fan-creates-airflow","hvac:d-return-plenum","hvac:d-diffuser","hvac:d-register","hvac:d-throw","hvac:d-coanda","hvac:d-dumping","hvac:d-smudging","hvac:d-laminar-or"]},{"id":"hvac-duct-leakage","title":"Duct leakage, SMACNA classes and location losses","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":170,"intro":"SMACNA pressure, seal and leakage classes at exam level, how leakage is tested and calculated, and what supply leaks, return leaks and attic runs cost.","mnemonic":"Seal classes: A adds All openings, B sits Between (joints and seams), C is Crossways joints only. Supply leaks push out; return leaks pull in.","units":["hvac:a-smacna-pressure-class","hvac:a-seal-class-a","hvac:a-seal-class-b","hvac:a-seal-class-c","hvac:a-leakage-class","hvac:a-leak-test","hvac:a-leakage-calc","hvac:a-supply-vs-return-leaks","hvac:a-duct-heat-gain","hvac:a-afue-duct-loss","hvac:a-attic-duct-r8"]},{"id":"hvac-exhaust","title":"Exhaust systems: grease, lab, toilet and dryer","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":180,"intro":"Which exhaust ducts fall under ACR and which move to NFPA 96 and IKECA, relief versus exhaust air, and the construction and material rules for grease, lab, toilet and dryer exhaust.","mnemonic":"NFPA 96 inspections: fuel monthly, heavy quarterly, normal twice a year, light once. IKECA: I inspects, C cleans, M maintains. Grease duct gauge: carbon 16, stainless 18.","units":["hvac:a-exhaust-in-scope","hvac:a-nfpa96-scope","hvac:a-ikeca","hvac:a-grease-duct","hvac:a-nfpa96-frequency","hvac:a-lab-exhaust-material","hvac:a-lab-exhaust-hazard","hvac:a-toilet-exhaust","hvac:a-dryer-duct","hvac:a-intake-separation","hvac:a-negative-building"]},{"id":"hvac-filters-merv","title":"MERV, HEPA and filter types","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":190,"intro":"How ASHRAE 52.2 builds a MERV, the size ranges and key thresholds, what HEPA means, typical filter families, and the ASHRAE 62.1 MERV 8 floor.","mnemonic":"Size breaks 0.3, 1, 3, 10 (E1 fine, E2 mid, E3 coarse). Families: 1-4 flat fiberglass, 5-8 pleated, 9-16 rigid box and pocket. MERV 13 is 50/85/90. HEPA is 99.97 at 0.3. Coil floor MERV 8.","units":["hvac:a-merv-def","hvac:a-merv-range","hvac:a-merv-not-others","hvac:a-e-ranges","hvac:a-merv13","hvac:a-merv16","hvac:a-merv-low","hvac:a-merv-a","hvac:a-hepa","hvac:a-hepa-type","hvac:a-filter-families","hvac:a-merv8-floor","hvac:a-prefilter"]},{"id":"hvac-filters-performance","title":"Filter pressure drop, bypass and air cleaners","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":195,"intro":"What filter gauges tell you, why bypass beats any MERV, and what electronic cleaners, UV-C and carbon do and do not remove.","mnemonic":"Rising gauge means loading; falling gauge means leaking. Electronic makes ozone, UV-C kills but does not catch, carbon catches gases.","units":["hvac:a-high-merv-effect","hvac:a-filter-dp-loading","hvac:a-filter-dp-falls","hvac:a-bypass","hvac:a-bypass-acr","hvac:a-electronic-cleaner","hvac:a-ozone-limit","hvac:a-uvc","hvac:a-uvc-loto","hvac:a-carbon"]},{"id":"acr-inspection-purpose","title":"What inspections decide and what the assessment delivers","dom":"Inspection","order":200,"intro":"The inspection gathers the facts, before and after every project. The system component assessment turns them into a recommendation and a scope. Tested because the two lists overlap and are easy to swap.","mnemonic":"Inspect before AND after. The assessment answers five questions: Clean it? (need) What? (scope) How? (techniques) How is the space protected? (engineering controls) Anything special? (unique requirements).","units":["acr:1.0-a","acr:1.0-b","acr:1.0-c","acr:1.1-a","acr:1.1-b","acr:1.1-c","acr:1.10-a","acr:1.10-b","acr:1.10-c","acr:1.10-d","acr:1.10-e","acr:1.10-f"]},{"id":"hvac-air-pressure","title":"Static, velocity and total pressure","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":200,"intro":"Static, velocity and total pressure, the pitot tube and traverse, 4005, total external static and component pressure drops, with worked examples.","mnemonic":"Tip reads Total, Side reads Static, the difference is Velocity: TP = SP + VP. Then V = 4005 times root VP. TESP: add the two sides, ignore the signs.","units":["hvac:a-iwc-fpm","hvac:a-sp-vp-tp","hvac:a-pitot-ports","hvac:a-v-4005","hvac:a-v-4005-example","hvac:a-traverse-why","hvac:a-flow-hood","hvac:a-tesp","hvac:a-tesp-example","hvac:a-component-drop","hvac:a-resistance-airflow","hvac:a-dp-flow-squared"]},{"id":"hvac-airflow-math","title":"Airflow math: cfm, tons and heat","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":205,"intro":"cfm = fpm x area, tons and 400 cfm per ton, the 1.08 sensible heat formula and temperature split, each with a worked example.","mnemonic":"A ton is 12,000; figure 400 cfm per ton. 1.08 = 0.075 x 0.24 x 60: density, specific heat, minutes.","units":["hvac:a-q-va","hvac:a-q-va-example","hvac:a-round-area","hvac:a-face-velocity","hvac:a-tons-example","hvac:a-sensible-108","hvac:a-sensible-example","hvac:a-heat-constants","hvac:a-temp-split","hvac:a-split-meaning"]},{"id":"acr-inspector-prep","title":"Who inspects and how to prepare","dom":"Inspection","order":210,"intro":"The mandatory knowledge an inspector needs, the recommended credentials, and the steps around the inspection itself: risk assessment, plans, and engineering controls. The trap is required knowledge versus recommended certification.","mnemonic":"Don't Enter The System: Design, Engineering, Techniques, Standards, the four areas of verifiable working knowledge. Credentials (ASCS, CVI) are only recommended.","units":["acr:1.2-a","acr:1.2-h","acr:1.2-b","acr:1.2-c","acr:1.2-d","acr:1.2-e","acr:1.2-f","acr:1.2-g","acr:1.2.1-a","acr:1.2.1-b","acr:1.3-a","acr:1.3-b","acr:1.3-c","acr:1.4-a","acr:1.4-b","acr:1.4-c","acr:1.4-d"]},{"id":"hvac-psychrometrics","title":"Psychrometrics: dew point and latent heat","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":210,"intro":"Dry bulb, wet bulb, dew point, RH, sensible versus latent heat and why surfaces below dew point sweat. Tested through condensation and coil scenarios.","mnemonic":"Dew point, then wet bulb, then dry bulb, lowest to highest; all equal at saturation. Colder than dew point, it sweats.","units":["hvac:a-dry-wet-bulb","hvac:a-dew-point","hvac:a-condensation","hvac:a-rh-rises","hvac:a-rh-misleads","hvac:a-sensible-latent","hvac:a-latent-needs-dew","hvac:a-reheat-dehumidify","hvac:a-latent-sources","hvac:a-enthalpy","hvac:a-grains","hvac:a-oversized-ac"]},{"id":"acr-table-1","title":"Routine inspections and the Table 1 schedule","dom":"Inspection","order":220,"intro":"How often ACR 2025 recommends inspecting air handlers, supply ducts, and return and exhaust ducts in five building uses, and what changes during construction. Table 1 intervals are among the most drilled numbers on both exams.","mnemonic":"AHU = 1 year for everyone. Homes and boats (Residential, Marine) get a break on ducts: 1, 2, 2. The workplaces (Commercial, Industrial, Healthcare) are 1, 1, 1.","units":["acr:1.5-a","acr:1.5-b","acr:1.5-c","acr:1.5-d","acr:1.5.1-a","acr:1.5.1-b","acr:1.5.1-c","acr:1.5.1-d","acr:1.5.1-e","acr:1.5.1-f","acr:1.6-a","acr:1.6-b","acr:1.6-c"]},{"id":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","title":"Mixed air, outdoor air and CO2","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":220,"intro":"Reading mixed-air temperatures to find outdoor air fraction, spotting damper and valve faults from readings, and using CO2 as a ventilation indicator without overreaching.","mnemonic":"OA fraction: how far the mix moved from return, over the whole outdoor-to-return gap. CO2: 15 cfm per person holds about 700 ppm over outdoors, once steady.","units":["hvac:a-mixed-air-temp","hvac:a-oa-fraction","hvac:a-oa-fraction-example","hvac:a-oa-fraction-spread","hvac:a-oa-vs-damper","hvac:a-fixed-min-oa","hvac:a-mixing-leak","hvac:a-leaking-heat-valve","hvac:a-co2-occupants","hvac:a-co2-700","hvac:a-co2-steady-state","hvac:a-co2-example","hvac:a-co2-no-limit"]},{"id":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","title":"The 10% rule and the air handler checklist","dom":"Inspection","order":230,"intro":"How much of a system a cleanliness inspection must cover, the S520 all-components exception, and the one component list in 1.7 written as SHALL. Expect the 10% figure, the exception, and the AHU list.","mnemonic":"Follow the air through the box: dampers, filters, coils, condensate, humidifier, fan, acoustic insulation, then door gaskets and unit integrity.","units":["acr:1.7-a","acr:1.7-b","acr:1.7-c","acr:1.7-d","acr:1.7-e","acr:X-7","acr:1.7.1-a","acr:1.7.1-b","acr:1.7.1-c","acr:1.7.1-d","acr:1.7.1-e","acr:1.7.1-f","acr:1.7.1-g","acr:1.7.1-h","acr:1.7.1-i"]},{"id":"hvac-efficiency","title":"Efficiency ratings and 2023 minimums","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":230,"intro":"SEER2, EER2, HSPF2, AFUE and COP, the SEER2 test static, and the federal minimums in force since January 1, 2023.","mnemonic":"SEER2 is the season, EER2 one 95 F point, HSPF2 the heating season. Split heat pump 14.3 and 7.5; package heat pump 13.4 and 6.7; split and package ACs start at 13.4.","units":["hvac:a-seer2","hvac:a-eer2","hvac:a-hspf2","hvac:a-afue","hvac:a-cop","hvac:a-seer2-test-static","hvac:a-tesp-vs-rating","hvac:a-min-hp-seer2","hvac:a-min-hp-hspf2","hvac:a-min-ac"]},{"id":"acr-other-components","title":"Supply, return, plenum, exhaust, and damaged components","dom":"Inspection","order":240,"intro":"What the supply, return, and exhaust inspections take in, how the new 2025 open air plenum clause works, and what to do with damaged parts. Tested for the new numbering and for may versus shall wording.","mnemonic":"1.7.1 to 1.7.6 follow the air: AHU, Supply out, Return back, Open plenum (new, a MAY), Exhaust leaves, Damage gets documented. 'All Supply Returns Open Every Door.'","units":["acr:1.7.2-a","acr:1.7.2-b","acr:1.7.2-c","acr:1.7.3-a","acr:1.7.3-b","acr:1.7.3-c","acr:1.7.3-d","acr:1.7.4-a","acr:1.7.4-b","acr:1.7.4-c","acr:1.7.4-d","acr:1.7.5-a","acr:1.7.5-b","acr:1.7.6-a","acr:1.7.6-b"]},{"id":"hvac-drawings","title":"Reading mechanical drawings","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":240,"intro":"Sheet numbering, legends, duct size tags, schedules, plan versus section versus detail, and damper and piping symbols, plus what ACR says about using plans.","mnemonic":"UDS sheet digits: 0 legend, 1 plan, 2 elevation, 3 section, 5 detail, 6 schedule.","units":["hvac:a-uds-sheet-types","hvac:a-legend","hvac:a-duct-size-notation","hvac:a-equipment-schedules","hvac:a-air-device-schedule","hvac:a-schedule-vs-field","hvac:a-plan-vs-section","hvac:a-detail","hvac:a-line-types","hvac:a-damper-tags","hvac:a-other-tags","hvac:a-piping-tags","hvac:a-acr-plans","hvac:a-field-controls"]},{"id":"acr-hazmat-microbial","title":"Hazardous materials and suspect microbial growth","dom":"Inspection","order":250,"intro":"What to do when an inspection may expose people to hazardous materials or turns up suspect microbial growth, and who takes it from there. A mandatory same-visit duct inspection sits among recommendations that read mandatory.","mnemonic":"Suspect growth in the box? Check both ducts now: supply and return on the same visit, Table 1 clock ignored. That is the only SHALL in 1.8 and 1.9. Every other action there is only recommended.","units":["acr:1.8-a","acr:1.8-b","acr:1.8-c","acr:X-8","acr:1.9-a","acr:1.9-b","acr:1.9-c","acr:1.9.1-a","acr:1.9.1-b","acr:1.9.1-c","acr:1.9.1-d","acr:1.9.2-a","acr:1.9.2-b"]},{"id":"acr-work-plan","title":"The written work plan: what must be in it","dom":"Inspection","order":300,"intro":"A written work plan is mandatory before any cleaning, but only some of its parts are. Covers its purpose, the required scope of work, and the recommended means, trades, schedule, and communication sections.","mnemonic":"Scope opens, Submittals and Safety close: 2.2, 2.7, and 2.8 are the SHALL sections. Everything between them (2.3 methods, 2.4 trades, 2.5 schedule, 2.6 communication) is only recommended.","units":["acr:2.0-a","acr:2.0-b","acr:2.0-c","acr:2.0-d","acr:2.1-a","acr:2.1-b","acr:2.2-a","acr:2.2-b","acr:2.2-c","acr:2.3-a","acr:2.4-a","acr:2.4-b","acr:2.5-a","acr:2.5-b","acr:2.6-a","acr:2.6-b"]},{"id":"acr-submittals-safety","title":"Product submittals, SDS, and the safety plan","dom":"Inspection","order":310,"intro":"The mandatory product, coating, and SDS requirements, controls for chemical vapors, and the life safety plan for detectors taken off-line. Watch for the one CAN and the one REC among the SHALLs.","units":["acr:2.7-a","acr:2.7-b","acr:2.7-c","acr:2.7.1-a","acr:2.7.1-b","acr:2.7.1-c","acr:2.7.2-a","acr:2.7.2-b","acr:2.8-a","acr:2.8-b","acr:2.8-c","acr:2.8-d"],"mnemonic":"Client CAN, representatives recommended; every other item in this lesson is a SHALL."},{"id":"acr-engineering-controls","title":"Engineering controls: what counts and when","dom":"Risk Management","order":350,"intro":"Section 3 opens by saying what engineering controls are for and closes by making them mandatory on every project. The exam tests the list, the fact that PPE is not on it, and which summary lines are only recommendations.","mnemonic":"The ten controls in 3.0 order: 'Some Inspectors Prefer Dust-free Homes Here, Despite The Humid South' = Source control, Isolation barriers, Pressure differentials, Dust suppression, HEPA vacuuming, HEPA filtration, Detailed cleaning, Temperature, Humidity, Sanitary approach. Nothing you wear is on the list.","units":["acr:3.0-a","acr:3.0-b","acr:3.0-c","acr:3.0-d","acr:3.0-e","acr:3.0-f","acr:3.0-g","acr:3.0-h","acr:X-12","acr:3.13-a","acr:3.13-b","acr:3.13-c","acr:3.13-d","acr:3.13-e"]},{"id":"acr-equipment-hygiene","title":"Contractor equipment: clean in, clean out","dom":"Risk Management","order":360,"intro":"Section 3.1 treats your own tools as a contamination source: what equipment it covers, and when it must be cleaned, serviced, opened, or sealed. The exam likes the one recommendation hiding among the shalls.","mnemonic":"Clean IN (3.1.1), service DURING (3.1.2), open a dirty vac only in containment or OUTSIDE (3.1.2.1), clean or seal before it MOVES or LEAVES (3.1.3). The on-site vac check (3.1.4) is the lone 'recommended'.","units":["acr:3.1-a","acr:3.1-b","acr:3.1-c","acr:3.1-d","acr:3.1-e","acr:3.1-f","acr:3.1-g","acr:3.1.1-a","acr:3.1.1-b","acr:3.1.2-a","acr:3.1.2-b","acr:3.1.2.1-a","acr:3.1.2.1-b","acr:3.1.2.1-c","acr:3.1.3-a","acr:3.1.3-b","acr:3.1.4-a","acr:3.1.4-b"]},{"id":"acr-site-controls","title":"Site controls: exhaust, pressure, waste and backup","dom":"Risk Management","order":370,"intro":"Clauses 3.2 to 3.8 cover site controls: where fuel-powered gear sits, HEPA on vacuums that exhaust indoors, negative pressure, containing waste, air scrubbing, chemicals, and backup equipment. Scrubbing and backup are only recommended here. Watch the 2025 wording changes.","mnemonic":"Fumes away, waste wrapped, backup ready: fuel-powered gear sits where emissions cannot enter, contaminated material is contained before it leaves, and spare negative air stays on site.","units":["acr:3.2-a","acr:3.2-b","acr:3.2.1-a","acr:3.3-a","acr:3.4-a","acr:3.4-b","acr:3.4-c","acr:3.4-d","acr:3.5-a","acr:3.5.1-a","acr:3.6-a","acr:3.6-b","acr:3.6-c","acr:3.7-a","acr:3.7-b","acr:3.8-a"]},{"id":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","title":"The containment ladder and Level 1","dom":"Risk Management","order":380,"intro":"Containment has four levels, and each level inherits everything below it. This lesson shows the whole ladder in one view, then the three parts of Level 1, the minimum every project stands on.","mnemonic":"Learn only what each rung adds: 1 pressure and cover, 2 barriers and 4 ACH, 3 one decon chamber and monitoring, 4 two chambers and a recording alarm.","units":["acr:3-L1","acr:3-L2","acr:3-L3","acr:3-L4","acr:3-Lx","acr:3.9-a","acr:3.9.1-a","acr:3.9.1-b","acr:3.9.2-a","acr:3.9.2-b","acr:3.9.3-a","acr:3.9.3-b","acr:3.9-b","acr:3.9-c","acr:3.9-d","acr:X-2"]},{"id":"acr-level-2-barriers","title":"Level 2: barriers, floor and entry flaps","dom":"Risk Management","order":390,"intro":"Level 2 is where temporary barriers arrive. This lesson covers the barrier specs, the two-layer floor, and the entry flaps, plus the split the exam loves: the same 6-mil material is recommended in one clause and mandatory in the next.","mnemonic":"'Walls required, wall specs are wishes, floors are laws': barriers themselves are required, but their specs (floor to ceiling, 6-mil, airtight seals) are recommended; the two-layer 6-mil floor running 6 inches up the wall is required. Zipper gets one flap, a slit gets two.","units":["acr:3.10.1-a","acr:3.10.2-a","acr:3.10.2.1-a","acr:3.10.2.2-a","acr:3.10.2.3-a","acr:3.10.2.4-a","acr:3.10.3-a","acr:3.10.3-b","acr:3.10.3-c","acr:3.10.4-a","acr:3.10.4.1-a","acr:3.10.4.1-b","acr:3.10-c","acr:X-3","acr:3.10-d"]},{"id":"acr-level-2-pressure","title":"Level 2: pressure, 4 ACH and teardown","dom":"Risk Management","order":400,"intro":"Once the Level 2 enclosure is up, it must stay negative, be proven with an instrument, be scrubbed at 4 air changes per hour, and be cleaned before it comes down. The 4 ACH figure is the only one in the book.","mnemonic":"Level 2 runs on 'M, 4, W': a Manometer to prove it, 4 ACH to scrub it, a Wipe or HEPA vac before it comes down (plus verification first on healthcare jobs).","units":["acr:3.10.5-a","acr:3.10.5-b","acr:3.10.6-a","acr:3.10.7-a","acr:3.10.7-b","acr:3.10.7-c","acr:X-1","acr:3.10.8-a","acr:3.10.8-b"]},{"id":"acr-level-3-4","title":"Levels 3 and 4: decon chambers and monitoring","dom":"Risk Management","order":410,"intro":"Level 3 adds a single decontamination chamber and live monitoring of pressure and particulate. Level 4 doubles the chamber and adds a recording monitor with an alarm. The exam checks what each level adds and the stop-work rule.","mnemonic":"3 = one room plus Watch (continuous pressure, particulate baseline, real-time counts). 4 = two rooms plus Record and Ring (recording monitor with alarm). Stop above background; restart only when Back to background AND the cause is fixed.","units":["acr:3.11-a","acr:3.11.1-a","acr:3.11.2-a","acr:3.11.2-b","acr:3.11.2-c","acr:3.11.2-d","acr:3.11.3-a","acr:3.11.3-b","acr:3.11.3-c","acr:3.11.3-d","acr:3.11.3-e","acr:3.11.3-f","acr:3.11.3-g","acr:3.11.3-h","acr:3.12-a","acr:3.12.1-a","acr:3.12.2-a","acr:3.12.2-b","acr:3.12.3-a","acr:3.12.3-b"]},{"id":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","title":"The cleaning goal and negative duct pressure","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":450,"intro":"Section 4 starts with the target, visibly clean unless the contract names a verification level, and with keeping the duct negative before and during cleaning. Watch the HEPA versus outdoor exhaust rule.","mnemonic":"Negative before you start, negative until you finish.","units":["acr:4.0-a","acr:4.0-b","acr:4.1-a","acr:4.1-b","acr:4.1-c","acr:4.1-d","acr:4.1.1-a","acr:4.1.2-a","acr:4.1.2-b","acr:4.1.3-a"]},{"id":"acr-service-openings","title":"Service openings: rules for every opening","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":460,"intro":"Cutting into a duct is part of cleaning it. These clauses say what any opening must not damage, how fibrous glass is repaired, the flex duct ban, and which codes apply. Watch the 2025 additions: duct wrap, IMC, provincial codes.","mnemonic":"Never cut flex, just unhook it. Cut fibrous glass goes back like for like, with no exposed edges.","units":["acr:4.2.1-a","acr:4.2.1-b","acr:4.2.2-a","acr:4.2.3-a","acr:4.2.4-a","acr:4.2.4-b","acr:4.2.8.1-a","acr:4.2.8.2-a","acr:4.2.8.2-b","acr:4.2.8.2-c","acr:4.2.8.2-d","acr:4.2.8.2-e","acr:4.2.9-a","acr:4.2.9.1-a","acr:4.2.10-a","acr:4.2.10-b","acr:4.2.10-c"]},{"id":"acr-service-panels","title":"Service panels, access doors and the 25/50 rule","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":470,"intro":"The most number-dense clause in Section 4: panel gauge, fastener spacing, overlap, sealing, the UL listings, access door gauge, and the flame-spread and smoke-developed limits. Nearly every line is a shall.","mnemonic":"Panel is 4, 1, seal, 181A/B: fasteners every 4 in., 1 in. overlap, sealed, UL 181A/B closures. Flame 25, smoke doubles it to 50.","units":["acr:4.2.5.1-a","acr:4.2.5.1-b","acr:4.2.5.2-a","acr:4.2.5.2-b","acr:4.2.5.2-c","acr:4.2.5.3-a","acr:4.2.5.3-b","acr:4.2.5.4-a","acr:4.2.6-a","acr:4.2.6-b","acr:4.2.6-c","acr:4.2.7-a","acr:4.2.7-b","acr:4.2.7-c","acr:4.2.7-d","acr:4.2.7-e","acr:X-14"]},{"id":"acr-agitate-capture","title":"The core method: agitate, capture, keep water off","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":480,"intro":"ACR's cleaning method in one line: agitate to dislodge, then capture with vacuum collection. This lesson covers that method, the limits on wet cleaning, and what a cleaned air duct must achieve.","mnemonic":"Knock it loose, catch it. Pressure alone is not cleaning. Water never touches wires or fiberglass, except electric coils done the 4.11.8 way.","units":["acr:4.3-a","acr:4.3-b","acr:4.3-c","acr:4.4-a","acr:4.4-b","acr:4.4-c","acr:4.4-d","acr:4.4-e","acr:4.7-a","acr:4.7-b","acr:4.7.1-a","acr:4.7.2-a","acr:4.7.3-a","acr:4.7.4-a"]},{"id":"acr-vacuum-velocities","title":"Vacuum collection and Table 2 velocities","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":490,"intro":"Vacuum collection runs the whole time you clean and must hold enough velocity and negative pressure. Table 2, taken from the ACGIH Industrial Ventilation manual, lists recommended minimum duct design velocities; from fumes upward they climb 500 fpm per contaminant class.","mnemonic":"Minimums climb 500 fpm a step from 2,000: 'Fire Fighters Don't Avoid Heavy Mud' = Fumes 2,000, Fine light dust 2,500, Dry dusts 3,000, Average industrial 3,500, Heavy 4,000, Heavy or Moist 4,500 and up. Vapors, gases, smoke: any velocity (optimum 1,000 to 2,000).","units":["acr:4.5-a","acr:4.5-b","acr:4.5.1-a","acr:4.5.1-b","acr:4.5.1-c","acr:T2-a","acr:T2-b","acr:T2-r1","acr:T2-r2","acr:T2-r3","acr:T2-r4","acr:T2-r5","acr:T2-r6","acr:T2-r7"]},{"id":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","title":"Air handlers, dampers, registers and detectors","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":500,"intro":"Short clauses with sharp obligation splits: wet cleaning of AHU parts is recommended but water control is required, dampers are marked and reset, registers come out if possible but always go back, and detection equipment is never impaired.","mnemonic":"Mark it, clean it, put it back. Pulling registers is recommended; putting them back is a must.","units":["acr:4.6-a","acr:4.6.1-a","acr:4.6.2-a","acr:4.6.2-b","acr:4.8-a","acr:4.8-b","acr:4.9-a","acr:4.9-b","acr:4.9-c","acr:4.10-a","acr:4.10-b"]},{"id":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","title":"Coil cleaning 1: access, inspection and Type 1","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":510,"intro":"Every coil job starts with access to both faces and a visual inspection of the coil and drain pan. That inspection decides Type 1 dry or Type 2 wet. This lesson covers the triggers, the replacement cases, and the Type 1 rules and tools.","mnemonic":"Coil path: Look, Dry, Look, Wet, Look, Repeat; Replace only when wet methods cannot remove it. Jump straight to Wet when microbes are suspected or the assessment says dry will not do it. Type 1 toolkit 'VBAF': HEPA contact Vacuum, Brushes, compressed Air, Fin straighteners.","units":["acr:4.11-a","acr:4.11-b","acr:4.11.1-a","acr:4.11.1-b","acr:4.11.1.1-a","acr:4.11.1.2-a","acr:4.11.1.3-a","acr:4.11.1.3-b","acr:4.11.2-a","acr:4.11.2-b","acr:4.11.2-c","acr:4.11.2-d","acr:4.11.2-e","acr:4.11.2-f","acr:4.11.2-g","acr:4.11.2-h","acr:4.11.3-a","acr:4.11.3-b"]},{"id":"acr-coil-type2","title":"Coil cleaning 2: Type 2 wet cleaning and the drain pan","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":520,"intro":"Type 2 is wet cleaning of the coil, drain pan, and drain line. This lesson covers its triggers, the permitted methods, the drain pan tests, product limits, and the repeat-or-replace rule, plus the big 2025 change: Type 1 no longer always comes first.","mnemonic":"Type 2 = 'water TOO': coil products, line-pressure water, pressure washer, hot water or steam. Three roads into Type 2: Microbes suspected, Assessment says dry will not do, Debris left after dry. Drain pan: Test, Clean and flush, Test again.","units":["acr:4.11.4-a","acr:4.11.4-b","acr:4.11.4-c","acr:4.11.4-h","acr:4.11.4-d","acr:4.11.4-e","acr:4.11.4-f","acr:4.11.4-g","acr:4.11.4-g2","acr:4.11.4-g3","acr:4.11.4.1-a","acr:4.11.4.1-b","acr:4.11.4.2-a","acr:4.11.4.2-b","acr:4.11.4.2-c","acr:4.11.5-a","acr:4.11.5-b","acr:4.11.5-c"]},{"id":"acr-coil-special","title":"Coil cleaning 3: measuring results, inline and electric coils","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":530,"intro":"Why a pressure drop reading beats your eyes, how to handle duct-mounted and inline coils without drain pans, and the four-step rule for wet cleaning electric resistance coils, the only exception to keeping water off electrical parts.","mnemonic":"Electric coil: 'Kill, Mild, Rinse, Dry': de-energize and lockout/tagout, non-corrosive detergent only, rinse free of chemicals, fully dry before power returns.","units":["acr:4.11.6-a","acr:4.11.6-b","acr:4.11.7-a","acr:4.11.7-b","acr:4.11.7-c","acr:4.11.8-a","acr:4.11.8-b","acr:4.11.8-c","acr:4.11.8-d"]},{"id":"acr-restoration-order","title":"Restoration after cleaning: order, repair and surface treatments","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":540,"intro":"Restoration never comes first. This lesson covers the order of operations, what happens to components that cannot take cleaning, the microbial remediation standard, and when surface treatments may go on.","mnemonic":"Clean, then restore. Clean, verify PASS, then coat. Coatings wear plain '181'; tapes and mastics wear '181A/B'.","units":["acr:4.12-a","acr:4.13-a","acr:4.13-b","acr:4.13-c","acr:4.14-a","acr:4.14.1-a","acr:4.14.2-a","acr:4.14.3-a","acr:4.14.4-a","acr:4.14.4-b","acr:4.14.4-c","acr:4.15-a","acr:4.15-b","acr:4.15-c","acr:4.15.1-a","acr:4.15.1-b","acr:X-10"]},{"id":"acr-fibrous-glass","title":"Fibrous glass: clean, resurface or replace","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":550,"intro":"Fibrous glass liner and duct board are cleaned like any duct, but gently. When cleaning is not enough, you may resurface if the surface will bond, and replace it if it will not. Cleaned non-porous surfaces can be restored too.","mnemonic":"Clean, then resurface, then replace. Cleaning is SHALL and gentle, with no abrasions, breaks, or tears; resurfacing is a MAY, and a surface that will not bond is not resurfaced; replacement is only recommended, once cleaning and resurfacing cannot restore the material.","units":["acr:4.17-a","acr:4.17.1-a","acr:4.18-a","acr:4.18-b","acr:4.18.1-a","acr:4.18.1-b","acr:4.18.2-a","acr:4.18.2-b","acr:4.18.2-c","acr:4.19-a","acr:4.19-b","acr:4.21-a"]},{"id":"acr-insulation-replacement","title":"Replacing insulation after microbial growth","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":560,"intro":"When porous material has Condition 3 growth, removal and replacement are recommended; the metal must then be mechanically cleaned, and on microbial jobs the base must reach Condition 1 first. Replacements must meet or exceed the original spec and be capable of passing verification.","mnemonic":"Out at 3, back in at 1: Condition 3 porous material should come out (recommended); the base must reach Condition 1 before new insulation goes in (required).","units":["acr:4.16-a","acr:4.16-b","acr:4.20-a","acr:4.20-b","acr:4.20.1-a","acr:X-11","acr:4.20.2-a","acr:4.20.2-b","acr:4.20.3-a","acr:4.20.4-a","acr:4.20.5-a","acr:4.20.5-b"]},{"id":"acr-damage-repair","title":"Water, fire and smoke damage, and pre-existing damage","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":570,"intro":"After a flood or a fire the job changes: water gets categorized under S500, fire-damaged parts are evaluated for friability and odor, and soot questions go to IESO/RIA 6001. Damage that was already there gets documented and reported.","mnemonic":"S500 for water, S520 for mold, 6001 for soot. Found old damage? Write it down, tell the client.","units":["acr:4.22-a","acr:4.22-b","acr:4.22-c","acr:4.22-d","acr:4.23-a","acr:4.23-b","acr:4.23.1-a","acr:4.23.2-a","acr:4.23.3-a","acr:4.23.4-a","acr:4.24-a","acr:4.24-b","acr:4.24.1-a","acr:4.24.1-b"]},{"id":"acr-verification-overview","title":"Verification: purpose, timing and the three methods","dom":"Inspection","order":600,"intro":"What verification proves (particulate only), when it happens, the three methods and which surfaces each can judge, and how a disputed result escalates. The 2025 renumbering and the wider Method 2 scope are heavily tested.","mnemonic":"LOOK, BRUSH, WEIGH: Method 1 you look, Method 2 you brush with a contact vacuum, Method 3 you weigh a filter. Only non-porous surfaces get weighed.","units":["acr:5.0-a","acr:5.0-b","acr:5.0-c","acr:5.0-d","acr:5.0-e","acr:5.0-f","acr:5.1-a","acr:5.1-b","acr:5-M1","acr:5-M2","acr:5-M3","acr:5.2-a","acr:5.2-b","acr:5.2-c","acr:5.2.1-a","acr:5.3-a","acr:5.3-c","acr:5.3-d","acr:5.3.3-a","acr:5.3.3-b","acr:5-M4","acr:5-M5"]},{"id":"acr-method-2","title":"Method 2: the Surface Comparison Test","dom":"Inspection","order":610,"intro":"The Method 2 kit and procedure: a HEPA contact vacuum, the brush and hose sizes, four passes, and the before-and-after visual comparison. Every number here is a SHALL and a drill favorite.","mnemonic":"4-80, 2.5 on 1.5: four passes, 80 in. of static lift, a 2.5 in. brush on a 1.5 in. hose (the brush is one inch bigger than the hose). Pass means no significant visible difference.","units":["acr:5.3-b","acr:5.3.1-a","acr:5.3.1-b","acr:5.3.1-c","acr:5.3.1-d","acr:5.3.1-e","acr:5.3.1-f","acr:5.3.1-g","acr:5.3.2-a","acr:5.3.2-b"]},{"id":"acr-method-3-kit","title":"Method 3: how the vacuum test works and its kit","dom":"Inspection","order":620,"intro":"How the NADCA Vacuum Test pulls particulate off a non-porous surface without touching it, and the exact kit it needs: pump, cassette, filters, calibrator and template. The Method 3 number set is the densest drill material in the Standard.","mnemonic":"TRIPLE 15: pump at least 15 L/min, calibrator accurate at 15 L/min, template 15 mil. The 37 mm filter hides the 3.7 cm maximum slot width.","units":["acr:5.4-a","acr:5.4-b","acr:5.4-c","acr:5.4-d","acr:5.4-e","acr:5.4-f","acr:5.4-g","acr:5.4-h","acr:5.4-i","acr:5.4-j","acr:5.4.1-a","acr:5.4.1-b","acr:5.4.1-c","acr:5.4.1-d","acr:5.4.1-e","acr:5.4.1-f","acr:5.4.1-g","acr:5.4.1-h","acr:5.4.1-i","acr:5.4.1.1-a","acr:5.4.1.1-b","acr:5.4.1.2-b","acr:5.4.1.2-c","acr:5.4.1.2-d","acr:5.4.1.2-e"]},{"id":"acr-method-3-field","title":"Method 3: taking the sample in the field","dom":"Inspection","order":630,"intro":"The Method 3 sampling protocol up to pump shutdown: preparing the surface and template, opening and connecting the cassette, setting the flow, and making the passes. Order, speed, pass count and the no-contact rules are tested.","mnemonic":"TAPE, FLAT, DRY, OFF, then PLUGS, OUTLET, 17: template taped flat on a dry surface with the unit off, cassette to the pump, 17 L/min.","units":["acr:5.4.2-a","acr:5.4.2-b","acr:5.4.2-c","acr:5.4.2-d","acr:5.4.2-e","acr:5.4.2-f","acr:5.4.2-g","acr:5.4.2-h","acr:X-4","acr:5.4.2-i","acr:5.4.2-j","acr:5.4.2-k","acr:5.4.2-l","acr:5.4.2-m","acr:X-5","acr:5.4.2-n","acr:5.4.2-o","acr:5.4.2-y"]},{"id":"acr-method-3-results","title":"Method 3 results, the pass limit and project documentation","dom":"Inspection","order":640,"intro":"What happens to the cassette after sampling, the scale and pass numbers, who should sample and analyze, and what the closing paperwork must hold. Several items here look mandatory but are only recommended.","mnemonic":"0.7 up, 0.75 down: the scale reads to 0.7 mg or finer, and net debris must be 0.75 mg/100 cm2 or less to pass.","units":["acr:5.4.2-p","acr:5.4.2-q","acr:5.4.2-r","acr:X-6","acr:5.4.2-s","acr:5.4.2-t","acr:5.5-a","acr:5.4.2-u","acr:5.4.2-v","acr:5.4.2-w","acr:5.4.2-x","acr:5.6-a","acr:5.6-b","acr:5.6.1-a","acr:5.6.2-a","acr:5.6.3-a","acr:5.6.3-b"]},{"id":"acr-substances","title":"The four substances, and porous vs non-porous","dom":"Inspection","order":700,"intro":"The definitions the whole Standard runs on: what cleaning removes, the near-identical substance terms, and which materials are porous. They decide what visibly clean means and which verification method can apply.","mnemonic":"ANY, LOOSE, BRUSHABLE: contaminant, debris, particulate. Porous is \"LBS of Wet Concrete\": liner, board, sound-attenuated duct, wood, concrete.","units":["acr:D-1","acr:D-11","acr:D-2","acr:D-3","acr:D-4","acr:D-5","acr:D-10","acr:D-6","acr:D-6a","acr:D-7","acr:D-8","acr:D-9","acr:D-12","acr:D-13","acr:D-14","acr:D-15","acr:D-16"]},{"id":"acr-microbial-conditions","title":"Microbial Conditions 1, 2 and 3","dom":"IAQ","order":710,"intro":"The S520 Condition 1, 2 and 3 definitions and what counts as microbial contamination. Questions test the small wording differences between the three levels.","mnemonic":"BASELINE, SPILLOVER, SOURCE. Condition 1 is the normal Baseline (traces allowed). Condition 2 is Spillover: settled spores or fragments that came from a 3. Condition 3 is the Source: actual growth, active or dormant, visible or hidden.","units":["acr:D-18","acr:D-19","acr:D-20","acr:D-20a","acr:D-21","acr:D-22","acr:D-23","acr:D-24","acr:D-25","acr:D-26"]},{"id":"acr-equipment-terms","title":"Equipment terms: HEPA, AFDs, scrubbers and collection devices","dom":"Inspection","order":720,"intro":"Equipment words: HEPA, air filtration devices, scrubbers, negative air machines, collection devices and agitation devices, plus two terms new in 2025. The scrubber versus negative air machine split and the HEPA numbers are common questions.","mnemonic":"HEPA is three nines and a seven at point three: 99.97% at 0.3 micron. The Scrubber Stays in the space; the negative air machine controls pressure.","units":["acr:D-27","acr:D-28","acr:D-29","acr:D-30","acr:D-31","acr:D-32","acr:D-33","acr:D-34","acr:D-35","acr:D-36","acr:D-37","acr:D-38","acr:D-39"]},{"id":"acr-duct-terms","title":"Duct, door and closure terms","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":730,"intro":"Duct, insulation, access door and closure terms. Door types and the fastener definitions feed the service-opening questions in Section 4.","mnemonic":"Flush goes IN, Surface sticks OUT, Sandwich has two slices (both sides of the wall), Spin is round. Mechanically fasten means Screws, Clamps, Locks or Straps, but a service panel gets only screws or rivets.","units":["acr:D-40","acr:D-41","acr:D-42","acr:D-43","acr:D-44","acr:D-45","acr:D-46","acr:D-47","acr:D-48","acr:D-49","acr:D-50","acr:D-51","acr:D-52","acr:D-53","acr:D-54","acr:D-55","acr:D-56","acr:D-57"]},{"id":"acr-ahu-terms","title":"Air-handling unit types and coils","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":740,"intro":"The air-handling unit family as ACR defines it, plus coils and total cooling. Know which unit types carry their own heating or cooling source.","units":["acr:D-70","acr:D-71","acr:D-72","acr:D-73","acr:D-74","acr:D-74a","acr:D-75","acr:D-76","acr:D-77","acr:D-78","acr:D-79","acr:D-80"],"mnemonic":"Units do what their names say. The odd one out is the Central-Station AHU: fans in a factory box, with no heating or cooling source of its own."},{"id":"acr-scope-roles","title":"Scope boundaries, roles and project terms","dom":"Standards","order":750,"intro":"Where the Standard stops (airside surfaces, the HVAC system, open air plenums) and the defined roles and project terms: ASCS, IEP, inspection versus assessment, restoration and chain of custody.","mnemonic":"Walk the air from entry to discharge: every part it passes through inside the system is in; a non-ducted open air plenum is out. Chain of custody: Some Couriers Carefully Transfer All Deliveries (Seizure, Custody, Control, Transfer, Analysis, Disposition). Inspection gathers, assessment evaluates.","units":["acr:D-58","acr:D-59","acr:D-60","acr:D-61","acr:D-62","acr:D-63","acr:D-64","acr:D-65","acr:D-66","acr:D-67","acr:D-68","acr:D-69","acr:D-81","acr:D-82","acr:D-83","acr:D-84","acr:D-85","acr:D-86","acr:D-87","acr:D-88","acr:D-89","acr:D-90"]},{"id":"acr-controls-terms","title":"Engineering controls and pressure terms","dom":"Standards","order":760,"intro":"Engineering controls, containment and pressure terms. The Standard defines negative pressure by what it must do, never by a number, and the exam leans on that.","mnemonic":"Negative pressure has a job, not a number: keep debris out of occupied space and inside the contained area.","units":["acr:D-91","acr:D-92","acr:D-93","acr:D-94","acr:D-95","acr:D-96","acr:D-97","acr:D-98","acr:D-99","acr:D-100"]},{"id":"acr-process-terms","title":"Process, access and obligation-word terms","dom":"Standards","order":770,"intro":"Process, material and access terms, the obligation-word definitions, and two 2025 leftovers: the porous-only Method 2 definition and the deleted AHJ term.","mnemonic":"\"Mother, MAY I?\" asks permission. \"Yes, I CAN\" states ability. RECOMMEND advises. Only SHALL makes a requirement.","units":["acr:D-101","acr:D-102","acr:D-103","acr:D-104","acr:D-105","acr:D-106","acr:D-107","acr:D-109","acr:D-110","acr:D-111","acr:D-112","acr:D-113","acr:D-114","acr:D-115","acr:D-116","acr:D-117","acr:D-118","acr:D-119","acr:D-108"]},{"id":"acr-references","title":"Referenced documents: which standard covers what","dom":"Standards","order":780,"intro":"Which referenced standard covers what. Exam options often swap S500, S520 and S590, or UL 181 and UL 181A/B.","mnemonic":"Follow the damage: S500 water gets in, S520 mold grows, S590 assesses the HVAC afterward. Plain UL 181 is for surface treatments; UL 181 with the A/B letters is for closures, the tapes and mastics that seal service openings.","units":["acr:R-1","acr:R-2","acr:R-3","acr:R-4","acr:R-7","acr:R-8","acr:R-10","acr:R-12","acr:R-13"]},{"id":"cvi-ashrae","title":"ASHRAE standards and the 62.1 equipment rules","dom":"Standards","order":1000,"intro":"Which ASHRAE standard answers which question, plus the ASHRAE 62.1 filter, drain pan, maintenance and construction rules an inspector cites in findings. CVI distractors are almost always a sibling standard.","mnemonic":"Standard numbers: 55 feels (comfort), 62.1 breathes (62.2 at home), 52.2 grades filters, 170 heals, 180 maintains, 188 is water, 241 is germs in the air. Table 8-1 clock: towers 1 month, filters 3, louvers 6, pans and coils 12.","units":["cvi:ashrae-621-scope","cvi:ashrae-55","cvi:ashrae-901","cvi:ashrae-522","cvi:ashrae-170","cvi:ashrae-188","cvi:ashrae-241","cvi:ashrae-180-role","cvi:621-merv6-old","cvi:621-merv11-pm25","cvi:621-pan-seal","cvi:621-t81-filters","cvi:621-t81-tower-louvers","cvi:621-t81-pans","cvi:621-no-filters"]},{"id":"cvi-dampers","title":"Fire and smoke dampers","dom":"Standards","order":1010,"intro":"Test intervals, listings, ratings, labels and the test method for fire, smoke and combination dampers. Damper compliance is a named CVI topic and the numbers are drilled hard.","mnemonic":"1, then 4, hospitals 6 (NFPA 80 fire, NFPA 105 smoke, combination = both). UL 555 fire, S for Smoke, C for Ceiling, 33 is the link. Rating: under 3 hours gets 1½, 3 hours gets 3.","units":["cvi:fd-interval","cvi:damper-hospital","cvi:sd-interval","cvi:damper-combination","cvi:damper-code-chain","cvi:damper-fusible-test","cvi:damper-command-test","cvi:ul-555-family","cvi:damper-rating","cvi:damper-dynamic","cvi:damper-label"]},{"id":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","title":"Contaminant types and IAQ terms","dom":"IAQ","order":1020,"intro":"The contaminant classes an ASCS must recognize and the IAQ vocabulary that shows up in benefit questions: SBS versus BRI, respirable particulate, VOCs, soot and biological contaminants.","mnemonic":"SBS Stops when you leave; BRI Brings a diagnosis. Biological means microbes and critters: bacteria, viruses, mold, animal and insect byproducts. Drywall dust, fiberglass and soot are non-biological particulate; VOCs are gases.","units":["ascs:sbs-vs-bri","ascs:soot","ascs:biological-vs-particulate","ascs:respirable-particulate","ascs:voc-sources","ascs:infestation-contaminates","ascs:construction-particulate","ascs:plenum-infiltration"]},{"id":"cvi-nfpa-codes","title":"NFPA scope, duct detectors, kitchen exhaust and the I-codes","dom":"Standards","order":1020,"intro":"Which NFPA document and which I-code governs a system, where duct smoke detectors go, kitchen exhaust inspection frequency, and the flex connector limit. Scope questions reward knowing the one number that splits two documents.","mnemonic":"B for the Bungalow: 90B is dwellings and 25,000 cu ft or less. Detectors: 2,000 cfm supply, 15,000 cfm multi-story return. NFPA 96: fuel monthly, heavy quarterly, normal twice, light once. IKECA: I inspects, C cleans, M maintains.","units":["cvi:nfpa-90b-scope","cvi:nfpa-90a-scope","cvi:nfpa-90a-supply-det","cvi:nfpa-90a-return-det","cvi:imc-return-det","cvi:irc-scope","cvi:imc-irc-exception","cvi:flex-connector"]},{"id":"cvi-duct-listings","title":"Duct construction documents and material listings","dom":"Standards","order":1030,"intro":"SMACNA documents, seal classes, liner and duct listings, closure tapes and fire test names. These show up as submittal and field-finding questions where the wrong listing is the trap.","mnemonic":"The listing follows the duct: 181A closures for rigid board, 181B for bendy flex, and cloth duct tape voids the rating.","units":["cvi:smacna-016","cvi:smacna-008","cvi:astm-c1071","cvi:ul-181","cvi:class-a-450","cvi:ul-900"]},{"id":"cvi-restoration-epa","title":"Restoration standards and EPA duct guidance","dom":"Standards","order":1040,"intro":"IICRC water categories and classes, what S590 assessment produces, and what EPA's duct cleaning and building guides actually say. Inspectors get tested on quoting EPA accurately and on picking the right IICRC document.","mnemonic":"Follow the loss: S500 is the Soaked system (water), S520 the Spores that follow (mold), S590 the Survey of the HVAC after either, 6001 the Soot test. EPA duct guide: 3 reasons (mold, vermin, clogged), no proven health benefit, no biocide on fiberglass.","units":["cvi:s500-categories","cvi:s500-time","cvi:s590-purpose","cvi:s590-output","cvi:epa-duct-triggers","cvi:epa-duct-health","cvi:epa-no-biocide-liner","cvi:epa-baq-guide"]},{"id":"ascs-loto-electrical","title":"Lockout/tagout and job-site electrical safety","dom":"Risk Management","order":1100,"intro":"OSHA's energy control rule (who locks, in what order, how to verify, who removes) and the electrical rules for cords and portable tools on a cleaning job. These are regulation facts with fixed answers.","mnemonic":"People Should Isolate, Lock, Release, Verify: Prepare, Shut down, Isolate, Lock or tag, Release stored energy, Verify with a start attempt. The hand that hung the lock takes it off. 1-4-7, lock it or go to heaven.","units":["ascs:osha-governs","ascs:loto-sequence","ascs:loto-authorized","ascs:loto-affected","ascs:loto-verify","ascs:loto-removal","ascs:osha-1910-147","ascs:gfci-tools","ascs:double-insulated","ascs:cord-handling","ascs:damaged-cord","ascs:extension-cord-type"]},{"id":"cvi-iaq-sources","title":"Where HVAC pollutants come from","dom":"IAQ","order":1100,"intro":"Pollutants the HVAC system makes versus pollutants it only carries, and the specific sources an inspector should name. The test asks whether cleaning or filtering would even help.","units":["cvi:hvac-made-vs-carried","cvi:distributed-fix","cvi:radon-action","cvi:radon-returns","cvi:legionella-growth","cvi:humidifier-legionella","cvi:ozone-air-cleaners","cvi:co-odorless","cvi:combustion-first","cvi:trap-seal","cvi:liner-past-filters","cvi:mite-reservoirs","cvi:mite-rh","cvi:hidden-mold","cvi:formaldehyde","cvi:amplification-site","cvi:ultrasonic-dust","cvi:pesticides"],"mnemonic":"Ask where it was born: growth is made in the system; radon, formaldehyde, pesticides and vehicle exhaust are only carried, so fix the source, not the ducts."},{"id":"ascs-confined-spaces","title":"Confined spaces and permit entry","dom":"Risk Management","order":1110,"intro":"When an air handler or plenum is a confined space, what makes it permit-required, the atmospheric limits, the test order, and what the attendant does. Walk-in AHUs make this core ASCS safety content.","mnemonic":"Spaces before Switches: .146 is Spaces, .147 is Switches. Oxygen window 19.5 to 23.5 (normal air 20.9 sits inside it); flammables trip at one tenth (10 percent) of the LFL. Test O, C, T: Oxygen, Combustibles, Toxics.","units":["ascs:confined-space-def","ascs:confined-not-permit","ascs:permit-space-triggers","ascs:entrapment-config","ascs:o2-deficient","ascs:o2-enriched","ascs:flammable-10-lfl","ascs:test-order","ascs:attendant-duty","ascs:attendant-rescue","ascs:permit-flow","ascs:osha-1910-146"]},{"id":"cvi-iaq-pathways","title":"How pollutants travel: intakes, pressure and pathways","dom":"IAQ","order":1110,"intro":"ASHRAE 62.1 intake separation distances and the pressure-driven pathways (stack effect, re-entrainment, short-circuiting, depressurization) that move contaminants. Expect a distance or a pressure sign as the answer.","mnemonic":"Intake distances: 5 street, 10 toilet exhaust or low plumbing vent, 15 garage door, Class 3 or tower basin, 25 truck dock or tower exhaust, 30 Class 4. Classes 2, 3, 4 = 10, 15, 30.","units":["cvi:intake-plumbing-vent","cvi:intake-truck","cvi:intake-garage-entry","cvi:intake-driveway","cvi:intake-cooling-tower","cvi:intake-exhaust-classes","cvi:air-class-examples","cvi:four-elements","cvi:pressure-drives","cvi:stack-effect","cvi:hot-humid-negative","cvi:reentrainment","cvi:short-circuiting","cvi:garage-ducts","cvi:crawlspace-return","cvi:open-return-dust","cvi:makeup-air-400","cvi:depressurization-flue","cvi:oa-exceeds-exhaust","cvi:exhaust-odor-rooms"]},{"id":"ascs-respirators-sds","title":"Respirators and Safety Data Sheets","dom":"Risk Management","order":1120,"intro":"When OSHA requires respirators, how they are selected, the program steps before a tight-fitting respirator is worn, and what an SDS gives the crew. Distractors swap the order and the intervals.","mnemonic":"Engineering first, respirator second. Doctor, fit, seal: medical evaluation, then fit test (yearly), then user seal check (every donning). APF ladder for air-purifying masks: quarter 5, half 10, full 50. 1-3-4, breathe some more; 1200 is HazCom, 16 SDS sections.","units":["ascs:engineering-first","ascs:resp-hazard-selection","ascs:resp-program","ascs:resp-order","ascs:facial-hair","ascs:apf-half-mask","ascs:sds-content","ascs:osha-1910-134","ascs:osha-1910-1200"]},{"id":"cvi-iaq-comfort","title":"Humidity, CO2, comfort and occupant complaints","dom":"IAQ","order":1120,"intro":"Humidity targets, sick building syndrome versus building-related illness, what CO2 does and does not tell you, and what to do with a contaminated system. These are the IAQ judgment calls an inspector writes into a report.","mnemonic":"RH: 30 to 50 ideal, never over 60, mites need over 50. CO2 tracks people, not pollution: no 62.1 limit, 700 above outdoors was old guidance, 5,000 is the OSHA PEL.","units":["cvi:rh-band","cvi:rh-low","cvi:sbs","cvi:bri","cvi:vrp","cvi:co2-not-iaq","cvi:co2-no-limit","cvi:co2-700-5000","cvi:filter-bypass","cvi:flat-pan-reset","cvi:epa-wet-insulation","cvi:epa-hvac-off","cvi:visible-no-sampling","cvi:no-mold-limit"]},{"id":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","title":"Asbestos, lead, mold guidance and plenum risk","dom":"Risk Management","order":1130,"intro":"What to do when you meet suspect asbestos or old paint, the dates that trigger OSHA and EPA presumptions, the mold guidance documents on the reference list, and NADCA's risk management guidance for open air plenums.","mnemonic":"Lead 1978, asbestos 1980: lead comes first, asbestos two years later. Presume, stop, notify: you never sample it, bag it or wet it. RRP minor repair: 6 inside per room, 20 outside.","units":["ascs:risk-starts-hazards","ascs:exposure-effects","ascs:suspect-acm-action","ascs:acm-licensed-only","ascs:osha-asbestos-lead-map","ascs:plenum-hazmat","ascs:lead-presume","ascs:rrp-scope","ascs:osha-mold-guide","ascs:epa-mold-guide","ascs:plenum-rmp","ascs:rmp-contents","ascs:plenum-training"]},{"id":"ascs-std-fire-duct","title":"Fire, UL and duct construction documents","dom":"Standards","order":1150,"intro":"Which NFPA, UL, ASTM, SMACNA and NAIMA document governs fire safety, closures, surface burning and duct construction. Questions give a title and ask for the designator, or the reverse.","mnemonic":"90A is Air-conditioning in big buildings, 90B is Bungalows, 96 is cooking. 181A is rigid (Angular), 181B is flexible (Bendy). E84, UL 723 and NFPA 255 are one burn test.","units":["ascs:nfpa-90a-life-safety","ascs:nfpa-90b","ascs:nfpa-90a-90b-split","ascs:surface-burning-tests","ascs:ul-181","ascs:ul-181a-181b","ascs:smacna-006","ascs:smacna-cleanliness-levels","ascs:naima-ah116","ascs:astm-c1071","ascs:nfpa-org","ascs:smacna-org"]},{"id":"ascs-std-reference-docs","title":"Ventilation, insulation and reference documents","dom":"Standards","order":1160,"intro":"The ASHRAE ventilation standards, the NAIMA and NADCA documents, and the other references behind ACR, plus the acronyms the candidate guide expects you to know.","mnemonic":"ASHRAE by number: 62.1 is everyone else (commercial), 62.2 is home (residential), 170 is the hospital, 180 keeps it running (maintenance). NAIMA: AH116 builds, AH122 cleans, AH110 breathes (IEQ). NADCA: 03 pulls (vacuum testing), 05 opens (service openings).","units":["ascs:ashrae-62-1","ascs:ashrae-62-2","ascs:ashrae-170","ascs:naima-ah122","ascs:naima-ah110","ascs:nadca-std-05","ascs:nadca-std-03","ascs:nadca-position-paper","ascs:nmam-5th","ascs:nyc-mold-guidelines","ascs:prelim-determination-s520","ascs:acca-org","ascs:iep-acronym","ascs:mua-acronym"]},{"id":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","title":"What inspection findings point to","dom":"Inspection","order":1200,"intro":"Field clues an inspector reads and what each one means: black fallout, filter bypass, leaking zone dampers, wet drain pans, growth on liner and suspect asbestos. Scenario questions test the root cause and the next step.","units":["ascs:p-mechanical-plans-walkthrough","ascs:p-black-fallout-liner","ascs:p-filter-bypass","ascs:p-multizone-damper-leak","ascs:p-drain-pan-root-cause","ascs:p-liner-growth-remediation","ascs:p-suspect-acm-scope"],"mnemonic":"Clues point to causes. Only two findings change the job: growth on porous liner makes it remediation, and suspect asbestos stops the work."},{"id":"cvi-insp-before","title":"Before and during the inspection: interview, access and instruments","dom":"Inspection","order":1200,"intro":"Reading complaint patterns in the pre-inspection interview, special site cautions, getting inside the system without damage, and which instrument measures what. About 40% of the CVI exam is inspection practice.","mnemonic":"Match the tool to the quantity: micromanometer = pressure, flow hood = cfm (anemometer only rough), chemical smoke = direction, thermo-hygrometer = air temp and RH, moisture meter = the material, CO2 meter = ventilation per person.","units":["cvi:timing-start","cvi:timing-other","cvi:spatial-local","cvi:spatial-wide","cvi:icra","cvi:detector-coordination","cvi:oap-exhausted-rooms","cvi:oap-asbestos","cvi:oap-raised-floor","cvi:borescope-neck","cvi:micromanometer","cvi:flow-hood","cvi:min-kit","cvi:moisture-meter","cvi:co2-meter","cvi:particle-counter"]},{"id":"cvi-insp-readings","title":"Reading the numbers: pressure, temperature and CO2","dom":"Inspection","order":1210,"intro":"How to turn field readings into defensible findings: pressure drops, mixing temperatures, CO2 estimates, filter logs and containment logs. The traps are comparisons made under changed conditions.","units":["cvi:dp-square","cvi:component-compare","cvi:oaf-formula","cvi:return-leak-mix","cvi:target-split","cvi:face-velocity","cvi:vav-failed","cvi:ach-cfm","cvi:manometer-lapse","cvi:npe-002","cvi:niosh-0500-0600"],"mnemonic":"Change nothing but what you credit: same airflow, same fan speed, same filters, part by part, and CO2 only after it levels off."},{"id":"cvi-reports-liability","title":"Reports, claims, liability and ethics","dom":"Risk Management","order":1300,"intro":"What an inspection report must show, what the inspector may and may not claim, and the insurance, confidentiality and conflict-of-interest rules behind it. The CVI carries liability for every word in the report.","mnemonic":"E&O for Errors in advice, GL for Getting hurt or property damaged. Claims-made needs the policy alive twice: at the event and at the claim.","units":["cvi:photo-keying","cvi:not-inspected","cvi:no-health-causation","cvi:no-certify-claims","cvi:eo-vs-gl","cvi:claims-made","cvi:confidentiality","cvi:coi-necessary","cvi:other-trades","cvi:damper-unsafe","cvi:damper-records"]},{"id":"cvi-osha-access","title":"OSHA: confined spaces, falls, ladders and power lines","dom":"Risk Management","order":1310,"intro":"The OSHA numbers for getting to the equipment safely: permit space entry, fall protection triggers, low-slope roofs, ladders and overhead lines.","mnemonic":"4 in the plant, 6 on the site, 19 inches needs a ladder. Roof edge: under 6 conventional, 6 to 15 designated area, 15 plus a work rule. Ladder: 3 feet past the landing, 1 out for every 4 up. Power lines: 10 feet.","units":["cvi:cs-entry","cvi:fall-gi-4","cvi:fall-cons-6","cvi:break-19","cvi:fixed-ladder-24","cvi:roof-under-6","cvi:roof-6-15","cvi:roof-15","cvi:prework-exempt","cvi:ladder-36","cvi:ladder-fixed","cvi:ladder-4to1","cvi:powerline-10","cvi:ladder-nonconductive"]},{"id":"cvi-osha-health","title":"OSHA: lockout, respirators, HazCom, heat and noise","dom":"Risk Management","order":1320,"intro":"Energy control, respirator fit testing, SDS sections, heat illness first aid and noise limits that apply to inspectors and their crews.","mnemonic":"Lock the disconnect, not the thermostat. Fit test: first use, new facepiece, every year. SDS 8 is the gear section. Noise: 85 starts the program, 90 starts the controls.","units":["cvi:loto-inspecting","cvi:loto-control-circuit","cvi:fit-test","cvi:seal-check","cvi:sds-8","cvi:sds-2-4-7","cvi:heat-stroke","cvi:noise-85","cvi:noise-90"]},{"id":"cvi-hazmat-bio","title":"Asbestos, lead and biological hazards","dom":"Risk Management","order":1330,"intro":"Presumed asbestos, owner surveys, NESHAP notice, lead RRP limits, and the droppings and water hazards found in HVAC systems. Inspectors must spot these before anything is cut or disturbed.","mnemonic":"PACM: TSI and surfacing, built by 1980. RRP: 6 in, 20 out, pre-1978 housing. Birds and bats = histoplasmosis (wet it), rodents = hantavirus (soak it 5 minutes), warm standing water = Legionella.","units":["cvi:owner-acm-survey","cvi:pacm","cvi:tsi-breeching","cvi:neshap-notice","cvi:neshap-thresholds","cvi:rrp-scope","cvi:rrp-minor","cvi:rrp-demolition","cvi:rrp-30-day","cvi:rrp-firm","cvi:histo-source","cvi:histo-wet","cvi:hanta-source","cvi:rodent-cleanup","cvi:rodent-pros"]},{"id":"nim-a-inspection-basics","title":"What an HVAC inspection is","dom":"Inspection","order":2000,"intro":"Covers what the manual means by an HVAC inspection, how the process runs from first contact to report, and what the report must deliver.","mnemonic":"Dirt, Obstructions, Moisture, Microbes: 'DOMM' is the cleanliness definition. Process: Contact, Walk, Scope, Plan, Inspect, Report.","units":["nim:a-first-step","nim:a-cleanliness-def","nim:a-written-plan","nim:a-scope-components","nim:a-samples-optional","nim:a-report-recs"]},{"id":"nim-a-why-and-when","title":"Why and how often to inspect","dom":"Inspection","order":2010,"intro":"Covers why clients order inspections and how often NADCA and NFPA 90A say they should happen.","mnemonic":"NADCA says once a year, NFPA says four times a year: 'N-1, N-4'. Reasons to inspect are the 3 Ds: Diligence, Documentation (sale), and Did the cleaning work.","units":["nim:a-acr-routine","nim:a-due-diligence","nim:a-documentation-sale","nim:a-annual","nim:a-nfpa90a-quarterly","nim:a-table-1-2"]},{"id":"nim-a-inspector-conduct","title":"Inspector qualifications and ethics","dom":"Risk Management","order":2020,"intro":"Covers who is qualified to inspect, what must be disclosed, and when to bring in credentialed specialists.","mnemonic":"Disclose Before you Begin. Balancers get NEBB or AABC (both have a B for Balance); Hygienists get AIHA (the H in Hygiene).","units":["nim:a-qual-knowledge","nim:a-disclose-cleaning","nim:a-disclose-interest","nim:a-objective","nim:a-refer-experts","nim:a-specialist-creds"]},{"id":"nim-a-insurance-licensing","title":"Insurance, licensing, and references","dom":"Risk Management","order":2030,"intro":"Covers E&O insurance, licensing duties, and citing standards so the report stands up to scrutiny.","mnemonic":"E&O covers what you Wrote, not what you Wrecked: report errors yes, auto, property, general liability, and workers comp no. Licensed tasks: Cut, Panel, Fan, Coil.","units":["nim:a-report-product","nim:a-eo-def","nim:a-eo-exclusions","nim:a-licensing-tasks","nim:a-licensing-responsibility","nim:a-cite-standards"]},{"id":"nim-a-ppe-respirators","title":"PPE and respirators for inspectors","dom":"Risk Management","order":2040,"intro":"Covers OSHA duties and the PPE and respirator rules an inspector must know.","mnemonic":"Respirators need a Program, a Physician, a Proper fit, and NIOSH plus MSHA: the 'N-M' stamp. Qualitative uses your senses; quantitative uses a meter.","units":["nim:a-osha-act","nim:a-foot","nim:a-eye-reqs","nim:a-resp-program","nim:a-resp-approval","nim:a-fit-test-types","nim:a-resp-medical"]},{"id":"nim-a-hazcom-loto-space","title":"Detectors, HazCom, lockout, and confined space","dom":"Risk Management","order":2050,"intro":"Covers detector permits, HazCom, lockout/tagout, and permit-required confined spaces as they apply to an inspector.","mnemonic":"HazCom is 'SLTW': SDS, Labels, Training, Written program. Physical hazards go FER: Flammable, Explosive, Reactive. Lock removal: Call, Contact, Supervisor, Safety, Verify, Remove.","units":["nim:a-detector-permits","nim:a-hazcom-elements","nim:a-physical-vs-health","nim:a-tagout-when","nim:a-lock-removal","nim:a-prcs"]},{"id":"nim-b-cav-vav","title":"Constant volume, VAV, and single zone systems","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":2100,"intro":"How single zone, CAV, and VAV systems control temperature and outdoor air, and why VAV can under-ventilate at part load. System types are a heavy exam topic.","mnemonic":"CAV changes the Temperature, VAV changes the Volume. VAV ventilation rule: count Cfm, not Percent (a percent of less is less). Fan on ON, not AUTO, or the outdoor air naps.","units":["nim:b-single-zone","nim:b-cav-def","nim:b-cav-uniform","nim:b-cav-vs-vav","nim:b-economizer","nim:b-fan-on","nim:b-vav-def","nim:b-vav-zone-size","nim:b-vav-oa-quantity","nim:b-vav-oa-partload","nim:b-vav-sat-low"]},{"id":"nim-b-multizone-dualduct","title":"Multizone and dual-duct systems","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":2110,"intro":"Two older systems that make hot and cold air at once and blend them: where they mix, why they waste energy, and what the inspector watches for.","mnemonic":"Multizone Mixes in the Mechanical room (100°F and 55°F); Dual duct mixes Down at the zone box, like a shower. Dual duct woes: Leaky dampers, Humidity swings, High velocity before the box (LHH).","units":["nim:b-mz-def","nim:b-mz-temps","nim:b-mz-energy","nim:b-mz-dampers","nim:b-dd-def","nim:b-dd-cav-vav","nim:b-dd-energy","nim:b-dd-highvel","nim:b-dd-leak","nim:b-dd-humidity"]},{"id":"nim-b-heatpump-fancoil","title":"Heat pumps and fan coil units","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":2120,"intro":"Heat pump types, the common water-source loop, frequent field problems, and how fan coils work and fail when maintenance skips them.","mnemonic":"Heat pump works down to about 50°F; below that the strips help. ARI five: HP, GWHP, PTHP, WSHP, GSHP. Tower cools the loop in summer, Boiler heats it in winter (T-S, B-W).","units":["nim:b-hp-def","nim:b-hp-50f","nim:b-hp-aux","nim:b-hp-ari","nim:b-wshp","nim:b-hp-problems","nim:b-hp-pan","nim:b-fcu-def","nim:b-fcu-use","nim:b-fcu-neglect"]},{"id":"nim-b-plenum-exhaust","title":"Ceiling plenums, local exhaust, and pressurization","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":2130,"intro":"What to look for in ceiling plenums, how exhausted rooms must be isolated and kept negative, and why buildings run slightly positive.","mnemonic":"Exhaust rooms: Negative, Isolated, Intact to the outdoors (NII). Building as a whole: slightly positive or neutral, and only if it is tight.","units":["nim:b-plenum-def","nim:b-plenum-materials","nim:b-plenum-moisture","nim:b-exhaust-recommended","nim:b-exhaust-isolate","nim:b-exhaust-duct","nim:b-pressurization"]},{"id":"nim-c-coils-humidity","title":"Coils, humidity, and condensate drains","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":2200,"intro":"How coils dehumidify and reheat, where the humidity set points sit, what humidifiers need so they do not wet the duct, and how pans and traps must drain. The humidity numbers are easy exam targets.","mnemonic":"Humidity ladder, low to high: 45 in the room, 65 for the office ceiling, 70 in the duct; chilled water under 45°F. Traps are Wet, Low, Primed: keep the seal wet, connect to the lowest fitting, prime it for heating season.","units":["nim:c-coil-45f","nim:c-reheat-coil","nim:c-leaky-valve","nim:c-steam-potable","nim:c-space-humidistat","nim:c-highlimit-70","nim:c-ashrae-rh65","nim:c-mist-eliminator","nim:c-ptrap-seal","nim:c-trap-negative","nim:c-lowest-fitting","nim:c-trap-primer","nim:c-heat-stack-trap"]},{"id":"nim-c-filters","title":"Filters and filter ratings","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":2210,"intro":"Filter efficiency bands, how loading changes performance, HEPA facts, and the ASHRAE 52.1/52.2 rating history in Table 2-1.","mnemonic":"Dust spot bands step up 'teens, thirties to sixties, eighties to nineties': low 10-20, medium 30-60, high 85-95. MERV groups by four: 1-4 coarse, 5-8 pleated, 9-12 better, 13-16 hospital. HEPA: 2 inches, 0.01 micron.","units":["nim:c-filter-low","nim:c-filter-bands","nim:c-filter-loading","nim:c-filter-blowout","nim:c-filter-medium-best","nim:c-hepa","nim:c-merv17-dropped","nim:c-std521","nim:c-merv-table"]},{"id":"nim-c-dampers-fire","title":"Dampers and fire dampers","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":2220,"intro":"The four common damper types, outdoor air intake checks, freezestats, and fire damper location, triggers, and design types.","mnemonic":"Opposed keeps the Original direction. Fire dampers go A-B-C: A in the Air, B Beyond the air, C with a Collar for high pressure. A and B stay under 3 inches.","units":["nim:c-damper-types","nim:c-bird-screen","nim:c-freezestat","nim:c-fd-location","nim:c-fd-fuselink","nim:c-fd-types","nim:c-fd-pressure"]},{"id":"nim-c-terminals","title":"Fans, packaged units, and air devices","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":2230,"intro":"Fan types, what packaged and split units contain and fail to provide, induction boxes, grilles versus registers versus diffusers, throw and dumping, and silencer pressure drops.","mnemonic":"Forward is Frequent, Backward will not overload, Axial is airy and quiet. Split: the hot side lives outside. Register = grille + damper. Throw ends at 50 fpm. Pods cost 0.3 to 0.5 inch.","units":["nim:c-fan-types","nim:c-packaged-oa","nim:c-split","nim:c-induction","nim:c-grille-register","nim:c-throw","nim:c-dump","nim:c-silencer"]},{"id":"nim-c-duct-types","title":"Duct materials and types","dom":"HVAC Systems","order":2240,"intro":"Porous versus non-porous duct materials, special ducts like concrete, PVC, CPVC, and Transite, and what the inspector looks for in each.","mnemonic":"Plastic temps: PVC 140, CPVC 200, the C adds 60. Transite = asbestos, hands off. Double wall is porous unless the inside is sealed.","units":["nim:c-dw-porous","nim:c-porous-area","nim:c-metal","nim:c-pvc-cpvc","nim:c-concrete","nim:c-flex-friction","nim:c-transite"]},{"id":"nim-d-iaq-role","title":"The inspector's role and the causes of indoor pollutants","dom":"IAQ","order":2300,"intro":"What NADCA expects an inspector to do, and not do, when IAQ problems come up, plus the manual's pollutant causes and six pollutant types. Exam items test role limits, category recall, and NADCA's view of HVAC as a pathway.","mnemonic":"Role: 10 to 100, no hunch, pipe not source, refer the rest. Pollutant types, small to living: Particulate, Gas, Aerosol, Bioaerosol, Biological, Fungi ('Pretty Good Air Breeds Bugs and Fungi').","units":["nim:d-indoor-10-100","nim:d-no-hypothesis","nim:d-pathway-not-source","nim:d-stay-in-lane","nim:d-co2-occupancy","nim:d-air-balance","nim:d-filtration-equipment","nim:d-new-construction","nim:d-outside-contaminants","nim:d-pollutant-types"]},{"id":"nim-d-pathways","title":"How air and pollutants move: pressure and pathways","dom":"IAQ","order":2310,"intro":"The HVAC airstreams that act as pollutant pathways and the driving forces that set pressure in buildings. The core rule is that air moves from high to low pressure.","mnemonic":"Driving forces are WSFFE: Wind, Stack, Fans, Flues/exhaust, Elevators. Stack in winter: 'hot heads, cold feet' means positive up high, negative down low.","units":["nim:d-supply-pathway","nim:d-exhaust-pathway","ibeam:d-stack-pressures","ibeam:d-wind","ibeam:d-fan-pressure","ibeam:d-flow-direction","ibeam:d-negative-building","ibeam:d-garage-negative"]},{"id":"nim-d-comfort","title":"Thermal comfort, humidity and occupant health","dom":"IAQ","order":2320,"intro":"How comfort fits inside IAQ, the Standard 55 reference, I-BEAM's humidity limits, and the SBS, BRI and MCS contrast that exams like to test.","mnemonic":"55 keeps you comfy, 62 keeps you breathing. Humidity: 60 stops mold, 50 stops mites, day and night. SBS = Symptoms But no Source; BRI = Bug, Real diagnosis, Identified cause.","units":["nim:d-comfort-in-iaq","nim:d-std55","nim:d-comfort-factors","nim:d-supply-leak-comfort","ibeam:d-warm-emissions","ibeam:d-dry-complaint","ibeam:d-rh-always","ibeam:d-acute","ibeam:d-sbs-bri","ibeam:d-bri-examples","ibeam:d-mcs","ibeam:d-bio-sources"]},{"id":"nim-d-ventilation","title":"Ventilation, outdoor air and HVAC fundamentals","dom":"IAQ","order":2330,"intro":"Exhaust versus dilution ventilation, ventilation measurements, outdoor air arithmetic from I-BEAM's summary of older ASHRAE 62 editions, and CV versus VAV behavior.","mnemonic":"Tools by job: Smoke shows direction, Hood gives volume, Velocity gives speed, CO2 gives percent outdoor air. CV changes the Temperature, VAV changes the Volume; VAV saves 10 to 20, ducts leak under 3.","units":["ibeam:d-dilution","ibeam:d-measure-tools","ibeam:d-oa-formula","ibeam:d-intermittent","ibeam:d-vav-savings","ibeam:d-vav-partload","ibeam:d-duct-3pct","ibeam:d-filter-fan-off"]},{"id":"nim-e-client","title":"Client contact, interview, and walk-through","dom":"Inspection","order":2400,"intro":"How an inspection starts: the first call, the half-day site visit, interview questions, who gets the findings, and the walk-through. Exam items test the order and the inspector's duties at each step.","mnemonic":"Call, Meet, Contact, Walk: the phone Call starts notes, the half-day Meeting clarifies purpose, one Contact gets findings, the Walk compares drawings to what is really installed.","units":["nim:e-client-first-call","nim:e-site-visit-half-day","nim:e-no-recommendations","nim:e-deliverables-question","nim:e-report-recipient","nim:e-no-asbuilt","nim:e-walkthrough-discrepancy"]},{"id":"nim-e-purpose","title":"Purpose statement, scope, and disclaimer","dom":"Inspection","order":2410,"intro":"The three documents drafted before any inspection work: why (purpose), what (scope), and what not (disclaimer). Their contents, order, and approvals are heavily tested.","mnemonic":"Why, What, What Not: Purpose is Why in 1 to 5 sentences, Scope is What in detail, Disclaimer is What Not. Scope steps spell Plans, Systems, Where, Scope, Disclaimer: 'Please Send Where Scope Declines'.","units":["nim:e-purpose-length","nim:e-purpose-no-opinion","nim:e-purpose-specific","nim:e-purpose-approved","nim:e-scope-vs-purpose","nim:e-scope-steps","nim:e-scope-facility-desc","nim:e-scope-report-intro","nim:e-disclaimer-what","nim:e-disclaimer-negligence"]},{"id":"nim-e-plans","title":"Marking plans and deciding how much to inspect","dom":"Inspection","order":2420,"intro":"Marking up mechanical plans, the Table 5-4 minimums, where debris collects, and how many access points a system needs.","mnemonic":"Tens everywhere: 10% of branches, 10% of similar systems; plus Intake and whole AHU always. Access: straight system, 2 to 4 holes. Colors: Supply, Return, Exhaust each their own.","units":["nim:e-plans-copies","nim:e-plans-colors","nim:e-plans-attached-spaces","nim:e-plans-upstream","nim:e-plans-deposition","nim:e-client-specified","nim:e-plenum-always","nim:e-complaint-return","nim:e-supply-always","nim:e-branch-10","nim:e-branch-inspected-def","nim:e-routine-10","nim:e-problems-expand","nim:e-debris-areas","nim:e-wet-sections","nim:e-access-2-4"]},{"id":"nim-e-controls","title":"Engineering controls during inspection","dom":"Risk Management","order":2430,"intro":"How the inspector avoids spreading contamination: barriers, negative pressure, clean equipment, shutting the system off, and handling released debris.","mnemonic":"Barrier, Negative, HEPA, Off: build a barrier, pull negative, vacuum with HEPA, and turn the system off or block the supply side.","units":["nim:e-contain-barriers","nim:e-containment-extent","nim:e-equipment-hygiene","nim:e-duct-shutoff","nim:e-negair-not-vac","nim:e-service-cuttings"]},{"id":"nim-e-tools","title":"Inspection tools and access openings","dom":"Inspection","order":2440,"intro":"Choosing visualization tools with minimum impact, their access hole sizes and limits, photo documentation issues, and licensing for service openings.","mnemonic":"1, 4, 6: borescope 1 inch hole, push camera 4 inch hole, robot 6 x 6 inches. Short scope for cavities, long camera for long runs, robot until the vanes.","units":["nim:e-pushcam-focal","nim:e-borescope-cavity","nim:e-hole-sizes","nim:e-robot-limits","nim:e-borescope-cons","nim:e-photo-litigation","nim:e-license-openings"]},{"id":"nim-e-methods","title":"Inspection methods, documentation, and sampling","dom":"Inspection","order":2450,"intro":"Where to start, what to look for, the manual's case studies, checklists and field notes, and the rules for sampling and chain of custody.","mnemonic":"Start at the Intake, follow the air. Sample only when the client says so, and every sample gets a chain of custody.","units":["nim:e-start-oa-intake","nim:e-dust-patterns","nim:e-slight-dust-normal","nim:e-case2-visual","nim:e-cold-deck","nim:e-checklist-guideline","nim:e-field-notes-content","nim:e-sample-client-directed","nim:e-sample-insurers","nim:e-sample-record","nim:e-sample-interpretation","nim:e-chain-custody-always"]},{"id":"nim-f-report-build","title":"Building the inspection report","order":2500,"intro":"How to gather materials and structure the written report NADCA requires: summary, body, and recommendations. Exam items test what goes where and how findings are worded.","mnemonic":"S-B-R, like 'Short, Bare, Recommend': Summary is Short (1 to 4 paragraphs, facts, overview of recs), Body is Bare facts ranked by severity, Recommendations stand alone. Raw data goes to the Appendix.","dom":"Inspection","units":["nim:f-deliverable","nim:f-gather","nim:f-checklist-prep","nim:f-format","nim:f-three-parts","nim:f-scope-summary","nim:f-summary-length","nim:f-summary-facts","nim:f-summary-recs","nim:f-summary-readall","nim:f-body-factual","nim:f-body-order","nim:f-appendix"]},{"id":"nim-f-recommend","title":"Cleaning triggers and writing recommendations","order":2510,"intro":"The four ACR cleaning triggers the manual quotes, and the §7.2 rules for writing recommendations. These are NADCA positions that exam writers like to test directly.","mnemonic":"Triggers are D-M-P-P: Debris seen, Microbes seen or confirmed, Particulate puffing into the room, Performance restricted. Writing rules: one per finding, no severity words, send specialties to specialists.","dom":"Inspection","units":["nim:f-trig-debris","nim:f-trig-microbial","nim:f-trig-discharge","nim:f-trig-performance","nim:f-building-use","nim:f-one-per-finding","nim:f-no-severity","nim:f-specialist"]},{"id":"nim-f-present","title":"Presenting, following up, and routine inspection","order":2520,"intro":"How the report is delivered, when interim drafts help, what follow-up looks like, and why Standard 180 supports routine inspection.","mnemonic":"Verification is V-S-V: Visual, Surface comparison, Vacuum test. Drafts get a label so no one mistakes them for Final.","dom":"Inspection","units":["nim:f-in-person","nim:f-interim","nim:f-draft-label","nim:f-limitations","nim:f-verification","nim:f-referral","nim:f-std180"]},{"id":"nim-f-table-moisture","title":"Table 7-1: moisture, microbes, and leaks","order":2530,"intro":"Table 7-1 findings about water, humidity, and microbial growth, with the recommendation and cited document for each.","mnemonic":"Monthly look, yearly scrub for plenums and pans. NYC for Fungi, EPA for drying. Upstream of fan means sUcked (negative).","dom":"Inspection","units":["nim:f-microbial-signs","nim:f-nyc-doh","nim:f-debris-interval","nim:f-debris-cause","nim:f-carryover-where","nim:f-carryover-velocity","nim:f-rh-control","nim:f-pan-leak","nim:f-trap","nim:f-humidifier-boiler","nim:f-vav-leaks","nim:f-refrigerant"]},{"id":"nim-f-table-air","title":"Table 7-1: air, filters, and pressure","order":2540,"intro":"Table 7-1 findings about intakes, filters, ducts, diffusers, ventilation, and building pressure, including the numbers the table cites.","mnemonic":"25 feet from vents, 50 fpm on skin, slightly Positive building, no Wood in the system, 62 for ventilation.","dom":"Inspection","units":["nim:f-intake-25","nim:f-diffusers","nim:f-duct-dust-normal","nim:f-earmark","nim:f-spacer","nim:f-poor-filtration","nim:f-delamination","nim:f-vav-oa","nim:f-ashrae62","nim:f-pressure","nim:f-mech-room","nim:f-velocity-50"]},{"id":"ibeam-g-diagnosis","title":"Diagnosing IAQ complaints the I-BEAM way","dom":"IAQ","order":2600,"intro":"How I-BEAM works an IAQ complaint: rule out an emergency, then use what, where, when and who to point toward biological, chemical, outdoor air or thermal causes.","mnemonic":"E then 4 Ws: Emergency first, then What, Where, When, Who. Symptoms map: Cough and fever = Bugs; Mild and meh = More air; Many itch = Materials (fiberglass), few itch = allergen.","units":["ibeam:g-diag-three-steps","ibeam:g-emergency-examples","ibeam:g-emergency-actions","ibeam:g-occupational-limits","ibeam:g-four-questions","ibeam:g-sym-respiratory-bio","ibeam:g-sym-mild-oa","ibeam:g-sym-skin-numbers","ibeam:g-odor-dirty-socks","ibeam:g-where-widespread","ibeam:g-interior-zone","ibeam:g-when-latter-week","ibeam:g-when-mild-weather","ibeam:g-filters-change-anyway"]},{"id":"ibeam-g-measurements","title":"Smoke, airflow, and CO2 measurements","dom":"Inspection","order":2610,"intro":"The simple measurements I-BEAM relies on: smoke tubes for pressure, a ranked set of airflow methods, and how to read CO2 without overtrusting it.","mnemonic":"Hood, Velocity, CO2 = Best, OK, Last resort. Percent OA = (R minus S) over (R minus O). CO2 peaks at 11 and 3; above 1,000 check flues and outdoors before blaming the damper.","units":["ibeam:g-smoke-puffs","ibeam:g-smoke-exhausted-room","ibeam:g-oa-min-flow","ibeam:g-airflow-ranking","ibeam:g-velocity-formula","ibeam:g-co2-percent-oa","ibeam:g-co2-peak-times","ibeam:g-co2-above-1000","ibeam:g-co2-compare-areas","ibeam:g-co2-below-threshold"]},{"id":"ibeam-g-construction","title":"Protecting occupants during renovation","dom":"Risk Management","order":2620,"intro":"I-BEAM's isolation and sequencing rules for construction in occupied buildings, plus how chemical storage and post-construction ventilation are handled.","mnemonic":"ENCLOSE, SEAL, SUCK, SEND AWAY: enclose the zone, seal the returns, hold it 5 to 10 Pa negative, and keep the exhaust away from intakes. Wet before fleecy.","units":["ibeam:g-contain-pressure","ibeam:g-seal-returns","ibeam:g-fleecy-delay","ibeam:g-exhaust-reentry","ibeam:g-chem-storage","ibeam:g-post-construction-vent"]},{"id":"ibeam-g-maint-energy","title":"Maintenance, housekeeping, and energy trade-offs","dom":"Standards","order":2630,"intro":"I-BEAM's maintenance and housekeeping numbers and the IAQ lessons from EPA's energy modeling of CV and VAV systems.","mnemonic":"30 to 60 RH, dry in 24, 10 to 20 filters blow out, 600 to 800 starts DCV. VAV with a fixed damper fraction gets only half to two thirds of its outdoor air.","units":["ibeam:g-rh-30-60","ibeam:g-dry-24h","ibeam:g-filter-blowout","ibeam:g-vav-fixed-oa","ibeam:g-co2-dcv","ibeam:g-pm-importance"]},{"id":"nim-h-sampling","title":"Sampling methods: bulk, filtration, tape, swab","dom":"Inspection","order":2700,"intro":"Appendix E's four ways to collect a sample when a client asks for one, and what each method suits. Exam items test matching the method to the surface and the handling details.","mnemonic":"Bulk is a Block (a cubic inch of stuff), Filter is a Quarter (fill a quarter, and it Dries bugs out), Tape is Flat (smooth surfaces, never folded), Swab stays Dry (dry circle, load heavy, fold).","units":["nim:h-samp-scope","nim:h-bulk-use","nim:h-bulk-amount","nim:h-filt-bacteria","nim:h-filt-setup","nim:h-filt-fill","nim:h-tape","nim:h-swab-dry","nim:h-swab-surfaces"]},{"id":"nim-h-case","title":"Case study decisions and particle deposition","dom":"Inspection","order":2710,"intro":"How the manual's Smith and Jones case study picks what to inspect, reacts to findings, and writes recommendations, plus where dust settles in ducts. Scenario items mirror these decisions.","mnemonic":"Light Leaves, Heavy Halts: light particles leave through registers unless snagged by screws or joints, heavy ones halt at bends, dampers, and take-offs.","units":["nim:h-dep-light","nim:h-dep-heavy","nim:h-gray-film","nim:h-rebalance","nim:h-case-random","nim:h-case-renovation","nim:h-case-residue","nim:h-case-no-sample","nim:h-case-shredder","nim:h-case-coil-baseline"]}],"sequences":[{"id":"seq-acr-project-flow","title":"ACR 2025 project flow, first inspection to final inspection","steps":["Inspect the system before the project starts","Document and evaluate findings in the system component assessment","Give a written work plan before any cleaning starts","Mechanically clean the components","Verify each component right after cleaning, before the system runs","Inspect the system again after the project"],"src":"ACR 2025 §1.1, §1.10, §2.0, §4, §5.1"},{"id":"seq-acr-containment-levels","title":"Containment levels, lowest to highest: what each level adds","steps":["Negative pressure, protective coverings, cross-contamination control","Temporary barriers, two-layer 6-mil floor, 4 ACH scrubbing, manometer","Single-chamber decon unit, continuous pressure monitoring, particulate baseline","Second decon chamber, constant recording pressure monitor with alarm"],"src":"ACR 2025 §3.9 to 3.12"},{"id":"seq-acr-pressure-instruments","title":"Negative pressure instrument rule as containment level rises","steps":["Validate negative pressure with a manometer or airflow measuring device","Monitor negative pressure continuously with an instrument that detects loss","Record pressurization constantly, with an appropriate alarm"],"src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.6, 3.11.3, 3.12.3"},{"id":"seq-acr-level3-particulate","title":"Level 3 particulate monitoring and the stop-work rule","steps":["Measure background total particulate before containment set-up","Monitor total particulate in real time during the work","Stop work when levels rise above background","Correct the cause and let levels return to background","Restart the work"],"src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3"},{"id":"seq-acr-equipment-project","title":"Contractor equipment through a project","steps":["Clean and inspect equipment before it enters the work site","Verify vacuum equipment on site before work starts (recommended)","Clean or seal equipment before relocating it within the building","Clean or seal equipment before it leaves the building"],"src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.1 to 3.1.4"},{"id":"seq-acr-coil-path","title":"Coil cleaning path when Type 1 is tried first","steps":["Visually inspect the coil and the drain pan","Clean with Type 1 dry methods, negative air running, coil isolated","Inspect the coil after Type 1","Debris remains or coil impacted: clean with Type 2 wet methods","Inspect the coil after Type 2","Debris still remains: repeat the process","Type 2 cannot remove the debris: replacement may be necessary"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1 to 4.11.5"},{"id":"seq-acr-drain-pan","title":"Drain pan handling during Type 2 coil cleaning","steps":["Inspect the drain pan and test its drainage","Clean and flush the drain pan and drain line","Inspect the drain pan and test its drainage again"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.1"},{"id":"seq-acr-electric-coil","title":"Wet cleaning an electric resistance coil","steps":["De-energize the power source and apply lockout/tagout","Wash with a non-corrosive detergent only","Rinse the coil free of chemicals","Dry the coil thoroughly","Return the coil to an energized state"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.8"},{"id":"seq-acr-dampers","title":"Handling dampers and air-directional devices","steps":["Mark the damper position","Do the cleaning work","Return the damper to its marked position"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.8"},{"id":"seq-acr-restoration-order","title":"Order of operations before a surface treatment","steps":["Mechanically clean: agitate, then capture with vacuum collection","Confirm clean with a PASS on the specified cleanliness verification","Apply the surface treatment, which must meet UL 181"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.3, 4.14, 4.15"},{"id":"seq-acr-fibrous-glass","title":"Restoring damaged fibrous glass","steps":["Mechanically clean the fibrous glass per Section 4.7","Assess whether the cleaned surface gives a strong bondable surface","Resurface it, or replace it if it cannot be restored"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.17 to 4.19"},{"id":"seq-acr-insulation-replacement","title":"Replacing insulation removed for microbial growth","steps":["Remove the porous material with Condition 3 growth","Mechanically surface clean and scrape the metal clean","Bring the base surface to Condition 1, free of loose debris","Install replacement insulation to original spec, per manufacturer instructions"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.16, 4.20"},{"id":"seq-acr-table2-velocities","title":"Table 2 contaminant classes, lowest to highest duct velocity","steps":["Vapors, gases, smoke","Fumes and metal smokes, such as welding","Fine light dust, such as cotton lint","Dry dusts and powders, such as soap dust","Average industrial dust, such as grinding dust","Heavy dusts, such as wet sawdust","Heavy or moist dusts, such as moist cement dust"],"src":"ACR 2025 Table 2"},{"id":"seq-acr-verification-escalation","title":"Verification escalation, from cleaning to system start-up","steps":["Clean the component","Verify immediately with Method 1, visual inspection","Method 1 inconclusive or disputed: use Method 2 or Method 3","Method 2 disputed: Method 3 decides if non-porous; porous stops at Method 2","Put the system back into operation after verification"],"src":"ACR 2025 §5.1, §5.2.1, §5.3.3"},{"id":"seq-acr-method2-procedure","title":"Method 2 Surface Comparison Test procedure","steps":["Note the visible characteristics of the test area before vacuuming","Pass the HEPA contact vacuum brush over the area four times","Compare the visible characteristics after vacuuming","No significant visible difference: the surface is clean","Inconclusive or disputed on a non-porous surface: Method 3 decides"],"src":"ACR 2025 §5.3"},{"id":"seq-acr-method3-sampling","title":"Method 3 NADCA Vacuum Test sampling steps","steps":["Tape the template flat on the dry surface, air handler off","Remove the protective plugs from the new cassette","Attach the cassette outlet end to the vacuum pump tubing","Calibrate the flow to 17 L/min, plus or minus 2","Vacuum each slot twice, once each direction, at 5 cm/s or less","Turn the pump off and put the plugs back","Label the cassette and record the sampled surface area","Weigh per NIOSH 0500 and compare with 0.75 mg/100 cm2"],"src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2, §5.5"},{"id":"seq-acr-method3-airflow","title":"How Method 3 moves particulate onto the filter","steps":["Template holds the cassette above the sampled surface","Air accelerates through the narrow gap between template and surface","Increased velocity dislodges latent particulate onto the filter media","Cassette is prepared and weighed for total debris","Net weight is compared with 0.75 mg/100 cm2"],"src":"ACR 2025 §5.4, §5.5"},{"id":"seq-ascs-lockout-tagout","title":"Applying lockout/tagout before servicing an air handler","steps":["Learn the type, size and hazards of every energy source","Shut the equipment down in an orderly way","Operate the isolating devices to cut off each energy source","Apply a lock or tag to each isolating device","Relieve, disconnect or restrain stored energy","Try a normal start to verify it will not run","Return the controls to off, then begin work"],"src":"29 CFR 1910.147(d); Appendix A"},{"id":"seq-ascs-permit-entry","title":"Permit-required confined space entry, start to finish","steps":["Identify and evaluate the hazards of the permit space","Isolate the space and ventilate to control atmospheric hazards","Test the atmosphere: oxygen, then combustibles, then toxics","Record the preparations and test results on the entry permit","Entry supervisor signs the permit; post it at the portal","Attendant takes station outside, then entrants go in","Supervisor cancels the permit when the work is complete"],"src":"29 CFR 1910.146(d), (e), (f)"},{"id":"seq-ascs-atmos-test","title":"Atmospheric testing order before confined space entry","steps":["Test for oxygen","Test for combustible gases and vapors","Test for toxic gases and vapors"],"src":"29 CFR 1910.146(d)(5)(iii)"},{"id":"seq-ascs-respirator","title":"Putting a worker in a required tight-fitting respirator","steps":["Select the respirator from the hazard assessment","Complete the medical evaluation","Fit test with the same make, model, style and size","Perform a user seal check every time it is put on"],"src":"29 CFR 1910.134(d), (e), (f), (g)"},{"id":"seq-cvi-inspection-flow","title":"From pre-inspection to post-cleaning check","steps":["Pre-inspection: interview, history, plans, and effect on occupants","Set engineering controls before opening the system","Inspect components, keying photos to tags and plan locations","Document and evaluate the findings","Issue assessment: need, scope, techniques, controls, unique requirements","Contractor submits the written work plan before cleaning starts","Inspect and verify after cleaning, before the system runs"],"src":"ACR 2025 §1.2.1, §1.3, §1.4, §1.7, §1.10, §2.0, §1.1, §5.1; EPA Building Air Quality Sec. 6"},{"id":"seq-cvi-s590-assessment","title":"ANSI/IICRC S590 post-loss HVAC assessment","steps":["Assess the HVAC system after the water, fire, or mold loss","Sort each internal surface into impacted or non-impacted","Write the assessment report and Restoration Work Plan (RWP)","Remediation contractor carries out the Restoration Work Plan"],"src":"ANSI/IICRC S590-2023 (IICRC; C&R Magazine summary)"},{"id":"seq-cvi-fusible-damper-test","title":"NFPA 80 fusible-link fire damper test","steps":["Confirm the fire damper is fully open","Remove or release the fusible link","Confirm the damper closes fully on its own","Confirm it latches, if designed to latch","Reset the damper and reinstall the link"],"src":"NFPA 80-2019 §19.5.2.2"},{"id":"seq-cvi-damper-code-chain","title":"Life Safety Code reference chain to damper testing","steps":["NFPA 101 sends HVAC ductwork to NFPA 90A, or 90B as applicable","NFPA 90A sends fire dampers to NFPA 80 and smoke dampers to NFPA 105","NFPA 80 and NFPA 105 set the test and inspection schedule"],"src":"NFPA 101 (2012) §9.2.1; NFPA 90A (2012) §5.4.8"},{"id":"seq-cvi-rodent-cleanup","title":"CDC cleanup of rodent droppings and nests","steps":["Spray droppings and nest with bleach or EPA-registered disinfectant until very wet","Let the material soak about 5 minutes","Wipe it up with paper towels"],"src":"CDC rodent clean-up guidance"},{"id":"seq-ahu-airflow","title":"Air path through a typical draw-through air handler","src":"HVAC fundamentals","steps":["Return air travels from the rooms back toward the AHU","Return and outdoor air blend in the mixing box","Filters clean the mixed air ahead of the coils","Preheat coil, if present, warms cold mixed air","Cooling coil cools and dehumidifies the air","Supply fan pulls air through the coils","Supply duct carries air to terminals and diffusers"]},{"id":"seq-vav-zone-satisfies","title":"VAV reheat box response as a zone's cooling load falls","src":"DOE Minimizing Reheat Guide; HVAC fundamentals","steps":["Zone is warm: box passes maximum cooling airflow","Cooling load falls: damper throttles the primary air","Zone reaches setpoint: damper sits at minimum airflow","Zone keeps cooling below setpoint: reheat coil turns on at minimum"]},{"id":"seq-parallel-box-heating","title":"Parallel fan-powered box going from cooling to heating","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","steps":["Cooling: box fan off, primary air damper modulates","Load drops: primary air throttles to minimum","Heating call: box fan starts, pulling warm plenum air","More heat needed: reheat coil energizes"]},{"id":"seq-heat-pump-defrost","title":"Heat pump defrost cycle","src":"Carrier, What Is Auxiliary Heat; DOE Energy Saver, Air-Source Heat Pumps","steps":["Frost builds on the outdoor coil during heating","Reversing valve shifts to cooling mode and the outdoor fan stops","Hot gas melts frost while aux strips warm the supply air","Frost cleared: system returns to heating mode"]},{"id":"seq-refrigeration-cycle","title":"Refrigerant path through the cooling cycle, starting at the compressor","src":"HVAC fundamentals","steps":["Compressor squeezes low-pressure vapor into hot high-pressure gas","Condenser rejects heat and the gas turns to liquid","Metering device drops the pressure into a cold mist","Evaporator boils the refrigerant, absorbing heat from the air"]},{"id":"seq-hvac-read-mech-sheet","title":"Reading a mechanical drawing set","steps":["Read the legend, abbreviations and general notes on the M-0 sheet","Trace ducts, tags and devices on the M-1 floor plans","Use M-3 sections for vertical runs through shafts and floors","Check M-5 details for enlarged views of specific connections","Look up each plan tag's design data on the M-6 schedules"],"src":"NCS/UDS Module 1; drawing practice"},{"id":"seq-hvac-pitot-traverse","title":"Pitot tube duct traverse for airflow","steps":["Choose a straight duct section away from elbows and fittings","Lay out equal-area or log-Tchebycheff points across the duct","Point the pitot upstream, tip and side ports across one manometer","Read velocity pressure at every traverse point","Convert each reading to fpm with 4005 times root VP","Average the point velocities","Multiply average velocity by duct area in square feet for cfm"],"src":"TSI AF-106; TSI-107"},{"id":"seq-hvac-acca-manuals","title":"ACCA residential design order","steps":["Manual J: calculate heating and cooling loads","Manual S: select equipment to match the loads","Manual D: design and size the duct system","Manual T: select registers and grilles"],"src":"ACCA Manuals J, S, D, T"},{"id":"seq-hvac-oa-fraction","title":"Checking outdoor air with mixed-air temperatures","steps":["Read OAT, RAT and MAT with the unit running steady","Confirm OAT and RAT differ by more than about 5 F","Compute fraction = (MAT - RAT) / (OAT - RAT)","Multiply the fraction by supply airflow for outdoor air cfm","Compare with the scheduled minimum and the damper command"],"src":"PNNL-29078 §9.2, §9.4.1"},{"id":"seq-hvac-co2-estimate","title":"Estimating outdoor air per person from CO2","steps":["Log room CO2 until it levels off at steady state","Subtract outdoor CO2 from the steady indoor value","Divide 15 x 700 by that difference for cfm per person","Report the result as an estimate, noting occupancy and activity"],"src":"ASHRAE Position Document on Indoor CO2 (2025); ASTM D6245"},{"id":"seq-nim-a-inspection-process","title":"Steps of an HVAC inspection, first contact to report","steps":["Contact the client and review drawings, history, and documents","Walk through the building to learn its layout and HVAC system","Discuss the inspection purpose and scope with the client","Draft a written inspection plan for review and approval","Perform the detailed inspection of components in scope","Write the formal report and present it to the client"],"src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1"},{"id":"seq-nim-a-lock-removal","title":"Removing a lock installed by someone else","steps":["Call the employee who installed the lock","Attempt outside plant contact","Notify the employee's supervisor","Notify the facility's safety coordinator or designate","Verify the equipment is clear and safe to turn on","Remove the lock"],"src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 E"},{"id":"seq-nim-dual-duct-airpath","title":"Path of air through a dual-duct system","steps":["Supply fan discharges air","Air passes through the cooling and/or heating coil","Air travels in separate hot deck and cold deck ducts","Zone mixing box blends hot and cold air","Mixed air is delivered to the zone"],"src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2 D"},{"id":"seq-nim-d-stack-winter","title":"Path of outside air through a heated tall building by stack effect","steps":["Outside air infiltrates at the lower levels","Warm inside air rises up shafts and stairwells","Air passes the neutral pressure plane","Air exfiltrates at the upper levels"],"src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.4; EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Driving Forces"},{"id":"seq-nim-info-gathering","title":"Pre-inspection information gathering model","steps":["Client contacts the inspector, who listens to the need","Inspector sets a time to meet with the client","Agree on the facility's main point of contact","Review building information and history, maybe a walk-through","Draft the purpose statement"],"src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.1"},{"id":"seq-nim-scope-steps","title":"Steps in developing the scope of work","steps":["Review plans and reports","Determine systems to be inspected","Determine where to inspect","Draft the scope of inspection","Draft the disclaimer statement"],"src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 A"},{"id":"seq-nim-plan-marking","title":"Marking up the building plans","steps":["Make readable paper copies of the HVAC plans","Note system changes made since the plans were drawn","Outline the spaces to be investigated","Color-code supply, return, and exhaust at the boundary","Mark airflow direction with arrows","Trace upstream to the AHU and outdoor air intake","Mark areas where deposition is expected"],"src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B"},{"id":"seq-nim-inspection-order","title":"Typical order of inspection along the air path","steps":["Outdoor air intake","Filters","Heating coils","Cooling coils","Supply air ducts","Return air ducts","Exhaust ducts"],"src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2"},{"id":"seq-nim-f-report-steps","title":"Steps to create and present the inspection report","steps":["Review all information collected during the inspection","Organize the findings and draft the report","Summarize the inspection and its outcomes","Determine which recommendations to give","Describe the recommendations to the client in the report","Present the report to the client","Follow up after the report is presented"],"src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1"},{"id":"seq-ibeam-g-diagnostic","title":"I-BEAM's general diagnostic process for commercial buildings","steps":["Determine whether the situation is an emergency","Characterize the problem and communicate with occupants","Check specific causes until the problem is solved"],"src":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems"},{"id":"seq-ibeam-g-pm-setup","title":"Setting up a preventive maintenance system per I-BEAM","steps":["Select an administrative system","Survey and inventory all building equipment","Record equipment condition and write repair work orders","Set up master equipment list and history file","Develop PM charts for each piece of equipment","Develop PM work orders from the PM charts","Organize work orders into a master schedule","Even out the workload and leave time for repairs"],"src":"EPA I-BEAM, IAQ Maintenance and Housekeeping Programs"},{"id":"seq-nim-swab-sample","title":"Collecting a swab sample with a filter circle","steps":["Get a 4 inch filter circle from the laboratory","Wipe the dry circle along the settled suspect dust","Keep wiping until the circle is heavily loaded","Fold the circle in half or in quarters","Enclose it in a sealed container"],"src":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E"}],"questions":[{"id":"cvi-0000","stem":"An inspector finds no indoor air handler in a slab-on-grade house. Insulated supply and return trunks pass through an exterior wall to one cabinet on a pad outside. Where should he expect the evaporator coil and its drain pan?","options":["In the cabinet out on the pad, walled off from the compressor","Inside a cased coil box on top of a furnace in the hall closet","Behind the return filter grille in the hallway ceiling","In the attic, where a split system's air handler hangs"],"expl":"A single-package unit encloses all major assemblies in one cabinet, so the evaporator coil, blower, and drain pan are outdoors and get inspected there. The hallway grille on a packaged system usually holds only the filter; the closet coil box and attic air handler belong to split systems.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Split vs packaged","tier":0,"ref":"10 CFR 430 App. M1 definitions","units":["hvac:s-packaged-unit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0001","stem":"On a split system, the inspector finds a cased A-coil sitting on top of a gas furnace, with no blower of its own in the coil cabinet. Which component moves indoor air across that coil?","options":["The furnace's circulating air blower","The outdoor unit's condenser fan","The furnace's induced-draft blower","A cross-flow fan inside the coil cabinet"],"expl":"A coil-only indoor unit has no air mover and relies on the furnace blower (or a modular blower), so the furnace blower compartment is part of the same air-side inspection. The condensing unit fan moves outdoor air, and the inducer moves flue gas, not supply air.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Split systems","tier":0,"ref":"10 CFR 430 App. M1 definitions","units":["hvac:s-coil-only"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0002","stem":"During a January inspection, the inspector notices a heat pump's indoor coil is warm and dry and its drain pan is empty while the system heats. What explains this?","options":["The reversing valve has made the indoor coil the condenser","The defrost cycle has made the indoor coil the evaporator","The outdoor coil is rejecting heat as the system's condenser","The indoor coil is boiling refrigerant as the evaporator"],"expl":"In heating mode the reversing valve routes hot gas to the indoor coil, which condenses refrigerant and releases heat, so it runs warm and dry and makes no condensate. Defrost does the opposite for a few minutes, briefly turning the indoor coil into a cold evaporator.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Heat pumps","tier":0,"ref":"DOE Energy Saver, Air-Source Heat Pumps","units":["hvac:s-reversing-valve"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0003","stem":"On a 30°F morning, an inspector sees a heat pump's outdoor fan stop while steam rises off the outdoor coil, and the indoor electric strips come on for a few minutes. What is the system doing?","options":["Running defrost, with the reversing valve in cooling mode","Running emergency heat, with the compressor locked out","Running a dual-fuel changeover, with the furnace firing","Running heat mode, with the outdoor coil as condenser"],"expl":"Defrost briefly reverses the heat pump to cooling so hot gas melts frost off the outdoor coil, and auxiliary heat comes on to keep supply air warm. Emergency heat is a manual setting that shuts the heat pump off, so the outdoor coil would not steam.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Heat pump defrost","tier":0,"ref":"Carrier, What Is Auxiliary Heat","units":["hvac:s-defrost"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0004","stem":"An all-electric home's winter bills doubled. The inspector finds the heat pump thermostat set to EM HEAT and the outdoor unit idle. What does that setting do?","options":["It runs the resistance strips with the compressor off","It adds the strips automatically when the compressor falls behind","It runs the compressor alone and locks the resistance strips out","It fires a backup gas furnace when the compressor falls behind"],"expl":"Emergency heat is selected by hand and shuts the heat pump off, leaving only resistance strips, which cost far more to run. Adding strips automatically when the compressor falls behind is auxiliary heat, and there is no furnace in an all-electric home.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Aux vs emergency heat","tier":0,"ref":"Carrier, What Is Auxiliary Heat","units":["hvac:s-aux-heat","hvac:s-emergency-heat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0005","stem":"In a dual-fuel system, the heat pump's indoor coil sits on the gas furnace's discharge. How do the two heat sources normally share the heating load?","options":["They take turns, handing off as the outdoor temperature changes","They run together, with the furnace boosting output","They take turns, switched over by the indoor humidity level","They run together below the thermal balance point"],"expl":"Dual fuel is sequential: the heat pump carries mild weather and the furnace takes over when it gets cold, typically near the heat pump's balance point. Running together is how electric auxiliary strips work, not a gas furnace sharing the same coil.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Dual fuel","tier":0,"ref":"Trane, Dual Fuel Heat Pump glossary","units":["hvac:s-dual-fuel"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0006","stem":"A house has no outdoor condensing unit. Two plastic pipes rise from the slab into a mechanical-room cabinet holding a compressor, blower, coil, and drain pan, with ducts leaving the top. What system is this?","options":["A ground-source heat pump running on a buried piping loop","A hydronic fan-coil air handler fed by a gas boiler","A packaged terminal heat pump set in a wall sleeve","A ducted mini-split air handler on a copper line set"],"expl":"A closed-loop geothermal heat pump exchanges heat with the ground through buried loop pipes, and its indoor cabinet holds the compressor, refrigerant-to-air coil, blower, and pan, so the air side is inspected like any air handler. A hydronic air handler has no compressor, and PTHPs and mini-splits have outdoor sections.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Geothermal","tier":0,"ref":"DOE Energy Saver, Geothermal Heat Pumps; 10 CFR 431.92","units":["hvac:s-geothermal"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0007","stem":"A tenant reports a musty smell and black specks blowing from a wall-mounted ductless head. The mesh filters look clean. What is the most likely source for the inspector to confirm?","options":["Growth on the cross-flow blower wheel and the coil","Debris in the return duct that feeds air to the wall head","Mold on the outdoor unit's coil and fan blade","Biofilm in the line-set insulation at the wall"],"expl":"A ductless head has no ducts; its cross-flow (tangential) blower wheel sits right behind the wet evaporator coil and pan, a classic biofilm site that throws specks into the room. The outdoor coil and line insulation are outside the indoor airstream. ACR classes mini-splits as cooling-heating units.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Ductless mini-split","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Definitions (Cooling-Heating Unit)","units":["hvac:s-mini-split-blower"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0008","stem":"A home has one outdoor unit piped to four wall-mounted indoor heads, each with its own remote. How should the inspector count the air-side equipment in the scope?","options":["As four indoor units, each with its own fan and coil","As one air handler, since one outdoor unit serves all","As one duct system, with four supply outlets on it","As four zones, served by bypass dampers on one air handler"],"expl":"A multi-split has one outdoor unit and two or more indoor units that run independently, so each head is its own air-side component with its own coil, blower, and condensate pan. There is no shared duct system or zoning damper to count.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Multi-split","tier":0,"ref":"10 CFR 430 App. M1 definitions","units":["hvac:s-multi-split"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0009","stem":"In a one-story slab house, a gas furnace in a hall closet takes return air in at the top and discharges down into ducts in the slab. Where should the inspector expect to find the cooling coil?","options":["Below the furnace, on top of the supply plenum","On top of the furnace, where an upflow unit's coil sits","In the outdoor unit, as on a single-package system","Beside the furnace, in a horizontal attic coil box"],"expl":"This is a downflow (counterflow) furnace, so the supply side is at the bottom, and the IRC bars cooling coils upstream of the heat exchanger unless listed for it. The top of a downflow unit is its return side, which is where the upflow coil location trap lands.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Furnace configurations","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1411.8","units":["hvac:s-furnace-coil-location"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0010","stem":"An inspector finds a horizontal furnace and cooling coil in an attic above a finished bedroom with a water-stained ceiling. The only drain is the primary line. What does the IRC require here?","options":["A secondary drain or a float that shuts the unit off","A drain pan sloped 1/8 inch per foot to its outlet","An overflow line tied into the primary drain line","A MERV 8 filter installed ahead of the cooling coil"],"expl":"Where overflow would damage the building, the IRC calls for an auxiliary pan with its own drain, a separate overflow drain, or a water-level shutoff. An overflow tied into the primary line fails with it. The 1/8 in. per foot slope and MERV 8 do not protect against overflow.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Condensate protection","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1411.9.1 (M1411.3.1 in earlier editions); IMC 307.2.3","units":["hvac:s-overflow-protection"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0011","stem":"A zoned house has a short duct with a weighted damper linking the supply plenum to the return plenum. On a hot day with one small zone calling, the inspector finds frost on the indoor coil. What links the two findings?","options":["Bypassed supply air cools the return air and drives the coil colder","The damper pulls hot attic air into the return and coil","The closed zones raise the total airflow across the coil","The bypass duct adds static pressure across the indoor coil"],"expl":"A bypass duct dumps supply air back into the return, so cold air is reconditioned, return temperature falls, and the coil can freeze during long cooling calls. Closed zones cut airflow, and the bypass exists to relieve static, not add it.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Zoning bypass","tier":0,"ref":"JLC, Troubleshooting Zone Damper Systems","units":["hvac:s-zoning-bypass"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0012","stem":"A homeowner with a new 96% AFUE furnace in a vented attic asks why heating bills barely dropped. The inspector finds long supply runs in the attic. Which loss does the AFUE rating leave out?","options":["Heat lost from ducts running outside conditioned space","Heat carried up the flue along with the hot combustion gases","Latent heat caught by the secondary heat exchanger","Fuel burned to keep a standing pilot flame lit"],"expl":"AFUE compares annual heat output with fossil fuel consumed at the furnace, so duct losses are outside it, and DOE says they can reach 35% of output when ducts run through attics or garages. Flue loss, condensing recovery, and pilot fuel are all inside the AFUE test.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"AFUE","tier":0,"ref":"DOE Energy Saver, Furnaces and Boilers","units":["hvac:a-afue-duct-loss"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0013","stem":"The ceiling filter grille of a house feeds a return duct that crosses the attic to a garage air handler. Pink loose-fill fibers like the attic insulation keep showing up at the supply registers. Which mechanism fits best?","options":["Return leaks pull attic air in just past the filter","Supply leaks pull attic air in toward the supply registers","Filter bypass at the air handler admits garage air","Stack effect pushes attic air in through the registers"],"expl":"A leaky return run is under suction, so it draws unconditioned attic air, and loose fill, into the system after the filter. Supply leaks push conditioned air out instead, which is why supply leaks waste energy while return leaks import contaminants.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct leakage","tier":0,"ref":"DOE Energy Saver, Minimizing Energy Losses in Ducts","units":["hvac:a-supply-vs-return-leaks"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0014","stem":"In a new house, the inspector finds a heat recovery ventilator (HRV) with no condensate drain connected. Why does an HRV typically need a drain when many ERVs do not?","options":["Its core moves only heat, so exhaust moisture condenses","Its core moves heat and moisture, so supply air condenses","Its rotating wheel carries water between the two airstreams","Its paper core is washed and rinsed in place yearly"],"expl":"An HRV core transfers sensible heat only, so moisture in warm exhaust air condenses on the cold core in winter, and Broan requires a drain on every HRV but not on its ERVs. ERV cores transfer moisture, and paper ERV cores are vacuumed, never washed.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"HRV and ERV","tier":0,"ref":"Broan HRV/ERV installation guide; RenewAire EV manual","units":["hvac:s-hrv-drain"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0015","stem":"On a mechanical sheet whose legend uses common abbreviations, the inspector sees FSD tags at shaft wall penetrations and BDD tags at toilet exhaust fan outlets. What devices do those tags mark?","options":["Combination fire and smoke dampers and backdraft dampers","Fusible-link fire dampers and manual balancing dampers","Motorized smoke dampers and manual volume dampers","Duct smoke detectors and balancing dampers at the fans"],"expl":"On a typical legend FSD is a combination fire and smoke damper and BDD is a backdraft damper. FD, BD, SD, and VD are the tags for the other dampers in the distractors, and a duct smoke detector is not a damper at all, so read the legend before counting dampers into the scope.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Mechanical drawings","tier":0,"ref":"Sample mechanical legend (ASHRAE design competition set)","units":["hvac:s-backdraft","hvac:s-combination-damper","hvac:a-damper-tags","hvac:a-other-tags"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0016","stem":"Mechanical plans show one constant-volume rooftop unit and one thermostat serving an open big-box sales floor. Which supply-side components should the inspector expect downstream of the unit?","options":["Trunk and branch ducts to diffusers","VAV boxes with a reheat coil at each zone","Mixing boxes fed by separate hot and cold ducts","Zone ducts leaving separate unit dampers"],"expl":"A single-zone constant-volume unit varies supply temperature at fixed airflow for one space, so it needs only ducts and outlets downstream. Reheat VAV boxes, dual-duct mixing boxes, and unit-mounted zone dampers belong to VAV, dual-duct, and multizone systems.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Single-zone CAV","tier":0,"ref":"Titus terminal unit types","units":["hvac:s-single-zone"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0017","stem":"An inspector finds ceiling-hung water-source heat pumps above a hard drywall ceiling with no access panels. What does ASHRAE 62.1 require for units like these?","options":["Access doors sized for inspection and maintenance of each unit","Access to both faces of each coil that is four rows deep or less","Access to coils over four rows deep but not to the thinner coils","Access panels unless the owner approves a hard ceiling in writing"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1 lists water-source heat pumps, fan-coil units, and other terminal units among components that need access doors or panels sized for inspection and maintenance, and the owner cannot opt out. Coils of four rows or fewer need upstream-face access; both faces are required only above four rows.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Water-source HP","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.19.2, §5.19.3","units":["hvac:s-wshp-access"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0018","stem":"In a dual-duct building in July, one zone runs hot while its neighbors are comfortable and the cold duct is at design temperature. Which fault best fits?","options":["The terminal's hot-air inlet damper is stuck open","The multizone unit's hot-deck damper is stuck open","The VAV box's hot-water reheat valve is stuck wide open","The parallel box backdraft damper is stuck open"],"expl":"A dual-duct mixing box blends hot and cold duct air at the zone, so a hot-inlet damper stuck open overheats only that zone. A multizone unit mixes at the unit, while reheat valves and backdraft dampers belong to single-duct VAV and parallel fan-powered boxes.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Dual duct","tier":0,"ref":"Titus, Dual Duct Mixing terminal","units":["hvac:s-dual-duct-fault"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0019","stem":"During a July walk-through of a VAV reheat system, one zone is warm though its box reads maximum cooling airflow. The air leaving the box coil is warmer than the primary air. Which fault fits?","options":["The heating water valve is leaking or stuck open","The box damper is stuck at its minimum position","The economizer outdoor damper is stuck closed","The inlet flow ring is coated and reads high"],"expl":"Only heat added at the box explains air leaving the coil warmer than the primary air, so the hot water reheat valve is passing water, heating and cooling at once. A damper stuck at minimum shows low airflow, and an AHU economizer fault would affect every zone.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"VAV reheat","tier":0,"ref":"Titus, Single Duct with Heating Coil","units":["hvac:s-leaking-reheat-valve"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0020","stem":"A series fan-powered box's fan is found spinning slowly backward with its primary damper open and the fan motor off. What control practice prevents this?","options":["Energize the fan ahead of any primary airflow","Close the backdraft damper before starting the fan","Start the box fan only on a call for zone heating","Hold the primary damper at minimum when occupied"],"expl":"A forward-curved blower pressurized from its inlet with the motor off spins backward, so series boxes must never get primary air unless the fan is running. Backdraft dampers and heating-only fan starts are parallel-box features.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan-powered boxes","tier":1,"ref":"Krueger white paper, Anti-Backward Rotation for SFPTUs","units":["hvac:s-series-backward"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0021","stem":"During cooling season, the fan in a parallel fan-powered box is off as designed, yet cold primary air is spilling out of the box's plenum inlet into the ceiling. Which component has failed?","options":["The backdraft damper on the fan's outlet","The primary air damper actuator linkage","The dust filter on the fan's plenum air inlet","The reheat coil's hot water valve actuator"],"expl":"In a parallel box the fan is off during cooling, and a backdraft damper stops primary air from flowing backward through the fan into the ceiling plenum. The plenum-inlet filter loads with dust but does not block reverse flow.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan-powered boxes","tier":0,"ref":"Titus, Fan Powered Parallel Type","units":["hvac:s-parallel-backdraft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0022","stem":"Why can the central fan in a system of series fan-powered boxes run at a lower duct static pressure than one serving parallel boxes?","options":["Each box fan takes over, so primary air only needs to reach it","Each box fan runs only on a heating call, so primary flow falls","Each box fan handles only plenum air, so primary ducts shrink","Each box damper stays at minimum, so the main duct is unloaded"],"expl":"In a series box the primary air only has to reach the box fan, which is sized for total airflow and pushes everything downstream. Heating-only fans that handle just plenum air describe the parallel box.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan-powered boxes","tier":0,"ref":"Titus, Fan Powered Series Type","units":["hvac:s-series-low-static"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0023","stem":"In a 1960s high-rise, cabinets under each perimeter window hold a water coil and a plenum lined with small nozzles fed by a high-pressure primary air duct. What are they?","options":["Induction units, where venturi jets pull room air over the coil","Fan coil units, where a blower wheel pushes room air over the coil","Unit ventilators, where a fan draws outdoor air through the wall","PTACs, where a compressor in the wall sleeve cools room air"],"expl":"Induction terminals have no fans: primary air through the nozzles creates a venturi that induces room air across the secondary coil, so lint on that coil and in the nozzles is the inspection target. Fan coils, unit ventilators, and PTACs all have their own fans.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Induction units","tier":0,"ref":"Energy Efficiency Manual 4.9, Induction Systems","units":["hvac:s-induction-unit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0024","stem":"In April, tenants on a hotel's north side want heat while the south side wants cooling, but the plant can serve only one mode at a time. Each fan coil has a single coil with one supply and one return pipe. What explains the limit?","options":["A two-pipe system carries hot or chilled water but not both","A four-pipe system has lost the three-way valve at each coil","A water-source loop has no boiler to add heat in spring","A multizone unit's hot-deck damper is stuck in one position"],"expl":"A two-pipe fan coil system supplies either chilled or hot water and must change over by season, so it cannot heat one unit while cooling another. Four-pipe systems have separate hot and chilled water and can do both at once.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan coil units","tier":0,"ref":"Krueger Fan Coil Engineering","units":["hvac:s-two-four-pipe"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0025","stem":"A scope covers 40 ceiling-concealed fan coil units in a condo tower. Under ACR 2025, which inspection list applies to each fan coil?","options":["The air handling unit list in 1.7.1","The supply duct list in 1.7.2","The return duct list in 1.7.3","The exhaust duct list in 1.7.5"],"expl":"ACR places fan coil units and evaporative coolers in the air-handling unit category, so each one gets the full 1.7.1 list: filters and bypass, coils, pans and drains, humidification, insulation, fans, dampers and gaskets, and unit integrity. The supply list covers boxes and reheat coils in the ducts.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan coil units","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","units":["acr:1.7.1-i"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0026","stem":"An inspector pulls a hotel PTAC chassis and finds water standing in the outdoor-side base pan, with no drain kit installed. What normally removes that condensate?","options":["The condenser fan's slinger ring throws it onto the hot coil","A P-trap sized to the unit's negative static pressure","A float switch that stops the compressor so the pan drains","A condensate pump that lifts it to the building drain"],"expl":"Many PTACs use a slinger ring on the condenser fan to pick condensate out of the base pan and spray it onto the hot condenser coil, where it evaporates, so some water there is normal in cooling. Trap sizing to negative static belongs to draw-through air handlers.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"PTAC/PTHP","tier":1,"ref":"Friedrich ZoneAire PTAC service manual","units":["hvac:s-ptac-slinger"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0027","stem":"A hotel spec sheet lists packaged terminal heat pumps (PTHPs) instead of PTACs. What distinguishes a PTHP under the federal definition?","options":["Reverse cycle refrigeration is its main source of heat","Electric resistance heat is its prime heat source","A circulating water loop is its heat source and heat sink","A buried ground loop is its heat source and its heat sink"],"expl":"Federal rules define a PTHP as a PTAC that uses reverse-cycle refrigeration as its prime heat source, with supplementary hot water, steam, or electric heat available. A PTAC's heat comes from hot water, steam, or electric resistance, and a water loop is the water-source heat pump definition.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"PTAC/PTHP","tier":0,"ref":"10 CFR 431.92","units":["hvac:s-pthp"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0028","stem":"An office has dozens of ceiling-hung heat pumps on one closed water loop, plus a boiler and a closed-circuit cooling tower in the plant. What do the boiler and tower do?","options":["Keep the loop water in a moderate temperature band","Send hot and chilled water to separate coils at once","Feed chilled water to the units' air-side cooling coils","Heat and cool outdoor air in a separate DOAS cabinet"],"expl":"In a boiler-tower water-source heat pump system the boiler adds heat when the loop runs cold and the tower rejects heat when it runs warm; in shoulder seasons the units balance each other. The loop is a heat source and sink, not chilled water for air-side coils.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Water-source HP","tier":0,"ref":"Armstrong WSHP application guide; 10 CFR 431.92","units":["hvac:s-wshp-loop"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0029","stem":"On the plans, the inspector sees piping tagged CHWS and CHWR at an AHU coil, and CWS and CWR running from the chiller up to the roof. Which loop feeds the air-side cooling coil?","options":["The chilled water supply and return piping circuit","The condenser water supply and return piping loop","The heating hot water supply and return piping loop","The refrigerant suction and liquid line pair"],"expl":"CHWS/CHWR is chilled water from the chiller evaporator to the AHU coil. CWS/CWR is condenser water between the chiller and the cooling tower, so it never reaches an air-side coil; HWS/HWR serves heating coils.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Chilled water plant","tier":0,"ref":"Sample mechanical legend (ASHRAE design competition set)","units":["hvac:s-chilled-water","hvac:a-piping-tags"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0030","stem":"An inspector walks a DOAS unit that feeds ventilation air to fan coils in each room. Compared with a conventional rooftop unit, which section should he expect to be missing?","options":["The return air connection and mixing box","The outdoor air intake and its bird screen","The dehumidifying cooling coil and pan","The supply fan and its discharge plenum"],"expl":"A DOAS conditions only the outdoor air needed for ventilation, so there is no return-air mixing section. It must dehumidify that air deeply enough to carry the zone latent load, which makes its coil and pan a key inspection point.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"DOAS","tier":0,"ref":"Trane Engineers Newsletter ADM-APN062","units":["hvac:s-doas"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0031","stem":"A rooftop unit's coil section runs under negative pressure. Its drain line drops straight from the pan to the roof with no trap, and the pan stays full whenever the fan runs. Why?","options":["Suction pulls air up the drain so the pan cannot empty","Positive pressure pushes air out and sprays the condensate","Face velocity over 500 fpm carries the water off the fins","An oversized trap backs water into the coil section above"],"expl":"In a draw-through section the pan sits below room pressure, so an untrapped drain sucks air up the line and water cannot leave until the fan stops; ASHRAE 62.1 requires a P-trap or other seal for this case. Spraying out is the blow-through behavior, and there is no trap here to be oversized.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Condensate traps","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.7.3","units":["hvac:s-draw-through-trap"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0032","stem":"A draw-through AHU's drain pan section runs at a maximum of -2.0 in. w.c. Using a common coil-manufacturer rule, what is the minimum trap height H?","options":["3.0 in., the negative static plus 1 in.","2.5 in., the negative static plus 1/2 in.","2.0 in., equal to the negative static","1.5 in., the negative static less 1/2 in."],"expl":"For negative pressure, H equals the maximum negative static plus 1 in., and the second dimension is H/2. Static plus 1/2 in. is the positive-pressure (blow-through) rule. Mnemonic: pulling needs a full inch of margin, pushing needs half.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Condensate traps","tier":1,"ref":"Marlo Coil, Condensate Drain Trapping","units":["hvac:s-trap-height"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0033","stem":"Restroom odor shows up in the supply air of a DOAS with an enthalpy wheel. The inspector measures the exhaust side of the wheel at a higher static pressure than the outdoor-air side. What mechanism fits?","options":["Seal leakage from the exhaust into the supply","Condensate carryover from the supply-side cooling coil","Cross-leakage through a cracked plate-type core","Return air recirculating through a mixing damper"],"expl":"Wheels transfer exhaust air to supply through seal leakage and carryover, and when the exhaust side is at higher pressure, exhaust is forced into the supply. ASHRAE guidance is to keep the supply side higher so leakage moves toward the exhaust; plate cores belong to HRVs and plate exchangers.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Energy recovery","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE, Practical Guidance for Epidemic Operation of ERVs","units":["hvac:s-wheel-leakage"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0034","stem":"On a Gulf Coast office, the inspector finds the rooftop economizer controlled by a single outdoor dry-bulb sensor. What IAQ-related weakness should he note?","options":["It admits cool outdoor air that is also very humid","It shuts the outdoor damper as CO2 rises","It compares return and outdoor enthalpy before opening","It locks out free cooling whenever it is humid"],"expl":"A dry-bulb economizer judges outdoor air by temperature alone, so cool, humid air passes its test and adds moisture to the unit and space. Comparing enthalpy and locking out on humidity is what an enthalpy control adds.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Economizers","tier":0,"ref":"PNNL, Air-Side Economizer O&M Best Practices","units":["hvac:s-econ-drybulb-enthalpy"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0035","stem":"After a January cold snap, an inspector finds split tubes in an AHU's hot water heating coil. The outdoor air damper linkage is disconnected and the damper hangs wide open. What is the likely chain of events?","options":["Subfreezing air froze the water in the coil","Steam hammer from a failed trap split the coil tubes","Acid coil cleaner ate through the tube walls in winter","High face velocity eroded the tubes at the return bends"],"expl":"An economizer or outdoor damper stuck open can flood the unit with outdoor air below 32°F and freeze a hydronic coil. Steam hammer is a steam coil failure, not a hot water coil one.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Economizers","tier":0,"ref":"PNNL, Air-Side Economizer O&M Best Practices","units":["hvac:s-econ-stuck-open"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0036","stem":"In a blow-through AHU, the fan discharges onto the cooling coil and high-efficiency final filters sit just after the coil. In summer the final filters are soaked. What best explains it?","options":["Air at or near saturation goes from the coil to the filters","Fan heat after the coil drops the air below its dew point","Negative pressure at the coil draws drain water into the airflow","Outdoor air bypasses the coil and condenses on the media"],"expl":"Air leaving a cooling coil is at or near saturation, and in a blow-through unit it goes straight into whatever sits downstream, so uneven coil velocity can produce fog that soaks filters. In a draw-through unit, fan heat added after the coil moves air away from saturation.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Blow-through vs draw-through","tier":0,"ref":"HPAC Engineering, The Case of the Wet Filters","units":["hvac:s-blow-through-wet"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0037","stem":"An inspector finds open seams in the return-air plenum between the return fan discharge and the mixing box, inside a dusty mechanical room. The return fan runs whenever the supply fan runs. Which way does air move through those seams?","options":["Outward, since the return fan keeps the plenum positive","Inward, because the supply fan draws that plenum negative","Inward, because the relief damper holds it below ambient","Neither way, since the plenum matches room pressure"],"expl":"A return fan runs with the supply fan and pressurizes the return plenum, so leaks there push air out rather than drawing mechanical room dust in. The negative return plenum is the relief-fan arrangement when the relief fan is off.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Return vs relief fans","tier":1,"ref":"Trane Engineers Newsletter ADM-APN003","units":["hvac:s-return-relief-fans"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0038","stem":"A desert-climate house is cooled by a rooftop evaporative cooler with wetted pads, a water sump, and a blower that feeds ceiling registers through a roof duct. Under ACR 2025, which inspection category covers this unit?","options":["Air-handling unit, the same as fan coil units","Supply air duct, the same as fan-powered boxes","Open air plenum, the same as attic spaces","Exhaust air duct, the same as heat recovery exhaust"],"expl":"ACR puts evaporative coolers and fan coil units in the AHU category, so the pads, sump, fan, and unit integrity all fall under the mandatory 1.7.1 list, including humidification systems. VAV boxes are supply components, and open air plenums are outside the ACR scope.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Evaporative coolers","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","units":["acr:1.7.1-i"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0039","stem":"Inside a large AHU, an inspector sees a housed centrifugal wheel with about a dozen flat blades tilted away from the direction of rotation. What fan type is it, and what cleaning issue does its blade angle create?","options":["Backward-inclined; dust builds up on the blade faces","Forward-curved; its many small cupped blades load with dust","Radial-blade; its flat radial blades shed particulate well","Vaneaxial; dust collects on the outlet guide vanes"],"expl":"Blades that tilt away from rotation mark a backward-inclined wheel, and DOE notes their low angle of impingement promotes particulate buildup on the blades. Forward-curved wheels have many small cupped blades curved toward rotation, and radial blades are the type that limits buildup.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan types","tier":0,"ref":"DOE Fan Sourcebook, Fan Types","units":["hvac:s-fan-fc","hvac:s-fan-bi"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0040","stem":"After a heavily loaded forward-curved blower wheel is cleaned and a crushed return duct is replaced, the motor's amp draw rises close to nameplate. What explains it?","options":["FC fan power keeps climbing as the airflow increases","FC fan power drops as airflow nears free delivery","Less duct restriction raised the fan's static load","Cleaning knocked the balance clips off the wheel"],"expl":"A forward-curved fan's power curve climbs steadily with airflow, so restoring airflow raises motor load, and DOE warns FC drives need careful sizing to avoid overload. Power that falls off at high airflow is the backward-inclined (non-overloading) and propeller behavior.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan types","tier":1,"ref":"DOE Fan Sourcebook, Fan Types","units":["hvac:s-fc-overload"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0041","stem":"An inspector opens an AHU fan section and finds an unhoused centrifugal wheel spinning inside an insulated cabinet, with supply ducts leaving from two different sides. What is the fan, and what does it do to that cabinet?","options":["A plenum fan that pressurizes the whole surrounding section","A forward-curved fan that draws the whole section negative","A vaneaxial fan that straightens the airflow with guide vanes","A propeller fan that exhausts the section outdoors"],"expl":"A plenum (plug) fan is a centrifugal wheel with no housing that pressurizes the surrounding cabinet, which is why air can leave in several directions and why the whole section's insulation is a pressurized airstream surface. Negative pressure is found upstream of the fan, in a draw-through coil section.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan types","tier":0,"ref":"Trane Engineers Newsletter, Direct-Drive Plenum Fans","units":["hvac:s-fan-plenum"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0042","stem":"In a large supply duct riser, an inspector finds a cylindrical in-line fan with a ring of fixed, stationary blades just downstream of the rotor. Which fan type is this?","options":["Vaneaxial, with outlet vanes that straighten the flow","Tubeaxial, a propeller wheel inside a plain cylinder","Propeller, a low-pressure fan mounted in a panel","Plenum, an unhoused centrifugal wheel in a cabinet"],"expl":"A vaneaxial fan is a tubeaxial fan with outlet vanes that turn swirl into pressure, giving a more uniform airflow for medium to high pressure duty. A tubeaxial has no vanes and leaves a swirling discharge; propeller fans handle high flow at low pressure with little ductwork.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan types","tier":0,"ref":"DOE Fan Sourcebook, Axial Fans","units":["hvac:s-fan-axial"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0043","stem":"An inspector finds a cooling coil fed by a brass distributor with a dozen small tubes on one end and a large insulated copper line leaving a header. What type of coil is it?","options":["A direct expansion coil that runs on refrigerant","A chilled water coil with supply and return headers","A distributing steam coil with its inner feed tubes","A hot water reheat coil with a two-way control valve"],"expl":"A distributor with many small feeder tubes, usually after a TXV, marks a direct-expansion refrigerant coil; the large insulated line is the suction line. Chilled and hot water coils use pipe-thread or flanged supply and return headers, not a distributor.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Coil types","tier":0,"ref":"Marlo Coil, How to Identify the Four Main Types of Coils","units":["hvac:s-dx-coil"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0044","stem":"A preheat coil in an AHU sees outdoor air well below freezing. Why would a designer pick a distributing (tube-in-tube) steam coil there instead of a standard steam coil?","options":["Its inner tubes spread steam evenly so it resists icing","Its headers on opposite ends give a straight-through steam path","Its refrigerant distributor feeds each circuit evenly","Its three-way valve keeps water moving through the coil loop"],"expl":"Distributing steam coils spray steam from small inner tubes along the full tube length and are used where air falls below 40°F, earning the 'freeze proof' name. Opposite-end headers with a straight path describe the standard steam coil used above 40°F.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Coil types","tier":1,"ref":"Marlo Coil, How to Identify the Four Main Types of Coils","units":["hvac:s-water-steam-coils"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0045","stem":"A new AHU built to ASHRAE 62.1 has an access door only on the entering side of its 4-row cooling coil. For a cleaning scope written to ACR 2025, what should the inspector call for?","options":["A second door that reaches the coil's leaving face","No change, since 62.1 needs upstream access only","Coil removal, since both faces lack cleaning access","A static pressure test in place of downstream access"],"expl":"ACR requires access to both the upstream and downstream side of each coil section for cleaning, while ASHRAE 62.1 only requires the upstream face on coils of four rows or fewer, so a code-compliant unit can still need a new door. Removal applies only when neither side is accessible, and the pressure-drop test is a recommendation, not a substitute.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Coil access","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11; ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.19.3","units":["hvac:s-coil-access"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0046","stem":"A building's HVAC fans keep running during a fire alarm as part of its smoke control sequence. The inspector finds curtain fire dampers labeled for static systems. Why is that label a concern?","options":["Static dampers are rated to close only when fans are off","Static dampers are rated to close against the full airflow","Static dampers carry a 212°F link instead of 165°F","Static dampers are smoke dampers without heat release"],"expl":"A static-rated fire damper is meant for HVAC systems that shut down in a fire, so it is not rated to close against moving air; systems whose fans keep running need dynamic-rated dampers. The 165°F link is standard on both, with 212°F as an option.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fire and smoke dampers","tier":0,"ref":"Ruskin IBD2 static fire damper spec (UL 555)","units":["hvac:s-static-dynamic","hvac:s-link-165"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0047","stem":"Under the mechanical code, a fire damper's heat-actuating device in an ordinary duct operates at about 50°F above normal duct temperature, but not below what temperature?","options":["160°F","165°F","212°F","350°F"],"expl":"The IMC sets the fire damper actuation point near 50°F above normal duct temperature with a 160°F floor, and caps it at 350°F in smoke control systems. The common 165°F fusible link satisfies the 160°F floor, and 212°F is an optional link rating. Mnemonic: code floor 160, standard link 165, smoke control ceiling 350.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fire and smoke dampers","tier":1,"ref":"IMC 607.3.3.1","units":["hvac:s-link-165","hvac:s-imc-160"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0048","stem":"An inspector reviewing a hospital's fire damper records sees each damper was tested one year after installation. Under NFPA 80, how often must hospital fire dampers be tested after that?","options":["Every 6 years","Every 4 years","Every year","Every other year"],"expl":"NFPA 80 requires a test one year after installation, then every 4 years, except hospitals, which may go every 6 years; NFPA 105 sets the same cycle for smoke dampers. One year is ACR's recommended Table 1 healthcare cleanliness inspection interval, not the damper test. Mnemonic: 1 then 4, hospitals 6.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fire and smoke dampers","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA 80 §19.4.1 (2010 ed.)","units":["hvac:s-nfpa80-test"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0049","stem":"An inspector finds wet, rusting turning vanes in an elbow a few feet downstream of a steam humidifier's dispersion tubes. What design rule does this finding point to?","options":["Obstructions sit beyond the absorption distance","Humidifier supply water meets potable water standards","A MERV 8 or better filter sits ahead of wetted surfaces","Drain pans slope 1/8 inch per foot toward the drain"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1 5.8.2 requires turning vanes, volume dampers, duct offsets over 15 degrees, and air cleaners downstream of a humidifier to sit at or beyond the manufacturer's absorption distance, because steam that has not absorbed condenses on them. Only eliminators, coils, or evaporative media may sit closer, and then only over a drain pan. The other options are real 62.1 rules that do not explain wetting at the vanes.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Humidifiers","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.8.2","units":["hvac:s-absorption-distance"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0050","stem":"Fine white dust keeps settling on desks in an office served by an ultrasonic (atomizing) humidifier fed with tap water. What should the inspector report as the cause?","options":["Tank minerals are sprayed into the room air","Scale is flaking off the steam dispersion tubes downstream","Evaporative media is shedding fibers downstream","Duct liner is eroding in the humidified airstream"],"expl":"Ultrasonic and impeller humidifiers throw dissolved minerals, and any microorganisms in the tank, into the air as white dust, so the recommendation is low-mineral water. Steam and evaporative types are not expected to disperse significant minerals.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Humidifiers","tier":0,"ref":"EPA, Use and Care of Home Humidifiers","units":["hvac:s-ultrasonic"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0051","stem":"An inspector finds a flat, level drain pan under an office AHU cooling coil holding a film of standing water and slime. What does ASHRAE 62.1 require of this pan?","options":["A slope of at least 1/8 inch per foot to the outlet","A depth of 1-1/2 inches and 3 inches beyond the unit","A length reaching 18 inches past the coil's leaving face","An outlet set at the pan's midpoint for even drainage"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires pans to slope at least 0.125 in. per foot toward the drain, or otherwise drain freely fan on or off, with the outlet at the lowest point. The 1-1/2 in. by 3 in. dimensions are the IRC auxiliary pan, and 18 in. is 62.1's access space between coils.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Drain pans","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.7.1","units":["hvac:s-pan-slope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0052","stem":"Downstream of a supply fan, an inspector finds a silencer section with perforated metal over fibrous glass fill. How does ACR 2025 treat it for cleaning and verification?","options":["As porous, so it stays dry and skips Method 3 testing","As non-porous, so the NADCA Vacuum Test verifies its surface","As porous, so Type 2 wet coil methods apply to it","As an open air plenum, so it is outside the scope"],"expl":"ACR lists sound attenuated duct as porous, so water and cleaning agents may not contact it and the NADCA Vacuum Test (Method 3) does not apply; Method 1 and Method 2 remain. The perforated metal face is what tempts candidates to call it non-porous.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Sound attenuators","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Definitions; §4.4; §5.3.3","units":["hvac:a-attenuator"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0053","stem":"A client points to dark smudge rings on the ceiling tiles around several supply diffusers and asks if the ducts are dirty. What does that smudge pattern most often indicate?","options":["Room-air dirt induced onto the ceiling by the jet","Duct debris blowing out of the diffuser with the air","Condensation from cold supply air wetting the tiles","Filter bypass at the AHU loading the supply ducts"],"expl":"Supply jets induce room air along the ceiling, and the lightweight dirt in that secondary air deposits as smudging, so the pattern alone does not prove dirty ducts. Inspect the ducts before recommending cleaning.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Diffusers","tier":0,"ref":"ACHR News, Smudging Around Air Distribution Diffusers","units":["hvac:d-smudging"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0054","stem":"A homeowner asks why her new condenser's SEER2 is lower than the SEER number on the same model line. What changed in the federal test for ducted units?","options":["A higher external static, 0.50 in. w.c., in the test","A lower external static, 0.10 in. w.c., in the test","Resistance heat energy counted in the cooling ratio","A single 95°F test point replacing the seasonal bin method"],"expl":"Appendix M1 raised the minimum external static for conventional ducted systems to 0.50 in. w.c., closer to real duct systems, so the same equipment posts a lower number. 0.10 in. w.c. was the old Appendix M value for small systems, resistance heat belongs to HSPF2, and a single 95°F point is EER2.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"SEER2","tier":0,"ref":"10 CFR 430 App. M1 Table 4","units":["hvac:a-seer2-test-static"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0055","stem":"Comparing two heat pumps for a cold-climate house, the inspector explains HSPF2. What does the federal definition divide by what?","options":["Season's heating Btu by all watt-hours, strips included","Season's heating Btu by compressor watt-hours, strips excluded","Heating output by input at one 47°F test condition","Season's cooling Btu by all watt-hours the unit used"],"expl":"HSPF2 is the season's space heating in Btu divided by the heat pump system's watt-hours for the season, and the calculation includes resistance heat used when the compressor falls short. A single-condition ratio is COP, and seasonal cooling Btu per watt-hour is SEER2.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"HSPF2","tier":0,"ref":"10 CFR 430 App. M1 §1.2 and §4.2","units":["hvac:a-hspf2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0056","stem":"A Phoenix homeowner is choosing between two condensers with the same SEER2 but different EER2 ratings. Why does the inspector tell her EER2 deserves weight in her climate?","options":["It rates efficiency at one hot 95°F test point","It rates efficiency averaged over a full cooling season","It rates heating efficiency over a full heating season","It rates a gas furnace's fuel efficiency over a year"],"expl":"EER2 is a single-condition rating at 95°F outdoor, which is why the Southwest region adds an EER2 minimum on top of SEER2 for split air conditioners. The seasonal average is SEER2, seasonal heating is HSPF2, and annual furnace fuel use is AFUE.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"EER2","tier":0,"ref":"10 CFR 430.32(c)(6)","units":["hvac:a-eer2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0057","stem":"A geothermal heat pump brochure lists a heating COP of 4.0. What does that figure mean?","options":["It gives 4 units of heat output per unit of electric power input","It delivers 4 Btu of heat per watt-hour in a season","It draws 4 kW of power for each ton of heat moved","It moves 4 tons of heat at its rated loop water flow"],"expl":"COP is a unitless ratio of heat delivered to electrical energy used at one set of conditions, so 4.0 means four units of heat out per unit of electricity in. Btu per watt-hour over a season is the HSPF form, kW per ton is a chiller metric, and tons are capacity, not efficiency.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"COP","tier":0,"ref":"10 CFR 430 App. M1 §1.2","units":["hvac:a-cop"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0058","stem":"Under the federal standards effective January 1, 2023, what is the minimum HSPF2 for a split-system heat pump?","options":["7.5","8.2","6.7","6.1"],"expl":"The 2023 table sets split-system heat pumps at 14.3 SEER2 and 7.5 HSPF2. 8.2 was the old HSPF minimum before 2023, 6.7 is single-package heat pumps, and 6.1 is small-duct high-velocity. Mnemonic: split heat pump 14.3 and 7.5, split AC 13.4 up north.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Federal minimums","tier":1,"ref":"10 CFR 430.32(c)(5)","units":["hvac:a-min-hp-hspf2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0059","stem":"A system's SEER2 was rated at 0.50 in. w.c. external static, but the inspector measures 0.90 in. w.c. total external static across restrictive ducts. What should he tell the owner?","options":["Airflow and delivered efficiency are likely to fall below the rating","Efficiency rises, since more static improves coil contact","The rating likely holds, since SEER2 already covers duct static","Cooling holds, since the HSPF2 test absorbs the extra static"],"expl":"The rating assumes 0.50 in. w.c. for conventional ducted systems, so a duct system well above that cuts airflow or forces more fan power, and delivered capacity and efficiency fall below the label. Duct layout, sizing, and restrictions are why a high-SEER2 unit can still waste energy.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct static","tier":0,"ref":"10 CFR 430 App. M1 Table 4","units":["hvac:a-tesp-vs-rating"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0060","stem":"A CVI is scoping a house whose branch runs are UL 181 flexible duct with debris inside. How should his scope say the crew gains access to those runs?","options":["Disconnect each run at its ends and reattach it per the maker's instructions","Cut an opening in each run and seal it with UL 181B foil tape","Cut an opening in each run and close it with a gasketed panel","Fit a round spin-in access door into each run near its midpoint"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.2.9 says no service opening goes into flexible ductwork (SHALL), and 4.2.9.1 recommends following the manufacturer's instructions to disconnect and reattach it. UL 181B is the right closure listing for flex connections, but no closure makes a cut in flex acceptable.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Flexible duct","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2.9","units":["hvac:a-flex-no-openings"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0061","stem":"An inspector finds dry, intact fibrous glass duct board loaded with dust. The facility manager asks for a low-pressure water rinse. What should the scope specify?","options":["Dry mechanical cleaning, keeping water and cleaning agents off the board","Low-pressure rinse, with water extraction to catch the runoff","Wet cleaning with a pH-neutral agent, then a full dry-down period","Steam cleaning, since steam leaves no standing water in the duct"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.4 says cleaning agents and water never contact fibrous glass or other porous components, and 4.17 sends duct board to the Section 4.7 mechanical methods. Water extraction (4.6.1) and pH-neutral agents (4.11.4.2) belong to AHU and coil wet cleaning, not porous duct.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fibrous glass duct board","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.4, §4.17","units":["hvac:a-porous-dry-only"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0062","stem":"Two flexible duct submittals are labeled UL 181 Class 0 and UL 181 Class 1. What distinguishes the two classes?","options":["Class 0 has zero flame spread and smoke; Class 1 allows indexes up to 25/50","Class 0 allows 25/50; Class 1 has zero flame spread and smoke","Class 0 is listed as an air connector; Class 1 as an air duct","Class 0 passes UL 181B closure tests; Class 1 passes UL 181A tests"],"expl":"UL 181 Class 0 means surface burning characteristics of zero; Class 1 means flame spread index not over 25 and smoke developed index not over 50. Duct versus connector is a separate listing question, and UL 181A/B are closure standards, not duct classes. Mnemonic: Class 0 is zero, Class 1 is the 25/50 pair.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Flexible duct ratings","tier":0,"ref":"UL 181 (via DOE Building America)","units":["hvac:a-ul181-classes"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0063","stem":"At a post-cleaning inspection of internally lined duct, the CVI removes a new service panel. Which condition should he record as a deficiency?","options":["Cut liner edges left exposed to the airstream at the opening","Panel screws spaced 4 inches on center around the frame","Panel overlapping the duct surfaces by at least 1 inch on all sides","Replacement liner matching the original liner's thickness"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.2.8.1 requires that the opening and its closure leave no exposed fibrous glass edges in the airstream. The other three conditions comply: 4 in. maximum fastener spacing and 1 in. minimum overlap (4.2.5.2), and like material of the same thickness (4.2.8.2).","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Lined sheet metal duct","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8","units":["acr:4.2.8.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0064","stem":"A warehouse distributes air through fabric (textile) air dispersion duct that is visibly soiled. What cleaning method should the inspector's scope call for?","options":["Take the sections down and launder them as the maker directs","Pressure wash the fabric in place, capturing the runoff below","Cut service openings for brush access and close them with panels","Dry brush in place, then apply a UL 181 listed surface treatment"],"expl":"Fabric air dispersion duct (listed under UL 2518 and UL 723) is built to be taken down and machine washed per the manufacturer, not brushed or cut. Pressure washing is a Type 2 coil method (ACR 4.11.4), and UL 181 covers surface treatments (ACR 4.15.1), not textile duct.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fabric duct","tier":0,"ref":"Manufacturer instructions; UL 2518","units":["hvac:a-fabric-duct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0065","stem":"During a restaurant inspection, the CVI traces a duct rising from the kitchen's grease hood. Which construction does NFPA 96 require for that duct?","options":["Carbon steel of 16 MSG or stainless of 18 MSG, welded liquid-tight","Carbon steel of 18 MSG or stainless of 16 MSG, welded liquid-tight","PVC-coated steel with sealed joints, as used for corrosive exhaust","Spiral galvanized steel with slip joints sealed with UL 181A mastic"],"expl":"NFPA 96 requires grease duct of carbon steel at least 1.37 mm (No. 16 MSG) or stainless at least 1.09 mm (No. 18 MSG), with a liquid-tight continuous external weld on seams and joints. The gauges invert often: the heavier sheet (lower number) goes with carbon steel. PVC-coated steel is a lab exhaust material, and kitchen exhaust cleaning falls under ANSI/IKECA C10.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Grease duct","tier":1,"ref":"NFPA 96 §7.5","units":["hvac:a-grease-duct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0066","stem":"An inspection scope includes FRP fume hood exhaust ducts in a university lab. What should the CVI do before any of those ducts are opened?","options":["Get the chemical hazard data and consult a health and safety professional","Plan Method 2 as the final verification test, since FRP is a porous material","Specify service panels of equal gauge fastened at 4 inch centers","Specify a pH-neutral wash, with water extraction and runoff capture"],"expl":"FRP is chosen for corrosive fume exhaust, so residues may be hazardous. ACR 2025 1.8 recommends consulting a qualified IEP or health and safety professional when hazardous exposure is possible, and 3.5.1 sends government-defined hazardous materials to applicable law. Gauge-matched panels (4.2.5) are a sheet metal rule.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Lab exhaust duct","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.8, §3.5.1","units":["hvac:a-lab-exhaust-hazard"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0067","stem":"A crew cuts a service opening in fibrous glass duct board and closes it with foil tape. Which listing should the CVI look for on that tape?","options":["UL 181A, the closure standard for rigid ducts, including board","UL 181B, the closure standard for flexible ducts","UL 181, the standard for factory-made air ducts","UL 723, the flame spread and smoke test standard"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.2.5.4 requires closure tapes and mastics to meet UL 181A/B; for rigid duct board the listing is UL 181A (closure systems for rigid air ducts), while UL 181B covers flexible ducts and connectors. UL 181 lists the ducts themselves and covers surface treatments (4.15.1), and UL 723 sets the 25/50 ratings for panel materials (4.2.7).","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct board closures","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.4; UL 181A","units":["hvac:a-ul181-family","hvac:a-board-closures"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0068","stem":"A building has four duct types. On which one may the CVI specify the NADCA Vacuum Test as the verification method?","options":["Bare galvanized spiral duct in the supply","Sound attenuated duct section after the fan","Internally lined rectangular return duct","Fibrous glass duct board branches in the attic"],"expl":"Method 3 applies only to non-porous surfaces (ACR 2025 5.3.3, 5.4). Sound attenuated duct, duct liner, and duct board are all porous components under the ACR definitions, so Method 2 is the end of the escalation on them.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Porous duct types","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4; Definitions","units":["hvac:a-galvanized-nonporous","hvac:a-porous-vs-nonporous","hvac:a-porous-dry-only"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0069","stem":"A service opening is cut in sheet metal duct that carries external duct wrap. What must the CVI confirm about the wrap after the panel is closed?","options":["It is repaired with no break that degrades its R value or vapor barrier","It is left trimmed back 1 inch so the panel stays accessible","It is repaired on cooling ducts but left open on heating ducts","It is restored at the owner's option once the panel is closed"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.2.8.2, expanded in 2025 to include any disruption to external duct wrap, requires a repair with no break that degrades the R value, service rating, vapor barrier, or air barrier. The 1 inch figure is the service panel overlap, and 'owner's option' is the deferral trap.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct wrap","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","units":["hvac:a-wrap-repair"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0070","stem":"An inspector wants wet cleaning for baked-on residue in a bare galvanized exhaust duct. Which condition must the specified wet process meet?","options":["It keeps water and agents off electrical parts and porous materials","It uses non-corrosive detergent, then a chemical-free rinse and dry","It uses pH-neutral agents only, since ACR bars acid or alkaline ones","It follows a Method 3 test done on the duct before the wet process"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.4 permits wet process cleaning when it damages nothing and cleaning agents and water never contact electrical components or fibrous glass and other porous components. Non-corrosive detergent with a chemical-free rinse is the electric resistance coil rule (4.11.8), and pH neutral is only a coil recommendation (4.11.4.2).","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Bare sheet metal duct","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.4","units":["hvac:a-galvanized-nonporous"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0071","stem":"A lab exhaust duct serving hoods that use hydrochloric acid shows pitting. Which material would a lab design guide have favored for that duct?","options":["PVC-coated steel or FRP, which holds up to the vapor","Welded Type 316L stainless steel for the full run","Galvanized steel duct with an internal fibrous glass liner","Spiral aluminum duct with slip joints sealed by mastic"],"expl":"The UC Lab Safety Design Manual lists welded 316L stainless for many lab exhausts but calls it inappropriate where hydrochloric acid vapors are present (chloride attack); galvanized steel with a PVC coating inside and out, or UL-rated FRP, are its alternatives. Lined galvanized and aluminum are not on its list.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Lab exhaust duct","tier":1,"ref":"UC Lab Safety Design Manual, Ducting","units":["hvac:a-lab-exhaust-material"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0072","stem":"ASHRAE 52.2 builds a MERV from efficiency in three particle size ranges. Which range is E1?","options":["0.3 to 1.0 micron","1.0 to 3.0 micron","3.0 to 10.0 micron","0.3 micron only, as for HEPA"],"expl":"E1 is 0.3 to 1.0 micron, E2 is 1.0 to 3.0, and E3 is 3.0 to 10.0. A single 0.3 micron size is the HEPA test point (99.97%). Mnemonic: the break points run 0.3, 1, 3, 10.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"MERV size ranges","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 52.2","units":["hvac:a-e-ranges"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0073","stem":"A spec calls for MERV 13 filters. The installed filter's 52.2 report shows E1 42%, E2 86%, and E3 91%. What MERV does that report support?","options":["MERV 12, because E1 is under 50%","MERV 13, because E2 and E3 both qualify","MERV 11, because E1 is under the 50% mark","MERV 14, because E3 is over the 90% mark"],"expl":"A filter earns the highest MERV whose minimums it meets in all three ranges. MERV 13 needs E1 50%, E2 85%, E3 90%; MERV 12 needs 35/80/90, which 42/86/91 meets. Record it as below the MERV 13 spec. Mnemonic for MERV 11-12-13: E1 steps 20, 35, 50.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"MERV ratings","tier":1,"ref":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV table","units":["hvac:a-merv13"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0074","stem":"A 1 in. fiberglass panel filter's 52.2 report shows E3 efficiency of 12%. On what basis is its MERV assigned?","options":["Average arrestance, placing it in MERV 1 to 4","E3 efficiency alone, placing it at MERV 5","E1 efficiency, the basis used from MERV 11 to 16","Its MERV-A value after the conditioning step"],"expl":"Below 20% E3 efficiency, 52.2 falls back on average arrestance to assign MERV 1 through 4. MERV 5 starts at 20% E3, and E1 only enters the table at MERV 11. MERV-A is an optional added rating, not the base method.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Low-MERV filters","tier":1,"ref":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV table","units":["hvac:a-merv-low"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0075","stem":"A filter data sheet lists MERV 13 and MERV 11-A. What does the lower MERV-A value tell the inspector?","options":["Efficiency drops once media is conditioned by fine particles","Efficiency at final resistance is lower than when it was clean","Efficiency measured with arrestance dust, as done for MERV 1 to 4","Efficiency in the E3 range alone, ignoring the E1 and E2 ranges"],"expl":"ASHRAE 52.2 Appendix J is an optional conditioning step that reveals the efficiency loss some media show after exposure to fine particles; the result is reported as MERV-A beside the MERV. A wide gap warns that in-service capture may fall toward the MERV-A value.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"MERV-A","tier":1,"ref":"ASHRAE 52.2 Appendix J","units":["hvac:a-merv-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0076","stem":"A vendor offers a 'HEPA-type' filter rated 99% at 1 micron for a negative air machine that will exhaust indoors. Why does the CVI reject it?","options":["HEPA means 99.97% efficiency at 0.3 micron particles","HEPA means 99.97% efficiency at 1.0 micron and up","HEPA means 95% efficiency in E1, the same as MERV 16","HEPA means 90% efficiency in E3, the same as MERV 13"],"expl":"ACR 2025 3.3 requires vacuum equipment that exhausts indoors to use HEPA filtration at 99.97% collection efficiency at 0.3 micron, the DOE definition. MERV 16 requires only 95% in each range and MERV 13 only 90% in E3, so a MERV rating alone cannot show HEPA performance.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"HEPA","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.3; Definitions","units":["hvac:a-merv16","hvac:a-hepa","hvac:a-hepa-type"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0077","stem":"The AHU schedule calls for MERV 13, but the CVI finds 1 in. disposable fiberglass panels in the rack. Which MERV group do those panels typically fall in?","options":["MERV 1 to 4, far short of the schedule","MERV 5 to 8, well short of the schedule","MERV 9 to 12, still short of the schedule","MERV 13 to 16, meeting the schedule"],"expl":"Disposable fiberglass panels are MERV 1-4 filters on the 52.2 application charts; pleated media sits in 5-8, and rigid box, cartridge, and pocket filters fill 9-16. Record a filtration shortfall against the schedule.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filter types","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 52.2 application chart","units":["hvac:a-filter-families"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0078","stem":"A client asks the CVI whether to add an electronic air cleaner to the supply system. Which IAQ drawback should his recommendation address?","options":["It can produce ozone, a known lung irritant, as a by-product","It cannot capture particles, so it only inactivates the microbes","It adds far more pressure drop than a pleated media filter","It can remove gases only, like an activated carbon filter"],"expl":"EPA notes that electronic air cleaners use high voltage to create ionized fields and can produce ozone, either as a by-product or by design. Media filters, not electronic cleaners, carry the higher pressure drop, and inactivating microbes without capturing particles describes UV-C.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Electronic air cleaners","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Residential Air Cleaners, 2nd ed.","units":["hvac:a-electronic-cleaner"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0079","stem":"The UL 867 ozone test for electrostatic air cleaners sets the baseline California requires. What ozone concentration does it hold products to?","options":["0.050 ppm","0.005 ppm","0.070 ppm","0.10 ppm"],"expl":"UL 867 sets the 0.050 ppm (50 ppb) emission limit that CARB requires. 0.005 ppm is the UL 2998 zero-ozone threshold, 0.10 ppm is the OSHA 8-hour PEL, and 0.070 ppm is the EPA outdoor 8-hour standard. Since its 2020 amendments, CARB also covers in-duct air cleaners, which it tests to CSA C22.2 No. 187 instead of UL 867.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Ozone limits","tier":1,"ref":"UL 867; CARB air cleaner regulation","units":["hvac:a-ozone-limit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0080","stem":"A service firm says the UV-C lamps at the coil 'clean the air,' so the MERV 8 filters can come out. How should the CVI respond?","options":["UV-C inactivates living organisms at the coil, but it traps no dust particles","UV-C at 254 nm captures the particles a MERV 8 filter would stop","UV-C replaces MERV 8 filters if the lamps are changed once a year","UV-C replaces MERV 8 filters once the coil has been Type 2 cleaned"],"expl":"EPA states UVGI cleaners are not designed to remove particulate; they target microorganisms on surfaces such as coils and drain pans, usually with low-pressure mercury lamps at 253.7 nm. Pulling the filters would let dust load the coil and ducts, and ACR 1.7.1 keeps filters in every AHU inspection.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"UV-C","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Residential Air Cleaners, 2nd ed.","units":["hvac:a-uvc"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0081","stem":"A building has a persistent odor drawn in from a nearby process, and its MERV 13 filters are clean and sealed. What filtration addition would target the odor?","options":["A gas-phase filter using a sorbent such as activated carbon","An upgrade from MERV 13 to MERV 16 pleated or rigid box media","A UV-C lamp array aimed at the cooling coil and drain pan","An electrostatic precipitator mounted ahead of the coil"],"expl":"EPA notes particle filters cannot control gases and some odors; gas-phase filters use a sorbent, most commonly activated carbon, to adsorb them. Higher MERV, UV-C, and electrostatic precipitators all target particles or microbes.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Gas-phase filtration","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Residential Air Cleaners","units":["hvac:a-carbon"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0082","stem":"A gauge across a filter bank has risen from 0.25 to 0.90 in. w.c. over six months. The filter maker lists 1.0 in. w.c. as final resistance. What should the CVI record?","options":["Normal loading, close to but still below the change-out point","Air bypass at the rack, since bypass makes the reading climb","Past change-out, since the reading is more than triple initial","Filter damage, since readings over 0.5 in. w.c. mean torn media"],"expl":"Media filter pressure drop rises slowly over its life, and NAFA guidance is to replace at the manufacturer's final pressure drop, here 1.0 in. w.c. Bypass and torn media open an easier path and lower the reading, and 0.5 in. w.c. is a typical residential fan rating, not a filter limit.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filter pressure drop","tier":0,"ref":"NAFA best practice; EPA","units":["hvac:a-filter-dp-loading","hvac:a-filter-dp-falls"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0083","stem":"At a rooftop unit the filters are fairly clean, but one edge of the coil face is streaked with dust beside a 1 in. gap in the filter rack. What should the CVI document?","options":["Air bypass at the rack, which the AHU inspection has to include","Coil fouling only, since the filters in the rack are fairly clean","Normal edge loading that pleated filters are designed to allow","Condensate carryover, since streaks form where face velocity peaks"],"expl":"ACR 2025 1.7.1 says the AHU inspection shall include filters and air bypass. Unfiltered air slipping through a rack gap loads the coil even when the filters look fine, and NAFA calls for checking frames, gaskets, and caulking for leaks.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filter bypass","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","units":["hvac:a-bypass-acr"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0084","stem":"A traverse of a 24 in. by 12 in. duct averages 900 fpm. What airflow should the CVI record?","options":["1,800 cfm","450 cfm","2,700 cfm","3,600 cfm"],"expl":"cfm equals fpm times area in square feet: 24 x 12 = 288 sq in, divided by 144 = 2.0 sq ft, times 900 = 1,800 cfm. 450 divides by the area, 2,700 adds the sides (36 in. = 3 ft), and 3,600 squares the 24 in. side.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Airflow calculation","tier":0,"ref":"Q = V x A","units":["hvac:a-q-va","hvac:a-q-va-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0085","stem":"At one traverse point a pitot tube reads 1.10 in. w.c. total pressure and 0.85 in. w.c. static pressure. For standard air, what is the velocity there?","options":["Just over 2,000 fpm","Just over 1,000 fpm","Just under 3,700 fpm","About 4,200 fpm"],"expl":"Velocity pressure is total minus static: 1.10 - 0.85 = 0.25 in. w.c. Then V = 4005 x the square root of 0.25 = 4005 x 0.5, about 2,000 fpm. 1,000 skips the square root, and 3,700 and 4,200 plug in static or total pressure instead of velocity pressure.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Pitot measurement","tier":0,"ref":"V = 4005 x sqrt(VP)","units":["hvac:a-v-4005","hvac:a-v-4005-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0086","stem":"A CVI points a pitot tube upstream and connects its tip port and its side ports across one manometer. Which pressure does the manometer read?","options":["Velocity pressure, total minus static","Total pressure, static plus velocity","Static pressure, sensed at the side ports","External static, supply plus return"],"expl":"The tip (impact) port senses total pressure and the side ports sense static, so piping them across one manometer subtracts static from total and leaves velocity pressure. External static is a unit measurement taken at the supply and return plenums.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Pitot measurement","tier":0,"ref":"TSI-107","units":["hvac:a-pitot-ports"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0087","stem":"A belt-driven supply fan delivers 8,000 cfm at 900 RPM. A sheave change raises it to 1,080 RPM. What airflow should the CVI expect?","options":["About 9,600 cfm","About 11,500 cfm","About 13,800 cfm","About 6,700 cfm"],"expl":"Airflow varies directly with speed: 1,080 / 900 = 1.2, and 8,000 x 1.2 = 9,600 cfm. 11,500 applies the pressure law (1.2 squared), 13,800 the power law (1.2 cubed), and 6,700 inverts the ratio. Mnemonic: flow to the 1st, pressure to the 2nd, power to the 3rd.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan laws","tier":0,"ref":"Fan laws (DOE fan sourcebook)","units":["hvac:s-fan-law-flow"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0088","stem":"After cleaning, a balancer proposes speeding up a fan by 20%. Its motor now draws 5.0 bhp. About how much power will the fan need at the new speed?","options":["About 8.6 bhp","About 6.0 bhp","About 7.2 bhp","About 10.0 bhp"],"expl":"Power varies with the cube of speed: 1.2 x 1.2 x 1.2 = 1.728, and 5.0 x 1.728 is about 8.6 bhp, which can overload the existing motor. 6.0 uses the flow law, 7.2 the pressure law, and 10.0 doubles instead of cubing.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan laws","tier":0,"ref":"Fan laws (DOE fan sourcebook)","units":["hvac:s-fan-law-power"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0089","stem":"At a furnace the CVI reads +0.55 in. w.c. in the supply plenum and -0.35 in. w.c. in the return. The blower is rated for 0.50 in. w.c. What should he record?","options":["TESP of 0.90 in. w.c., well above the rating","TESP of 0.20 in. w.c., well within the rating","TESP of 0.55 in. w.c., slightly above the rating","TESP of 0.35 in. w.c., within the rating"],"expl":"Total external static is the sum of the supply and return readings with the signs ignored: 0.55 + 0.35 = 0.90 in. w.c., nearly double a 0.50 rating. Subtracting (0.20) or reading one side only are the usual mistakes. High TESP points to restrictions such as loaded filters or coils.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"External static pressure","tier":0,"ref":"TESP measurement practice","units":["hvac:a-tesp","hvac:a-tesp-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0090","stem":"A rooftop unit's nameplate lists 60,000 Btu/h of cooling. Using the 400 cfm per ton rule of thumb, what nominal airflow should the CVI expect?","options":["2,000 cfm","150 cfm","5,000 cfm","24,000 cfm"],"expl":"One ton is 12,000 Btu/h, so 60,000 / 12,000 = 5 tons, and 5 x 400 = 2,000 cfm. 150 divides the Btu/h straight by 400, 24,000 multiplies 60 by 400 as if the unit were 60 tons, and 5,000 divides by 12 and stops. ACCA guidance hosted by ENERGY STAR notes 350 or 450 cfm per ton can be just as valid, so 400 is a nominal figure.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Tonnage and airflow","tier":0,"ref":"12,000 Btu/h per ton; 400 cfm/ton","units":["hvac:s-ton","hvac:s-cfm-per-ton","hvac:a-tons-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0091","stem":"A coil moves 1,600 cfm with 78°F entering air and 58°F leaving air. Using the standard-air sensible heat formula, about how much sensible cooling is it doing?","options":["About 34,600 Btu/h","About 32,000 Btu/h","About 29,600 Btu/h","About 144,000 Btu/h"],"expl":"Sensible Btu/h = 1.08 x cfm x delta T = 1.08 x 1,600 x 20 = 34,560, about 2.9 tons. 32,000 drops the 1.08, 29,600 divides by it, and 144,000 uses 4.5, the total-heat constant that multiplies enthalpy change, not temperature. 1.08 comes from 0.075 lb/cu ft x 0.24 Btu/lb F x 60 min/h.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Sensible heat","tier":0,"ref":"Q = 1.08 x cfm x delta T","units":["hvac:a-sensible-108","hvac:a-sensible-example","hvac:a-heat-constants"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0092","stem":"Last summer a DX coil showed an 18°F split. Today, at similar conditions, it shows 26°F and the unit's static pressure has risen. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Reduced airflow from a loaded coil or dirty filter","A dirty condenser coil raising the head pressure","Excess airflow from a higher blower speed tap","More latent load from humid return air at the coil"],"expl":"From Q = 1.08 x cfm x delta T, a bigger split at a similar load means less air, and the higher static confirms a restriction such as coil or filter loading. A dirty condenser cuts capacity and does not raise indoor static, extra airflow shrinks the split, and extra latent load shifts capacity away from sensible.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Temperature split","tier":0,"ref":"Sensible heat relationship","units":["hvac:s-coil-delta-t","hvac:a-split-meaning"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0093","stem":"An uninsulated supply duct carrying 55°F air runs through a mechanical room whose air has a 62°F dew point. What should the CVI expect on the duct's outer surface?","options":["Condensation, since the surface is below the dew point","No condensation, since the surface is above the wet bulb","Condensation only if the room dry bulb exceeds 75°F","No condensation, since the room air is not at its dew point"],"expl":"Dew point is the temperature to which air must be cooled to reach saturation, so a surface colder than 62°F will sweat. Wet bulb sits at or above the dew point, so a 55°F surface is below it too, and room RH under 100% does not stop condensation on a colder surface. Record it as a moisture source.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Psychrometrics","tier":0,"ref":"NWS psychrometric definitions","units":["hvac:a-dew-point","hvac:a-condensation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0094","stem":"Through a humid summer the condensate pan under a running DX coil stays bone dry, with no leaks found anywhere. What does that tell the CVI about the coil's work?","options":["It is removing sensible heat but almost no moisture from the air","It is removing moisture but almost no sensible heat","It is removing moisture that a blocked drain is holding","It is removing moisture that leaves as vapor in the supply"],"expl":"Latent cooling happens only where the coil surface is below the air's dew point, and the removed moisture drains as condensate. A dry pan with no leaks means the coil surface is running above the dew point and doing nearly all sensible work. Total cooling is sensible plus latent (ACR definitions).","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Sensible and latent","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Definitions; NWS","units":["hvac:a-sensible-latent","hvac:a-latent-needs-dew"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0095","stem":"Why does a CVI take a multi-point pitot traverse instead of one centerline reading to find duct airflow?","options":["Velocity varies across the duct and is slower near the walls","Static pressure varies across the duct, higher near walls","A centerline reading gives total pressure, not velocity","A single point cannot be converted with the 4005 constant"],"expl":"Wall friction and upstream fittings make the velocity profile non-uniform, so readings on an equal-area or log-linear grid are averaged. Any single velocity pressure reading still converts with 4005 for standard air, and a centerline pitot still reads velocity pressure when piped tip to side ports.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Pitot traverse","tier":0,"ref":"Duct traverse practice","units":["hvac:a-traverse-why"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0096","stem":"A set follows National CAD Standard numbering, and its index lists M-001, M-101, M-301, M-501, and M-601. Which sheet should hold the AHU and VAV schedules?","options":["M-601","M-501","M-301","M-001"],"expl":"In the Uniform Drawing System the digit after the discipline letter is the sheet type: 0 general (legends, notes), 1 plans, 2 elevations, 3 sections, 4 large-scale views, 5 details, 6 schedules and diagrams. Mnemonic: 0 legend, 1 plan, 3 section, 5 detail, 6 schedule.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Sheet designations","tier":0,"ref":"NCS/UDS Module 1","units":["hvac:a-uds-sheet-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0097","stem":"On a mechanical plan the CVI finds an unfamiliar symbol where a duct crosses a rated wall. Where should he confirm what it means?","options":["The legend and notes on the M-0 series general sheet","The egress legend on the architectural life safety plan","The equipment rows on the M-6 series schedule sheet","The enlarged views on the M-5 series detail sheet"],"expl":"Symbols vary from office to office, so each set defines its own on the legend, which the Uniform Drawing System places on the 0-series general sheet. Schedules (6-series) and details (5-series) use the symbols but do not define them.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Legends and symbols","tier":0,"ref":"NCS/UDS Module 1","units":["hvac:a-legend"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0098","stem":"A rectangular supply duct on a mechanical plan is tagged 30x14. Under the usual convention, how does the CVI read that size?","options":["30 in. wide as seen in plan, 14 in. deep","14 in. wide as seen in plan, 30 in. deep","30 in. wide, with its bottom at 14 ft AFF","30 in. square main reducing to 14 in. square"],"expl":"Rectangular duct is tagged width first, then depth, so in plan view the first number is the side you see: 30x14 is 30 in. wide and 14 in. deep. Confirm the convention, and whether sizes are clear inside dimensions, on the set's legend.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct size notation","tier":0,"ref":"Drawing convention","units":["hvac:a-duct-size-notation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0099","stem":"The CVI needs the design airflow and external static pressure for AHU-2. Where in the drawing set should he find them?","options":["In the AHU schedule row tagged AHU-2","In the diffuser schedule for AHU-2 zones","In the size tags on the AHU-2 duct plan","In the VAV schedule for AHU-2's boxes"],"expl":"Each equipment tag on the plans points to a schedule row holding its design data, such as airflow, external static, capacity, filters, and electrical. Diffuser and VAV schedules list terminal airflows, and duct tags give sizes only.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Equipment schedules","tier":0,"ref":"Drawing practice","units":["hvac:a-equipment-schedules"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0100","stem":"A legend lists FD, SD, AD, and VD. Which tag marks a device that closes on heat at a rated wall and must be located during the inspection?","options":["FD","SD","AD","VD"],"expl":"FD is the fire damper, which closes on heat (a fusible link) at rated walls and floors. SD is a smoke damper closed by a detection signal, AD an access door, and VD usually a manual volume damper. Mark every FD on the plan so the scope covers access to it.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Damper symbols","tier":0,"ref":"Typical mechanical legend","units":["hvac:s-fire-damper","hvac:a-damper-tags","hvac:a-other-tags"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0101","stem":"The CVI needs to see how a supply duct drops through a shaft from a roof unit to the floors below. Which view type best shows that run?","options":["A section, a vertical cut taken through the building","A plan, a horizontal cut viewed from above","A detail, an enlarged view of one duct connection","A schedule, a table keyed to equipment tags"],"expl":"Plans are horizontal views and cannot show a vertical run through several floors; sections are vertical cuts that do. Details enlarge one connection and schedules are tables. In UDS numbering these are the 1, 3, 5, and 6 series.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Plans and sections","tier":0,"ref":"NCS/UDS Module 1","units":["hvac:a-plan-vs-section"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0102","stem":"On one set of plans some ducts are drawn with dashed lines. How should the CVI decide whether a dashed duct is return or exhaust?","options":["Check the legend for dashed lines and the RA or EA labels","Treat dashed lines as exhaust, per the NCS line conventions","Treat dashed lines as return, per the NCS line conventions","Check the AHU schedule for the dashed duct's cfm"],"expl":"Line types differ between firms: one guide draws return dashed and exhaust dot-dash, another draws both dashed. The set's own legend and the RA and EA labels at grilles settle it. The AHU schedule does not identify individual ducts.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Reading duct service","tier":0,"ref":"Drawing practice","units":["hvac:a-line-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0103","stem":"An owner says the mechanical plans exist but are in storage, and suggests the CVI inspect from a walk-through alone. What does ACR 2025 require?","options":["The plans be retrieved and kept in use throughout the inspection","The plans be used if the owner brings them to the site","The plans be used during cleaning, not during inspection","The plans be reviewed later, with the closeout documents"],"expl":"ACR 2025 1.3 says building floor plans and mechanical plans shall be in use during the inspection, and during cleaning and restoration, if they exist. It is a SHALL that does not wait on the owner's convenience. Plans in the closeout documentation (5.6.3) are the recommended part.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Plans during inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.3","units":["hvac:a-acr-plans"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0104","stem":"The plans show a return trunk as bare sheet metal, but the CVI finds it internally lined. What should his assessment do?","options":["Record what he found and scope the trunk as porous duct","Scope it as bare metal, since plans are the design record","Scope it as bare metal unless the owner asks otherwise","Stop the assessment until revised as-builts are issued"],"expl":"Plans inform the scope (ACR 2025 1.3), but the assessment documents and evaluates what the inspection finds (1.10). Liner is porous, which changes the wet cleaning limits (4.4) and the verification options (Method 3 is out), so the field condition controls.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Drawings versus field","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.3, §1.10","units":["hvac:a-field-controls"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0105","stem":"Which view type gives an enlarged look at one connection, such as the trap on an AHU condensate drain?","options":["A detail","A section","A plan","An elevation"],"expl":"Details (UDS 5-series) are large-scale drawings of one assembly such as a drain trap, a damper installation, or a duct support. Sections cut vertically through the building, plans are horizontal views, and elevations are vertical faces.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Detail drawings","tier":0,"ref":"NCS/UDS Module 1","units":["hvac:a-detail"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0106","stem":"The AHU schedule lists MERV 8 prefilters and MERV 14 final filters. At the unit the CVI finds only a MERV 8 bank. What should he record?","options":["Filtration below the design, with the final bank missing","Filtration matching the schedule, since MERV 8 is listed","Filtration acceptable, since the final bank is for startup","Filtration issue for the IEP to decide at a later visit"],"expl":"The schedule is the design basis, so a missing MERV 14 final bank is a documented deficiency; ACR 2025 1.7.1 includes filters in every AHU inspection and 1.10 requires the findings to be documented and evaluated. Deferring the finding to an IEP is not how the Standard treats inspection data.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Schedules versus field","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1, §1.10","units":["hvac:a-schedule-vs-field"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0107","stem":"A diffuser schedule lists CD-1 at 300 cfm with a 10 in. neck. With a flow hood the CVI measures 150 cfm at one CD-1. What should he record?","options":["Half the design flow; look for a restriction upstream","Within design, since the 10 in. neck size is correct","Below design, so resize the neck on the schedule to 8 in.","Within design, since schedules list maximum, not actual"],"expl":"The schedule gives design airflow, and a flow hood measures actual cfm at the outlet, so 150 of 300 cfm is half of design. Neck size does not prove airflow; look upstream for crushed flex, a closed damper, or loading. Resizing the design to fit a bad reading gets it backward.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Air device schedules","tier":0,"ref":"Drawing practice","units":["hvac:a-flow-hood","hvac:a-air-device-schedule"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0108","stem":"The pre-cleaning inspection finds light dust on a cooling coil face and suspect microbial growth in its drain pan. Which coil cleaning should the CVI specify?","options":["Type 2 wet cleaning, triggered by the drain pan condition alone","Type 1 dry cleaning first, then Type 2 if dirt remains","Type 1 dry cleaning, since the coil face has light dust","Type 2 wet cleaning, once an IEP confirms Condition 3 contamination"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.11.1.2 requires Type 2 when suspect microbial contamination appears on the coil or the drain pan; 'suspect' is enough, with no IEP confirmation needed. Type 1 first was the 2021 sequence and is no longer mandatory.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Coil cleaning type","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.2","units":["acr:4.11.1.2-a","acr:4.11.4-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0109","stem":"A coil is impacted deep in its fins with no sign of microbial growth. Under ACR 2025, may the CVI specify Type 2 cleaning without a Type 1 pass first?","options":["Yes, when his assessment finds Type 1 will not be sufficient","No, a Type 1 pass is required before any Type 2 cleaning","Yes, but only when the IEP approves the wet method first","No, unless suspect microbial growth appears on the coil"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.11.4 requires Type 2 when assessment determines Type 1 will not be sufficient, and the 2021 rule that Type 1 always comes first is gone. Suspect growth is a second trigger (4.11.1.2), not the only one.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Coil cleaning type","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","units":["acr:4.11.4-b","acr:4.11.4-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0110","stem":"A contractor plans to restart an AHU overnight and verify the cleaned ducts next week. What should the CVI tell him?","options":["Verify each component right after it is cleaned, before any restart","Verify a 10% sample of components within one week of the restart","Verify after restart, once the air handler has run for 24 hours","Verify at the IEP's post-remediation visit after the restart"],"expl":"ACR 2025 5.1 requires verification immediately after each component is cleaned and before the system operates, and 5.0 applies it to all specified components. The 10% figure belongs to the pre-cleaning inspection at 1.7, not to verification.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Verification timing","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.0, §5.1","units":["acr:5.1-a","acr:5.1-b","acr:5.0-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0111","stem":"After cleaning fibrous glass lined return duct, the owner disputes the Method 1 visual result. What should the CVI specify next?","options":["Method 2, the final step on porous surfaces such as liner","Method 3, the final word on porous and non-porous surfaces","Method 3, run on a wet-wiped section of the liner","Method 2, then Method 3 if the result is still disputed"],"expl":"When Method 1 is disputed, 5.2.1 recommends Method 2 or 3, but the NADCA Vacuum Test does not apply to porous components (5.3.3), so Method 2 ends the escalation on liner. Method 2 then Method 3 is the path for non-porous surfaces, and Method 3 needs a dry surface anyway.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Verifying porous surfaces","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.2.1, §5.3.3","units":["hvac:a-porous-dry-only"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0112","stem":"At post-cleaning inspection, a sheet metal duct has old paint overspray and mastic stains but no loose dust or debris. How should the CVI rate it under Method 1?","options":["Visibly clean, since adhered stains do not count against it under 5.2","Not clean, since the stains have to be fully removed to meet the standard","Not clean, since mastic has to come off before Method 1 applies","Visibly clean, if the owner accepts the stains in writing"],"expl":"Visibly clean means free of non-adhered substances (ACR 2025 5.2 and Definitions), and paint, mastic, sealant, and stains are the Standard's examples of adhered substances that Section 4 procedures do not remove. No owner sign-off is needed for adhered material.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Visibly clean","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.2; Definitions","units":["acr:D-9","acr:D-8"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0113","stem":"A lined duct has abraded, slightly friable liner, and the CVI plans to recommend resurfacing. In what order must the work occur?","options":["Mechanical cleaning first, then bond assessment, then resurfacing","Bond assessment, resurfacing, then mechanical cleaning","Resurfacing first, mechanical cleaning, then bond assessment","Mechanical cleaning, resurfacing, then bond assessment"],"expl":"Restoration follows mechanical cleaning (4.14), and 4.18.1 requires an assessment after proper cleaning that the surface gives a strong bondable surface before any resurfacing. If it will not bond, 4.18.2 bars resurfacing and 4.19 recommends replacement.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fibrous glass restoration","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.14, §4.18","units":["acr:4.18.1-b","acr:4.18.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0114","stem":"A sprinkler discharge soaked a lined supply duct system. What must happen to the water-damaged surfaces before cleaning methods are chosen?","options":["Evaluation and categorization by a method such as ANSI/IICRC S500","Remediation under ANSI/IICRC S520, since wet liner is Condition 3","Drying in place, then cleaning by the Section 4.7 mechanical methods","Sampling under ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 to classify the particulate"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.22 requires water-damaged surfaces to be evaluated and categorized by recognized methods such as S500, because the water category largely dictates the cleaning methods and engineering controls. S520 is the mold remediation standard and RIA 6001 is for soot and smoke questions.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Water-damaged components","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.22","units":["acr:4.22-a","acr:4.22-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0115","stem":"On a finished job the CVI finds a field-made service panel of 26-gauge sheet on 24-gauge duct, screwed at 6 in. centers. What should he record?","options":["Panel lighter than the duct and fasteners spaced too far apart","Panel lighter than the duct, but fastener spacing is compliant","Panel gauge compliant, but fasteners are spaced too far apart","Panel heavier than the duct, and fastener spacing is compliant"],"expl":"A higher gauge number is thinner sheet, so 26-gauge is lighter than 24-gauge, and ACR 2025 4.2.5.1 requires equal or heavier gauge. 4.2.5.2 requires screws or rivets at minimum every 4 in. on center, so 6 in. spacing fails too.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Service panels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5","units":["acr:4.2.5.1-a","acr:4.2.5.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0116","stem":"A closeout shows service panels screwed tight with no gasket or sealant, and the contractor cites the 2021 edition. How should the CVI rule under ACR 2025?","options":["Deficient; 2025 requires the seal that 2021 only recommended","Acceptable; sealing is recommended in 2025, as it was in 2021","Acceptable; sealing is required only on fibrous glass duct board","Acceptable; sealing is required only above 2 in. w.g. static"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.2.5.3 moved panel sealing (gaskets, sealants, mastic, or tape) from a 2021 recommendation to a SHALL, and 4.2.5.4 requires UL 181A/B tapes and mastics. No duct type or pressure trigger applies; 2 in. w.g. is the duct board pressure rating.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Service panels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.3","units":["acr:4.2.5.3-b","acr:4.2.5.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0117","stem":"The inspection finds brittle, crumbling duct board that cleaning would destroy. What does ACR 2025 require regarding those components?","options":["Document them, notify the customer, recommend repair or replacement","Clean them gently, record the damage, and have the project IEP review it","Document them and leave the repair or replacement call to the project IEP","Recommend resurfacing them, which ACR requires before any replacement"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.14.4 requires components that cannot withstand proper cleaning to be documented, the customer to be notified, and repair or replacement to be recommended. Resurfacing is only a MAY (4.18) and is barred on material that will not bond (4.18.2), and the IEP is not the decision point here.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Non-cleanable components","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.14.4","units":["acr:4.14.4-c","acr:4.14.4-a","acr:4.14.4-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0118","stem":"A contractor proposes an acid coil cleaner on an aluminum-fin coil whose maker's instructions prohibit acids. How should the CVI rule?","options":["Reject it; the manufacturer's guidance governs products","Allow it; ACR merely recommends pH-neutral coil cleaning products","Allow it, as long as the rinse water is captured and tested","Allow it, as long as the fins are straightened after the rinse"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.11.4.2 requires coil cleaning methods and products to conform to the coil manufacturer's recommendations where they exist and not to damage the coil or fins. The pH-neutral line is only a recommendation, which is why it cannot override a manufacturer prohibition.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Coil cleaning products","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.2","units":["acr:4.11.4.2-b","acr:4.11.4.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0119","stem":"A contract specifies Method 3 verification on all sheet metal ducts. After cleaning, the ducts pass Method 1 as visibly clean. Is the visual result enough?","options":["No; the method of verification the contract specifies still governs","Yes; visibly clean results end any further verification","Yes, provided a Method 2 comparison test also passes on site","No; ACR itself requires Method 3 on non-porous ducts"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.0 and 5.0 set visibly clean as the minimum, or the specified method of verification, so a contract that names Method 3 raises the bar. 'Visibly clean needs no further method' (5.2) is the default only when nothing more is specified, and ACR itself never makes Method 3 mandatory.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Specified verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.0, §5.0","units":["acr:5.0-e","acr:4.0-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0120","stem":"A property manager asks a CVI to write an ACR 2025 cleaning scope for an office building. Which requested task falls outside the Standard's scope?","options":["Preventive maintenance the OEM prescribes for the unit","Cleaning the return plenum built inside the ductwork","Inspecting the humidifier and the acoustic insulation","Cleaning the fan coil units and evaporative coolers"],"expl":"ACR 2025 excludes preventive maintenance the equipment manufacturer prescribes, along with electrical and pneumatic repair and non-ducted open air plenums. A return plenum built inside ductwork is in scope. Humidifiers and acoustic insulation are required AHU inspection items.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ACR scope","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 General (Scope); §1.7.1, §1.7.3, §1.7.5","units":["acr:G-9","acr:1.7.3-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0121","stem":"ACR 2025 rewrote its general compliance clause. The user now performs the work in strict accordance with which of these?","options":["Applicable codes, laws, and regulations","The Authority Having Jurisdiction's direction","The engineer of record's written specifications","The OEM's service manuals, labels, and bulletins"],"expl":"The 2025 edition points the user to applicable codes, laws, and regulations (a SHALL). The 2021 edition pointed to the Authority Having Jurisdiction here, and the 2025 Definitions no longer define AHJ, so the AHJ option is the previous-edition trap. ACR 2025 General, changed from 2021.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ACR 2021 to 2025","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 General (Δ)","units":["acr:G-14","acr:G-14a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0122","stem":"A CVI works in a state that licenses mold assessors. A client asks him to make preliminary Condition 1, 2, or 3 calls in an air handler. What does ACR 2025 require of him?","options":["Training, experience, and the license state law requires","A current CVI certificate, which ACR makes mandatory for this","An ASCS certificate, which ACR accepts in place of a state license","An IEP's written sign-off, which ACR accepts in place of a license"],"expl":"ACR 1.2 makes training and experience mandatory for a microbial inspector and requires a license wherever the law requires one to determine Conditions 1, 2, and 3. CVI or ASCS certification is only recommended (1.2-a), and no credential or sign-off replaces a license the law demands. ACR 2025 §1.2.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Inspector qualification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.2","units":["acr:1.2-g","acr:1.2-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0123","stem":"Under ACR 2025, what is the status of routine component inspections done as part of a building maintenance, energy, and IAQ plan?","options":["Recommended, per ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 and NFPA 90A","Required, per ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 and NFPA 90A","Recommended, per ANSI/ASHRAE/IES 90.1 and ASHRAE 62.1","Required, per ANSI/ASHRAE/IES 90.1 and ASHRAE 62.1"],"expl":"ACR 1.5 recommends routine inspections as part of the maintenance, energy, and IAQ plan, following ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 and NFPA 90A. Only the method is mandatory: routine cleanliness inspection is by visual means (1.5.1), and the Table 1 intervals are minimum recommendations. ACR 2025 §1.5, §1.5.1.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Routine inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.5","units":["acr:1.5-b","acr:1.5-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0124","stem":"Which statement correctly describes a change from the 2021 edition to ACR 2025?","options":["Method 2 now covers porous and non-porous surfaces","Level 2 no longer carries a 4 ACH air-cleaning rule","Service panel sealing dropped from shall to recommended","Type 1 dry cleaning must now come before any Type 2"],"expl":"The 2025 Section 5.3 extends the Surface Comparison Test to porous and non-porous surfaces (2021 limited it to porous). Each distractor inverts a real change: 4 ACH left the general ambient-air clause (3.6) but stays mandatory at Level 2 (3.10.7), panel sealing went from recommended to SHALL, and Type 1 is no longer required before Type 2. ACR 2025 §5.3, §3.6, §3.10.7, §4.2.5.3, §4.11.4.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ACR 2021 to 2025","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.3 (Δ)","units":["acr:5.3-c","acr:5.3-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0125","stem":"A general contractor asks what ACR 2025 calls for before a newly installed duct system that collected construction dust first operates. What does the Standard say?","options":["An inspection for cleanliness, recommended before startup","An inspection and verification as clean, required before startup","A Method 3 vacuum test on the new ducts, required before startup","An IEP clearance of the ductwork, recommended before startup"],"expl":"ACR 2025 1.6 recommends an inspection for cleanliness before a new or remodeled system operates. The 2021 edition required inspection and verification as clean, so that option is the previous-edition trap. ACR 2025 §1.6, changed from 2021.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ACR 2021 to 2025","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.6 (Δ)","units":["acr:1.6-b","acr:1.6-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0126","stem":"During an AHU inspection, a CVI sees suspect growth on water-stained drywall in the mechanical room. Which documents does ACR 2025 point to for that condition?","options":["ANSI/IICRC S520 and the applicable codes","ANSI/IICRC S500 and the EPA mold guide","ANSI/IICRC S590 and the EPA mold guide","ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 and building codes"],"expl":"Suspect growth or water damage on building products and furnishings is outside ACR scope, and ACR 1.9.2 sends it to ANSI/IICRC S520 (mold remediation) and applicable codes. S500 is water damage restoration, S590 is HVAC assessment after a loss, and 6001 is fire-residue evaluation. ACR 2025 §1.9.2.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Out-of-scope conditions","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.9.2","units":["acr:1.9.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0127","stem":"A proposed service-panel material that will touch the ducted airstream has an ASTM E84 flame-spread index of 20 and a smoke-developed index of 75. Does it meet ACR 2025?","options":["No, because its smoke-developed index exceeds the 50 limit of 4.2.7","Yes, because its smoke-developed index stays under the Class A 450 cap","No, because ACR accepts UL 723 test data but not ASTM E84 data","Yes, because ACR sets a flame-spread limit but no smoke limit"],"expl":"ACR 4.2.7 limits airstream door and panel materials to a flame-spread index of 25 and a smoke-developed index of 50, tested to UL 723, which is the same tunnel test as ASTM E84. The 450 smoke limit belongs to Class A interior finish ratings, not airstream materials. Mnemonic: 25/50, fire first, smoke doubles. ACR 2025 §4.2.7.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Surface burning 25/50","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7; UL 723 = ASTM E84","units":["ascs:surface-burning-tests","cvi:class-a-450"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0128","stem":"A specification calls for SMACNA Seal Class B on a return duct. What must the installer seal to meet it?","options":["All transverse joints and longitudinal seams","All transverse joints, seams, and wall penetrations","Transverse joints only, with seams left unsealed","Longitudinal seams and wall penetrations only"],"expl":"SMACNA Seal Class B covers all transverse joints and longitudinal seams. Class A adds duct wall penetrations, and Class C is transverse joints only. Mnemonic: A adds All openings, C is Crossways joints only, B sits Between. SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Standards, Table 1-2.","domain":"Standards","sub":"SMACNA seal classes","tier":0,"ref":"SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Stds, Table 1-2","units":["hvac:a-seal-class-a","hvac:a-seal-class-b","hvac:a-seal-class-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0129","stem":"A specification calls for pressurizing a 4 in. w.g. supply main and metering what escapes. Which SMACNA document gives the procedures, apparatus, and expected rates?","options":["HVAC Air Duct Leakage Test Manual, ANSI/SMACNA 016","HVAC Duct Construction Standards, ANSI/SMACNA 006","IAQ Guidelines for Occupied Buildings, ANSI/SMACNA 008","HVAC Systems Testing, Adjusting and Balancing manual"],"expl":"The HVAC Air Duct Leakage Test Manual (ANSI/SMACNA 016-2012) gives the test procedures, apparatus, setup, and expected leakage rates for ducts built to the construction standards. The Duct Construction Standards set gauge, reinforcement, and seal class, and they defer leakage testing to the Test Manual. SMACNA technical standards page.","domain":"Standards","sub":"SMACNA leakage testing","tier":0,"ref":"ANSI/SMACNA 016-2012","units":["cvi:smacna-016","hvac:a-leak-test"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0130","stem":"Patients remain in a hospital wing while the floor above is renovated. Which document organizes the contractor's plan into HVAC protection, source control, pathway interruption, housekeeping, and scheduling?","options":["SMACNA's IAQ Guidelines for Occupied Buildings","SMACNA's Duct Cleanliness for New Construction","ASHRAE 62.1, Section 7 construction-phase rules","NADCA ACR 2025, Section 3 engineering controls"],"expl":"SMACNA's IAQ Guidelines for Occupied Buildings Under Construction (2nd ed., ANSI/SMACNA 008) organizes construction IAQ into those five control measures, and LEED's construction IAQ credit points to it. The Duct Cleanliness guideline is the sibling SMACNA document with three cleanliness levels for new ductwork. SMACNA 008.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Construction IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"ANSI/SMACNA 008 (2nd ed. 2007)","units":["cvi:smacna-008"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0131","stem":"A submittal for a lined plenum cites ASTM C1071. What does that specification cover?","options":["Fibrous glass duct lining insulation","Surface burning characteristics testing","Closure systems for rigid air ducts","Factory-made air ducts and connectors"],"expl":"ASTM C1071 is the specification for fibrous glass insulation used as a thermal and sound-absorbing liner inside ducts, plenums, and air-handling equipment handling air up to 250 F. Surface burning is ASTM E84/UL 723, rigid-duct closures are UL 181A, and factory-made ducts are UL 181. ASTM C1071 scope.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASTM C1071 liner","tier":1,"ref":"ASTM C1071","units":["ascs:surface-burning-tests","cvi:astm-c1071","cvi:ul-181"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0132","stem":"A CVI finds fibrous glass duct board joints closed with cloth duct tape. For the system to keep its UL 181 Class 1 air duct rating, what must the closures be listed to?","options":["UL 181A, such as aluminum foil tape marked 181A-P","UL 181B, such as cloth tape marked 181B-FX","UL 723, such as foil tape rated 25/50","UL 181, such as tape marked Class 0"],"expl":"Duct board keeps its UL Class 1 air duct rating only when its joints use UL 181A listed closures (181A-P tape, 181A-H heat-activated tape, or 181A-M mastic). 181B-FX is the flexible duct closure listing, and UL 181 itself classifies the duct, not the tape. Mnemonic: A for rigid board, B for bendy flex. ACR 4.2.5.4 also requires UL 181A/B closures on service panels.","domain":"Standards","sub":"UL 181A closures","tier":0,"ref":"UL 181A; ACR 2025 §4.2.5.4","units":["cvi:ul-181","hvac:a-board-closures"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0133","stem":"A CVI is reviewing ventilation in a mid-rise apartment building. Which ASHRAE standard sets the ventilation requirements for its common corridors and lobby?","options":["ASHRAE 62.1, since corridors are outside dwelling units","ASHRAE 62.2, since the building is a residential use","ASHRAE 55, since corridors are occupied common spaces","ASHRAE 180, since common areas are owner-maintained"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1 applies to all spaces for human occupancy except those inside dwelling units with nontransient occupants, so corridors and lobbies fall under 62.1 while the apartments themselves go to 62.2. The trap is treating the whole residential building as 62.2. ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §2.1.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASHRAE 62.1 vs 62.2","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §2.1","units":["cvi:ashrae-621-scope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0134","stem":"Office tenants complain the space feels warm and drafty, though outdoor air rates meet design. Which ASHRAE standard defines what most occupants will find comfortable?","options":["ASHRAE 55, thermal environmental conditions","ASHRAE 62.1, ventilation and IAQ","ASHRAE 52.2, filter removal efficiency","ASHRAE/ACCA 180, HVAC system maintenance"],"expl":"ASHRAE 55, Thermal Environmental Conditions for Human Occupancy, defines the thermal conditions occupants find acceptable. ASHRAE 62.1 sets outdoor air and IAQ, which the stem says is already met. ASHRAE standards list.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASHRAE 55","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 55-2023","units":["cvi:ashrae-55","cvi:ashrae-180-role"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0135","stem":"A specification calls for MERV 13 final filters. Which document's test method produces the MERV number?","options":["ASHRAE 52.2, a test of removal efficiency by particle size","UL 900, combustibility and smoke generated by air filter units","ISO 16890, ePM1, ePM2.5, and ePM10 efficiency classes","ASHRAE 62.1, minimum filtration upstream of cooling coils"],"expl":"MERV comes from ASHRAE 52.2, Method of Testing General Ventilation Air-Cleaning Devices for Removal Efficiency by Particle Size. ISO 16890 rates filters in ePM classes, not MERV, and UL 900 is a fire test. ASHRAE 62.1 sets a minimum MERV but does not test for it.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 52.2-2025","units":["cvi:ashrae-522","cvi:ul-900","hvac:a-merv-def","hvac:a-merv-not-others"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0136","stem":"An office AHU has MERV 6 pleated filters just upstream of its chilled-water coil. What minimum does ASHRAE 62.1-2022 set for filters ahead of a wetted coil?","options":["MERV 8, or the minimum ISO ePM10 class","MERV 11, or the minimum ISO ePM2.5 class","MERV 13, or the minimum ISO ePM1 class","MERV 6, or the minimum ISO coarse class"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires MERV 8 (or the ISO ePM10 minimum) upstream of cooling coils and other wetted surfaces that supply occupied space, so MERV 6 is a finding. MERV 11 (ePM2.5) is the 62.1 outdoor-air rule where PM2.5 standards are exceeded, not the coil rule. ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.5, §6.1.4.2.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASHRAE 62.1 filtration","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.5","units":["cvi:621-merv11-pm25","hvac:a-merv8-floor"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0137","stem":"A CVI finds standing water in a flat condensate pan under a cooling coil. What minimum slope toward the drain outlet does ASHRAE 62.1 require for such pans?","options":["0.125 inch per foot","0.25 inch per foot","0.0625 inch per foot","0.5 inch per foot"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires pans that collect water to slope at least 0.125 in. per foot (1/8 in.) toward the outlet, or otherwise drain freely with the fan on or off. A flat pan holding water is a finding to record and a microbial risk. ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.7.1.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASHRAE 62.1 drain pans","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.7.1","units":["cvi:flat-pan-reset","hvac:s-pan-slope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0138","stem":"A facility manager asks how often ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 8-1 calls for checking filter pressure drop and replacement dates. What is the minimum frequency?","options":["Quarterly","Monthly","Semiannually","Annually"],"expl":"Table 8-1 sets a quarterly check of filter pressure drop and replacement date. Monthly is the cooling tower treatment interval, semiannually is for outdoor air louvers and bird screens, and annually is for drain pans, drain lines, and coils. ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 8-1.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASHRAE 62.1 maintenance","tier":1,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 Table 8-1","units":["cvi:621-t81-filters","cvi:621-t81-tower-louvers","cvi:621-t81-pans"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0139","stem":"To speed dust removal during drywall sanding, a general contractor wants to run the new AHU with its filters pulled. What does ASHRAE 62.1 say about this?","options":["It bars running a system designed with filters without them in place","It bars running any system until a TAB report is on file","It allows it if no occupied space shares that air handler","It allows it if the ducts are cleaned before the final system startup"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1 construction-phase rules forbid operating a system designed with particle filters unless the filters are in place. The start-up rule that the air distribution system be clean of dirt and debris (7.2.4) is an added requirement, not a waiver. ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §7.1.2, §7.2.4.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASHRAE 62.1 construction","tier":1,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §7.1.2","units":["cvi:621-no-filters"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0140","stem":"A hospital asks a CVI which standard's ventilation rates govern its operating rooms and inpatient areas. Which one is it?","options":["ASHRAE/ASHE 170, ventilation of health care facilities","ASHRAE 62.1, ventilation for acceptable IAQ","ASHRAE 241, control of infectious aerosols","ASHRAE/ACCA 180, HVAC inspection and maintenance"],"expl":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ASHE 170, Ventilation of Health Care Facilities, governs: 62.1 §6.2.1 requires 170's ventilation rates for occupancy categories within 170's scope, and its informative note says those rates target asepsis and odor migration control. ASHRAE 241 layers infection control on top of 62.1, 62.2, or 170 rather than replacing them. ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §6.2.1.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASHRAE 170","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE/ASHE 170-2025","units":["cvi:ashrae-170","cvi:ashrae-241","cvi:ashrae-180-role"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0141","stem":"A hospital needs a documented Legionella management program for its cooling towers and potable piping. Which ASHRAE standard sets the minimum risk management requirements?","options":["ASHRAE 188, building water systems","ASHRAE 241, infectious aerosol control","ASHRAE/ASHE 170, health care ventilation","ASHRAE/ACCA 180, HVAC maintenance practice"],"expl":"ASHRAE 188, Legionellosis: Risk Management for Building Water Systems, sets minimum legionellosis risk management requirements for centralized building water systems. ASHRAE 241 covers airborne infectious aerosols, not water systems. ASHRAE 188-2021.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASHRAE 188","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 188-2021","units":["cvi:ashrae-188","cvi:ashrae-180-role"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0142","stem":"After a respiratory outbreak, an owner wants a standard that sets equivalent clean airflow targets for an infection risk management mode. Which one is it?","options":["ASHRAE 241, infectious aerosol control","ASHRAE 62.1, ventilation and acceptable IAQ","ASHRAE 52.2, air-cleaner removal efficiency","ASHRAE/ASHE 170, health care facility ventilation"],"expl":"ASHRAE 241-2023, Control of Infectious Aerosols, defines equivalent clean airflow and an infection risk management mode. It does not replace 62.1: a building must still meet 62.1, 62.2, 170, or another AHJ-approved ventilation standard underneath it. ASHRAE 241-2023.","domain":"Standards","sub":"ASHRAE 241","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 241-2023","units":["cvi:ashrae-241"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0143","stem":"A CVI inspects the forced-air system in a detached single-family house. Which NFPA standard covers the installation of that system?","options":["NFPA 90B, warm air heating and air-conditioning","NFPA 90A, air-conditioning and ventilating systems","NFPA 96, commercial cooking ventilation control","NFPA 101, the Life Safety Code for occupancies"],"expl":"NFPA 90B covers warm air heating and air-conditioning systems in one- and two-family dwellings and spaces of 25,000 cu ft or less. NFPA 90A is its commercial sibling for larger spaces and taller Type III, IV, and V buildings. Mnemonic: B for the Bungalow. NFPA 90A/90B scope.","domain":"Standards","sub":"NFPA 90A vs 90B","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA 90B §1.3","units":["cvi:nfpa-90b-scope","cvi:nfpa-90a-scope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0144","stem":"Above what space volume does NFPA 90A, rather than NFPA 90B, apply to an air-conditioning system?","options":["25,000 cubic feet","15,000 cubic feet","2,000 cubic feet","50,000 cubic feet"],"expl":"NFPA 90A covers systems serving spaces over 25,000 cu ft, plus Type III, IV, and V buildings over three stories regardless of volume; 90B takes one- and two-family dwellings and spaces of 25,000 cu ft or less. The 2,000 and 15,000 figures are NFPA 90A airflow thresholds (cfm) for duct smoke detectors, not volumes. NFPA 90A §1.3.","domain":"Standards","sub":"NFPA 90A scope","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA 90A §1.3","units":["cvi:nfpa-90b-scope","cvi:nfpa-90a-scope","cvi:nfpa-90a-supply-det","cvi:nfpa-90a-return-det"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0145","stem":"Smoke dampers in a new office building were installed last spring. Under NFPA 105, when must they be tested and inspected?","options":["1 year after installation, then every 4 years","1 year after installation, then every 6 years","6 months after installation, then every year","2 years after installation, then every 5 years"],"expl":"NFPA 105 calls for smoke damper testing and inspection 1 year after installation and every 4 years after that. The 6-year cycle is the hospital exception, which does not apply to an office. NFPA 80 uses the same 1-then-4 pattern for fire dampers. NFPA 105 (2010 §6.5.2).","domain":"Standards","sub":"Smoke damper ITM","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA 105 (2010 §6.5.2)","units":["cvi:damper-hospital","cvi:sd-interval"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0146","stem":"A hospital facilities manager asks how often, after the first-year test, NFPA 80 lets the hospital test and inspect its fire dampers. What is the answer?","options":["Every six years","Every four years","Every year","Every five years"],"expl":"NFPA 80 requires the first test 1 year after installation, then every 4 years, except in hospitals, where the cycle is every 6 years. Four years is the rule for every other occupancy. Mnemonic: 1, then 4, hospitals 6. NFPA 80 Ch. 19 (2010 §19.4.1).","domain":"Standards","sub":"Fire damper ITM","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA 80 Ch. 19","units":["cvi:fd-interval","cvi:damper-hospital"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0147","stem":"A building has combination fire/smoke dampers at its rated corridor walls. Which standards govern their periodic inspection and testing?","options":["NFPA 80 and NFPA 105 together","NFPA 80 alone, as fire dampers","NFPA 105 alone, as smoke dampers","NFPA 90A alone, as duct devices"],"expl":"A combination fire/smoke damper is both a fire damper (NFPA 80) and a smoke damper (NFPA 105), so it must meet both. NFPA 90A is the document that sends fire dampers to NFPA 80 and smoke dampers to NFPA 105; it does not hold the test procedure itself. NFPA 80 and 105.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Combination dampers","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA 80 Ch. 19; NFPA 105","units":["cvi:damper-combination","cvi:damper-code-chain"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0148","stem":"A CVI watches a technician test a fusible-link fire damper for NFPA 80. Which procedure meets the standard?","options":["Release the link with the blades open and confirm full closure unaided","Command the damper closed from the panel and confirm the end switch","Check the fire alarm panel shows closed and log the status reading","Inspect the link and blades visually, then push the blades closed by hand"],"expl":"For a fusible-link damper, NFPA 80 has the link released with the damper fully open, and the damper must close completely on its own. Commanding closure from a panel is the test for dampers without fusible links and for smoke dampers, and a status light proves nothing.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Fire damper test method","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA 80-2019 §19.5.2.2, §19.5.2.3; NFPA 105","units":["cvi:damper-fusible-test","cvi:damper-command-test"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0149","stem":"A 24-hour diner runs charbroilers daily. Under NFPA 96, how often must its exhaust system be inspected for grease buildup?","options":["Every 3 months","Every month","Every 6 months","Every 12 months"],"expl":"NFPA 96 puts high-volume cooking (24-hour operation, charbroiling, wok cooking) on a quarterly inspection. Monthly is for solid-fuel cooking, semiannual for moderate volume, and annual for low volume such as churches, day camps, and seasonal businesses. Mnemonic: fuel monthly, heavy quarterly, normal twice, light once. NFPA 96-2024 Table 12.4 (Table 11.4 in 2021 and earlier editions).","domain":"Standards","sub":"NFPA 96 frequency","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA 96 Table 11.4","units":["hvac:a-nfpa96-frequency"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0150","stem":"A CVI is asked to check a restaurant's hood and exhaust duct for mechanical condition, structural integrity, fire safety, and cleanliness level. Which standard sets that inspection methodology?","options":["ANSI/IKECA I10","ANSI/IKECA C10","ANSI/IKECA M10","ANSI/IICRC S590"],"expl":"ANSI/IKECA I10 is the methodology for inspecting kitchen exhaust systems for mechanical condition, structural integrity, fire safety, and cleanliness. C10 is the cleaning methodology (the one ACR 2025 cites), M10 is end-user maintenance between cleanings, and ANSI/IICRC S590 assesses HVAC systems after water, fire, or mold damage, not kitchen exhaust. Mnemonic: I inspects, C cleans, M maintains.","domain":"Standards","sub":"IKECA standards","tier":0,"ref":"ANSI/IKECA I10","units":["hvac:a-ikeca"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0151","stem":"An air handler moves 5,000 cfm and serves a single floor. Where does NFPA 90A require its duct smoke detector?","options":["In the supply, downstream of filters and ahead of branches","In the return, upstream of filters and outdoor air inlets","At each story, before the connection to a common return","At the outdoor air intake, ahead of the economizer damper"],"expl":"At 5,000 cfm on one floor, only the NFPA 90A supply rule (over 2,000 cfm) applies: a detector downstream of the filters and ahead of any branch takeoff. The return-side location upstream of filters is the IMC 606.2.1 rule, and the at-each-story location is 90A's rule for returns over 15,000 cfm serving more than one story. NFPA 90A §6.4.2.1.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Duct smoke detectors","tier":1,"ref":"NFPA 90A §6.4.2.1; IMC 606.2.1","units":["cvi:nfpa-90a-supply-det","cvi:nfpa-90a-return-det","cvi:imc-return-det"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0152","stem":"A CMS surveyor cites a hospital for untested fire dampers under the Life Safety Code. In the chain of references from NFPA 101 to NFPA 80, which standard sits in between?","options":["NFPA 90A","NFPA 90B","NFPA 96","NFPA 72"],"expl":"NFPA 101 requires HVAC ductwork to comply with NFPA 90A, and NFPA 90A requires fire dampers to be maintained per NFPA 80 and smoke dampers per NFPA 105. NFPA 90B is the dwelling-scale sibling and NFPA 96 is commercial cooking. NFPA 101 (2012) §9.2.1; NFPA 90A (2012) §5.4.8.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Code chain","tier":1,"ref":"NFPA 101 §9.2.1; NFPA 90A §5.4.8","units":["cvi:damper-code-chain"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0153","stem":"A submittal for the dampers at a smoke barrier shows only a UL 555 listing. Which UL listing should the CVI expect those dampers to carry?","options":["UL 555S","UL 555C","UL 33","UL 900"],"expl":"UL 555S is the Standard for Smoke Dampers. UL 555 covers fire dampers, UL 555C covers ceiling dampers, UL 33 covers heat responsive links, and UL 900 covers air filter units. A combination damper carries both UL 555 and UL 555S. Mnemonic: S for Smoke, C for Ceiling.","domain":"Standards","sub":"UL damper listings","tier":0,"ref":"UL 555S","units":["cvi:ul-555-family"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0154","stem":"A duct penetrates a 2-hour fire-rated shaft wall. What fire protection rating does the fire damper at that penetration need?","options":["1½ hours, since the wall is rated under 3 hours","2 hours, since the damper matches the wall rating","3 hours, since the damper sits in the shaft wall","45 minutes, since the wall is under 3 hours"],"expl":"Fire dampers are rated 1½ hours for assemblies rated less than 3 hours and 3 hours for assemblies rated 3 hours or more. Matching the damper to the 2-hour wall is the trap. IBC 717 and IMC 607; UL 555.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Fire damper ratings","tier":0,"ref":"IBC 717 / IMC 607; UL 555","units":["cvi:damper-rating"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0155","stem":"A building's smoke control sequence keeps the supply fans running during a fire. What kind of fire damper must its ductwork use?","options":["A dynamic-rated damper that closes against airflow","A static-rated damper that closes after the fans shut down","A UL 555C ceiling radiation damper that closes on heat","A UL 555S leakage-rated smoke damper that closes on signal"],"expl":"Where fans keep running in a fire, the fire damper must be dynamic-rated, tested to close against the system's airflow and pressure and marked with its velocity and pressure limits. Static dampers are only for systems that shut the fans down automatically. UL 555; ICC CodeNotes.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Dynamic fire dampers","tier":0,"ref":"UL 555 (dynamic rating)","units":["cvi:ul-555-family","cvi:damper-dynamic"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0156","stem":"A filter specification requires units classified under UL 900. What property does that classification address?","options":["Combustibility and smoke generated by the filter","Particle removal efficiency across size ranges","Resistance to mold growth and to erosion","Leakage of the filter frame and housing seal"],"expl":"UL 900 determines the combustibility and the smoke generated by air filter units. Removal efficiency comes from ASHRAE 52.2, and mold-growth and erosion resistance come from UL 181 tests that ASHRAE 62.1 cites for airstream surfaces. UL 900.","domain":"Standards","sub":"UL 900 filters","tier":0,"ref":"UL 900","units":["cvi:ul-900","hvac:a-merv-not-others"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0157","stem":"A CVI finds a 20-foot run of flex labeled 'Listed Air Connector' feeding a diffuser. How should he record it?","options":["Deficient; connectors are limited to 14 feet in length","Compliant; flex air ducts have no length limit in the IMC","Deficient; connectors are limited to 8 feet in length","Compliant; connectors are allowed 25 feet if supported"],"expl":"A flexible air connector (round label, 'Listed Air Connector') is limited to 14 feet and cannot pass through walls, floors, or ceilings. A flexible air duct (rectangular 'Listed Air Duct' label) has no code length limit, which is the trap: read the label. IMC 603.6.2.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Flexible air connectors","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 603.6.2","units":["cvi:flex-connector"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0158","stem":"A CVI inspects a three-story townhouse that has its own means of egress. Which I-code normally governs its mechanical systems?","options":["The International Residential Code, per R101.2","The International Fire Code","The International Building Code","The International Energy Conservation Code"],"expl":"The IRC covers detached one- and two-family dwellings and townhouses up to three stories above grade plane with a separate means of egress, including their mechanical systems. The 2024 IMC 101.2 exception lets such buildings comply with the IMC or the IRC, but the IRC is their home code. A fourth story or shared egress moves the building to the IBC and IMC. IRC R101.2.","domain":"Standards","sub":"IRC vs IMC scope","tier":0,"ref":"IRC R101.2; IMC 101.2","units":["cvi:irc-scope","cvi:imc-irc-exception"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0159","stem":"A ceiling access panel serving a fire damper has no marking. What identification do the IMC and NFPA 90A call for?","options":["A permanent label reading FIRE DAMPER in letters at least 1/2 in. high","A permanent label reading FIRE DAMPER in letters at least 1/4 in. high","A permanent label reading ACCESS PANEL in letters at least 1/2 in. high","A permanent tag giving the UL 555 listing number and the test date"],"expl":"Damper access points must be permanently labeled FIRE DAMPER, SMOKE DAMPER, or FIRE/SMOKE DAMPER in letters at least 1/2 inch high. An unlabeled panel is a finding to record. IMC 607.4; NFPA 90A (2012) §4.3.5.2.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Damper access labels","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 607.4; NFPA 90A §4.3.5.2","units":["cvi:damper-label"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0160","stem":"A sewer backup floods a basement mechanical room and soaks the bottom of a return plenum. How does ANSI/IICRC S500 classify that water?","options":["Category 3, grossly contaminated","Category 2, significantly contaminated","Category 1, from a sanitary source","Class 4, specialty drying situation"],"expl":"S500 puts sewage and backflow from beyond the toilet trap in Category 3, grossly contaminated water that can carry pathogens. Category 2 is gray water with significant contamination, and Classes 1 to 4 rate drying difficulty, not contamination. ACR 4.22 sends flood-damaged components to S500 for evaluation and categorization.","domain":"Standards","sub":"IICRC S500 categories","tier":0,"ref":"ANSI/IICRC S500; ACR 2025 §4.22","units":["cvi:s500-categories"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0161","stem":"After a fire, a CVI must sample duct surfaces for soot and assess the whole HVAC system as restorable or not. Per ACR 2025's referenced documents, which pairing is correct?","options":["Soot sampling: ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001; system assessment: ANSI/IICRC S590","Soot sampling: ANSI/IICRC S590; system assessment: ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001","Soot sampling: ANSI/IICRC S520; system assessment: ANSI/IICRC S500","Soot sampling: ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001; system assessment: NIOSH Method 0500"],"expl":"ACR recommends ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 sampling when soot or smoke residue is in question, and it references ANSI/IICRC S590 (new in 2025) for assessing HVAC systems after water, fire, or mold damage. S520 is mold remediation, S500 is water damage, and NIOSH Method 0500 is the total nuisance dust analysis behind Method 3, not a system assessment. ACR 2025 §4.23.4; References.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Post-fire documents","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.23.4; ANSI/IICRC S590","units":["cvi:s590-purpose"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0162","stem":"Which condition does EPA's 'Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?' list as a reason to consider duct cleaning?","options":["Substantial visible mold growth on the inside of sheet metal ducts","A light, even layer of household dust on the inside of the supply ducts","More than two years passing since the ducts were last cleaned","A home sale that just closed, with a new family moving in soon"],"expl":"EPA lists three triggers: substantial visible mold growth inside hard-surface ducts, vermin infestation, and ducts clogged with debris or releasing particles into the home. EPA also says duct cleaning has never been shown to prevent health problems. The two-year figure is ACR's residential duct inspection interval, not a cleaning trigger. EPA 402-K-97-002.","domain":"Standards","sub":"EPA duct cleaning guide","tier":0,"ref":"EPA 402-K-97-002","units":["cvi:epa-duct-triggers","cvi:epa-duct-health"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0163","stem":"A contractor proposes spraying an antimicrobial inside fiberglass-lined ducts instead of cleaning them. What does EPA's 'Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?' state?","options":["No biocides are registered for fiber glass liner or duct board","Biocides are registered for fiber glass liner if applied before cleaning","Biocides are registered for liner if a NADCA member applies them","Biocides are registered for liner if the IEP approves the product"],"expl":"EPA's guide states that no products are registered as biocides for use on fiber glass duct board or fiber glass lined ducts, and that any biocide on other surfaces goes on only after proper cleaning. Spraying instead of cleaning inverts that order. EPA 402-K-97-002.","domain":"Standards","sub":"EPA on biocides","tier":0,"ref":"EPA 402-K-97-002","units":["cvi:epa-no-biocide-liner"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0164","stem":"An office tower's property manager wants the EPA guide, co-published with NIOSH, on preventing and resolving IAQ problems in commercial buildings. Which one is it?","options":["Building Air Quality: A Guide for Building Owners","Indoor Air Pollution: An Introduction for Health Professionals","Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings","Indoor Air Quality in Commercial and Institutional Buildings"],"expl":"Building Air Quality: A Guide for Building Owners and Facility Managers (EPA and NIOSH, 1991) covers IAQ profiles, management plans, and diagnosing problems in public and commercial buildings. The health professionals primer is EPA's joint work with the ALA, AMA, and CPSC for health care providers, the mold remediation guide covers mold alone, and Indoor Air Quality in Commercial and Institutional Buildings is an OSHA booklet (OSHA 3430). EPA 402-F-91-102.","domain":"Standards","sub":"EPA guidance documents","tier":0,"ref":"EPA 402-F-91-102","units":["cvi:epa-baq-guide"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0165","stem":"Which pollutant is generated inside the HVAC system itself rather than carried in from a source elsewhere?","options":["Microbial growth on a wet cooling coil and drain pan","Radon drawn through return duct seams in a crawlspace","Vehicle exhaust pulled in at a street-level air intake","Drywall dust drawn into a return during a renovation"],"expl":"EPA's Building Air Quality guide lists microbial growth in drip pans, humidifiers, ductwork, and coils as an HVAC-system source. Radon, vehicle exhaust, and construction dust come from outside the system; the system only distributes them, so the fix starts at their source and pathway, not in the ducts.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality, pollutant sources","units":["cvi:hvac-made-vs-carried","cvi:distributed-fix"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0166","stem":"A client's basement radon test reads 6 pCi/L, and the inspector finds open seams on a return trunk in the adjoining crawlspace. How should the report frame the HVAC system's role?","options":["Leaks in the return trunk may be drawing soil gas into the airstream","The reading is below EPA's action level, so the report needs no HVAC note","Cleaning the return trunk should bring the level under 4 pCi/L","The radon is outdoor air carried in through a fresh-air intake"],"expl":"EPA's action level is 4 pCi/L, so 6 is above it. Leaky return ducts in crawlspaces and under slabs collect and distribute soil gas, which is why Florida's radon program wants seamless ducts there; the system distributes radon, it does not make it, and duct cleaning does nothing for it. Record the seams and refer testing and mitigation to a radon professional.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"EPA radon action level; FL DOH radon-resistant construction","units":["cvi:distributed-fix","cvi:radon-action","cvi:radon-returns"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0167","stem":"During an AHU survey, which water-related component should the inspector flag as the strongest Legionella amplification concern?","options":["A heated pan humidifier with warm water left standing","A direct steam humidifier that holds no standing water","A drain pan that slopes to its outlet and holds no water","A closed chilled-water loop serving the cooling coil"],"expl":"OSHA notes that heated pan humidifiers let warm water stagnate and can support Legionella, while direct steam humidifiers normally run above 158°F and inhibit it. CDC says Legionella grows best at 77 to 113°F in stagnant, low-disinfectant water and spreads by aerosol, so a sealed loop and a pan that drains fully are low concern. Record the humidifier type, water condition, and upkeep.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA Legionnaires' disease control; CDC Legionella","units":["cvi:legionella-growth","cvi:humidifier-legionella"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0168","stem":"Soon after an in-duct electronic air cleaner is installed, occupants report a sharp odor and throat irritation near the supply outlets. What should the inspector record and recommend?","options":["Possible ozone byproduct; verify its low-ozone listing","Possible CO leak; shut down the air handler until it is vented","Possible refrigerant leak; have the coil pressure-tested at once","Possible biocide residue; flush the ducts with clean water now"],"expl":"EPA warns that electrostatic precipitators, ionizers, some UV lamps, and plasma cleaners can emit ozone, a lung irritant, and notes that CARB and AHAM list cleaners tested to emit little or no ozone. CO is odorless, so an odor complaint does not point to it, and flushing ducts with water conflicts with ACR 4.4.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Guide to Air Cleaners in the Home","units":["cvi:ozone-air-cleaners","cvi:co-odorless","hvac:a-electronic-cleaner","hvac:a-ozone-limit"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0169","stem":"Pigeon droppings are caked on an outdoor air intake screen and have spilled into the intake plenum. Which hazard should the inspector flag before anyone disturbs them?","options":["Histoplasma spores made airborne by disturbing dry droppings","Hantavirus particles that go airborne when dry nests are stirred up","Legionella bacteria that multiply in the dried droppings on the screen","Stachybotrys mycotoxins from black mold growing on the dry droppings"],"expl":"NIOSH ties histoplasmosis to Histoplasma capsulatum in material enriched by bird or bat droppings, names HVAC installers and service workers as at risk, and advises wetting the material instead of dry sweeping; it also describes a school outbreak spread through courtyard air intakes. Hantavirus comes from rodents, not birds, and Legionella needs warm water, not dry droppings.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"NIOSH Histoplasmosis: Protecting Workers at Risk","units":["cvi:histo-source","cvi:histo-wet","cvi:hanta-source"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0170","stem":"Inside a sheet metal return plenum, the inspector finds fresh mouse droppings and nesting material. What should the scope specify for handling that material?","options":["Wet it with disinfectant and let it soak before any removal","Dry-brush it loose, then capture it with a HEPA contact vacuum","Fog the plenum with an antimicrobial and leave the nest in place","Blow it out with compressed air toward a negative air machine"],"expl":"CDC says not to sweep or vacuum rodent urine, droppings, or nests because that puts virus-carrying droplets in the air; spray them with bleach solution or an EPA-registered disinfectant, let them soak about 5 minutes, then remove them. The trap is applying the normal ACR agitate-and-capture method to fresh droppings before they are wetted.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"CDC rodent clean-up guidance","units":["cvi:hanta-source","cvi:rodent-cleanup"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0171","stem":"Offices beside a mechanical room report an intermittent sewer odor. The room has a floor drain that rarely gets water, and its exhaust fan runs continuously. What should the inspector check first?","options":["Whether the drain trap has dried out and lost its liquid seal","Whether a plumbing vent sits within 10 ft of the air intake","Whether the condensate drain pan is overflowing into the room","Whether the fan discharge is re-entering at the roof level"],"expl":"A dried-out trap passes sewer gas into the building, and the North Dakota Department of Health notes the problem worsens when fans lower indoor pressure; the fix is to add water regularly or install a trap primer. A plumbing vent near an intake is a real sewer-gas path under ASHRAE 62.1, but that gas would arrive with outdoor air, not from a depressurized room with a dry drain.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"ND Dept of Health Sewer Gas Guide","units":["cvi:trap-seal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0172","stem":"Occupants find fine, glittering fibers on desks below several diffusers, and the inspector sees frayed, eroded liner in the supply trunk just downstream of the fan. The owner proposes better filters. What should the inspector tell the owner?","options":["The source lies past the filters, so filters will not stop it","A MERV 13 filter will catch the fibers before they reach the fan","The fibers are outdoor debris that slipped through filter rack gaps","The fibers are construction debris left in the supply trunk at startup"],"expl":"Eroded liner past the fan is an HVAC-sourced pollutant that no upstream filter can intercept. ACR lists deteriorated fibrous glass as a reason to clean, and porous material that cleaning cannot restore gets resurfacing (4.18, MAY) or replacement (4.19, REC). Record the location and extent, and recommend restoring or replacing the liner.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 N-10, 4.18, 4.19","units":["cvi:liner-past-filters"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0173","stem":"A client with a dust mite allergy asks whether duct cleaning will cut her exposure. Which statement is best supported by research on mite allergen?","options":["Most mite allergen sits in bedding and carpet and settles fast","Mite allergen stays airborne for hours and loads the return ducts","A MERV 8 return filter removes most mite allergen before it settles","Holding indoor RH near 65% dries out the mites and controls them"],"expl":"The Institute of Medicine's Indoor Allergens report found almost no airborne mite allergen in undisturbed rooms; it rides on 10 to 35 micron fecal particles that fall quickly after disturbance, and the reservoirs are mattresses, pillows, carpets, and upholstery. Mites depend on humidity, so the control target is below 50% RH, not 65%. Do not promise that duct cleaning will relieve a mite allergy.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"IOM, Indoor Allergens (dust mites)","units":["cvi:mite-reservoirs","cvi:mite-rh"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0174","stem":"A musty odor comes from the supply registers each time the system starts, yet no growth is visible in the accessible duct sections. What should the inspector do with this finding?","options":["Suspect hidden growth and inspect the coil, drain pan, and duct liner","Record the system as clean, since no growth is visible","Order an ozone treatment to neutralize the odor source","Attribute it to sewer gas and refill the floor drain traps"],"expl":"EPA says to suspect hidden mold when a building smells moldy but no source is visible; the coil, drain pan, liner, and humidifier are the usual places. ACR 1.9 recommends a preliminary Condition call and an IEP assessment once suspect growth turns up. EPA also finds ozone ineffective against mold at concentrations that meet health standards.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Mold Remediation, Ch. 3; ACR 2025 1.9","units":["cvi:hidden-mold"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0175","stem":"After a renovation with new particleboard casework and furniture, office occupants report eye and throat irritation. The AHU and ducts are clean and dry. How should the inspector describe the HVAC system's role?","options":["The system dilutes emissions from sources it does not contain","The clean ducts are still the likely source of the emissions","Standard particulate filters will capture the formaldehyde gas","Duct cleaning will remove the formaldehyde that settled inside"],"expl":"EPA lists composite wood such as particleboard, plywood, and MDF, plus glues, paints, and fabrics, as formaldehyde sources. Formaldehyde is a gas, so particulate filters and duct cleaning do not remove it; outdoor air dilutes it, and EPA calls source control usually the most effective fix.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"EPA formaldehyde facts; EPA Improving IAQ","units":["cvi:distributed-fix","cvi:formaldehyde"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0176","stem":"During a residential inspection, the inspector notes soot at the furnace draft hood and rust in the burner compartment. The owners mention headaches and fatigue that ease when they are away from home. What should the inspector do first?","options":["Advise a combustion safety check by a qualified tech at once","Schedule duct cleaning to remove soot from the supply trunk","Note it for review at next year's Table 1 air handler inspection","Refer the owners to an IEP for mold sampling in the air handler"],"expl":"Soot at the draft hood points to incomplete combustion or spillage, and EPA notes CO is colorless and odorless, comes from worn or poorly maintained combustion devices, and causes fatigue at low levels. This is life safety: send it to a qualified combustion tech now and record the finding without diagnosing anyone. Soot cleaning, deferral, and mold sampling all miss the hazard.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"EPA, CO and indoor air quality","units":["cvi:co-odorless","cvi:combustion-first"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0177","stem":"An inspector can open only one section of an air handler during the cooling season. Where is microbial amplification most likely?","options":["Damp porous liner just downstream of the cooling coil","The mixing box where return and outdoor air first combine","The dry filter bank upstream of the cooling coil in the return","The outdoor air louver with its bird screen and damper"],"expl":"Amplification needs water plus a food source. EPA lists coils, drip pans, humidifiers, and ductwork as microbial sources, and porous liner just past a cooling coil catches carryover and stays damp; EPA adds that wet or moldy duct insulation cannot be effectively cleaned and should be removed and replaced.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality; EPA duct cleaning guidance","units":["cvi:amplification-site","cvi:epa-wet-insulation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0178","stem":"A homeowner reports fine white dust settling on furniture in rooms served by a new humidifier. Which humidifier type and water supply best explain the finding?","options":["An ultrasonic unit filled with hard tap water","A steam vaporizer fed from a softened water line","An evaporative pad unit fed with hard city water","A bypass drum unit fed from the cold water line"],"expl":"EPA says ultrasonic and impeller (cool mist) humidifiers can disperse minerals and microorganisms from the tank into the air, which shows up as white dust, while steam vaporizers and evaporative units are not expected to disperse substantial minerals. EPA advises low-mineral water and not humidifying above 50% RH.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"EPA, Use and Care of Home Humidifiers","units":["cvi:ultrasonic-dust"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0179","stem":"How should an inspector classify indoor pesticide residues as an HVAC-related pollutant?","options":["Used indoors or tracked in on soil, then distributed","Generated by microbial growth on wet coils and drain pans","Emitted as a byproduct by ionizing air cleaners in the duct","Shed by aging duct liner as its resin binder breaks down"],"expl":"EPA puts about 80% of most people's pesticide exposure indoors, from products used inside and from contaminated soil or dust tracked or blown in; the HVAC system distributes these residues, it does not make them. The other options describe other pollutants: microbial growth, ozone from some ionizers, and fibers from deteriorated liner.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"EPA, Pesticides and indoor air quality","units":["cvi:hvac-made-vs-carried","cvi:ozone-air-cleaners","cvi:pesticides"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0180","stem":"On a rooftop survey, a plumbing vent terminates 1 ft above the level of an outdoor air intake. What minimum separation does the ASHRAE 62.1 intake table require?","options":["10 ft (3 m), since the vent ends less than 3 ft above the intake","3 ft (1 m), since the vent ends above the intake level","15 ft (5 m), since a plumbing vent counts as a combustion appliance flue","30 ft (10 m), since sewer gas counts as Class 4 exhaust air"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 5-1 (5.5.1 in older editions) gives plumbing vents two rows: 10 ft when the vent ends less than 3 ft above the intake and 3 ft when it ends at least 3 ft above. This vent is only 1 ft above, so 10 ft applies. Mnemonic: the vent earns the short 3 ft only when it is 3 ft up.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 5.5.1","units":["cvi:intake-plumbing-vent"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0181","stem":"A new AHU's outdoor air intake sits 18 ft from a dock where delivery trucks idle. What should the inspector record?","options":["Short of the 25 ft minimum ASHRAE 62.1 sets for truck loading areas","Clear of the 15 ft minimum ASHRAE 62.1 sets for truck loading areas","Clear of the 5 ft minimum ASHRAE 62.1 sets for truck loading areas","Short of the 30 ft minimum ASHRAE 62.1 sets for truck loading areas"],"expl":"Table 5.5.1 sets 25 ft for a truck loading area or dock and for bus parking or idling. 15 ft is the garage entry and automobile loading row, 5 ft is driveways, streets, and parking places, and 30 ft is a Class 4 exhaust outlet. Record the distance and recommend relocating the intake or controlling idling.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 5.5.1","units":["cvi:intake-truck","cvi:intake-garage-entry","cvi:intake-driveway","cvi:intake-exhaust-classes"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0182","stem":"A makeup air unit's intake sits near a cooling tower on the roof. Which pair of ASHRAE 62.1 minimum separations applies?","options":["15 ft from the tower intake or basin, 25 ft from its exhaust","25 ft from the tower intake or basin, 15 ft from its exhaust","10 ft from the tower intake or basin, 30 ft from its exhaust","5 ft from the tower intake or basin, 15 ft from its exhaust"],"expl":"Table 5.5.1 sets 15 ft from a cooling tower intake or basin and 25 ft from cooling tower exhaust. The exhaust gets more room because its plume carries drift, and CDC names cooling towers among the devices that spread Legionella through aerosolized water. The inverted pair is the trap.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":1,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1 Table 5.5.1","units":["cvi:legionella-growth","cvi:intake-cooling-tower"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0183","stem":"A rooftop intake sits 12 ft from the exhaust outlet serving a janitor closet and trash room. How does that distance compare with ASHRAE 62.1?","options":["Short of the 15 ft minimum, since it is Class 3 exhaust","Clear of the 10 ft minimum, since it is Class 2 exhaust","Short of the 30 ft minimum, since it is Class 4 exhaust","Clear of any minimum, since it counts as Class 1 air"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1's exhaust table rates janitor closets, trash rooms, and recycling as Class 3 air, and Table 5.5.1 requires 15 ft from a Class 3 outlet. Toilet exhaust is the Class 2 trap at 10 ft, and lab hoods and spray booths are Class 4 at 30 ft. Mnemonic: 10, 15, 30 for Classes 2, 3, 4.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":1,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1 Tables 5.5.1 and 6.5","units":["cvi:intake-exhaust-classes","cvi:air-class-examples"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0184","stem":"In a house with an attached garage, the furnace and a return duct with unsealed joints sit inside the garage. Occupants notice exhaust odor indoors after a car is started. What should the inspector record?","options":["Suction at the leaks is pulling garage air into the rooms","Pressurized supply ducts keep the garage exhaust from entering","The odor points to a cracked heat exchanger in the furnace","Garage exhaust is an outdoor source outside the inspection scope"],"expl":"Ducts and air handlers in a garage act as a conduit: return-side leaks under suction pull in car exhaust, fuel vapors, and chemicals, which is why DOE Building America guidance, aligned with EPA Indoor airPLUS, keeps them out of garages. The odor follows the car, not the furnace cycle, so the heat exchanger is the wrong lead. Record the leaks, recommend sealing, and flag CO as a life-safety concern.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"DOE Building America; EPA Indoor airPLUS","units":["cvi:garage-ducts"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0185","stem":"A home's return plenum sits in a damp crawlspace with a torn vapor barrier, and one side of the plenum is open. The owner notices a musty smell that is strongest while the system runs. What is the most likely pathway?","options":["Crawlspace air drawn into the return and supplied to rooms","Supply air leaking out and wetting the crawlspace framing","Coil condensate overflowing onto the torn vapor barrier","Attic air pulled down through wall cavities by stack effect"],"expl":"Air moves from higher to lower pressure through any opening (EPA Building Air Quality), and an open return in a crawlspace is the low-pressure point that pulls damp, spore-laden air into the supply. Florida's radon program makes the same point about soil gas: ducts in crawlspaces should be seamless. Record the opening and recommend sealing plus crawlspace moisture control; cleaning alone will not stop the odor.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality, pathways","units":["cvi:radon-returns","cvi:pressure-drives","cvi:crawlspace-return"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0186","stem":"In a 10-story office during a cold winter, exhaust odor from a ground-level parking garage shows up on upper floors near the elevator shaft. What best explains the migration?","options":["Stack effect draws garage air in low and carries it up the shaft","Positive pressure at the base pushes garage air up the shaft","Wind on the windward face drives garage air across each floor","Diffusion through the floor slabs carries the gas upward"],"expl":"EPA defines stack effect as flow driven by rising heated air, which puts positive pressure at the top of a building and negative pressure at the bottom. In winter the negative base draws garage air in and vertical shafts carry it up. The trap is the sign: the base is negative, not positive.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality glossary, stack effect","units":["cvi:stack-effect"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0187","stem":"A hotel in a hot, humid climate has mold behind vinyl wallcovering on exterior walls on every floor, and the building tests slightly negative to outdoors. Which explanation fits the finding?","options":["Humid outdoor air is pulled into the cool wall cavities and condenses","Humid room air is pushed out through the walls and condenses","Room air above 65% RH wets the wallcovering from the room side","Roof leaks run down the stud bays behind the wallcovering"],"expl":"EPA notes that in hot, humid climates even slight negative interior pressure can pull hot, moist outdoor air into chilled wall cavities, where vinyl wallcovering traps it at the cold surface, and that a slightly positive building helps. Pushing moist room air outward is the cold-climate version of the problem, so it is the trap, and a roof leak would not reach every floor. Recommend rebalancing toward slight positive pressure.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA IAQ Design Tools for Schools, Moisture Control","units":["cvi:hot-humid-negative"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0188","stem":"Restroom exhaust odor shows up in offices whenever the wind blows from the west. On the roof, the toilet exhaust fan discharges a few feet west of the AHU's outdoor air intake. Which term names this pathway?","options":["Re-entrainment","Short-circuiting","Stack effect","Infiltration"],"expl":"EPA defines re-entrainment as exhaust air brought right back in through the air intake or other envelope openings. Short-circuiting is supply air reaching an exhaust before the breathing zone, a room-level problem. Toilet exhaust is Class 2 air, which ASHRAE 62.1 keeps at least 10 ft from an intake.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality glossary; ASHRAE 62.1 Table 5.5.1","units":["cvi:intake-exhaust-classes","cvi:air-class-examples","cvi:reentrainment","cvi:short-circuiting"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0189","stem":"A conference room's supply diffuser sits 2 ft from a ceiling return grille, and CO2 climbs steadily during meetings. What does this layout most likely cause?","options":["Short-circuiting of supply air that bypasses the breathing zone","Re-entrainment of exhaust air at the outdoor air intake","Stack-driven infiltration through the room's exterior wall","Filter bypass that sends unfiltered air into the room"],"expl":"EPA defines short-circuiting as supply air flowing to exhaust registers before it enters the breathing zone, and a return grille 2 ft away does the same thing, so the room gets less effective ventilation and CO2 climbs. Re-entrainment is an exhaust-to-intake problem at the envelope, not a diffuser layout problem.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality glossary, short-circuiting","units":["cvi:short-circuiting"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0190","stem":"A tenant renovation removes ceiling tiles and sands drywall while the AHU runs, in a building that uses the ceiling cavity as an open return plenum. What should the inspector expect to find afterward?","options":["Dust carried through the plenum to the filters and the coil","Little change, since ACR excludes open plenums from scope","Little change, since return air is filtered before the coil","Dust mostly in supply ducts, since the plenum runs positive"],"expl":"An open ceiling return carries room and ceiling-cavity air straight back to the AHU, so renovation dust loads the filters and, through bypass or modest efficiency, the coil and ductwork; ACR 1.6 recommends a cleanliness inspection before the system runs after construction. ACR excluding open plenums from its cleaning scope says nothing about where the dust travels, and a return plenum runs negative.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 G-6, 1.6; EPA Building Air Quality App. B","units":["cvi:open-return-dust"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0191","stem":"A tight new house has a 600 cfm kitchen range hood, no makeup air, and a natural-draft gas water heater inside the living space. What distribution risk should the inspector note?","options":["Depressurization that could pull flue gases back down the vent","Pressurization that pushes cooking grease into the returns","Short-circuiting that pulls supply air straight to the hood","Stack effect that drives hood exhaust back down the duct"],"expl":"Depressurization can pull flue gases back down the water heater vent. The IRC requires makeup air for exhaust over 400 cfm when a natural-draft appliance sits inside the air barrier. Flue spillage is a CO hazard, so record the hood rating and the missing makeup air.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"IRC M1503.6 (2018 through 2024)","units":["cvi:makeup-air-400","cvi:depressurization-flue"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0192","stem":"Entry doors whistle and are hard to open, and the building automation trends show 4,000 cfm of outdoor air against 5,500 cfm of combined exhaust. What should the inspector recommend?","options":["Rebalance so outdoor air slightly exceeds exhaust and relief","Close the outdoor air damper further to limit the unfiltered air","Add a lobby exhaust fan so the entry draft is removed at its source","Match outdoor air to exhaust and relief exactly and seal the vestibule"],"expl":"EPA's Building Air Quality guide says the make-up air drawn in at the air handler should always be slightly greater than total relief and exhaust so the building does not run negative. At 4,000 in and 5,500 out, about 1,500 cfm of unfiltered, unconditioned air is coming through doors and cracks; cutting outdoor air or adding exhaust makes it worse, and exact balance leaves no positive margin.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality App. B, pressurization","units":["cvi:oa-exceeds-exhaust"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0193","stem":"Odors from a janitor closet and a copy room spread into adjoining offices that share the same return. What should the inspector recommend for those two rooms?","options":["Exhaust them outdoors so they stay negative to the offices","Return their air to the AHU at a higher rate for more dilution","Supply extra air to them so they stay positive to the offices","Add MERV 13 filters on their return grilles to catch the odors"],"expl":"EPA's Building Air Quality guide says air from rooms that produce significant odors and contaminants, such as copy rooms and bathrooms, should be exhausted outdoors, not recirculated. ASHRAE 62.1 sets exhaust rates for both rooms (janitor closets and trash rooms are Class 3 air), and exhausting them keeps them negative so air flows in, not out.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality; ASHRAE 62.1 Table 6.5","units":["cvi:air-class-examples","cvi:exhaust-odor-rooms"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0194","stem":"A solvent odor from a print shop travels through an open pipe chase to adjoining offices, but only when the shop's exhaust fan is off. What factor controls whether the odor reaches the offices?","options":["The pressure difference that drives the air along the open pathway","The MERV rating of the filters in the shop's air handler","The CO2 level measured in the offices during occupied hours","The total length of the pipe chase between the two spaces"],"expl":"EPA's Building Air Quality guide frames IAQ problems as four elements: a source, an HVAC system that cannot control it, a pathway, and occupants, with a driving force moving pollutants along the pathway because air moves from higher to lower pressure. With the shop exhaust running, the shop is negative and the chase flows toward it; with the fan off, the flow reverses.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality, four elements","units":["cvi:four-elements","cvi:pressure-drives"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0195","stem":"Under the ASHRAE 62.1 Ventilation Rate Procedure, breathing-zone outdoor airflow for an office combines which two components?","options":["A rate per person plus a rate per square foot","A rate per person plus an air change rate per hour","A fixture rate per toilet plus an air change rate per hour","A rate per square foot plus a CO2 setpoint in ppm"],"expl":"The ventilation rate procedure adds a people rate (Rp times occupants) and an area rate (Ra times floor area); for offices, 5 cfm per person and 0.06 cfm per square foot. Per-fixture rates are exhaust rates, and the Table 6-1 CO2 values are only DCV setpoints.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1 VRP; IMC Table 403.3.1.1; Addendum ab to 62.1-2022","units":["cvi:vrp"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0196","stem":"An inspector checks the filters ahead of a cooling coil against ASHRAE 62.1. What minimum rating has the standard required upstream of cooling coils and wetted surfaces since its 2013 edition?","options":["MERV 8, as rated by ASHRAE 52.2","MERV 6, as rated by ASHRAE 52.2","MERV 11, as rated by ASHRAE 52.2","MERV 13, as rated by ASHRAE 52.2"],"expl":"MERV 8, as rated by ASHRAE 52.2, has been the 62.1 minimum upstream of cooling coils since the 2013 edition; later editions also accept ISO ePM10. MERV 6 was the older value, MERV 11 is the outdoor air rule in PM2.5 areas, and MERV 13 is EPA's home suggestion.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2016 §5.8; 62.1-2022 §5.5; Addendum n to 62.1-2010","units":["cvi:621-merv6-old","cvi:621-merv11-pm25","hvac:a-merv8-floor"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0197","stem":"A rack of MERV 13 filters has 1-inch gaps along two sides, and the cooling coil face behind it is matted with dust. What should the inspector record and recommend?","options":["Bypass defeats the rating; seal gaps before coil cleaning","Loading is normal; upgrade to MERV 16 to capture the finer dust","Filters are too efficient; drop to MERV 8 to restore the airflow","Coil fouling is from the supply side; clean the diffusers first"],"expl":"EPA's Building Air Quality guide warns that filtration efficiency can be seriously reduced if filter cells are not sealed against bypass, and ACR 1.7.1 makes filters and air bypass part of every AHU inspection. Unfiltered air through the gaps loaded the coil; a higher MERV in a leaky rack changes nothing.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality App. B; ACR 2025 1.7.1","units":["cvi:filter-bypass","hvac:a-bypass","hvac:a-bypass-acr"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0198","stem":"Summer readings in a school show 68% RH in classrooms for weeks at a time. Which consequence should the inspector flag?","options":["Conditions that favor mold growth and thriving dust mites","Conditions that favor static shocks and dry, irritated eyes","Conditions that favor dry throats and itchy, flaking skin","Conditions that fall within EPA's target band for schools"],"expl":"EPA wants school RH below 60%, ideally 30 to 50%; above that, mold grows and dust mites thrive (mites need RH above about 50%). Static shocks, dry eyes, dry throats, and flaking skin are low-humidity problems. Record the readings and look for the cause, such as oversized equipment or excess humid outdoor air.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"EPA IAQ Design Tools for Schools","units":["cvi:mite-rh","cvi:rh-band","cvi:rh-low"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0199","stem":"Winter readings in an office show 15% RH, and occupants report dry, itchy skin and irritated eyes. How should the inspector interpret the readings?","options":["Below the 30% floor of EPA's band, which fits these complaints","Within EPA's band, since its 60% ceiling is what applies in winter","Below the band, which raises the risk of mold on cold surfaces","Below the band, which favors dust mite growth in the carpets"],"expl":"EPA's guidance holds RH below 60% and ideally 30 to 50%, and its Building Air Quality guide suggests keeping RH above 20 or 30% in the heating season. Office research links RH below 40% with more dry skin, throat, and eye complaints. Mold and mites are high-humidity problems, so they are the traps.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"EPA mold guidance; EPA Building Air Quality App. B","units":["cvi:rh-band","cvi:rh-low"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0200","stem":"Inspection confirms visible suspect growth throughout an AHU and its supply trunk in an occupied school, and remediation cannot start for two weeks. What should the inspector recommend in the meantime?","options":["Keep the HVAC system off and ventilate the space by other means","Run the fan continuously so the growth dries out until then","Order air sampling first to confirm that the growth is mold","Upgrade to MERV 13 filters and keep the system in service"],"expl":"EPA's remediation guide says not to run the HVAC system if you know or suspect it is contaminated with mold, and in most cases visible growth makes sampling unnecessary. A filter upstream cannot stop growth downstream of it, and running the fan spreads spores; it does not dry the growth.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Mold Remediation in Schools, Ch. 3","units":["cvi:epa-hvac-off","cvi:visible-no-sampling"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0201","stem":"An inspector finds standing water across a flat drain pan under a cooling coil, with slime in the corners. What should the inspector record and recommend?","options":["A pan that cannot drain fully; correct its slope before cleaning","Normal operating water; flush the drain line at the next service","Microbial growth; apply an antimicrobial and leave the pan as is","Coil carryover; lower the fan speed so the pan keeps up"],"expl":"EPA notes standing water builds up when a drain pan cannot drain completely and that molds and bacteria then proliferate; ASHRAE 62.1 requires pans sloped at least 1/8 in. per foot (0.125 in./ft) toward the outlet. Cleaning a flat pan only resets the clock, and ACR 4.11.4.1 requires a drainage test before and after cleaning.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1 drain pans; EPA Building Air Quality App. B","units":["cvi:flat-pan-reset","hvac:s-pan-slope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0202","stem":"Porous liner downstream of a cooling coil is soaked from repeated pan overflow and shows visible growth. What should the inspector recommend?","options":["Fix the water source and then replace the wet liner","Clean the liner in place with a HEPA vacuum and let it dry","Coat the liner with an encapsulant to seal the growth in","Spray an EPA-registered biocide on it and re-inspect later"],"expl":"EPA says wet or moldy duct insulation cannot be effectively cleaned and should be removed and replaced, that no chemical biocides are registered for internally insulated ducts, and that sealants are not recommended for encapsulating contaminants. ACR 4.16 recommends removal of porous material with Condition 3 growth. Without fixing the overflow, new liner gets wet again.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"EPA duct cleaning guidance; ACR 2025 4.16","units":["cvi:epa-no-biocide-liner","cvi:epa-wet-insulation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0203","stem":"Office workers report headaches, fatigue, and eye irritation that ease soon after they leave the building, and no physician has identified a specific illness or cause. Which term fits this pattern?","options":["Sick building syndrome","Building-related illness","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Legionnaires' disease"],"expl":"EPA describes sick building syndrome as acute health or comfort effects linked to time in a building, with relief soon after leaving and no specific illness or cause identified. Building-related illness needs a diagnosable illness whose cause can be traced to building contaminants, and patients may need prolonged recovery; hypersensitivity pneumonitis and Legionnaires' disease fit that BRI definition.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Indoor Air Facts No. 4","units":["cvi:sbs","cvi:bri"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0204","stem":"Conference room CO2 holds near 700 ppm during meetings, yet occupants still complain of a chemical odor from new carpet. What does the CO2 reading tell the inspector?","options":["Ventilation per person looks adequate, yet the VOC level is still unknown","VOC levels are low as well, since VOCs track with CO2 levels","The room is under-ventilated, since CO2 is well above 400 ppm","The carpet odor is CO2, which people can smell near 700 ppm"],"expl":"ASHRAE's position document says indoor CO2 is not an overall indicator of IAQ: it tracks occupant bioeffluents, and contaminants from building materials and outdoor air are not correlated with it. With outdoor air near 420 ppm, 700 ppm indoors points to reasonable ventilation per person, so the carpet emissions need source control or direct measurement.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE Position Document on Indoor CO2","units":["cvi:co2-not-iaq","hvac:a-co2-occupants","hvac:a-co2-700"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0205","stem":"An occupant claims that a 1,100 ppm CO2 reading means the building violates ASHRAE 62.1. Which statement is accurate?","options":["ASHRAE 62.1 sets no CO2 level that serves as an IAQ limit","ASHRAE 62.1 caps indoor CO2 at 1,000 ppm in occupied rooms","ASHRAE 62.1 limits indoor CO2 to 700 ppm above outdoor air","ASHRAE 62.1 limits indoor CO2 to 5,000 ppm over 8 hours"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1 sets no CO2 level that serves as an IAQ limit. The 2023 addendum added CO2-above-ambient values only as DCV setpoints. 700 ppm above outdoors was old informative odor guidance, 1,000 ppm is its shorthand, and 5,000 ppm is the OSHA 8-hour PEL.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"NIST (Persily 2020), Quit Blaming ASHRAE 62.1 for 1000 ppm CO2; Addendum ab to 62.1-2022","units":["cvi:co2-not-iaq","cvi:co2-no-limit","cvi:co2-700-5000","hvac:a-co2-no-limit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0206","stem":"Occupants call a space stuffy, and the inspector wants to separate thermal comfort from air quality. Which factor is NOT one of the six that ASHRAE 55 uses to define thermal comfort?","options":["Outdoor air ventilation rate","Mean radiant temperature","Clothing insulation level","Air speed at occupants"],"expl":"ASHRAE 55 uses six factors: metabolic rate, clothing insulation, air temperature, radiant temperature, air speed, and humidity, and it states that it does not address nonthermal factors such as air quality. Ventilation rate belongs to ASHRAE 62.1. Mnemonic: two personal factors (met and clo) and four environmental ones (air temperature, radiant temperature, air speed, humidity).","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 55, purpose and scope","units":["cvi:ashrae-55"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0207","stem":"An inspector wants to quote EPA accurately in a residential report. Which statement reflects EPA's published guidance on having air ducts cleaned?","options":["Duct cleaning has never been shown to prevent illness","Ducts need routine cleaning at the ACR Table 1 intervals","Registered biocides are approved for internally insulated ducts","Dirty ducts always raise indoor particle levels in homes"],"expl":"EPA states that duct cleaning has never been shown to actually prevent health problems, that studies do not conclusively show dirty ducts raise home particle levels, that ducts should be cleaned as needed rather than routinely, and that no chemical biocides are registered for internally insulated ducts. ACR Table 1 sets inspection intervals, not cleaning intervals.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","units":["cvi:epa-duct-health","cvi:epa-no-biocide-liner"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0208","stem":"A duct system just passed the NADCA Vacuum Test at 0.3 mg/100 cm². The owner asks the inspector to state in writing that the system is now mold-free. What should the inspector write?","options":["The Vacuum Test measures particulate, and it does not show microbial status","The system is mold-free, since it passed the 0.75 mg standard","The system is mold-free, since Method 3 is the final ACR word","The system is at Condition 1, since it is under 0.75 mg/100 cm²"],"expl":"ACR 5.0 says verification determines the presence of particulate and does not determine microbial contamination or hazardous materials, and Condition 1, 2, and 3 calls belong to a qualified IEP. The 0.75 mg/100 cm² line is a particulate pass criterion, and NADCA gives no warranty that the work corrects IAQ concerns.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 5.0, 5.5, G-17","units":["cvi:no-certify-claims"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0209","stem":"The inspector finds visible suspect growth on an AHU coil in a home where a child has asthma. The parents ask for a statement that the mold caused the asthma. What should the report say?","options":["The observed conditions and scope, leaving health questions to a doctor","That the visible growth likely caused the asthma and cleaning will fix it","That no link is possible, since EPA sets no federal limits for indoor mold","That the growth is harmless until air sampling shows elevated spore counts"],"expl":"The inspector documents conditions and recommends scope; causation is a medical judgment, and EPA advises consulting a health professional when occupants report serious health concerns. NADCA gives no warranty that the work corrects IAQ concerns. EPA having no federal mold limits means sampling cannot show compliance, not that growth is harmless or unrelated.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 G-17; EPA Mold Remediation, Ch. 3","units":["cvi:no-mold-limit","cvi:no-health-causation"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0210","stem":"A property manager asks a CVI why a formal inspection is needed before a duct cleaning job that 'obviously' needs doing. Under ACR 2025 Section 1.0, which set of determinations shall that inspection produce?","options":["The need to clean, the scope, and needed controls","The need to clean, the price, and the project schedule","The scope, the written work plan, and the SDS binder","The scope, the Condition level, and the project schedule"],"expl":"ACR 1.0 says the inspection shall determine the need for cleaning, the scope of work, and the engineering controls, safety measures, and equipment. The written work plan and SDS list are the contractor's Section 2 duties after the inspection, and a schedule is only a recommended work plan item (2.5).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection purpose","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.0","units":["acr:1.0-a","acr:1.0-b","acr:1.0-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0211","stem":"After a kitchen fire in a two-story home, an insurer hires a CVI to assess the HVAC system under ANSI/IICRC S590 before any restoration bid. What is the assessor's core call on each internal HVAC surface?","options":["Whether fire residue or smoke odor shows the surface was impacted","Which IICRC S500 water category, 1, 2, or 3, applies to it","Which IICRC S520 Condition, 1, 2, or 3, applies to it","Whether it holds over 0.75 mg/100 cm² of settled debris"],"expl":"S590 (first edition 2023) is an assessment standard: the assessor sorts internal surfaces into impacted and non-impacted, then writes a report and remediation work plan for the restorer. Water Categories come from S500 and Conditions 1 to 3 from S520, both restoration standards, and 0.75 mg/100 cm² is the ACR Method 3 pass limit.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Post-event assessment","tier":1,"ref":"ANSI/IICRC S590-2023; ACR 2025 references","units":["cvi:s590-purpose","cvi:s590-output"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0212","stem":"Ductwork in a new elementary school is installed while drywall sanding goes on nearby, and the owner asks what ACR 2025 says about a cleanliness inspection before first start-up. What does Section 1.6 say?","options":["A cleanliness inspection is recommended before it first runs","An inspection and a verified-clean result shall precede start-up","An inspection is required only if the owner reports dust","An inspection may be skipped when filters were in the units"],"expl":"ACR 2025 1.6 recommends an inspection for cleanliness before the system operates after construction. The 2021 edition required inspection AND verification as clean, which is the trap in the 'shall' option; 2025 dropped verification and softened the clause.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"New construction","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.6","units":["acr:1.6-b","acr:1.6-c"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0213","stem":"A lender hires a CVI for a due-diligence inspection of an office building's HVAC system before a sale closes. Which content does ACR 1.10 require the written assessment to include?","options":["A recommendation on the need for cleaning plus a clearly defined scope","A cleaning cost estimate and a remaining-service-life figure","A Method 3 result from each supply and return trunk line","A preliminary Condition rating for each air handler found"],"expl":"ACR 1.10 says the assessment shall document and evaluate findings and give a recommendation on the need for cleaning, a clearly defined scope, recommended techniques, required engineering controls, and unique requirements. A cost estimate is not on that list, Method 3 is optional when need is in question (N-17), and a preliminary Condition call is only recommended when suspect growth appears (1.9).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Due diligence","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.10","units":["acr:1.10-b","acr:1.10-c"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0214","stem":"During the client interview on an IAQ complaint, office staff say their headaches are worst first thing in the morning, especially Mondays, and ease by afternoon. Per the EPA Building Air Quality guide, which lead should the CVI follow first?","options":["Review the HVAC operating cycles and start-up schedule","Refer a suspected illness cluster to the state health agency","Check whether ventilation keeps up as the workday goes on","Look for a single spill or other one-time chemical release"],"expl":"EPA's timing table says symptoms worst at the start of the occupied period point to reviewing HVAC operating cycles, because emissions from materials or the HVAC system itself build up while it is off. Symptoms that worsen through the day point to ventilation, a single episode to a spill, and rare serious illness clusters go to the health department.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Client interview","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","units":["cvi:timing-start","cvi:timing-other"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0215","stem":"Musty-odor complaints come only from rooms served by AHU-3, while the rest of the building is fine. Which check does the EPA Building Air Quality guide suggest for a localized pattern like this?","options":["Local HVAC parts as pollutant sources or distributors","Ventilation and temperature control across the whole building","Drafts and radiant heat near the individuals complaining","Outdoor air quality at the intakes serving the building"],"expl":"For complaints localized to one zone or air handler, EPA says check ventilation and temperature control in that area, review nearby sources, and check local HVAC components that may be acting as sources or distributors of pollutants. Whole-building and outdoor air checks are EPA's advice for widespread complaints, and drafts are for single-person complaints.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Complaint history","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","units":["cvi:spatial-local","cvi:spatial-wide"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0216","stem":"In the pre-inspection interview, which piece of homeowner history, if the CVI's own inspection bears it out, would change what ACR 2025 says the CVI shall inspect on this visit?","options":["A tech saw suspect growth in the air handler last week","The supply and return ducts were last cleaned about two years ago","The attic ducts were sealed with mastic by the installer","The owners plan to put the house on the market next spring"],"expl":"Suspect microbial contamination in the air handler triggers ACR 1.9.1: once the CVI confirms it on this visit, the supply and return ducts shall be inspected during that same inspection, and this replaces the Table 1 interval. Table 1 sets recommended inspection intervals, not cleaning intervals, so a two-year-old cleaning changes nothing, and mastic sealing or a planned sale changes the context, not the ACR requirement.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Complaint history","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","units":["acr:1.9.1-a","acr:1.9.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0217","stem":"At a 1990s hospital wing, the facility engineer offers the CVI a set of mechanical drawings but warns they are probably out of date. What does ACR 1.3 require?","options":["Use them in the inspection and the cleaning","Set them aside until an engineer certifies them","Set them aside and map the system from the walk-through","Give them to the cleaning crew but inspect without them"],"expl":"ACR 1.3 says building floor plans and mechanical plans shall be used during the inspection if they exist, and also during the cleaning and restoration work. Old drawings still show equipment tags and duct routes; the inspector notes where the field differs rather than setting them aside, and nothing requires certification first.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Mechanical plans","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.3","units":["acr:1.3-b","acr:1.3-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0218","stem":"On the walk-through, the CVI finds two supply ducts, one warm and one cold, running to a box above each zone. Compared with a single-duct VAV building, what extra components fall under ACR's supply inspection here?","options":["Both deck ducts and the mixing box where they blend at each zone","Deck dampers at the unit that feed one duct to each zone","Parallel fan boxes that pull warm return air from the ceiling plenum","Reheat coils at each single-duct terminal on the floor"],"expl":"A dual-duct system blends hot-deck and cold-deck air in a mixing box at each zone, and ACR 1.7.2 lists mixing boxes in the supply inspection, so both runs and the boxes belong in scope. Deck dampers at the unit describe a multizone system, and reheat coils and fan boxes belong to single-duct VAV designs.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"System identification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.2","units":["hvac:s-dual-duct","acr:1.7.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0219","stem":"A CVI is inspecting a parallel fan-powered box whose fan sits idle in cooling mode. Which part should be checked because it keeps primary air from leaking back through the idle fan into the ceiling plenum?","options":["A backdraft damper at the fan's discharge","The induced-air filter at the plenum inlet","The inlet velocity sensor on the primary collar","The reheat coil at the box discharge outlet"],"expl":"Titus notes that parallel units must include a backdraft damper so primary air does not leak back through the blower into the ceiling plenum when the fan is off; a stuck-open or missing damper is an inspection finding. The induced-air filter is real (optional MERV 8 or 13 on Titus parallel units) but it filters plenum air and does not stop backflow.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Fan-powered boxes","tier":1,"ref":"Titus FPTU guidance","units":["hvac:s-parallel-backdraft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0220","stem":"A CVI must lift ceiling tiles to inspect return ducts above a hospital's oncology unit. Which pre-inspection step best meets ACR's duty to understand the inspection's effect on occupants?","options":["Coordinate an infection control risk assessment with hospital staff","Have the owner sign a waiver for any dust the inspection releases","Wear a powered air-purifying respirator for the whole inspection","Set up Level 4 containment with a constant recording pressure monitor"],"expl":"ACR 1.2.1 says the inspector shall understand the inspection's effect on the building and occupants before starting, and in a hospital that means working through the facility's infection control risk assessment (ICRA) with infection-control staff, as CDC guidance calls for before dust-generating work. A waiver shifts liability without protecting patients, a respirator protects only the inspector, and Level 4 is a cleaning containment step, not the pre-inspection assessment.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Risk assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.2.1; CDC EIC Guidelines","units":["cvi:icra"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0221","stem":"A school district wants its HVAC maintenance lead, who holds no NADCA certification, to do its inspections before cleaning jobs. What does ACR 1.2 set as the mandatory minimum for that person?","options":["Verifiable knowledge of HVAC design, practice, and standards","A current ASCS, CVI, or other NADCA certification in good standing","An IEP, CIH, CSP, or other health and safety credential on file","A current state HVAC, plumbing, or mechanical contractor license"],"expl":"ACR 1.2 makes verifiable working knowledge of basic HVAC design, fundamental engineering practice, current cleaning and restoration techniques, and applicable standards the mandatory minimum. Naming an ASCS or CVI is only a recommendation (1.2-a), so the certification option turns a recommended credential into a requirement.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspector qualifications","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.2","units":["acr:1.2-b","acr:1.2-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0222","stem":"A CVI working in a state that licenses mold assessors finds suspect growth on a cooling coil but holds no such license. How should the inspection report handle the Condition determination?","options":["Note the suspected mold and refer the Condition call","Assign Condition 3, since the growth is visible","Assign Condition 2 until lab results confirm growth","Leave it out, since only a licensed assessor may report it"],"expl":"ACR 1.2 says anyone determining Conditions 1, 2, and 3 shall hold a license where the law requires one, so an unlicensed CVI documents the suspect growth and refers the call to a licensed assessor or IEP. Leaving the finding out is the trap: documenting what was seen is still the inspector's job.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Licensing","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.2, §1.9","units":["acr:1.2-g"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0223","stem":"A CVI must open return ducts above occupied elementary classrooms. Which plan best fits ACR 1.2.1 and 1.4?","options":["Inspect when rooms are empty, with engineering controls in place","Inspect during class, since a visual inspection disturbs little dust","Inspect during class, with the inspector wearing an N95 respirator","Inspect when rooms are empty, so no engineering controls are needed"],"expl":"ACR 1.2.1 requires understanding the inspection's effect on occupants, and 1.4 says appropriate engineering controls shall manage the workspace during an inspection because disturbed settled particulate is the primary concern. Empty rooms reduce exposure but do not waive the controls, and a respirator protects the inspector, not the children.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Scheduling","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.2.1, §1.4","units":["acr:1.4-d","acr:1.2.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0224","stem":"A CVI plans to open a return duct access door right beside a duct smoke detector in an occupied office building. What should be arranged before the door is opened?","options":["Agreeing on detector handling with the owner and the alarm company","Removing the detector head so the dust cannot set off an alarm","Taping over the detector's sampling tube during the work","Moving the detector to a spot away from the access door"],"expl":"ACR treats detection equipment as life safety: 2.8 says the plan shall address life safety when detection equipment goes off-line and that this needs other parties' input, and 4.10 says the work shall not impair, alter, or damage detection equipment. Removing, taping, or moving the detector are all impairments the inspector has no authority to make.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Life-safety coordination","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.8, §4.10","units":["cvi:detector-coordination"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0225","stem":"A CVI wants numbers, not just photos, to show how much a loaded cooling coil restricts airflow before cleaning. Which reading gives that?","options":["Static pressure drop across the coil, read with a micromanometer","Relative humidity on each side of the coil, read with a hygrometer","Particle counts on each side of the coil, read with a laser counter","Moisture content of the coil fins, read with a pinless moisture meter"],"expl":"ACR 4.11.6 warns that visual observation of coils can mislead and recommends measuring static pressure drop before and after cleaning, and EPA's investigation guide lists a micromanometer for pressure differentials. Humidity, particle counts, and moisture content describe other conditions, not airflow resistance.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection instruments","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11.6","units":["cvi:micromanometer"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0226","stem":"An IAQ complaint says a conference room 'gets no air,' and the CVI wants an accurate supply airflow reading in cfm at its ceiling diffuser. Which tool does the EPA Building Air Quality guide name for accurate readings?","options":["A flow hood set squarely over the diffuser face","A vane anemometer held at the diffuser face","A micromanometer read across the room's doorway","A chemical smoke tube puffed at the diffuser"],"expl":"EPA lists an anemometer or velometer as a rough quantitative tool for diffuser airflow and a flow hood as the accurate quantitative tool. A micromanometer reads pressure differentials and chemical smoke shows airflow patterns, not cfm.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection instruments","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","units":["cvi:flow-hood"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0227","stem":"According to the EPA Building Air Quality guide, which instruments does an IAQ investigation generally use at a minimum?","options":["Heatless chemical smoke plus temperature and humidity meters","A carbon dioxide meter plus a logging hygrothermograph","An optical particle counter plus tracer gas measurement equipment","A flow hood plus a pitot tube traverse set for the ducts"],"expl":"EPA says IAQ investigations generally use, at a minimum, heatless chemical smoke devices and instruments for temperature and humidity; CO2 devices are called helpful for most investigations. Hygrothermographs, tracer gas, and particle instruments are tools professional consultants add as needed.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection instruments","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","units":["cvi:flow-hood","cvi:min-kit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0228","stem":"Downstream of a cooling coil, fibrous-glass duct liner looks darker than the surrounding liner. Which instrument lets the CVI confirm whether the liner itself is wet?","options":["A moisture meter pressed against or into the liner","A thermo-hygrometer placed in the air beside the liner","A manometer reading static pressure across the liner","An anemometer measuring air velocity past the liner"],"expl":"EPA describes moisture meters as direct-reading devices that measure moisture content in building materials by probe or surface contact. A thermo-hygrometer reads the air's temperature and RH, not the material, and a manometer or anemometer reads pressure or velocity.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection instruments","tier":0,"ref":"EPA mold remediation guide, Equipment","units":["cvi:moisture-meter"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0229","stem":"A 10-inch round sheet-metal supply branch above a hard ceiling has no access door. Which approach lets the CVI see inside it while staying within ACR?","options":["Feed a borescope camera in through the existing opening at the diffuser neck","Cut a small inspection port in the flex runout that feeds the diffuser","Blow compressed air through it and watch what comes out the diffuser","Cut a port in the round branch and seal it with cloth duct tape"],"expl":"An existing opening such as a diffuser neck lets a borescope see the branch with the least disturbance. A port in flex duct is prohibited by ACR 4.2.9, a cut port needs a service panel of equal or heavier gauge that is fastened, lapped, and sealed (4.2.5), so tape alone is not a closure, and blowing compressed air is agitation that releases the settled particulate ACR 1.4 is concerned about.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Access and openings","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2, §1.4","units":["cvi:borescope-neck"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0230","stem":"A CVI's scope for work next to a hospital surgical suite calls for Level 3 containment. What must the scope say about airborne particulate before the containment goes up?","options":["Take background readings of total particulate to set a baseline","Take readings once the containment is up and running, and not before","Log 4 air changes per hour on the air scrubbers in place of readings","Rely on the constant recording pressure monitor in place of readings"],"expl":"ACR 3.11.3 says Level 3 shall have background monitoring for total particulate before containment set-up to set a baseline, then real-time monitoring during the work, stopping work if levels exceed background. Monitoring during set-up is only recommended, 4 ACH is the Level 2 scrubbing rule, and the constant recording monitor is the Level 4 pressure alarm.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Particle monitoring","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","units":["acr:3.11.3-c","acr:3.11.3-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0231","stem":"Packing for an attic inspection, a CVI adds a respirator, coveralls, and gloves. Which statement about PPE matches ACR 2025?","options":["It names no PPE, which is not an engineering control","It lists PPE as the first engineering control","It requires PPE rated for Level 2 containment and up","It requires N95 or better PPE for return duct work"],"expl":"ACR 3.0 lists engineering controls such as source control, isolation, pressure differentials, HEPA filtration, and humidity control, and the Standard names no PPE anywhere; PPE selection comes from OSHA rules and the hazard present. Any option saying ACR requires a respirator is the trap.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Equipment kit","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.0","units":["acr:3.0-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0232","stem":"A CVI's video inspection robot was used yesterday in a return duct with suspect microbial growth. Before it goes into a hospital's ducts today, what does ACR require of that equipment?","options":["Cleaning and inspection before it enters the site","Cleaning and sealing after it leaves the hospital today","A wipe of the lens only, since the body stays outside","An on-site maintenance check, which is recommended"],"expl":"ACR 3.1.1 says equipment shall be cleaned and inspected before it enters the work site, and the 3.1 equipment list names robotic equipment, which covers an inspection robot. Cleaning or sealing before it leaves a building (3.1.3) is also required but does not replace the entry step, and the on-site maintenance verification in 3.1.4 is a recommendation for vacuum equipment.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Equipment kit","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.1.1","units":["acr:3.1.1-a","acr:3.1-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0233","stem":"A CVI will reach into an AHU fan section to photograph the blower wheel, touching nothing but the camera. Does OSHA's lockout/tagout standard apply to this inspection-only task?","options":["Yes, since OSHA lists inspecting as part of servicing work","No, since OSHA lockout covers repair or cleaning work","No, as long as a helper stays at the AHU's electrical disconnect","Yes, but just when the AHU has electric heat strips"],"expl":"OSHA 1910.147 covers servicing and maintenance where unexpected start-up could injure someone, and its definition of servicing lists inspecting alongside adjusting and maintaining. Reaching into a fan section is exactly that exposure, so a helper at the disconnect is no substitute for lockout.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspector safety","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147(a)(1), (b)","units":["cvi:loto-inspecting"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0234","stem":"A client's RFP uses 'inspection' and 'assessment' as if they meant the same thing. How does ACR 2025 define the difference?","options":["An inspection gathers information; an assessment evaluates the system","An inspection is a visual check; an assessment adds Method 3 lab testing","An inspection comes before cleaning; an assessment comes after the work","An inspection samples 10% of the system; an assessment covers each component"],"expl":"ACR defines an inspection as gathering information for determinations and assessments, and an assessment as a review and evaluation of the system or representative portions. The 10% figure is the minimum inspection sample (1.7), and inspections occur both before and after cleaning (1.1).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection purpose","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Definitions","units":["acr:D-85","acr:D-86"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0235","stem":"A data center supplies cold air through the space under its raised floor, with no ductwork below the tiles. How does ACR 2025 treat that underfloor space for inspection?","options":["As a 1.7.4 open air plenum, in scope only if contracted","As supply duct, inspected under 1.7.2 with the trunks","As a return plenum, inspected under 1.7.3 with the returns","As AHU casing, covered by the 1.7.1 AHU checklist"],"expl":"ACR defines an open air plenum as a non-ducted air pathway in building cavities and spaces, and 1.7.4 (new in 2025) says its inspection may be part of the contractual scope, with the NADCA white paper as recommended guidance. The white paper names raised-floor data center air as a positive supply plenum, so calling it supply duct under 1.7.2 is the trap.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Open air plenums","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.4; Definitions","units":["cvi:oap-raised-floor"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0236","stem":"After a nearby wildfire, a homeowner and the insurer disagree over whether fine dark dust inside the supply ducts is soot. What does ACR 2025 recommend to settle that question?","options":["Soot sampling per the IESO/RIA 6001 standard","Water categories per the ANSI/IICRC S500","Dust weight per NIOSH Method 0500 analysis","Condition rating per the ANSI/IICRC S520"],"expl":"ACR 4.23.4 recommends sampling per ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 when there is a question about soot or smoke on a component. NIOSH 0500 only weighs total dust for Method 3, S500 categorizes water, and S520 assigns microbial Conditions, so none of them identifies soot.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Post-event assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.23.4","units":["acr:4.23.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0237","stem":"A sewer backup floods a basement mechanical room, and the furnace and return plenum sat in the water. What does ACR 4.22 require for the water-damaged surfaces?","options":["Evaluation and categorization by a method such as ANSI/IICRC S500","Soot sampling per ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 before any cleaning is scoped","A Condition 3 rating once any floodwater touches them","Method 3 testing of the porous parts before they are cleaned"],"expl":"ACR 4.22 says water-damaged surfaces shall be evaluated and categorized by recognized methods such as ANSI/IICRC S500, because the water category largely dictates cleaning methods and controls. Method 3 never applies to porous components, and 6001 sampling is the recommended step for soot questions.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Post-event assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.22","units":["cvi:s500-categories"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0238","stem":"A client wants the CVI to use NADCA Vacuum Test results to prove the cleaned ducts are free of mold and asbestos. What does ACR 5.0 say about that request?","options":["Verification detects particulate, not mold or hazardous material","The vacuum test identifies mold if the lab cultures the cassette filter","Method 3 screens for asbestos fibers under NIOSH Method 0500 rules","Method 2 detects mold growth, while Method 3 detects hazardous material"],"expl":"ACR 5.0 says verification determines the presence of particulate and does not determine microbial contamination or hazardous materials. NIOSH 0500 is a total nuisance dust weighing method, so it cannot identify asbestos, and mold questions go to an IEP.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification purpose","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.0","units":["cvi:no-certify-claims"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0239","stem":"ACR 1.4 names two concerns when an inspection is done in an occupied building. Which pair, in order, does it give?","options":["Disturbing settled particulate, then its release to occupied areas","Releasing particulate into occupied areas, then exposure of the workers","Cross-contaminating the occupied areas, then chemical vapors and odors","Tripping smoke detectors in occupied areas, then building pressure loss"],"expl":"ACR 1.4 says inspection activities can influence the indoor environment: the primary concern is disturbance of settled particulate and the second is release of disturbed particles into occupied areas, so appropriate engineering controls shall manage the workspace. Worker exposure and chemical odors are real concerns handled elsewhere, not the pair 1.4 names.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection controls","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.4","units":["acr:1.4-b","acr:1.4-c"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0240","stem":"A routine, non-remediation inspection covers an office floor served by 60 VAV boxes. What is the fewest boxes ACR 1.7 allows the cleanliness inspection to include?","options":["Six boxes, meeting the 10% minimum","Three boxes, meeting a 5% minimum","Fifteen boxes, meeting a 25% minimum","Sixty boxes, meeting the S520 rule"],"expl":"ACR 1.7 says the cleanliness inspection shall include a minimum of 10% of the components under inspection, and VAV boxes are named components, so 10% of 60 is 6. All components are required only on an ANSI/IICRC S520 microbial remediation project, where that rule replaces the 10% floor.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Representative sampling","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7","units":["acr:1.7-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0241","stem":"Suspect growth turns up in several of an office floor's 60 VAV boxes, and the job becomes a microbial remediation under ANSI/IICRC S520. How many boxes shall the inspection now include?","options":["All sixty, since that rule replaces the 10% minimum","Six, plus each box within 10 feet of the growth","Twelve, since microbial work doubles the 10% minimum","Six, since the 10% minimum still governs"],"expl":"ACR 1.7 says an inspection for an ANSI/IICRC S520 microbial remediation project shall include all components, and that rule replaces the 10% minimum rather than adding to it. The '10% plus the suspect boxes' idea sounds prudent but is not what the Standard specifies.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Representative sampling","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7","units":["acr:1.7-d","acr:1.7-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0242","stem":"After cleaning an office floor's 60 VAV boxes, the owner suggests verifying only six of them, 'the same 10% you inspected.' What does ACR 2025 require?","options":["Verify every specified box; the 10% figure is for inspection","Verify six boxes, since 10% is the verification minimum too","Verify six by Method 1 and the other boxes by Method 2","Verify fifteen boxes, the 25% sample set for verification"],"expl":"ACR 5.0 says verification shall occur on all specified components, and the Standard notes that the 10% figure belongs to component inspection at 1.7, not to verification. Carrying the inspection sample over to verification is the classic trap.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Representative sampling","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.0, §1.7","units":["acr:5.0-d","acr:X-7"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0243","stem":"A CVI's air handler checklist from a former employer covers filters, coils, fans, and dampers. Which items on ACR 1.7.1's 'shall include' list is it missing?","options":["Acoustic insulation and general unit integrity","Reheat coils and fan-powered terminal units","Return plenums and dampers in the return ducts","Bathroom exhaust and heat recovery exhaust ducts"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.1 says the AHU inspection shall include filters and air bypass, heating and cooling coils, condensate pans and drain lines, humidification systems, acoustic insulation, fans and fan compartments, dampers and door gaskets, and general unit integrity. Reheat coils and terminal boxes are supply items (1.7.2), return plenums are return items (1.7.3), and exhaust is 1.7.5.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"AHU inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","units":["acr:1.7.1-e","acr:1.7.1-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0244","stem":"On a rooftop unit, the condensate drain line leaves the pan and runs down through the roof curb out of sight. How does ACR 1.7.1 treat that drain line?","options":["It shall be inspected as part of the air handling unit","It is recommended once water is seen standing in the pan","It shall be left to the plumbing trade under the code","It may be included when the contract lists it by name"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.1 says the air handler inspection shall include condensate pans and condensate drain lines, and a rooftop unit is an air-handling unit under the ACR definition. A hidden run means the CVI documents how drainage was checked and what could not be seen, not that the line drops out of scope.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"AHU inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","units":["acr:1.7.1-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0245","stem":"Which set of components does ACR 1.7.2 place in the supply air duct inspection?","options":["Ducts, mixing boxes, terminal boxes, and reheat coils","Ducts, humidifiers, condensate pans, and door gaskets","Ducts, general, bathroom, and heat recovery exhaust","Ducts, dampers, return plenums, and open air plenums"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.2 lists air ducts, mixing boxes, VAV boxes, fan-powered terminal boxes, and reheat coils in the supply inspection. Humidifiers, pans, and gaskets are AHU items (1.7.1), return plenums and dampers are return items (1.7.3), exhaust is 1.7.5, and open air plenums enter only by contract (1.7.4). Mnemonic: supply follows the air out to the boxes and their reheat.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Supply inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.2","units":["acr:1.7.2-a","acr:1.7.2-b","acr:1.7.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0246","stem":"A building has a sheet-metal return plenum, built to SMACNA and connected to the AHU, and also returns air through the open ceiling cavity above the offices. How does ACR 2025 treat the two for inspection?","options":["Ducted plenum is in; ceiling cavity per the contract","Both are inspected as return plenums under ACR 1.7.3","Neither is inspected, since plenums are outside ACR","Ceiling cavity is in; the ducted plenum is AHU casing"],"expl":"A return plenum within the ductwork is part of the return air inspection in ACR 1.7.3, while a non-ducted ceiling cavity is an open air plenum that 1.7.4 says may be part of the contractual scope. The Standard itself notes the two are different, so treating both the same way, in or out, is the trap.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Return and plenums","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.3, §1.7.4","units":["acr:1.7.3-c","acr:1.7.4-a","acr:1.7.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0247","stem":"A client adds the ceiling return cavity of a 1970s office building to the CVI's contract. Which document does ACR 1.7.4 recommend for guidance on that work?","options":["NADCA's white paper on open air plenums","ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180 on upkeep","SMACNA's duct cleanliness guidelines","ANSI/IICRC S590 on HVAC assessment"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.4 says open air plenum inspection may be part of the contractual scope and recommends the NADCA White Paper on open air plenums for guidance. That paper also warns that plenums in older buildings may hold asbestos and other hazardous materials, so the 1970s date matters. S590 covers post-event assessment and ASHRAE/ACCA 180 covers maintenance programs.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Open air plenums","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.4","units":["cvi:oap-asbestos"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0248","stem":"While inspecting an open ceiling return plenum, the CVI plans to lift tiles over a janitor's chemical storage closet that has its own exhaust. What does the NADCA open air plenum white paper stress about such areas?","options":["Keep the ceiling and walls intact where rooms are exhausted","Leave the tiles out so the closet exhausts into the plenum","Seal the closet's transfer grille to isolate the plenum","Treat the closet as a positive-pressure supply plenum"],"expl":"The white paper says removing ceiling tiles disrupts airflow patterns and that keeping the ceiling and adjacent walls intact is particularly important over areas designed to be exhausted, such as supply closets, bathrooms, and chemical storage. Opening the ceiling there can pull vapors into the return path.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Open air plenums","tier":1,"ref":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §IV","units":["cvi:oap-exhausted-rooms"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0249","stem":"A hotel's inspection request lists only the air handlers and supply ducts. What does ACR 1.7.5 say about the guest-bathroom exhaust risers and the energy recovery exhaust?","options":["They belong to the ventilation system and the overall inspection","They fall under ANSI/IKECA C10 as building exhaust systems","They are open air plenums, which join the inspection scope by contract","They fall outside ACR, which covers supply and return air"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.5 says general exhaust, bathroom exhaust, and heat recovery exhaust are part of the ventilation system and of the overall inspection, so a request that stops at supply leaves a gap the CVI should flag. IKECA C10 is the referenced method for commercial kitchen exhaust, not general or bathroom exhaust.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Exhaust inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.5","units":["hvac:a-exhaust-in-scope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0250","stem":"In an office building supply duct, the CVI finds a fire damper whose fusible link is painted over and whose blade track is packed with debris. What should the inspection record and recommend?","options":["Document it as unsafe and refer it for testing under NFPA 80","Clean the link and track per ACR 4.10, then note the repair","Note it only if the damper falls in the ACR 10% sample","Hold it for the next ACR Table 1 visit, one year out"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.6 says damaged or unsafe components seen during inspection shall be documented; it requires documentation, not repair. A damper that cannot close belongs in the owner's NFPA 80 program, which tests fire dampers one year after installation and then every four years (six in hospitals), with the link removed to prove full closure.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Fire dampers","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.6; NFPA 80 ch. 19","units":["cvi:fd-interval","cvi:damper-fusible-test","cvi:damper-unsafe"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0251","stem":"A hospital's facility director and an office tower's manager each say their fire dampers were last tested five years ago. Under NFPA 80, whose dampers are overdue?","options":["Only the office tower's; hospitals are allowed six years","Only the hospital's, since hospitals test every four years","Both, since dampers are retested yearly after the first test","Neither, since dampers are retested every six years anywhere"],"expl":"NFPA 80 (and NFPA 105 for smoke dampers) calls for a test and inspection one year after installation, then every four years, except hospitals, which are allowed six. Five years is past the four-year cycle but inside the hospital allowance. Mnemonic: 1, then 4, hospitals 6.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Fire dampers","tier":1,"ref":"NFPA 80 ch. 19; NFPA 105","units":["cvi:fd-interval","cvi:damper-hospital","cvi:sd-interval"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0252","stem":"In a middle school air handler, the CVI sees dark spotting on the leaving face of the cooling coil and in the drain pan. What does ACR 1.9 recommend next?","options":["A preliminary Condition call and an IEP to assess cause and extent","A NADCA Method 3 vacuum test to confirm the spotting is microbial growth","An EPA-registered antimicrobial fog to halt the spread before the report","An ANSI/IICRC S520 remediation scope written by the CVI that same day"],"expl":"ACR 1.9 recommends a preliminary determination of Condition 1, 2, or 3 when suspect microbial contamination appears, with an IEP or other appropriate professional assessing cause and extent; 1.9.1 then requires the supply and return ducts to be inspected on the same visit. Method 3 cannot identify microbial growth (5.0), and chemicals never come before source removal.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Suspect microbial","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.9","units":["acr:1.9-a","acr:1.9-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0253","stem":"During a routine home inspection, the CVI finds suspect growth in the air handler. The supply and return ducts were last inspected eight months ago. What does ACR require for those ducts?","options":["Inspect both on this visit, whatever the table would allow","Inspect both at the next two-year residential interval","Inspect the return now and the supply at the two-year mark","Inspect both once an IEP has confirmed Condition 3 growth"],"expl":"ACR 1.9.1 says suspect microbial contamination in the AHU components shall trigger inspection of the supply and return ducts during that same inspection, and this replaces the Table 1 interval for that system. Waiting for the recommended two-year residential interval or for an IEP's confirmation defers a mandatory step.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Suspect microbial","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","units":["acr:1.9.1-b","acr:1.9.1-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0254","stem":"While inspecting the HVAC system in a flooded daycare, the CVI also sees suspect growth on the drywall behind a return grille. How does ACR 2025 treat the drywall?","options":["Outside ACR's scope; IICRC S520 and applicable codes apply","Inside its scope, since it forms part of the return air path","Inside its scope under the 1.9.1 same-visit duct trigger","Outside its scope; S500 alone governs since water caused it"],"expl":"ACR 1.9.2 says water damage or suspect growth on building products or furnishings is outside the Standard's scope and recommends ANSI/IICRC S520 and applicable codes for that condition. The 1.9.1 trigger concerns growth in the air handler, and S500 governs water damage restoration, not the mold now on the wall.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Suspect microbial","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.9.2","units":["acr:1.9.2-a","acr:1.9.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0255","stem":"In a 1972 office building, pipe and duct insulation in the mechanical room looks like aged thermal system insulation with a canvas jacket. Before anyone disturbs it, what does ACR 1.8 recommend?","options":["Consulting a qualified IEP or a health and safety professional","Consulting a state-licensed mold assessor under IICRC S520 rules","Sampling it with a NADCA vacuum test cassette for fibers","Sealing it with a UL 181 surface treatment before any work starts"],"expl":"ACR 1.8 recommends consulting a qualified IEP, or a health and safety professional as the alternative, when exposure to hazardous materials is possible; it is a recommendation, not a requirement. OSHA separately presumes thermal system insulation in buildings built no later than 1980 to contain asbestos, which is why the date matters. Method 3 weighs dust and cannot identify fibers, and UL 181 covers duct surface treatments.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Hazardous materials","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.8; OSHA 1926.1101(b)","units":["cvi:pacm"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0256","stem":"A CVI pulls a filter bank and finds 1-inch gaps at the rack edges, with dust streaks on the coil face lined up with the gaps. How should the report record this?","options":["As filter bypass, which ACR 1.7.1 says the AHU inspection shall include","As a Condition 2 finding, since dust settled from elsewhere","As duct leakage, which the ACR restoration scope shall seal","As a MERV problem, since a higher MERV filter would stop the dust"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.1 says the AHU inspection shall include filters and air bypass, and streaks in line with rack gaps show unfiltered air loading the coil. ACR restoration expressly excludes sealing air leaks in duct systems (4.24.1), Condition 2 describes settled fungal spores rather than dust, and a higher MERV filter does nothing for air that goes around it.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Filter bypass","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","units":["cvi:filter-bypass","hvac:a-bypass-acr"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0257","stem":"A commercial AHU has 1-inch fiberglass panel filters rated MERV 4 ahead of a wet cooling coil that is visibly fouled. Which benchmark should the CVI cite in the report?","options":["ASHRAE 62.1: a MERV 8 floor upstream of cooling coils","ASHRAE 62.1: a MERV 6 floor upstream of cooling coils","ACR 1.7.1: a MERV 8 floor in each AHU filter bank","ASHRAE 62.1: HEPA filtration upstream of cooling coils"],"expl":"ASHRAE 62.1 (since the 2013 edition) requires filters of at least MERV 8 upstream of cooling coils and other wetted surfaces; earlier editions said MERV 6, which is the trap. ACR sets no MERV value; it requires the AHU inspection to include filters and air bypass.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Filtration","tier":1,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.5 (2013 §5.8)","units":["cvi:621-merv6-old","hvac:a-merv8-floor"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0258","stem":"A draw-through AHU's drain pan slopes 1/8 inch per foot to an outlet at its low point, yet water stands in it while the fan runs, and air can be heard pulling in at the drain outlet. What should the CVI record as the likely cause?","options":["No P-trap or other drain seal on a pan under negative pressure","A pan slope below the ASHRAE 62.1 minimum for pans","A drain outlet located higher than the pan's lowest point","A pan too short to catch water leaving the coil face"],"expl":"The missing P-trap is the finding. This pan meets the 1/8 in. per foot slope, but 62.1 also requires a trap or seal on a pan under negative pressure, as in a draw-through unit. Without it, the fan pulls air up the drain and holds water in the pan.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Drain pans","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.7 (2013 §5.10); ACR 2025 §1.7.1","units":["cvi:621-pan-seal","hvac:s-pan-slope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0259","stem":"Downstream of a leaking coil, the CVI finds fibrous-glass liner that is wet, sagging, and pulling away from the duct wall, with no growth seen yet. Which ACR need-to-clean trigger does this finding meet?","options":["Deterioration of fibrous glass liner or other porous components","Microbial contamination that has reached Condition 2 or Condition 3","Components discharging visible debris into the space","Components at risk for fire hazard from accumulated debris"],"expl":"ACR's need-to-clean list includes deterioration of fibrous glass duct liner, duct board, or other porous components, which fits wet, delaminating liner even before growth appears. Condition 2 or 3 would need a qualified microbial determination, and nothing here shows discharge or a fire risk. Like every item on the list, it is a recommendation.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Need-to-clean triggers","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Need to Clean list","units":["acr:N-10"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0260","stem":"A supply duct's fibrous-glass liner is wet and delaminating from a coil leak that has since been repaired. What should the CVI's scope recommend for the liner?","options":["Remove it, then scrape the bare metal clean before new liner goes in","HEPA-vacuum it, then coat it with a UL 181 surface treatment","Wet-clean it with an alkaline coil cleaner, then let it air-dry in place","Dry it with scrubbers in place, then verify it by Method 3"],"expl":"EPA advises that wet duct insulation cannot be effectively cleaned and should be removed and replaced, and ACR 4.19 recommends replacement for delaminated material that cleaning and resurfacing cannot restore; 4.20 then requires the metal to be scraped clean first. Water on fibrous glass is prohibited (4.4), coatings need a sound bondable surface (4.18), and Method 3 never applies to porous material.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Wet liner","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.19, §4.20; EPA","units":["cvi:epa-wet-insulation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0261","stem":"A CVI must see inside several long sheet-metal supply trunks in an occupied office. Which access plan fits ACR?","options":["Use existing doors and grilles, then cut to the closure rules","Cut new openings as needed, since inspection ports are exempt","Cut ports in the flex runouts, since they are cheap to replace","Leave cut openings unsealed for the cleaning crew"],"expl":"Existing access doors, grilles, and terminal panels disturb the least and add no new closures; where a new opening is needed, ACR 4.2 requires it to keep structural and thermal integrity, allow proper closure, and not alter airflow, with panels sealed and fastened. No opening may go into flexible duct (4.2.9), and nothing exempts openings cut for inspection.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Access and openings","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2","units":["cvi:borescope-neck"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0262","stem":"Cutting an inspection opening in a sheet-metal duct slices through its external fibrous-glass duct wrap. What does ACR 2025 add about the wrap that the 2021 edition did not?","options":["The cut wrap shall be repaired with like material","The wrap is left open, since it is outside the airstream","The wrap shall be patched with UL 181A/B foil tape alone","The wrap shall be closed with duct mastic, not new material"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.2.8.2 says removed fibrous glass shall be repaired or replaced with like material of the same thickness without degrading R-value or the vapor barrier, and it now expressly includes any disruption to the external duct wrap. The 2021 text did not name the wrap, which is why 'outside the airstream, leave it' is the old-edition trap.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Access and openings","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","units":["acr:4.2.8.2-e","acr:4.2.8.2-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0263","stem":"An attic system feeds each ceiling register through a flex runout, and the CVI wants a closer look inside one runout. Which approach does ACR allow?","options":["Disconnect it, then reattach it per the maker's instructions","Cut a small port in the jacket and seal it with UL 181A/B foil tape","Cut a port and close it with a panel lapped 1 inch on each side","Cut a port after slipping a sheet-metal sleeve inside the flex core"],"expl":"ACR 4.2.9 says no service opening shall go into flexible ductwork, and 4.2.9.1 recommends following the manufacturer's instructions to disconnect and reattach it. UL 181A/B tape and a 1-inch panel overlap are the closure rules for openings where openings are allowed, not a way to make a cut in flex acceptable.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Access and openings","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.2.9","units":["hvac:a-flex-no-openings"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0264","stem":"A CVI shoots about 200 photos across a four-floor office inspection. Which practice best supports ACR's documentation duties and a fair before-and-after comparison later?","options":["Key each photo to a component tag and plan location","Keep just the worst photos to make the case for cleaning","Label the photos by date and time, in the order they were shot","Let the cleaning contractor caption the photos afterward"],"expl":"ACR 1.3 requires plans to be used during the inspection, 1.10 requires findings to be documented and evaluated, and 5.6.3 recommends photos and HVAC plans in the project documentation. Keying each photo to a tagged component and plan location lets the same spot be re-shot after cleaning; a set of worst-case photos misrepresents the system.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Photo documentation","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.3, §1.10, §5.6.3","units":["cvi:photo-keying"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0265","stem":"A CVI finds a thin, even layer of loose dust in a supply trunk, with no airflow loss, odor, or discharge at the registers. How does ACR 2025's need-to-clean list treat this finding?","options":["It meets the revised trigger, which needs only the presence of particulate","It falls short, as the trigger requires an accumulation of particulate","It falls short until a Method 3 test shows more than 0.75 mg/100 cm²","It meets the trigger only if the dust is fire or smoke residue"],"expl":"ACR 2025 lowered the first trigger from components 'contaminated with an accumulation of particulate' to the presence of particulate, so a light, even layer now qualifies as a recommended reason to clean. The accumulation wording is the 2021 edition, which the NADCA plenum white paper still quotes; Method 2 or 3 may be used only when the need is in question (N-17).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Need-to-clean triggers","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Need to Clean list","units":["acr:N-1","acr:N-18"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0266","stem":"A CVI documents rodent droppings and nesting in a return trunk. What obligation level does ACR 2025's need-to-clean list attach to cleaning it?","options":["Recommended, like every trigger on the need-to-clean list","Required, since infestation is a SHALL item","Permitted, since the list says cleaning may occur","Recommended only after a Method 3 test fails"],"expl":"Every item on ACR's need-to-clean list, infestation included, is phrased as a recommendation that follows a cleanliness inspection or building history, not a requirement and not a bare permission. Method 2 or 3 may be used only when the need is in question, which a documented infestation is not.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Need-to-clean triggers","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Need to Clean list","units":["acr:N-0","acr:N-6"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0267","stem":"In a busy restaurant, the return grilles and the first few feet of return duct near the fry line are coated with sticky, grease-laden lint. Which ACR need-to-clean condition fits best?","options":["Components at risk for fire hazard","Components damaged by fire or smoke","Components that are part of a LEED effort","Components deteriorated by moisture"],"expl":"Grease-laden lint is a fuel load, which matches ACR's trigger for components at risk for fire hazard. The fire, smoke, or water trigger applies after an event has contaminated the components; the hood's grease exhaust duct is a separate system, cleaned per ANSI/IKECA C10.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Need-to-clean triggers","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Need to Clean list","units":["acr:N-7"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0268","stem":"A homeowner's air handler, supply ducts, and return ducts were all last inspected 18 months ago. Under ACR Table 1, which are past the recommended interval?","options":["The air handler, but not yet the supply or return ducts","The supply and return ducts, but not the air handler","The air handler along with the supply and return ducts","No component, as each residential interval is two years"],"expl":"Table 1 gives residential systems 1 year for the air handler and 2 years for supply and for return and exhaust ducts, so at 18 months only the air handler is past due. The intervals are minimum recommendations. Mnemonic: the AHU is 1 year for every building type; residential and marine ducts get 2.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection intervals","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Table 1","units":["acr:1.5.1-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0269","stem":"A CVI services a passenger ferry and a downtown office building, and both had their supply ducts inspected 15 months ago. Under ACR Table 1, whose supply ducts are past the recommended interval?","options":["Only the office's, as commercial ducts are one year","Only the ferry's, as marine supply ducts are one year","Both, as supply ducts are one year in every building type","Neither, as supply ducts are two years in every building type"],"expl":"Table 1 sets supply ducts at 1 year for commercial, industrial, and healthcare buildings and 2 years for residential and marine, so at 15 months only the office is past due. Mnemonic: marine rides with residential (1, 2, 2); everything else is 1, 1, 1.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection intervals","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Table 1","units":["acr:1.5.1-f","acr:1.5.1-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0270","stem":"A hospital sits beside a highway project that will throw dust at its outdoor air intakes for two years. What does ACR 1.5.1 say about the HVAC inspection schedule?","options":["It recommends inspecting more often than its minimums","It requires the one-year healthcare interval and no more often","It allows a two-year interval if better filters are installed","It recommends waiting for an occupant complaint to inspect"],"expl":"ACR 1.5.1 says Table 1 intervals are minimum recommendations and recommends more frequent inspection when geographical, human, or mechanical conditions make it necessary, as heavy construction dust at the intakes does. Reading one year as a ceiling, or letting a complaint drive the schedule, gets the table backward.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection intervals","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1","units":["acr:1.5.1-c","acr:1.5.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0271","stem":"In an AHU, the CVI can see the coil's entering face, but the leaving face is boxed in by the fan section with no access door. What should the cleaning scope address?","options":["Access to both faces, or removal of the coil if access is impossible","Cleaning from the entering face, the side facing the filters","Type 1 dry methods, since they work from a single coil face","A coating on the entering face to lock the debris in place"],"expl":"ACR 4.11 says both the upstream and downstream sides of each coil section shall be accessed for cleaning, and when both sides cannot be reached, removal or replacement can become necessary. One-face cleaning can push debris deeper, and coatings apply only after cleaning passes verification (4.15).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Coil access","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11","units":["acr:4.11-a","acr:4.11-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0272","stem":"Tape lifts from a return duct show settled spores and fragments matching growth found in the AHU mixing section, with no growth on the duct itself. Which Condition best describes the duct?","options":["Condition 2, dispersed from a nearby growth area","Condition 1, since the duct shows no actual growth","Condition 3, since spores and fragments are present","Condition 1, since settled spores fit normal ecology"],"expl":"Condition 2 is an environment primarily contaminated with settled spores or fragments that came directly or indirectly from a Condition 3 area, which is what the matching tape lifts show. Condition 1 can include settled spores, but only as a normal fungal ecology, and Condition 3 requires actual growth, active or dormant, on the surface. The final call belongs to a qualified, licensed-where-required professional.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Microbial Conditions","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Definitions (from S520)","units":["acr:D-20","acr:D-21"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0273","stem":"After an AHU remediation, the owner asks the CVI to certify that the unit is back to Condition 1. Under ACR's definitions, whose role is that?","options":["An IEP, who verifies the fungal ecology is restored","The ASCS, who is responsible for project oversight","The CVI, who made the preliminary determination","The contractor, through a passing Method 3 test"],"expl":"ACR defines the IEP as the professional who determines Condition 1, 2, and 3 status and verifies the return to Condition 1 after remediation. ASCS oversight is a recommended project role, the CVI's preliminary determination is only a starting point, and Method 3 measures particulate, not fungal ecology.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Microbial Conditions","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Definitions","units":["acr:D-84"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0274","stem":"A client questions whether a fibrous-glass-lined supply trunk needs cleaning at all. Which test may the CVI use to settle that question?","options":["Method 2, the surface comparison test run with a vacuum brush","Method 3, the vacuum test with a cassette and pump","Method 1, a visual review of the liner by the client","Method 3, the vacuum test on a template over the liner"],"expl":"ACR says that when the need to clean is in question, Method 2 or Method 3 may determine it, but Method 3 does not apply to porous components such as fibrous glass liner. Method 2 covers porous and non-porous surfaces in the 2025 edition, so it is the test that fits.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Need-to-clean testing","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 N-17, §5.3","units":["acr:N-17","acr:5.3.3-b","acr:5-M2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0275","stem":"To settle a dispute over the need to clean, a CVI runs Method 3 on a bare sheet-metal return trunk and the lab reports 1.2 mg/100 cm². How should the report read that result?","options":["It is over the pass limit, so the surface is not clean","It is within the pass limit set for sheet metal","It cannot decide need, since Method 3 is for verification","It needs a Method 2 retest, since Method 2 settles disputes"],"expl":"The Method 3 pass limit is a net 0.75 mg/100 cm², so 1.2 mg shows the surface is not clean, and N-17 allows Method 2 or 3 to decide the need to clean when it is in question. The escalation runs the other way: Method 3 settles a disputed Method 2 (5.3.3), not the reverse.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Need-to-clean testing","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.5, N-17","units":["acr:5.5-a","acr:N-17"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0276","stem":"A client wants the CVI to use a handheld particle counter at the diffusers to prove the ducts need cleaning. What does ACR say about that approach?","options":["It names Method 2 or 3, not airborne counts, for that call","It accepts counts under its Level 3 monitoring rules","It accepts counts taken while scrubbers run at 4 air changes","It accepts counts from a meter calibrated to ±5% at 15 L/min"],"expl":"ACR's need-to-clean list says Method 2 or Method 3 may be used when the need is in question; both test settled material on surfaces, while a particle counter reads what is airborne at that moment. Level 3 real-time monitoring, 4 ACH scrubbing, and the ±5% calibrator are real ACR specs for containment and Method 3, not a basis for the cleaning decision.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Need-to-clean testing","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 N-17","units":["cvi:particle-counter"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0277","stem":"An AHU has a wetted-media evaporative humidifier whose sump holds slimy water. How does ACR 1.7.1 treat that humidifier during the inspection?","options":["As a humidification system the AHU inspection shall include","As an OEM maintenance task that ACR excludes from its scope","As building plumbing that shall stay outside the ACR scope","As an item the IEP adds to the scope after sampling the sump"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.1 says the AHU inspection shall include humidification systems, and a slimy sump is exactly the finding it exists to catch. The scope exclusion for OEM preventative maintenance tasks (G-9) covers routine upkeep the manufacturer prescribes, not the cleanliness inspection.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"AHU inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","units":["acr:1.7.1-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0278","stem":"A hotel has 300 fan coil units in its guest rooms and no central air handler. How does ACR classify these units for inspection?","options":["As AHUs, inspected to the same list as a central unit","As terminal boxes, inspected with the supply ducts","As exhaust devices, part of the overall inspection","As open air plenums, in scope by contract alone"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.1 places fan coil units and evaporative coolers in the air-handling unit category, so each gets the filter, coil, pan and drain, fan, insulation, damper and gasket, and integrity checks. Terminal boxes are the VAV and fan-powered boxes of 1.7.2.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"AHU inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","units":["acr:1.7.1-i"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0279","stem":"The inspection finds that the AHU to be cleaned sits directly above an occupied operating suite. Where does ACR require the needed engineering controls to be determined and written down?","options":["In the system component assessment that follows the inspection","In the post-project report, as lessons for later jobs","Nowhere; ACR leaves containment choices to the IEP","In the contractor's product submittal and SDS binder"],"expl":"ACR 1.0 says the inspection shall determine engineering controls, and 1.10 says the assessment shall give a determination of the required environmental engineering controls; the scope of work then carries them (2.2). IEP advice on project-specific controls is only recommended (3.13), and the product submittal section lists products and SDS, with controls only for chemical vapors and odors (2.7.2), not the containment an AHU job needs.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"System assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.10","units":["acr:1.10-e","acr:1.0-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0280","stem":"A contractor asks the CVI to skip the post-cleaning inspection because the crew already took photos. What does ACR 1.1 say?","options":["An inspection shall occur after the project as well as before","An inspection after the project is recommended, not required","Photos may stand in for the after inspection if well labeled","An after inspection shall occur only if the client disputes"],"expl":"ACR 1.1 says an inspection shall occur before the cleaning and restoration project and after it; only routine inspections are recommended (1.1-c). Crew photos can support the record but do not replace the required post-project inspection.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection timing","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.1","units":["acr:1.1-b","acr:1.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0281","stem":"A courthouse requires security escorts, night-only work, and badge checks for anyone above its ceilings. Where do these site rules belong under ACR?","options":["Under the unique requirements the assessment shall give","Under the product submittals the work plan shall list","Under the safety plan for the off-line smoke detectors","Under the routine inspection schedule in ASHRAE/ACCA 180"],"expl":"ACR 1.10 says the assessment shall give a recommendation on need, a defined scope, recommended techniques, required engineering controls, and any unique requirements, and the scope of work carries unique requirements too (2.2). Site rules like escorts and night work belong there so the work plan reflects them.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"System assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.10, §2.2","units":["acr:1.10-f","acr:2.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0282","stem":"An IEP's report puts a return duct at Condition 2 with no actual growth on it. Does that finding meet ACR 2025's need-to-clean list?","options":["Yes, Condition 2 or 3 contamination is listed","No, the list names Condition 3 actual growth by itself","No, microbial findings leave the list for S520 alone","Yes, as the list names Condition 1, 2, or 3 results"],"expl":"ACR 2025 lists microbial contamination that reaches Condition 2 or Condition 3 as a recommended reason to clean; the 2025 edition changed 'mold' to 'microbial'. Condition 1 is normal ecology and is not a trigger, and Condition 2 alone is enough.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Need-to-clean triggers","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Need to Clean list","units":["acr:N-9"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0283","stem":"Suspect growth is found on the liner inside one supply branch, while the air handler looks clean. Does ACR 1.9.1 force a same-visit inspection of the supply and return ducts?","options":["No, 1.9.1 is triggered by suspect growth in the AHU components","Yes, 1.9.1 covers suspect growth found anywhere in the HVAC system","Yes, but 1.9.1 adds just the return ducts on that visit","No, suspect growth in supply branches falls outside ACR"],"expl":"ACR 1.9.1 is triggered by suspect microbial contamination in the air-handling unit components; only then shall the supply and return ducts be inspected during the same inspection. Growth in a supply branch still calls for the recommended preliminary Condition determination and IEP assessment under 1.9, and it is squarely inside ACR.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Suspect microbial","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.9, §1.9.1","units":["acr:1.9.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0284","stem":"A daycare's AHU shows widespread suspect growth on the coil and in the drain pan, and the director asks whether to keep it running until remediation. What does EPA's mold guidance advise?","options":["Shut it down while known or suspected to be contaminated","Run it with MERV 8 filters installed until the remediation","Run it on fan mode to dry the drain pan until cleaning","Run it until an IEP confirms Condition 3 with sampling"],"expl":"EPA's mold remediation guide says not to run the HVAC system if you know or suspect it is contaminated with mold. EPA does suggest considering MERV 8 filters during remediation, but that is about filtering air, not a reason to run a contaminated unit; ACR 1.9 separately recommends a preliminary Condition call and IEP assessment.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Suspect microbial","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings","units":["cvi:epa-hvac-off"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0285","stem":"Staff say a packed conference room feels stuffy by mid-afternoon but fine in the morning. Which instrument helps the CVI judge whether outdoor-air ventilation keeps up with occupancy?","options":["A direct-reading carbon dioxide meter at the meeting table","A pin-type moisture meter on the wall surfaces","A handheld optical particle counter at the table","A digital manometer across the corridor door"],"expl":"EPA ties symptoms that worsen over the occupied day to ventilation that may not keep up, and lists CO2 measuring devices, detector tubes or direct-reading meters, as helpful for most investigations. Moisture meters read materials, particle counters read airborne particles, and manometers read pressure differences.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection instruments","tier":0,"ref":"EPA Building Air Quality, Sec. 6","units":["cvi:timing-other","cvi:min-kit","cvi:co2-meter","hvac:a-co2-occupants"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0286","stem":"A small tenant space has only five VAV boxes, and the owner asks whether the 10% inspection minimum can be waived since it works out to less than one box. What is the right answer under ACR 1.7?","options":["No, at least one box is inspected to meet the 10% minimum","Yes, the 10% minimum may be waived at the client's option","Yes, jobs under ten components are exempt from Section 1.7","No, five boxes, since small jobs follow the S520 rule"],"expl":"ACR 1.7 sets a minimum of 10% of the components under inspection and offers no exemption or client waiver, so a five-box space still gets at least one box inspected. The all-components rule belongs only to S520 remediation projects, and an option that lets the client waive a stated minimum is the deferral trap.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Representative sampling","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7","units":["acr:1.7-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0287","stem":"On the negative-pressure side of an AHU, torn door gaskets whistle and rusted casing seams let attic air in downstream of the filters. How should this be recorded?","options":["As door gasket and unit integrity items the AHU inspection covers","As duct leakage that the ACR restoration work is meant to seal","As OEM maintenance items that ACR leaves out of its inspection","As a Condition 2 finding from attic spores drawn inside"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.1 says the AHU inspection shall include dampers and door gaskets and general unit integrity, and casing leaks downstream of the filters let unfiltered attic air into the airstream. ACR restoration expressly excludes sealing duct air leaks (4.24.1), so the finding is documented and referred rather than folded into cleaning.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"AHU inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","units":["acr:1.7.1-g","acr:1.7.1-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0288","stem":"Your system component assessment for a hotel already recommends cleaning and names the containment level. Under ACR 2025, what else must the assessment give?","options":["The recommended techniques to use for the cleaning","A firm price for each component in the cleaning scope","The project start and finish dates for each phase","The other trades on site, with names and contacts"],"expl":"ACR 2025 1.10 requires the assessment to give the need for cleaning, a clearly defined scope, the recommended cleaning techniques, the required engineering controls, and any unique requirements. Dates and other trades are only recommended work plan items (2.4, 2.5), and price is not an ACR item. Mnemonic: Need, Scope, Techniques, Controls, Unique.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Component assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.10","units":["acr:1.10-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0289","stem":"A property manager asks you to drop containment from your scope and let the contractor decide it on site. How should you respond?","options":["Keep containment in your scope","Defer the controls to the site foreman","Leave containment to the project IEP","Move the controls into the safety plan"],"expl":"ACR 2025 2.2 says the scope of work shall include the environmental engineering controls, and 1.10 makes the assessment determine them. IEP advice on controls (3.13) is only a recommendation and does not move them out of the scope. The safety plan (2.8) covers life safety detection, not workspace containment.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Scope of work","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.2, §1.10","units":["acr:2.2-b","acr:1.10-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0290","stem":"A hospital requires an infection control permit before any duct in its surgical wing is opened. Under ACR 2025, how must your project documents handle that condition?","options":["It shall appear in the scope of work","It is recommended for the schedule","It must be kept with the SDS binder","It shall go in the communication plan"],"expl":"A permit condition like this is a unique requirement, and ACR 2025 2.2 says the scope of work shall include any unique requirements (1.10 also makes the assessment give them). The schedule (2.5) and communication plan (2.6) are only recommended sections, and the SDS binder (2.7.1) is for chemical products, so parking the permit in any of them drops a mandatory scope item.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Scope of work","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.2","units":["acr:2.2-c","acr:1.10-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0291","stem":"An inspection finds a return trunk made of duct board so friable that brushing would shred it. Which recommendation fits ACR 2025?","options":["Replace it and list it as a finding","Coat it now and clean it afterward","Clean it gently and skip verification","Seal it with mastic, then vacuum it"],"expl":"Components that cannot withstand proper cleaning shall be documented, reported to the customer, and recommended for repair or replacement (ACR 2025 4.14.4), and friable fibrous glass that cleaning and resurfacing cannot restore should be replaced (4.19). Coating first is backward: resurfacing comes only after proper mechanical cleaning (4.18.1).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Component assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.14.4, §4.19","units":["acr:4.14.4-c","acr:4.19-a","acr:4.14.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0292","stem":"Your pre-cleaning inspection covered 10% of a building's 200 VAV boxes. The contractor now asks how many boxes need post-cleaning verification. What do you tell him?","options":["Every VAV box listed in the specified cleaning scope","Only the same 10% of boxes you inspected earlier","A random 10% of the boxes, chosen after cleaning","Twenty boxes spread evenly across the floors"],"expl":"Verification shall occur on all specified components (ACR 2025 5.0). The 10% figure is the minimum for the cleanliness inspection at 1.7, not a verification sample, and 'twenty boxes' is the same 10% trap in disguise.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.0, §1.7","units":["acr:5.0-d","acr:X-7"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0293","stem":"A school district's bid request says only 'clean the ductwork.' Your inspection finds fouled coils and a slimed drain pan in the same AHU. How should your written scope handle them?","options":["List the coil and drain pan as components","Omit them, since the bid names ducts","Note them in the closing report as add-ons","Defer them to the Table 1 interval"],"expl":"The assessment shall recommend on the need for cleaning, and the scope shall identify the components to be cleaned (ACR 2025 1.10, 2.2). A fouled coil and a slimed pan meet the need-to-clean triggers, and suspect growth on a pan sends the coil to Type 2 (4.11.1.2). Table 1 sets routine inspection intervals, not a reason to defer found contamination.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Scope of work","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.10, §2.2","units":["acr:2.2-a","acr:1.10-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0294","stem":"A small dental office wants its ducts cleaned with 'no containment at all' to save money. What minimum must your specification still call for?","options":["Level 1 negative pressure and clean coverings","Level 2 barriers and a two-layer 6-mil floor","Protective coverings with no negative pressure","HEPA scrubbers at 4 air changes per hour"],"expl":"Level 1 is the minimum containment on every component cleaning project, with no exemption (ACR 2025 3.9): negative pressure, clean protective coverings, and cross-contamination control. Barriers, the two-layer floor, and 4 ACH scrubbing belong to Level 2 (3.10), so they overshoot the minimum.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.9","units":["acr:3.9-a","acr:3-L1"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0295","stem":"You are writing a Level 2 containment specification for an occupied open office. Which element must you list as mandatory rather than recommended?","options":["A two-layer 6-mil fire retardant floor","Barrier walls from floor to ceiling","6-mil fire retardant barrier walls","Airtight barrier seal at the ceiling"],"expl":"At Level 2 the two-layer 6-mil fire retardant floor, turned up at least 6 inches and sealed airtight to the walls, is a shall (ACR 2025 3.10.3). Floor-to-ceiling barriers, 6-mil barrier material, and the barrier's airtight seal at the ceiling, floor, and walls are all recommendations (3.10.2.1 to 3.10.2.4). Same material, adjacent clauses, opposite obligation.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2, §3.10.3","units":["acr:3.10.3-a","acr:X-3"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0296","stem":"Your Level 2 specification covers a 20 x 30 ft room with a 10 ft ceiling, and the HVAC system will be off. What minimum total HEPA air scrubbing rate must it require?","options":["400 cfm","40 cfm","24,000 cfm","600 cfm"],"expl":"400 cfm. Level 2 scrubbers must hold at least 4 ACH: 20 x 30 x 10 = 6,000 cu ft, and 6,000 x 4 / 60 = 400 cfm. 40 cfm forgets the ceiling height, 24,000 is per hour, and 600 cfm is 6 ACH.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.10.7","units":["cvi:ach-cfm"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0297","stem":"A Level 2 containment around a hospital AHU room has just had its interior surfaces HEPA vacuumed. The contractor wants to take the poly down now. What must happen first?","options":["A post-remediation verification done on the work area","A new particulate baseline reading in the room","A review of the recorded pressure log for the containment","A second 4 ACH air scrubbing cycle inside the enclosure"],"expl":"In healthcare, ACR 2025 3.10.8 requires a post-remediation verification before the containment comes down, on top of the wet wipe or HEPA vacuum. A particulate baseline is a Level 3 step taken before setup (3.11.3), and a recorded pressure log belongs to Level 4 (3.12.3).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.10.8","units":["acr:3.10.8-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0298","stem":"Your specification moves a project from Level 2 to Level 3 containment. Which monitoring step must you now add that Level 2 did not need?","options":["A background particulate reading before containment setup","A manometer check that validates the negative pressure","A constant recording pressure monitor with an alarm","HEPA air scrubbing at four air changes per hour"],"expl":"Level 3 adds background total particulate monitoring before containment setup, real-time particulate monitoring during work, and continuous pressure monitoring (ACR 2025 3.11.3). The manometer and 4 ACH are already Level 2 items (3.10.6, 3.10.7), and the recording monitor with alarm is the Level 4 addition (3.12.3). Ladder: validate, monitor, record.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","units":["acr:3.11.3-c","acr:3-L3"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0299","stem":"On a Level 3 project, real-time monitoring shows airborne particulate above background, and the crew finds a torn barrier seam. When may work resume?","options":["Once back at background with the tear fixed","As soon as the torn seam is re-taped","Once readings fall back to background","After an IEP signs off on the repaired barrier seam"],"expl":"Work shall stop when levels exceed background and restart only after the levels return to background AND the cause has a correction (ACR 2025 3.11.3). Fixing the seam alone, or waiting for levels alone, meets only half the condition, and no IEP sign-off is named for this step.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","units":["acr:3.11.3-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0300","stem":"You upgrade a containment specification from Level 3 to Level 4. Which pressure instrument does the upgrade add?","options":["A constant recording monitor with alarm","A manometer to validate the negative pressure","A continuous monitor that shows pressure loss","An airflow measuring device at each doorway"],"expl":"Level 4 adds a constant recording pressurization monitor with an appropriate alarm, plus a second decon chamber (ACR 2025 3.12). The manometer is the Level 2 validation tool (3.10.6), and continuous monitoring able to detect loss is already required at Level 3 (3.11.3). Ladder: validate (L2), monitor (L3), record with alarm (L4).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.12.3","units":["acr:3.12.3-b","acr:3-L4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0301","stem":"A remediation specification calls for Level 4 containment. How should you specify the decontamination unit?","options":["Two chambers, each to Level 3 rules","One chamber sealed to the containment","Two flaps on a wall cut, no chamber","A single zipper door, no chamber"],"expl":"Level 4 requires a two-chamber decontamination facility, each chamber meeting the Level 3 chamber construction rules (ACR 2025 3.12.2). One sealed chamber is Level 3 (3.11.2). A zipper door or a two-flap wall cut with no chamber is Level 2 access (3.10.4); at Levels 3 and 4 those same details only separate a chamber from the containment (3.11.2).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.12.2","units":["acr:3.12.2-a","acr:3.12.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0302","stem":"Walking a Level 2 containment before work starts, you see the two-layer floor poly stops flat at the base of the walls. What do you require?","options":["Turn it 6 inches up the walls and seal it","Tape the edge flat where it meets the wall","Add a third layer along the wall base","Turn it 1 inch up the walls and tape it"],"expl":"The Level 2 floor shall extend at least 6 inches (15.2 cm) up the side walls and be sealed airtight to them (ACR 2025 3.10.3). One inch is the service panel overlap from 4.2.5.2, a different clause, and a third layer adds nothing the Standard asks for.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.10.3","units":["acr:3.10.3-b","acr:3.10.3-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0303","stem":"A contractor says he can 'feel the suction' at the duct opening, so he sees no need to check the pressure differential during the job. What does ACR 2025 require?","options":["Verify it during the job, in all circumstances","Verify it once, at setup, with a digital manometer","Skip the checks when the air machine is HEPA rated","Check it only on the jobs where barriers are in use"],"expl":"Verification of the pressurization differential shall occur during the project under all circumstances (ACR 2025 4.1.1), and negative pressure shall be verifiable at representative locations during cleaning (3.4). A one-time setup check or a HEPA rating does not satisfy it. Note that the Standard gives no numeric negative pressure value anywhere.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Negative pressure","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.1.1, §3.4","units":["acr:4.1.1-a","acr:3.4-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0304","stem":"A contractor plans to park a gas-powered vacuum truck beside the rooftop outside air intake of the AHU being cleaned. What should your plan review require?","options":["Relocate it to a spot where its exhaust cannot get in","Keep it there with a HEPA filter on the exhaust","Add a CO alarm inside the AHU's mechanical room","Keep it there if the outside air intake is taped"],"expl":"Fuel-powered equipment shall be positioned so combustion and exhaust emissions cannot enter, and that location shall be monitored and managed during the project (ACR 2025 3.2). A HEPA filter does not stop combustion gases, and an alarm detects a problem rather than preventing entry.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Fuel-powered equipment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.2","units":["acr:3.2-a","acr:3.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0305","stem":"On day 3 of a project, the wind shifts and blows generator exhaust toward an open loading dock door. The generator was placed correctly on day 1. What does ACR 2025 expect?","options":["Manage the location as conditions change","Leave it, since day 1 placement complied","Log it for the closeout documentation","Move it only if an occupant complains"],"expl":"Beyond initial placement, the location of fuel-powered equipment shall be monitored and managed during the project (ACR 2025 3.2.1). Correct day 1 placement does not end the duty, and waiting for complaints lets emissions enter first.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Fuel-powered equipment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.2.1","units":["acr:3.2.1-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0306","stem":"Your plan review for a high-rise finds duct smoke detectors will be off-line during return cleaning. Which plan content for that outage is mandatory, not just recommended?","options":["Addressing the life safety concerns","Naming each party's representative","Giving the outage dates and times","Listing each firm with its contacts"],"expl":"When detection equipment goes off-line, the work plan shall address life safety concerns (ACR 2025 2.8), a task that needs input from other parties. Defining each designated representative (2.8), the schedule (2.5), and other trades with contacts (2.4) are all recommendations.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Life safety planning","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.8","units":["acr:2.8-a","acr:2.8-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0307","stem":"A contractor plans to run a portable vacuum that exhausts into the mechanical room. Its filter is rated 99.9% at 1 micron. What do you tell him?","options":["Use a HEPA unit, 99.97% at 0.3 micron, or exhaust outdoors","Keep it, since 99.9% at 1 micron meets the HEPA rating","Keep it, as long as the room door stays closed","Use it as is, with an air scrubber running in the room"],"expl":"Vacuum equipment exhausting inside the building envelope shall be HEPA-filtered at 99.97% at 0.3 micron (ACR 2025 3.3), and non-HEPA pressurization equipment shall exhaust outdoors (4.1.3). A 99.9% at 1 micron filter is not HEPA, and a closed door or a scrubber substitutes for neither rule.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"HEPA and exhaust","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.3, §4.1.3","units":["acr:3.3-a","acr:4.1.3-a","acr:D-27"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0308","stem":"Your inspection finds a cooling coil packed solid with matted lint and grease on its entering face. The contractor's plan says 'Type 1 first, per NADCA.' What do you recommend?","options":["Type 2 now, as Type 1 will not suffice","Type 1 first, as the Standard requires","Type 1 only, to protect the coil fins","Replacement, without any cleaning try"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.11.4 sends the coil to Type 2 when the assessment determines Type 1 will not be sufficient; the 2021 rule that Type 1 always comes first is gone. Matted, greasy loading is exactly that case. Replacement is for damaged or corroded coils, or when cleaning fails (4.11.1.3, 4.11.5).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Coil cleaning","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","units":["acr:4.11.4-b","acr:4.11.4-h"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0309","stem":"A coil looks lightly dusted, but the drain pan under it shows suspect microbial growth. Which coil method should your scope specify?","options":["Type 2 wet cleaning of coil and pan","Type 1 dry cleaning, then re-inspect","Type 1 on the coil, Type 2 on the pan","Surface treatment of the pan first"],"expl":"Suspect microbial contamination on any portion of the coil OR the drain pan triggers Type 2 (ACR 2025 4.11.1.2), and Type 2 covers coil, pan, and drain line, with pan drainage tested before and after (4.11.4, 4.11.4.1). Splitting methods ignores the trigger, and surface treatments come only after verified cleaning (4.15).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Coil cleaning","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.2","units":["acr:4.11.1.2-a","acr:4.11.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0310","stem":"An owner doubts a cleaned coil is really cleaner because it 'looks the same.' Which objective measure should your scope have recommended?","options":["Coil static pressure drop, before and after cleaning","Coil pressure drop, after cleaning but not before","A NADCA vacuum test on the coil's leaving air face","Supply air temperature at the nearest diffuser, after cleaning"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.11.6 notes that visual observation of coils can mislead and recommends a static pressure drop measurement before AND after cleaning. An after-only reading has no baseline. The vacuum test is a duct surface verification method, not the coil measure the Standard names.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Coil cleaning","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11.6","units":["acr:4.11.6-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0311","stem":"An inspected coil has corroded fins that flake when touched, and any pressure washing would strip them further. What should your assessment recommend?","options":["Replacement of the coil section rather than cleaning","Type 2 cleaning with a stronger coil cleaner","Type 1 cleaning with high-pressure compressed air","Fin combing, then a Type 2 pressure wash"],"expl":"Replacement can become necessary for damaged, deteriorating, or corroded fins, and replacement is recommended when cleaning would cause more damage (ACR 2025 4.11.1.3). Methods and products shall not damage or erode the coil surface or fins (4.11.4.2), which rules out stronger chemicals and harder air or water.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Coil cleaning","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.3","units":["acr:4.11.1.3-a","acr:4.11.1.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0312","stem":"After mechanical cleaning, a lined supply trunk has abraded, slightly friable liner. The client wants it coated instead of replaced. What must happen before you approve a coating?","options":["An assessment that it gives a firm bond","A Method 3 pass on the lined surface","An IEP clearance to Condition 1 status","A static pressure test across the trunk"],"expl":"Resurfacing may be used on abraded or friable fibrous glass, but an assessment after cleaning shall first determine that the surface gives a strong bondable surface (ACR 2025 4.18.1); material that cannot hold a long-term bond shall not be resurfaced (4.18.2). Method 3 never applies to porous liner, and Condition 1 is the target only for microbial liner replacement (4.20.1).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Porous restoration","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.18","units":["acr:4.18.1-a","acr:4.18.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0313","stem":"Liner with Condition 3 growth was removed from a supply plenum. The installer wants to glue new liner onto the scraped metal today. What must your hold point require first?","options":["The base surface reaches Condition 1 status","The base surface passes a Method 2 comparison test","The metal gets an antimicrobial coat","The metal reaches Condition 2 status"],"expl":"When liner is removed for a microbial cause, the base surface shall reach Condition 1 before new insulation goes on (ACR 2025 4.20.1), after being scraped free of loose debris (4.20). It is the only clause where Condition 1 is a cleaning target. Condition 2 is still contamination by settled spores.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Porous restoration","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.20.1","units":["acr:4.20.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0314","stem":"A replacement liner submittal shows a thinner product with a lower R-value than the liner removed, and it falls short of current code. How should you rule?","options":["Reject it; it meets neither requirement","Accept it, provided it passes a Method 2 test","Accept it, provided the owner signs a waiver","Accept it, since its UL 181 listing is enough"],"expl":"Replacement thermal-acoustic materials shall meet or exceed the original specifications or current codes (ACR 2025 4.20.2), and installation shall follow SMACNA, NAIMA, and applicable codes (4.20.3). This product fails both tests. An owner waiver cannot set aside a shall, and UL 181 is the surface treatment listing (4.15.1).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Porous restoration","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.20.2","units":["acr:4.20.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0315","stem":"A contractor proposes steam cleaning a fibrous glass lined supply duct to get it 'really clean.' What is your response?","options":["Reject it; no water on porous liner","Allow it, if the liner dries in a day","Allow it at normal water line pressure","Allow it if a UL 181 surface coat follows"],"expl":"Cleaning agents and water shall never contact fibrous glass or other porous components (ACR 2025 4.4), and liner is cleaned by the mechanical methods of 4.7 without abrasions or tears (4.17). Normal water line pressure is a Type 2 coil method (4.11.4), not a liner method.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Porous restoration","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.4, §4.17","units":["acr:4.4-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0316","stem":"After a kitchen fire, lined return ducts were mechanically cleaned. On re-inspection the liner still holds a strong smoke odor. What does ACR 2025 require?","options":["Resurface or replace the odor-retaining liner","Fog it with a deodorizer, then close it up","Clean it again, this time using a wet method","Accept it, since the liner now looks clean"],"expl":"After cleaning, porous surfaces shall be evaluated for friability and odor retention, and odor-retaining areas shall be resurfaced or replaced (ACR 2025 4.23.1, 4.23.2). Wet methods are barred on porous components (4.4), and visibly clean does not end the fire damage evaluation.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Fire and smoke","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.23","units":["acr:4.23.2-a","acr:4.23.1-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0317","stem":"An insurance adjuster disputes whether dark deposits in ducts two floors above a fire are fire residue at all. What should you recommend?","options":["Surface sampling per IESO/RIA 6001","Surface sampling per ANSI/IICRC S500","Surface sampling per ANSI/IICRC S520","A Method 3 vacuum test per NIOSH 0500"],"expl":"When soot or smoke contamination is in question, ACR 2025 4.23.4 recommends sampling per ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001, the standard for evaluating HVAC interior surfaces for fire-related particulate. S500 covers water damage and S520 mold remediation. Method 3 weighs total dust; it does not identify what the dust is.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Fire and smoke","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.23.4","units":["acr:4.23.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0318","stem":"A client asks why your water damage scope sets the water category before it names any cleaning methods. What is the ACR 2025 reason?","options":["It largely dictates methods and controls","It sets which verification method is used","It decides who is allowed to do the cleaning","It sets the drying time that is allowed"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.22 states that the category of the water largely dictates the cleaning methods and the engineering controls, which is why categorization (a shall) comes first. Verification methods come from Section 5 and the contract, not from the water category.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Water damage","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.22","units":["acr:4.22-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0319","stem":"After a potable supply pipe burst, some sheet metal ducts are salvageable and some lined sections are soaked. What does ACR 2025 require for the salvageable ducts?","options":["Give each of them a thorough cleaning","Replace them all, since they were wetted","Dry them in place, with no cleaning","Clean them only if above Category 1"],"expl":"All components with salvage value shall be thoroughly cleaned (ACR 2025 4.22), after the water is categorized per S500. Wetting alone does not condemn sheet metal, and a Category 1 source does not waive the cleaning.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Water damage","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.22","units":["acr:4.22-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0320","stem":"Water from a burst potable supply line sat for five days in a warm ceiling space and soaked a return plenum. How should you treat the water category?","options":["Reassess it; time and temperature can raise the category","Keep Category 1, since the source alone can set it","Call it Condition 2 under ANSI/IICRC S520 criteria","Hold Category 1 until a lab result comes back"],"expl":"S500 categories follow the contamination present, not just the source. IICRC states that time and temperature affect the amplification of contaminants and therefore the category, so a clean-source leak left warm for days can degrade to Category 2 or 3. ACR 2025 4.22 then lets that category drive methods and controls.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Water damage","tier":1,"ref":"ANSI/IICRC S500; ACR 2025 §4.22","units":["cvi:s500-time"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0321","stem":"A remediation contractor asks you, the CVI who wrote the scope, to sign off that the remediated AHU is back to Condition 1. Who does ACR 2025 describe as verifying that return?","options":["An IEP qualified to assess fungal ecology","The ASCS who supervised the cleaning crew","The lab that weighed the Method 3 filters","The CVI who wrote the original scope"],"expl":"ACR 2025 defines the IEP as the professional who determines Condition 1, 2, and 3 status and verifies the return to Condition 1. A Method 3 weight measures particulate only, since verification does not determine microbial contamination (5.0). Your role is the scope and particulate verification, not the fungal ecology call.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Microbial remediation","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Definitions (IEP), §5.0","units":["acr:D-84"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0322","stem":"A contractor wants to spray a liner encapsulant the same afternoon he finishes brushing, before anyone verifies the duct. What do you tell him?","options":["Verify and pass first, then treat it","Treat it now; verification comes after","Treat it now if the duct looks clean","Wait 24 hours, then apply the coat"],"expl":"Surface treatments shall be applied only after the system is confirmed clean AND passes the specified level of cleanliness verification (ACR 2025 4.15). 'Looks clean' is not a documented pass, and a waiting period is not the test. Order: clean, verify, then treat.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Surface treatments","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.15","units":["acr:4.15-c","acr:4.15-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0323","stem":"The contractor wants to start cleaning Monday and send the written work plan 'by the end of the week.' What do you tell the owner?","options":["No cleaning until the plan arrives","Cleaning starts now if the plan follows","A verbal plan is enough to start work","The plan is due before verification starts"],"expl":"ACR 2025 2.0 says the contractor shall give a written work plan before any cleaning work starts. A late or verbal plan does not comply, and 'before verification' is far too late.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Work plan","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.0","units":["acr:2.0-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0324","stem":"Reviewing a submitted work plan, you find it has no project schedule and no list of other trades. How do you grade that under ACR 2025?","options":["Missing recommended sections, but not the required ones","Missing required sections; recommend a resubmittal","Missing required sections; stop the work at once","Missing sections required on healthcare jobs"],"expl":"The written plan itself is a shall, but most of its sections are recommendations: means and methods (2.3), other trades (2.4), schedule (2.5), and the communication plan (2.6). The mandatory parts are the scope (2.2), product submittals and SDS (2.7), and life safety for off-line detection (2.8). Mnemonic: the shall sections are Scope, Submittals, Safety.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Work plan","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.0 to §2.8","units":["acr:2.5-a","acr:2.4-a","acr:2.0-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0325","stem":"On a site visit you ask for the SDS for the coil cleaner in use. The technician says they are 'back at the office.' What does ACR 2025 require?","options":["SDS in the plan and on site all project","SDS at the office, on call within a day","SDS on site only for hazardous products","SDS given to the client at closeout"],"expl":"The work plan shall include SDS for all chemical products, and the SDS shall stay on site for review for the duration of the project (ACR 2025 2.7.1). Office or closeout copies miss the on-site rule, and the rule covers all chemical products on the project.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Work plan","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.7.1","units":["acr:2.7.1-b","acr:2.7.1-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0326","stem":"Your scope includes applying a solvent-based coating inside the ducts of an occupied office. What must the work plan also describe?","options":["Controls for chemical vapors and odors","HEPA air scrubbing at 4 air changes per hour","A second decon chamber for the coating crew","Respirators issued to the building occupants"],"expl":"Where applicable, the work plan shall describe engineering controls for chemical vapors and odors to limit occupant and worker exposure (ACR 2025 2.7.2), and products shall follow manufacturer procedures with exhaust ventilation where required (3.7). 4 ACH is a Level 2 containment figure, and the Standard specifies no PPE at all.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Work plan","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.7.2","units":["acr:2.7.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0327","stem":"The work plan lists the coil cleaner and a liner coating, but not the general-purpose degreaser the crew also uses. Is the plan compliant?","options":["No; all products used belong in the list","Yes; only the coatings need to be listed","Yes; only EPA-registered products count","Yes; the SDS binder alone covers the gap"],"expl":"The work plan shall list all general use products and specific chemical type products, plus all project coatings (ACR 2025 2.7), with SDS for every chemical product (2.7.1). An SDS binder does not replace the product list, and EPA registration is not the listing test.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Work plan","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.7","units":["acr:2.7-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0328","stem":"Three contractors (duct cleaning, controls, and fire alarm) will work in the same AHU room. What does ACR 2025 say about a work site communication plan?","options":["Recommended, naming contacts and their duties","Required, with contacts and duties for each firm","Required, but limited to each smoke detector outage","Recommended, but limited to healthcare job sites"],"expl":"When more than one company works on the project, ACR 2025 2.6 recommends a communication plan naming the individuals, companies, contacts, and responsibilities. It is not a shall. The off-line detector rule (2.8) is a separate mandatory life safety item.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Work plan","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.6","units":["acr:2.6-a","acr:2.6-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0329","stem":"An owner asks you to state in your inspection report that cleaning will cut the building's energy use by 20%. How should you respond?","options":["Keep the savings promise out of the report","Include it if the coils are heavily fouled","Include it beside a pressure drop reading","Put it in the report as a range, not a figure"],"expl":"ACR 2025 states NADCA gives no warranty of energy savings, and an inspection report records findings and recommendations, not performance guarantees. A pressure drop reading supports a coil finding; it does not license a savings promise, and a range is still a promise.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection report","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 General (no warranty)","units":["acr:G-16"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0330","stem":"A daycare director wants your report to certify that the cleaned system is 'safe for children with asthma.' What should the report do?","options":["Report findings with no health claim","Certify safety once Method 3 has passed","Certify safety if an IEP co-signs","Certify safety for one cooling season"],"expl":"NADCA gives no warranty that the work corrects indoor air quality concerns (ACR 2025 General), and verification only determines the presence of particulate, not microbial or hazardous contamination (5.0). A Method 3 pass is a dust weight, not a health certificate.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection report","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 General, §5.0","units":["cvi:no-health-causation","cvi:no-certify-claims"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0331","stem":"Your draft report says 'heavy debris in several ducts.' Which revision best turns that into a finding a contractor can scope from?","options":["Key each item to a location and photo","Replace it with one overall building grade","Add an estimated energy penalty for each duct","Add the brand and age of each duct liner"],"expl":"Plans shall be in use during the inspection (ACR 2025 1.3), inspection information shall be documented and evaluated (1.10), and photos and HVAC plans are recommended documentation (5.6.3). Location-keyed, photographed findings are what let the scope identify components (2.2); a single grade or an energy estimate tells no one where to clean.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection report","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.3, §1.10, §5.6.3","units":["cvi:photo-keying"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0332","stem":"Your inspection could not reach the ducts above a hard lid ceiling in the lobby. How should the report handle those ducts?","options":["State it as a limit of the inspection","Leave it out to keep the report short","Rate them clean, since none were seen","Rate them dirty, to be safe on scope"],"expl":"The assessment rests on documented, evaluated information (ACR 2025 1.10), so an area you could not see is recorded as a limitation, not guessed at. Rating unseen ducts either way invents a finding, and omitting the gap hides it from the client who relies on your scope.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection report","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.10","units":["cvi:not-inspected"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0333","stem":"While inspecting a 1965 school, you find damaged, friable pipe wrap in the AHU room that you suspect contains asbestos. What does ACR 2025 recommend?","options":["Consult a qualified IEP or health and safety professional","Sample it yourself and send it to an accredited lab","Clean around it under Level 1 containment controls","Ignore it, since it lies outside the duct system"],"expl":"When exposure to hazardous materials is possible, ACR 2025 1.8 recommends consulting a qualified IEP or a health and safety professional; it is a recommendation, not a shall. Materials that agencies deem hazardous then follow applicable codes and regulations (3.5.1).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection report","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.8","units":["acr:1.8-a","acr:1.8-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0334","stem":"A client wants your post-cleaning verification report to confirm the ducts are free of asbestos and lead. What should you tell her?","options":["Verification tests for particulate, but it does not cover asbestos or lead","A passing Method 3 result clears the ducts of asbestos and lead","A passing Method 2 result clears the ducts of asbestos and lead","Verification covers both, using samples sent to an accredited lab"],"expl":"Cleanliness verification determines the presence of particulate; it does not determine microbial contamination or the presence of hazardous materials (ACR 2025 5.0). Hazardous material questions go to a qualified professional and the applicable regulations (1.8, 3.5.1).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection report","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.0","units":["cvi:no-certify-claims"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0335","stem":"Your report notes several leaky transverse duct joints. The owner assumes the cleaning contract's 'restoration' line covers sealing them. What should you clarify?","options":["Sealing duct leaks lies outside restoration","Sealing duct leaks is a Level 2 containment task","Sealing duct leaks is part of the service panel seal rule","Sealing duct leaks falls under the 4.2 closure rules"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.24.1 states that restoration does not include sealing air leaks in duct systems, and repair of malfunctioning mechanical devices is also outside its scope. Clause 4.2 governs closing the service openings the contractor creates, not existing joint leaks.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection report","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.24.1","units":["acr:4.24.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0336","stem":"After remediating a Condition 3 AHU, the contractor offers a passing Method 3 result as proof the unit is back to Condition 1. What do you tell the owner?","options":["Method 3 measures dust, not microbes","The pass confirms Condition 1 status","A Method 2 pass is needed, not Method 3","The pass confirms only Condition 2"],"expl":"Verification determines particulate only; it does not determine microbial contamination (ACR 2025 5.0). The return to Condition 1 is verified by an IEP (Definitions). Switching to Method 2 does not help, since it is also a particulate test.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.0","units":["acr:5.0-b","acr:D-84"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0337","stem":"The contractor finished a supply branch Friday and wants to run the AHU over the weekend, then verify it Monday. What do you tell him?","options":["Verify before the system runs again","Verify Monday, before occupants return","Verify within 24 hours of cleaning","Verify once all branches are finished"],"expl":"Verification shall occur immediately after the cleaning of the component and before the system operates (ACR 2025 5.1). Running the AHU first can reload or scour the branch and make the result meaningless.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.1","units":["acr:5.1-b","acr:5.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0338","stem":"The contract specifies Method 3 for all sheet metal ducts. After cleaning, the crew says a clean Method 1 visual means no vacuum test is needed. What do you tell them?","options":["Run Method 3 anyway; the spec governs","Skip it, since Method 1 found it clean","Skip it unless the owner disputes it","Run Method 2, since it covers both types"],"expl":"Procedures shall reach visibly clean at minimum, or the level of verification specified in the contract documents (ACR 2025 4.0, 5.0). Method 1 ends the process only when nothing more is specified or disputed (5.2). A contract that names Method 3 gets Method 3.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.0, §5.0","units":["acr:4.0-b","acr:5.0-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0339","stem":"The owner's rep disputes your Method 1 finding on a cleaned, lined return plenum. What is the correct escalation?","options":["Method 2, which settles it on liner","Method 3, as the final determination","Method 3 first, then Method 2 to confirm","An IEP visual review of the liner"],"expl":"When Method 1 is disputed, Method 2 or Method 3 is recommended (ACR 2025 5.2.1), but Method 3 does not apply to porous components (5.3.3). On liner, Method 2 is the end of the escalation; on sheet metal, Method 3 would make the final call.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.2.1, §5.3.3","units":["acr:5-M5","acr:5.3.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0340","stem":"A contractor trained on the 2021 edition says Method 2 cannot be used on the cleaned sheet metal plenum. What do you tell him?","options":["Incorrect; 2025 extends it to non-porous surfaces","Correct; 2025 still limits it to porous liner and duct board","Correct; sheet metal requires Method 3 in both editions","Incorrect; 2025 limits it to non-porous sheet metal ducts"],"expl":"ACR 2025 5.3 widened Method 2 to porous AND non-porous surfaces; the 2021 edition limited it to porous. The Definitions entry still says porous, but the Section 5.3 body governs. Method 3 remains non-porous only.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.3","units":["acr:5.3-a","acr:5.3-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0341","stem":"You watch a technician run Method 2 on duct board. He makes two brush passes and compares. What do you tell him?","options":["Method 2 takes four passes, not two","Two passes are right, one each way","Four passes, but only on sheet metal","Two passes are right at 5 cm per second"],"expl":"Method 2 requires four passes of the brush over the test area with light to moderate pressure (ACR 2025 5.3.1), on porous or non-porous surfaces. Two passes per slot, one each direction, at no more than 5 cm per second, is the Method 3 cassette rule (5.4.2). Mnemonic: Method 2 = 4 passes, Method 3 = 2 passes.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","units":["acr:5.3.1-b","acr:X-5"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0342","stem":"For Method 2, a technician brings a HEPA-filtered shop vacuum rated at 60 inches WC static lift. Is it acceptable?","options":["No; it needs at least 80 inches WC","Yes; HEPA is the only requirement","Yes; 40 inches WC is the minimum","No; it needs at least 100 inches WC"],"expl":"The Method 2 contact vacuum shall be HEPA-filtered AND achieve at least 80 inches of static lift (WC), with a 2.5 inch round nylon brush on a 1.5 inch hose (ACR 2025 5.3.1). HEPA alone is half the spec, and 60 inches falls short.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","units":["acr:5.3.1-e","acr:5.3.1-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0343","stem":"Setting up to witness Method 2, which brush and hose combination should you require on the contact vacuum?","options":["2.5 in. round nylon brush, 1.5 in. hose","1.5 in. round nylon brush, 2.5 in. hose","2.5 in. horsehair brush, 1.25 in. hose","37 mm nylon brush on a 1.5 in. hose"],"expl":"ACR 2025 5.3.1 specifies a 2.5 inch round nylon brush on a 1.5 inch diameter vacuum hose, on a HEPA contact vacuum with at least 80 inches WC lift. The swapped pair is the classic trap, and 37 mm is the Method 3 filter size. Mnemonic: bigger brush, smaller hose.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","units":["acr:5.3.1-f","acr:5.3.1-g"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0344","stem":"During Method 2, the technician bears down hard on the brush to 'really test it.' What should you tell him?","options":["Use light to moderate brush pressure, as in routine cleaning","Use firm pressure so the brush reaches embedded dust","Use no downward pressure, letting the brush float","Use enough pressure to flex the liner surface"],"expl":"Method 2 brushing uses light to moderate pressure, as in routine cleaning (ACR 2025 5.3.1). 'No downward pressure' is the Method 3 rule for the cassette on the template (5.4.2). Heavy pressure distorts the before-and-after comparison.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","units":["acr:5.3.1-c","acr:5.4.2-n"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0345","stem":"Method 1 on a cleaned trunk shows old mastic streaks and stains at the joints but no loose debris. What is your verdict?","options":["Visibly clean, since mastic and stains are adhered substances","Not clean until the mastic streaks are removed","Not clean until the joint stains are scrubbed off","Inconclusive; mastic and stains need a Method 3 test"],"expl":"Visibly clean means free of non-adhered substances (ACR 2025 5.2, Definitions), and mastic, sealant, paint, and stains are the Standard's examples of adhered substances that Section 4 procedures do not remove. Stains therefore do not fail Method 1.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Verification","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.2; Definitions","units":["acr:D-8","acr:D-9"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0346","stem":"After cleaning a lined supply trunk, the contractor asks you to run the NADCA vacuum test on the liner. What do you tell him?","options":["Use Method 2; Method 3 is non-porous only","Run it at 17 L/min, as on sheet metal","Run it only after Method 2 is disputed","Run it with the template taped at corners"],"expl":"The NADCA Vacuum Test (Method 3) evaluates non-porous surfaces and does not apply to porous components (ACR 2025 5.3.3, 5.4). Fibrous glass liner is porous, so Method 1, then Method 2 if disputed, is the whole escalation. The other options borrow real Method 3 details (17 L/min, taped corners) and apply them to the wrong surface.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.3.3, §5.4","units":["acr:5.3.3-b","acr:5-M5"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0347","stem":"You arrive to witness a vacuum test and find the AHU still running to 'keep the duct negative.' What do you require?","options":["Shut the AHU off while sampling","Keep it running so the duct stays negative","Run the AHU fan at low speed for the test","Keep it running if the filters are new"],"expl":"During Method 3 sampling the air handler shall not operate (ACR 2025 5.4.2). Negative pressure is a cleaning-phase control (4.1), and airflow across the template would disturb the sample.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","units":["acr:5.4.2-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0348","stem":"The crew just wet-wiped a sheet metal plenum and wants to run the vacuum test right away while the surface is still damp. What do you say?","options":["Wait until the sampled surface has dried out completely","Go ahead; the lab dries the cassette filters first","Go ahead, sampling at 15 L/min to limit pickup","Wait a full 24 hours after any wet cleaning"],"expl":"The sampled surface shall be dry for Method 3 (ACR 2025 5.4.2). No fixed waiting period exists; dry is the test. The pump draws at least 15 L/min (5.4.1) and is set to 17 L/min, plus or minus 2, before sampling (5.4.2), and no flow rate makes a damp surface acceptable.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","units":["acr:5.4.2-c"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0349","stem":"A technician sets his sampling pump to 15 L/min 'because the Standard says 15.' What do you tell him?","options":["Set it to 17 L/min, plus or minus 2","Keep 15; that is the sampling flow","Set it to 15 L/min, plus or minus 5%","Set it to 2 L/min, per NIOSH 0500"],"expl":"Set the sampling flow to 17 L/min, plus or minus 2. The pump must be capable of at least 15 L/min and the calibrator accurate at 15 L/min. NIOSH 0500's 1 to 2 L/min is an air sampling rate. Mnemonic: capable of 15, calibrate at 15, sample at 17.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1, §5.4.2","units":["cvi:niosh-0500-0600"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0350","stem":"You watch a technician drag the cassette once down each template slot at about 10 cm per second. Which correction do you make?","options":["Two passes per slot, at 5 cm/s or less","Four passes per slot, at 5 cm/s or less","Two passes per slot, at 10 cm/s or less","Four passes per slot, at 2.5 cm/s max"],"expl":"Each template opening is vacuumed twice, once in each direction, at no more than 5 cm per second, about 5 seconds per slot each way (ACR 2025 5.4.2). Four passes is the Method 2 brush count (5.3.1).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","units":["acr:5.4.2-m","acr:5.4.2-j"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0351","stem":"During the vacuum test, the technician presses the cassette down so it scrapes the duct metal through the slot. What do you tell him?","options":["Rest its edges on the template with no downward pressure","Press it lightly on the metal, as in routine cleaning","Press it on the metal hard enough to lift adhered dust","Hold it just clear of the template, a finger's width above the slot"],"expl":"The cassette edges always rest on the template with no downward pressure, and the cassette shall not touch the duct surface (ACR 2025 5.4.2); the template holds it up so accelerated airflow lifts the particulate. Light to moderate routine pressure is the Method 2 brush rule.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","units":["acr:5.4.2-n","acr:5.4.2-k","acr:5.4.2-l"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0352","stem":"The lab reports 0.72 mg/100 cm2 net weight on a sheet metal trunk sample. The contractor calls it a fail because '0.7 mg is the limit.' How do you rule?","options":["It passes; the debris limit is 0.75 mg/100 cm2","It fails; the limit is 0.70 mg/100 cm2","It fails; 0.72 mg/100 cm2 exceeds the 0.7 mg limit","It is void; the scale reads to 0.7 mg"],"expl":"The passing criterion is a net debris weight not exceeding 0.75 mg/100 cm2 (ACR 2025 5.5), so 0.72 passes. 0.7 mg is the minimum scale sensitivity (5.4.2), a lab equipment spec, not the pass line.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.5, §5.4.2","units":["acr:5.5-a","acr:5.4.2-r"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0353","stem":"The cleaning contractor offers to pull the Method 3 samples himself to save the owner money. Who does ACR 2025 recommend procure them?","options":["A qualified agent acting for the client","The cleaning contractor's own tech","The lab that will later weigh the filters","The supplier that sealed the cassettes"],"expl":"ACR 2025 5.4.2 recommends that a qualified individual of the client or the client's agent procure the samples, and that an accredited lab analyze them. Because it is a recommendation the contractor is not barred, but the independent sampler is what the Standard advises. The supplier's role is sealing new cassettes with shrink tape.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","units":["acr:5.4.2-u"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0354","stem":"A lab quote for Method 3 analysis lists 'NIOSH 0600, respirable fraction.' What should you ask the lab to use instead?","options":["NIOSH 0500, for total nuisance dust","NIOSH 0600, reported in mg per 100 cm2","ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001, for fire residue","NIOSH 0500, for the respirable fraction"],"expl":"Method 3 analysis shall follow NIOSH Method 0500 for total nuisance dust (ACR 2025 5.4.2). NIOSH 0500 is 'Particulates Not Otherwise Regulated, Total'; NIOSH 0600 is the respirable method and uses a cyclone. Results are then reported in mg/100 cm2.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":1,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2; NIOSH NMAM 0500","units":["cvi:niosh-0500-0600"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0355","stem":"A Method 3 cassette reaches the lab unmarked, with nothing showing how much surface was tested. What does ACR 2025 require the sampler to do?","options":["Label it and record the sampled area","Let the lab assign an ID on arrival","Photograph it beside the template","Record only the pump flow rate used"],"expl":"The cassette shall be labeled and the sampled surface area recorded (ACR 2025 5.4.2), since results are expressed per 100 cm2. Photos are recommended project documentation (5.6.3), not the cassette record.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","units":["acr:5.4.2-p"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0356","stem":"The Method 3 cassettes will be shipped to an outside lab for weighing. Which record must go with them for your closeout file?","options":["Chain of custody documentation","An IEP's signed sampling strategy","The calibration log for the lab scale","The owner's signed sample approval"],"expl":"When outside laboratories or agencies do the work, chain of custody documentation shall apply (ACR 2025 5.6.2); it records seizure, custody, control, transfer, analysis, and disposition of the samples. A copy of the lab results shall also go in the documentation (5.6.1).","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Documentation","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.6.2","units":["acr:5.6.2-a","acr:D-90"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0357","stem":"The contractor's closeout binder states 'Method 3: PASS' but holds no lab report. What does ACR 2025 require?","options":["A copy of the lab results in the file","A pass statement signed by the ASCS","A photo of each cassette and template","A second test witnessed by the owner"],"expl":"When the NADCA Vacuum Test is used, a copy of the lab results shall go into the post-project documentation (ACR 2025 5.6.1), and chain of custody applies for outside labs (5.6.2). A signed summary or photos do not replace the lab sheet.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Documentation","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.6.1","units":["acr:5.6.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0358","stem":"A technician's homemade Method 3 template is cut from 40 mil plastic with two 2 x 25 cm slots. What is wrong with it?","options":["The thickness; it should be 15 mil","The slot width; it should be 3.7 cm","The slot count; it should be four","The area; it should total 50 cm2"],"expl":"The template shall be 15 mil (0.381 mm) thick with two 2 x 25 cm slots at least 2.5 cm apart, giving 100 cm2 (ACR 2025 5.4.1). 3.7 cm is the maximum width for an alternate slot, not the standard width, and 50 cm2 is one slot's area.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":1,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","units":["acr:5.4.1-g","acr:5.4.1-i"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0359","stem":"A technician loads a single 25 mm PVC filter in an open-face cassette for Method 3. Which setup should you require?","options":["Two 37 mm MCE filters, closed-face","A single 37 mm PVC filter, closed-face","Two 25 mm MCE filters, open-face, in series","A single 37 mm MCE filter, open-face"],"expl":"Method 3 uses a closed-face three-piece cassette preloaded with two 37 mm mixed cellulose ester matched weight filters in series, pore size 0.8 or 0.45 micrometer (ACR 2025 5.4.1). A single 37 mm PVC filter is what NIOSH 0500 uses for air sampling, which is why it looks right.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"NADCA vacuum test","tier":1,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","units":["cvi:niosh-0500-0600"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0360","stem":"An owner asks which parts of your closeout documentation ACR 2025 actually requires. Which item is mandatory rather than recommended?","options":["Lab results when Method 3 is used","Photos of every component cleaned","The HVAC plans marked with findings","Submittal forms and warranty papers"],"expl":"A copy of the lab results after a NADCA Vacuum Test is a shall (ACR 2025 5.6.1), as is chain of custody for outside labs (5.6.2). Documentation of all work (5.6), photos and HVAC plans, and submittals, warranties, and guarantees (5.6.3) are recommendations.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Documentation","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.6","units":["acr:5.6.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0361","stem":"A cruise ship HVAC cleaning project closes out. What re-inspection intervals should your report recommend for the AHU, supply ducts, and return ducts, per the ACR inspection schedule?","options":["AHU yearly; supply and return ducts every 2 years","AHU, supply, and return ducts each yearly","AHU, supply, and return ducts every 2 years","AHU and return yearly; supply every 2 years"],"expl":"Table 1 in ACR 2025 1.5.1 treats Marine like Residential: AHU 1 year, supply 2 years, return and exhaust 2 years. Commercial, industrial, and healthcare are 1 year across the board. Mnemonic: the AHU is always 1 year; homes and ships stretch the ducts to 2.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Re-inspection interval","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1 Table 1","units":["acr:1.5.1-e","acr:1.5.1-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0362","stem":"A commercial office sits next to an active quarry, and your report documents heavy dust loading. What re-inspection advice fits ACR 2025?","options":["Shorter than the 1-year Table 1 minimum","The 1-year Table 1 interval, no sooner","The 2-year interval used for its ductwork","Longer, since cleaning was just done"],"expl":"Table 1 intervals are minimum recommendations, and inspections are recommended more often when geographical, human, or mechanical conditions make it necessary (ACR 2025 1.5.1). Commercial buildings are 1 year for AHU, supply, and return; the 2-year duct figure belongs to residential and marine.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Re-inspection interval","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1","units":["acr:1.5.1-c","acr:1.5.1-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0363","stem":"A hospital's ducts were inspected three months ago. Today you find suspect microbial growth in its AHU. When should the supply and return ducts be inspected?","options":["During this same inspection","At the next 1-year Table 1 interval","After the AHU remediation is complete","After an IEP confirms Condition 3"],"expl":"Suspect microbial contamination in the AHU shall start an inspection of the supply and return ducts during that same inspection, and this replaces the Table 1 interval for that system (ACR 2025 1.9.1). A recent duct inspection does not change that.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Re-inspection interval","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","units":["acr:1.9.1-b","acr:1.9.1-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0364","stem":"A facility manager wants your re-inspection interval built into the building's formal commercial HVAC inspection and maintenance program. Which standard does ACR 2025 name for that kind of program?","options":["ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180","ANSI/IICRC Standard S590","ANSI/IKECA Standard C10","ANSI/IESO/RIA Standard 6001"],"expl":"ACR 2025 1.5 recommends routine component inspections as part of the maintenance, energy, and IAQ plan, following ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 (with NFPA 90A), and lists work done under a 180 program as a reason to clean. Standard 180 sets minimum inspection and maintenance requirements for commercial HVAC. S590 is post-damage HVAC assessment, IKECA C10 is kitchen exhaust, and 6001 is fire residue.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Maintenance programs","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.5; ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180","units":["cvi:ashrae-180-role"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0365","stem":"A building's ASHRAE 180 program calls for duct cleaning on a set cycle, but your inspection finds little debris. How does ACR 2025 treat cleaning done as part of that program?","options":["As one of the listed, recommended reasons to clean","As one of the listed, mandatory reasons to clean","As routine maintenance work that falls outside the ACR scope","As a reason you may cite, but not before a fail"],"expl":"The need-to-clean list in ACR 2025 includes work that is part of an ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 maintenance program, and every item on that list is a recommendation, not a requirement. When the need is in question, Method 2 or Method 3 may be used to decide, but no test failure is a precondition.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Maintenance programs","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 Determining the need to clean","units":["acr:N-11","acr:N-0"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0366","stem":"An owner wants to skip your post-project inspection to save a day, since the crew 'already looked at everything.' What does ACR 2025 say?","options":["It is mandatory, like the one before","It is recommended, unlike the one before","It may be waived once Method 1 is signed","It is required unless the owner has waived it"],"expl":"ACR 2025 1.1 says an inspection shall occur before AND after the cleaning and restoration project. Routine inspections as part of a maintenance plan are the recommended ones (1.1-c), which is the trap behind the 'recommended' option.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Post-project inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.1","units":["acr:1.1-b","acr:1.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0367","stem":"A CVI will open supply ductwork above an occupied open office to photograph its interior. Under ACR 2025, what is the obligation for engineering controls during that inspection?","options":["Required during the inspection visit itself","Recommended once the cleaning scope is written","Required once a Level 2 scope is written","Optional during a brief, visual inspection"],"expl":"ACR 2025 1.4 makes appropriate engineering controls for the general workspace a SHALL during the inspection itself, because opening the system disturbs settled particulate. The trap is thinking controls begin with the cleaning scope; they apply from the first opened access door.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Controls in inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.4","units":["acr:1.4-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0368","stem":"According to ACR 2025, what is the primary environmental concern when an inspection opens an HVAC system?","options":["Disturbance of particulate that settled on interior surfaces","Release of disturbed particles into occupied areas","Entry of combustion exhaust at the outdoor intake","Exposure of occupants to cleaning product vapors"],"expl":"ACR 1.4 names disturbance of settled particulate as the primary concern and the release of those disturbed particles into occupied areas as the second concern. The trap is picking the consequence (migration) over the cause (disturbance).","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Controls in inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.4","units":["acr:1.4-b","acr:1.4-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0369","stem":"During post-cleaning verification, a CVI sees the contractor's portable vacuum exhausting into a mechanical room inside the building through a standard cartridge filter. How should the CVI record this?","options":["Deviation, since indoor vacuum exhaust requires HEPA","Acceptable, since the mechanical room is unoccupied","Acceptable, since an air scrubber runs in the same room","Deviation, but just on Level 2 or higher projects"],"expl":"ACR 3.3 and 4.1.2 require HEPA filtration (99.97% at 0.3 micron) on any vacuum equipment that exhausts inside the building envelope, on every project. An air scrubber is a supplemental control (3.6) and does not excuse a non-HEPA exhaust; non-HEPA units must exhaust outdoors (4.1.3).","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"HEPA exhaust","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.3, §4.1.2","units":["acr:3.3-a","acr:4.1.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0370","stem":"At noon on a hospital project, the wind shifts and exhaust from the contractor's vacuum truck drifts toward a rooftop outdoor air intake. Which ACR 2025 duty should the CVI's scope have carried for this case?","options":["Keeping the truck location monitored and managed","Setting the truck location once, at mobilization","Moving the truck once an indoor CO alarm sounds","Choosing a truck location the hospital IEP approves"],"expl":"ACR 3.2 requires generators, vacuum trucks, and compressors to be positioned so emissions cannot enter, and 3.2.1 requires that location to be monitored and managed for the whole project. A one-time placement check misses exactly this wind-shift case.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Fuel equipment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.2, §3.2.1","units":["acr:3.2.1-a","acr:3.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0371","stem":"An owner's representative tells the CVI that ACR 2025 requires the duct section being cleaned to hold at least -0.02 inches of water column. How should the CVI respond?","options":["ACR sets no number, only pressure that stops migration","ACR sets that value, but for Level 2 containments only","ACR sets that value, verified at every access opening","ACR sets that value only for healthcare occupancies"],"expl":"ACR 2025 gives no numeric negative pressure anywhere; it requires continuous negative pressure, verifiable at representative locations, that keeps debris from entering occupied space or leaving the work area (3.4, Definitions). The -0.02 inch figure is OSHA's minimum for an asbestos negative pressure enclosure (1926.1101), not an ACR value.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Negative pressure","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.4; Definitions","units":["cvi:npe-002"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0372","stem":"A CVI specified Level 1 containment for a small office. The owner's representative says the contractor violated Level 1 by not building poly barrier walls. What is correct?","options":["Barriers first appear at Level 2, so none were missing","Barriers are part of Level 1, so the plan fell short","Barriers are part of Level 1 when the space is occupied","Barriers first appear at Level 3, with the decon unit"],"expl":"Level 1 (ACR 3.9) has three parts: negative pressure in the component, clean protective coverings, and controls that limit cross-contamination. Temporary barriers are the first element added at Level 2 (3.10.2). Mnemonic: L1 is pressure, cover, control; L2 adds walls and floor.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.9, §3.10.2","units":["acr:3.9-b","acr:3-L1"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0373","stem":"On a Level 2 job, the contractor builds barrier walls of 4-mil poly but lays a two-layer 6-mil fire retardant floor run 6 inches up the walls. Judged against ACR 2025 alone, how should the CVI classify the 4-mil walls?","options":["A departure from a suggestion, not a rule","A departure from a requirement stated in 3.10.2.3","A departure from a requirement shared with Level 1","A departure the IEP approves before any work resumes"],"expl":"The 6-mil fire retardant barrier material in 3.10.2.3 is only recommended, while the two-layer 6-mil floor turned up 6 inches (3.10.3) is mandatory. Barriers themselves are required at Level 2 (3.10.2); their material specification is not. Level 1 has no barrier requirement at all.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.3, §3.10.3","units":["acr:3.10-c","acr:X-3"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0374","stem":"On a Level 3 project, the real-time monitor shows airborne particulate above the pre-setup baseline, and work stops. The crew then repairs a torn chamber flap. When may work restart under ACR 2025?","options":["After levels return to baseline and the cause is fixed","After the flap is fixed, once a manometer confirms pressure","After levels return to baseline, even with cause unknown","After the scrubbers run one hour at four air changes"],"expl":"ACR 3.11.3 requires work to stop when airborne particulate exceeds background and to restart only when levels return to background AND the cause has been corrected. Either condition alone is not enough, and a pressure reading says nothing about particulate.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Containment levels","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","units":["acr:3.11.3-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0375","stem":"The CVI's scope for an occupied school calls for a chemical coil cleaner. Which control does ACR 2025 require for the product's emissions?","options":["Following the label with exhaust where needed","Keeping the SDS at the contractor's office for review","Running HEPA scrubbers at four air changes per hour","Obtaining an IEP clearance before each application"],"expl":"ACR 3.7 requires cleaning agents to be used per the manufacturer's procedures and application instructions, with exhaust ventilation where required. SDSs must stay on site for the whole project (2.7.1), and 4 air changes per hour is the Level 2 air-cleaning rule, not a product-emission control.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Product emissions","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.7, §2.7.1","units":["acr:3.7-b","acr:3.7-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0376","stem":"A contractor's work plan for an occupied office lists 'P100 respirators for all crew' as its engineering control. How should the CVI evaluate that entry?","options":["Deficient, because PPE does not modify the workspace","Adequate, because P100 media counts as HEPA filtration","Adequate, because respirators act as source control","Adequate, if each worker is fit tested yearly"],"expl":"ACR defines environmental engineering controls as modifications to the work environment that prevent escape or transfer of contaminants, and Section 3 names no PPE at all. Respirators protect the wearer but do nothing to stop cross-contamination of the occupied space. Annual fit testing (OSHA 1910.134) is a respirator rule, not an engineering control.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Specifying controls","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.0; Definitions","units":["cvi:fit-test"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0377","stem":"A CVI writes a Level 1 scope for a school. Under ACR 2025, what is the status of ambient air cleaning with HEPA air scrubbers?","options":["Recommended as a supplemental control, with no ACH rate set","Recommended as a supplemental control, at four ACH","Required as a supplemental control, at a 4 ACH minimum","Required as a supplemental control, with no ACH rate set"],"expl":"ACR 2025 3.6 recommends HEPA air scrubbers as a supplemental control and dropped the 2021 figure of 4 air changes per hour. The only ACH number left in the 2025 edition is the mandatory 4 ACH inside a Level 2 containment (3.10.7), which a Level 1 scope does not trigger.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Ambient air cleaning","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.6, §3.10.7","units":["acr:3.6-c","acr:3.6-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0378","stem":"In the ACR 2025 sequence, which step first produces a determination of the required environmental engineering controls?","options":["The inspection and its system component assessment","The contractor's written work plan before cleaning","The post-project documentation after verification","The IEP's assessment of microbial cause and extent"],"expl":"ACR 1.0 and 1.10 require the inspection and its system component assessment to determine the required environmental engineering controls. The contractor's work plan (2.0, 2.2) then carries those controls into the scope, so the inspector specifies and the contractor executes.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Specifying controls","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.0, §1.10, §2.0","units":["acr:1.10-e","acr:1.0-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0379","stem":"Which statement about engineering controls does ACR 2025 Section 3.13 make a recommendation rather than a requirement?","options":["An IEP gives advice on project-specific controls","Appropriate controls are used on every project","Prevention of cross-contamination is a priority","Protection of workers and occupants is a priority"],"expl":"In ACR 3.13, using appropriate controls on every project, protecting workers and occupants, and preventing cross-contamination are SHALL statements; IEP advice on project-specific controls is only recommended (3.13-e). The pattern: the Standard recommends outside expertise but mandates the outcomes.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Specifying controls","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.13","units":["acr:3.13-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0380","stem":"Before inspecting air handlers that serve an occupied hospital wing, what does ACR 2025 require the inspector to understand first?","options":["The effect of the inspection on the building and its occupants","The cleaning method the contractor will propose","The Table 1 interval last applied to each air handler","The IEP's Condition rating from earlier sampling"],"expl":"ACR 1.2.1 makes a pre-inspection risk assessment a SHALL: the inspector must understand how the inspection will affect the building and its occupants, which then drives the controls used during the visit (1.4). The contractor's cleaning method comes later in the work plan (2.3), the Table 1 interval is a scheduling fact, and an IEP Condition rating is a microbial assessment (1.9), not the inspector's risk assessment.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Controls in inspection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.2.1","units":["acr:1.2.1-a","acr:1.2.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0381","stem":"A CVI's specification lists four equipment hygiene tasks. Which one does ACR 2025 recommend rather than require?","options":["Verifying vacuum maintenance on site before work starts","Cleaning and inspecting equipment before it enters the site","Cleaning or sealing vacuums before moving within the building","Servicing equipment during the project as conditions need"],"expl":"ACR 3.1.1 (clean and inspect before entering the site), 3.1.2 (service during the project), and 3.1.3 (clean or seal before relocating or leaving) are SHALL items. The on-site maintenance verification of vacuum equipment directly below them (3.1.4) is only recommended.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Equipment hygiene","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.1.1 to §3.1.4","units":["acr:3.1.4-a","acr:3.1.4-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0382","stem":"An occupant tells the CVI her asthma flares at work and asks whether the ductwork is the cause. What response stays within the CVI's role?","options":["Record system conditions and refer her to a physician","Attribute her symptoms to visible debris in the ducts","Recommend cleaning as the treatment for her symptoms","Confirm the cause once Method 3 results come back"],"expl":"Inspectors document system conditions; diagnosing health effects is outside the role, and ACR 2025 gives no warranty that cleaning corrects IAQ concerns (General). Method 3 weighs particulate only and does not determine microbial contamination or cause (5.0).","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Health claims","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 General; §5.0","units":["cvi:no-health-causation"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0383","stem":"In a state that licenses mold assessors, an unlicensed CVI sees suspect growth on an AHU cooling coil. What may the CVI's report state?","options":["The observed condition and a referral to a licensee","A Condition 3 determination based on visible growth","A Condition 2 determination pending the lab results","A Condition 1 finding once the coil is cleaned"],"expl":"ACR 1.2 requires anyone determining Condition 1, 2, or 3 to be qualified by training and experience and licensed where the law requires it. An unlicensed CVI records what was seen and refers the Condition call; 1.9 recommends an IEP or other appropriate professional assess cause and extent.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Qualifications","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.2, §1.9","units":["acr:1.2-g","acr:1.9-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0384","stem":"While inspecting a furnace cabinet under a duct-cleanliness contract, the CVI sees a cracked heat exchanger. What should the report do?","options":["Document it as unsafe and notify the client","Omit it, since gas-control repair is outside ACR scope","Refer it to the IEP for assessment before recording it","Note it in the contractor's internal job file alone"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.6 requires damaged or unsafe components seen during inspection to be documented, even though repair of gas and oil controls is outside the Standard's scope (General). Documentation plus client notice is the inspector's defense; silence is the liability.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Damaged components","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7.6; General","units":["acr:1.7.6-a","acr:G-8"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0385","stem":"A system passed Method 3 at 0.4 mg/100 cm2. The owner wants the CVI's report to add 'system is free of mold and asbestos.' Why can't the CVI write that?","options":["Verification is for particulate, not mold or hazardous materials","The Method 3 pass limit is 0.075 mg per 100 square centimeters","A pass requires Method 2, then Method 3, on the same surface","An IEP rather than the CVI signs off on Method 3 verifications"],"expl":"ACR 5.0 says verification determines the presence of particulate only; it does not determine microbial contamination or hazardous materials. Writing 'mold-free' on a gravimetric pass certifies something the test never measured. The limit is 0.75 mg/100 cm2 (5.5), so this system did pass.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Report language","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.0, §5.5","units":["cvi:no-certify-claims"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0386","stem":"In a dispute, opposing counsel claims the CVI's Method 3 cassettes could have been swapped before analysis. Which record answers that claim?","options":["Chain of custody records showing each transfer and handler","Pump calibration record at 15 L/min within 5 percent","Laboratory accreditation for NIOSH Method 0500","Photos of each template taped at its four corners"],"expl":"ACR defines chain of custody documentation as the record of seizure, custody, control, transfer, analysis, and disposition of samples, and 5.6.2 requires it when outside labs do the work. Calibration (5.4.1) and lab accreditation (5.4.2) show the test was run correctly, not that the cassette is the same one.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Documentation defense","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §5.6.2; Definitions","units":["acr:D-90","acr:5.6.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0387","stem":"A CVI's firm inspects a school and will also bid the cleaning. The supply ducts are visibly clean, but the sales manager wants the report to recommend full cleaning plus an antimicrobial fogging. Which principle governs the report?","options":["Recommend only services the findings show are needed","Recommend cleaning whenever the Table 1 interval ends","Recommend treatment once visibly clean is confirmed","Recommend the full scope if the client is told of the bid"],"expl":"NADCA's member Code of Ethics commits firms to accurate inspections used to decide what cleaning is required, if any, and to provide only necessary and desired services. Table 1 sets inspection intervals, not cleaning intervals, and disclosing the dual role does not make an unsupported scope acceptable.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Conflict of interest","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA Code of Ethics items 4-5; ACR 2025 §1.5.1","units":["cvi:coi-necessary"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0388","stem":"A CVI cannot reach a return plenum above a fixed hard-lid ceiling. How should the inspection report handle that plenum?","options":["List it as not inspected and state the reason","Rate it dirty so the cleaning scope will include it","Rate it clean because adjoining ducts were clean","Leave it out since the contract said visual inspection"],"expl":"List it as not inspected and state the reason. ACR 1.10 requires inspection information to be documented, and an unseen component gives none. Inferring its condition reports something nobody saw, and rating it dirty to grow the scope breaks the NADCA Code of Ethics.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Scope limitations","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.7, §1.10; NADCA Code of Ethics item 4","units":["cvi:not-inspected","cvi:coi-necessary"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0389","stem":"Which claim against a CVI firm is professional liability (errors and omissions) insurance designed to cover, rather than general liability?","options":["A report missed contamination and cost the client money","A tenant tripped over an inspection camera cable","A ladder cracked a skylight during the rooftop walk","Coil rinse water stained the carpet below the air handler"],"expl":"Professional liability, also called E&O, covers claims of negligence, misrepresentation, and inaccurate advice that general liability does not. Bodily injury to third parties and damage to their property are the general liability exposures (Insurance Information Institute).","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Insurance","tier":0,"ref":"Insurance Information Institute","units":["cvi:eo-vs-gl"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0390","stem":"A CVI retires and lets his claims-made E&O policy lapse. A year later a client sues over an inspection he did while the policy was active. What is the likely coverage result?","options":["No coverage, as the policy lapsed before the suit","Coverage, as the policy was in force at the inspection","Coverage, as claims-made policies follow the work date","No coverage, as E&O policies exclude inspection work"],"expl":"Most professional liability policies are claims-made: the policy must be in effect both when the event happened and when the suit is filed. An occurrence policy is the type that covers an event from the policy period even if the suit comes later, which is why the Insurance Information Institute flags it for people who retire or change careers.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Insurance","tier":1,"ref":"Insurance Information Institute","units":["cvi:claims-made"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0391","stem":"A tenant asks the CVI for a copy of the duct inspection report that the landlord commissioned. The report shows no immediate safety hazard. What is the right course?","options":["Release it only if the landlord, as client, approves","Release it, since occupants are entitled to IAQ data","Release the photos but withhold the written findings","Refer the tenant to the IEP who reviewed the report"],"expl":"The report belongs to the client. Inspection codes of ethics bar disclosing results or client information without client approval, with a discretionary exception for immediate safety hazards to exposed occupants (ASHI Code of Ethics 2.C). Releasing part of the report is still a disclosure.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Confidentiality","tier":0,"ref":"ASHI Code of Ethics 2.C (inspection profession norm)","units":["cvi:confidentiality"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0392","stem":"Before cutting inspection ports in insulated ductwork in a 1975 office building, what should the CVI firm obtain from the building owner?","options":["The owner's survey of the asbestos present","The owner's lead-safe firm certification from EPA","The owner's EPA NESHAP notice filed 10 days ahead","The owner's clearance report from the most recent IEP"],"expl":"OSHA 1926.1101(k)(2) requires building owners to determine the presence, location, and quantity of ACM and PACM before work begins and to notify employers bidding the work. Thermal system insulation in a building built no later than 1980 is presumed asbestos, so the survey comes before any cut. RRP certification belongs to the renovating firm, not the owner.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Asbestos awareness","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1926.1101(k)(2)","units":["cvi:owner-acm-survey","cvi:pacm","cvi:neshap-notice","cvi:rrp-firm"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0393","stem":"During an inspection, the owner asks the CVI to fix a failed VAV box damper actuator and rewire it while the ceiling is open. What is the appropriate response?","options":["Record the failure and refer repair to that trade","Repair it, since ACR lists VAV boxes as components","Repair it, provided the fix precedes verification","Omit it, since electrical parts fall outside ACR"],"expl":"ACR's scope excludes mechanical repair of electrical and pneumatic components (General), but 1.7.6 still requires damaged components to be documented. NADCA's member Code of Ethics also directs members to use other trades when the work calls for skills they do not have.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Qualifications","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 General; §1.7.6; NADCA Code of Ethics item 7","units":["cvi:other-trades"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0394","stem":"Site conditions make an ACR 2025 recommended practice impractical, and the CVI's scope departs from it. How does the Standard treat that departure?","options":["It may be justified by experience and professional judgment","It is barred, as the Standard permits no deviation","It may not proceed without written NADCA approval","It may not proceed unless an IEP concurs in writing"],"expl":"ACR 2025's General section says common sense, experience, and professional judgment may justify a deviation in certain circumstances. 'No deviation' describes SHALL requirements (G-19), and the Standard names no approval body for departures. Record the reason in the scope.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Standard of care","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 General","units":["acr:G-18"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0395","stem":"A plenum is classified as a permit-required confined space. Without a permit, the CVI leans his head and one arm through its access door to take a photo. Under 1910.146, has entry occurred?","options":["Yes, entry is any body part breaking the plane","No, entry requires the whole body inside the space","No, entry occurs once assigned work begins in the space","Yes, provided the atmosphere has tested hazardous"],"expl":"OSHA 1910.146 says entry occurs as soon as any part of the entrant's body breaks the plane of an opening into the permit space. A head and arm through the door is entry, so the permit program applies before the photo, not after.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Confined spaces","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","units":["cvi:cs-entry"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0396","stem":"Under OSHA's general industry rule (1910.28), at what height does fall protection for an unprotected side or edge first become required?","options":["At 4 feet (1.2 m) or more above a lower level","At 6 feet or more above a lower level","At 24 feet or more above a lower level","At 19 inches or more above a lower level"],"expl":"General industry (1910.28(b)(1)) triggers fall protection at 4 feet; construction (1926.501(b)(1)) triggers at 6 feet. 19 inches is the construction break in elevation that requires a stairway or ladder (1926.1051), and 24 feet is the fixed-ladder height that brings a ladder safety or fall arrest requirement (1910.28(b)(9)). Mnemonic: 4 in the plant, 6 on the construction site.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Fall protection","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(1); 1926.501(b)(1)","units":["cvi:fall-gi-4","cvi:fall-cons-6","cvi:break-19","cvi:fixed-ladder-24"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0397","stem":"A CVI firm's crew will clean the coil of an RTU that sits 20 feet from the edge of a low-slope roof. The one-hour task happens once a year. Under 1910.28(b)(13), what fall protection is the minimum?","options":["No fall protection, plus a 15-foot work rule","A designated area, as used from 6 to 15 feet out","A guardrail, net, or fall arrest system on the roof","Travel restraint, since the roof is over 4 feet up"],"expl":"For work 15 feet or more from a low-slope roof edge that is both infrequent and temporary, 1910.28(b)(13)(iii) requires no fall protection, but the employer must enforce a work rule keeping workers out of the 15-foot zone without protection. A designated area is the option from 6 to under 15 feet, and conventional systems are mandatory inside 6 feet. Mnemonic: 6, 15, rule.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Fall protection","tier":1,"ref":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(13)","units":["cvi:roof-under-6","cvi:roof-6-15","cvi:roof-15"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0398","stem":"Before any cleaning contract starts, a CVI walks a low-slope roof to assess access to the RTUs. No guardrails, anchors, or other fall protection are installed. How does 1910.28 apply to this walk?","options":["It is exempt as a pre-work assessment of conditions","It applies, since the 4-foot trigger covers roof edges","It applies, requiring a designated area for each trip","It applies, requiring fall arrest within 15 feet"],"expl":"1910.28(a)(2)(ii) says the duty-to-have-fall-protection section does not apply when employers are inspecting or assessing conditions before work starts or after all work is done, unless compliant systems are installed and available. The construction subpart has the same inspection exception (1926.500(a)(1)). Once cleaning begins, the normal triggers apply.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Fall protection","tier":1,"ref":"29 CFR 1910.28(a)(2)(ii); 1926.500(a)(1)","units":["cvi:prework-exempt"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0399","stem":"A CVI sets an extension ladder to reach the roof of a one-story building. What minimum side-rail extension above the roof edge does OSHA specify for a portable ladder used to reach an upper landing?","options":["36 inches (3 feet) above the upper landing","42 inches (3.5 feet) above the upper landing","30 inches above the upper landing surface","19 inches above the upper landing surface"],"expl":"Both 1910.23(c)(11) and 1926.1053(b)(1) require portable ladder side rails to extend at least 3 feet (36 inches) above the upper landing. 42 inches is the side-rail extension for fixed through or side-step ladders (1910.23(d)(4)), 30 inches is a fixed ladder's climbing-side clearance (1910.23(d)(13)), and 19 inches is the construction break in elevation (1926.1051).","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Ladders","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1910.23(c)(11); 1926.1053(b)(1)","units":["cvi:break-19","cvi:ladder-36","cvi:ladder-fixed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0400","stem":"A CVI leans a non-self-supporting ladder against a wall with 24 feet of working length between its foot and top support. About how far from the wall should the foot be under OSHA 1926.1053?","options":["About 6 feet, a quarter of the working length","About 8 feet, one-third of the working length","About 12 feet, one-half of the working length","About 4 feet, one-sixth of the working length"],"expl":"1926.1053(b)(5)(i) sets the foot out about one-quarter of the working length, the 4-to-1 rule, so 24 feet of working length puts the foot about 6 feet from the wall. One-third or one-half lays the ladder too flat; one-sixth stands it too steep.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Ladders","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(5)(i)","units":["cvi:ladder-4to1"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0401","stem":"Before the CVI opens an AHU fan section, the building engineer sets the thermostat to OFF and commands the unit off through the BAS. Why is that not lockout under 1910.147?","options":["Control circuit devices do not isolate energy","Thermostats may carry a tag but not a lock","The BAS command needs a second person to verify","Lockout is limited to cord-and-plug equipment"],"expl":"1910.147 states that push buttons, selector switches, and other control circuit devices are not energy isolating devices; the lock goes on a disconnect or breaker that physically blocks energy, and inspecting is listed as servicing. Cord-and-plug equipment under the worker's exclusive control is an exception to the standard, not its scope.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Lockout/tagout","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1910.147(b)","units":["cvi:loto-inspecting","cvi:loto-control-circuit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0402","stem":"Mid-year, a CVI whose employer requires respirator use switches from one brand of medium half-mask respirator to a medium half-mask of a different make. What does 1910.134 require before he wears the new one?","options":["A fit test because the facepiece make changed","A new medical evaluation before the change","A fit test at the next annual refit date","A user seal check in place of a fit test"],"expl":"1910.134(f)(2) requires fit testing before first use, whenever a different facepiece (size, style, model, or make) is used, and at least annually. A user seal check is done every time the respirator is put on but never replaces a fit test, and a brand change is not one of the listed triggers for a new medical evaluation (e)(7).","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Respiratory protection","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1910.134(f)(2)","units":["cvi:fit-test","cvi:seal-check"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0403","stem":"A CVI must specify gloves and a respirator for crews applying a coil cleaner. Which SDS section lists that information?","options":["Section 8, exposure controls and personal protection","Section 2, hazard identification for the product","Section 4, first-aid measures after an accidental exposure","Section 7, precautions for the safe handling and storage"],"expl":"Under HazCom (1910.1200 Appendix D), Section 8 of the 16-section SDS covers exposure controls and personal protection, including PPE. Section 2 identifies hazards, Section 4 covers first aid, and Section 7 covers handling and storage. Mnemonic: 8 is the gear section.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Hazard communication","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(2)","units":["cvi:sds-8","cvi:sds-2-4-7"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0404","stem":"In a building constructed in 1978, a CVI sees insulation wrapped on a boiler breeching and sprayed-on fireproofing above the ceiling. Under OSHA 1926.1101, which are presumed asbestos-containing material?","options":["Both, since TSI and surfacing material of that era are presumed","Neither, until bulk samples show more than 1% asbestos","The breeching wrap alone, as TSI is the one presumed class","The fireproofing alone, as spray is the one presumed class"],"expl":"1926.1101 defines PACM as thermal system insulation and surfacing material in buildings constructed no later than 1980, and TSI expressly includes breeching insulation. Both finds are PACM until rebutted. The over-1% figure defines confirmed ACM, but presumed material is treated as ACM without a lab result.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Asbestos awareness","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1926.1101(b)","units":["cvi:pacm","cvi:tsi-breeching"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0405","stem":"A CVI firm must cut out openings totaling 4 square feet of painted drywall in one room of a 1962 home where a toddler lives, using a hand saw that leaves each cutout intact. How does the EPA RRP rule treat this work?","options":["Exempt as minor repair, at 6 square feet or less per room","Exempt as minor repair, at 20 square feet or less per room","Covered, as any paid work in target housing is covered","Covered, as homes with a child under 6 have no exemption"],"expl":"Exempt as minor repair. RRP excludes minor HVAC work that disturbs 6 square feet or less of painted surface per room inside (20 outside), with no prohibited practice, window replacement, or demolition. A clean cut-out is not demolition. The exemption applies whether or not a child lives there.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Lead-safe work","tier":0,"ref":"40 CFR 745.83; EPA FAQ 23002-18515","units":["cvi:rrp-scope","cvi:rrp-minor","cvi:rrp-demolition"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0406","stem":"Which pairing of an HVAC contaminant source with the disease it can transmit is correct?","options":["Bat guano piled in an intake plenum and histoplasmosis","Deer mouse droppings in a return and histoplasmosis","Pigeon droppings on a louver and hantavirus","Cooling tower mist near an intake and hantavirus"],"expl":"Histoplasma lives in soil and spreads in bird and bat droppings mixed into it; people get sick by breathing the spores (CDC). Hantavirus comes from rodent urine, droppings, and nesting material, and cooling tower mist is a Legionella source.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Biological hazards","tier":0,"ref":"CDC histoplasmosis, hantavirus, Legionella","units":["cvi:legionella-growth","cvi:histo-source","cvi:hanta-source"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0407","stem":"A CVI finds fresh mouse droppings and nesting material in the return plenum of a closed-up cabin. What cleanup method does CDC guidance call for?","options":["Soak it with disinfectant, then wipe it up","HEPA vacuum the dry droppings, then disinfect","Sweep the droppings out, then fog the plenum","Blow droppings loose with compressed air"],"expl":"CDC says never to sweep or vacuum rodent urine, droppings, or nests because that puts virus-laden particles in the air; spray with bleach solution or an EPA-registered disinfectant until very wet, soak 5 minutes, then wipe up with paper towels. For rodents in ventilation systems, CDC points to a professional exterminator and duct cleaning specialists.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Biological hazards","tier":0,"ref":"CDC rodent cleanup guidance","units":["cvi:rodent-cleanup","cvi:rodent-pros"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0408","stem":"In a very hot attic, a CVI's helper becomes confused, slurs his speech, and has hot skin. What should the CVI do first?","options":["Call 911 and cool him quickly with cold water or ice","Give him water to sip and recheck in 30 minutes","Give him salt tablets and let him rest in shade","Drive him home to rest and rehydrate overnight"],"expl":"Confusion, slurred speech, and hot skin are heat stroke signs; NIOSH says call 911, stay with the worker, and cool him quickly with cold water, wet cloths, or ice. Sips of water and rest are heat exhaustion first aid, and delaying heat stroke treatment can be fatal.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Heat stress","tier":0,"ref":"NIOSH heat-related illnesses","units":["cvi:heat-stroke"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0409","stem":"A CVI's employees work 8-hour shifts in a mechanical room where their measured exposure is 88 dBA as a time-weighted average. What does 1910.95 require?","options":["A hearing conservation program, as the 85 dBA action level is met","No program, since exposure is under the 90 dBA PEL","Engineering controls to bring the level under 85 dBA","Audiometric tests that begin after a 10 dB threshold shift"],"expl":"1910.95 sets the PEL at 90 dBA for 8 hours but requires a hearing conservation program (monitoring, audiometric testing, hearing protectors) at the 85 dBA action level. Mnemonic: 85 starts the program, 90 starts the controls. A 10 dB standard threshold shift triggers follow-up, not the program itself.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Noise","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1910.95(c)","units":["cvi:noise-85","cvi:noise-90"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0410","stem":"A CVI, who is not a qualified electrical worker, carries an aluminum extension ladder across a roof near an energized overhead line of 50 kV or less. Under 1910.333, how close may that conductive ladder come to the line?","options":["No closer than ten feet from the line","No closer than fifteen feet from the line","No closer than three feet from the line","No closer than twenty feet from the line"],"expl":"For lines of 50 kV or less to ground, 1910.333(c)(3) keeps an unqualified person and any conductive object he handles at least 10 feet from unguarded energized overhead lines, plus 4 inches per 10 kV above 50 kV. Portable ladders used where they could contact exposed energized parts need nonconductive side rails (1910.333(c)(7)).","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Electrical hazards","tier":0,"ref":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(3)","units":["cvi:powerline-10","cvi:ladder-nonconductive"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0411","stem":"An inspector pulls trend records for three zones as their cooling loads fall from noon to 6 p.m. Record A: zone airflow 1,200 to 450 cfm, air to the zone 55 to 55°F. Record B: zone airflow 1,200 to 1,200 cfm, air to the zone 55 to 68°F, one inlet duct fed by a main that holds 55°F. Record C: zone airflow 1,200 to 1,200 cfm, box inlets at 55°F and 95°F, air to the zone 55 to 68°F. Which system produced Record B?","options":["Constant-volume reheat, adding heat at each zone","VAV with reheat, holding constant 55°F air as flow drops","Dual-duct constant volume, blending hot and cold ducts per box","Multizone, blending hot and cold deck air inside the AHU"],"expl":"Record B is constant-volume reheat: airflow stays fixed while the air warms between the 55°F main and the zone, so heat is added at the terminal. VAV cuts airflow first, a dual-duct box shows two inlets, and a multizone blends at the unit.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"System identification","tier":0,"ref":"DOE Minimizing Reheat Guide; PNNL VAV O&M","units":["hvac:s-cav-reheat","hvac:s-trend-id"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0412","stem":"Two VAV terminals on one floor log these readings from a cooling morning into a heating evening. Record A: primary air 1,000 to 300 cfm, discharge 1,100 cfm all day, box fan 2.1 A all day. Record B: primary air 1,000 to 300 cfm, discharge equal to primary air in cooling, box fan 0 A until the zone calls for heat, then 1.8 A with discharge at 900 cfm while primary air stays at 300 cfm. Which terminal type produced Record B?","options":["A parallel-flow fan-powered VAV box","A series-flow fan-powered VAV box","A dual-duct VAV mixing box","A single-duct VAV box with reheat"],"expl":"A parallel box's fan sits beside the primary air path and runs only in heating, so discharge tracks primary air in cooling and jumps when the fan adds plenum air (300 + 600 = 900 cfm). Record A is the series pattern: the fan runs all occupied hours and holds discharge constant. A dual-duct box can also raise airflow in heating, but it has no fan current to log.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan-powered boxes","tier":0,"ref":"Titus Engineering Corner","units":["hvac:s-series-fan","hvac:s-series-constant","hvac:s-parallel-fan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0413","stem":"On a January morning a constant-volume AHU moves 12,000 cfm, and its schedule calls for at least 2,400 cfm of outdoor air. The CVI reads outdoor air 30°F, return air 72°F, and mixed air 62°F on an averaging sensor across the mixing plenum. What outdoor airflow do these readings indicate?","options":["About 2,860 cfm, above the 2,400 cfm minimum","About 3,750 cfm, above the 2,400 cfm minimum","About 1,670 cfm, below the 2,400 cfm minimum","About 9,140 cfm, nearly four times the 2,400 cfm minimum"],"expl":"About 2,860 cfm. Outdoor-air fraction = (MAT - RAT) / (OAT - RAT) = (62 - 72) / (30 - 72) = 0.238, and 0.238 x 12,000 is about 2,860 cfm. The 3,750 trap divides by MAT - OAT, and 9,140 is the return-air share.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Mixed-air readings","tier":0,"ref":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","units":["hvac:s-oa-fraction","hvac:a-oa-fraction","hvac:a-oa-fraction-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0414","stem":"A VAV AHU's minimum outdoor-air damper is locked at the position that gave 2,000 cfm when the supply fan delivered its 10,000 cfm design flow. On a mild afternoon the supply fan reads 5,500 cfm and the damper has not moved. About how much outdoor air is the unit taking in?","options":["About 1,100 cfm, falling in step with the supply flow","About 600 cfm, falling with the square of the supply flow","About 2,000 cfm, holding steady at the fixed damper position","About 1,480 cfm, falling with the square root of the supply flow"],"expl":"About 1,100 cfm. With the damper fixed, outdoor air falls about in proportion to supply: 2,000 x (5,500 / 10,000) = 1,100 cfm, short of the 2,000 cfm minimum. The square law (605 cfm) is for pressure, not flow.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Minimum outdoor air","tier":0,"ref":"CEC Nonres. Compliance Manual 2016 §4.3","units":["hvac:s-min-oa-fixed","hvac:a-fixed-min-oa"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0415","stem":"During economizer cooling the BAS shows the outdoor-air damper commanded 100% open. At the unit the CVI reads outdoor air 55°F, return air 75°F, and mixed air 74°F. What do these readings show?","options":["About 5% outdoor air, far below what the damper command implies","About 95% outdoor air, very close to what the damper command implies","About 100% outdoor air, exactly what the damper command implies","About 1% outdoor air, far below what the damper command implies"],"expl":"About 5% outdoor air: (74 - 75) / (55 - 75) = 0.05, with the damper commanded wide open. PNNL says the calculated fraction should track the command within about 25%, so check blades, linkage and actuator. 95% is the return-air share.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Economizer readings","tier":0,"ref":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","units":["hvac:s-econ-readings","hvac:a-oa-fraction","hvac:a-oa-vs-damper"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0416","stem":"An air handler's filter is rated 0.15 in. w.c. and its wet coil 0.25 in. w.c. at the measured airflow. The CVI reads (all in. w.c.): return duct before the filter -0.08, between filter and blower -0.52, blower outlet before the coil +0.38, after the coil +0.16. Which component is restricting airflow?","options":["The filter, which drops 0.44 against its 0.15 rating","The coil, which drops 0.54 against its 0.25 rating","The coil, which sits at the highest reading of +0.38","The return duct, which sits at the deepest reading of -0.52"],"expl":"A component's drop is the difference between the readings on its two sides: the filter drops 0.52 - 0.08 = 0.44 in. w.c., about three times its 0.15 rating, while the coil drops 0.38 - 0.16 = 0.22, inside its 0.25 rating. The biggest single readings mark locations, not restrictions, and adding two readings of the same sign (0.38 + 0.16) invents a 0.54 coil drop.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Static pressure profile","tier":0,"ref":"NCI static pressure guide (ENERGY STAR)","units":["hvac:a-component-drop"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0417","stem":"In a 14 in. round supply duct, a single centerline pitot reading gives 1,200 fpm. A log-Tchebycheff traverse on two diameters averages 1,050 fpm. What airflow should the CVI record?","options":["About 1,120 cfm","About 1,280 cfm","About 1,430 cfm","About 4,490 cfm"],"expl":"Area = pi x (7/12 ft)^2 = 1.069 sq ft, and 1,050 fpm x 1.069 = about 1,120 cfm. The centerline reading (1,280 cfm) runs high because air moves fastest mid-duct; TSI calls a lone center reading unreliable. 1,430 cfm treats the duct as a 14 in. square, and 4,490 cfm uses the diameter as the radius.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct traverse","tier":0,"ref":"TSI AF-106","units":["hvac:a-traverse-why","hvac:a-round-area"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0418","stem":"An AHU schedule lists 12,000 cfm. With every VAV box held at a fixed position, a traverse at the supply fan reads 10,000 cfm, and capture-hood readings at all the diffusers add up to 8,200 cfm. What leakage figure should the CVI report?","options":["About 18%, measured against the 10,000 cfm at the supply fan","About 22%, measured against the 8,200 cfm leaving the diffusers","About 32%, measured against the 12,000 cfm on the schedule","About 15%, measured against the 12,000 cfm on the schedule"],"expl":"About 18%. Leakage is fan airflow minus the outlet total, 10,000 - 8,200 = 1,800 cfm, stated against the 10,000 cfm entering the ducts. Dividing by the diffuser total gives 22%, and the 12,000 cfm schedule is a design figure.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct leakage","tier":0,"ref":"LBNL, Wray, Diamond & Sherman, duct leakage measurement","units":["hvac:a-leakage-calc"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0419","stem":"An AHU trend shows duct static holding its 0.6 in. w.c. setpoint with the supply fan at 62% speed and supply air on its 55°F setpoint. Nine of its ten VAV boxes sit 90 to 100% open, their airflows are below their cooling maximums, and those zones run 2 to 3°F warm. What do these readings point to?","options":["A duct static setpoint set too low for the boxes it feeds","A duct static setpoint set too high for the boxes it feeds","Box dampers failed shut while their commands read wide open","Reheat valves leaking hot water into the nine warm zones"],"expl":"A duct static setpoint set too low. PNNL reads most boxes over about 75 to 80% open in cooling as starved boxes; the fan has speed to spare, so raising the setpoint delivers the air. A setpoint too high shows boxes mostly throttled.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"VAV static pressure","tier":0,"ref":"PNNL-29078 §4.3.8","units":["hvac:s-static-too-low"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0420","stem":"On a summer afternoon the CVI reads the office at 72°F, 55% RH, dew point 55°F, and the vented crawlspace at 80°F, 50% RH, dew point 60°F. A supply duct jacket in the crawlspace reads 58°F, and a supply diffuser face in the office also reads 58°F. Where should the CVI expect condensation?","options":["In the crawlspace, where the jacket runs below the dew point","In the office, where the diffuser face has the higher RH","In both spaces, since 58°F is colder than both air temperatures","In neither space, since both RH readings are under 60%"],"expl":"In the crawlspace. Water condenses on a surface colder than the air's dew point: the 60°F dew point is above the 58°F jacket, while the office's 55°F dew point sits below the diffuser. RH misleads, and EPA's under-60% advice is a room target.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Condensation risk","tier":0,"ref":"NWS dew point guidance; EPA mold guide","units":["hvac:a-condensation","hvac:a-rh-misleads"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0421","stem":"An AHU trend on a 48°F morning shows the economizer 100% open and mixed air at 49°F. The heating valve is commanded closed, yet air enters the heating coil at 49°F and leaves it at 58°F, while the cooling valve sits 35% open to hold a 55°F discharge setpoint. What do these readings show?","options":["A leaking heating valve, with the cooling coil removing the heat","Stuck economizer dampers, with the cooling coil making up the gap","Normal fan heat, with the cooling coil trimming it back to 55°F","Normal control, with the cooling coil holding its 55°F setpoint"],"expl":"A leaking heating valve. A 9°F rise across a heating coil whose valve is commanded shut means hot water is leaking through, and the cooling coil removes that heat. Mixed air at 49°F with 48°F outdoors rules out a stuck economizer.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Leaking heating valve","tier":0,"ref":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","units":["hvac:s-leaking-heat-valve","hvac:a-leaking-heat-valve"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0422","stem":"A two-deck multizone unit runs its cold deck at 55°F and its hot deck at 90°F. Zone 3's damper is commanded to full cooling with the hot-deck side shut, yet Zone 3's duct leaving the unit reads 62°F. What do the readings show?","options":["About 20% of Zone 3's air is hot-deck air leaking past the shut blade","About 25% of Zone 3's air is hot-deck air leaking past the damper","About 80% of Zone 3's air is leaking past the hot-deck blade","About 13% of Zone 3's air is leaking past the hot-deck blade"],"expl":"Mixing gives the hot share = (zone - cold) / (hot - cold) = (62 - 55) / (90 - 55) = 7/35 = 20%. The 25% trap divides by hot minus zone (a hot-to-cold ratio), 80% is the cold-deck share, and 13% divides the 7°F rise by the 55°F deck reading. Leakage through zone dampers is the classic multizone energy loss; record it for the controls or damper contractor.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Multizone readings","tier":1,"ref":"USACE ERDC-CERL Multizone Tech Note","units":["hvac:s-multizone-leak","hvac:a-mixing-leak"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0423","stem":"Supply air leaves an AHU at 55°F. The first diffuser, on a short run inside the conditioned space, reads 56°F. The last diffuser, at the end of a 60 ft trunk crossing a 125°F attic at +0.30 in. w.c., reads 63°F. What best explains the rise along the attic trunk?","options":["Attic heat conducted in through thin duct insulation","Hot attic air drawn into the trunk through its leaky seams","Fan motor heat added to the air along the length of the trunk","A discharge sensor at the unit reading 7°F below the air"],"expl":"The trunk runs at positive pressure, so its leaks push supply air out; pulling attic air in is how a return duct behaves. The 56°F first diffuser confirms the discharge sensor and shows fan heat was already added at the unit, which leaves heat conducted from the 125°F attic through weak insulation. Record the insulation, and any leakage, as separate findings.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct heat gain","tier":0,"ref":"DOE Energy Saver: duct losses","units":["hvac:a-supply-vs-return-leaks","hvac:a-duct-heat-gain"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0424","stem":"Before cleaning, the CVI recorded a 0.80 in. w.c. drop across a cooling coil at 8,000 cfm. After cleaning, the crew reports 0.50 in. w.c., but a new traverse shows the fan was slowed to 6,000 cfm. What do these readings show about the cleaning?","options":["No gain is shown until the coil is retested at 8,000 cfm","A 38% cut in coil resistance, from 0.80 down to 0.50 in. w.c.","A 60% cut in coil resistance, measured against the 0.50 reading","A 17% cut in coil resistance, net of the 25% airflow change"],"expl":"No gain is shown until the coil is retested at 8,000 cfm. Coil pressure drop scales with airflow squared, so the dirty coil would read about 0.45 in. w.c. at 6,000 cfm; 0.50 shows no improvement. Before-and-after readings only compare at the same airflow.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Coil pressure drop","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11.6; DOE Fan Sourcebook","units":["cvi:dp-square","hvac:a-dp-flow-squared"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0425","stem":"Before cleaning, a DX coil read return air 80°F dry bulb and 67°F wet bulb with 62°F supply air. After cleaning, on a cooler day, it read return air 74°F dry bulb and 66°F wet bulb with 59°F supply air. The unit's target-split chart gives 18°F and 16°F for those return conditions. A tech says the 3°F smaller split proves cleaning hurt capacity. What do the readings show?","options":["Each split sits within about 1°F of the target for its day","Capacity fell 3°F because the cleaning bent the coil fins","Capacity fell 3°F because refrigerant was lost during cleaning","The after split marks a fault by missing the 18 to 20°F band"],"expl":"Target temperature split depends on return dry bulb and wet bulb, so 18°F at 80/67 and 15°F at 74/66 both sit within about 1°F of their targets: the entering air changed, not the coil. Proctor Engineering calls a universal 18 to 20°F split 'common knowledge' that is wrong and treats splits within 3°F of target as normal. ACR 4.11.6 points to static pressure drop, not split, to show cleaning results.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Coil temperature split","tier":0,"ref":"Proctor/Carrier target split table","units":["cvi:target-split","hvac:a-temp-split"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0426","stem":"To check a minimum outdoor-air setting on a mild spring day, a CVI reads outdoor air 69°F, return air 72°F, and mixed air 71°F, and computes 33% outdoor air. His thermometer is accurate to plus or minus 1°F. How should he treat that 33% figure?","options":["As unreliable, since a 3°F spread from outdoor to return is too small","As valid, since three readings within 3°F of each other show good mixing","As valid, and above the typical 10 to 20% minimum damper position","As valid, since the formula works at any temperature spread"],"expl":"(71 - 72) / (69 - 72) = 33%, but a 1°F error in the mixed-air reading swings the answer from 0% (72°F) to 67% (70°F). PNNL limits the calculated outdoor-air fraction to times when outdoor and return air differ by more than about 5°F, since accuracy collapses as they converge. Retest on a day with a wide spread or measure the outdoor airflow directly.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Outdoor-air estimates","tier":1,"ref":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","units":["cvi:oaf-formula","hvac:a-oa-fraction","hvac:a-oa-fraction-spread"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0427","stem":"Twenty people meet from 1:00 to 2:00 p.m. Outdoor CO2 is 410 ppm. Room CO2 reads 430 ppm at 1:00, 620 at 1:20, 790 at 1:40, and 950 at 2:00. A colleague applies the steady-state rule that 15 cfm per person holds CO2 about 700 ppm above outdoors and reports 19 cfm per person. What should the CVI tell him?","options":["The CO2 had not leveled off, so the 19 cfm figure runs high","The room passes, since 19 cfm beats the 15 cfm in the rule","The room passes, since 950 ppm stays under the 1,000 ppm limit","The rising curve shows the outdoor-air damper shut in the meeting"],"expl":"The steady-state estimate works only after CO2 stops rising, and ASHRAE's CO2 position document warns that a reading taken before steady state overestimates the ventilation rate. Here CO2 was still climbing about 160 ppm every 20 minutes (15 x 700 / 540 = 19 cfm is therefore too generous). A rising curve is normal while a room fills, and Standard 62.1 sets no 1,000 ppm limit.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"CO2 interpretation","tier":0,"ref":"ASHRAE PD Indoor CO2 (2025)","units":["cvi:co2-no-limit","hvac:a-co2-700","hvac:a-co2-steady-state","hvac:a-co2-no-limit"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0428","stem":"Twelve adults have worked in an office for three hours, and CO2 has held flat at 1,470 ppm for the last hour while outdoor air reads 420 ppm. Using the reference that 15 cfm of outdoor air per sedentary adult holds CO2 about 700 ppm above outdoors at steady state, what outdoor airflow per person do the readings suggest?","options":["About 10 cfm per person","About 7 cfm per person","About 22 cfm per person","About 120 cfm per person"],"expl":"At steady state, outdoor air per person varies inversely with the indoor-minus-outdoor difference: 15 x 700 / (1,470 - 420) = 15 x 700 / 1,050 = 10 cfm. Using the raw 1,470 reading gives 7, inverting the ratio gives 22.5, and 120 cfm is the room total (10 x 12), not a per-person figure. CO2 output varies with age, size, and activity, so report the result as an estimate.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"CO2 ventilation estimate","tier":1,"ref":"ASHRAE PD Indoor CO2 (2025); ASTM D6245","units":["hvac:a-co2-700","hvac:a-co2-steady-state","hvac:a-co2-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0429","stem":"An owner's damper log lists FD-14 at the corridor wall on grid C-4 of sheet M-201, tested and passed in 2024. At C-4 the CVI finds a damper tagged FD-41 with no access door, and the drawing puts FD-41 at grid F-7, where he finds no damper. What should he record?","options":["A mismatch leaving FD-14 and FD-41 test status unverified","FD-14 as tested and passed, since the log places it at grid C-4","A tag typo, with FD-14's 2024 result carried over to the C-4 damper","FD-41 as missing, with FD-14's 2024 test result applied at C-4"],"expl":"Fire damper test records identify each damper by location, and ACR 1.3 has the inspector work from the plans; when the tag, the log, and the drawing disagree, no test result can be tied to the damper in the wall. The CVI records the mismatch, the missing access door, and the empty location at F-7, and refers testing to the owner's fire damper contractor instead of carrying a result over.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Fire damper records","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA 80 (2010) 19.4; ACR 2025 §1.3","units":["cvi:damper-records"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0430","stem":"The manometer log for a Level 2 containment, read against the adjacent corridor, shows -0.012 in. w.c. at 8:00, -0.010 at 9:00, +0.003 at 10:00, and -0.011 at 11:00. Cleaning ran all morning. Under ACR 2025, what should the CVI record?","options":["A lapse at 10:00, when the containment turned positive during work","A passing log, since the average held at about -0.008 in. w.c.","A passing log, since the Standard sets no numeric pressure value","A failing log, since no reading got down to the -0.02 in. w.c. minimum"],"expl":"ACR 3.10.5 requires a Level 2 containment to stay negative at all times, and 3.10.6 has a manometer validate it, so the +0.003 reading during cleaning is a lapse whatever the average (-0.0075) shows. ACR sets no numeric value; the -0.02 in. w.c. figure comes from OSHA's asbestos negative pressure enclosure rule, not from ACR.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Containment monitoring","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.10.5-3.10.6","units":["cvi:manometer-lapse","cvi:npe-002"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0431","stem":"A MERV 13 bank rated 0.35 in. w.c. clean and 1.0 in. w.c. final logs 0.38 in January, 0.60 in March, 0.97 on June 1, and 0.22 on June 8. The airflow station reads 12,000 cfm throughout, and no change-out is logged. What does the June 8 reading most likely mean?","options":["Air is slipping past torn media or gaps in the bank","The media passed its final rating and shed its dust cake","The bank is loading normally and has life left before 1.0","The fan slowed and cut the airflow through the loaded bank"],"expl":"Air is slipping past torn media or gaps in the bank. At steady airflow a filter's resistance only climbs as it loads, so a reading below its clean rating means a leak or rupture. Also check for an unlogged filter swap or a loose gauge tube.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Filter pressure log","tier":0,"ref":"ACHR News filter gages; Donaldson","units":["hvac:a-filter-dp-falls"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0432","stem":"An AHU serves twelve VAV boxes. Eleven modulate 45 to 70% open and hold their zones. Box 7's damper command has read 100% open for five days, its airflow reads 0 to 10 cfm, and its zone sits 5°F above setpoint while duct static holds setpoint. What should the CVI check first?","options":["Box 7's damper actuator and flow sensor, for a failed component","The unit's duct static setpoint, for a setting that is too low","Box 7's reheat valve, for hot water leaking into the zone","The unit's supply air setpoint, for a setting too warm"],"expl":"PNNL reads a box commanded 100% open with near-zero airflow as a failed component: a damper failed shut from actuator or linkage failure, a plugged or loose sensing tube, or a failed controller. Eleven healthy boxes and steady duct static rule out AHU-level settings; a low static setpoint starves most boxes at once. A leaking reheat valve warms a zone but does not stop its airflow.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"VAV box trends","tier":0,"ref":"PNNL-29078 §3.3.4","units":["cvi:vav-failed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0433","stem":"In July the CVI reads 75°F at a house's return grille, 90°F in the return plenum at the air handler, and 135°F in the attic that the return duct crosses. If the whole rise comes from attic air leaking in, about what share of the return air is attic air?","options":["About 25%, a quarter of the flow","About 33%, a third of the flow","About 75%, most of the flow","About 17%, a sixth of the flow"],"expl":"About 25%. Attic share = (plenum - grille) / (attic - grille) = (90 - 75) / (135 - 75) = 25%. The 33% trap divides by attic minus plenum, and 75% is the room-air share. Some rise is conduction, so 25% is an upper bound.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Return duct leakage","tier":0,"ref":"DOE Energy Saver: duct losses","units":["cvi:return-leak-mix"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0434","stem":"Duct liner just past an AHU cooling coil is soaked, yet the drain pan drains freely. The cabinet interior measures 4 ft by 6 ft, the finned coil face 3 ft by 5 ft, and the supply duct leaving the unit 2 ft by 3 ft. A traverse in that duct reads 9,000 cfm. What should the CVI record?","options":["600 fpm at the coil face, high enough to risk carryover","1,500 fpm at the coil face, triple the carryover guideline","375 fpm at the coil face, low enough to rule out carryover","375 fpm at the coil face, too low to spread the air evenly"],"expl":"600 fpm. Face velocity = airflow / finned face area = 9,000 / (3 x 5) = 600 fpm, above the 500 fpm rule of thumb for carryover. 375 fpm divides by the cabinet, and 1,500 fpm divides by the duct.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Coil carryover","tier":0,"ref":"Trane ENL Coil Selection (APP-CMC054)","units":["cvi:face-velocity","hvac:a-face-velocity"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0435","stem":"Pre-cleaning pressure drops (in. w.c.) were filter 0.42, coil 0.30, supply ducts 0.18, and return ducts 0.10. Post-cleaning, with a new filter bank and the same fan speed, they are filter 0.12, coil 0.27, supply ducts 0.17, and return ducts 0.09. The contractor credits duct cleaning for the 0.35 in. w.c. total drop. What do the readings show?","options":["The new filters, not the duct cleaning, gave most of the drop","The duct cleaning cut the system's resistance by about a third","The coil cleaning, not the duct cleaning, gave most of the drop","The duct cleaning gave most of the drop, with the coil next"],"expl":"Subtract component by component: the filter fell 0.30 in. w.c., the coil 0.03, and each duct run 0.01, so the fresh filter bank made about 86% of the 0.35 in. w.c. change. A before-and-after comparison credits cleaning only for the parts that were cleaned, and only when everything else, filters and fan speed included, stays the same.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Before-after comparison","tier":0,"ref":"NCI static pressure guide; ACR 2025 §4.11.6","units":["cvi:component-compare","hvac:a-component-drop"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0500","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, where does the visual inspection fall in NADCA's recommended procedure for assessing, cleaning, and restoring HVAC systems?","options":["It is the first step in the procedure","It is the last step, done after cleaning","It comes right after air sampling","It follows the contractor's cost bid"],"expl":"The manual calls the visual inspection of HVAC components the first step in the NADCA procedure outlined in ACR. Post-cleaning inspection exists too, but it is not where the procedure starts.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection purpose","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","units":["nim:a-first-step"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0501","stem":"A client asks how the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual defines the cleanliness an inspector assesses. Which set of conditions makes up that definition?","options":["Dirt, obstructions, excess moisture, and microbial contamination","Dust, odors, high relative humidity, and elevated CO2 readings","Debris, rust, fan noise, vibration, and poor air balance","Particulate, VOC levels, ozone, and radon in the airstream"],"expl":"The manual defines system cleanliness by the presence of dirt, obstructions, excess moisture, and microbial contamination that could affect performance or occupant health and comfort. Odors, CO2, VOCs and radon are IAQ measures, not the cleanliness definition.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection purpose","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","units":["nim:a-cleanliness-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0502","stem":"After the walkthrough, an inspector and the building owner agree on the purpose and scope of an inspection. According to the manual, what happens next, before the detailed inspection begins?","options":["A written plan is drafted and then reviewed and approved","Samples are collected to set a contamination baseline","A baseline NADCA Vacuum Test is run on each duct","The formal report outline is sent to the owner for a signature"],"expl":"The manual has the inspector draft a written plan that is reviewed and approved before any detailed inspection. Sampling and vacuum testing, if done at all, happen during or after the inspection, not before the plan.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection process","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","units":["nim:a-written-plan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0503","stem":"An inspection covers one AHU and its duct systems. Besides supply and return ducts, which ducts does the manual include in the actual inspection when they fall within the agreed scope?","options":["Outdoor intake make-up ducts and air exhaust systems","Fire sprinkler branch lines and standpipe riser pipes","Plumbing vent stacks and boiler flue connectors","Refrigerant line sets and condensate drain piping"],"expl":"The manual lists AHU equipment plus supply, return, outdoor intake make-up ducts, and air exhaust systems within the scope set by inspector and client. Sprinkler lines, flues, vents, and piping are not air distribution ducts.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection scope","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","units":["nim:a-scope-components"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0504","stem":"An inspector finds a small office's AHU and ducts visibly clean with no suspect growth. The client asks whether surface samples are required. What does the manual say?","options":["The inspector decides based on whether contamination is found","Samples must be taken at every inspection to document it","Samples must be taken whenever the ducts are internally insulated","Samples must be taken whenever occupants report complaints"],"expl":"The manual says that depending on whether contamination is found, the inspector may or may not take measurements and samples. No rule requires sampling at every inspection or for lined duct.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Measurements and samples","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","units":["nim:a-samples-optional"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0505","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, which part of the inspection report matters most?","options":["Whether to clean, and which issues need a specialist","Photographs of every component examined in the system","Lab sample results with the chain of custody records","A summary of the building history from the interviews"],"expl":"The manual says the report most importantly recommends whether the system should be cleaned and whether any condition needs a specialist's attention. Photos and lab results support those recommendations but are not the core.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection report","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.1","units":["nim:a-report-recs"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0506","stem":"Under ACR, the NADCA Standard, as cited in the manual, what should trigger cleaning of an HVAC system?","options":["An inspection showing accumulated particulate or microbial growth","A fixed interval since the last cleaning, set by the building type","Occupant complaints of odors or dust near supply diffusers","A change in the building's ownership or its main tenants"],"expl":"ACR recommends cleaning when a cleanliness inspection shows the system is contaminated with accumulated particulate or microbial growth. Fixed intervals apply to inspections, not to cleaning.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Why inspect","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.2","units":["nim:a-acr-routine"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0507","stem":"A tenant sues a property manager, claiming the building's HVAC system was neglected. According to the manual, how can past inspection reports help the manager?","options":["They help show due diligence in how the facility was maintained","They shift liability for the HVAC system to the inspector's insurer","They prove the building met ASHRAE 62.1 ventilation rates","They certify the building free of mold under an EPA program"],"expl":"The manual notes that inspection information lets a client establish due diligence if questioned about facility maintenance. A visual inspection does not certify ventilation rates or mold status, and it does not transfer liability.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Why inspect","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.2","units":["nim:a-due-diligence"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0508","stem":"Which reason for an HVAC inspection does the manual connect with the sale of a building?","options":["Compiling a complete documentation package on the building","Verifying that a recent duct cleaning was successful","Setting the new owner's annual energy use baseline","Establishing the insurance replacement value of the HVAC equipment"],"expl":"The manual says clients may want a complete documentation package on the building, which is sometimes required when a building is sold. Verifying a cleaning is a separate, real reason for inspection but is not the one tied to a sale.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Why inspect","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.2","units":["nim:a-documentation-sale"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0509","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, how often does NADCA formally recommend inspecting a commercial building's HVAC system?","options":["Once a year","Four times a year","Twice a year","Once in two years"],"expl":"NADCA formally recommends an annual inspection of commercial HVAC systems, matching the ACR guidance for AHUs and supply and return ducts. Quarterly is the NFPA 90A Annex B suggestion, not NADCA's.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection frequency","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.3","units":["nim:a-annual"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0510","stem":"The manual quotes NFPA 90A Section B-4 on inspecting ducts for dust and waste. What interval does that section suggest?","options":["Quarterly, lengthening the interval if found unnecessary","Annually, shortening the interval whenever heavy dust is found","Monthly, until two clean results in a row are recorded","Every two years, as long as filters are changed on time"],"expl":"NFPA 90A B-4 suggests quarterly inspections of discharge and return ducts, and permits a longer interval if several inspections show that frequency is unnecessary. Annual is NADCA's own recommendation.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection frequency","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.3","units":["nim:a-nfpa90a-quarterly"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0511","stem":"Table 1-2 of the manual lists inspection guidance from several organizations. Which pairing of organization and publication is correct?","options":["ACGIH: Bioaerosols: Assessment and Control","NAFA: A Guide to Insulated Air Duct Systems","NAIMA: HVAC Duct Construction Standards","SMACNA: Bioaerosols: Assessment and Control"],"expl":"Table 1-2 pairs ACGIH with Bioaerosols: Assessment and Control. The insulated duct guide belongs to NAIMA, and the duct construction standards belong to SMACNA.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Inspection references","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 1-2","units":["nim:a-table-1-2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0512","stem":"Besides a working knowledge of HVAC design, engineering practice, cleaning methods, and standards, what experience does the manual say inspection personnel should also have?","options":["Verifying cleanliness and making and sealing access openings","Balancing airflow and writing certified test and balance reports","Collecting and analyzing microbial samples in a laboratory","Designing duct layouts and stamping mechanical drawings"],"expl":"The manual adds experience performing cleanliness verification and the experience and tools to create and seal openings. Balancing, lab analysis, and design are specialties the inspector refers out.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Inspector qualifications","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.4","units":["nim:a-qual-knowledge"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0513","stem":"A CVI whose company also cleans duct systems is hired to inspect an office building. According to the manual, what must he do?","options":["Tell the client his firm also cleans, before the inspection starts","Tell the client his firm also cleans, when the report is delivered","Decline the job, since cleaning firms may not inspect the same system","Have a second CVI from his company sign off on the final report"],"expl":"The manual requires an inspector who also does cleaning or restoration to disclose it before beginning the inspection. Disclosing at report time is too late, and the manual does not bar cleaners from inspecting.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Disclosure","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.4","units":["nim:a-disclose-cleaning"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0514","stem":"An inspector owns shares in the coil cleaning firm a building usually hires. What does the manual require of the inspector regarding this?","options":["Disclosing any such outside interest to the client up front","Disclosing it only if the report recommends that firm","Reporting it to NADCA rather than to the client","Declining the job, as any such interest bars the inspector"],"expl":"The manual says the inspector must disclose to the client any outside interests related to the outcome of the inspection. It does not make disclosure conditional on the recommendation, route it to NADCA, or bar the inspector outright.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Disclosure","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.4","units":["nim:a-disclose-interest"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0515","stem":"During the inspection itself, what does the manual say the inspector should focus on recording and reporting?","options":["Whether each component is clean or has an identified issue","The estimated cost to clean each dirty component found","Which cleaning contractor is best suited to each needed repair","How the system compares with others the inspector has seen"],"expl":"To stay objective, the manual has the inspector record and report whether a component is observed clean or an issue has been identified, staying direct and factual. Pricing and contractor picks are outside that role.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Objectivity","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.4","units":["nim:a-objective"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0516","stem":"An inspector sees suspect bioaerosol sources and airflow imbalance problems beyond his training. According to the manual, what is his responsibility?","options":["Contacting experts such as industrial hygienists and balancers","Reporting only what his CVI training has specifically covered","Treating his CVI certification as proof of expertise in both fields","Collecting bioaerosol samples himself to confirm his findings"],"expl":"The manual says the training does not make an inspector an expert in any one IAQ field, so he must contact experts such as industrial hygiene, air balancing, filtration, microbiology, and bioaerosol specialists when needed.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Using specialists","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §1.4","units":["nim:a-refer-experts"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0517","stem":"Why does the manual caution inspectors that a standard liability insurance policy may not be enough?","options":["Report errors arise from a professional service it may not cover","Standard policies exclude all work performed inside air handling units","Standard policies may lapse when the work crosses state lines","Standard policies cover only damage to the owner's building"],"expl":"The inspection is a professional service and the report is its product, so standard liability insurance may not protect against errors in the report. The other statements are not in the manual.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Insurance","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.1","units":["nim:a-report-product"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0518","stem":"What is professional liability insurance commonly called, as the manual describes it for inspectors?","options":["Errors and omissions insurance","Commercial general liability","Fidelity and surety bonding","Contractor's pollution liability"],"expl":"The manual calls professional liability insurance Errors and Omissions (E&O) coverage. General liability and fidelity are lines that E&O specifically does not replace.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Insurance","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.1","units":["nim:a-eo-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0519","stem":"A CVI's employee is hurt falling from a ladder while the firm holds only an E&O policy. Based on the manual, why would that policy likely not respond?","options":["E&O covers professional service claims but not workers' comp","E&O covers injuries only after the final report is issued","E&O excludes injuries that happen on the client's roof","E&O pays once general liability limits are used up"],"expl":"The manual says E&O covers claims tied directly to professional services, not automobile, general liability, fidelity, property, or workers compensation losses. An employee injury is a workers compensation matter.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Insurance","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.1","units":["nim:a-eo-exclusions"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0520","stem":"According to the manual, which inspection task may require a license in some states or provinces?","options":["Cutting an access opening in the ductwork","Photographing duct interiors with a camera","Interviewing the client about the building","Reviewing the mechanical drawings on site"],"expl":"The manual lists cutting an access opening, removing unit access panels, pulling and cleaning a fan assembly, and cleaning the evaporator coil as tasks some jurisdictions license. Photography, interviews, and plan review are not listed.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Licensing","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.2","units":["nim:a-licensing-tasks"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0521","stem":"An inspector takes a job in another province with its own licensing rules. According to the manual, who is responsible for meeting those requirements?","options":["The inspector, as set by the authority having jurisdiction","The building owner, as set by the authority having jurisdiction","The facility manager who signed the inspection contract","NADCA, as the body that grants the CVI certification"],"expl":"The manual places it on the inspector to meet all local, state, country, or province licensing and certification requirements set by the authority having jurisdiction. The owner and NADCA do not carry that duty.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Licensing","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.2","units":["nim:a-licensing-responsibility"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0522","stem":"When an inspection report recommends that a system be cleaned, what does the manual say makes that recommendation credible?","options":["Citing peer-reviewed industry standards that support it","Citing the inspector's years of field experience with it","Attaching a cost comparison from three cleaning firms","Getting the building owner's signature on the finding"],"expl":"The manual tells inspectors to keep a library of standards and cite the industry recognized, peer-reviewed documents behind each recommendation. Experience alone is not what it names as the basis for credibility.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Standards library","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.3","units":["nim:a-cite-standards"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0523","stem":"An inspector brings a test and balance firm onto a follow-up project. Which credential does the manual recommend that firm hold?","options":["NEBB or AABC certification","AIHA or ACGIH certification","NAFA or NADCA certification","UL or ETL listing marks"],"expl":"The manual recommends Test and Balance companies certified by NEBB or AABC. AIHA certification is what it recommends for industrial hygienists, not balancing firms.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Other specialties","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.4","units":["nim:a-specialist-creds"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0524","stem":"An inspection team needs an industrial hygienist. Per the manual, certification through which organization, or an equivalent, is recommended?","options":["AIHA","NEBB","AABC","NAFA"],"expl":"The manual recommends industrial hygienists certified through the American Industrial Hygiene Association or equivalent. NEBB and AABC are the credentials it names for test and balance firms.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Other specialties","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §2.4","units":["nim:a-specialist-creds"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0525","stem":"An inspection requires temporarily deactivating a duct smoke detector, and code requires notice to the fire department. According to the manual, whose responsibility are the permits and notifications?","options":["The building's facility manager","The CVI performing the inspection","The fire alarm service company","The local fire marshal's office"],"expl":"The manual assigns special permits, code-required notification, and related communications to the facility manager. The inspector's duty is not to impair, alter, or damage the detection equipment.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Detectors and alarms","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 A","units":["nim:a-detector-permits"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0526","stem":"The manual summarizes the Occupational Safety and Health Act of 1970. What does it say about who must comply?","options":["It is mandatory for every employer and employee, with few exceptions","It is mandatory for employers, and voluntary for their employees","It is voluntary unless the state has adopted its own OSHA plan","It is mandatory only for firms that handle hazardous chemicals"],"expl":"The manual states compliance with the Act is mandatory for every employer and employee, with few exceptions. It does not exempt employees or limit the law to chemical handlers.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"OSHA basics","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 B","units":["nim:a-osha-act"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0527","stem":"An inspector will spend the day on a sun-baked roof checking rooftop units. Which work boot feature does the manual tie to that hazard?","options":["Heat-resistant outsoles","Steel toe protection","Firm ankle support","Tightly tucked laces"],"expl":"The manual says heat-resistant soles protect against hot surfaces such as rooftops. Steel toes address dropped objects, not heat.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Foot protection","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","units":["nim:a-foot"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0528","stem":"The manual lists minimum requirements that eye protectors must meet. Which of these is NOT one of them?","options":["Be tinted to cut glare on bright rooftops","Be capable of being disinfected and cleaned","Fit snugly without limiting movement or vision","Be reasonably comfortable under stated conditions"],"expl":"The manual's list includes adequate protection, reasonable comfort, a snug fit that does not interfere with movement or vision, durability, being disinfectable and easily cleanable, and being kept clean and in good repair. Tinting is not on it.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Eye protection","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","units":["nim:a-eye-reqs"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0529","stem":"Under OSHA's respirator standard as described in the manual, what must an employer develop and implement where respirators are needed?","options":["A written respiratory protection program with site procedures","A written hazard communication program with chemical labels","A written confined space entry permit for every work area entered","A written lockout program listing each isolating device"],"expl":"The manual says the OSHA standard requires a written respiratory protection program with workplace-specific procedures. A written hazard communication program is a HazCom element, not the respirator rule.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Respirators","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","units":["nim:a-resp-program"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0530","stem":"A respirator catalog shows several approval marks. According to the manual, what makes a respirator an approved one?","options":["Joint certification by NIOSH and MSHA","Joint certification by OSHA and EPA","Joint certification by UL and ANSI","Joint certification by OSHA and MSHA"],"expl":"The manual defines an approved respirator as one certified jointly by NIOSH and MSHA. OSHA sets the respirator program rule but does not certify respirators. Note that under current federal rules (42 CFR Part 84) NIOSH alone certifies respirators; the joint MSHA role reflects the older 30 CFR 11 approvals.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Respirators","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","units":["nim:a-resp-approval"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0531","stem":"During a fit test, an inspector wears his respirator while the tester releases an irritant around the face seal. He notices no irritation. What does this describe?","options":["A qualitative fit test showing a proper fit","A quantitative fit test showing a proper fit","A qualitative fit test showing a poor fit","A quantitative fit test showing a poor fit"],"expl":"A qualitative fit test introduces a harmless odorous or irritating substance, and detecting nothing indicates a proper fit. Quantitative tests measure aerosol, particulates, or pressure instead of relying on the wearer's senses.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Fit testing","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","units":["nim:a-fit-test-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0532","stem":"Before an employee first wears a respirator, who does the manual say must medically evaluate him, and with what tool?","options":["A licensed health care professional, using OSHA's questionnaire","The employer's safety officer, using OSHA's medical questionnaire","The respirator maker's rep, using an on-site fit test","A certified industrial hygienist, using a lung function test"],"expl":"The manual requires medical clearance by a physician or other licensed health care professional, using the OSHA Respirator Medical Evaluation Questionnaire. A safety officer is not qualified to give medical clearance.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Respirators","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 C","units":["nim:a-resp-medical"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0533","stem":"Which set matches the four major elements of HazCom compliance given in the manual?","options":["SDS, labels, training, and a written program","SDS, labels, fit tests, and air monitoring","SDS, placards, medical exams, and training","Labels, PPE, lockout, and a written program"],"expl":"The manual lists Safety Data Sheets, labels, training, and a written hazard communication program. Fit testing and medical exams belong to the respirator rule.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"HazCom","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 D","units":["nim:a-hazcom-elements"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0534","stem":"An SDS shows that a duct sealant is flammable and reactive. How does the manual classify that kind of hazard?","options":["A physical hazard","A health hazard","A chronic health hazard","An acute health hazard"],"expl":"The manual says a substance that is flammable, explosive, or reactive is a physical hazard. Health hazards are those causing acute or chronic health problems from exposure.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"HazCom","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 D","units":["nim:a-physical-vs-health"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0535","stem":"The disconnect feeding an AHU fan has no way to accept a lock. According to the manual, what must the inspector do?","options":["Use tagout, attaching a tag that stays on during the inspection","Skip isolation, since lockout does not apply to that device","Ask the client's staff to stand guard at the switch while he works","Turn the unit off at the thermostat and tag the thermostat"],"expl":"When an energy-isolating device cannot be locked out, the manual says the inspector must use tagout, securing the tag so it will not come off during the inspection. A thermostat is not an energy-isolating device.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Lockout/tagout","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 E","units":["nim:a-tagout-when"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0536","stem":"A lock was left on an AHU disconnect by an inspector who has since left the site. According to the manual, what is the first step before anyone else removes it?","options":["Call the employee who installed the lock","Notify the facility's safety coordinator of the lock","Verify the equipment is clear to energize","Notify that employee's direct supervisor"],"expl":"The manual's documented sequence starts with calling the employee, then outside plant contact, the supervisor, the safety coordinator, verifying the equipment is clear, and finally removing the lock.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Lockout/tagout","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 E","units":["nim:a-lock-removal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0537","stem":"A large plenum is big enough to enter, has one small access door, and is not designed for continuous occupancy. Which added feature would make it a permit-required confined space?","options":["A floor that slopes down and tapers to a smaller section","A door that must be propped open during the entry","Lighting too dim to photograph without a flashlight","A supply branch that serves more than one building zone"],"expl":"A configuration that could trap or asphyxiate an entrant, such as a floor sloping down to a smaller cross section, is one of the manual's permit-required characteristics. The other features do not create a recognized serious hazard.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Confined space","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.5 F","units":["nim:a-prcs"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0550","stem":"How does a constant air volume (CAV) system change the temperature of a space?","options":["By heating or cooling the air or by cycling the AHU on and off","By throttling a damper in the terminal box that serves each zone","By slowing the supply fan as the load in the zone drops off","By resetting the duct static pressure as the zone load drops"],"expl":"A CAV system holds airflow constant and changes the air temperature or cycles the unit. Throttling a terminal damper, slowing the fan, and resetting duct static pressure are VAV responses; CAV has no terminal dampering or fan modulation.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Constant volume","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-cav-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0551","stem":"A designer wants one constant volume system serving every zone of a building at a single supply temperature, with no reheat. Which space suits that design best?","options":["An exhibit hall with few windows and an even load throughout","A perimeter office floor with large windows on every exposure","A hotel wing where each guest room sets its own thermostat","A multi-tenant floor where each suite has different occupancy"],"expl":"Without reheat, CAV supplies one uniform temperature to all zones, so it is typically limited to large open areas with few windows and uniform loads such as exhibit halls, factories, and auditoriums. Perimeter offices have varied solar loads that one supply temperature cannot match.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Constant volume","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-cav-uniform"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0552","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, how does a CAV system compare with a VAV system?","options":["CAV uses more energy, but its outdoor air control is simpler","CAV uses less energy, and its outdoor air control is simpler","CAV uses more energy, and its outdoor air control is more complex","CAV uses less energy, but its outdoor air control is harder"],"expl":"The manual says CAV systems are less energy-efficient than VAV, but their outdoor air delivery controls are simpler to manage. VAV saves fan energy but makes holding a minimum outdoor air flow harder as supply volume drops.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Constant volume","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-cav-vs-vav"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0553","stem":"What is an air economizer?","options":["Dampers and controls adding outdoor air to return air for mild-weather cooling","A heat wheel that moves energy between the exhaust air and incoming outdoor air","A fan speed control that cuts the supply airflow as the building load drops off","A bypass damper that routes return air around the cooling coil in mild weather"],"expl":"An economizer is a damper and control system that brings outdoor air directly into the recirculated air for cooling or dehumidification when the weather is mild. A heat wheel recovers energy but does not use outdoor air as free cooling.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Outdoor air control","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-economizer"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0554","stem":"In a small office served by a packaged unit, the inspector finds the thermostat fan switch set to AUTO. Why does the manual want it set to ON?","options":["So the fan runs nonstop and outdoor air always reaches occupants","So the fan cycles with heating and cooling calls and saves fan energy","So the economizer can open fully whenever the coil is not running","So the filter loads more slowly because air moves only on demand"],"expl":"The fan that brings in outdoor air runs only on a call for heating or cooling when the switch is on AUTO, so ventilation stops between calls. ON keeps outdoor air flowing to occupants continuously; cycling with calls is what AUTO does.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Outdoor air control","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-fan-on"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0555","stem":"How does a variable air volume (VAV) system respond as the load in a space changes?","options":["It varies how much heated or cooled air is delivered to the zone","It varies the supply air temperature while holding the volume fixed","It blends warm and cool air streams in a box that serves the zone","It cycles the air handling unit on and off as the zone calls"],"expl":"VAV changes the quantity of conditioned air sent to each zone through a thermostat-controlled damper in the VAV box. Changing temperature at a fixed volume, or cycling the unit, describes a constant volume system.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Variable air volume","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-vav-def"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0556","stem":"Why do VAV zones typically cover a large area made up of several spaces with similar loads?","options":["Each zone needs its own terminal box, and those boxes are costly","Each zone needs its own supply fan, and those fans are noisy","Each zone needs a dedicated duct run back to the central AHU","Each zone needs its own outdoor air intake and control damper"],"expl":"Because every VAV zone requires a relatively costly VAV box, designers group spaces with similar solar exposure, occupancy, and loads into one zone. A dedicated duct to the central AHU for each zone describes a multizone system.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Variable air volume","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-vav-zone-size"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0557","stem":"To avoid under-ventilation as a VAV system throttles back from full airflow, what should the system be arranged to introduce?","options":["At least a minimum quantity of outdoor air, not a set percentage","At least a minimum percentage of outdoor air, not a set quantity","A colder supply air temperature so the zone boxes will open up wider","A constant fan speed, with every zone box set to a zero minimum"],"expl":"The manual stresses a minimum quantity (cfm) of outdoor air, because a fixed percentage of a shrinking supply volume delivers less and less outdoor air. Setting supply air too cold is itself listed as a cause of under-ventilation.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"VAV ventilation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-vav-oa-quantity"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0558","stem":"On a mild afternoon, a VAV system with a fixed outdoor air percentage throttles its supply airflow down to 40% of design. What happens to the outdoor air it delivers?","options":["The cfm of outdoor air falls in step with the total flow","The cfm of outdoor air holds steady at its full design value","The cfm of outdoor air rises to offset the lower total supply flow","The outdoor air percentage drops, but its cfm stays the same"],"expl":"With a constant outdoor air percentage, the total volume of outdoor air shrinks as supply volume shrinks, which can under-ventilate at part load. A fixed percentage cannot hold outdoor cfm steady while total flow falls.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"VAV ventilation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-vav-oa-partload"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0559","stem":"Occupants in one VAV zone complain the air feels stale. The inspector finds the supply air temperature set well below what the zone's load needs. Why does that setting hurt ventilation?","options":["The box throttles to low airflow, so less outdoor air reaches the zone","The box opens to full airflow, so outdoor air overwhelms the zone","The colder air holds more moisture, so humidity climbs in the zone","The colder air closes the outdoor damper, so the economizer locks out"],"expl":"Air that is colder than the load needs satisfies the thermostat with less volume, so the box throttles down and delivers less outdoor air. The manual names a supply temperature set too low as a cause of VAV under-ventilation.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"VAV ventilation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-vav-sat-low"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0560","stem":"How is a single zone system controlled?","options":["One thermostat cycles the AHU or adjusts coil flow for the zone","Zone thermostats blend hot and cold deck air back at the central AHU","Zone thermostats modulate a damper in a VAV box for each space","Zone thermostats mix hot and cold duct air in a terminal box"],"expl":"A single zone system answers to one thermostat that turns the AHU on and off or adjusts flow through the coils, so the whole zone gets one supply temperature. Blending decks at the AHU is multizone; mixing at a terminal box is dual duct.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Single zone","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-single-zone"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0561","stem":"What defines a multizone system?","options":["Each zone has its own duct from the AHU carrying air blended for that zone","Each zone has hot and cold ducts that mix in a terminal box in the zone","Each zone has a VAV box that varies the volume of air sent to that zone","Each zone has its own heat pump connected to a shared closed water loop"],"expl":"In a multizone system each zone is fed by a dedicated duct from the central AHU, where dampers blend warm and cool air to the zone thermostat's call. Hot and cold ducts mixing at a terminal box describes dual duct.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Multizone","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-mz-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0562","stem":"In the most common multizone design, at roughly what temperatures does the AHU produce its warm and cool air?","options":["About 100°F warm air and 55°F cool air","About 120°F warm air and 45°F cool air","About 90°F warm air and 65°F cool air","About 140°F warm air and 50°F cool air"],"expl":"The manual gives about 100°F for the warm air and about 55°F for the cool air, which dampers then blend for each zone. The other pairs are not the manual's figures.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Multizone","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-mz-temps"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0563","stem":"A building owner asks why the 1970s multizone unit on his roof could not simply be replaced in kind. What does the manual say drove multizone systems out of use?","options":["High energy use from heating and cooling air at the same time","No way to deliver different temperatures to the different zones","The cost of a separate VAV box for every zone in the building","No path to bring outdoor air in through the central AHU"],"expl":"Multizone systems heat and cool simultaneously, and that energy penalty led to their decline and to bans in local building codes. They do deliver different temperatures to different zones; that is their purpose.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Multizone","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-mz-energy"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0564","stem":"In a multizone AHU, the zone blending dampers sit in the high-velocity section near the fan. What does the manual say this arrangement causes?","options":["Significant pressure drop and added energy use","Damper leakage that occurs only with the fan off","Reduced outdoor air whenever the fan slows down","Warm air stratifying in the zone supply ducts"],"expl":"Multiple dampers placed in high-velocity areas near the fan create significant pressure drop and extra energy use. Leakage past closed blades is the manual's note on dual-duct mixing boxes, and it is not limited to fan-off periods.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Multizone","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-mz-dampers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0565","stem":"In a dual-duct system, where are the warm and cool airstreams mixed for each zone?","options":["In a terminal mixing box located at each zone it serves","In the fan room, before one duct leaves for the zone","At the cooling coil, before air enters the hot deck","At the supply diffuser, where both decks discharge"],"expl":"Dual-duct systems run separate hot deck and cold deck ducts in parallel through the building and mix them at a terminal mixing box in each zone. Blending in the fan room and sending one duct per zone is the multizone approach.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Dual duct","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-dd-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0566","stem":"Which statement about dual-duct systems is correct?","options":["They can be built as either CAV or VAV systems","They can be built as CAV systems, but not VAV","They can be built as VAV systems, but not CAV","They can be built with one supply duct per zone"],"expl":"The manual states that dual-duct systems may be either CAV or VAV. One supply duct per zone, blended at the AHU, describes a multizone system, not dual duct.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Dual duct","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-dd-cav-vav"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0567","stem":"An inspector is planning access openings in a dual-duct building. Where does the manual say high-velocity ductwork is likely?","options":["Upstream of the mixing boxes","Downstream of the mixing boxes","At the zone supply diffusers","In the ceiling return plenum"],"expl":"Dual-duct systems are likely to have high-velocity ductwork upstream of the mixing boxes, where the hot and cold decks run to the zones. Downstream of the box the air is at normal low-velocity distribution.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Dual duct","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-dd-highvel"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0568","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, what is a known weakness of the dampers in dual-duct mixing boxes?","options":["They can leak even when they are supposed to be fully closed","They can stick open, but only when the cold deck is shut off","They sit in the fan room, which makes them hard to reach","They respond to duct static pressure, not zone temperature"],"expl":"The manual warns that mixing box dampers can leak even when fully closed, letting unwanted hot or cold air into the zone. The boxes are at the zones, not in the fan room, and their dampers follow the zone thermostat.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Dual duct","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-dd-leak"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0569","stem":"Occupants in a dual-duct building complain of clammy air even though temperatures hold. What does the manual give as the reason these systems can control humidity poorly?","options":["The hot and cold airstreams being mixed carry different humidity levels","The hot and cold ducts run through unconditioned spaces on the way","The cold deck air is reheated by a hot water coil at every box","The outdoor air enters the system only through the hot deck duct"],"expl":"Mixing a dehumidified cold stream with an un-dehumidified hot stream gives poor humidity control. Reheat coils belong to reheat systems, not to the dual-duct mixing concept.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Dual duct","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-dd-humidity"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0570","stem":"What does the manual say about dual-duct systems and energy codes?","options":["Like multizone systems, they are now prohibited under many new construction codes","Unlike multizone systems, they are now required for most large new office projects","Like VAV systems, they are now favored under most codes for their energy savings","Unlike CAV systems, they are now exempt from most new construction energy codes"],"expl":"Dual-duct systems, like multizone systems, are not energy efficient and are prohibited under many new construction codes. They lost popularity after the 1973 energy crisis.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Dual duct","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-dd-energy"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0571","stem":"How does a heat pump heat a building?","options":["It moves heat from outdoor air or the ground to indoors at a higher temperature","It burns fuel in a combustion chamber and passes that heat into the airstream","It runs electric current through resistance elements placed in the airstream","It circulates hot boiler water through a coil inside the air handling unit"],"expl":"Like a refrigerator, a heat pump extracts heat from the atmosphere or ground and releases it at a higher temperature inside. Resistance elements are only the auxiliary backup, not the heat pump itself.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Heat pumps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-hp-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0572","stem":"According to the manual, in what kind of climate is an air-source heat pump a practical heating system?","options":["Where the outdoor air rarely drops much below 50°F","Where the outdoor air rarely drops much below 32°F","Where the outdoor air rarely drops much below 0°F","Where the outdoor air rarely drops much below 65°F"],"expl":"The manual says a heat pump is practical where air temperature does not drop much below 50°F; below that, heat may come from the ground or groundwater at added cost. 32°F is a common guess but is not the manual's figure. Current cold-climate air-source units heat well below freezing, so treat 50°F as the manual's answer for this exam.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Heat pumps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-hp-50f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0573","stem":"On a cold morning, an inspector notices a heat pump's electric heat strips energize along with the compressor. What purpose does the manual give for those strips?","options":["They add capacity once the unit alone becomes too inefficient","They defrost the indoor coil so it can keep cooling in the summer","They preheat outdoor air before it reaches the economizer damper","They dry the condensate pan between cooling cycles to stop growth"],"expl":"Most heat pumps installed now must include an auxiliary electric resistance element to supplement heating when the heat pump becomes inefficient, which also shortens heating time. Defrost of the indoor coil in summer is not a function the manual describes.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Heat pumps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-hp-aux"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0574","stem":"Which is NOT one of the heat pump categories that ARI lists in its standard ratings?","options":["Gas-fired absorption","Packaged terminal","Ground water-source","Ground-source closed loop"],"expl":"ARI lists unitary air-source (HP), ground water-source (GWHP), packaged terminal (PTHP), water-source (WSHP), and ground-source closed loop (GSHP). A gas-fired absorption unit is not on that list.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Heat pumps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-hp-ari"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0575","stem":"Which heat pump arrangement does the manual say inspectors most commonly encounter in commercial buildings?","options":["Water-source units in each zone on a closed water loop","Unitary air-source units on the roof of each building","Ground-source closed loop units drawing on geothermal wells","Packaged terminal units set through the exterior wall"],"expl":"Water-source heat pumps (WSHPs), one per zone on a closed piping loop tied to a cooling tower and a boiler, are the most common commercial arrangement. Packaged terminal units are an ARI category but not the one the manual names as most common.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Water-source HP","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-wshp"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0576","stem":"Field studies of commercial and residential heat pumps commonly report all of these problems EXCEPT which?","options":["Excessive outdoor air intake","Incorrect refrigerant charge","Excessive leakage in the ducts","Low airflows through the unit"],"expl":"The manual lists low airflows, incorrect refrigerant charge, refrigerant leaks, control problems, and excessive duct leakage. Excessive outdoor air intake is not among the reported problems.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Heat pumps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-hp-problems"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0577","stem":"Above a ceiling, an inspector finds a suspended heat pump hanging out of level, with water standing at one end of its internal condensate pan. What does the manual tie this to?","options":["Improper suspension that ruins the pitch the pan needs to drain","Refrigerant leaks that flood the pan with compressor oil and water","Auxiliary heat strips that boil off the water in the pan too fast","A thermostat fan switch left on ON instead of on AUTO all day"],"expl":"The manual says heat pumps should be evaluated for installation and internal condensate pan pitch, because a unit not suspended properly may not drain. Refrigerant leaks are a real heat pump problem but do not put water in the pan.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Heat pumps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-hp-pan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0578","stem":"How does a fan coil unit condition air?","options":["A fan pulls air through a filter and across a hot or chilled water coil","A fan pulls air across a refrigerant coil that is served by its own compressor","Jets of primary air induce room air across a coil inside the cabinet","Hot and cold duct air mix in a box before discharging into the room"],"expl":"A fan coil unit draws air through a filter and blows it across a hot water or chilled water coil. A self-contained refrigerant circuit describes a heat pump or packaged terminal unit, not a fan coil.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan coil units","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-fcu-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0579","stem":"An engineer is choosing equipment for a building with dozens of small rooms along its outside walls. Where does the manual say fan coil units are typically used?","options":["Buildings with many perimeter zones, like schools and hotels","Buildings with open areas and even loads, like exhibit halls","Buildings with few windows and interior loads, like factories","Buildings with one thermostat for one zone, like big box stores"],"expl":"Fan coils suit buildings with many zones located mainly along exterior walls, such as schools, hotels, apartments, and offices. Open areas with uniform loads are the typical CAV application.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan coil units","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-fcu-use"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0580","stem":"A hotel's maintenance program services its central AHUs but has skipped the fan coil units in the guest rooms for years. What risk does the manual flag?","options":["The fan coils can become a major contaminant source for nearby guests","The fan coils can starve the central AHUs of their return air supply","The fan coils can raise static pressure in the building's supply trunks","The fan coils can pull the building into a positive pressure condition"],"expl":"Self-contained units such as fan coils and unit ventilators that are overlooked in maintenance often become a significant source of contaminants, especially for occupants near them. Fan coils usually discharge directly to the room, so they have little effect on central trunk static.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fan coil units","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-fcu-neglect"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0581","stem":"What is the main trade-off of using the cavity above the ceiling as a supply or return plenum?","options":["Lower first cost, but strict life safety rules for plenum materials","Lower first cost, but no outdoor air can ever reach the occupied zones","Higher first cost, but no code limits on materials in the cavity","Higher first cost, but easier cleaning than a ducted return system"],"expl":"Using building cavities as plenums often reduces initial HVAC cost, but designers, maintenance staff, and contractors must obey strict life and safety code rules for materials and devices in the plenum. Code limits on plenum materials are stricter, not absent.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Ceiling plenums","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-plenum-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0582","stem":"Above a dropped ceiling used as a return plenum, the inspector finds cans of paint and cleaning products stored on the tiles. How should he treat them?","options":["As a finding, since they can taint or block airflow and codes usually bar them","As acceptable, since sealed containers cannot release anything into the air","As a concern only if the ceiling cavity is being used as a supply plenum","As a fire issue only, since stored goods have no effect on the airstream"],"expl":"The manual tells inspectors to look for materials such as paint and cleaners in the plenum, because they can contaminate circulating air or disrupt airflow and are usually prohibited by building codes. A return plenum carries air to the AHU, so the concern is not limited to supply plenums.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Ceiling plenums","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-plenum-materials"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0583","stem":"In a ceiling return plenum, the inspector finds sweating chilled water pipes and stained ceiling tiles below them. Why does the manual flag these?","options":["They point to moisture that can support microbial growth","They point to refrigerant that can displace the plenum air","They point to a fire rating problem in the ceiling cavity","They point to overheating in the return air plenum"],"expl":"Condensation on plenum pipes creates a growth area for microbes, and water-damaged tiles indicate leakage and possible microbial growth. Neither sign points to refrigerant, a fire rating problem, or overheating.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Ceiling plenums","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-plenum-moisture"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0584","stem":"For which area does the manual say exhaust is frequently recommended, though codes do not always require it?","options":["Reprographics areas","Parking garages","Toilet facilities","Janitorial closets"],"expl":"Reprographics areas, graphic arts facilities, beauty salons, and other known contaminant sources are recommended for exhaust but not always required. Toilets, janitorial closets, cooking facilities, and parking garages are the areas codes typically require.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Local exhaust","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-exhaust-recommended"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0585","stem":"A copy room has its own exhaust fan, but its ceiling cavity is open to the building's return air plenum. What does the manual require for an exhausted area like this?","options":["Isolate it from the return air system so contaminants stay put","Connect it to the nearest return grille so exhaust is shared","Pressurize it above the adjoining spaces so odors are pushed out","Supply extra air to it until it matches the return plenum"],"expl":"Any area designed to be exhausted must be isolated from the return air system so its contaminants are not carried elsewhere, and it must stay at lower pressure than its surroundings. Pressurizing it would push contaminants out into the building.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Local exhaust","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-exhaust-isolate"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0586","stem":"When checking a print room's local exhaust, what should the inspector confirm about the ducting on the discharge fan?","options":["It is intact all the way out to the exterior vent","It is tied into the nearest return air duct run","It ends in the ceiling plenum above the print room","It is sized to keep the room positive to the hall"],"expl":"The manual says to check that the discharge ducting is intact to the outside vent, or contaminated air is released back into the building. Print rooms should run negative, not positive, to surrounding areas.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Local exhaust","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-exhaust-duct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0587","stem":"During a walk-through, entry doors whistle inward and the inspector notes outdoor air dampers nearly shut while exhaust fans run. What pressure condition does the manual say a building should be designed and run at?","options":["Slightly positive or neutral, to limit entry of outdoor pollutants","Slightly negative, to draw outdoor pollutants toward the exhaust fans","Strongly positive, whether or not the building is tightly sealed","Strongly negative, so that every space acts like an exhausted room"],"expl":"Operating at slightly positive or neutral pressure reduces outdoor pollutants entering through unintended pathways, and true slight positive pressure also requires tight construction. The inward whistle shows the building running negative, which works against that goal.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Pressurization","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.2","units":["nim:b-pressurization"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0600","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, a chilled-water cooling coil can dehumidify only when the chilled water is kept at about what temperature?","options":["Below 45°F","Below 50°F","Below 55°F","Below 60°F"],"expl":"The manual says dehumidification needs chilled fluid generally below 45°F for water. 55°F is a common supply air temperature, not the chilled water limit.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Coils","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 A","units":["nim:c-coil-45f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0601","stem":"Why does the manual say steam humidifiers should be fed with potable water instead of treated boiler water?","options":["To keep occupants from breathing boiler treatment chemicals","To keep mineral scale from fouling the steam dispersion tubes","To keep the steam from condensing inside the duct lining","To keep the boiler from losing pressure during peak demand"],"expl":"Treated boiler water carries treatment chemicals that would reach occupants in the steam. Scale and condensation are real humidifier issues, but they are not the reason the manual gives.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Humidification","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 B","units":["nim:c-steam-potable"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0602","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, mold growth problems become more likely when the humidistat in the occupied space is set above what level?","options":["45% RH","35% RH","55% RH","65% RH"],"expl":"The manual flags an occupied-space humidistat set point above 45%. 65% is the ASHRAE 62.1 office guidance ceiling, a different number for a different purpose.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Humidification","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 B","units":["nim:c-space-humidistat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0603","stem":"A humidifier's high-limit humidistat is mounted in the supply duct downstream of the point where water vapor is added. What setting does the manual call typical for it, and why?","options":["About 70% RH, to avoid condensation inside the ductwork","About 45% RH, to avoid mold growth in occupied rooms","About 65% RH, to meet the ASHRAE office humidity limit","About 90% RH, to avoid water carryover past the coil"],"expl":"The duct high-limit is generally set at 70% so vapor does not condense in the duct. 45% is the occupied-space set point threshold, not the duct high-limit.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Humidification","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 B","units":["nim:c-highlimit-70"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0604","stem":"Citing ASHRAE Standard 62.1-2013, the manual says relative humidity in office facilities is generally best kept below what level?","options":["65%","45%","70%","55%"],"expl":"The manual cites ASHRAE 62.1-2013 for keeping office RH below 65%. 45% is the occupied-space humidistat level tied to mold risk, and 70% is the duct high-limit setting.","domain":"Standards","sub":"IAQ standards","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 B","units":["nim:c-ashrae-rh65"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0605","stem":"Why do humidifiers that use high-pressure spray or compressed-air atomization need mist eliminators downstream?","options":["Their large droplets do not evaporate within the AHU","Their fine vapor condenses on the downstream filter bank","Their steam carries boiler chemicals into the duct","Their nozzles shed scale that clogs the coil fins"],"expl":"Atomizing systems make comparatively large droplets that cannot evaporate in the space a typical AHU offers, so baffles catch them. Boiler chemicals are a steam humidifier concern, not an atomizer one.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Mist eliminators","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 T","units":["nim:c-mist-eliminator"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0606","stem":"The manual describes low-efficiency filters as having an ASHRAE dust spot rating of about 10% to 20% or less. What is their main job?","options":["Keeping lint and debris from clogging the coils","Removing bacteria and pollen for occupant health","Capturing superfine particles down to 0.01 micron","Removing gases and volatile organic compounds"],"expl":"Low-efficiency filters mainly protect equipment by keeping lint and dust off the coils. The manual says filters must also remove bacteria and pollen, which takes higher efficiency than these provide.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filtration","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","units":["nim:c-filter-low"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0607","stem":"Which ASHRAE dust spot range does the manual assign to medium-efficiency filters?","options":["30% to 60%","10% to 20%","85% to 95%","60% to 80%"],"expl":"Medium-efficiency filters rate 30% to 60% dust spot. 10% to 20% is the low band, 85% to 95% the high band, and 60% to 80% is the arrestance range for MERV 1 to 4.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filtration","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","units":["nim:c-filter-bands"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0608","stem":"As a filter loads with particles over its service life, what happens to its performance?","options":["Efficiency rises, and pressure drop rises with it","Efficiency falls, and pressure drop falls with it","Efficiency falls, while pressure drop stays steady","Efficiency rises, while airflow through it rises"],"expl":"A loading filter captures particles better but resists airflow more, so pressure drop climbs and airflow falls. The common belief that a dirty filter filters worse is the trap.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filtration","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","units":["nim:c-filter-loading"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0609","stem":"An inspector finds low-efficiency panel filters bowed and partly pulled out of their rack, with dust caked on the cooling coil behind them. Which explanation matches the manual?","options":["Overloaded filters blew out and let unfiltered air bypass","Undersized filters raised face velocity and shed fibers","Wet filters collapsed and released mold onto the coil face","High-efficiency filters starved the fan and reversed flow"],"expl":"The manual says low-efficiency filters loaded to excess deform and blow out of the rack, and the bypassed air clogs coils and dirties ducts. The other chains are not what the manual describes.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filtration","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","units":["nim:c-filter-blowout"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0610","stem":"According to the manual, why is a medium-efficiency pleated filter the best choice in many buildings?","options":["It removes more than low-efficiency filters and outlasts high-efficiency ones","It removes gases as well as particles and needs no separate pre-filter","It costs less than flat panel filters and can be washed and reused","It matches HEPA efficiency with a fraction of the HEPA pressure drop"],"expl":"The manual's reasoning is a balance: better removal than low-efficiency filters and longer life without clogging than high-efficiency filters. It does not claim HEPA-level efficiency or gas removal.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filtration","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","units":["nim:c-filter-medium-best"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0611","stem":"A pharmaceutical suite's AHU is being fitted with HEPA filters. Although HEPA media is heavily pleated to lower face velocity, what pressure drop does the manual say it can still add?","options":["Up to 2 inches w.g.","Up to 0.5 inch w.g.","Up to 2.5 inches w.g.","Up to 3 inches w.g."],"expl":"The manual says HEPA filters still contribute pressure drops of up to 2 inches w.g. 0.5 inch is the top of the passive silencer range, and 2.5 inches is the induction box supply pressure.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filtration","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C","units":["nim:c-hepa"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0612","stem":"Why are the MERV 17 to 20 classes marked as deleted in the ASHRAE filter table reproduced in the manual?","options":["ASHRAE has no test procedure for HEPA filters","ASHRAE 52.1 was discontinued in January 2009","NAFA moved HEPA filters into the MERV 16 class","ULPA filters failed the ASHRAE 52.2 dust test"],"expl":"Table 2-1 notes that ASHRAE has no HEPA test procedure and so dropped MERV 17 to 20. The 52.1 discontinuation is also in the table notes, but it explains why dust spot and arrestance are for reference only.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Filter standards","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C, Table 2-1","units":["nim:c-merv17-dropped"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0613","stem":"The manual's filter table lists dust spot efficiency and average arrestance from ANSI/ASHRAE 52.1 for reference only. Why?","options":["Standard 52.1 was discontinued as of January 2009","Standard 52.1 applies only to HEPA and ULPA filters","Standard 52.1 was replaced by ASHRAE Standard 62.1","Standard 52.1 covers residential filters only"],"expl":"The table notes that ANSI/ASHRAE 52.1 was discontinued in January 2009, so its ranges are reference values. MERV comes from Standard 52.2, and 62.1 is the ventilation standard, not a filter test.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Filter standards","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C, Table 2-1","units":["nim:c-std521"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0614","stem":"According to the filter application table in the manual, which MERV range is typically used for general surgery, hospitals and health care, and smoke removal?","options":["MERV 13 to 16","MERV 9 to 12","MERV 5 to 8","MERV 1 to 4"],"expl":"Table 2-1 puts smoke removal, general surgery, and hospitals in MERV 13 to 16, the 0.30 to 1.0 micron band. MERV 9 to 12 covers better commercial buildings and superior residential.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filtration","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 C, Table 2-1","units":["nim:c-merv-table"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0615","stem":"What is the job of the P-trap on an air handler's condensate drain?","options":["It holds a water seal that isolates the pan from the drain","It holds a reserve of water that keeps the pan surface wetted","It slows the condensate flow so the pan can fill evenly","It collects debris and slime so the drain line does not clog"],"expl":"The U-shaped trap holds water that isolates the drain system from the pan, like a sink trap, so air is not drawn in through the drain. It is not meant to hold water in the pan.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Drains and traps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 F","units":["nim:c-ptrap-seal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0616","stem":"A draw-through AHU has a poorly built condensate trap, and the drain line ends near a sewer vent. What risk does the manual name for this setup?","options":["Contaminants being sucked into the HVAC system","Condensate backing up into the sewer vent stack","Sewer gas pressurizing the supply duct downstream","Chilled water leaking into the building drain line"],"expl":"Under negative pressure a poor trap lets the unit pull air through the drain, so a nearby sewer vent becomes a contaminant source. The pan may also fail to drain, but backflow into the vent is not the named risk.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Drains and traps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 F","units":["nim:c-trap-negative"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0617","stem":"An air handler's drain pan has several drain fittings at different heights. Which one should the inspector confirm the condensate drain is connected to?","options":["The lowest fitting, so standing water cannot collect","The highest fitting, so the trap stays fully charged","The fitting nearest the fan, so fan suction clears it","The fitting farthest from the coil, to catch drip"],"expl":"Connecting to the lowest fitting lets the pan drain to its lowest level instead of holding water. A higher fitting leaves standing water that feeds mold and bacteria.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Drains and traps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 F","units":["nim:c-lowest-fitting"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0618","stem":"A properly sized trap can still let unconditioned air into the unit. According to the manual, why are trap primers sometimes installed?","options":["To keep the traps from drying out in heating season","To keep the traps from freezing on winter rooftops","To add a biocide that stops slime forming in the trap","To keep the traps from overflowing in cooling season"],"expl":"When there is no condensate, as in heating season, trap water can evaporate and let air flow through the trap. Primers keep the seal wet. Freezing and biocides are not the purpose the manual gives.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Drains and traps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 D, F","units":["nim:c-trap-primer"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0619","stem":"Which damper type keeps the direction of airflow unchanged when its blades are partly closed?","options":["Opposed-blade box damper","Parallel-blade box damper","Single-blade damper","Round butterfly damper"],"expl":"In an opposed-blade damper, adjacent blades turn in opposite directions, so partly closed blades do not redirect the air. Parallel blades all tilt the same way and deflect the airflow.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Dampers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 G","units":["nim:c-damper-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0620","stem":"The outdoor air intake on a rooftop unit has a bird screen. What condition does the manual call for?","options":["Unobstructed, with mesh no larger than 1/2 inch","Unobstructed, with mesh no larger than 1/4 inch","Unobstructed, with mesh no larger than 3/4 inch","Unobstructed, with mesh no larger than 1 inch"],"expl":"The manual wants the screen free of obstructions such as bird droppings and no more than 1/2 inch mesh. The other sizes are not the manual's figure.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Outdoor air intake","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 G","units":["nim:c-bird-screen"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0621","stem":"On a cold morning an office has stale air and high CO2 readings. The inspector finds the outdoor air damper shut and a tripped freezestat in the AHU. What does the manual point to?","options":["The freezestat tripped, was not reset, and cut off outdoor air","The economizer locked out and raised the mixed air temperature","The fire damper closed and isolated the building's return air path","The high-limit humidistat tripped and shut down the supply fan"],"expl":"Freezestats protect coils by closing the outdoor air damper, and inadequate ventilation follows if one trips and is not reset or is set too high. The other devices do not close the outdoor air damper.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Dampers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 G","units":["nim:c-freezestat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0622","stem":"Where does the manual say fire dampers are typically installed in an HVAC system?","options":["Where a duct crosses a rated wall, partition, or floor","Where a duct leaves the fan, coil, or filter section","Where outdoor, return, or relief air meets the mixing box","Where a branch, runout, or flex duct meets a diffuser"],"expl":"Fire dampers go where ducts penetrate fire-rated barriers so fire cannot travel through the duct. The other locations have no fire separation to protect.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Fire and smoke dampers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 H","units":["nim:c-fd-location"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0623","stem":"What makes the blade stack of a typical fire damper close automatically?","options":["A fuse link that responds to elevated temperature","A pressure switch that senses a dirty filter bank","A thermostat that senses low supply air temperature","A plastic gear drive turned by a damper motor"],"expl":"The accordion-folded blade stack drops when a temperature-sensitive fuse link detects heat. A low-temperature sensor is a freezestat, which closes the outdoor air damper instead.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fire and smoke dampers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 H","units":["nim:c-fd-fuselink"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0624","stem":"Through an access door, the inspector sees a fire damper whose folded blade stack sits in a channel at the top of the frame, clear of the airstream. Which design type is it?","options":["B-type, used in low pressure systems","A-type, used in low pressure systems","C-type, used in high pressure systems","C-type, used in low pressure systems"],"expl":"A B-type damper keeps its blade stack in a channel out of the airstream. An A-type stack also sits at the top but protrudes into the air, and C-type adds a transition plate and collar.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fire and smoke dampers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 H","units":["nim:c-fd-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0625","stem":"According to the manual, A-type and B-type fire dampers are intended for systems at roughly what pressure?","options":["Low pressure, generally under 3 inches w.g.","Low pressure, generally under 1 inch w.g.","Medium pressure, generally under 6 inches w.g.","High pressure, generally above 3 inches w.g."],"expl":"The manual says both A and B-type dampers are for low pressure systems, generally under 3 inches w.g. Higher pressure systems use the C-type.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Fire and smoke dampers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 H","units":["nim:c-fd-pressure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0626","stem":"What feature lets a C-type fire damper keep its blades closed in higher pressure systems?","options":["A transition plate and collar","A second fuse link at the bottom","A motor actuator and linkage","A blade stack out of the airstream"],"expl":"C-type dampers add a transition plate and collar, available round, rectangular, or oval, to hold the blades shut at higher pressure or flow. Keeping the stack out of the airstream describes the B-type.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fire and smoke dampers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 H","units":["nim:c-fd-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0627","stem":"What does the non-overloading characteristic of a backward-inclined fan mean?","options":["Power demand falls once flow rises past a certain point","Power demand keeps rising as the flow rate increases","Airflow stays constant as the system static pressure rises","Motor current is capped by a built-in thermal overload"],"expl":"For a backward-inclined fan the required horsepower actually drops once flow passes a certain point. Power that keeps climbing with flow describes a forward-curved fan.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Fans","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 I","units":["nim:c-fan-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0628","stem":"A small office is served only by self-contained packaged units. What does the manual say the inspector should keep in mind about ventilation?","options":["They seldom supply outdoor air, so it must come by other means","They always supply 100% outdoor air, so exhaust must be balanced","They supply outdoor air only in heating mode, so check dampers","They supply outdoor air through the condenser, so check the coil"],"expl":"The manual notes self-contained or packaged units seldom supply outdoor air, so ventilation must come from windows, doors, or infiltration. The 100% outdoor air claim is the opposite of the manual.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Packaged and split units","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 K","units":["nim:c-packaged-oa"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0629","stem":"In a typical split-system DX unit as the manual describes it, which components are housed in the outdoor unit?","options":["Compressor, condenser, and outdoor coil","Fan, indoor coil, and furnace section","Compressor, air filters, and dampers","Furnace, condenser, and air filters"],"expl":"The split system separates the hot side, with compressor, condenser, and outdoor coil outside, from the indoor fan, coil, furnace, filters, and dampers. Filters and dampers stay with the indoor unit.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Packaged and split units","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 J","units":["nim:c-split"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0630","stem":"As the call for supply air falls, how does an induction box keep a nearly constant airflow to its diffusers?","options":["Its nozzle closes down while the room air damper opens","Its fan speeds up while the supply air damper closes","Its reheat coil turns on while the nozzle opens wider","Its nozzle opens wider while the room air damper closes"],"expl":"High pressure primary air through a modulating nozzle induces room air. As demand drops the nozzle closes and the recirculated air damper opens, holding total flow while delivery temperature changes. A fan belongs to a fan-powered mixing box.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Terminal units","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 M","units":["nim:c-induction"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0631","stem":"According to the manual, what distinguishes a register from a grille?","options":["A register has a damper for volume or direction control","A register mixes supply air with room air at high velocity","A register serves only return and exhaust air openings","A register uses a slotted panel with no moving parts"],"expl":"A register is a grille fitted with a damper. Injecting air at high velocity for mixing describes a diffuser, and a plain slotted panel with no control describes a grille.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Air devices","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 P","units":["nim:c-grille-register"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0632","stem":"Diffuser throw is the horizontal distance supply air travels before it slows to a specified speed. What speed does the manual say is usually used?","options":["50 feet per minute","100 feet per minute","150 feet per minute","25 feet per minute"],"expl":"The manual defines throw to a terminal speed usually of 50 feet per minute. Spread is the lateral dispersion and drop is the downward migration.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Air devices","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 Q","units":["nim:c-throw"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0633","stem":"In a VAV office, people sitting right under a ceiling diffuser complain of cold drafts whenever their zone runs at minimum airflow. What does the manual call this?","options":["The diffuser dumps its air in a narrow column","The diffuser throws its air past the far wall","The diffuser short-circuits air to the return","The diffuser spreads its air too far along the ceiling"],"expl":"At reduced airflows diffusers may dump air in a narrow column, chilling people below. Throw past the wall and excessive spread are high-flow behaviors, the opposite condition.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Air devices","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 Q","units":["nim:c-dump"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0634","stem":"Passive silencers force air through narrow passages between pods of sound-absorbing material. What air-side pressure drop does the manual give for them?","options":["0.3 to 0.5 inch w.g.","1.0 to 2.0 inches w.g.","0.05 to 0.1 inch w.g.","2.0 to 3.0 inches w.g."],"expl":"Pod-type passive silencers typically add 0.3 to 0.5 inch w.g. Designs with concentric perforated duct and no interior pods avoid most of this drop. Up to 2 inches is the HEPA figure.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Sound attenuators","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 S","units":["nim:c-silencer"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0635","stem":"A double-wall duct has fiberglass sandwiched between inner and outer metal layers. When does the manual place it in the porous family?","options":["When its inner wall is not continuously sealed","When its outer wall is not continuously sealed","When it carries return air instead of supply","When its fiberglass layer is over one inch thick"],"expl":"DW-lined duct counts as porous if the inside wall is not continually sealed, because moisture can reach the fiberglass. The outer wall does not face the airstream.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct types","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 L, §3.4 B","units":["nim:c-dw-porous"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0636","stem":"Compared with sheet metal, why does the manual say ducts with fibrous glass liner need extra attention to design, filtration, humidity, and maintenance?","options":["Their porous surface traps more dirt and then water","Their foil facing tears easily and draws in plenum air","Their fibers conduct heat faster than bare sheet metal","Their joints need UL 181A tape to keep a tight seal"],"expl":"The porous liner surface presents more area than sheet metal to trap dirt and then collect water, which supports growth. The other options are not the manual's reason.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct types","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.4 C","units":["nim:c-porous-area"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0637","stem":"Why does the manual describe metal ducts as able to be cleaned aggressively?","options":["They are the most durable and do not absorb moisture","They are the smoothest and do not rust when wetted","They are the lightest and have the fewest seam joints","They are the easiest to reach through flex runouts"],"expl":"Metal is the most durable duct material and does not absorb moisture, so it tolerates aggressive cleaning. Galvanized steel can still rust, so the second option is false.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct types","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.4 A","units":["nim:c-metal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0638","stem":"A lab fume exhaust carries vapors at about 180°F. Which duct pipe material from the manual suits this service?","options":["CPVC, rated to about 200°F","PVC, rated to about 200°F","PVC, rated to about 140°F","CPVC, rated to about 140°F"],"expl":"CPVC has a maximum operating temperature of 200°F, so it handles elevated-temperature fumes. PVC tops out at 140°F, below this exhaust temperature.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct types","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.4 H, I","units":["nim:c-pvc-cpvc"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0639","stem":"A building returns air through a concrete duct that runs below the slab. What does the manual warn that cracks or holes in it can allow?","options":["Soil gases, moisture, and mold spores to enter the airstream","Asbestos, lead, and silica from the concrete to enter the air","Supply air, dust, and odors to short-circuit into the return","Condensate from the cooling coil to drain into the soil"],"expl":"Cracked concrete returns in crawl spaces or below slabs can draw in soil gases, moisture, and mold spores. Asbestos cement is Transite, a different duct material.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct types","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.4 E","units":["nim:c-concrete"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0640","stem":"At the end of a long supply run, a room gets weak airflow. The inspector finds a 25-foot flex runout with two sharp bends feeding its diffuser. What does the manual identify?","options":["Excessive friction from the long bent flex runout","Excessive leakage through the flex duct's fiber scrim","Excessive condensation inside the flex duct's core","Excessive velocity that makes the diffuser dump air"],"expl":"The manual says long runs of flexible duct with sharp bends cause excessive friction, and end-of-run rooms suffer first as friction nears the fan's limit. Dumping comes from too little flow, not too much velocity.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct types","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.3 I, §3.4 D","units":["nim:c-flex-friction"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0641","stem":"An inspector finds a sub-slab duct identified as Transite. How does the manual say it should be treated?","options":["As asbestos cement that is never cut into or damaged","As porous duct material that is always replaced if wetted","As nonporous metal duct that can be cleaned aggressively","As PVC duct pipe that must stay below 140°F in service"],"expl":"Transite is a trade name for asbestos cement, so it is treated as ACM for inspection, repair, or cleaning and must not be cut or damaged. Aggressive cleaning is the manual's note for metal ducts.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Hazardous materials","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §3.4 G","units":["nim:c-transite"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0650","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, EPA and NIOSH studies found indoor pollutant levels can be up to how many times higher than outdoor levels?","options":["10 to 100 times","2 to 5 times","5 to 10 times","100 to 1,000 times"],"expl":"The manual cites EPA and NIOSH studies showing indoor pollutants up to 10 to 100 times outdoor levels. 5 to 10 times understates the upper end the studies report.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.1","units":["nim:d-indoor-10-100"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0651","stem":"A property manager tells an inspector on the phone that the ducts are surely making staff sick. How should the inspector approach the site visit?","options":["Arrive without any hypothesis already formed","Arrive with the manager's theory as the focus","Arrive planning to confirm duct contamination","Arrive with the likely source already chosen"],"expl":"The manual recommends against coming in with a formed hypothesis because it biases how the investigation is designed. Adopting the manager's theory is exactly that bias.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.1","units":["nim:d-no-hypothesis"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0652","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, what role does the ventilation system often play in a building's IAQ problems?","options":["A pathway carrying pollutants from elsewhere","The primary source of most building pollutants","A filter that removes most indoor pollutants","A sink that traps pollutants for good"],"expl":"The manual says the ventilation system is often the conduit or pathway rather than the source, and several sources may exist. Calling it the primary source is the common assumption it warns against.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.1","units":["nim:d-pathway-not-source"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0653","stem":"During a duct inspection, an office manager reports that several employees have been diagnosed with respiratory illness and asks what is causing it. What should the inspector do?","options":["Refer the concern to an industrial hygienist","Identify the cause from the duct findings","Recommend duct cleaning to fix the illness","Collect air samples to identify the agent"],"expl":"The manual says inspectors must know the limits of their qualifications and bring in IAQ professionals such as industrial hygienists or IEPs. Diagnosing the cause from duct findings goes beyond an inspector's expertise.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.1","units":["nim:d-stay-in-lane"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0654","stem":"A water heater in a furnace room shows scorch marks above its burner. The inspector finds the air handler moves far more supply air than its return can pull back. Which result of this imbalance does the manual list?","options":["Back drafting of gas appliances","Freezing of the evaporator coil","Short cycling of the compressor","Overloading of the filter media"],"expl":"The manual says unequal supply and return volumes create pressure imbalances, increased infiltration, and back drafting of gas appliances. Coil freezing comes from low airflow across the coil, not a supply and return imbalance.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.2","units":["nim:d-air-balance"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0655","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, what are filtration systems in most commercial buildings designed to do first?","options":["Protect the building's equipment","Capture fine respirable particles","Remove gases and odors from air","Control airborne microbial spores"],"expl":"The manual says most commercial filtration is designed first to protect equipment and does little for fine particles. That is why dust gradually builds up in the system.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.2","units":["nim:d-filtration-equipment"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0656","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, when are building materials and components normally the most significant pollutant source?","options":["In new construction, before products off-gas","In old buildings, as the materials break down","In heating season, when windows stay closed","After cleaning, when the ducts are resealed"],"expl":"The manual says building materials are normally a bigger issue in new construction, before products have had time to off-gas. Older materials do fail, but the manual ties material emissions to newness.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.2","units":["nim:d-new-construction"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0657","stem":"The NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual gives carbon monoxide as its example of which type of pollutant?","options":["Gas phase","Particulate","Aerosol","Bioaerosol"],"expl":"The manual lists carbon monoxide as the gas phase example. Aerosols are solid or liquid airborne particles, and CO is a gas, not a particle.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.3","units":["nim:d-pollutant-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0658","stem":"The NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual lists microbial spores, MVOCs, and deteriorated insulation as pollutants spread by which airstream?","options":["Supply air","Return air","Exhaust air","Make-up air"],"expl":"The manual's supply air pathway covers dust, microbial spores and MVOCs, and failing insulation delivered to occupied space. Return air covers contaminants drawn into the system from building use, maintenance, and construction.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.4","units":["nim:d-supply-pathway"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0659","stem":"Occupants near a restroom corridor smell sewer odor, and the inspector finds a disconnected joint in the duct serving the restroom fan above the ceiling. Which HVAC pollutant pathway from the manual does this match?","options":["Exhaust air leaking into the occupied space","Return air drawing in fumes from floor care","Outside air pulling in pollutants from traffic","Supply air carrying dust and debris to rooms"],"expl":"The manual's exhaust air pathway is exhaust ducts leaking contaminants into occupied space, which is what a broken exhaust joint does. Return air pathways involve pollutants from building use drawn into the system.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.4","units":["nim:d-exhaust-pathway"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0660","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, when air inside a tall building is warmer than outside air, how does stack effect pressurize the floors?","options":["Positive up high, negative down low","Negative up high, positive down low","Positive on every floor equally","Negative on every floor equally"],"expl":"Warm air rises, giving positive pressure on higher floors and negative pressure on lower floors, with a neutral plane between. That is why outside air infiltrates low and exfiltrates high.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Driving Forces","units":["ibeam:d-stack-pressures"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0661","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, what does wind create on the leeward side of a building?","options":["Negative pressure, causing exfiltration","Positive pressure, causing infiltration","Negative pressure, causing infiltration","Positive pressure, causing exfiltration"],"expl":"Wind builds positive pressure and infiltration on the windward side and negative pressure and exfiltration on the leeward side. Positive pressure with infiltration describes the windward face.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Driving Forces","units":["ibeam:d-wind"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0662","stem":"A lab bench releases solvent vapor. According to EPA's I-BEAM, which way should air move relative to the workers and the bench?","options":["From the workers toward the bench, then out","From the bench toward the workers, then out","From the bench into the ceiling return plenum","From outdoors across the bench to the workers"],"expl":"I-BEAM says air should flow from occupants toward a source and out of the building, not from the source past occupants. Pressure differences set that direction.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Air Movement and Pressure","units":["ibeam:d-flow-direction"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0663","stem":"Doors in a building with more exhaust than outdoor air intake whistle when closed, and pollen shows up inside near the entrances. According to EPA's I-BEAM, what does a negatively pressurized building do?","options":["Draws outdoor air and pollutants in at any opening","Pushes indoor air and pollutants out at any opening","Draws outdoor air in only through the air intake","Holds outdoor air out at the doors and the cracks"],"expl":"I-BEAM says a negatively pressurized building draws air and outside pollutants in through any available opening, such as doors, windows, and cracks. Pushing air out describes a positively pressurized building.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Common Airflow Pathways","units":["ibeam:d-negative-building"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0664","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, what pressure should an underground parking garage hold relative to the building above it?","options":["Negative pressure","Positive pressure","Neutral pressure","Alternating pressure"],"expl":"I-BEAM says to use exhaust ventilation and keep the garage negative relative to the building so vehicle exhaust cannot flow into occupied space. A positive garage would push exhaust into the building.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Pollutant distribution paths","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Indoor Sources, Vehicles","units":["ibeam:d-garage-negative"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0665","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, how does thermal comfort fit into an indoor air quality investigation?","options":["It falls within IAQ but does not prove a pollutant issue","It falls outside IAQ and is left to the test and balancer","It confirms that a pollutant issue is present in the space","It is covered only by ASHRAE Standard 62.1 on ventilation"],"expl":"The manual says thermal comfort falls within IAQ and may prompt inspections, but does not necessarily indicate an air quality problem. It still has a place in the investigation, so it is not handed off entirely.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.5","units":["nim:d-comfort-in-iaq"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0666","stem":"Which standard does the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual cite as the reference for acceptable thermal comfort ranges?","options":["ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 55","ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 62.1","ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 52.2","ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 90.1"],"expl":"Standard 55, Thermal Environmental Conditions for Human Occupancy, covers thermal comfort. Standard 62.1 covers ventilation for acceptable IAQ, which the manual cites for CO2 guidance.","domain":"Standards","sub":"IAQ standards","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.5","units":["nim:d-std55"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0667","stem":"A call center has packed 40% more staff into its floor than the code design allows. CO2 runs high, yet the air handler delivers its design outdoor air. According to the manual, what is the likely cause?","options":["More occupants than the space was built for","A failing ventilation system that needs repair","Dirty ductwork restricting the outdoor airflow","A combustion appliance leaking into the space"],"expl":"The manual gives this exact case: occupancy above the building's code design raises CO2 and the ventilation system is not the cause. The air handler here meets its design outdoor air, so blaming it misreads the data.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC effects on IAQ","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.4","units":["nim:d-co2-occupancy"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0668","stem":"Which of these is NOT one of the common thermal comfort factors listed in the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual?","options":["Filter efficiency","Air stratification","Temperature changes","Duct leakage"],"expl":"The manual lists temperature, drafts, humidity, temperature changes, air stratification or poor mixing, and duct leakage. Filter efficiency affects particle removal, not the manual's comfort list.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Thermal comfort","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.5","units":["nim:d-comfort-factors"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0669","stem":"A far office never reaches setpoint in summer. Above the ceiling, the inspector finds open seams along the supply branch feeding it. According to the manual, why does the room run warm?","options":["Supply leaks deliver less tempered air than designed","Supply leaks raise the air temperature leaving the coil","Supply leaks pull warm plenum air into the branch","Supply leaks make the thermostat read too cold"],"expl":"The manual says leaking supply ducts do not deliver the design quantity of tempered air to the space, and the system works harder to control temperature. Supply ducts are under positive pressure, so they push air out rather than pull plenum air in.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Thermal comfort","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.5","units":["nim:d-supply-leak-comfort"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0670","stem":"The NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual lists mist from cooling towers under which category of indoor air pollutant causes?","options":["Outside air contaminants","Indoor air contaminants","Equipment/component failures","Building material/components"],"expl":"Cooling tower mist, smog, pollen, and re-entrained exhaust are listed as outside air contaminants. The tower is building equipment, but its mist enters through outdoor air, not from a component failure.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §4.2","units":["nim:d-outside-contaminants"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0671","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, acute effects are those that occur within about how long after exposure?","options":["24 hours","1 hour","72 hours","7 days"],"expl":"I-BEAM defines acute effects as occurring immediately, for example within 24 hours, and usually fading soon after exposure ends. Chronic effects come from long-term or repeated exposure.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Occupant health effects","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Acute Effects","units":["ibeam:d-acute"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0672","stem":"Staff complain the office air is too dry, but the inspector measures 45% RH, well within the normal range. According to EPA's I-BEAM, what else can cause this complaint?","options":["Particles irritating the mucous membranes","Room temperature too warm with no air movement","A mild odor that is hard to identify","Too much outdoor air in the supply"],"expl":"I-BEAM notes a too dry complaint may come from particle irritation of mucous membranes rather than low humidity. Too warm or still air is what I-BEAM links to a stuffy complaint, and a faint odor to a stale one.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Occupant health effects","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Discomfort","units":["ibeam:d-dry-complaint"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0673","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, what separates building related illness (BRI) from sick building syndrome (SBS)?","options":["BRI is a defined illness with a known cause","BRI symptoms clear once occupants leave","BRI seldom involves biological agents","BRI has no single identifiable cause"],"expl":"BRI is a defined illness with a known causative agent, often biological. Symptoms that clear on leaving and no single identifiable cause describe SBS.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Occupant health effects","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Building Associated Illnesses","units":["ibeam:d-sbs-bri"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0674","stem":"Three workers in one building are diagnosed with hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and a contaminated humidifier is found in their air handler. How does EPA's I-BEAM classify this?","options":["Building related illness","Sick building syndrome","Multiple chemical sensitivity","A chronic discomfort effect"],"expl":"I-BEAM names hypersensitivity pneumonitis, Legionnaires' disease, and humidifier fever as common BRI examples, with humidifiers a typical source. SBS involves complaints with no diagnosed illness or clear cause.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Occupant health effects","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Building Related Illness","units":["ibeam:d-bri-examples"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0675","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, what is the professional view of multiple chemical sensitivity (MCS)?","options":["There is significant disagreement over whether it exists","There is broad agreement on its cause and mechanism","There is agreement that formaldehyde is its main cause","There is agreement that more ventilation resolves it"],"expl":"I-BEAM says there is significant professional disagreement about whether MCS exists and what causes it. It adds that resolving such complaints may or may not be possible with building fixes.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Occupant health effects","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Multiple Chemical Sensitivity","units":["ibeam:d-mcs"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0676","stem":"Occupants in a south-facing suite say it is too warm and also report a chemical smell from new furniture. According to EPA's I-BEAM, how are the two linked?","options":["Warmer rooms usually raise emissions from materials","Warmer rooms usually lower emissions from materials","Warmer air usually carries odors away from people","Warmer air usually settles particles out faster"],"expl":"I-BEAM says chemicals are usually released from building materials faster at higher temperatures, so occupants who are too warm are likely exposed to more pollutants. Lower emissions at higher temperature is the reverse.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Thermal comfort","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Factors Affecting Indoor Climate","units":["ibeam:d-warm-emissions"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0677","stem":"EPA's I-BEAM lists damp duct insulation, cooling coils, and drain pans as potential sources of which pollutant class?","options":["Biological contaminants","Volatile organic compounds","Combustion contaminants","Soil gases"],"expl":"I-BEAM's source table puts wet materials, cooling coils, drain pans, and damp duct insulation or filters under biological contaminants. VOC sources are products such as paints, adhesives, and solvents.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"HVAC pollutant sources","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Types of Pollutants","units":["ibeam:d-bio-sources"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0678","stem":"To save energy, a building manager lets indoor RH drift to 65% on nights and weekends while the offices are empty. According to EPA's I-BEAM, why is that a problem?","options":["RH limits for mold and mites apply at all times","RH limits for comfort apply only when occupied","RH above 60% only matters when people are there","RH affects comfort but has no effect on microbes"],"expl":"I-BEAM says keep RH below 60% for mold and 50% for dust mites at all times, including unoccupied hours. Comfort ranges matter when people are present, but microbial growth continues when they are not.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Humidity control","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, Humidity and Microbial Growth","units":["ibeam:d-rh-always"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0679","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, which kind of ventilation handles area sources such as people, building materials, and office equipment?","options":["Dilution with outdoor air","Local exhaust at each source","Exhaust hoods over each source","Filtration of recirculated air"],"expl":"I-BEAM says area sources such as people and office equipment are diluted with outdoor air. Exhaust ventilation and hoods are for fixed sources such as stoves and lab benches.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Ventilation","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Dilution Ventilation","units":["ibeam:d-dilution"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0680","stem":"An inspector wants to estimate what percentage of an office's supply air is outdoor air. Which measurement does EPA's I-BEAM list for that purpose?","options":["Carbon dioxide concentration","Smoke tube airflow pattern","Flow hood air volume reading","Velocity meter air speed"],"expl":"I-BEAM lists measuring CO2 to estimate the percentage of outdoor air or evaluate outdoor air ventilation. A flow hood measures air volume at an outlet, not how much of it is outdoor air.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Ventilation","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Ventilation Measurements","units":["ibeam:d-measure-tools"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0681","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, what pressure pattern does a fan create around itself?","options":["Positive in front of the fan, negative behind it","Negative in front of the fan, positive behind it","Positive both in front of and behind the fan","Negative both in front of and behind the fan"],"expl":"I-BEAM says fans push air in one direction and create positive pressure in front and negative pressure behind. That is why leaks on the inlet side draw air in and leaks on the discharge side push air out.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Airflow and pressure","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Fundamentals of IAQ in Buildings, Driving Forces","units":["ibeam:d-fan-pressure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0682","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, VAV systems reduce HVAC energy cost by about how much compared with constant volume systems?","options":["10 to 20 percent","2 to 4 percent","30 to 50 percent","5 to 10 percent"],"expl":"I-BEAM puts VAV savings at 10 to 20% over CV, while noting VAV complicates outdoor air delivery. The 2 to 4% figure is I-BEAM's estimated performance loss from poor IAQ.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"System types","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, VAV Systems","units":["ibeam:d-vav-savings"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0683","stem":"A VAV air handler holds a fixed outdoor air fraction set for design load. On a mild day, supply airflow drops well below design. According to EPA's I-BEAM, what happens to outdoor air?","options":["The total outdoor air volume drops with supply","The total outdoor air volume stays at design","The outdoor air fraction rises to make up for it","The outdoor air volume rises as supply drops"],"expl":"With a constant fraction, outdoor air volume falls as supply volume falls, which I-BEAM flags as a part-load IAQ risk. Holding outdoor air at design takes a constant volume system or separate VAV outdoor air controls.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"System types","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, VAV Systems","units":["ibeam:d-vav-partload"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0684","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, sealed duct systems with a leakage rate below what percentage usually have a superior life cycle cost?","options":["3 percent","1 percent","5 percent","10 percent"],"expl":"I-BEAM says sealed ducts leaking under 3% usually have a superior life cycle cost and fewer leaky-duct problems. The 5% and 10% figures are looser than the target I-BEAM names.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Duct leakage","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, Ducts","units":["ibeam:d-duct-3pct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0685","stem":"A technician plans to swap loaded filters in a running air handler to avoid downtime. According to EPA's I-BEAM, what should happen first?","options":["Shut the fan off before the change","Leave the fan on at reduced speed","Leave the fan on to hold filters in","Switch the fan to heating mode first"],"expl":"I-BEAM says fans should be shut off when changing filters so dislodged dust is not drawn into the airstream. Keeping the fan running to hold filters in place blows loosened debris downstream.","domain":"HVAC Systems","sub":"Filtration","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, Air Filters","units":["ibeam:d-filter-fan-off"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0686","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM guidance on ASHRAE 62-1999, a 50-person office at 20 cfm of outdoor air per occupant needs what total outdoor airflow?","options":["1,000 cfm","750 cfm","1,500 cfm","20% of supply"],"expl":"I-BEAM gives OA = cfm per occupant x number of occupants, so 20 x 50 = 1,000 cfm. It warns that a 20 cfm per person rate does NOT mean a 20% outdoor air fraction.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Ventilation rates","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, ASHRAE Standard 62-1999","units":["ibeam:d-oa-formula"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0687","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM, citing ASHRAE 62-1989, a training room with peak occupancy under 3 hours may base outdoor air on average occupancy, but never below what share of the maximum?","options":["One-half","One-third","Two-thirds","One-quarter"],"expl":"I-BEAM, citing the 1989 standard, allows average occupancy for short peaks but never less than one-half the maximum. One-third comes from I-BEAM's part-load VAV outdoor air discussion, not this rule.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Ventilation rates","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, HVAC, Intermittent Occupancy","units":["ibeam:d-intermittent"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0700","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, how does an inspection typically begin?","options":["The client phones the inspector about the facility","The inspector walks the building with the engineer","The client signs the scope of inspection statement","The inspector reviews the as-built mechanical plans"],"expl":"The manual says an inspection typically starts with the client calling the inspector to discuss the facility. The walk-through, plan review, and signed scope all come later in the pre-inspection process.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Client first contact","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2","units":["nim:e-client-first-call"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0701","stem":"A property manager calls an inspector about dust complaints in a leased suite. From what point should the inspector keep detailed notes on the case?","options":["From this first phone call onward","From approval of the purpose statement","From signing of the scope of inspection","From the start of the building walk-through"],"expl":"The manual says detailed notes on all discussions and observations begin with the first call. Waiting for the walk-through or the approved scope loses information that shapes the proposal and fee.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Client first contact","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2","units":["nim:e-client-first-call"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0702","stem":"During the first phone consultation, how long should the inspector tell the client a typical site visit will take?","options":["About a half day","About one hour","About one full day","About two full days"],"expl":"The manual says a typical site visit requires about half a day and that this should be communicated in the initial phone call. Every project differs, but half a day is the stated norm.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Site visit","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2","units":["nim:e-site-visit-half-day"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0703","stem":"In the client interview, a building owner says she does not want recommendations in the inspection report. What should the inspector do?","options":["Respect her wishes and format the report to match","Put the recommendations in an appendix anyway","Decline the job, since every report must recommend","Add recommendations only for the worst findings"],"expl":"Most clients want recommendations, but the manual says some have reasons not to, and the inspector should respect that and format the report accordingly. Slipping them into an appendix ignores the client's decision.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Client interview","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 Table 5-2","units":["nim:e-no-recommendations"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0704","stem":"In the Table 5-2 client interview, what does asking 'What type of deliverables are you looking for?' tell the inspector?","options":["Whether to collect photos, samples, or field notes","Whether to present the report in person or by email","Which areas of the system will need to be inspected","How well the client understands the HVAC system"],"expl":"The deliverables question tells the inspector what to collect, such as photos, samples, and detailed field notes. Report delivery format comes from a separate question about how the final report should be presented.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Client interview","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 Table 5-2","units":["nim:e-deliverables-question"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0705","stem":"Midway through an inspection, a department head who is not the designated report recipient asks the inspector what he has found so far. What should the inspector do?","options":["Refer her to the contact person agreed on earlier","Share observations but hold back recommendations","Give her a brief verbal summary of the findings","Copy her on the field notes at the end of the day"],"expl":"The reporting structure agreed with the facility decides who hears findings, and others who ask are directed to that contact person. Even a partial verbal summary breaks that agreement.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Reporting structure","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 C","units":["nim:e-report-recipient"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0706","stem":"A 1960s warehouse has no as-built drawings, so the inspector relies on experience and educated assumptions. What should the final report include?","options":["A note that no as-built drawings existed and assumptions were made","A full set of as-built drawings drafted by the inspector on site","A statement that the inspection results cannot be considered valid","A requirement that the owner commission new drawings before cleaning"],"expl":"The manual says the report should carry a special note that no as-built documentation was available and that assumptions had to be made. It does not require the inspector to draw plans or void the results.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Building information","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 D","units":["nim:e-no-asbuilt"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0707","stem":"On the walk-through, the inspector finds a return trunk that the building drawings do not show. What should he do?","options":["Note the discrepancy on the drawings","Stop until the engineer issues new drawings","Leave that trunk out of the inspection scope","Report it to the local building official"],"expl":"During the walk-through the inspector compares drawings to the installed systems and notes any discrepancies on the drawings. Nothing in the manual calls for halting the job or leaving the unmapped duct out.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Walk-through","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 E","units":["nim:e-walkthrough-discrepancy"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0708","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, how long should a purpose statement be?","options":["One to five sentences","Six to ten sentences","Two to three paragraphs","One full written page"],"expl":"The manual calls for a short, descriptive purpose statement of one to five sentences. The detailed overview of what will be examined belongs in the scope, which is longer.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Purpose statement","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 F","units":["nim:e-purpose-length"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0709","stem":"Which of these should a purpose statement leave out?","options":["An opinion on the facility's current condition","The problem that led to the inspection request","The question the inspection is meant to answer","The system serving the area with the problem"],"expl":"The purpose statement sums up why the inspection is happening. It does not judge or give an opinion on the condition of the facility. Naming the problem, the system, and the question to answer is what makes it specific.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Purpose statement","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 F","units":["nim:e-purpose-no-opinion"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0710","stem":"Occupants blame the HVAC system for heavy dust in one room, while the manager blames a busy exterior door. Which purpose statement best fits NADCA guidance?","options":["Determine if the system serving the room adds to its dust","Determine whether the building's HVAC system is dirty overall","Confirm that the exterior door is the main source of the dust","Show that the room's HVAC system is the source of the dust"],"expl":"A good purpose statement is specific about the problem and the question the inspection will answer. 'Is the system dirty' is not wrong but too general, and the other two presume an answer, which is an opinion.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Purpose statement","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 F","units":["nim:e-purpose-specific"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0711","stem":"An inspector has drafted the purpose statement and wants to start writing the scope. What must happen first?","options":["The client approves the purpose statement","Legal counsel reviews the purpose statement","The client signs the disclaimer statement","The inspector completes the site inspection"],"expl":"The manual says the purpose statement should be presented to and approved by the client before the inspector moves on. Legal review applies to the disclaimer, which is drafted later as the last scope-of-work step.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Purpose statement","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 F, §5.3","units":["nim:e-purpose-approved"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0712","stem":"How does the scope of an inspection differ from its purpose statement?","options":["The scope details what will be examined","The scope sums up why the job is being done","The scope lists what will not be inspected","The scope reports what the inspection found"],"expl":"The manual notes the scope gives a detailed overview of what will be examined, while the purpose statement summarizes the reason. What will not be inspected is the disclaimer's job.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Scope of work","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.2 F","units":["nim:e-scope-vs-purpose"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0713","stem":"Which is the first of the steps the manual lists for developing the scope of work?","options":["Reviewing plans and reports","Determining systems to inspect","Determining where to inspect","Drafting the disclaimer statement"],"expl":"The scope-of-work steps run: review plans and reports, determine systems, determine where to inspect, draft the scope, draft the disclaimer. Choosing systems uses the marked-up plans, so plan review comes first.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Scope of work","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 A","units":["nim:e-scope-steps"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0714","stem":"What does a well-written scope say about the facility itself?","options":["A short description of it and its current use","An assessment of its present overall condition","The inspector's view of how clean it looks now","Its market value and its history of ownership"],"expl":"The scope includes a short description of the facility and its current use but does not describe its condition. Condition findings belong in the report, and value is expressly outside an inspection.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Scope of work","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 E","units":["nim:e-scope-facility-desc"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0715","stem":"When the inspection is finished and the final report is being written, how is the approved scope statement used?","options":["As the introduction to the report","As the conclusion of the report","As the recommendations section","As a signed appendix to the report"],"expl":"The manual says the inspector uses the scope statement as the introduction to the final report. The scope itself typically ends with the purpose statement.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Scope of work","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 E","units":["nim:e-scope-report-intro"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0716","stem":"In the manual's pre-inspection paperwork, what does the disclaimer statement outline?","options":["What will not be inspected or included","Which systems and portions will be inspected","Why the client asked for the inspection","What the inspection found and recommends"],"expl":"The scope outlines what will be inspected, and the disclaimer outlines what will not be inspected or included. After legal review, the client signs it before the inspection.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Disclaimers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 F","units":["nim:e-disclaimer-what"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0717","stem":"A client asks whether the inspector's signed disclaimer shields him from liability for negligent work. What does the manual indicate?","options":["Not necessarily; it clarifies accountability limits","Yes; the client's signature makes it fully binding","Yes; approval by legal counsel makes it fully binding","Yes; its placement in the proposal makes it binding"],"expl":"The manual warns that a disclaimer may not protect the inspector from responsibility for mistakes or negligent work. Its purpose is to make the limits of the inspector's accountability clear. Legal review and the client's signature are steps the manual recommends, but they do not make it a shield.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Disclaimers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 F","units":["nim:e-disclaimer-negligence"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0718","stem":"What is the first step the manual lists for reviewing and marking the building's plans?","options":["Make readable paper copies of the HVAC plans","Outline the general spaces to be investigated","Mark HVAC airflow direction with arrows","Mark where deposits are likely to collect"],"expl":"Step 1 is making paper copies of the applicable HVAC plans in a manageable, readable size so notes can go directly on them. Outlining spaces, arrows, and deposition areas are later steps.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Marking plans","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B","units":["nim:e-plans-copies"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0719","stem":"While examining the study area boundary for duct penetrations, how should the inspector mark the ducts on the plans?","options":["A different color each for supply, return, and exhaust","One color for all ducts that cross the study boundary","A different color for each floor that the ducts serve","Solid lines for supply ducts and dashed lines for return"],"expl":"The manual has the inspector mark air supply, air return, and separate exhaust (such as kitchen hoods) each in a different color. Airflow direction is then shown with arrows, not line styles.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Marking plans","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B","units":["nim:e-plans-colors"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0720","stem":"Complaints come from a school cafeteria. After outlining the cafeteria on the plans, what should the inspector mark next as part of the study space?","options":["Attached spaces such as the kitchen and dish room","The rooftop cooling tower and intake relief dampers","The AHU serving the area and its outdoor air intake","Every room fed from the same electrical sub-panel"],"expl":"Step 3 of plan marking starts with the complaint space and then adds attached spaces like the kitchen and dishwashing areas. Tracing back to the AHU and intake, and locating cooling towers, are later steps.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Marking plans","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B","units":["nim:e-plans-attached-spaces"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0721","stem":"Working upstream from the study space on the plans, how far back does the manual have the inspector trace each supply system?","options":["To the AHU or fan and on to the outdoor air intake","To the first VAV box or damper upstream of it","To the takeoff point on the nearest main supply trunk","To the boundary of the marked study area only"],"expl":"After noting elements like turning vanes, mixing boxes, dampers, humidifiers, and coils, the inspector works back to the AHU or fan serving the area and back to the outdoor air intake. Stopping at the first damper misses the source.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Marking plans","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B","units":["nim:e-plans-upstream"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0722","stem":"On the marked-up plans, where should the inspector flag areas where material deposition might be expected?","options":["Where the velocity of the air stream slows","Where the velocity of the air stream rises","Where ducts pass through fire-rated walls","Where supply and return ducts cross paths"],"expl":"Step 9 marks likely deposition areas, which are where air stream velocity slows and particles drop out. Faster air tends to keep particles moving.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Marking plans","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 B","units":["nim:e-plans-deposition"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0723","stem":"According to the manual, a client says to inspect only the third-floor AHU and its ducts. What should the inspector do about the other floors?","options":["Examine only the areas the client has specified","Add a 10% sample of the other floors' systems","Expand the scope, as similar systems may be dirty","Inspect the other floors and bill them separately"],"expl":"When the client gives guidance on which areas to inspect, the manual says to respect those instructions and examine only the specified areas. Expanding to similar systems applies within an agreed scope when problems are found.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Selecting systems","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 C","units":["nim:e-client-specified"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0724","stem":"Along with the Table 5-4 minimums, what does the manual say should be inspected in every situation?","options":["A portion of the ceiling or wall cavity plenums","The full return air system serving the building","The exhaust ducts serving any kitchen hoods","The humidifiers and their dispersion sections"],"expl":"Whatever the situation, the manual says to check some of the ceiling or wall cavity plenums for cleanliness. Branch sampling is 10%, returns depend on the supply findings, and similar systems are sampled at 10% on routine jobs.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sample size rules","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 C","units":["nim:e-plenum-always"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0725","stem":"In an office complaint area, the supply and ventilation inspection fully explains the problem and shows no sign that the return is contaminated. What does Table 5-4 say about the return ducts?","options":["Inspecting them is not called for in this case","Inspect at least 10% of the return branches","Inspect the entire return system to be thorough","Inspect only the main return trunk at the AHU"],"expl":"For complaint areas, Table 5-4 says to inspect return ducts only if the supply and ventilation inspections fail to explain the problem or suggest the return may be contaminated. Neither condition applies here.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sample size rules","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","units":["nim:e-complaint-return"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0726","stem":"When inspecting an air supply system, which parts does Table 5-4 say to always inspect in full?","options":["The outdoor air intake and the entire AHU","The supply diffusers and the entire return","The main trunk and every one of its VAV branches","The cooling coil and the condensate pan only"],"expl":"Table 5-4 says to always inspect the outdoor air intake and the entire AHU. The main trunk is inspected too, but branches are sampled at a minimum of 10%, not all of them.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sample size rules","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","units":["nim:e-supply-always"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0727","stem":"A supply main leaving an AHU feeds 60 branch ducts. What minimum share of those branches does Table 5-4 recommend inspecting?","options":["10%, so at least 6 branches","5%, so at least 3 branches","20%, so at least 12 branches","25%, so at least 15 branches"],"expl":"Table 5-4 calls for the main trunk plus at least 10% of the branches leaving it, which is 6 of 60. The 20% option mirrors the manual's AHU example, which is not the branch rule.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sample size rules","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","units":["nim:e-branch-10"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0728","stem":"Under Table 5-4, when is a branch duct considered to have been inspected?","options":["Both ends are problem-free and nothing in the run is a concern","A camera has traveled the full length of the run end to end","Its takeoff from the main trunk has been checked and found clean","At least half of the branch's length has been viewed directly"],"expl":"A branch counts as inspected when its beginning and end are examined and found problem-free and nothing within the run might cause concern. Full-length camera travel is not required.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sample size rules","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","units":["nim:e-branch-inspected-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0729","stem":"During a routine inspection with no local problems indicated, what minimum share of similar systems does Table 5-4 state should be inspected?","options":["At least 10% of them","At least 5% of them","At least 25% of them","At least 50% of them"],"expl":"Table 5-4 states at least 10% of similar systems for routine inspections. Its own example (2 of 10 AHUs) works out to 20%, so read the example as a practical minimum count, not a different rule.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sample size rules","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","units":["nim:e-routine-10"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0730","stem":"An inspector checks 2 of 10 identical kitchen hood exhaust systems and finds heavy deposition in both. What does Table 5-4 say to do next?","options":["Inspect the remaining eight systems as well","Report the two, since the sample size is met","Inspect one more system to confirm a pattern","Sample the deposits in the two dirty systems"],"expl":"When problems turn up in a sample of similar systems, Table 5-4 says to inspect more or even all of them, and the hood example says the inspector should almost certainly examine the rest. The minimum sample is only a starting point.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sample size rules","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 5-4","units":["nim:e-problems-expand"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0731","stem":"Which of these locations does the manual flag as likely to collect significant dust and debris?","options":["Where metal duct changes over to duct board","The middle of a straight, level metal duct run","The outer jacket of an externally insulated duct","The top of a vertical shaft, above the airflow"],"expl":"Any change from one duct material or design to another, such as metal to duct board or metal to flex, is on the manual's list. Gravity areas are the bottom of a shaft, dips, and low points, not the top.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Where to inspect","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 D","units":["nim:e-debris-areas"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0732","stem":"Which supply duct section does the manual list among the wet sections an inspector should examine?","options":["The first several duct diameters after the cooling coil","The first several duct diameters after the heating coil","The last several feet of duct before each supply diffuser","The first several feet of duct after the outdoor intake"],"expl":"Wet sections include the condensate drain pan, humidification systems, and the first several duct diameters of supply duct after the cooling coil, where carried-over moisture lands. A heating coil does not wet the air stream.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Where to inspect","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 D","units":["nim:e-wet-sections"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0733","stem":"An office system has simple, fairly straight duct runs. About how many access points does the manual say may be enough to judge the ductwork's condition?","options":["Two to four access points","One access point per run","Five to eight access points","Ten to twelve access points"],"expl":"The manual says a simple system with relatively straight runs may need only two to four access points. Complex systems with longer runs may need significantly more.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Access points","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.3 D","units":["nim:e-access-2-4"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0734","stem":"According to the manual, when an inspector does work that might release contamination, how must it be contained?","options":["With physical barriers and negative air pressure","With physical barriers and positive air pressure","By running the system at full supply airflow","By wetting surfaces and opening outside windows"],"expl":"The manual says contamination must be contained using physical barriers and negative air pressure, with released material removed by equipment such as a HEPA vacuum. Positive pressure would push contaminants out into the building.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Engineering controls","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 A","units":["nim:e-contain-barriers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0735","stem":"What does the manual say determines the extent of work site containment used during an inspection?","options":["The building's use and the walk-through contamination evaluation","The number of access openings the inspector will need to cut","The size of the inspection crew and the amount of equipment","The client's budget and the total length of the inspection schedule"],"expl":"Containment extent depends on the building use and the initial HVAC contamination evaluation made during the walk-through. A hospital with visible heavy deposits needs more than a clean office.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Engineering controls","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 C","units":["nim:e-containment-extent"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0736","stem":"An inspector's scope crawler was just used in ducts with severe microbial growth. What does the manual call for in situations like this?","options":["An on-site hygiene and integrity inspection of the equipment","A new crawler, since contaminated tools can never be reused","A lab swab of the crawler before it leaves the building","A written release from the client covering the equipment"],"expl":"Where severe microbial growth or known hazardous substances are present, the manual calls for an on-site hygiene/integrity inspection of the equipment. Equipment is serviced as needed, not discarded.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Engineering controls","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 D","units":["nim:e-equipment-hygiene"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0737","stem":"At a minimum, what does the manual say should be done with the HVAC system while it is being inspected?","options":["Shut it off, or block off the section at the supply side and registers","Run it at low fan speed so that any loose dust stays in the air stream","Keep it running normally so the dust patterns stay undisturbed","Shut off only the return fan and leave the supply fan running"],"expl":"The minimum is to shut the system off, or block off the portion under inspection on the air supply side and at the supply registers. Where deposits are heavy, a negative pressure differential may also be needed.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Engineering controls","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 F","units":["nim:e-duct-shutoff"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0738","stem":"A helper wants to use the intake of the negative air machine to pick up debris knocked loose from an access opening. What does the manual say?","options":["It collects dust but is not meant to serve as a vacuum","It is suitable, since its HEPA filter traps the debris","It is suitable if its carbon filter is replaced after","It works as a vacuum only when set to positive pressure"],"expl":"A negative air machine (fan with HEPA and often carbon filters) holds an area under negative pressure and will collect dust, but the manual says it is not intended to function as a vacuum cleaner. Released material goes to a HEPA vacuum.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Engineering controls","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §5.4 F, G","units":["nim:e-negair-not-vac"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0739","stem":"An inspector must check a 90 ft straight supply run and wants to cut as few openings as possible. Why does the manual favor a push camera here?","options":["Its long focal length reduces the entry points needed","Its short focal length is well suited to confined spaces","It can drive from the main trunk into the branches","It needs only a small 1 inch hole to be inserted"],"expl":"The manual's example is a push camera with a long focal length on long straight runs, which minimizes entry points. Short focal length and a 1 inch hole describe a borescope, and driving into branches describes a robot.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Visualization tools","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.1 A","units":["nim:e-pushcam-focal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0740","stem":"Suspected mold growth sits inside a wall cavity next to a return. Which visualization tool does the manual suggest for this space?","options":["A borescope or fiberscope","A push camera on a cable reel","A robotic camera car","A mirror and periscope"],"expl":"Confined spaces like wall cavities are best explored with a borescope or fiberscope, whose short focal length is enough there and which need only small access ports. Push cameras and robots suit long duct runs.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Visualization tools","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.1 A, Table 6-2","units":["nim:e-borescope-cavity"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0741","stem":"According to Table 6-2, what size access hole does a push camera need?","options":["About a 4 inch hole","About a 1 inch hole","About a 6 x 6 inch opening","A large walk-in access door"],"expl":"Table 6-2 lists a 1 inch hole for a borescope, a 4 inch hole for a push camera, and about 6 x 6 inches or larger for a robotic camera. Large openings are for direct visual inspection.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Visualization tools","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 6-2","units":["nim:e-hole-sizes"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0742","stem":"An inspector plans to drive a robotic camera down a long horizontal main, through an elbow fitted with turning vanes, and on to a branch. Which Table 6-2 limitation applies?","options":["It cannot get past turning vanes or similar restrictions","It cannot travel from a main duct into a branch duct","Its range is limited to about 25 feet of horizontal duct","It needs a separate 1 inch hole for every few feet of run"],"expl":"Robots can cover horizontal runs of 100 feet or more and can travel from main to branch ducts, but they cannot go past turning vanes and other restrictions. They also need setup time and can get stuck far from the user.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Visualization tools","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 6-2","units":["nim:e-robot-limits"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0743","stem":"Which is a disadvantage of a borescope listed in Table 6-2?","options":["Short focal length and a short range of view","It requires a 4 inch hole to be cut for access","It works only where large access doors exist","It needs setup time before it can be operated"],"expl":"Table 6-2 lists short focal length, dust-stirring cable, and short range for the borescope. A 4 inch hole is the push camera, large doors are direct visual inspection, and setup time is the robot.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Visualization tools","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 6-2","units":["nim:e-borescope-cons"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0744","stem":"An inspection is tied to possible litigation between an owner and a contractor. What does the manual say the inspector should do about photographs?","options":["Agree with the client on photo deliverables and format","Use digital photos only, since courts always accept them","Skip photos and rely on written field notes alone","Use film only, since the manual bars digital images"],"expl":"Digital photos may not be admissible in some court proceedings, so when litigation is possible the inspector should discuss photography with the client and settle what photo deliverables are needed and in what format.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Photo documentation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.1 E","units":["nim:e-photo-litigation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0745","stem":"Before cutting a new service opening, the inspector reads NADCA Standard 05. What else does the manual say he must check?","options":["Whether state or local law requires a license for it","Whether the client's insurer approves the new opening","Whether the duct maker's warranty allows the opening","Whether OSHA has issued a permit for the cutting work"],"expl":"Creating a service opening may require a special license in some areas, and the manual calls compliance with state and local contractor licensing absolutely essential. The licensing board, not OSHA or an insurer, answers that.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Access openings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.1 C","units":["nim:e-license-openings"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0746","stem":"An inspector arrives to inspect a rooftop-unit system serving a complaint area. Where does the manual say the inspection should start?","options":["At the outdoor air intake","At the complaint-area diffusers","At the filters inside the unit","At the return grille nearest the unit"],"expl":"Inspections start at the outdoor air intake and move progressively through filters, AHU, coils, supply ducts, return ducts, and exhaust. Starting at the diffusers reverses the airflow path.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Order of inspection","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2","units":["nim:e-start-oa-intake"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0747","stem":"In a new AHU with 95% final filters, the inspector finds no dust streaks at the filter rack but clear streaks fanning out from gaps around sensor and damper-linkage penetrations. What do these patterns point to?","options":["AHU casing defects rather than filter blow-by","Filter blow-by around the edges of the AHU rack","Dust carried back upstream from the supply ducts","A normal light dust film for a unit this new"],"expl":"In the manual's case study, dust fanning out from crevices showed the source: gaps at AHU wall penetrations and filter housings, with no signs of blow-by at the rack. Patterns that fan out from a gap point to that gap.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Case studies","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 Case Study #1","units":["nim:e-dust-patterns"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0748","stem":"The inspector finds a slight, even layer of dust in an office supply duct. The client asks whether this means the system needs cleaning. What should the inspector explain?","options":["Not necessarily, since some deposits are normal","Yes, since any deposit means cleaning is needed","Yes, if the dust is found on any supply surface","Only a lab sample can show whether it is normal"],"expl":"The manual says some dust is normal in most commercial environments and the client should know that slight debris does not necessarily indicate a need for cleaning. Sampling is not needed to make that judgment.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"What to look for","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 B","units":["nim:e-slight-dust-normal"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0749","stem":"An industrial hygienist swabbed one supply diffuser and found several common mold species, none linked to long-term moisture. The engineer asks if the HVAC system needs cleaning. What did the inspector suggest in the manual's case study?","options":["A visual inspection to see if the system is a source","Cleaning the system and adding an antimicrobial","More swab samples taken at every supply diffuser","Replacing the diffuser and its flex duct branch"],"expl":"Mold spores are found on nearly all indoor surfaces, so one diffuser swab says little about the system. The inspector proposed a visual inspection to rule the HVAC system in or out as a source; cleaning would not be 'necessary' without significant visible mold.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Case studies","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 Case Study #2","units":["nim:e-case2-visual"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0750","stem":"On a dual-duct system, which deck does the manual say deserves the more intense inspection?","options":["The cold deck, which is likelier to be contaminated","The hot deck, which is likelier to be contaminated","Neither deck, as the mixing boxes matter more","The hot deck, since heat bakes the deposits onto surfaces"],"expl":"Both decks must be inspected, but contamination is more likely in the cold deck, so the manual calls for a more intense inspection there. Mixing boxes get more focus in medium and high velocity systems but do not replace the decks.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"What to look for","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 B","units":["nim:e-cold-deck"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0751","stem":"How does the manual say an inspector should treat its sample HVAC Inspection Checklist?","options":["As a guideline, adding or removing fields as needed","As a required form to be completed without changes","As a full substitute for field notes and photographs","As the introduction section of the inspection report"],"expl":"The checklist is a guideline: add needed fields, remove unneeded ones, and arrange sections in the order they will be done. Larger issues still need detailed field notes, and the scope, not the checklist, becomes the report introduction.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Documentation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","units":["nim:e-checklist-guideline"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0752","stem":"Which item does the manual list as belonging in an inspector's field notes?","options":["Whether a sample or measurement was taken there","The fee charged for any added inspection work","The disclaimer terms that apply to the finding","The name of the person receiving the report"],"expl":"Field notes cover where the situation was seen, a description, whether a sample or measurement was taken, and any potential causes observed. Fees, disclaimers, and report recipients are set before the inspection.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Documentation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","units":["nim:e-field-notes-content"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0753","stem":"During a visual inspection, the inspector sees suspect growth on a coil, but sampling was never agreed with the client. What does the manual say about taking a sample?","options":["Take samples only when the client has directed it","Take a sample, since visible growth justifies it","Take a sample only if an insurer has requested it","Take a sample and let the lab decide if it is needed"],"expl":"In general an inspector should take samples only when directed by the client, and the need for sampling is agreed at the first meeting. The finding can still be documented and discussed with the client.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","units":["nim:e-sample-client-directed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0754","stem":"Sampling is not required by any government agency. According to the manual, who may still ask for samples when suspicious material is observed?","options":["Insurance companies","OSHA compliance officers","The EPA regional office","The local fire marshal"],"expl":"The manual notes that no government agency requires sampling, but insurance companies may ask for samples if suspicious material is observed.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","units":["nim:e-sample-insurers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0755","stem":"Along with a digital photo of each sample location, which detail does the manual say to record for every sample?","options":["The temperature and relative humidity at the site","The laboratory's price quote for the analysis","The name of the building's report recipient","The static pressure reading in the duct at that point"],"expl":"Each sample record should include the exact location, time and date, who collected it, temperature and relative humidity at the site, and moisture meter readings if applicable. Static pressure is not on that list.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","units":["nim:e-sample-record"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0756","stem":"What does the manual call the most difficult part of the sampling process?","options":["Interpreting the sample results","Choosing the sample locations","Completing chain of custody forms","Collecting enough sample material"],"expl":"Interpretation is the hardest part because no established guidelines exist for interpreting microbiological results and mold and bacteria are normally present in the environment.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","units":["nim:e-sample-interpretation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0757","stem":"A client wants one tape-lift sample from a return plenum out of curiosity, and no dispute is expected. Does the inspector need chain of custody forms?","options":["Yes, they should always be used for any sampling","No, they are needed only if litigation arises","No, they apply only to Method 3 vacuum cassettes","No, they are needed only when an insurer asks"],"expl":"The manual says that whenever sampling is done, the inspector should create and use chain of custody forms to track the sample from collector to analyst. They show professionalism and are essential if the inspection ever reaches court.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Chain of custody","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §6.2 D","units":["nim:e-chain-custody-always"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0770","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, why must the inspection report be clear, accurate, and well organized?","options":["It is the only physical deliverable the client gets","It is filed with the code official for plan review","It serves as the cleaning contractor's bid document","It is submitted to NADCA to renew the CVI credential"],"expl":"The manual stresses report quality because the written report is the one physical product the client receives. Nothing in the manual says reports go to code officials, NADCA, or serve as a bid document.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report preparation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-deliverable"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0771","stem":"An inspector finishes a three-day inspection and sits down to write the report. What does the manual say to do before drafting anything?","options":["Gather all collected materials in one place and review them first","Write the recommendations first, then fit the findings in underneath","Send the raw field notes to the client for comment","Draft the Summary from memory while the site is still fresh in mind"],"expl":"Section 7.1A says to gather interview notes, walk-through notes, purpose and scope statements, field notes, forms, results, and photos into one location and review all of it first. Drafting from memory skips that review.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report preparation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-gather"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0772","stem":"An inspector's checklist has several sections marked N/A because the building has no humidifiers or heat pumps. How should the checklist be handled before it becomes part of the report?","options":["Remove the unused sections and type any notes written on it","Keep every section so the client sees what was not inspected","Leave the checklist out, since the body already covers it","Scan the handwritten pages as they are into the appendix"],"expl":"The manual says checklists are reworked: sections not used or marked N/A are removed and handwritten notes are typed. The cleaned checklist then goes in the appendix, so leaving it out is also wrong.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report preparation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-checklist-prep"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0773","stem":"What does NADCA require regarding the format of the inspection report?","options":["A formal written report, with no single required format","The NADCA report form, filled out the same way each time","A verbal debrief, with a written report left optional","The ACR appendix template, used for every inspection"],"expl":"NADCA does not define a particular report format, but it does require some type of formal written report after each inspection. A verbal debrief alone does not meet that requirement.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report structure","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-format"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0774","stem":"Whatever section headings an inspector chooses, which three main portions does every inspection report include?","options":["Summary of findings, body of report, and recommendations","Introduction, materials and methods, and references","Scope and objectives, test results, and appendices","Cover letter, discussion of results, and final conclusions"],"expl":"The manual lists many optional headings (introduction, methods, results, discussion, conclusions, references, appendices) but says every report has three main portions: summary of findings, body, and recommendations.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report structure","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-three-parts"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0775","stem":"Which document written earlier in the inspection process can the inspector adapt, with very few changes, to begin the report's Summary section?","options":["The project scope statement","The client interview form","The building walk-through notes","The completed field checklist"],"expl":"The scope statement already sums up the purpose, the client's goals, the building's history, and what was looked at, so it can open the Summary with few changes. Interview and walk-through notes feed the scope but are not reused directly.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report summary","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-scope-summary"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0776","stem":"According to the NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual, how long should the report's Summary section be?","options":["One to four paragraphs","Five to eight paragraphs","One paragraph per finding","Two to three pages"],"expl":"The manual says the Summary should be relatively short, one to four paragraphs. A paragraph per finding would grow with the job and turn the Summary into the body.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report summary","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-summary-length"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0777","stem":"Which sentence is written the way the manual wants main findings stated in the Summary?","options":["Light gray dust covered the supply trunk floor at access point 3","The supply trunk at access point 3 was dirty","The supply trunk at access point 3 had an unacceptable dust level","The supply trunk at access point 3 will need cleaning in the future"],"expl":"Summary findings are stated as observed facts, not subjective assessments. Calling the duct dirty or unacceptable is a judgment, and a cleaning statement is a recommendation, which belongs elsewhere.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report summary","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-summary-facts"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0778","stem":"How should the Summary section handle the report's recommendations?","options":["Give a broad overview of the kinds of recommendations made","List every recommendation with its cost and priority ranking","Leave them out, since they have their own portion","Spell out in detail only the single most urgent recommendation"],"expl":"The Summary gives an overview of the type of recommendations in the report without detailing each one. Leaving them out entirely is wrong because the manual says the Summary previews them.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report summary","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-summary-recs"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0779","stem":"A facility director tells the inspector he usually reads only the summary of any report. What does the manual say the Summary itself should do about this?","options":["Tell the reader to read the full report for all findings","Repeat every finding so the rest is optional","Put the full list of recommendations first for quick action","Attach the lab data so the appendix can be skipped entirely"],"expl":"The manual says the Summary should clearly direct the client to read the whole report to see the full range of findings and recommendations. It should stay short, so repeating every finding defeats its purpose.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report summary","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-summary-readall"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0780","stem":"What belongs in the body of the inspection report?","options":["Factual observations stated without assessments or recommendations","Each observation paired with the recommendation that addresses it","Each observation paired with the inspector's severity rating","Each observation followed by the inspector's opinion of its cause"],"expl":"The body is an organized, factual presentation of the field notes and should not include assessments or recommendations. Recommendations go in their own separate portion of the report.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report body","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-body-factual"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0781","stem":"In what order does the manual say findings should be organized in the body of the report?","options":["In order of priority or severity","In the order they were inspected","Following the airflow path to the diffusers","Grouped alphabetically by component name"],"expl":"The manual says findings in the body are organized by priority/severity. Airflow order is how an inspection is often walked, not how the report ranks findings.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report body","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-body-order"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0782","stem":"Where does the manual say laboratory reports, collected data, and the used checklists should go?","options":["In the appendix of the report","In the Summary of findings","Beside each finding in the body","In a separate cover letter"],"expl":"Lab reports, data, and checklists (with non-applicable sections discarded) go in the appendix. The body presents the findings themselves, not the raw attachments.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Report body","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-appendix"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0783","stem":"An inspector has taken no samples and run no tests yet. Under the ACR guidance quoted in the manual, which finding makes cleaning necessary by itself?","options":["Significant debris buildup seen inside a supply trunk","A light gray dust film on the bottom of a supply duct","One occupant's complaint of dust near a supply diffuser","A filter gauge reading slightly above its clean value"],"expl":"In the ACR guidance the manual quotes, a significant accumulation of debris seen during the visual inspection is enough on its own to make cleaning necessary. A light gray film on the duct bottom is described as common and of little concern, and a single complaint or a slightly loaded filter is not a cleaning trigger. ACR 2025 adds that a Surface Comparison Test or Vacuum Test may be used when the need to clean is in question.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Cleaning triggers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-trig-debris"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0784","stem":"Under the cleaning guidance the manual draws from ACR, when does microbial growth make cleaning required?","options":["When it is seen visually or confirmed by analytical methods","Only when a laboratory confirms it, since sight is not enough","Only when it is found along with occupant health complaints","When it covers more of the duct than the surrounding debris"],"expl":"In the ACR guidance quoted in the manual, microbial growth that is either seen or confirmed by analytical methods makes cleaning required. Lab confirmation is not required first, because visual evidence alone is enough. Note: ACR 2025 now bases this on microbial Condition 2 or 3, so this item reflects the manual's quoted version.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Cleaning triggers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-trig-microbial"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0785","stem":"Office workers see particles puff out of supply diffusers whenever the fan starts. The ducts have not been opened yet. How does the ACR guidance in the manual treat this condition?","options":["Cleaning is necessary, since the system discharges visible particulate","Cleaning is premature until a vacuum test shows the ducts are dirty","Cleaning the diffusers alone is enough, since the ducts look sealed","Cleaning should wait until an upgraded filter has been tried first"],"expl":"A system that discharges visible particulate into the occupied space meets one of the stated cleaning triggers. The trigger does not depend on first testing the duct surfaces.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Cleaning triggers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-trig-discharge"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0786","stem":"Which finding meets the performance-based cleaning trigger in the ACR guidance quoted by the manual?","options":["Deposits on a coil that restrict airflow below design intent","A light gray dust film that dulls light on the duct floor","Dry mineral scale stuck firmly to sheet metal duct walls","Minor surface rust in isolated spots inside an older air handler"],"expl":"Restrictions or deposits on coils, airflow control devices, filtration devices, or air handling equipment that hurt performance or design intent make cleaning necessary. The manual's appendix calls a light gray film and adhered water scale generally not a problem, and the case study calls minor rust not an air quality issue.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Cleaning triggers","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.1","units":["nim:f-trig-performance"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0787","stem":"The same thin film of dust is found in the supply ducts of a hospital and of a metal fabrication plant. How does the manual say the recommendations should compare?","options":["They may differ, because building use affects what counts as dirty","They must match, because ACR sets one cleanliness level for all","They must match, because the dust film has the same thickness","They may differ, but only if a lab test is run in each building"],"expl":"The manual tells inspectors to consider building use: a thin dust coat may be unacceptable in a hospital yet within bounds at a manufacturing facility. Recommendations are made case by case, not by one fixed rule.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Writing recommendations","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.2","units":["nim:f-building-use"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0788","stem":"According to the manual's drafting tips, how should recommendations relate to the findings described in the report?","options":["One recommendation for each finding that is described","Recommendations only for findings that need duct cleaning","One overall recommendation covering the whole system","Recommendations for only the three most severe findings"],"expl":"The first drafting tip in §7.2 is to present a recommendation for each finding described. Limiting them to cleaning items or the worst findings leaves findings without guidance.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Writing recommendations","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.2","units":["nim:f-one-per-finding"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0789","stem":"A draft recommendation reads: \"This is only a minor issue, but the coil should be cleaned.\" What change fits the manual's guidance?","options":["Drop the severity opinion and state what should be done and how","Replace it with a hazard rating so the client can set priorities","Move the sentence into the Summary where opinions are allowed","Cite ACR after the opinion so the severity call carries weight"],"expl":"The manual says to avoid opinions about severity, since labels like \"hazard\" or \"only a minor issue\" can lead the client to overreact or underreact. The recommendation should give only what should be done and how.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Writing recommendations","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.2","units":["nim:f-no-severity"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0790","stem":"During a cleanliness inspection, the inspector finds the building's airflow is badly out of balance. How should the recommendation be written?","options":["Advise contacting an air balancing specialist, without prescribing the fix","Specify the new damper settings needed to bring each zone back into balance","Leave it out of the report, since balancing is outside the scope","Recommend duct cleaning, since clean ducts will bring airflow back to design"],"expl":"When a condition falls in another HVAC specialty, the manual says not to describe the specific action but to tell the client to contact the right specialist. Omitting the finding would break the one-recommendation-per-finding rule.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Writing recommendations","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.2","units":["nim:f-specialist"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0791","stem":"According to the manual, how is the inspection report best presented to the client?","options":["In person, with all appropriate people present","By email, so a written record of delivery exists","By mail, followed by a phone call a week later","Through the cleaning contractor bidding the work"],"expl":"Mailing or emailing is allowed, but the manual says presentation is best done in person with all appropriate persons present, which helps ensure the client understands what the report says.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Presenting the report","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.3","units":["nim:f-in-person"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0792","stem":"A two-week inspection was prompted by a suspected hazardous condition. On day two the inspector photographs suspected growth in an AHU. What does the manual support doing?","options":["Sending interim text and photos to the client marked as preliminary","Holding all findings until the final report is complete and reviewed","Sending the photos as final findings so the client acts on them fast","Reporting the photos to the local health department before the client"],"expl":"Interim or draft reporting is helpful when an inspection runs days or weeks and a suspected hazard is driving it; text, pictures, or video can be emailed soon after collection. Anything sent early must be labeled so it is not taken as final.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Presenting the report","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.3","units":["nim:f-interim"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0793","stem":"Why does the manual say interim information sent to a client should be clearly marked \"Draft\" or \"Preliminary Findings\"?","options":["So it is not mistaken for the finished report","So NADCA can audit the draft during renewal","So the client is barred from sharing it onward","So it can be billed apart from the final report"],"expl":"Labels such as \"Draft\" or \"Preliminary Findings\" ensure interim information is not misconstrued as a finished product. The manual ties the label to that purpose, not to billing or audits.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Presenting the report","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.3","units":["nim:f-draft-label"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0794","stem":"A contractor has hired the inspector to find out whether it is responsible for suspected contamination in a building. Why does the manual stress that the client fully understand the report, including its limitations?","options":["A fully informed client is best able to handle disputes that follow","Stating the limitations shifts all legal liability over to the client","A client who knows the limits will not need any follow-up consultation","The limitations section must be approved by NADCA before the report goes out"],"expl":"The manual warns that conflict, finger-pointing, and even legal action can follow a report, and a fully informed client who understands its message and limitations is best able to handle those issues.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Presenting the report","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.3","units":["nim:f-limitations"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0795","stem":"A client had its system cleaned after the inspection and wants to confirm the cleaning worked. Which method is NOT one of the ACR verification methods the manual says to explain?","options":["Airborne particle counts taken in the occupied space","A visual inspection of the cleaned system surfaces","Surface comparison testing on the cleaned surfaces","The NADCA Vacuum Test on the cleaned duct surfaces"],"expl":"The manual names three ACR cleanliness verification methods: visual inspection, surface comparison testing, and the NADCA Vacuum Test. Room particle counts are not one of them.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Following up","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.4","units":["nim:f-verification"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0796","stem":"As follow-up, the manual gives examples of referring clients to other professionals. A facility found to have improperly balanced systems should be referred to whom?","options":["An air balancing professional","A mold remediation specialist","An industrial hygienist","A duct cleaning contractor"],"expl":"The manual's follow-up examples pair suspected microbial growth with a growth removal specialist and improper balance with an air balancer. The hygienist and remediator fit the microbial example, not this one.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Following up","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.4","units":["nim:f-referral"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0797","stem":"According to the manual's summary of ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180, what happens to HVAC systems that are not maintained?","options":["They stop performing at the level they were designed for","They lose the UL listing of their duct and liner materials","They must be recommissioned before being returned to use","They have their manufacturer warranties voided at once"],"expl":"Standard 180 states that systems that are not maintained do not continue to provide the level of work they were designed for, which is why the inspector should encourage routine inspection.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Routine inspection","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual §7.5","units":["nim:f-std180"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0798","stem":"Which observation does Table 7-1 list as a possible sign of microbial growth in coils, drain pans, or internal insulation?","options":["A downy or furry surface on the material","A light gray film that dulls reflected light","Dry mineral scale on the duct walls","A yellowish-white dust with a paper content"],"expl":"Table 7-1 describes apparent growth as unusual-looking dust with contrasting colors, a downy or furry surface, or a musty odor. A gray film is normal dust, and adhered scale and paper dust appear elsewhere in the manual as non-microbial.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-microbial-signs"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0799","stem":"Table 7-1 says HVAC systems with microbial growth should be decontaminated in accordance with which documents?","options":["New York City DOH fungi guidelines and ACR","ASHRAE Standard 62.1 and ASHRAE Standard 55","NFPA 90A and the SMACNA duct standards","ASHRAE Standard 52.2 and the NAFA Guide"],"expl":"Table 7-1 points to the NYC Department of Health Guidelines on Assessment and Remediation of Fungi in Indoor Environments and ACR, the NADCA Standard. The ASHRAE ventilation and comfort standards do not govern decontamination.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Cited documents","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-nyc-doh"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0800","stem":"For debris in fan chambers, mixing plenums, and condensate drain pans, how often does Table 7-1 say EPA suggests inspection, and how often should thorough cleaning be done at minimum?","options":["Inspect monthly; clean thoroughly at least annually","Inspect weekly; clean thoroughly at least quarterly","Inspect quarterly; clean thoroughly at least every 2 years","Inspect annually; clean thoroughly at least every 5 years"],"expl":"EPA suggests monthly inspection of these areas, and while cleaning frequency should follow the rate of accumulation, thorough cleaning should occur at least annually.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Maintenance intervals","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-debris-interval"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0801","stem":"An inspector finds heavy debris loading in an AHU mixing plenum. Besides recommending removal, what does Table 7-1 tell the inspector to try to determine?","options":["Whether poor maintenance or another cause such as filter bypass is behind it","Whether the debris has enough mold to require lab analysis before removal","Whether the outdoor air damper is set at its code-required minimum open position","Whether the plenum was built from materials rated for moist, dirty air"],"expl":"If significant loading is occurring, Table 7-1 says to try to find out whether poor maintenance/cleaning or another condition, such as filter bypass, is the cause. The other options are not what the table asks.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-debris-cause"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0802","stem":"Condensate mist is carried off a cooling coil. Where does Table 7-1 say this moisture often causes microbial amplification?","options":["In the first several feet of internally insulated supply duct","At the outdoor air intake louver and bird screen upstream","In the return duct section nearest to the air handling unit","At the last several supply diffusers at the ends of the runs"],"expl":"Moisture carryover is often the cause of microbial amplification in the first several feet of internally insulated supply ductwork, just downstream of the coil. The return and intake are upstream of the coil.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-carryover-where"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0803","stem":"Moisture is seen blowing off a cooling coil into the duct. Besides measuring air velocity against the manufacturer's value, what does Table 7-1 say the inspector should try to find out?","options":["Whether the system was modified to increase its airflow","Whether the drain pan was sloped when the unit was built","Whether the coil fins were combed during the last service","Whether the outdoor air damper was closed down last season"],"expl":"Table 7-1 says to measure system velocity, compare it with the manufacturer's recommended velocity, and determine whether modifications were made to increase airflow, since more airflow can carry moisture into the supply duct or filters.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-carryover-velocity"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0804","stem":"Relative humidity in a building stays high while a permanent control fix is being designed. What interim measure does Table 7-1 mention?","options":["Console dehumidifiers until permanent controls are in place","Shutting the AHU off entirely until the indoor humidity comes down","Opening outdoor air dampers fully to dilute the moisture","Adding a steam humidifier bypass at the AHU discharge"],"expl":"Table 7-1 notes that data-logging humidistats can help indicate or control high RH and that console dehumidifiers can control it until more permanent controls are installed. If all efforts fail, a qualified HVAC humidity professional should evaluate the system.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-rh-control"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0805","stem":"A condensate pan leak has wetted drywall and ceiling tiles. Which document does Table 7-1 name for drying the wetted material?","options":["EPA Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings","New York City DOH Guidelines on Assessment of Fungi","ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 62.1 Ventilation for Acceptable IAQ","ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180 Inspection and Maintenance"],"expl":"For leaking condensate pans, Table 7-1 says leaks must be repaired immediately and wetted material dried per documents such as EPA's Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings. The NYC guidelines are cited for decontaminating microbial growth, not for drying.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Cited documents","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-pan-leak"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0806","stem":"An AHU's condensate drain sits upstream of the supply fan and has a poorly designed trap. According to Table 7-1, what pressure is the drain under and what is the likely problem?","options":["Negative pressure, so the pan may not drain properly","Positive pressure, so the pan may not drain properly","Negative pressure, so conditioned air blows out the drain","Positive pressure, so sewer gas is drawn into the unit"],"expl":"Upstream of the supply fan the drain is under negative pressure, and a poor trap there can keep the pan from draining; a drain near a contaminant source can also pull it in. Positive pressure applies downstream of the fan.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-trap"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0807","stem":"A steam humidifier in an office AHU is fed with steam from the building's central heating boiler. Why does Table 7-1 flag this?","options":["Boiler water holds chemicals that irritate occupants","Boiler steam is too hot for the humidifier's control valve","Boiler steam carries too little moisture to raise the RH","Boiler steam will overload the AHU's condensate drain pan"],"expl":"Table 7-1 says to make sure humidifiers do not use steam or hot water from a plant boiler, because that water contains treatment chemicals that may irritate occupants.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-humidifier-boiler"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0808","stem":"Ceiling tiles near several VAV boxes are stained, and maintenance staff say they bleed the chilled water lines there periodically. What does Table 7-1 recommend for the wetted tiles?","options":["Dry or replace them quickly to prevent microbial growth","Paint them with a stain-blocking primer and leave them","Leave them in place until the next scheduled renovation","Seal them with an antimicrobial and recheck in a year"],"expl":"Table 7-1 says tiles wetted when pipes are bled should be quickly dried or replaced, because chronic wetting may lead to microbiological contamination. Leaks at VAVs often come from poorly insulated chilled lines and bad valves.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-vav-leaks"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0809","stem":"An inspector finds a refrigerant leak at a split system's evaporator coil. What does Table 7-1 say should happen?","options":["The leak should be identified and repaired immediately","The leak should be logged for the next maintenance visit","The leak should wait until air sampling shows exposure","The leak should be referred to the duct cleaning crew"],"expl":"Leaking refrigerants may contain irritants, asphyxiants, and toxins, so Table 7-1 says leaks must be identified and repaired immediately rather than deferred.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-refrigerant"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0810","stem":"A plumbing vent stack sits 15 feet from a rooftop unit's outdoor air intake. How does this compare with the EPA guidance cited in Table 7-1?","options":["Too close, because EPA calls for at least 25 feet","Acceptable, because EPA calls for at least 10 feet","Too close, because EPA calls for at least 50 feet","Acceptable, because EPA calls for at least 12 feet"],"expl":"According to the EPA as cited in Table 7-1, make-up air intakes should be at least 25 feet from fugitive emissions such as vents and local exhaust. At 15 feet the vent is too close.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Intake separation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-intake-25"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0811","stem":"A client asks how to clean dusty supply diffusers. Which cleaning method does Table 7-1 give?","options":["A HEPA vacuum or a mild detergent","Compressed air blown into the duct","A solvent degreaser and a dry wipe","Pressure washing them on the ceiling"],"expl":"For dusty diffusers, Table 7-1 names two cleaning methods: HEPA vacuuming or washing with a mild detergent. It links diffuser dust to the Coanda effect and notes that dirty diffusers can shape what occupants think of the building's air quality.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-diffusers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0812","stem":"Which duct condition does Table 7-1 describe as not unusual?","options":["A slight coating of gray dust or an adhering oily film","Large deposits of dust at the supply branch takeoffs","Scattered mold spots on the internally insulated duct","Loose debris piled along the bottom of the main trunk"],"expl":"Table 7-1 says a slight gray dust coat or adhering oily film is not unusual, but large deposits of dust, dirt, or mold should not be present in air distribution systems.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-duct-dust-normal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0813","stem":"Large dust deposits are found in a building's ductwork. Along with finding and correcting the cause, what budget recommendation does Table 7-1 suggest?","options":["Earmark operating funds for periodic duct inspection and cleaning","Set aside capital funds to replace the ductwork within five years","Budget for a filter upgrade before any duct cleaning is considered","Reserve funds for monthly lab testing of the settled duct dust"],"expl":"Table 7-1 says the inspector should determine why the deposits built up, correct the failure, and recommend that the client earmark funds in the operating budget for periodic duct inspection and cleaning.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-earmark"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0814","stem":"A filter rack is not completely filled, and someone has closed the gap with a strip of plywood. What does Table 7-1 support recommending?","options":["Replace the wood with a sheet metal or other non-porous spacer","Keep the plywood in place if it is painted and sealed at the edges","Replace the plywood with a stiff cardboard spacer cut to fit","Fill the gap with fiberglass batt so air still passes through"],"expl":"For filter bypass, Table 7-1 calls for properly sized filters, seals, and spacers of sheet metal or another non-porous material, and says wood should not be used for any HVAC system component.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-spacer"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0815","stem":"An older AHU runs with low-efficiency filters, and the owner asks whether to upgrade. What does Table 7-1 suggest the inspector do?","options":["Refer the client to an air filtration professional to evaluate it","Specify the highest MERV rating that will physically fit the rack","Recommend changing the existing filters twice as often as before","Recommend adding a second filter bank downstream of the coil"],"expl":"Filtration efficiency should be matched to equipment capabilities and airflows, so Table 7-1 suggests referring the client to an air filtration professional. Simply maxing out MERV ignores the equipment match.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-poor-filtration"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0816","stem":"According to Table 7-1, what usually causes delamination or erosion of fiberboard duct or internal duct insulation over time?","options":["High velocity air passing over its surface","Aggressive brushing during an earlier cleaning","Off-gassing from the duct joint sealants","Low airflow letting dust settle and cake"],"expl":"Table 7-1 attributes delamination or erosion to high velocity air passing over the surface over time. Damaged material should be repaired or replaced, with precautions against exposure to repair chemicals.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-delamination"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0817","stem":"A VAV zone gets little or no airflow, and the box minimum appears to be set incorrectly. What does Table 7-1 say this likely indicates?","options":["Sufficient outdoor air is probably not being inducted","The zone thermostat is probably located in a warm spot","The supply fan belt is probably slipping on its sheave","The ceiling return plenum is probably short-circuiting"],"expl":"Table 7-1 says that if little or no air reaches a zone, there is a good chance sufficient outdoor air is not being inducted, especially in VAV systems with improperly set minimums.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-vav-oa"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0818","stem":"Table 7-1 cites which ASHRAE standard for minimum ventilation rates in breathing zones?","options":["ASHRAE Standard 62","ASHRAE Standard 55","ASHRAE Standard 180","ASHRAE Standard 52.2"],"expl":"For unbalanced systems with insufficient outdoor air, Table 7-1 cites ASHRAE Standard 62 (the 2013 edition) for minimum breathing zone ventilation rates. That standard is now published as ASHRAE 62.1. Standard 55 covers thermal comfort, and 52.2 covers filter testing.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Ventilation standards","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-ashrae62"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0819","stem":"According to Table 7-1, how should a commercial building's pressure be kept relative to outdoors, and why?","options":["Slightly positive, to keep fugitive emissions out","Slightly negative, to keep indoor odors contained","Neutral, to prevent drafts at the entry doors","Strongly positive, to purge contaminants quickly"],"expl":"Table 7-1 says buildings should be at a slight positive pressure, with outdoor air brought in at a designated location, so unfiltered and untempered air and fugitive emissions are not drawn in elsewhere.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Building pressure","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-pressure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0820","stem":"The mechanical room serves as the return plenum for its AHU, and cardboard boxes and cleaning chemicals are stored in it. What does Table 7-1 recommend?","options":["Stop using the room for storage and keep it clean","Keep the chemicals but move the cardboard out","Keep the storage but raise the filter efficiency","Keep the storage but cover it with plastic sheet"],"expl":"Many mechanical rooms are part of the HVAC system, especially with a return plenum there, so the room should be kept clean and not used for storage. Wet cardboard can become a microbial reservoir and chemicals can be drawn into the system.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Table 7-1 findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-mech-room"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0821","stem":"Office occupants complain of drafts and have taped cardboard over the diffusers. Above what air velocity on exposed skin does Table 7-1 say occupants may feel uncomfortable?","options":["50 feet per minute","25 feet per minute","100 feet per minute","150 feet per minute"],"expl":"Table 7-1 says occupants may be uncomfortable when air velocity on exposed skin exceeds 50 feet per minute (0.8 feet per second). Blocked diffusers call for checking design and balance.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Occupant comfort","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Table 7-1","units":["nim:f-velocity-50"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0830","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM guidance, what is the first step of the diagnostic process for an IAQ complaint in a commercial building?","options":["Decide whether the situation is an emergency","Interview occupants about symptoms and timing","Check the most likely causes one at a time","Measure CO2 in the complaint and other areas"],"expl":"I-BEAM's three steps are: decide if it is an emergency and act at once if so, then characterize the problem, then check specific causes until it is solved. Interviewing occupants is part of the second step, not the first.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Diagnostic process","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-diag-three-steps"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0831","stem":"Within an hour, several workers on one floor report sudden headaches, dizziness, and nausea, and one notices a combustion odor. How should the inspector treat this under I-BEAM?","options":["As an emergency, with possible carbon monoxide","As a lack of outdoor air, to trend with CO2 data","As a biological problem, to sample for mold","As a thermal comfort issue, to log with RH data"],"expl":"I-BEAM lists sudden headaches, dizziness, drowsiness or nausea with combustion odors as an emergency that could be carbon monoxide poisoning: notify authorities, evacuate if needed, and ventilate. Mild headaches with no sudden onset or combustion odor are what its symptom table ties to low outdoor air.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Emergencies","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-emergency-examples","ibeam:g-emergency-actions"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0832","stem":"Several occupants report cough, chest tightness, shortness of breath, and fever with chills. In I-BEAM's symptom table, which potential cause should be investigated first?","options":["Biological sources, such as microbes and molds","Lack of outdoor air reaching the occupied space","Temperature or relative humidity out of range","Chemical or particle sources, such as cleaners"],"expl":"For cough, congestion, chest tightness, shortness of breath, fever and chills, I-BEAM makes biological the primary cause and says to check the room, ductwork, cooling tower and air handler. Chemical or particle sources are only the secondary choice.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Symptom patterns","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-sym-respiratory-bio"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0833","stem":"Complaints describe mild eye and throat irritation, mild headaches, and general lethargy, with no specific illness. Per I-BEAM, which causes are the primary suspects?","options":["Low outdoor air or temperature and humidity issues","Biological growth in the ductwork or at the coil","Chemical or particle emissions from new materials","Carbon monoxide spilling from a combustion appliance flue"],"expl":"I-BEAM's table lists lack of outdoor air and temperature or humidity as the primary causes of mild eye, nose or throat discomfort, mild headache and lethargy. Chemical and biological causes are secondary. Sudden symptoms with combustion odor would point to CO instead.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Symptom patterns","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-sym-mild-oa"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0834","stem":"After insulated ductwork is disturbed, dozens of workers across a floor report itching and skin rash. Per I-BEAM's table, what is the primary suspected cause type?","options":["Chemical or particle, as large numbers are affected","Biological allergen, as skin reactions point to mold","Lack of outdoor air, as dilution has fallen off","Thermal comfort, as low humidity dries the skin"],"expl":"For swelling, itching and rash, I-BEAM makes chemical or particle the primary cause when large numbers are affected, and says to check for fiberglass from duct insulation. A biological allergen is primary only when small numbers are involved.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Symptom patterns","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-sym-skin-numbers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0835","stem":"Per I-BEAM's odor guidance, occupants describing a 'dirty socks' smell at the diffusers should first lead the inspector to check what?","options":["Coil and filter dirt, plus low outdoor air supply","Dry drain traps and nearby building sanitary vents","Combustion flues for spillage of flue gases indoors","Fuel tanks and piping for leaking petroleum products"],"expl":"I-BEAM links a dirty socks odor to dirty coils or filters and lack of outdoor air. Dry traps and sanitary vents go with sewer odor, and flue spillage and fuel tanks go with gas fumes and petroleum odors.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Odor clues","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-odor-dirty-socks"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0836","stem":"Similar complaints come from every floor of an office tower served by several air handlers. Per I-BEAM, what kind of cause does this pattern point toward?","options":["A building-wide source or a problem common to all AHUs","A VAV box malfunction in the zone that complained first","A contaminated local duct serving a single office area","An individual sensitivity in the people who complained"],"expl":"I-BEAM says widespread symptoms suggest a source spread through the building, a central HVAC problem, or something common to all air handlers such as overall poor maintenance. A VAV box or a local duct would produce local complaints.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Localized vs widespread","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-where-widespread"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0837","stem":"In a building with perimeter and core zones, stuffiness complaints come only from interior offices. Which explanation does I-BEAM offer for this pattern?","options":["Interior zones get less supply and outdoor air, as loads are low","Interior zones get more outdoor air, which dries the air too much","Perimeter zones are pressurized, pushing return air into the core","Perimeter thermostats control the core, so it overheats in winter"],"expl":"Because thermal loads are lower in the interior, the core gets less supply air and so less outdoor air. EPA's modeling in I-BEAM found the core got only about two thirds of the building's average outdoor airflow.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Localized vs widespread","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-interior-zone"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0838","stem":"Complaints are mild on Monday but build steadily toward Thursday and Friday. What does I-BEAM suggest this time pattern indicates?","options":["Occupant sources with too little outdoor air for dilution","Failure to flush the building on nights and over weekends","Morning traffic exhaust entering the outdoor air intake","A freezestat tripping the air handler off in cool weather"],"expl":"I-BEAM ties complaints in the latter part of the week to occupants or their activities as the source, with too little outdoor air to dilute them. Failure to flush and morning traffic exhaust fit the opposite pattern: worst Monday morning, easing as the week goes on.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Timing patterns","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-when-latter-week"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0839","stem":"In a variable air volume building, complaints appear mainly in mild spring and fall weather. Per I-BEAM, which cause fits this pattern?","options":["Part-load VAV operation delivering little outdoor air","Emissions from new furnishings driven by the summer heat","A freezestat control tripping the air handler off","Cooling capacity too small for the peak summer load"],"expl":"I-BEAM links mild-weather complaints to economizer control problems or a VAV system at part load delivering little supply and outdoor air. Furnishing emissions and short capacity are hot-weather causes, and a freezestat trip is a cool-weather cause.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"Timing patterns","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-when-mild-weather"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0840","stem":"A mid-afternoon CO2 reading in an occupied office is 1,250 ppm. Per I-BEAM, what should the inspector check before presuming outdoor air is too low?","options":["Improperly vented appliances and the outdoor CO2 level","The filter pressure drop and the supply fan belt tension","New furniture and cleaning products that emit CO2 indoors","Relative humidity and the thermostat's temperature set"],"expl":"Above 1,000 ppm, I-BEAM says to check for improperly vented combustion appliances (which could also make CO) and to measure outdoor CO2 and work with the indoor-outdoor difference. If neither explains it, low outdoor air is a valid presumption.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"CO2 interpretation","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-co2-above-1000"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0841","stem":"CO2 in the complaint area runs well above CO2 in non-complaint areas of the same building. What does I-BEAM say this comparison suggests?","options":["Outdoor air ventilation in the complaint area may be the cause","The complaint area may hold a combustion source needing a CO test","The difference proves a biological source in the complaint area","The building's outdoor CO2 has risen above the assumed 300"],"expl":"I-BEAM says higher CO2 in the complaint area than in non-complaint areas suggests its outdoor air ventilation rate may be causing the problem. CO2 comes mainly from occupants' breath, so it says nothing about a biological source.","domain":"IAQ","sub":"CO2 interpretation","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-co2-compare-areas"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0842","stem":"Per I-BEAM, how should an inspector release smoke from a smoke tube to learn the most about room air movement?","options":["In a series of small puffs rather than one large cloud","In one large cloud so the whole pattern shows at once","In a steady stream held for a full minute at one spot","At return grilles alone, where the airflow is strongest"],"expl":"I-BEAM says a series of small puffs or dribbles gives more information than a single large cloud, and smoke that disperses within several seconds in an occupied area suggests good circulation. A single cloud hides the direction and speed of the airstream.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Smoke tube tests","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-smoke-puffs"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0843","stem":"An inspector puffs smoke at the doorway of a copy room that has its own exhaust fan. What should the smoke do if the room is working as I-BEAM intends?","options":["Drift into the copy room, showing it is negative","Drift out to the corridor, showing it is positive","Hang still at the door, showing neutral pressure","Rise straight to the ceiling return in the corridor"],"expl":"An exhausted source room should be negative to the occupied spaces, so smoke at its entrance should be drawn in. Smoke moving outward is what I-BEAM expects at the entrance to a clean room, which should be positive.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Smoke tube tests","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-smoke-exhausted-room"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0844","stem":"Per I-BEAM, to confirm outdoor air requirements are met under all operating conditions, how should the system be set when outdoor airflow is measured?","options":["At minimum airflow, with the economizer off or set lowest","At maximum airflow, with the economizer at full outdoor air","At design airflow, with VAV boxes set at their peak positions","At any airflow, since the outdoor air fraction is fixed"],"expl":"I-BEAM says to measure at minimum airflow: turn the economizer off or set its shutoff to the lowest setting, and drive VAV controls to minimum flow. A reading taken at full economizer or peak VAV flow overstates what occupants get at part load.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Outdoor airflow","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-oa-min-flow"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0845","stem":"I-BEAM ranks three ways to find outdoor airflow. Which one does it call a last resort, used only when outdoor air cannot be measured directly?","options":["Calculating percent outdoor air from carbon dioxide","Measuring the intake directly with a flow hood","Traversing the duct with a pitot tube or anemometer","Estimating from the outdoor damper's percent open"],"expl":"I-BEAM ranks the flow hood as preferred, the velocity meter (pitot tube or anemometer) as less preferred, and the CO2 calculation as not preferred, a last resort. The pitot or anemometer traverse is the middle option, not the last.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Airflow methods","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-airflow-ranking"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0846","stem":"An inspector takes a velocity traverse across the open face of a 2 ft by 1.5 ft duct and averages 400 fpm. Using I-BEAM's formula, what is the airflow?","options":["1,200 cfm","800 cfm","600 cfm","2,400 cfm"],"expl":"Airflow equals free open area times average velocity: 2 ft x 1.5 ft = 3 sq ft, and 3 x 400 fpm = 1,200 cfm. The 800 and 600 cfm answers multiply velocity by only one side of the opening.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Airflow methods","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-velocity-formula"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0847","stem":"Using I-BEAM's CO2 method, an inspector reads 1,000 ppm in return air, 800 ppm in supply air, and 400 ppm outdoors. What percent outdoor air is the unit taking in?","options":["About 33%","About 50%","About 20%","About 67%"],"expl":"Percent outdoor air = (return minus supply) / (return minus outdoor) x 100 = 200 / 600 x 100, about 33%. Dividing 200 by the supply-minus-outdoor difference of 400 gives the wrong 50%.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Airflow methods","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-co2-percent-oa"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0848","stem":"When should an inspector take indoor CO2 readings in a typical office to compare against ventilation thresholds, per I-BEAM?","options":["At peak times, usually late morning and mid-afternoon","At start-up, before occupants arrive in the morning","At lunchtime, when most occupants have left the space","At closing, after the space has emptied for the day"],"expl":"I-BEAM says indoor CO2 should be measured at peak values, which in a typical office usually occur around 11 a.m. and 3 p.m. Readings with few occupants present tell little about ventilation per person.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"CO2 measurement","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-co2-peak-times"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0849","stem":"I-BEAM's occupant complaint record gathers the basic clues for an IAQ investigation. Which set of questions does it organize that information around?","options":["What the symptoms are, and where, when, and who","Why they began, how much, how long, how often","The source, pathway, driving force, and receptor","Their location, duration, severity, and cost"],"expl":"I-BEAM builds characterization on what the symptoms are, where they occur, when they occur, and who experiences them. Source, pathway, driving force and receptor is a framework from a different EPA guide, not the I-BEAM complaint record.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Complaint records","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-four-questions"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0850","stem":"During an IAQ walkthrough, an inspector finds AHU filters left in place for many months because pressure drop never reached the change point. What does I-BEAM advise?","options":["Change them anyway, as long-installed filters are prone to mold","Leave them in, since light loading shows they still work well","Vacuum them in place, then reinstall them for another season","Upgrade them to HEPA filters, since low loading shows poor capture"],"expl":"I-BEAM notes that filters not changed for a long time because they are not loaded may have developed mold, and says to change them anyway. Light loading does not show a filter is clean and dry.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Checking causes","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-filters-change-anyway"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0851","stem":"According to EPA's I-BEAM guidance, what negative pressure should a renovation containment zone in an occupied building hold relative to its surroundings?","options":["About 5 to 10 Pa (0.02 to 0.04 in. w.c.)","About 1 to 2 Pa (0.004 to 0.008 in. w.c.)","About 25 to 50 Pa (0.10 to 0.20 in. w.c.)","About 50 to 75 Pa (0.20 to 0.30 in. w.c.)"],"expl":"I-BEAM gives 5 to 10 Pa (0.02 to 0.04 in. w.c.) as an example of significant negative pressure for a containment zone, set up with existing and temporary exhaust fans. ACR 2025 sets no numeric value, so attribute this figure to I-BEAM.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Construction containment","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","units":["ibeam:g-contain-pressure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0852","stem":"A tenant renovation will run while the rest of the floor stays occupied. Which step does I-BEAM list for keeping construction dust out of the HVAC system?","options":["Seal all return ducts serving the construction zone","Raise supply airflow to the zone to dilute the dust","Open the zone's return grilles to capture the dust","Switch the zone's supply ducts to full outdoor air"],"expl":"I-BEAM's isolation steps include a full physical enclosure and sealing all return ducts so contaminants cannot enter the HVAC system. It also says supply air to the construction area may need to be shut down, not raised.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Construction containment","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","units":["ibeam:g-seal-returns"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0853","stem":"A project will apply paint and adhesives, then install carpet and ceiling tile. Per I-BEAM, how should those fleecy materials be sequenced?","options":["Install them after the wet-product emissions dissipate","Install them first so they are protected from paint drips","Install them alongside the wet products to save schedule","Install them before painting so they absorb odors early"],"expl":"I-BEAM says to delay adsorbent (fleecy) materials such as carpet, furniture and ceiling tile until wet-product emissions have dissipated. Installed earlier, they adsorb the contaminants and release them later during occupancy.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Construction sequencing","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","units":["ibeam:g-fleecy-delay"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0854","stem":"A contractor's negative air machines discharge renovation exhaust just below the outdoor air intake of the building's air handler. Which I-BEAM principle does this violate?","options":["Exhausted contaminants must not re-enter through the intakes","The containment must stay positive to the occupied spaces","Occupied spaces must be kept negative relative to the outdoors","Supply air to the zone must run at full design airflow"],"expl":"I-BEAM says to make sure exhausted contaminants do not re-enter through open windows or the HVAC air intake, and to keep occupied spaces positive to the outside. The containment itself must be negative, not positive.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Protecting occupants","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","units":["ibeam:g-exhaust-reentry"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0855","stem":"Per I-BEAM, how should the closet where housekeeping chemicals are mixed and stored be ventilated?","options":["Exhausted outdoors under negative pressure, not to the return","Exhausted to the return plenum, which keeps the closet negative","Supplied with extra air so the closet stays positive to the hall","Sealed with no ventilation so vapors cannot reach the hallway"],"expl":"I-BEAM says chemical products belong in a room or closet exhausted to the outside and under negative pressure, with no opening to the return air plenum. Venting to the return plenum would spread the vapors through the building.","domain":"Risk Management","sub":"Chemical storage","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, IAQ Maintenance and Housekeeping Programs","units":["ibeam:g-chem-storage"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0856","stem":"An owner wants to judge an office IAQ complaint by comparing measured contaminants with OSHA PELs and ACGIH TLVs. What does I-BEAM say about that approach?","options":["Those industrial limits are not appropriate for commercial buildings","Those limits apply only after CO2 exceeds 1,000 ppm in the space","Those limits are the benchmark I-BEAM applies to office buildings too","Those limits apply to offices once readings are cut in half first"],"expl":"I-BEAM says occupational standards such as PELs and TLVs are not appropriate for commercial facilities, whose many sources run far below industrial levels. Exceeding them in an office would signal a very serious problem, so staying under them proves little.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Occupational limits","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Diagnosing and Solving Problems","units":["ibeam:g-occupational-limits"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0857","stem":"I-BEAM cites ASHRAE Standard 62-1999 on relative humidity to limit allergenic and pathogenic organisms. What range does that edition prefer in habitable spaces?","options":["30% to 60%","40% to 70%","20% to 50%","50% to 70%"],"expl":"I-BEAM's table of ASHRAE 62-1999 provisions says RH in habitable spaces should preferably be kept between 30% and 60%. I-BEAM separately suggests below 50% for dust mites; treat these as I-BEAM era figures, not current ASHRAE text.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Humidity guidance","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","units":["ibeam:g-rh-30-60"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0858","stem":"According to EPA's modeling summarized in I-BEAM, what happened when VAV systems kept the fixed outdoor air damper strategy of constant volume systems?","options":["Outdoor air fell to half to two thirds of design most of the year","Outdoor air stayed at design, but fan energy rose sharply in summer","Outdoor air stayed at design with no meaningful energy penalty","Outdoor air stayed at design, but core zones overheated in winter"],"expl":"A fixed outdoor air fraction on a VAV system cut outdoor air at the air handler to about one half to two thirds of design most of the year, and the core zone got only about a third. A VAV system controlled for constant outdoor airflow fixed this with no meaningful energy penalty.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Energy and ventilation","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Indoor Air Quality and Energy Efficiency","units":["ibeam:g-vav-fixed-oa"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0859","stem":"Per I-BEAM's housekeeping guidance, within what time should carpet or other porous material wet by a spill be thoroughly dried before it may have to be discarded?","options":["24 hours","36 hours","72 hours","12 hours"],"expl":"I-BEAM says to dry wet porous material immediately after a spill; if it is not dry within 24 hours it may have to be discarded, because wet materials breed mold. Other EPA mold guidance gives a 24 to 48 hour window, but I-BEAM's figure is 24 hours.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Housekeeping practice","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, IAQ Maintenance and Housekeeping Programs","units":["ibeam:g-dry-24h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0860","stem":"What does I-BEAM warn can happen when low-efficiency filters (10% to 20% dust-spot) are allowed to load to excess?","options":["They deform or blow out and then foul coils and ducts","They hold their shape but drop to zero efficiency","They shed fibers into the duct only at fan start-up","They start to capture fine particles like HEPA filter units"],"expl":"I-BEAM says overloaded low-efficiency filters deform and may blow out, leading to clogged coils, dirty ducts, poorer IAQ and more energy use. It notes moderate filters (40% to 65% dust-spot) have more body and resist blowout.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Filtration guidance","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Renovation and New Construction","units":["ibeam:g-filter-blowout"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0861","stem":"Per I-BEAM, a typical CO2-controlled ventilation system starts raising outdoor air at what CO2 level, aiming to keep peaks under 1,000 ppm?","options":["600 to 800 ppm","900 to 1,000 ppm","400 to 500 ppm","1,000 to 1,200 ppm"],"expl":"I-BEAM says a typical system increases outdoor air when CO2 rises to 600 to 800 ppm so maximum levels stay under 1,000 ppm. It should also include a minimum outdoor air setting to dilute building-related contaminants when few people are present.","domain":"Standards","sub":"CO2-controlled ventilation","tier":0,"ref":"EPA I-BEAM, Indoor Air Quality and Energy Efficiency","units":["ibeam:g-co2-dcv"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0870","stem":"A client wants a water-stained section of carpet and a cut piece of duct insulation analyzed for microbial growth. Which sampling method does the manual list as useful for materials like these?","options":["Bulk sampling, removing the material itself","Tape sampling, dabbing tape on the surface","Filtration sampling, vacuuming to a cassette","Wet swab sampling, wiping with a damp pad"],"expl":"Bulk sampling physically removes material and is named as useful for wallboard, insulation, carpet and larger deposits. Tape sampling is meant mainly for smooth surfaces, and swabs are always used dry.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling methods","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","units":["nim:h-bulk-use"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0871","stem":"An inspector scoops loose dust from a duct into a sealed container as a bulk sample. How much material does the manual say is more than sufficient for analysis?","options":["About one cubic inch of the dust","About one cubic foot of the dust","About one quart of the loose dust","Enough to load a 4 inch filter circle"],"expl":"A cubic inch of material is more than sufficient for a bulk sample. The 4 inch filter circle belongs to the swab method, not bulk sampling.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling methods","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","units":["nim:h-bulk-amount"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0872","stem":"A client wants to know whether culturable bacteria are present in settled duct dust. Why does the manual advise against filtration (cassette) sampling for this purpose?","options":["Collection puts desiccation stress on bacteria","The cassette filter is too coarse to trap bacteria","The pump airflow is too weak to lift settled dust","The plastic tubing leaches residue into the dust"],"expl":"Filtration sampling is not recommended for culturable bacteria because drawing air through the filter dries them out (desiccation stress). The filter captures the dust well; the problem is keeping bacteria alive to culture.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling methods","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","units":["nim:h-filt-bacteria"],"reserved":true},{"id":"cvi-0873","stem":"Using a pump, 6 inch tubing, and a 37 mm air monitoring cassette, an inspector vacuums thinly spread dust off a duct surface. How full should the cassette be, if at all possible?","options":["At least one quarter full","At least one half full","No more than one tenth full","Filled to about three quarters"],"expl":"The manual says the cassette, which acts as the dust collection container, should be at least a quarter full if at all possible. Half or three quarters are not stated targets.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling methods","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","units":["nim:h-filt-setup","nim:h-filt-fill"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0874","stem":"An inspector has dabbed a strip of 1 inch clear tape on a smooth duct surface until it is loaded with particles. What should be done with the tape next?","options":["Stick it on a glass slide or in a thick zip-lock bag","Fold it sticky side in on itself so no particles escape","Drop it in a vial of sterile water to keep it moist","Wrap it around a dry swab and seal it in a tube"],"expl":"The loaded tape goes onto a glass microscope slide or the inside of a thick-walled zip-lock bag. The manual specifically says the tape should not be folded in upon itself, and it is not wetted or wrapped on a swab.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling methods","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","units":["nim:h-tape"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0875","stem":"An inspector will wipe a 4 inch filter circle across suspect dust on a coil casing for a swab sample. How should the filter circle be used?","options":["Kept dry, loaded as heavily as possible, then folded","Wetted with sterile water, loaded lightly, then folded","Dry, touched lightly to the surface, then kept flat","Wetted with alcohol, loaded heavily, then kept flat"],"expl":"The circle is wiped dry (never wet), loaded as heavily as possible, then folded in halves or quarters and sealed in a container. A wetted pad is the common mistake.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling methods","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","units":["nim:h-swab-dry"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0876","stem":"Suspect dust lies thinly on a roughened, irregular metal surface, and the client wants a culturable sample as well as microscopy. Which method from the manual fits best?","options":["Swab sampling with a dry filter circle","Tape sampling with clear 1 inch tape","Filtration sampling into a cassette","Bulk sampling scooped into a sealed container"],"expl":"Swabs work on smooth, roughened, or irregular surfaces and serve both microscopy and culturable samples. Tape is meant mainly for smooth surfaces, and filtration is not recommended for culturable bacteria.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Sampling methods","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix E","units":["nim:h-swab-surfaces"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0877","stem":"According to the manual, where in a duct system do heavier particles tend to drop out of the airstream?","options":["At bends, dampers, and branch take-offs","At screws, joints, and turning vanes","At registers, diffusers, and room air","Along straight, smooth runs of steady flow"],"expl":"Heavier particles settle where smooth flow is interrupted, such as bends, dampers, and take-offs, because the unsteady airflow lets them fall out. Screws and joints are where lighter particles tend to stick; otherwise light particles blow through to the registers.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Particle deposition","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","units":["nim:h-dep-light","nim:h-dep-heavy"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0878","stem":"Camera footage of a supply duct shows a thin gray film on the duct bottom. It is transparent but dulls the reflection of the camera light. How does the manual characterize this?","options":["Not unusual and generally of little concern","A heavy deposit calling for duct cleaning","Likely microbial growth that calls for sampling","A sign of filter bypass that calls for repair"],"expl":"A light gray film on the interior bottom of ductwork is described as not unusual and generally of little concern; it typically leaves a transparent film that dulls reflected light. It does not by itself call for cleaning or sampling.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Particle deposition","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","units":["nim:h-gray-film"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0879","stem":"A building owner plans to rebalance the HVAC system and replace a supply fan next month. What does the manual suggest doing before that work?","options":["Check the cleanliness of the ductwork","Upgrade the filters to a higher MERV","Seal every joint in the supply ducts","Sample the ducts for microbial growth"],"expl":"Rebalancing, damper changes, and fan replacement can raise air velocity and blow settled particles into occupied space, so ductwork cleanliness should be checked first. Sampling is not the step the manual names here.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Particle deposition","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","units":["nim:h-rebalance"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0880","stem":"An office building has four floors, each served by a nearly identical duct system from its own rooftop unit, and no complaints exist. In the manual's case study, how were duct systems picked for detailed inspection?","options":["Two chosen with a random number generator","The two systems nearest the roof access","Every one of the four duct systems","The one system with the oldest filters"],"expl":"Because the four duct systems were largely identical, a representative sample of two was chosen with a random number generator (floors 2 and 4). All four air handlers were inspected, but not all four duct systems.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Case study","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","units":["nim:h-case-random"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0881","stem":"In the case study, the client mentions that the third floor was extensively renovated four years ago for a tenant. How does the inspector handle System #3?","options":["He flags it for inspection rather than leave it to chance","He skips it, since its newer ducts are likely to be cleaner","He leaves it in the random draw with the other floors","He tests its insulation for asbestos before any work"],"expl":"Construction may have let debris and dust into System #3, and the renovation means it may no longer match the other systems, so random selection is no longer advisable and it is flagged for inspection.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Case study","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","units":["nim:h-case-renovation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0882","stem":"In the case study, the crew finds dried residue in AHU #1's condensate pan, suggesting standing water last summer. What did the inspector do next?","options":["Looked down that unit's riser with a push camera","Took a swab sample of the residue for lab culturing","Recommended cleaning all four duct systems right away","Replaced System #2 with System #1 in the plan"],"expl":"He lowered a push camera down AHU #1's riser to make sure pooled water had not caused microbial growth. It looked clean, so he kept to the original plan of Systems #2 and #4.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Case study","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","units":["nim:h-case-residue"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0883","stem":"In a case study alternative, the client sees a dark suspect stain in an AHU on video and asks that it not be sampled, preferring to just fix the problem. How does the inspector respond?","options":["He advises cleaning the coil and ductwork without sampling","He insists on a sample before recommending any cleaning","He leaves the stain out of the report because it is unconfirmed","He takes a sample anyway to limit his own liability"],"expl":"Sampling is generally outside an inspection's scope and done at the client's request, so Acme honored the request and recommended cleaning the coil and ductwork. The stain was still documented and shown to the client.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Case study","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D (Alternative D)","units":["nim:h-samp-scope","nim:h-case-no-sample"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0884","stem":"Two duct systems hold a yellowish, whitish dust. The client agrees to a bulk sample, and the lab finds a cellulose component. What source did the case study trace it to?","options":["Paper dust from a shredder near the return","Fibrous glass eroding off the duct liner surface","Mineral scale left by past water damage","Mold spores from a poorly draining pan"],"expl":"Cellulose indicated paper or cardboard dust, and a paper shredder sat near the air return for those systems, so moving it was recommended. Liner erosion would show glass fibers, not cellulose.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Case study","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D (Alternative F)","units":["nim:h-case-shredder"],"reserved":false},{"id":"cvi-0885","stem":"The case study building had no record of its last coil cleaning and no as-built static pressure across the coils. What did the sample report recommend for the coils?","options":["Clean them and record pressure drop as a baseline","Run a static pressure test now to find internal coil dirt","Leave them alone, since the coil faces looked clean","Replace them, since their history was not known"],"expl":"Without as-built values a pressure drop test for internal dirt was not practical, so the report recommended cleaning the coils and collecting static pressure drop as a baseline for timing future cleanings.","domain":"Inspection","sub":"Case study","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA HVAC Inspection Manual Appendix D","units":["nim:h-case-coil-baseline"],"reserved":false}],"built":"2026-09-24"});
